like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HLX_1                                             @1£˜ŒŸá¿GÖ∏QÏ@rSÀ)^û¿Qß¿6‚Î@qµçπ@ÎwQ±	eÉ@Îw÷x%◊J30001030002A␀C]óˆ+A␀˙πıY¥A?˘wökAB;Çﬁ␀“SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‰CVê‡Ö␀␀›-Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀¡Ü                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cas_A_MOS2                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îw÷x%◊J@Îw◊x%◊J40021002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‰B˙·GÆ␀␀ﬁ[Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Shell supernova remnants are of interest to NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization addresses questions of particle acceleration and the origin of cosmic rays. Pulsar-wind nebulae exhibit particles accelerated in pulsar magnetospheres and/or at relativistic wind termination shocks. Their synchrotron radiation contains morphological and spectral clues to the nature of particle acceleration and propagation.                                                                                