tions of a sample of nearby galaxy clusters will be undertaken to detect and characterize the controversial non-thermal component emerging at high energies and to characterize the hot thermal emission in the vicinity of shock fronts. Merger shocks in galaxy clusters are important because they allow us to study the merger phenomenon by providing indications of the velocity and geometry of mergers and also provide important clues on the physical properties of the ICM.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GCL       Galaxy Clusters and Relics                                                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Abell2256_NW                                      @o˝Ø∑È@SØ‡ﬁ“àŒ@[»∞Ú{≤ˇ@?∞ÿD–©@b9Dg8~@ÎÖk±	eÉ@ÎÖ~±	eÉ70001054003@ﬁüÃ2 W®@ﬁñ∆&ÄùI@‡VÿŒp:˚@‡X8Œp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„ˇOtnæ␀␀›vHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of a sample of nearby galaxy clusters will be undertaken to detect and characterize the controversial non-thermal component emerging at high energies and to characterize the hot thermal emission in the vicinity of shock fronts. Merger shocks in galaxy clusters are important because they allow us to study the merger phenomenon by providing indications of the velocity and geometry of mergers and also provide important clues on the physical properties of the ICM.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GCL       Galaxy Clusters and Relics                                                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Arp299                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÖ~±	eÉ@ÎÖ"–Åı50002041001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï„ˇ!¡␀␀␀›–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Our goal is to characterize the hard X-ray properties of nearby star-forming galaxies. We will investigate the bulk hard X-ray properties of the binaries, characterize the hard X-ray emission from the "famous" ULX source M82 X-1, and place a constraint on any diffuse Inverse Compton component, which might have implications for starburst galaxies' contribution to the 10-40 keV cosmic X-ray background. We also plan to perform deep observations of Local Group galaxies M31 and M33.                                                                             