ucture of AGN jets, primarily their content and the location of the dissipation region. Emission from those sources is variable, and crucial constraints are derived from multi-band monitoring campaigns. The program includes two classes of jet-dominated AGN, those with powerful jets detected as strong GeV emitters by Fermi, and less luminous BL Lac type objects weaker in the GeV band, but are strong TeV emitters detected by Cerenkov ground-based arrays.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀BLZ       Blazars                                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mkn421                                            @d¬y=Ÿc@CÀ)^û@fz∏QÎÖ@P6è\(ı√@5æ!ñRΩ<@Îä ±	eÉ@Îä+^∫Ÿ60002023039@œ*ÌîØO@œ!Fµ(@–ø,ﬁ“àŒ@–ø,ﬁ“àŒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„Ÿ∏„é9␀␀›–Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The goal of the Blazar program is to determine the structure of AGN jets, primarily their content and the location of the dissipation region. Emission from those sources is variable, and crucial constraints are derived from multi-band monitoring campaigns. The program includes two classes of jet-dominated AGN, those with powerful jets detected as strong GeV emitters by Fermi, and less luminous BL Lac type objects weaker in the GeV band, but are strong TeV emitters detected by Cerenkov ground-based arrays.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀BLZ       Blazars                                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              MAGIC VERITAS Swift                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC5204_X1                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îä+^∫Ÿ@Îä+îóûg30002037001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï„Ÿ/˘Óé␀␀ﬁ[Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                     