œX␀␀ﬁ[Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Shell supernova remnants are of interest to NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization addresses questions of particle acceleration and the origin of cosmic rays. Pulsar-wind nebulae exhibit particles accelerated in pulsar magnetospheres and/or at relativistic wind termination shocks. Their synchrotron radiation contains morphological and spectral clues to the nature of particle acceleration and propagation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀SNR       Supernovae Remnants (SNRs) and Pulsar Wind Nebulae (PWNe)                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429Cas_A_MOS3                                        @uÓˆ˝!ˇ.@Mk–|Ñµ›@[Ï ƒõ•„¿Hçπ@bÊi≠B√ @Îèx%◊J@ÎèıM©»40021003002@…b˝zxl"@…iAT…Ö@Ã€W>´6@Ã€W>´6SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„¶Çÿ-É␀␀ﬁ[Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Shell supernova remnants are of interest to NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization