␀␀ﬁ[Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Shell supernova remnants are of interest to NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization addresses questions of particle acceleration and the origin of cosmic rays. Pulsar-wind nebulae exhibit particles accelerated in pulsar magnetospheres and/or at relativistic wind termination shocks. Their synchrotron radiation contains morphological and spectral clues to the nature of particle acceleration and propagation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀SNR       Supernovae Remnants (SNRs) and Pulsar Wind Nebulae (PWNe)                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cas_A_Ti1                                         @uÌ◊€Ùá˝@Miå~($@[Èè≈Åo¿QN;Õ5®@␀N§®¡U@Î¢j±	eÉ@Î£Õ{,†40021011002A˛7È˘AÙ`ﬁ“âA©zπ#¢úA™*π#¢úSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„}µ⁄’%␀␀ﬁ[Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Shell supernova remnants are of interest to NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization ad