ee components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C279                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î™?"–Åı@Î™?±	eÉ60002020003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‚tßA␀␀›–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The goal of the Blazar program is to determine the structure of AGN jets, primarily their content and the location of the dissipation region. Emission from those sources is variable, and crucial constraints are derived from multi-band monitoring campaigns. The program includes two classes of jet-dominated AGN, those with powerful jets detected as strong GeV emitters by Fermi, and less luminous BL Lac type objects weaker in the GeV band, but are strong TeV emitters detected by Cerenkov ground-based arrays.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀BLZ       Blazars                                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C279                                             @hBÀí:*¿É‰%ÆÊ@sºj~˘€@LíπıY≥–@f#o“ÚÂ@Î™?±	eÉ@Î™]îóûg60002020004@‰Áﬁ“àŒ@‰›óØ∑È@ÊÖø6zë@ÊÜ6zëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‚vT2␀␀›–Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The goal of the Blazar program is to determine the structure of AGN jets, primarily their content and the location of the dissipation region. Emission from those sources is variable, and crucial constraints are derived from multi-band monitoring campaigns. The program includes two classes of jet-dominated AGN, those with powerful jets detected as strong GeV emitters by Fermi, and less luminous BL Lac type objects weaker in the GeV band, but are strong TeV emitters detected by Cerenkov ground-based arrays.                                                                                                      