NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization addresses questions of particle acceleration and the origin of cosmic rays. Pulsar-wind nebulae exhibit particles accelerated in pulsar magnetospheres and/or at relativistic wind termination shocks. Their synchrotron radiation contains morphological and spectral clues to the nature of particle acceleration and propagation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀SNR       Supernovae Remnants (SNRs) and Pulsar Wind Nebulae (PWNe)                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Tycho                                             @âQÇ©ì@PÆ}VlÙ@^␀ÎÌ˙C˛?ÙÁ’fœAÚ@kﬁHé@Î∂·?BI@Î∑ùx%◊J40020001002A¨ê§Z¨A£Bu%FABî(ı√AB¿(ı√SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·◊L¥á␀␀ﬂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Shell supernova remnants are of interest to NuSTAR for two principal reasons. X-ray synchrotron emission is known to extend beyond 10 keV in several young remnants; its characterization addresses questions of particle acceleration and the origin of cosmic rays. Pulsar-wind nebulae exhibit particles accelerated in pulsar magnetospheres and/or at relativistic wind termination shocks. Their synchrotron radiation contains morphological and spectral clues to the nature of particle acceleration and propagation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀SNR       Supernovae Remnants (SNRs) and Pulsar Wind Nebulae (PWNe)                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429V4641_Sgr                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î∑ùx%◊J@Î∑û[¥.80002012003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï·÷ÎTì'␀␀ﬁ[Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                       