N 069 and NGC 2617, establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as an unexplored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn. We propose three joint XMM-Newton / NuSTAR observations (start, maximum and 1/e) of the next suited AGN outburst whereas an X-ray/optical/UV light curve will be established with short Swift observations. True AGN outbursts will allow us to trace accretion physics, X-ray spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive stellar tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime. We will identify outbursts mainly based on Swift, XMM-Newton slew and Gaia observations.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HE1136m2304                                       @e’iDg8¿7aN;Õ5®@qùä	ﬁ@BE:í£@OAÚ◊s@Î¡^∫Ÿ@Î¡^∫Ÿ80002031002@◊?Ω•ù@◊>Ñ]càf@ÿ˜àﬁ“àŒ@ÿ˚àﬁ“àŒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·ÿvT2␀␀ﬂÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀!éRecent X-ray observations of the two Seyfert galaxies, GSN 069 and NGC 2617, establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as an unexplored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn. We propose three joint XMM-Newton / NuSTAR observations (start, maximum and 1/e) of the next suited AGN outburst whereas an X-ray/optical/UV light curve will be established with short Swift observations. True AGN outbursts will allow us to trace accretion physics, X-ray spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive stellar tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime. We will identify outbursts mainly based on Swift, XMM-Newton slew and Gaia observations.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429HE1136m2304                                       @e’©˚Álã¿7\V’œ™Œ@qùÚ‰éä@BGßÜ¬&Å@3–|Ñµ›@Î¡^∫Ÿ@Î¡M[¥.80002031003@Ô	ßqué@Ô‰éär@´Ym≈÷9@¨M‘,<üSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·XE»°␀␀ﬂÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀!éRecent X-ray observations of the two Seyfert galaxies, GSN 069 and NGC 2617, establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as an unexplored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn. We propose three joint XMM-Newton / NuSTAR observations (start, maximum and 1/e) of the next suited AGN outburst whereas an X-ray/optical/UV light curve will be established with short Swift observations. True AGN outbursts will allow us to trace accretion physics, X-ray spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive stellar tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime. We will identify outbursts mainly based on Swift, XMM-Newton slew and Gaia observations.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀aim point refinement                                                                                                    archived  20200429SgrAstar                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¡M[¥.@Î¡N"–Åı30001002009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï·A;ã␀␀ﬂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations.  We also plan to study diffuse X-ray reflection nebulae near the Galactic center that may provide evidence for a more active period of accretion for the supermassive black hole at Sgr A*.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison          