SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·è+ùe␀␀ﬂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations.  We also plan to study diffuse X-ray reflection nebulae near the Galactic center that may provide evidence for a more active period of accretion for the supermassive black hole at Sgr A*.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GPS       Galactic Plane Survey                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Spitzer Chandra                                                                                     ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC1313                                           @HŒö‘,<ü¿Pù=Ÿb∑@qµﬁ“àŒp¿FQXbM”@\èÙá¸π$@Î¡y[¥.@Î¡z?BI80001032001@b¥PH@b™∫«À)@c‡␀␀␀␀␀@c‡␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï·QY‚k␀␀ﬂÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"Recently our understanding of Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULX) has advanced significantly due to broad band spectro-temporal studies with XMM Newton, Suzaku and NuSTAR. Evidence now suggests that many ULXs with LX 1040ergs 1 are powered by super-Eddington accretion onto black holes with masses up to 100 Mo. This extreme accretion regime remains poorly understood. Observations show that ULXs are variable on timescales of weeks and exhibit distinct spectral states characterized by multiple components in the 0.3 - 30 keV band. To understand these components we propose to trigger a NuSTAR and XMM Newton observation of one of the bright ULXs in our sample in a different spectral state than previously observed.                                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Sampling ULX spectral states with XMM Newton and NuSTAR                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC1313                                           @H…«y¶µ¿PúÉnóç@qµÁlãCñ¿FS¿6‚Î@\∫a|⁄Q@Î¡z?BI@Î¡°±	eÉ80001032002@Ó˛j~˘€@ÓS8}ø@äk4÷°b@å[4÷°bSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·Êﬂ√R␀␀ﬂÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"Recently our understanding of Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULX) has advanced significantly due to broad band spectro-temporal studies with XMM Newton, Suzaku and NuSTAR. Evidence now suggests that many ULXs with LX 1040ergs 1 are powered by super-Eddington accretion onto black holes with masses up to 100 Mo. This extreme accretion regime remains poorly understood. Observations show that ULXs are variable on timescales of weeks and exhibit distinct spectral states characterized by multiple components in the 0.3 - 30 keV band. To understand these components we propose to trigger a NuSTAR and XMM Newton observation of one of the bright ULXs in our sample in a different spectral state than previously observed.                                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Sampling ULX spectral states with XMM Newton and NuSTAR                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429V709_Cas                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¡°±	eÉ@Î¡¢±	eÉ30001145001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï·≥ß¢␀␀ﬂÉHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"HWe propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 3 intermediate polars (IPs) to test our understanding of reflection, widely observed in accreting binaries and in active galactic nuclei. A physical fit requires a consistent treatment of the reflection bump and the 6.4 keV line, but only the latter has been reliably observed in IPs to date. We will fit the phase-averaged spectra of our targets to derive the reflection amplitude. We will also measure the spin modulation in the reflection component in IPs for the first time. This will help verify the theory of angle dependent reflection, help break any remaining degeneracy in fitting the phase-averaged spectra, and allow us to determine the system geometry of individual IPs with non-sinusoidal X-ray spin modulations.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Testing Reflection in Intermediate Polars: Joint XMM-Newton+NuSTAR Observations                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V709_Cas                                          @◊
=p£◊@Mß8}øH@]¸$xG¿ÑM:ì@`Ÿb∂Æ}@Î¡¢±	eÉ@Î¡¥?BI30001145002@ÿˆ±ûÉ‰&@ÿÛAâ7L@⁄’åC,•z@⁄⁄LC,•zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·iX␀␀ﬂÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"HWe propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 3 intermediate polars (IPs) to test our understanding of reflection, widely observed in accreting binaries and in active galactic nuclei. A physical fit requires a consistent treatment of the reflection bump and the 6.4 keV line, but only the latter has been reliably observed in IPs to date. We will fit the phase-averaged spectra of our targets to derive the reflection amplitude. We will also measure the spin modulation in the reflection component in IPs for the first time. This will help verify the theory of angle dependent reflection, help break any remaining degeneracy in fitting the phase-averaged spectra, and allow us to determine the system geometry of individual IPs with non-sinusoidal X-ray spin modulations.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Testing Reflection in Intermediate Polars: Joint XMM-Newton+NuSTAR Observations                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PG_1211p143                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¡¥?BI@Î¡µ"–Åı60001100006␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï·>c]≠␀␀ﬂÉHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR observations of bright, nearby AGN will provide the best constraints to date on the corona temperature, breaking the degeneracy between two key corona parameters, temperature and optical depth. The target sample be used to test how Comptonization models, and particularly the assumed geometry, depend on physical parameters. NuSTAR will be an ideal instrument to use in the effort to constrain black hole spin and differentiate between complex absorption and reflection models.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀AGN       AGN Physics:Black Hole Spin + Coronal Temperature                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1211p143                                       @fÒì›óˆ+@,ñªò«„@p≠>´6z@Rêy=Ÿc@v-V@Î¡µ"–Åı@Î¡Ï±	eÉ60001100007@ÚH^o␀h‹@Ú?'=Ÿc@Ûó<‚e,@Ûó|‚e,SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï·∂Â‘ƒ␀␀ﬂÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR observations of bright, nearby AGN will provide the best constraints to date on the corona temperature, breaking the degeneracy between two key corona parameters, temperature and optical depth. The target sample be used to test how Comptonization models, and particularly the assumed geometry, depend on physical parameters. NuSTAR will be an ideal instrument to use in the effort to constrain black hole spin and differentiate between complex absorption and reflection models.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀AGN       AGN Physics:Black Hole Spin + Coronal Temperature                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC5907                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¡Ï±	eÉ@Î¡Ì?BI80001042001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï·}—∫˙␀␀ﬂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                  