 X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ03502377m5018354                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î≈√îóûg@Î≈ƒ^∫Ÿ60061331001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡Ôe‘√≥␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ03502377m5018354                            @Lœ\(ı¬è¿I"-V@p;eîØO¿HÖ*ô0æ@a5m\˙¨⁄@Î≈ƒ^∫Ÿ@Î≈”^∫Ÿ60061331002@÷¢¥€ã¨q@÷ëñi≠Bƒ@ÿJEóˆ+k@ÿKEóˆ+kSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡Ô"≥ƒ÷␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NY_Lup                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î≈”^∫Ÿ@Î≈”Õ{,†30001146001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡Ó¸ç␀␀ﬂ™Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"HWe propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 3 intermediate polars (IPs) to test our understanding of reflection, widely observed in accreting binaries and in active galactic nuclei. A physical fit requires a consistent treatment of the reflection bump and the 6.4 keV line, but only the latter has been reliably observed in IPs to date. We will fit the phase-averaged spectra of our targets to derive the reflection amplitude. We will also measure the spin modulation in the reflection component in IPs for the first time. This will help verify the theory of angle dependent reflection, help break any remaining degeneracy in fitting the phase-averaged spectra, and allow us to determine the system geometry of individual IPs with non-sinusoidal X-ray spin modulations.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Testing Reflection in Intermediate Polars: Joint XMM-Newton+NuSTAR Observations                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NY_Lup                                            @m°B√…ÓÃ¿FøOÑM@tƒoiDg@£◊
=p§@uJá¸π#£@Î≈”Õ{,†@Î≈Á"–Åı30001146002@÷{Ê0U2a@÷{π˙C˛]@ÿ^•ú‡@ÿcŸ•ú‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡Ó≥333␀␀ﬂ™Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"HWe propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 3 intermediate polars (IPs) to test our understanding of reflection, widely observed in accreting binaries and in active galactic nuclei. A physical fit requires a consistent treatment of the reflection bump and the 6.4 keV line, but only the latter has been reliably observed in IPs to date. We will fit the phase-averaged spectra of our targets to derive the reflection amplitude. We will also measure the spin modulation in the reflection component in IPs for the first time. This will help verify the theory of angle dependent reflection, help break any remaining degeneracy in fitting the phase-averaged spectra, and allow us to determine the system geometry of individual IPs with non-sinusoidal X-ray spin modulations.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Testing Reflection in Intermediate Polars: Joint XMM-Newton+NuSTAR Observations                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HESSJ1640m465                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î≈Á"–Åı@Î≈Áx%◊J30002021024␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ÓåSo‚␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESSJ1640m465                                     @oES&¬¿GE„º”[@u#:˚~ë␀?¡|⁄QŒ@tÆá¸π#£@Î≈Áx%◊J@Î≈ˆ[¥.30002021025@’mOéÛMj@’fﬁnóçP@÷œ‘ÃÃÃÕ@÷œ‘ÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡ÓO\(ˆ␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Monitoring Campaign                                                                                                     archived  20200429PKS2356m61                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î≈ˆ[¥.@Î≈˜^∫Ÿ60061330001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡Ó(NÇ␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                              