GNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC7130                                           @tq∏QÎÖ¿A{a@N•@#°$›/¿I3úæ‡@n∞Ú{≤˛≈@Î∆ﬂ[¥.@Î∆Ó^∫Ÿ60061347002@‘∞°fœAÚ@‘™≤-V@÷65‰˜e˛@÷8u‰˜e˛SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡ÈÛ›∞␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC1068                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î∆Ó^∫Ÿ@Î∆ÓÈÏÛº60002033001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡È≤âJ∑␀␀‡dHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀!We propose four, logarithmically spaced, 50 ks XMM-Newton observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2. This source is likely accreting at a high rate, so it is expected to be intrinsically highly variable. Previous works have indeed claimed both spectral and continuum variability, although between observations performed with different spectrometers and satellites. The four proposed observations will permit to search for variations of the reflected components on time scales spanning from a week to half a year and, when compared with previous XMM-Newton observations, up to several years. This program will allow us to both unambiguously confirm previous claims of such variations and tightly constrain                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Locating the reflecting regions in the archetypal Compton-thick Sy2 NGC1068                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC1068                                           @DV’œ™Õü?ä‘,<ûÏ¿@e}Êö‘,=¿J	N;Õ5®@a•Ú◊s@Î∆ÓÈÏÛº@Î«[¥.60002033002@ÈjÁä⁄πı@È^E˚µt@ÎCõ=»@ÎFçõ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡È@␀␀␀␀␀‡dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀!We propose four, logarithmically spaced, 50 ks XMM-Newton observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2. This source is likely accreting at a high rate, so it is expected to be intrinsically highly variable. Previous works have indeed claimed both spectral and continuum variability, although between observations performed with different spectrometers and satellites. The four proposed observations will permit to search for variations of the reflected components on time scales spanning from a week to half a year and, when compared with previous XMM-Newton observations, up to several years. This program will allow us to both unambiguously confirm previous claims of such variations and tightly constrain                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Locating the reflecting regions in the archetypal Compton-thick Sy2 NGC1068                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429eta_Car                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î«[¥.@Î«?BI30002010009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡È'qf␀␀ﬂ™Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316eta_Car                                           @d