8F‹]SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡ìUÖ‡Á␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HESSJ1640m465                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î…Ó"–Åı@Î…Óîóûg30002021026␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ì1~K␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESSJ1640m465                                     @oDπıY≥–¿GF±ƒ2 X@u#F‹]d?¡$›/†@uÖ≈m\˙≠@Î…Óîóûg@Î…¸x%◊J30002021027@”ﬂæqﬁi≠@”€Àí:@’C‰À)^û@’E‰À)^ûSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡íÏ_í∆␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Monitoring Campaign                                                                                                     archived  202004291FGLJ1018d6m5856                                  @cV—∑X‚¿Ms√a@O@q√®Xy=Ÿø˙1&Èx‘˛@T9\˙¨ŸË@Î…¸x%◊J@Î…˝^∫Ÿ30002020005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡í«}àÍ␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161FGLJ1018d6m5856                                  @cU⁄QŒ¿Mtõ•„S¯@q√Å◊€Ùàø˙jÁ’fœB@TIîØOÑ@Î…˝^∫Ÿ@Î ^∫Ÿ30002020006@◊Óø6zë@◊ﬁã◊
=q@ŸÑºxG@ŸÑ<xGSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡íÑ∏≥␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SDSSJ0758p3923                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î ^∫Ÿ@Î ^∫Ÿ60001131001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡í\q«␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ0758p3923                                    @]Á~êˇó$@C±#¢úwö@fôﬁi≠Bƒ@=¢3úø@f@€ã¨q@Î ^∫Ÿ@Î ,^∫Ÿ60001131002@Ê	n±ƒ2 @ÊÌ+J@Áï@∑Ä4n@Áï†∑Ä4nSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡í␀a#␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SwiftJ1753d5m0127                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î ,^∫Ÿ@Î -"–Åı30001148001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ë—∫˘ç␀␀ﬂ—Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"¿We propose a 40ksec joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observation of the black hole binary Swift J1753.5-0127 in its persistent faint low/hard state. This will constrain the radial stratification of the corona and the spectral curvature of the low/hard state continuum in the unprecedented 0.7-70keV band. This will then give the most unambigious measure of the strength of the soft X-ray component and resolve some of the controversies over the accretion geometry. The observation will also help with ongoing calibration/cross- calibration efforts for both satellites. This observation is not time critical, and will be feasible when it best suits the satellites' schedules. An ongoing AMI LA radio monitoring programme of Swift J1753.5-0127 will be adapted to coincide with this XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observationGalactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Kolehmainen                   Mari                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       The soft X-ray component and the hard continuum curvature in black hole binaries                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SwiftJ1753d5m0127                                 @p≈ù≤-Vø˜ßÔù≤-@8«ökP∞Ú@(≤ WßÜ¬@uiDg8@Î -"–Åı@Î Mîóûg30001148002@„æ-B√…Ô@„√„ó$tT@ÂÌ$‚e,@ÂÔd‚e,SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡ëb	‹Z␀␀ﬂ—Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"¿We propose a 40ksec joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observation of the black hole binary Swift J1753.5-0127 in its persistent faint low/hard state. This will constrain the radial stratification of the corona and the spectral curvature of the low/hard state continuum in the unprecedented 0.7-70keV band. This will then give the most unambigious measure of the strength of the soft X-ray component and resolve some of the controversies over the accretion geometry. The observation will also help with ongoing calibration/cross- calibration efforts for both satellites. This observation is not time critical, and will be feasible when it best suits the satellites' schedules. An ongoing AMI LA radio monitoring programme of Swift J1753.5-0127 will be adapted to coincide with this XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observationGalactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Kolehmainen                   Mari                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       The soft X-ray component and the hard continuum curvature in black hole binaries                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk3                                              @W}Dg8~@Q√ZÖáìﬁ@aÁúæﬂ§@6ùŸb∂Æ@dëÂ…‰@Î Mîóûg@Î Nx%◊J60002048003@_≈#¢úwö@_´¢úwök@`‡␀␀␀␀␀@`‡␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ë0*z␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR observations of bright, nearby AGN will provide the best constraints to date on the corona temperature, breaking the degeneracy between two key corona parameters, temperature and optical depth. The target sample be used to test how Comptonization models, and particularly the assumed geometry, depend on physical parameters. NuSTAR will be an ideal instrument to use in the effort to constrain black hole spin and differentiate between complex absorption and reflection models.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀AGN       AGN Physics:Black Hole Spin + Coronal Temperature                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                           