␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î aÕ{,†@Î bÈÏÛº40032018001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡êºç␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations.  We also plan to study diffuse X-ray reflection nebulae near the Galactic center that may provide evidence for a more active period of accretion for the supermassive black hole at Sgr A*.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GPS       Galactic Plane Survey                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GalSurvey_B_MOS018                                @p†0æÌ)¿=1&Èx’@vzBZÓc ?⁄YJÙÿE@t¡I∫^@Î bÈÏÛº@Î ÇÈÏÛº40032018002@‰É@N•@‰oø‰%ÆÊ@ÊàÍJå@Ê®ÍJåSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡êaÒñë␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations.  We also plan to study diffuse X-ray reflection nebulae near the Galactic center that may provide evidence for a more active period of accretion for the supermassive black hole at Sgr A*.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GPS       Galactic Plane Survey                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429V1223_Sgr                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î ÇÈÏÛº@Î É?BI30001144001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ê5yæ␀␀ﬂ–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"HWe propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 3 intermediate polars (IPs) to test our understanding of reflection, widely observed in accreting binaries and in active galactic nuclei. A physical fit requires a consistent treatment of the reflection bump and the 6.4 keV line, but only the latter has been reliably observed in IPs to date. We will fit the phase-averaged spectra of our targets to derive the reflection amplitude. We will also measure the spin modulation in the reflection component in IPs for the first time. This will help verify the theory of angle dependent reflection, help break any remaining degeneracy in fitting the phase-averaged spectra, and allow us to determine the system geometry of individual IPs with non-sinusoidal X-ray spin modulations.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Testing Reflection in Intermediate Polars: Joint XMM-Newton+NuSTAR Observations                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V1223_Sgr                                         @qª”ZÖáî¿?1o␀h€å@Np:˚~ë¿,\Ñµ‹∆?@td◊€Ùá˝@Î É?BI@Î ëÕ{,†30001144002@”ıSÕ5®X@”ÔK¥¢3ú@’É¸ÃÃÃÕ@’Ö|ÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡èãëB␀␀ﬂ–Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"HWe propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 3 intermediate polars (IPs) to test our understanding of reflection, widely observed in accreting binaries and in active galactic nuclei. A physical fit requires a consistent treatment of the reflection bump and the 6.4 keV line, but only the latter has been reliably observed in IPs to date. We will fit the phase-averaged spectra of our targets to derive the reflection amplitude. We will also measure the spin modulation in the reflection component in IPs for the first time. This will help verify the theory of angle dependent reflection, help break any remaining degeneracy in fitting the phase-averaged spectra, and allow us to determine the system geometry of individual IPs with non-sinusoidal X-ray spin modulations.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Testing Reflection in Intermediate Polars: Joint XMM-Newton+NuSTAR Observations                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GRS1734m292                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î ëÕ{,†@Î í"–Åı60061279001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡è ûá∆␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harri