ona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀AGN       AGN Physics:Black Hole Spin + Coronal Temperature                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGRJ18214m1318                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î ¿x%◊J@Î ¡±	eÉ30001140001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡éâ2q␀␀ﬂ’Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀!£Through its hard X-ray survey of the Galactic Plane, INTEGRAL has uncovered large numbers of HMXBs, and many of them have not been well-studied to date. We propose to observe two INTEGRAL HMXBs that have not previously shown signatures of having a neutron star: IGR J18214-1318 and IGR J08262-3736. With XMM-Newton and NuSTAR, we will search for such signatures (pulsations, cyclotron lines, exponential cutoffs with e-folding energies below ~20 keV). If these features are lacking and if the source instead has a power-law that extends to the top of the NuSTAR bandpass, this would be an excellent black hole candidate and motivation would be very strong for follow-up optical or IR spectroscopy to determine the mass of the compact object.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Is there a Black Hole in any of the INTEGRAL HMXBs?                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ18214m1318                                    @q4ËßÊõ¿*≠w1è≈@1åC,•zx?Â"–Â`Aâ@t‚ÆÊ1¯°@Î ¡±	eÉ@Î ‘îóûg30001140002@Ÿ¨9≤ïÍ@Ÿ†;Uõ=@€ã9îØO@€çyîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡éBß°Ú␀␀ﬂ’Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀!£Through its hard X-ray survey of the Galactic Plane, INTEGRAL has uncovered large numbers of HMXBs, and many of them have not been well-studied to date. We propose to observe two INTEGRAL HMXBs that have not previously shown signatures of having a neutron star: IGR J18214-1318 and IGR J08262-3736. With XMM-Newton and NuSTAR, we will search for such signatures (pulsations, cyclotron lines, exponential cutoffs with e-folding energies below ~20 keV). If these features are lacking and if the source instead has a power-law that extends to the top of the NuSTAR bandpass, this would be an excellent black hole candidate and motivation would be very strong for follow-up optical or IR spectroscopy to determine the mass of the compact object.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Is there a Black Hole in any of the INTEGRAL HMXBs?                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429H1743m322                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î ‘îóûg@Î ‘ÈÏÛº80001044001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡éåSp␀␀ﬂ‰Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316H1743m322                                         @p®Ÿb∂Æ¿@ÄùIQÉ@vRcàeîØø˙åLò_@t¸µsÍ≥@Î ‘ÈÏÛº@Î ˇîóûg80001044002@Ë°µsÍ≥@Ë¨öÍJå@Ïrﬁa|⁄@Ïxﬁa|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡çsß¢␀␀ﬂ‰Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuS