[¥.@Î–[¥.30002041003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡t‡ÊñÚ␀␀‡7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SS433                                             @qˇ"–Â`B@»Œp:˚@C«'ª/ÏW¿␀·|⁄Q@sÂœ™ÕûÑ@Î–[¥.@Î–"–Åı30002041004@ÿ≥…GÆ{@ÿ≠ƒ64@⁄ÒClãCñ@⁄ÙlãCñSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡täU∂l␀␀‡7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ 2/10 observations                                                                                                      archived  202004294C74d26                                           @sjìtºj@R«v_ÿ≠¨@[<,<ûÏ¿@3|<ûÏø±@u
†ê-‡@Î–"–Åı@Î–Õ{,†60001080005?ÿ,<ûÏø±␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀?Ÿôôôôôö␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡t`m:␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR observations of bright, nearby AGN will provide the best constraints to date on the corona temperature, breaking the degeneracy between two key corona parameters, temperature and optical depth. The target sample be used to test how Comptonization models, and particularly the assumed geometry, depend on physical parameters. NuSTAR will be an ideal instrument to use in the effort to constrain black hole spin and differentiate between complex absorption and reflection models.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀AGN       AGN Physics:Black Hole Spin + Coronal Temperature                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004294C74d26                                           @sj{≤˛≈m@R«‚Ç@∑Ä@[<∆?@3{”√a@@t˛cÒA \@Î–Õ{,†@Î–Lx%◊J60001080006@ˆ2Jzêó@ˆ+/á+@˜”û4Í@˜÷>4ÍSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡sÂº}Í␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR observations of bright, nearby AGN will provide the best constraints to date on the corona temperature, breaking the degeneracy between two key corona parameters, temperature and optical depth. The target sample be used to test how Comptonization models, and particularly the assumed geometry, depend on physical parameters. NuSTAR will be an ideal instrument to use in the effort to constrain black hole spin and differentiate between complex absorption and reflection models.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀AGN       AGN Physics:Black Hole Spin + Coronal Temperature                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429WISEJ1036p0449                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î–Lx%◊J@Î–M[¥.60001156001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡s≤@y]␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WISEJ1036p0449                                    @cÁä⁄πıZ@]càeîØ@n.åÁØ∏@Iq	kπå~@eø¸PH@Î–M[¥.@Î–T±	eÉ60001156002@≈—›´üUõ@≈ Eˆ˝!ˇ@«º–©*@«º–©*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡ss¨ê␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                  