ardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429LMC_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î—Gx%◊J@Î—H[¥.30001143001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡m∞BøË␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"BWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of the stellar mass black hole X-ray binary LMC X-1. These broadband observations will be the first to simultaneously constrain both the soft thermal component from the accretion disk around the black hole and the non-thermal continuum emission that extends into the hard X-ray band. The improved energy resolution of NuSTAR and the overlap between the XMM and NuSTAR bands will improve the measurement of the black hole spin via the broad iron line. The soft thermal component has short timescale (hours) variability of unknown origin; we will improve the temporal resolution of the continuum components by roughly an order of magnitude over previous observations and shed light on the nature of these variations.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Grefenstette                  Brian                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       A broadband, time-resolved study of the persistent Stellar Mass Black Hole LMC X1                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316LMC_X1                                            @U<Œp:˚¿QoJ#9¿Ï@qÉ>´6z¿?}Ÿb∂Æ@i«é!ñRΩ@Î—H[¥.@Î—h"–Åı30001143002@ÁUònóçP@ÁPÜLò_@ÈJ>Ô4÷°@ÈK.Ô4÷°SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡mCQän␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"BWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of the stellar mass black hole X-ray binary LMC X-1. These broadband observations will be the first to simultaneously constrain both the soft thermal component from the accretion disk around the black hole and the non-thermal continuum emission that extends into the hard X-ray band. The improved energy resolution of NuSTAR and the overlap between the XMM and NuSTAR bands will improve the measurement of the black hole spin via the broad iron line. The soft thermal component has short timescale (hours) variability of unknown origin; we will improve the temporal resolution of the continuum components by roughly an order of magnitude over previous observations and shed light on the nature of these variations.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Grefenstette                  Brian                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       A broadband, time-resolved study of the persistent Stellar Mass Black Hole LMC X1                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ04532576p0403416                            @RVö‘,<ü@=!ˇ.HÈ@hQõ=»K¿8\ë—N<@f»◊s¸@Î—h"–Åı@Î—hÈÏÛº60001158001@9f’œ™Õü@9m}øHÃ@;␀␀␀␀␀␀@;␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡m»?∑␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004292MASXJ04532576p0403416                            @RW√a@O@RΩ<64@hQP∞Ú{≥¿8ì›óˆ+@fy=Ÿc@Î—hÈÏÛº@Î—´îóûg60001158002@˜*␀ÛMj@˜˚˝Û∂F@¯–E2a|@¯—%2a|SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡lÇˇ>␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SS433                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î—´îóûg@Î—¨ÈÏÛº30002041005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡lE˘,`␀␀‡7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                        