We request a simultaneous monitoring with XMM and NuSTAR of Mrk915, a local, X-ray bright and obscured AGN where a significant variation in the NH (Delta NH~10^23cm-2) has been revealed by short exposure Swift data. We ask for a long observation (130ks) with XMM followed by 2 shorter (50ks each) at a distance of about 4 and 10 days from the first one, combined with 3 observations with NuSTAR of 50ks each, to be performed simultaneously to XMM. This will allow us to probe variability on timescales from <2 days (in the 130ks observation) to 10 days, placing strong constraints on the absorber structActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ballo                         Lucia                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Absorption variability in Mrk 915                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SADA_2014_342_52                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î‘Ò^∫Ÿ@Î‘Òîóûg10002105001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡]ãëB␀␀‡7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SS433                                             @qˇe+”√a@øó$tSè@C«º¿_o“Û@r:˙¨ŸË>@Î‘Òîóûg@Î’îóûg30002041010@¶•W>´6z@¶ô◊
=q@®√&Èx’@®œ&Èx’SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡]•‘√≥␀␀‡7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ 5/10 observations                                                                                                      archived  202004291RXSJ174538d1p290823                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î’îóûg@Î’^∫Ÿ60160674001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡]~Fã␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161RXSJ174538d1p290823                              @p¶˜ŒŸá@=r∞ ƒú@JÊYJÙÿ@:RZÓcä@pÊ1¯†ê@Î’^∫Ÿ@Î’Õ{,†60160674002@”—◊‚Ç@∏@”…ú4m≈÷@’I≤-V@’I≤-VSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡]>KÂ␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429UGC_12348                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î’Õ{,†@Î’îóûg60001147001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡]¶ﬂ√␀␀‡1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"=We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 10 AGN newly detected in the Swift-BAT all- sky maps using a weighting scheme optimized to detect nearby heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. These targets are likely among the brightest examples of reflection-dominated AGN available across the sky, providing the best targets for detailed study. The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology (XMM), and constrain the absorbing column and reflection (NuSTAR). The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN. This flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examplesActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC_12348                                         @u•Lò_˜?∆¬&ÄùIR@RŒÆ}VlÙ¿JJu%F
¶@syM:í£@Î’îóûg@Î’+îóûg60001147002@⁄É¸PH@⁄ÚˆîFt@‹#nd¬¯8@‹*.d¬¯8SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡\º˜FÏ␀␀‡1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"=We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 10 AGN newly detected in the Swift-BAT all- sky maps using a weighting scheme optimized to detect nearby heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. These targets are likely among the brightest examples of reflection-dominated AGN available across the sky, providing the best targets for detailed study. The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology (XMM), and constrain the absorbing column and reflection (NuSTAR). The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN. This flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examplesActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_21483p1352                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î’+îóûg@Î’,^∫Ÿ60001146001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡E–ó¥␀␀‡6Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"=We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 10 AGN newly detected in the Swift-BAT all- sky maps using a weighting scheme optimized to detect nearby heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. These targets are likely among the brightest examples of reflection-dominated AGN available across the sky, providing the best targets for detailed study. The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology (XMM), and constrain the absorbing column and reflection (NuSTAR). The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN. This flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examplesActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_21483p1352                                   @tzÊ1¯†ê@,%…„Ω@Qê∑Ä4m∆¿=≈Åo␀i@s+˘rGE9@Î’,^∫Ÿ@Î’?±	eÉ60001146002@Ÿlí_o“@Ÿaáêñªô@€Equé"@€Hqué"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡D‘z·H␀␀‡6Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"=We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of 10 AGN newly detected in the Swift-BAT all- sky maps using a weighting scheme optimized to detect nearby heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. These targets are likely among the brightest examples of reflection-dominated AGN available across the sky, providing the best targets for detailed study. The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology (XMM), and constrain the absorbing column and reflection (NuSTAR). The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN. This flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examplesActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Coma_MOS001                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î’?±	eÉ@Î’@±	eÉ70023001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡D©áeC␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of a sample of nearby galaxy clusters will be undertaken to detect and characterize the controversial non-thermal component emerging at high energies and to characterize the hot thermal emission in the vicinity of shock fronts. Merger shocks in galaxy clusters are important because they allow us to study the merger phenomenon by providing indications of the velocity and geometry of mergers and also provide important clues on the physical properties of the ICM.                                                                                                                                         