dies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC5135                                           @i/'ª/Ï¿=’'ª0@sydZ¨@@F°a‰˜f@eﬁ—∑X‚@ÎŸî"–Åı@ÎŸî±	eÉ60001153001@6ì@N§®¡@6ú˙¨ŸË>@8␀␀␀␀␀␀@8␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡,ã¡Õ.␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration reg