                     ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will be able to observe solar hard X-ray sources 200 times fainter than our current capabilities allow. This increase in sensitivity will open the door to truly pioneering observations, providing diagnostics for solar flare and, in particular, for nanoflare observations. Almost as important is NuSTAR's advantage in dynamic range, the ability to see a faint source (e.g. coronal source at the reconnection point) at the same time as a brighter one (e.g. source at the magnetic footpoint of a flare).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀SOL       Solar Observations + Solar Axions                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO438mG009                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î€ﬁÈÏÛº@Î€ﬂîóûg60160423001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ÚqÓ␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO438mG009                                       @d˜jÁ’fœ¿<'ª/ÏW@qU‚e+‘@=a¯†ê-‡@i∫($x@Î€ﬂîóûg@Î€Ô^∫Ÿ60160423002@’)ë0æÌ@’)„Ω@÷∏A…‰@÷∏Å…‰SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡Æç◊Ã␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                         