with NuSTAR has clearly revealed a relativistic Fe K line (confirming prior XMM-Newton results), as well as the Compton reflection hump. We propose to build on this by making two 40 ks XMM-Newton + NuSTAR observations of a transient neutron star LMXB in outburst. Leveraging their complementary strengths, this program will aim to constrain the stellar radius, search for disk winds like those seen in black holes (and a small but growing number of neutron stars), and to understand the evolution of neutron star accretion flows over a broader range of accretion rates than can be observed in persistent "Z'' and "atoll'' sources.                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Spectroscopy of a Neutron Star Transient in Outburst                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161RXSJ180408d9m342058                              @pÍ≥g†˘¿A)o␀h€å@vSûö¿ÃÁØ∑È@b”ÆÊ1¯°@ÎﬂÃx%◊J@ÎﬂÍ"–Åı80001040002@‚wÇ"h	’@‚àu•ú‡@Â¡uò«‚Ç@Â¿ıò«‚ÇSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡]››ﬁ␀␀‡õHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀!≤A recent observation of Serpens X-1 with NuSTAR has clearly revealed a relativistic Fe K line (confirming prior XMM-Newton results), as well as the Compton reflection hump. We propose to build on this by making two 40 ks XMM-Newton + NuSTAR observations of a transient neutron star LMXB in outburst. Leveraging t