   Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀NGS       Starburst Galaxies and Local Group Galaxies                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀2/2 observations                                                                                                        archived  20200429M31_MOS003                                        @&ß»K]Ãd@D…£n.±ƒ@^b)«y¶µ¿5[˚µsÎ@qñRΩ<6@Î‡4^∫Ÿ@Î‡Ä[¥.50026003003@˙9™=p£◊@˙/“Â`Aâ@¸ÓÅo␀i@¸>Åo␀iSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÍ~OWú␀␀‡ÙHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Our goal is to characterize the hard X-ray properties of nearby star-forming galaxies. We will investigate the bulk hard X-ray properties of the binaries, characterize the hard X-ray emission from the "famous" ULX source M82 X-1, and place a constraint on any diffuse Inverse Compton component, which might have implications for starburst galaxies' contribution to the 10-40 keV cosmic X-ray background. We also plan to perform deep observations of Local Group galaxies M31 and M33.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀NGS       Starburst Galaxies and Local Group Galaxies                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀2/2 observations                                                                                                        archived  20200811SS433                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î‡Ä[¥.@Î‡Å?BI30002041013␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡ï=ç␀␀‡7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SS433                                             @qˇ√a@O@©ì‡ﬂ@CŒˆîFsÇ¿␀ﬁûö@d"ﬂ§?Â…@Î‡Å?BI@Î‡ì±	eÉ30002041014@Ÿêı—N;Õ@ŸáÀº@€©å¬¯7µ@€©å¬¯7µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡@ë¢¥␀␀‡7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX339_4                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î‡ì±	eÉ@Î‡î[¥.80001015002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡\(ˆ␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX339_4                                           @o˜¡Ω•ù¿Hao␀h€å@u.Vì¿™ÕûÉ‰&@d+o␀h€å@Î‡î[¥.@Î‡≤"–Åı80001015003@›Wÿ÷°aÂ@ﬁ*´üæw@Á⁄AüUõ=@Á⁄ÅüUõ=SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‡␀}àÈ†␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR's hard X-ray sensitivity will allow for detailed studies of fainter X-ray binaries for the first time. This will include studies of HMXBs discovered by INTEGRAL but too faint to detect pulsations, cyclotron lines, or spectral breaks that would provide evidence that they have neutron star accretors. NuSTAR's energy resolution is unprecedented, and there are spectral features such as cyclotron lines from accreting pulsars and absorption edges in type I X-ray bursts that will be accessible to NuSTAR.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀XRB       Galactic Binaries, including HMXBs and LMXBs                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429HESSJ1640m465                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î‡≤"–Åı@Î‡≤[¥.30002021037␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‡␀P6ù␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                 