ü␀␀ﬂ“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SgrAstar                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î„Õ{,†@Î„?BI30002002007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÚ;B^—␀␀‡©Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"{A gas cloud, dubbed G2, was recently discovered to be falling towards Sgr A*. This allows us to witness the unique occurrence of a cloud interacting with the BH's close environment and likely partially accreting. We propose 4*50 ks XMM observations to: i) monitor the long term variations of the X-ray flux produced by Sgr A* as it interacts with the cloud; ii) monitor any variation, induced by the cloud flyby, to the frequency and intensity of Sgr A*'s flaring activity; iii) use the magnetar's light echo and its variations to scan the Galactic centre matter distribution around Sgr A*; iv) monitor the evolution of the superluminal echo reflected from the Bridge.                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Monitoring a close encounter between a small cloud and a supermassive BH                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SgrAstar                                          @pß/É{J#¿<˘rGE8Ô@v~!-w1ê?¿_ˆîFt@cπ©˚Álã@Î„?BI@Î„±	eÉ30002002008@Ÿ∞bM“Ú@Ÿ.ï∏QÏ@€ºµƒ2 X@€øıƒ2 XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂÒË≈6˛␀␀‡©Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"{A gas cloud, dubbed G2, was recently discovered to be falling towards Sgr A*. This allows us to witness the unique occurrence of a cloud interacting with the BH's close environment and likely partially accreting. We propose 4*50 ks XMM observations to: i) monitor the long term variations of the X-ray flux produced by Sgr A* as it interacts with the cloud; ii) monitor any variation, induced by the cloud flyby, to the frequency and intensity of Sgr A*'s flaring activity; iii) use the magnetar's light echo and its variations to scan the Galactic centre matter distribution around Sgr A*; iv) monitor the evolution of the superluminal echo reflected from the Bridge.                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Monitoring a close encounter between a small cloud and a supermassive BH                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM Keck VLA                                                                                        ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429UM614                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î„±	eÉ@Î„[¥.60160560001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÒ¿␀␀␀␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             