[¥.30001045003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÊ«q«␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations.  We also plan to study diffuse X-ray reflection nebulae near the Galactic center that may provide evidence for a more active period of accretion for the supermassive black hole at Sgr A*.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GPS       Galactic Plane Survey                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESSJ1834m087_TeV                                 @qj≥g†˘	¿!r˛≈m\˚@7(€ã¨qøªöu%F@bO·∞â†'@ÎÂ
[¥.@ÎÂ.[¥.30001045004@Á«ë≈Åo@Á∑{ïÅ%@Èì␀I∫^@ÈîÄI∫^SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂÊc€¯␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations.  We also plan to study diffuse X-ray reflection nebulae near the Galactic center that may provide evidence for a more active period of accretion for the supermassive black hole at Sgr A*.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀GPS       Galactic Plane Survey                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_6240                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÂ.[¥.@ÎÂ.Õ{,†60102042001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÊ1Møá␀␀·“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀@We propose to monitor the hard X-ray behavior of the Compton-thick AGN pair in the galaxy merger NGC 6240 with NuSTAR. Together with the existing 30-ks observation performed in March 2014, this series of three 20-ks snapshots will allow us to probe the source variability on timescales ranging from a few months to almost two years. Recent results from the Swift/BAT survey suggest a strong energy dependence of the excess variance, peaking around 20 keV. If driven by column density changes, the different spectral states would bring further insights on the structure and dynamics of the gas in proximity of the black holes. In any case, the increased total exposure will make it possible to perform a comprehensive analysis and take into account the dual AGN nature of the hard X-ray source.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nardini                       Emanuele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUCLEAR OBSCURATION IN THE AGN MERGER NGC 6240                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_6240                                          @o®+jÁ’g@zêñªô@4ØÀí:)«@;y†'RTa@aW˝ä⁄πı@ÎÂ.Õ{,†@ÎÂ=[¥.60102042002@‘Ù0æÌ@‘Èqv@÷C‡⁄Q@÷C‡⁄QSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂÂÔÄë£␀␀·“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀@We propose to monitor the hard X-ray behavior of the Compton-thick AGN pair in the galaxy merger NGC 6240 with NuSTAR. Together with the existing 30-ks observation performed in March 2014, this series of three 20-ks snapshots will allow us to probe the source variability on timescales ranging from a few months to almost two years. Recent results from the Swift/BAT survey suggest a strong energy dependence of the excess variance, peaking around 20 keV. If driven by column density changes, the different spectral states would bring further insights on the structure and dynamics of the gas in proximity of the black holes. In any case, the increased total exposure will make it possible to perform a comprehensive analysis and take into account the dual AGN nature of the hard X-ray source.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nardini                       Emanuele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUCLEAR OBSCURATION IN THE AGN MERGER NGC 6240                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SGR_1806m20                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÂ=[¥.@ÎÂ=Õ{,†30102038001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÂøûË›␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀éMagnetars are young isolated neutron stars with bright persistent X-ray emission and bursting behavior in hard X-rays/soft gamma-rays, caused by their extremely high magnetic fields. Here we propose to monitor one of the most prolific bursting magnetars, SGR 1806-20, with NuSTAR over the course of one year, to study the low-level bursting behavior of this source, its broad-band persistent X-ray emission, and the connection between these two properties. This will allow us to characterize the spectral and temporal properties of any low-level bursting activity, study the effect of low-level bursting activity on the erratic timing behavior of the source, track any change in torque, and characterize the hard X-ray spectral and temporal properties of SGR 1806-20.                                 Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    van der Horst                 Alexander                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE MAGNETAR SGR 1806-20                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_1806m20                                       @qﬁi≠Bƒ¿4ctºj~˙@#÷+jÁ’gøØæv»¥9X@c?|Ìëh@ÎÂ=Õ{,†@ÎÂWx%◊J30102038002@‡;MjÁ’g@‡+Sá+@·¿üA [¿@·¿A [¿SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂÂ`$h≠␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀éMagnetars are young isolated neutron stars with bright persistent X-ray emission and bursting behavior in hard X-rays/soft gamma-rays, caused by their extremely high magnetic fields. Here we propose to monitor one of the most prolific bursting magnetars, SGR 1806-20, with NuSTAR over the course of one year, to study the low-level bursting behavior of this source, its broad-band persistent X-ray emission, and the connection between these two properties. This will allow us to characterize the spectral and temporal properties of any low-level bursting activity, study the effect of low-level bursting activity on the erratic timing behavior of the source, track any change in torque, and characterize the hard X-ray spectral and temporal properties of SGR 1806-20.                                 Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    van der Horst                 Alexander                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE MAGNETAR SGR 1806-20                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SS433                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÂWx%◊J@ÎÂWÈÏÛº30002041017␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÂ.uí␀␀‡7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SS433                                             @qˇ∫^5?}@ÃÃÃÃÕ@Cœˆ+jÁ’¿␀—N;Õ5®@aŸáì›óˆ@ÎÂWÈÏÛº@ÎÂm"–Åı30002041018@⁄û¡†'RT@⁄ù¨j~˘€@›?ª>´6z@›Aª>´6zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ‰◊õ‡$␀␀‡7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Ultraluminous X-ray sources are point-like sources in nearby normal galaxies for which the implied luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit for an isotropically emitting black hole of 10 M_sun. Variability observed on the timescale of days, weeks, and years signals that most ULXs must be accreting sources, likely black holes feeding from companion stars in binary systems. Their luminosity suggests that the black hole is more massive than what one would expect to originate from normal stellar evolution.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀ULX       Ultraluminous X-ray Sources (ULXs)                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀9/10 observations                                                                                                       archived  20200429SADA_2015_109_52                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÂm"–Åı@ÎÂm±	eÉ10002107001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ‰®¨Ò5␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u50U2a|@A6_o“ @Wf¨1'¿4º"h	‘ï@eMçOﬂ;d@ÎÂm±	eÉ@ÎÂ}"–Åı40102014001@’ÊÈ[W>´@’‹hπ#¢ú@◊N∆kP∞Ú@◊N∆kP∞ÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ‰R≈˘,␀␀·#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≤Mass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of a H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our radio-to-gamma-ray follow-up. Our goal is to constrain the density and proximity of the ejected material, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor star. Unlike all the other H-stripped SNe, the radio and X-ray emission of SN14C is still increasing at t>300 days, giving us the unprecedented opportunity to map the density profile of the ejected material and constrain the epoch of its ejection in fine detail.Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C: FROM A H-STRIPPED EXPLOSION TO A STRONGLY INTERACTING SN                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk231                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÂ}"–Åı@ÎÂ}ÈÏÛº90102001003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ‰(¨Ò5␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           