ion of the transitional ms pulsar XSS J12270-4859 during its first radio pulsar phase ever discovered, aimed at constraining the physical mechanism producing its X-ray emission (e.g. the intrabinary shock between the pulsar wind and the mass lost by the companion), at deriving the first broad-band spectral energy distribution from radio to gamma-rays, and at understanding if transitional pulsars represent unique few cases or instead are representative of the whole class of ms pulsar binaries.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Papitto                       Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       THE HARD X-RAY EMISSION IN A ROTATION-POWERED RADIO PULSAR STATE OF THE TRANSITIONAL MS PULSAR BINARY XSS J12270-4859   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HESSJ1834m087_TeV                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÊÅîóûg@ÎÊÇx%◊J30001045005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ‡&fff␀␀ﬂ¬Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will provide an improvement in hard X-ray (sensitivity by orders of magnitude over previous satellites, allowing us to probe more deeply into the Galaxy than ever before.  This will provide the capability of detecting large fractions of known hard X-ray populations and perhaps to find new populations. 