shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IC0588                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÁáx%◊J@ÎÁà?BI60101011001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ∞-RLú␀␀‡ºHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*We propose XMM-Newton observations of 6 AGN (5 jointly with NuSTAR) newly detected in the Swift-BAT all-sky maps using a weighting scheme optimized to detect nearby, heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. These are among the brightest examples of reflection-dominated AGN available across the sky, providing the best targets for detailed study. The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology (XMM), and constrain the absorbing column and reflection (XMM+NuSTAR). The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC0588                                            @b–D–©*@VlÙ!-@m~Ù!-w@Ef˘€"–Â@v°a‰˜f@ÎÁà?BI@ÎÁòÈÏÛº60101011002@÷¢|"h	’@÷ñfz·H@ÿÑŸË>B@ÿÑŸË>BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂØÁqf␀␀‡ºHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*We propose XMM-Newton observations of 6 AGN (5 jointly with NuSTAR) newly detected in the Swift-BAT all-sky maps using a weighting scheme optimized to detect nearby, heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. These are among the brightest examples of reflection-dominated AGN available across the sky, providing the best targets for detailed study. The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology (XMM), and constrain the absorbing column and reflection (XMM+NuSTAR). The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGR_J17062m6143                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÁòÈÏÛº@ÎÁôÈÏÛº30101034001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂØøûË›␀␀‡≥Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀qLittle is known about the properties of accretion flows around neutron stars accreting at <1E-2 of the Eddington limit. Here we propose to exploit the unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR at >10 keV to observe the remarkable neutron star low-mass X-ray binary IGR J17062-6143, which accretes persistently at ~1E-3 of the Eddington limit. With a 75 ks observation we will test the emission mechanism, geometry and origin of the poorly understood hard X-ray emission, which will yield important new insight into the morphology of low-level accretion flows around neutron stars.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HOW NEUTRON STARS ACCRETE AT LOW EDDINGTON RATES                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J17062m6143                                   @p
3úæ‡¿N⁄C˛\ë—@täüæv»¥¿(éz·GÆ@iPˆîFsÇ@ÎÁôÈÏÛº@ÎÁ∆±	eÉ30101034002@Òÿ!-w@Òe≠B√ @ÚW≈äqﬁj@ÚW˘$xSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂØDçû␀␀‡≥Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀qLittle is known about the properties of accretion flows around neutron stars accreting at <1E-2 of the Eddington limit. Here we propose to exploit the unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR at >10 keV to observe the remarkable neutron star low-mass X-ray binary IGR J17062-6143, which accretes persistently at ~1E-3 of the Eddington limit. With a 75 ks observation we will test the emission mechanism, geometry and origin of the poorly understood hard X-ray emission, which will yield important new insight into the morphology of low-level accretion flows around neutron stars.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HOW NEUTRON STARS ACCRETE AT LOW EDDINGTON RATES                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429CXOUJ171405d7m381031                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÁ∆±	eÉ@ÎÁ«"–Åı30001130001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂØd‚
␀␀‡´Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀˚∆>The spin-down power of the 3.82 s AXP in the SNR CTB 37B is among the largest of magnetars, and it may be the youngest one as well. We propose to continue to monitor its spin-down rate, which varies by a factor of 2. Magnetars with the largest spin-down power are SGRs, and a marked increase in their spin-down torque often precedes an outburst. In this regard, CXOU J171405.7-381031 is very similar to SGR/AXP 1E 1547.0-5408, the magnetar with the largest spin-down power. By analogy with 1E 1547.0-5408, we may be observing the behavior of an SGR before its outburst. The pulse profiles show a change in shape above 8 keV, which suggests that a separate high-energy spectral component is present. We will examine the high-energy spectrum and pulse with NuSTAR.                                      Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀C16       The Energetic Magnetar in HESS J1713-381/CTB 37B                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXOUJ171405d7m381031                              @p(ç∏ª¿Cµ‹∆?@u…¶L/É{?‡˜e˝ä⁄∫@f≤[¿6„@ÎÁ«"–Åı@ÎÁ˛±	eÉ30001130002@Û◊∆ßÔû@Û¡∑◊
