                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Chakrabarty                   Deepto                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SAX_J1808d4m3658                                  @qGE8Ô5¿By∫^5?}@v5"–Â`B¿ÁlãCïÅ@e©˚Álã@ÎÁˇ^∫Ÿ@ÎË±	eÉ90102003004@’␀›fœAÚ@‘ˆ ≤ïÈ‚@÷ó÷lÙ!@÷ölÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ≠˛2“␀␀‡*Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Chakrabarty                   Deepto                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Rho_Oph                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎË±	eÉ@ÎË^∫Ÿ30102028001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ≠÷¡l␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀FBright X-ray flares, presumed to be analogous to the magnetic flares in the solar corona, are common on Young Stellar Objects (YSO) as well as on nearby magnetic red dwarfs. Using NuSTAR to extend the spectral coverage above that provided by Chandra and XMM-Newton is particularly important in that it allows us to understand the influence of the flares on the protostellar system as well as identifying deeply shrouded YSOs. The main core of the rho Ophiuchi cloud complex, well observed by both Chandra and XMM-Newton, makes an ideal target for NuSTAR in terms of the brightness, number, and spatial extent of the expected flaring targets.                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Grefenstette                  Brian                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROTOSTAR FLARES FROM RHO OPH: EXTENDING OUR REACH INTO THE HARD X-RAYS                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Rho_Oph                                           @nŸv_ÿ≠¨¿8£éÛMj@v&¡Ω•@0Õä⁄πıZ@eèfœAÚ@ÎË^∫Ÿ@ÎË3Õ{,†30102028002@Í¬„[W>´@Í¥„*ô1@Ïô»—∑Y@ÏõH—∑YSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ≠rÌÃ␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀FBright X-ray flares, presumed to be analogous to the magnetic flares in the solar corona, are common on Young Stellar Objects (YSO) as well as on nearby magnetic red dwarfs. Using NuSTAR to extend the spectral coverage above that provided by Chandra and XMM-Newton is particularly important in that it allows us to understand the influence of the flares on the protostellar system as well as identifying deeply shrouded YSOs. The main core of the rho Ophiuchi cloud complex, well observed by both Chandra and XMM-Newton, makes an ideal target for NuSTAR in terms of the brightness, number, and spatial extent of the expected flaring targets.                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Grefenstette                  Brian                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROTOSTAR FLARES FROM RHO OPH: EXTENDING OUR REACH INTO THE HARD X-RAYS                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀1/3 observations                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk1498                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎË3Õ{,†@ÎË4±	eÉ60160640001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ≠Bè\)␀␀ﬂCHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk1498                                           @nﬂº”ZÖà@IÊ($x@SÙ-‡q@EzÕûÉ‰&@Tﬂ¥¢3ú@ÎË4±	eÉ@ÎËDÈÏÛº60160640002@◊$O‰%ÆÊ@◊€Ôù≤-@ÿúgCïÅ@ÿúgCïÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ≠ÿ-É␀␀ﬂCHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_F11119p3257                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎËDÈÏÛº@ÎËEx%◊J60101045001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ¨⁄[«ﬂ␀␀‡√Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀nRecent Herschel observations have identified powerful molecular outflows in ultraluminous infrared galaxies (ULIRGs), indicating that AGN feedback can indeed have a strong impact on star formation and galaxy evolution. Theoretical models describing the formation of such large-scale outflows require the existence of a putative inner accretion disk wind. Recently, we found the first observational evidence of such wind in a ULIRG, IRAS F11119+3257, in the form of blue-shifted Fe XXV/XXVI absorption lines with velocity of 0.25c in a Suzaku observation obtained in 2013. Here, we request a 100ks NuSTAR observation to independently confirm the Fe K absorber at E=8.5 keV and hard excess at E>10 keV. Only NuSTAR allows the continuous coverage of the critical E=7 30 keV energy band.                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tombesi                       Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       REVEALING THE BLACK HOLE WIND IN THE ULTRALUMINOUS INFRARED GALAXY IRAS F11119+3257 WITH NUSTAR                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_F11119p3257                                  @e◊€Ùá˝@@XAâ7K«@h…„Ω@Qﬁ“àŒp@º£n/@ÎËEx%◊J@ÎËçÈÏÛº60101045002@˘Äl∏QÏ@˘oCØOÑ@˚@°a‰˜@˚@°a‰˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ¨Eò=␀␀‡√Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀nRecent Herschel observations have identified powerful molecular outflows in ultraluminous infrared galaxies (ULIRGs), indicating that AGN feedback can indeed have a strong impact on star formation and galaxy evolution. Theoretical models describing the formation of such large-scale outflows require the existence of a putative inner accretion disk wind. Recently, we found the first observational evidence of such wind in a ULIRG, IRAS F11119+3257, in the form of blue-shifted Fe XXV/XXVI absorption lines with velocity of 0.25c in a Suzaku observation obtained in 2013. Here, we request a 100ks NuSTAR observation to independently confirm the Fe K absorber at E=8.5 keV and hard excess at E>10 keV. Only NuSTAR allows the continuous coverage of the critical E=7 30 keV energy band.                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tombesi                       Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       REVEALING THE BLACK HOLE WIND IN THE ULTRALUMINOUS INFRARED GALAXY IRAS F11119+3257 WITH NUSTAR                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ14104482m4228325                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎËçÈÏÛº@ÎËéÕ{,†60160571001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ¨
