                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÎÍîóûg@ÎÎÍÈÏÛº30101024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂû—Aú¢␀␀‡ŸHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀NuSTAR spectra of neutron star LMXBs can place interesting limits on the neutron star radii and equation of state. Early-phase observations of Serpens X-1 with NuSTAR strongly require three continuum components, and disk reflection. Importantly, the reflection spectrum placed interesting limits on the neutron star radius. We propose to build on these successes by observing GX 17+2, 4U 1636-53, and 4U 1705-44 for 20 ks each. These bright targets have advantageous properties that will be effective in constraining the emission mechanisms and geometry (inner disk extent, inclination) of the inner accretion flow. We will coordinate Swift exposures to support our NuSTAR observations.                                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       REVEALING NEUTRON STAR X-RAY BINARY INNER ACCRETION FLOWS                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1636m53                                        @oH4m≈÷9¿J‚©ì‡ﬂ@tÕç∏∫«¿¬√…ÓÀ˚@p1PH@ÎÎÍÈÏÛº@ÎÎ˘"–Åı30101024002@”V␀—∑@”tÌT…Ö@÷}@Lò_@÷}@Lò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂûvÑΩ°␀␀‡ŸHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀NuSTAR spectra of neutron star LMXBs can place interesting limits on the neutron star radii and equation of state. Early-phase observations of Serpens X-1 with NuSTAR strongly require three continuum components, and disk reflection. Importantly, the reflection spectrum placed interesting limits on the neutron star radius. We propose to build on these successes by observing GX 17+2, 4U 1636-53, and 4U 1705-44 for 20 ks each. These bright targets have advantageous properties that will be effective in constraining the emission mechanisms and geometry (inner disk extent, inclination) of the inner accretion flow. We will coordinate Swift exposures to support our NuSTAR observations.                                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       REVEALING NEUTRON STAR X-RAY BINARY INNER ACCRETION FLOWS                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4507                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÎ˘"–Åı@ÎÎ˘±	eÉ60102051003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂûOtnæ␀␀·=Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ƒX-ray monitoring of bright Seyfert that show changes in the amount of X-ray absorption, are the key programs in assessing the nature and location of the X-ray absorbing media present in the central regions of AGN. We propose a monitoring (5x30ks) of the obscured and bright AGN NGC4507. NGC4507 shows dramatic changes of its X-ray emission altering from transmission to reflection dominated states and a strong variability of its X-ray flux in the 14-195keV band. NuSTAR sensitivity over the 3-79 keV energy range offers a unique tool to investigate the X-ray emission of NGC4507, allowing us to decouple any possible ambiguity on the variability of the intrinsic emission and amount of absorption. This will be the first monitoring on short timescale of this AGN in the hard X-ray band.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE ABSORPTION AND CONTINUUM VARIABILITY IN THE SEYFERT 2 NGC4507                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4507                                          @gú($x¿C˜ÏV’œ´@r∑($x@6Ás¸PH@uP‡ﬁ“àŒ@ÎÎ˘±	eÉ@ÎÏ"–Åı60102051004@‡”◊s¸@‡Œ~kP∞Ú@‚ã5®Xy@‚´5®XySCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂûπ␀␀·=Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ƒX-ray monitoring of bright Seyfert that show changes in the amount of X-ray absorption, are the key programs in assessing the nature and location of the X-ray absorbing media present in the central regions of AGN. We propose a monitoring (5x30ks) of the obscured and bright AGN NGC4507. NGC4507 shows dramatic changes of its X-ray emission altering from transmission to reflection dominated states and a strong variability of its X-ray flux in the 14-195keV band. NuSTAR sensitivity over the 3-79 keV energy range offers a unique tool to investigate the X-ray emission of NGC4507, allowing us to decouple any possible ambiguity on the variability of the intrinsic emission and amount of absorption. This will be the first monitoring on short timescale of this AGN in the hard X-ray band.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE ABSORPTION AND CONTINUUM VARIABILITY IN THE SEYFERT 2 NGC4507                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀2/5 observations                                                                                                        archived  20200429CXOUJ163709d7m473157                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÏ"–Åı@ÎÏ±	eÉ30160001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂù◊"É9␀␀ﬂgHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXOUJ163709d7m473157                              @o*	‘ï+¿G…=Ÿb∑@uØOÑMøΩOﬂ;dZ@pºè≈Åo@ÎÏ±	eÉ@ÎÏ7ÈÏÛº30160001002@Ë†O+”√a@ËåfT`™e@ÍÖπqué@ÍáquéSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂùsß¢␀␀ﬂgHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC7469                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÏ7ÈÏÛº@ÎÏ8ÈÏÛº60101001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂùC„-"␀␀·ßHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)Using a multiwavelength campaign on NGC 7469, led by XMM-Newton, HST, Swift, NuSTAR, and optical, we propose to achieve new physical insights into the enigmatic AGN winds, by obtaining the deepest ever RGS spectrum of this highly accreting source. It will allow us to determine the accurate ionisation and chemical structure of the wind. Using EPIC and RGS, we will measure the response of the outflow to continuum variations, which combined with HST/COS data will unambiguously determine the location, and abundances of the outflow components.Our team pioneered this approach, and applied it successfully (14 refereed papers) to Mrk 509 that featured only a multi-year response. The unique properties of NGC 7469 make it the ideal candidate to respond on shorter, better-monitored time scales.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Behar                         Ehud                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Tracking Down the Ionized Outflow of NGC 7469                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                         