tunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Extragalactic Legacy Survey                                                                                             archived  202004293C273                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎîØ‰/@Î±!´L10002020002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂmŒQ)W␀␀ﬂ¡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C273                                             @ggßÜ¬&Å@␀Np:˚~ë@rZ¨1@PU2a|@vCjÁ’fœ@Î±!´L@Î6Í9Ö10002020003@Ë ≈,•zx@Ë5S˜ŒŸ@ÍC8Ô4◊@Í8Ô4◊SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂmd8!`␀␀ﬂ¡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM Chandra INTEGRAL Swift                                                                          ␀␀␀IACHEC cross calibration                                                                                                archived  20200429Mrk841                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î6Í9Ö@Î7x>60101023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂm7‘c␀␀‡˝Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*[The physical origin of the soft excess in AGN remains hotly debated. Blurred ionised reflection, Comptonization, ionised absorption and other models can account for it, but 0.4-50keV observations are needed to break the degeneracy between models. Our recent work shows that short, simultaneous snapshots with XMM and NuSTAR can distinguish between reflection and absorption using only the strengths of the soft and hard excesses, without requiring more complex model fits. We propose to observe 7 extreme AGN from the hard X-ray-selected Swift/BAT catalogue jointly with XMM and NuSTAR in a 140 ks campaign. Our observations will constrain the soft excess production mechanisms in these sources using a novel, powerful technique, shedding light on this long-standing question in AGN science.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vasudevan                     Ranjan                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Hard X-ray Perspective on the Soft X-ray excess                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk841                                            @l?<64@$◊ŒŸá+@&&‹]càf@Kj	ﬁ␀“?Î¢úwökQ@Î7x>@ÎJw␀°60101023002@÷ﬁ¿_ˆî@÷€qﬁi≠@ÿÕ|bM”@ÿ”|bM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂlÎ¡Õ␀␀‡˝Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*[The physical origin of the soft excess in AGN remains hotly debated. Blurred ionised reflection, Comptonization, ionised absorption and other models can account for it, but 0.4-50keV observations are needed to break the degeneracy between models. Our recent work shows that short, simultaneous snapshots with XMM and NuSTAR can distinguish between reflection and absorption using only the strengths of the soft and hard excesses, without requiring more complex model fits. We propose to observe 7 extreme AGN from the hard X-ray-selected Swift/BAT catalogue jointly with XMM and NuSTAR in a 140 ks campaign. Our observations will constrain the soft excess production mechanisms in these sources using a novel, powerful technique, shedding light on this long-standing question in AGN science.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vasudevan                     Ranjan                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Hard X-ray Perspective on the Soft X-ray excess                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4507                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎJw␀°@ÎJ±!´L60102051005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂl¡#Eh␀␀·=Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ƒX-ray monitoring of bright Seyfert that show changes in the amount of X-ray absorption, are the key programs in assessing the nature and location of the X-ray absorbing media present in the central regions of AGN. We propose a monitoring (5x30ks) of the obscured and bright AGN NGC4507. NGC4507 shows dramatic changes of its X-ray emission altering from transmission to reflection dominated states and a strong variability of its X-ray flux in the 14-195keV band. NuSTAR sensitivity over the 3-79 keV energy range offers a unique tool to investigate the X-ray emission of NGC4507, allowing us to decouple any possible ambiguity on the variability of the intrinsic emission and amount of absorption. This will be the first monitoring on short timescale of this AGN in the hard X-ray band.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE ABSORPTION AND CONTINUUM VARIABILITY IN THE SEYFERT 2 NGC4507                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4507                                          @gõ–|Ñµ›¿CÙ¨1&È@r∂˚ÁlãD@6Ì≈÷8ÜY?Ë˘€"–Â`@ÎJ±!´L@ÎaîØ‰/60102051006@ﬂxMS&¬@ﬂpUXbN@‡·:í£@‡„':í£SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂlrâJ∑␀␀·=Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ƒX-ray monitoring of bright Seyfert that show changes in the amount of X-ray absorption, are the key programs in assessing the nature and location of the X-ray absorbing media present in the central regions of AGN. We propose a monitoring (5x30ks) of the obscured and bright AGN NGC4507. NGC4507 shows dramatic changes of its X-ray emission altering from transmission to reflection dominated states and a strong variability of its X-ray flux in the 14-195keV band. NuSTAR sensitivity over the 3-79 keV energy range offers a unique tool to investigate the X-ray emission of NGC4507, allowing us to decouple any possible ambiguity on the variability of the intrinsic emission and amount of absorption. This will be the first monitoring on short timescale of this AGN in the hard X-ray band.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE ABSORPTION AND CONTINUUM VARIABILITY IN THE SEYFERT 2 NGC4507                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Eta_Carinae                                       @d&HËßÁ¿M’∏∫«À@q˜P∞Ú{≥ø„„º”[@6\ë—N<@ÎaîØ‰/@Îbw␀°30101005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂlI2q␀␀‡˝Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*The massive binary systems, Eta Car and WR140, emit strong hard X-rays from the wind-wind collision (WWC). The joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of Eta Car around periastron in AO13 presented detailed views of extreme hard X-ray emission from the star for the first time. The >10 keV slope has an excess from the thermal spectral model measured between 5-10 keV, suggesting that the electrons and highly ionized iron ions are not in equilibrium, or reflection emission is contaminated. We therefore propose to revisit Eta Car at orbital phase ~0.2 when the WWC is relatively stable. For comparison, we also propose to observe WR140 at a similar orbital geometry, which does not show reflection emission and whose stellar, orbital and wind parameters are precisely determined, to study the electron ioGalactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Electron and Ion Equilibrium in Colliding Wind Plasma                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Eta_Carinae                                       @d&Èx‘˝Ù¿M◊ˆîFt@q˜á¸π#£ø„A [¿7@6]Oﬂ;dZ@Îbw␀°@Îp±!´L30101005002@◊©Y≥–}@◊RC˛\í@ÿ©¨∫«À@ÿ™¨∫«ÀSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂlrâ␀␀‡˝Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*The massive binary systems, Eta Car and WR140, emit strong hard X-rays from the wind-wind collision (WWC). The joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of Eta Car around periastron in AO13 presented detailed views of extreme hard X-ray emission from the star for the first time. The >10 keV slope has an excess from the thermal spectral model measured between 5-10 keV, suggesting that the electrons and highly ionized iron ions are not in equilibrium, or reflection emission is contaminated. We therefore propose to revisit Eta Car at orbital phase ~0.2 when the WWC is relatively stable. For comparison, we also propose to observe WR140 at a similar orbital geometry, which does not show reflection emission and whose stellar, orbital and wind parameters are precisely determined, to study the electron ioGalactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Electron and Ion Equilibrium in Colliding Wind Plasma                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429RX_J0852_NW                                       @`Ñ¡T…Ö¿F‘ØOÑM@pî:í£SøÙí£S&@GπÓÀ˚µ@Îp±!´L@Îq"Ë«æ40101011003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂkﬁ–ó¥␀␀·␀Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀wThe X-ray synchrotron emission in SNRs provides insights into the population of accelerated particles as well as on the mechanism of acceleration itself. The electron population emitting in X-rays is in the spectral cutoff region and its shape can provide important constraints on the limiting factor of the acceleration. However, there are currently poor observational constraints on the curvature of this cutoff because the leverage arm of "soft" (0.5-10 keV) X-ray telescopes is too narrow. We propose the first broad-band study of a synchrotron filament using the hard X-ray focusing telescope NuSTAR. To study the acceleration in a simple context, we target an isolated filament in the large diameter SNR Vela , allowing to isolate the physical processes taking place at the shock front.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Acero                         Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       MEASURING THE SHAPE OF THE SYNCHROTRON CUT-OFF IN THE VELA JR. SNR                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RX_J0852_NW                                       @`ÜnóçOﬂ¿F’Û∂E°À@pî∏∫«ÀøÙ7K∆ßÔû@F3•„S˜œ@Îq"Ë«æ@Î∑[ÃU˜40101011004@¯Ò3‡uˆ˝@¯Ëó$tT@˙“BÙ!-@˙‘¢Ù!-SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂk>Fã␀␀·␀Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀wThe X-ray synchrotron emission in SNRs provides insights into the population of accelerated particles as well as on the mechanism of acceleration itself. The electron population emitting in X-rays is in the spectral cutoff region and its shape can provide important constraints on the limiting factor of the acceleration. However, there are currently poor observational constraints on the curvature of this cutoff because the leverage arm of "soft" (0.5-10 keV) X-ray telescopes is too narrow. We propose the first broad-band study of a synchrotron filament using the hard X-ray focusing telescope NuSTAR. To study the acceleration in a simple context, we target an isolated filament in the large diameter SNR Vela , allowing to isolate the physical processes taking place at the shock front.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Acero                         Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       MEASURING THE SHAPE OF THE SYNCHROTRON CUT-OFF IN THE VELA JR. SNR                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ07084326m4642494                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î∑[ÃU˜@Î∑±!´L60160284001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂkΩ°/␀␀ﬂéHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ07084326m4642494                            @Z…o␀h€å¿GVzêñº@pç∏∫«¿0Æp:˚~ë@R∂1¯†ê.@Î∑±!´L@Î«Í9Ö60160284002@Ÿ›óˆ+@Ÿ@ìtº@⁄¸;lãCñ@⁄˛ªlãCñSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂj∏!_Ù␀␀ﬂéHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_1266                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î«Í9Ö@Î»îØ‰/60101048001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂjë”?V␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ùNGC1266 is a nearby lenticular galaxy hosting a massive molecular outflow likely driven by its AGN. The turbulence injected into the ISM by the outflow may account for the suppression its star formation by a factor of 50-150. Recent ALMA and CARMA measurements of the dense gas tracers H13CN and CS show its nuclear region lies behind a column of N(H2)~3x10^24 cm^-2. Current XMM-Newton and Chandra observations of NGC1266 cannot conclusively differentiate between a moderately bright (LX~10^42 erg/s) Compton-thick AGN or a ultra-compact central starburst with a top-heavy IMF. We propose to observe NGC1266 using NuSTAR to measure the hard X-ray properties of its central source and thereby conclusively determine which of these mechanisms is responsible for the feedback observed in this galaxy.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanz                          Lauranne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NGC1266: A LOCAL COMPTON-THICK AGN IN A QUENCHING GALAXY                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1266                                          @HÇ©ì‡ﬂ¿%F
¶L0@fÒﬁ␀—∑¿G”πå~($@`löu@Î»îØ‰/@ÎÏx>60101048002@È'ΩoiD@Èƒ2 X@Î.6zë@Î.6zëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂj$…ƒ␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ùNGC1266 is a nearby lenticular galaxy hosting a massive molecular outflow likely driven by its AGN. The turbulence injected into the ISM by the outflow may account for the suppression its star formation by a factor of 50-150. Recent ALMA and CARMA measurements of the dense gas tracers H13CN and CS show its nuclear region lies behind a column of N(H2)~3x10^24 cm^-2. Current XMM-Newton and Chandra observations of NGC1266 cannot conclusively differentiate between a moderately bright (LX~10^42 erg/s) Compton-thick AGN or a ultra-compact central starburst with a top-heavy IMF. We propose to observe NGC1266 using NuSTAR to measure the hard X-ray properties of its central source and thereby conclusively determine which of these mechanisms is responsible for the feedback observed in this galaxy.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanz                          Lauranne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NGC1266: A LOCAL COMPTON-THICK AGN IN A QUENCHING GALAXY                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429V0332p53                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÏx>@ÎÌ"Ë«æ80102002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂiÒ«r␀␀‡éHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V0332p53                                          @JË*ô0æ@Jô^ûö@b>∏QÎÖ¿QN;Õ5®@dÀ)^û@ÎÌ"Ë«æ@Î˚Õìrh80102002002@ƒ~Ç	ﬁ@≈9ÆóçOﬂ@◊√\–©*@◊ƒ\–©*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂh¬ÿ-É␀␀‡éHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO (1/3)                                                                                                               archived  20200429IGRJ14091m6108                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˚Õìrh@Î˝"Ë«æ30101001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂhòÔ±␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)¢The INTEGRAL satellite has been discovering extreme sources that were missed by previous surveys. While this is providing a much more complete view of hard X-ray source populations, follow-up efforts are essential for determining the nature of the sources. This proposal, which focuses on Galactic sources, such as the population of High Mass X-ray Binaries (HMXBs), includes three sources for which short Chandra observations made during the past year have allowed for the identification of optical counterparts. We propose to perform a complete and definitive identification of IGR J14091-6108, IGR J18381-0924 and IGRJ17164-3803 by combining XMM, VLT (optical spectroscopy), and NuSTAR observations.                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Determining the Nature of Unidentified IGR Sources in the Galactic Plane                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ14091m6108                                    @jÑL/É{J¿Nì˜ŒŸá@s◊s¸P?‹SéÛMj@upìtºj@Î˝"Ë«æ@ÎÒw␀°30101001002@◊qÒ-w1ê@◊k_]ÃcÒ@Ÿx1õ=»@Ÿ{±õ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂhK0*z␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀)¢The INTEGRAL satellite has been discovering extreme sources that were missed by previous surveys. While this is providing a much more complete view of hard X-ray source populations, follow-up efforts are essential for determining the nature of the sources. This proposal, which focuses on Galactic sources, such as the population of High Mass X-ray Binaries (HMXBs), includes three sources for which short Chandra observations made during the past year have allowed for the identification of optical counterparts. We propose to perform a complete and definitive identification of IGR J14091-6108, IGR J18381-0924 and IGRJ17164-3803 by combining XMM, VLT (optical spectroscopy), and NuSTAR observations.                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsi