eline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M31_Field_A                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÚq[ÃU˜@ÎÚqÍ9Ö50110001003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂaÁ∫7␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀äWe propose a 100 ks NuSTAR exposure for each of three fields along the M31 disk as a first step in long-term monitoring of X-ray Binary (XRB) populations to LX~6e36 erg/s (4-25 keV) in an environment similar to the Milky Way (MW). These three pointings will follow ~3-12 months after the initial NuSTAR observations planned during early 2015 to connect XRB variability from hour to year-long timescales. We will (1) study individual XRBs similar to those seen in the MW but with slightly different environments and star formation histories and (2) establish a baseline for long-term monitoring. NuSTAR observations are critical as the different XRB accretion states are distinguishable at E>10 keV and only subtly different at 0.5-10 keV.                                                              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Hornschemeier                 Ann                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       MONITORING THE X-RAY BINARY POPULATIONS OF THE M31 DISK WITH NUSTAR                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_Field_A                                       @%√Ø∑È˘@D¶E° ¿É@^JóçOﬂ;¿5üÂ…‰@]≥≤˛≈m]@ÎÚqÍ9Ö@ÎÚ¢Õìrh50110001004@ÒlŒƒ2 X@Òbå'ª/Ï@Úúì÷8ÜY@Úù£÷8ÜYSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂavµI␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀äWe propose a 100 ks NuSTAR exposure for each of three fields along the M31 disk as a first step in long-term monitoring of X-ray Binary (XRB) populations to LX~6e36 erg/s (4-25 keV) in an environment similar to the Milky Way (MW). These three pointings will follow ~3-12 months after the initial NuSTAR observations planned during early 2015 to connect XRB variability from hour to year-long timescales. We will (1) study individual XRBs similar to those seen in the MW but with slightly different environments and star formation histories and (2) establish a