	1    Walton                        Dom                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Mrk1040                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙ%?Zé⁄@ÎÙ&îØ‰060101002003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂEÓ,¡␀␀·%Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)The study of the X-ray spectral components in AGN allows us in principle to infer the properties of the accretion disk and in some cases, the black hole spin. This proposal concerns the study of the nearby, Xray bright Seyfert 1 galaxy, Mrk 1040, which shows the presence of an unusually intense and highly broadened (FWHM: 60000 km/s) Fe K line, as well as a strong hard X-ray excess above 10 keV. Mrk 1040 is also rapidly variable and shows a tentative detection of lags at soft X-rays. Here we request simultaneous XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations (160 ks and 120 ks), enabling us to determine the origin of the hard X-ray excess and ultimately the spin of the central black hole. We will also determine the presence the X-ray lags above 10 keV, as expected in disc reverberation scenarios.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       A hard X-ray view of the inner disc in Mrk 1040                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk1040                                           @Bâ#¢úwö@?U÷8ÜYK@b>WßÜ¬'¿;Ge˝ä⁄∫@`Ø€"–Â`@ÎÙ&îØ‰0@ÎÙXw␀°60101002004@Ô^2<ûÏ¿@ÔPb+jÁ’@˜YùIQÉ@˘ŸùIQÉSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂDé˚”␀␀·%Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀)The study of the X-ray spectral components in AGN allows us in principle to infer the properties of the accretion disk and in some cases, the black hole spin. This proposal concerns the study of the nearby, Xray bright Seyfert 1 galaxy, Mrk 1040, which shows the presence of an unusually intense and highly broadened (FWHM: 60000 km/s) Fe K line, as well as a strong hard X-ray excess above 10 keV. Mrk 1040 is also rapidly variable and shows a tentative detection of lags at soft X-rays. Here we request simultaneous XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations (160 ks and 120 ks), enabling us to determine the origin of the hard X-ray excess and ultimately the spin of the central black hole. We will also determine the presence the X-ray lags above 10 keV, as expected in disc reverberation scenarios.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       A hard X-ray view of the inner disc in Mrk 1040                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429H1743m322                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙXw␀°@ÎÙY[ÃU˜80002040005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂD[∞[␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀˙@¡We propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, INTEGRAL, JVLA/ATCA, Swift) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds before the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next ~2 months.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀C16       An Integrated Approach to Winds, Jets, and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316H1743m322                                         @p®äqﬁi≠¿@!kπå~(@vQ‘ï*ôø˙RT`™d√@t£ÕûÉ‰&@ÎÙY[ÃU˜@ÎÙl"Ë«æ80002040006@ŸºM◊€Ùà@Ÿùo–|Ñ∂@€æp£n/@€¬0£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂDLúA␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀˙@¡We propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, INTEGRAL, JVLA/ATCA, Swift) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds before the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next ~2 months.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀C16       An Integrated Approach to Winds, Jets, and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429M31_Field_C                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙl"Ë«æ@ÎÙm[ÃU˜50110003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂCÍ€6<␀␀·#Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀äWe propose a 100 ks NuSTAR exposure for each of three fields along the M31 disk as a first step in long-term monitoring of X-ray Binary (XRB) populations to LX~6e36 erg/s (4-25 keV) in an environment similar to the Milky Way (MW). These three pointings will follow ~3-12 months after the initial NuSTAR observations planned during early 2015 to connect XRB variability from hour to year-long timescales. We will (1) study individual XRBs similar to those seen in the MW but with slightly different environments and star formation histories and (2) establish a baseline for long-term monitoring. NuSTAR observations are critical as the different XRB accretion states are distinguishable at E>10 keV and only subtly different at 0.5-10 keV.                                                              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Hornschemeier                 Ann                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       MONITORING THE X-RAY BINARY POPULATIONS OF THE M31 DISK WITH NUSTAR                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_Field_C                                       @&Ø\(ı¬è@D«ŒŸá+@^bÊ1¯†ê¿5_∑È˘r@\20U2a|@ÎÙm[ÃU˜@ÎÙ≤îØ‰050110003002@˜àRˆîF@˜~z8Ô4◊@˘DIˇ.HÈ@˘H9ˇ.HÈSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂC[F␀␀·#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀äWe propose a 100 ks NuSTAR exposure for each of three fields along the M31 disk as a first step in long-term monitoring of X-ray Binary (XRB) populations to LX~6e36 erg/s (4-25 keV) in an environment similar to the Milky Way (MW). These three pointings will follow ~3-12 months after the initial NuSTAR observations planned during early 2015 to connect XRB variability from hour to year-long timescales. We will (1) study individual XRBs similar to those seen in the MW but with slightly different environments and star formation histories and (2) establish a baseline for long-term monitoring. NuSTAR observations are critical as the different XRB accretion states are distinguishable at E>10 keV and only subtly different at 0.5-10 keV.                                                              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Hornschemeier                 Ann                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       MONITORING THE X-RAY BINARY POPULATIONS OF THE M31 DISK WITH NUSTAR                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SGR_1806m20                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙ≤îØ‰0@ÎÙ≥±!´L30102038005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂC#Egä␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀éMagnetars are young isolated neutron stars with bright persistent X-ray emission and bursting behavior in hard X-rays/soft gamma-rays, caused by their extremely high magnetic fields. Here we propose to monitor one of the most prolific bursting magnetars, SGR 1806-20, with NuSTAR over the course of one year, to study the low-level bursting behavior of this source, its broad-band persistent X-ray emission, and the connection between these two properties. This will allow us to characterize the spectral and temporal properties of any low-level bursting activity, study the effect of low-level bursting activity on the erratic timing behavior of the source, track any change in torque, and characterize the hard X-ray spectral and temporal properties of SGR 1806-20.                                 Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    van der Horst                 Alexander                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE MAGNETAR SGR 1806-20                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_1806m20                                       @qÇ©ì·¿4n.±ƒ2 @#æ5?|Ìëø∞bM“Ò©¸@t˝ª/ÏV÷@ÎÙ≥±!´L@ÎÙ ±!´L30102038006@ﬁrÍ≥g°@ﬁL˚AÚ◊@‡iËŒp:˚@‡kàŒp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂBÿ¶ﬂ√␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀éMagnetars are young isolated neutron stars with bright persistent X-ray emission and bursting behavior in hard X-rays/soft gamma-rays, caused by their extremely high magnetic fields. Here we propose to monitor one of the most prolific bursting magnetars, SGR 1806-20, with NuSTAR over the course of one year, to study the low-level bursting behavior of this source, its broad-band persistent X-ray emission, and the connection between these two properties. This will allow us to characterize the spectral and temporal properties of any low-level bursting activity, study the effect of low-level bursting activity on the erratic timing behavior of the source, track any change in torque, and characterize the hard X-ray spectral and temporal properties of SGR 1806-20.                                 Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    van der Horst                 Alexander                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE MAGNETAR SGR 1806-20                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429HESSJ1640m465                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙ ±!´L@ÎÙÀ"Ë«æ30002021043␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂB∞[∞␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESSJ1640m465                                     @oEB√…ÓÃ¿GE˝ä⁄πı@u#çπ?¡¿ÎÌ˙C˛@t˘sÅ◊€ı@ÎÙÀ"Ë«æ@ÎÙÎ"Ë«æ30002021044@ÊhtæÌ)@Ê]≥‡ﬁ“â@Ë␀©*0@Ë®©*0SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂBW:…␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀50days monitoring                                                                                                       archived  20200429IGR_J16493m4348                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙÎ"Ë«æ@ÎÙÎ[ÃU˜30102054001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂB,_í∆␀␀·1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀There are now at least 5 supergiant High-Mass X-ray Binaries where superorbital periods have been found - predominantly at energies above 15 keV. Curiously, these periods increase monotonically with orbital period. We propose to obtain two NuSTAR observations of IGR J16493-4348 to determine the detailed spectral properties and their changes over its superorbital period. Suggested driving mechanisms for the modulation include pulsations in the primary star, 3 body systems, and precession of the primary with an equatorial wind. To allow detailed modeling to proceed, good characterization of the modulation properties in hard X-rays is first required. We will also determine whether a previously claimed cyclotron feature at ~30 keV is present.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROPERTIES AND CAUSE OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J16493m4348                                   @oä&ÄùIR¿EÏxF‹@uSÆz·H?Ë64ÍK@t™d¬¯8@ÎÙÎ[ÃU˜@ÎÙ˙Õìrh30102054002@÷)îØO@÷`y†'RT@◊ﬂ„3333@◊ﬂ„3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂAË|e§␀␀·1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀There are now at least 5 supergiant High-Mass X-ray Binaries where superorbital periods have been found - predominantly at energies above 15 keV. Curiously, these periods increase monotonically with orbital period. We propose to obtain two NuSTAR observations of IGR J16493-4348 to determine the detailed spectral properties and their changes over its superorbital period. Suggested driving mechanisms for the modulation include pulsations in the primary star, 3 body systems, and precession of the primary with an equatorial wind. To allow detailed modeling to proceed, good characterization of the modulation properties in hard X-rays is first required. We will also determine whether a previously claimed cyclotron feature at ~30 keV is present.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROPERTIES AND CAUSE OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO253_3                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎÙ˙Õìrh@ÎÙ˚îØ‰060101014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂA¬øÁ∫␀␀·,Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          