␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îıüx>@ÎıüÍ9Ö30101049001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ>[∞[␀␀·#Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ˇWe propose two 40~ks NuSTAR observations of two persistent wind accreting X-ray pulsars in high-mass X-ray binaries (HMXB), IGR J18027-2016 and 4U1909+07, which have similar orbital periods and optical companions with supergiant fast X-ray transient IGR J17544-2619. The observations are aimed at (i) detecting cyclotron resonance scattering spectral features (CRSF), (ii) pulsar timing measurements. The properties of the sources will be compared with those of IGR J17544-2619 in order to determine the physical parameters (or their combination) responsible for different variability character observed in these two classes of accreting neutron stars.                                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lutovinov                     Alexander                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       OBSERVATIONS OF SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARY SYSTEMS: SEARCH OF THE CLUE FOR THE SFXT MECHANISMS                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J18027m2016                                   @pÍsÍ≥g°¿4Q≤ïÈ‚@"ê:˚~ë␀?Ûä	ﬁ␀“@t˝º£n/@ÎıüÍ9Ö@ÎıøÕìrh30101049002@„nÏ_ˆî@„pL*ô0æ@Â∏Rffff@Âº2ffffSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ=Á@⁄t␀␀·#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ˇWe propose two 40~ks NuSTAR observations of two persistent wind accreting X-ray pulsars in high-mass X-ray binaries (HMXB), IGR J18027-2016 and 4U1909+07, which have similar orbital periods and optical companions with supergiant fast X-ray transient IGR J17544-2619. The observations are aimed at (i) detecting cyclotron resonance scattering spectral features (CRSF), (ii) pulsar timing measurements. The properties of the sources will be compared with those of IGR J17544-2619 in order to determine the physical parameters (or their combination) responsible for different variability character observed in these two classes of accreting neutron stars.                                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lutovinov                     Alexander                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       OBSERVATIONS OF SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARY SYSTEMS: SEARCH OF THE CLUE FOR THE SFXT MECHANISMS                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PG2112p059                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎıøÕìrh@Îı¿x>60001159003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ=ª*␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG2112p059                                        @sÎ¸PH@v4ÍK@Ljô0æÌ¿;‚√…ÓÀ˚@1OÉ{J#:@Îı¿x>@Îı–±!´L60001159004@”-ïx‘˝Ù@”6¿ƒõ•„@‘ÌJv»¥9@‘Úäv»¥9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ=mRLú␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of obscured and/or Compton-thick AGN with NuSTAR provide us with an opportunity to constrain the nature of X-ray obscurers that are characteristic among AGN, potentially without the powerful continuum to complicate things. The primary goal of this research line is to observe a few key objects across three obscuration regimes. These will yield detailed spectra of the reflection hump and reflector efficiency/covering fraction that can be compared against existing models of unobscured AGN.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀OAGN      Obscured AGN, Including Compton-Thick AGN, BALQSOs, and ULIRGs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX339m4                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îı–±!´L@Îı—x>80102011001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ==p£◊␀␀·QHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1  