        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀ToO (1/6)                                                                                                               archived  20200429SN2014C                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îı‚w␀°@Îı„"Ë«æ40102014002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ<¨/5␀␀·#Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≤Mass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of a H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our radio-to-gamma-ray follow-up. Our goal is to constrain the density and proximity of the ejected material, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor star. Unlike all the other H-stripped SNe, the radio and X-ray emission of SN14C is still increasing at t>300 days, giving us the unprecedented opportunity to map the density profile of the ejected material and constrain the epoch of its ejection in fine detail.Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C: FROM A H-STRIPPED EXPLOSION TO A STRONGLY INTERACTING SN                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u3·GÆ{@A6¡Ω•@Wb6‚ÎC¿4≤‰éäqﬁ@P‡⁄QŒ@Îı„"Ë«æ@Îı˘Í9Ö40102014003@›ÉbÁ’fœ@›wπ4ÍK@ﬂ•âûÉ‰&@ﬂ™IûÉ‰&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ<]|∆ª␀␀·#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≤Mass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of a H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our radio-to-gamma-ray follow-up. Our goal is to constrain the density and proximity of the ejected material, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor star. Unlike all the other H-stripped SNe, the radio and X-ray emission of SN14C is still increasing at t>300 days, giving us the unprecedented opportunity to map the density profile of the ejected material and constrain the epoch of its ejection in fine detail.Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C: FROM A H-STRIPPED EXPLOSION TO A STRONGLY INTERACTING SN                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_B1937p21                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îı˘Í9Ö@Îı˙x>30101031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ<+¡Õ␀␀·-Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀fMillisecond pulsars (MSPs) are old neutron stars spun up by accretion from companion stars. The recent launch of the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope has greatly increased the MSP sample and identified a new subclass, known as Class II, MSPs that show aligned pulse profiles in different energy bands. This could represent a new radio emission mechanism in the outer magnetosphere. We suggest that the radio waves could inverse-Compton scatter with energetic particles to produce hard X-ray emission, and we developed a model to account for the high-energy emission. To directly test this model, we propose NuSTAR observations to measure the hard X-ray spectra of several Class II MSPs. The results will also offer predictions to the low-frequency radio spectra for a comparison with on-going studies.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ng                            C.-Y.                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HARD X-RAY EMISSION OF CLASS II MILLISECOND PULSARS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_B1937p21                                      @rn␀—∑Y@5ë&Èx‘˛@LØ€Ùá¸πø√√a@N•@µ%F
