ents: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC4500                                           @gz—∑X‚@L¯Œp:˚@`5?|Ìë@Mqx‘˝Û∂@qâ&¡Ω•@ÎˆÍ9Ö@Îˆ(x>60161497002@‘Ö≈-w1ê@‘Ä	fœAÚ@’Ï>êˇó$@’Ô˛êˇó$SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ;X¶ﬂ√␀␀ﬂøHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGR_J16493m4348                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îˆ(x>@Îˆ)x>30102054003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ;*¬s␀␀·1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀There are now at least 5 supergiant High-Mass X-ray Binaries where superorbital periods have been found - predominantly at energies above 15 keV. Curiously, these periods increase monotonically with orbital period. We propose to obtain two NuSTAR observations of IGR J16493-4348 to determine the detailed spectral properties and their changes over its superorbital period. Suggested driving mechanisms for the modulation include pulsations in the primary star, 3 body systems, and precession of the primary with an equatorial wind. To allow detailed modeling to proceed, good characterization of the modulation properties in hard X-rays is first required. We will also determine whether a previously claimed cyclotron feature at ~30 keV is present.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROPERTIES AND CAUSE OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J16493m4348                                   @oâˇ.HËß¿EÏ9XbN@uS†˘	k∫?ËC˛\ë—N@u-¸PH@Îˆ)x>@ÎˆA[ÃU˜30102054004@ﬁ~4ﬁ“àŒ@ﬁ–r:)«z@‡ä/üæ@‡åﬂ/üæSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ:…{B_␀␀·1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀There are now at least 5 supergiant High-Mass X-ray Binaries where superorbital periods have been found - predominantly at energies above 15 keV. Curiously, these periods increase monotonically with orbital period. We propose to obtain two NuSTAR observations of IGR J16493-4348 to determine the detailed spectral properties and their changes over its superorbital period. Suggested driving mechanisms for the modulation include pulsations in the primary star, 3 body systems, and precession of the primary with an equatorial wind. To allow detailed modeling to proceed, good characterization of the modulation properties in hard X-rays is first required. We will also determine whether a previously claimed cyclotron feature at ~30 keV is present.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROPERTIES AND CAUSE OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Sol_SADA_15244                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎˆA[ÃU˜@ÎˆBx>20101002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÒﬂb:␀␀‡vHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will be able to observe solar hard X-ray sources 200 times fainter than our current capabilities allow. This increase in sensitivity will open the door to truly pioneering observations, providing diagnostics for solar flare and, in particular, for nanoflare observations. Almost as important is NuSTAR's advantage in dynamic range, the ability to see a faint source (e.g. coronal source at the reconnection point) at the same time as a brighter one (e.g. source at the magnetic footpoint of a flare).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀SOL       Solar Observations + Solar Axions                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                