lare observations. Almost as important is NuSTAR's advantage in dynamic range, the ability to see a faint source (e.g. coronal source at the reconnection point) at the same time as a brighter one (e.g. source at the magnetic footpoint of a flare).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀SOL       Solar Observations + Solar Axions                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Solar ToO AR 2403 / no valid CHU4 attitude                                                                              archived  20150316Sol_SADA_15244                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îˆo[ÃU˜@Îˆo±!´L20101002008␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ'O␀␀‡vHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will be able to observe solar hard X-ray sources 200 times fainter than our current capabilities allow. This increase in sensitivity will open the door to truly pioneering observations, providing diagnostics for solar flare and, in particular, for nanoflare observations. Almost as important is NuSTAR's advantage in dynamic range, the ability to see a faint source (e.g. coronal source at the reconnection point) at the same time as a brighter one (e.g. source at the magnetic footpoint of a flare).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀SOL       Solar Observations + Solar Axions                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀INERTIAL                                                                                                                archived  20150316GX339m4                                           @oıZÖáìﬁ¿Hf¡Ω•@u-C,•z¿ôJÙÿE@uÊ1¯†ê@Îˆo±!´L@ÎˆpÕìrh80102011003@M‘Ñµ‹∆?@M”öu%@P†␀␀␀␀␀@P†␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÈü´␀␀·QHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429GX339m4                                           @oı,•zxl¿Hgs¸PH@u,oiDg¿ú‡uˆ˝"@uç∏∫«@ÎˆpÕìrh@ÎˆÕìrh80102011004@—ﬁŒ.±ƒ3@—Úã1è≈@‘ffff@‘ffffSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂîÙ>3␀␀·QHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO (2/6)                                                                                                               archived  2020042930_Dor_C                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎˆÕìrh@ÎˆÄ[ÃU˜40101015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂhı¬è␀␀·THea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∏We propose to observe the superbubble (SB) 30 Dor C with NuSTAR for 200 ks to probe particle acceleration within the region. SBs are plausible candidates for sites of cosmic-ray acceleration, yet observational evidence of diffuse non-thermal X-ray emission in SBs has remained controversial. Portions of 30 Dor C have been imaged off axis serendipitously by NuSTAR in its monitoring of nearby SN 1987A, and these data demonstrate that electrons are accelerated to TeV energies in the SB shell. However, at the off axis position of 30 Dor C, vignetting dramatically reduces the throughput of the instrument, and robust background subtraction is not possible. Thus, we request new observations close to the optical axis that are specifically designed to probe the hard X-ray emission of 30 Dor C.      Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Lopez                         Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE HIGHEST ENERGY ELECTRONS ACCELERATED BY THE SUPERBUBBLE 30 DOR C WITH NUSTAR                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  2015031630_Dor_C                                          @Uå~($¿QM+”√a@qz4ÍK¿?·Œ_p@a≠◊
=p§@ÎˆÄ[ÃU˜@Îˆ◊w␀°40101015002AXT⁄QAMÌ7K∆®A¡4;Õ5®A¡Ù;Õ5®SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂÖrâ␀␀·THea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∏We propose to observe the superbubble (SB) 30 Dor C with NuSTAR for 200 ks to probe particle acceleration within the region. SBs are plausible candidates for sites of cosmic-ray acceleration, yet observational evidence of diffuse non-thermal X-ray emission in SBs has remained controversial. Portions of 30 Dor C have been imaged off axis serendipitously by NuSTAR in its monitoring of nearby SN 1987A, and these data demonstrate that electrons are accelerated to TeV energies in the SB shell. However, at the off axis position of 30 Dor C, vignetting dramatically reduces the throughput of the instrument, and robust background subtraction is not possible. Thus, we request new observations close to the optical axis that are specifically designed to probe the hard X-ray emission of 30 Dor C.      Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Lopez                         Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE HIGHEST ENERGY ELECTRONS ACCELERATED BY THE SUPERBUBBLE 30 DOR C WITH NUSTAR                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004294U1636m53                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îˆ◊w␀°@Îˆ◊îØ‰030102014003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂàÈü´␀␀·BHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*ÇFe K- lines have now been detected in about half a dozen neutron-star accreting X-ray binaries. Under the commonly accepted interpretation, these lines can be used to set tight constrains on the accretion disk geometry and on the radius of the neutron star. Using simultaneous XMM-Newton/RXTE observations of 4U 1636-53, we have recently shown that the inner disc radius deduced from the characteristic frequency of the timing features and from the Fe-line profile does not change consistently with time, contradicting the expected scenario. It is intriguing that the Fe line appears to behave differently. Here we propose to observe this system with XMM-Newton three times for 30 ks, simultaneously with NuStar observations.                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sanna                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Tracing the accretion disk using Fe lines and kHz QPOs                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U1636m53                                         @oE˚µsÎ¿J‚Ú‰éä@tÃ§?Â…¿†[¿6„@u\i≠B√ @Îˆ◊îØ‰0@ÎˆÓ±!´L30102014004@›üùŒ_p@›≤œöu@‡ÆØ4ÍK@‡±O4ÍKSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ$‚	‹␀␀·BHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*ÇFe K- lines have now been detected in about half a dozen neutron-star accreting X-ray binaries. Under the commonly accepted interpretation, these lines can be used to set tight constrains on the accretion disk geometry and on the radius of the neutron star. Using simultaneous XMM-Newton/RXTE observations of 4U 1636-53, we have recently shown that the inner disc radius deduced from the characteristic frequency of the timing features and from the Fe-line profile does not change consistently with time, contradicting the expected scenario. It is intriguing that the Fe line appears to behave differently. Here we propose to observe this system with XMM-Newton three times for 30 ks, simultaneously with NuStar observations.                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sanna                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Tracing the accretion disk using Fe lines and kHz QPOs                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_6240                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎˆÓ±!´L@ÎˆÔ?Zé⁄60102042003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ˜.¶␀␀·“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀@We propose to monitor the hard X-ray behavior of the Compton-thick AGN pair in the galaxy merger NGC 6240 with NuSTAR. Together with the existing 30-ks observation performed in March 2014, this series of three 20-ks snapshots will allow us to probe the source variability on timescales ranging from a few months to almost two years. Recent results from the Swift/BAT survey suggest a strong energy dependence of the excess variance, peaking around 20 keV. If driven by column density changes, the different spectral states would bring further insights on the structure and dynamics of the gas in proximity of the black holes. In any case, the increased total exposure will make it possible to perform a comprehensive analysis and take into account the dual AGN nature of the hard X-ray source.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nardini                       Emanuele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUCLEAR OBSCURATION IN THE AGN MERGER NGC 6240                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_6240                                          @oß@N§®¡@ìtºj@4üæv»¥9@;z	ﬁ␀“@uNá+J@ÎˆÔ?Zé⁄@Î˜?Zé⁄60102042004@÷wfÊ1¯°@÷q á+@ÿA‰À)^û@ÿHóˆ+kSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂÆæc^␀␀·“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀@We propose to monitor the hard X-ray behavior of the Compton-thick AGN pair in the galaxy merger NGC 6240 with NuSTAR. Together with the existing 30-ks observation performed in March 2014, this series of three 20-ks snapshots will allow us to probe the source variability on timescales ranging from a few months to almost two years. Recent results from the Swift/BAT survey suggest a strong energy dependence of the excess variance, peaking around 20 keV. If driven by column density changes, the different spectral states would bring further insights on the structure and dynamics of the gas in proximity of the black holes. In any case, the increased total exposure will make it possible to perform a comprehensive analysis and take into account the dual AGN nature of the hard X-ray source.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nardini                       Emanuele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUCLEAR OBSCURATION IN THE AGN MERGER NGC 6240                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  202004292MASXJ18241083p1846088                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜?Zé⁄@Î˜±!´L60160686001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÑçû␀␀ﬂøHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ18241083p1846088                            @q@eîØO@2øÂ…‰@Go»K]Ãd@,‹(ı¬è\@u∫N§®¡U@Î˜±!´L@Î˜"Ë«æ60160686002@◊V?@◊`1&È@ÿµ€F‹]d@ÿ∂õF‹]dSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ=àÈ†␀␀ﬂøHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX339m4                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜"Ë«æ@Î˜Õìrh80102011005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ %␀␀·QHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX339m4                                           @oıjÁ’fœ¿Hf˘€"–Â@u-	ﬁ␀“¿ûûö@uñRΩ<6@Î˜Õìrh@Î˜$Í9Ö80102011006@”T C˛\í@”cX]càf@’’®qv@’‹hqvSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ¿Úπ÷␀␀·QHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀ToO (3/6)                                                                                                               archived  20200429BZB_J0123m2310                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜$Í9Ö@Î˜%Õìrh60101060001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂô‚jÛ␀␀·1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‚We wish to exploit the unique capabilities of NuSTAR supported by simultaneous observations by the Swift and Fermi-LAT to search for the most powerful BL Lac objects. NuSTAR observations of a carefully chosen sample of high-peaked BL Lacs at 3-79keV will provide strong constraints on the high-energy portion of the electron distribution in the primary jet emitting region. Since these are the same electrons responsible for the very high energy (VHE, E>100 GeV) gamma-ray emission, it will allow testable predictions of the VHE emission. This will identify the best sources to observe with atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes for the goal of constraining the extragalactic background light (EBL) and the intergalactic magnetic field (IGMF).                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Becerra Gonzalez              Josefa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       SEARCHING FOR COSMOLOGICAL PROBES WITH NUSTAR                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZB_J0123m2310                                    @4ÒŒ_p¿7*d¬¯7µ@gD WßÜ¬¿TwRT`™e@f¶L/É{@Î˜%Õìrh@Î˜4?Zé⁄60101060002@’@ç∏ª@’5ØÔù≤-@÷Æxl"h@÷Æxl"hSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂQY‚k␀␀·1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‚We wish to exploit the unique capabilities of NuSTAR supported by simultaneous observations by the Swift and Fermi-LAT to search for the most powerful BL Lac objects. NuSTAR observations of a carefully chosen sample of high-peaked BL Lacs at 3-79keV will provide strong constraints on the high-energy portion of the electron distribution in the primary jet emitting region. Since these are the same electrons responsible for the very high energy (VHE, E>100 GeV) gamma-ray emission, it will allow testable predictions of the VHE emission. This will identify the best sources to observe with atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes for the goal of constraining the extragalactic background light (EBL) and the intergalactic magnetic field (IGMF).                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Becerra Gonzalez              Josefa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       SEARCHING FOR COSMOLOGICAL PROBES WITH NUSTAR                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GK_Per                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜4?Zé⁄@Î˜5w␀°30101021001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ*Û|␀␀·1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÎGK Persei is a peculiar cataclysmic variable (CV) system that exploded as a classical nova in 1901, and is currently emiting hard X-rays as a typical intermediate polar -type magnetic CV. Based on a nobel method that fits optical nova light curve, Hachisu & Kato (2007) estimated a white dwarf (WD) mass to be 1.15+/-0.05 Msun. By observing GK Per with NuSTAR for 70 ks, we aim to estimate a WD mass based on our broad-band X-ray spectral model (Yuasa et al., 2010) with a statistical fitting error smaller than 0.05 Msun. The estimated WD mass will be used to cross-validate our method against the optical light curve fitting method to establish reliable WD mass estimation methods for nova and magnetic CV systems in optical and X-ray wavelength, respectively.                                      Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Yuasa                         Takayuki                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CROSS-VALIDATION OF WHITE DWARF MASS ESTIMATION METHODS                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GK_Per                                            @Jg‚Ç@∑Ä@E˜o“ÚÂ@b⁄T`™d√¿$rïÈ·∞ä@`©5®Xy>@Î˜5w␀°@Î˜eÍ9Ö30101021002@Ò¶πŸá+@ÒûDä	ﬁ@ÚÊ∫m\˙≠@ÚÁm\˙≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ∂Õé˚␀␀·1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÎGK Persei is a peculiar cataclysmic variable (CV) system that exploded as a classical nova in 1901, and is currently emiting hard X-rays as a typical intermediate polar -type magnetic CV. Based on a nobel method that fits optical nova light curve, Hachisu & Kato (2007) estimated a white dwarf (WD) mass to be 1.15+/-0.05 Msun. By observing GK Per with NuSTAR for 70 ks, we aim to estimate a WD mass based on our broad-band X-ray spectral model (Yuasa et al., 2010) with a statistical fitting error smaller than 0.05 Msun. The estimated WD mass will be used to cross-validate our method against the optical light curve fitting method to establish reliable WD mass estimation methods for nova and magnetic CV systems in optical and X-ray wavelength, respectively.                                      Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Yuasa                         Takayuki                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CROSS-VALIDATION OF WHITE DWARF MASS ESTIMATION METHODS                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004291ES_0347m121                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜eÍ9Ö@Î˜fx>60101036001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÜr(␀␀·1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Some BLLacs are characterized by a hard intrinsic TeV spectrum irrespective of EBL absorption, locating the Compton peak above few TeV. This is difficult to reconcile with the typically softer spectrum of the synchrotron emission observed in these objects, for standard leptonic emission models and parameters. A different particle population might be responsible. We propose NuSTAR observations to pin down the synchrotron emission of these hard TeV electrons, either as a new hard component emerging in the hard X-ray band, or by constraining the synchrotron peak in the SED at the highest X-ray energies. The result can shed light on the origin of this hard TeV emission, as yet unexplained, and on the underlying acceleration mechanism.                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Costamante                    Luigi                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       UNVEILING THE ORIGIN OF THE HARD TEV EMISSION IN BLLACS                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161ES_0347m121                                      @LØ'ª/ÏW¿'Ôù≤-V@i;n.±ƒ3¿FÌm\˙¨⁄@c>Öáì›ò@Î˜fx>@Î˜}[ÃU˜60101036002@‡M64@‡CfbM”@·qºêñº@·r‹êñºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ=p£◊␀␀·1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Some BLLacs are characterized by a hard intrinsic TeV spectrum irrespective of EBL absorption, locating the Compton peak above few TeV. This is difficult to reconcile with the typically softer spectrum of the synchrotron emission observed in these objects, for standard leptonic emission models and parameters. A different particle population might be responsible. We propose NuSTAR observations to pin down the synchrotron emission of these hard TeV electrons, either as a new hard component emerging in the hard X-ray band, or by constraining the synchrotron peak in the SED at the highest X-ray energies. The result can shed light on the origin of this hard TeV emission, as yet unexplained, and on the underlying acceleration mechanism.                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Costamante                    Luigi                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       UNVEILING THE ORIGIN OF THE HARD TEV EMISSION IN BLLACS                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429V0332p53                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜}[ÃU˜@Î˜~w␀°80102002003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬂÈ>î␀␀·EHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V0332p53                                          @J· [¿7@Jòöu%F@b=˘rGE9¿Çu%F
¶@`gÛ∂E°À@Î˜~w␀°@Î˜çx>80102002004@Õª6zë@Õ√H≠´üV@◊Áﬁqﬁi≠@◊Èqﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬂHW¸˜␀␀·EHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGRJ18293m1213                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜çx>@Î˜éîØ‰030161002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ´¬␀␀ﬂ√Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ18293m1213                                    @qU⁄QŒ¿(xl"h	’@3nÀ˚µtø·å~($x@tı§®¡T @Î˜éîØ‰0@Î˜•x>30161002002@›3)áì›ò@›5Ü¬&Å@ﬂß©ñRΩ<@ﬂ™ÈñRΩ<SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ␀ŒÅ¥Ë␀␀ﬂ√Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGR_J16493m4348                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜•x>@Î˜•Í9Ö30102054005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ␀¶ï␀␀·1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀There are now at least 5 supergiant High-Mass X-ray Binaries where superorbital periods have been found - predominantly at energies above 15 keV. Curiously, these periods increase monotonically with orbital period. We propose to obtain two NuSTAR observations of IGR J16493-4348 to determine the detailed spectral properties and their changes over its superorbital period. Suggested driving mechanisms for the modulation include pulsations in the primary star, 3 body systems, and precession of the primary with an equatorial wind. To allow detailed modeling to proceed, good characterization of the modulation properties in hard X-rays is first required. We will also determine whether a previously claimed cyclotron feature at ~30 keV is present.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROPERTIES AND CAUSE OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J16493m4348                                   @oãŒ_p¿EÎS&¬@uT␀␀␀␀␀?ÁŸb∂Æ}@unHËßÁ@Î˜•Í9Ö@Î˜¥±!´L30102054006@’Xï*ô@’?`ƒõ•„@÷»k3333@÷»k3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬂ␀X¶ﬂ√␀␀·1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀There are now at least 5 supergiant High-Mass X-ray Binaries where superorbital periods have been found - predominantly at energies above 15 keV. Curiously, these periods increase monotonically with orbital period. We propose to obtain two NuSTAR observations of IGR J16493-4348 to determine the detailed spectral properties and their changes over its superorbital period. Suggested driving mechanisms for the modulation include pulsations in the primary star, 3 body systems, and precession of the primary with an equatorial wind. To allow detailed modeling to proceed, good characterization of the modulation properties in hard X-rays is first required. We will also determine whether a previously claimed cyclotron feature at ~30 keV is present.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROPERTIES AND CAUSE OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX339m4                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜¥±!´L@Î˜µw␀°80102011007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬂ␀3¨ê␀␀·QHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀         