                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜≈Í9Ö@Î˜∆Í9Ö60061346001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁˇº5ß␀␀ﬂ√Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ARP318                                            @@2¿Énò¿$C◊
=p§@e∏5?|Ìë¿PG±[W>´@däqﬁi≠@Î˜∆Í9Ö@Î˜’?Zé⁄60061346002@‘0BßÔù≤@‘&a«y¶µ@’∞D‡uˆ˝@’±ƒ‡uˆ˝SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁˇ~KÂ␀␀ﬂ√Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M31_Bulge                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˜’?Zé⁄@Î˜÷w␀°50101001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁˇ+Tì'␀␀·=Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ŸWe propose a 100 ks NuSTAR observation of the M31 bulge to investigate the resolved point source population at E >10 keV. This observation will allow us to investigate, for the first time, source populations in an older stellar environment at harder energies outside our own galaxy. Our primary objectives are (1) to explore the previously detected, luminous, E > 10 keV emission by Swift BAT with NuSTAR's better PSF, (2) to investigate the nature and accretion states of the resolved point sources, and (3) to compare and examine hard X-ray point sources in the different stellar environments (disk vs. bulge).                                                                                                                                                                                            Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Yukita                        Mihoko                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       BINARIES IN THE M31 BULGE: A HARD X-RAY VIEW OF OLD STELLAR POPULATIONS                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_Bulge                                         @%HXy=Ÿ@Dûªò«‚Ç@^=˙C˛\í¿5≠5®Xy>@VÑÜYJÙÒ@Î˜÷w␀°@Î¯"Ë«æ50101001002@¯?√…ÓÃ@¯␀Îo“Û@˘ºñŒŸá@˘Ωg5?|ÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˛ûoÄí␀␀·=Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ŸWe propose a 100 ks NuSTAR observation of the M31 bulge to investigate the resolved point source population at E >10 keV. This observation will allow us to investigate, for the first time, source populations in an older stellar environment at harder energies outside our own galaxy. Our primary objectives are (1) to explore the previously detected, luminous, E > 10 keV emission by Swift BAT with NuSTAR's better PSF, (2) to investigate the nature and accretion states of the resolved point sources, and (3) to compare and examine hard X-ray point sources in the different stellar environments (disk vs. bulge).                                                                                                                                                                                            Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Yukita                        Mihoko                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       BINARIES IN THE M31 BULGE: A HARD X-RAY VIEW OF OLD STELLAR POPULATIONS                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GRS_1739m278                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¯"Ë«æ@Î¯?Zé⁄80101050001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˛fN û␀␀·DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)ΩWe propose to make a joint 80 ks XMM-Newton plus 40 ks NuSTAR observation of a new or known black hole transient during the end of an outburst, after it entered the low-hard state. The goal is to measure the inner truncation radius of the accretion disk and to study the accretion geometry before the black hole goes into quiescence again. We aim to observe a source at luminosities below 1% L_edd, to measure the most significant changes in the inner radius and taking full advantage of the combined sensitivity of XMM and NuSTAR. The observations will be supported by radio observations to study the connection between the accretion disk and jet power.                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The accretion geometry in a black-hole binary at very low luminosities                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1739m278                                      @pöAâ7K«¿;¬‰éäqﬁ?·iDg8~?ıÁlãCïÅ@u!A [¿@Î¯?Zé⁄@Î¯<w␀°80101050002@Â.ï⁄QŒ@ÂF
¶L@Ê€| Wßá@Ê›| WßáSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˛ß@⁄␀␀·DHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀)ΩWe propose to make a joint 80 ks XMM-Newton plus 40 ks NuSTAR observation of a new or known black hole transient during the end of an outburst, after it entered the low-hard state. The goal is to measure the inner truncation radius of the accretion disk and to study the accretion geometry before the black hole goes into quiescence again. We aim to observe a source at luminosities below 1% L_edd, to measure the most significant changes in the inner radius and taking full advantage of the combined sensitivity of XMM and NuSTAR. The observations will be supported by radio observations to study the connection between the accretion disk and jet power.                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The accretion geometry in a black-hole binary at very low luminosities                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429V0332p53                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¯<w␀°@Î¯=?Zé⁄80102002005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˝ﬂIÙü␀␀·EHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V0332p53                                          @JﬂÚ‰éär@Jôﬁi≠Bƒ@b=•ú‡v¿x†ê-‡@`␀eîØO@Î¯=?Zé⁄@Î¯L"Ë«æ80102002006@–ûóg†˘	@–ÌÃ[¿7@◊‰ã1è≈@◊Â1è≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˝:7_2␀␀·EHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO (2/2)                                                                                                               archived  20200429GX339m4                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¯L"Ë«æ@Î¯Mx>80102011009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˝?V␀␀·QHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX339m4                                           @oı[W>´6¿HgØ∑È@u-ÍJå¿ùÃcÒA @ulÂ`Aâ7@Î¯Mx>@Î¯i"Ë«æ80102011010@‚ÃµHÀí@‚ŒúPH@‰ã¡ì‡ﬂ@‰çAì‡ﬂSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ¸®ı¬è␀␀·QHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀) We propose to perform (as a ToO) a series of 4x10ks+2x40 ks joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations (spaced by a few days) of a microquasar during its outburst decline phase to follow its broadband X-ray spectral evolution with an unprecedented precision. We will perform simultaneous radio, optical and IR observations to observe the reactivation of the jet. This will allow us i) to carefully investigate the physical/geometrical changes of the disc/corona system through the transition and ii) to reveal the drivers of the quenching/reappearance of the jets during the state transitions and how they connect to the properties of the accretion flow.                                                                                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       X-ray spectral evolution of microquasars during soft-to-hard state transitions                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(5/6)                                                                                                                   archived  202004294U1636m53                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¯i"Ë«æ@Î¯ix>30102014005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ¸{£uÛ␀␀·BHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*ÇFe K- lines have now been detected in about half a dozen neutron-star accreting X-ray binaries. Under the commonly accepted interpretation, these lines can be used to set tight constrains on the accretion disk geometry and on the radius of the neutron star. Using simultaneous XMM-Newton/RXTE observations of 4U 1636-53, we have recently shown that the inner disc radius deduced from the characteristic frequency of the timing features and from the Fe-line profile does not change consistently with time, contradicting the expected scenario. It is intriguing that the Fe line appears to behave differently. Here we propose to observe this system with XMM-Newton three times for 30 ks, simultaneously with NuStar observations.                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sanna                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Tracing the accretion disk using Fe lines and kHz QPOs                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U1636m53                                         @oE‡qv¿J‚í£S&@tÃîFsÅÿ¿°-w1è≈@uøË>BZÓ@Î¯ix>@Î¯?Zé⁄30102014006@‹>ß/ÏV÷@‹GRh	‘ï@ﬂpK6zë@ﬂtK6zëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ¸“ﬁ?␀␀·BHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*ÇFe K- lines have now been detected in about half a dozen neutron-star accreting X-ray binaries. Under the commonly accepted interpretation, these lines can be used to set tight constrains on the accretion disk geometry and on the radius of the neutron star. Using simultaneous XMM-Newton/RXTE observations of 4U 1636-53, we have recently shown that the inner disc radius deduced from the characteristic frequency of the timing features and from the Fe-line profile does not change consistently with time, contradicting the expected scenario. It is intriguing that the Fe line appears to behave differently. Here we propose to observe this system with XMM-Newton three times for 30 ks, simultaneously with NuStar observations.                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sanna                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Tracing the accretion disk using Fe lines and kHz QPOs                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSRB0656p14                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¯?Zé⁄@Î¯Ä[ÃU˜40101004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˚Û›∞␀␀·AHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*We propose XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of PSR B0656+14. Its X-ray spectrum shows complex variability with the pulsar‚Äôs rotation phase. Our re-analysis of archival data suggests that much of the variability could be due to spectral feature(s) rather than a hot polar cap. If confirmed, it would only be the second case when such features are seen in the spectrum of a rotation-powered pulsar. The origin of the features must be intimately linked to the properties of NS atmospheres or magnetospheres. However, one should study the features in more than one pulsar before a definitive interpretation can be given. In addition, our observations will fill in the gap in the multiwavelength spectrum of B0656+14 by measuring its non-thermal spectrum above 6 keV.                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Kargaltsev                    Oleg                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Detailed study of PSR B0656+14                                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSRB0656p14                                       @Z>i≠B√ @,Ñµ‹∆?@i©*0U2@ 9≥–|Ñ∂@c≤WßÜ¬'@Î¯Ä[ÃU˜@Î¯Î"Ë«æ40101004002A4÷RΩ<6A)@^“àŒA{/kπå~A{ØûÏø±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˚ôôö␀␀·AHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*We propose XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of PSR B0656+14. Its X-ray spectrum shows complex variability with the pulsar‚Äôs rotation phase. Our re-analysis of archival data suggests that much of the variability could be due to spectral feature(s) rather than a hot polar cap. If confirmed, it would only be the second case when such features are seen in the spectrum of a rotation-powered pulsar. The origin of the features must be intimately linked to the properties of NS atmospheres or magnetospheres. However, one should study the features in more than one pulsar before a definitive interpretation can be given. In addition, our observations will fill in the gap in the multiwavelength spectrum of B0656+14 by measuring its non-thermal spectrum above 6 keV.                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Kargaltsev                    Oleg                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Detailed study of PSR B0656+14                                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SADA_2015_265_37                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î¯Î"Ë«æ@Î¯Ï"Ë«æ10002109001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˙—Aú¢␀␀ﬂœHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4507                                          @gõƒ2 W®¿CÚC˛\ë—@r∂ÿD–@6Úè\(ı√@H®Ô4÷°b@Î¯Ï"Ë«æ@Î˘x>60102051009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˙åkµ™␀␀·=Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ƒX-ray monitoring of bright Seyfert that show changes in the amount of X-ray absorption, are the key programs in assessing the nature and location of the X-ray absorbing media present in the central regions of AGN. We propose a monitoring (5x30ks) of the obscured and bright AGN NGC4507. NGC4507 shows dramatic changes of its X-ray emission altering from transmission to reflection dominated states and a strong variability of its X-ray flux in the 14-195keV band. NuSTAR sensitivity over the 3-79 keV energy range offers a unique tool to investigate the X-ray emission of NGC4507, allowing us to decouple any possible ambiguity on the variability of the intrinsic emission and amount of absorption. This will be the first monitoring on short timescale of this AGN in the hard X-ray band.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PROBING THE ABSORPTION AND CONTINUUM VARIABILITY IN THE SEYFERT 2 NGC4507                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(5/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20150316T_CrB                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˘x>@Î˘[ÃU˜30101046001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˙G}àÍ␀␀·=Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¯Symbiotic stars are powered by a white dwarf accreting from a late type giant mass donor. We now know that many symbiotic stars are medium energy (2-10 keV) X-ray sources, but some stand out as luminous, hard sources detected in the INTEGRAL and Swift/BAT surveys. The X-rays likely originate in the accretion disk boundary layer, and so we expect reflection from the white dwarf surface and the disk. The highest temperature plasma present allows us to set a lower limit to their white dwarf mass, probably to values not too far from the Chandrasekhar limit. We therefore propose NuSTAR observations of three X-ray luminous symbiotic systems, T CrB, RT Cru and V648 Car, to (1) confirm the presence of an accretion disk via the presence of reflection, and (2) constrain the white dwarf mass.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nelso