0429IGR_J16418m4532                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˘o"Ë«æ@Î˘o±!´L30101036001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˜˚‘Ñ␀␀·DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÑThe detection of cyclotron absorption features in Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXT) is a powerful tool to unveil the physical processes driving their accretion modes. We propose a 40 ks observation by NuSTAR of the candidate SFXT IGR J16418 4532. The recent discovery by NuSTAR of a CSRF in the SFXT IGR J17544 2619 (Bhalerao et al. 2014) has motivated us to build upon this result by exploring the role of the magnetic field to characterize the accretion mode in these objects. A 40ks observation by NuSTAR will allow a sensitive cyclotron line search and a detailed study of the high energy spectrum of IGR J16418 4532 in two distinct accretion modes, characterized by low intensity and flaring emission.                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Natalucci                     Lorenzo                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       INVESTIGATION OF ACCRETION MODES AND MAGNETIC FIELD IN THE TRANSIENT HMXB IGR J16418 4532                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J16418m4532                                   @oMõ=»K¿F«RT`™e@u0ﬁi≠Bƒ?Âº£@u›i≠B√ @Î˘o±!´L@Î˘ã"Ë«æ30101036002@„–#¢úx@„ ”õ=»@ÂX{˝ä⁄∫@ÂY˝ä⁄∫SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˜u™Iî␀␀·DHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÑThe detection of cyclotron absorption features in Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXT) is a powerful tool to unveil the physical processes driving their accretion modes. We propose a 40 ks observation by NuSTAR of the candidate SFXT IGR J16418 4532. The recent discovery by NuSTAR of a CSRF in the SFXT IGR J17544 2619 (Bhalerao et al. 2014) has motivated us to build upon this result by exploring the role of the magnetic field to characterize the accretion mode in these objects. A 40ks observation by NuSTAR will allow a sensitive cyclotron line search and a detailed study of the high energy spectrum of IGR J16418 4532 in two distinct accretion modes, characterized by low intensity and flaring emission.                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Natalucci                     Lorenzo                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       INVESTIGATION OF ACCRETION MODES AND MAGNETIC FIELD IN THE TRANSIENT HMXB IGR J16418 4532                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASSJ16561677m3302127                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˘ã"Ë«æ@Î˘ãx>60160657001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ˜HÈü´␀␀ﬂ÷Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASSJ16561677m3302127                            @o¡B√…ÓÃ¿@áº@uÁVlÙ!@)x‘˝Û∂@u}0U2a|@Î˘ãx>@Î˘ö?Zé⁄60160657002@‘§”6zë@‘°⁄è\(ˆ@÷Üa|⁄@÷∆a|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ˜␀Úπ÷␀␀ﬂ÷Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429RXJ1713d7m3946_NW_P1                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˘ö?Zé⁄@Î˘öîØ‰040111001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁˆŸˆÊ␀␀·DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀~RX J1713.7-3946 is the strongest synchrotron X-ray and TeV gamma-ray emitter among galactic SNRs, and therefore an excellent SNR to compare the spectral shapes of the synchrotron X-ray and TeV gamma-ray emissions in great details. Our recent study with Chandra has revealed the presence of unresolved very hard X-ray sources along time-variable synchrotron X-ray filaments. The properties of the hard point-like sources challenge the standard perception of shock acceleration. Here we propose NuSTAR observations of SNR RX J1713.7-3946 to measure hard X-ray spectra of diffuse synchrotron X-rays and the very hard point-like source. The NuSTAR measurements can constrain the key parameters of shock acceleration such as diffusion coefficient at shocks.                                                Non-T