                             ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will undertake a comprehensive program of observations of magnetars and rotation-powered pulsars, designed to study the surprising hard X-ray emission discovered by INTEGRAL from these objects and to shed light on the broad-band spectra between the soft X-ray and high-energy gamma-ray bands. With its sensitive hard X-ray response NuSTAR can extend the soft X-ray spectrum by an order of magnitude in energy, allowing detailed constraints on the high-energy emission mechanisms of these objects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀MAG       Magnetars and Rotation-Powered Pulsars                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_301m2                                          @gSÙá¸π$¿O^˘€"–Â@røûö?∞â†'RTa@PÎ¢úwök@Î˙j?Zé⁄@Î˙j±!´L30101042001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÚ/4∏␀␀·DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∆We propose to observe the HMXB GX301-2 for 30ks around orbital phase 0.75-0.85 to measure the luminosity dependence of its cyclotron line. This observation will allow us to understand the accretion geometry of GX301-2 and provide a missing link between archival observations. A first NuSTAR observation was performed in October 2014 around orbital phase 0.5 during which the source was in an anomalous low state. The new data will allow us to compare a higher luminosity observation to this very low luminosity data and compare spectral changes as function of luminosity and orbital phase to investigate the accretion geometry. Additionally they will allow us to perform time- and phase-resolved spectral analysis to study short term changes in the X-ray production region.                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       LUMINOSITY DEPENDENCE OF THE CYCLOTRON LINE IN GX 301-2                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_301m2                                          @gS$tSéÛ¿O^ÄùIQÉ@røm\˙¨⁄?±-w1è≈@Q?Â…@Î˙j±!´L@Î˙~?Zé⁄30101042002@·p∑ÆÊ1˘@·|¥œAÚ@‰		ökP±@‰
