egions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V0332p53                                          @JﬂÚ‰éär@Jôkπå~(@b=µsÍ≥h¿~\ë—N<@]#”√a@@Î˙x>@Î˙èÕìrh80102002010@‘VDcÒA @‘pU∏QÏ@◊$Œp:˚@◊‰Œp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÒ(d‹␀␀‡éHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*;In spite of more than forty years of studies open questions remain on the structure and physical properties of the emission regions of accreting X-ray pulsars. Here, we propose to observe the transient X-ray pulsar V0332+53 with two 20ks pointings during the decline of an outburst with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. These joint TOO observations will allow us to observe the source both in high and low luminosity states when the accretion regime and the emission region are expected to drastically change. The unique sensitivity and broadband coverage offered by a simultaneous XMM Newton/NuSTAR observations will allow a detailed spectral and timing analysis in both states. This is essential to constrain the properties of the emission region and the critical luminosity.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Accretion column: from dawn till dusk                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO (3/3)                                                                                                               archived  20200429Fornax_A_Offset                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˙èÕìrh@Î˙ê±!´L60101041001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁˇ%ãÚ␀␀·KHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀=Radio lobes produced by AGN can strongly impact their environment, so it is important to understand the energetics of these systems. Radio synchrotron emission tells us the slope of the energy distribution, but the energy and magnetic field strength are degenerate. This degeneracy is broken if one also has the inverse Compton flux produced by scattering of CMB photons, which is visible in the hard X-rays. There are few systems with detected IC lobes and even fewer where one can measure changes in the spectral index as a function of position. We will make a hard X-ray spectral index map in the best target for this type of work, Fornax A, to map the particle energy and field strength in young and old plasma.                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Hodges-Kluck                  Edmund                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       A HARD X-RAY MAP OF THE NEARBY RADIO GALAXY FORNAX A                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Fornax_A_Offset                                   @IHD–©*¿Bùp£◊
