¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˝îØ‰0@Î˝îØ‰040101001003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÂæƒt–␀␀·QHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)¡We propose XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of the Arches cluster to improve our knowledge of the X-ray reflection, which has recently been shown to be the likely origin of the non-thermal emission from the cluster. The aims are to obtain a measurement of the variable iron line flux, an accurate morphology map of the fluorescent iron line emission along with 10-20 keV non-thermal continuum, and a broadband spectrum of the neutral material outside the star cluster. The proposed observation is a unique possibility (and maybe the last chance) to study fading fluorescent emission of this isolated and compact molecular cloud in the Galactic center.                                                                                                                                                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Krivonos                      Roman                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Non-thermal X-rays from the Arches cluster region                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Arches_cluster                                    @p¶Ÿá+¿<◊s¸PH@v WßÜ¬?À=»K]Ã@u9¬¯7¥¢@Î˝îØ‰0@Î˝h?Zé⁄40101001004@˙T=«‚ÇA@˙3®zêó@¸{;Õ5®@¸a;Õ5®SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ‰˝X^␀␀·QHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀)¡We propose XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of the Arches cluster to improve our knowledge of the X-ray reflection, which has recently been shown to be the likely origin of the non-thermal emission from the cluster. The aims are to obtain a measurement of the variable iron line flux, an accurate morphology map of the fluorescent iron line emission along with 10-20 keV non-thermal continuum, and a broadband spectrum of the neutral material outside the star cluster. The proposed observation is a unique possibility (and maybe the last chance) to study fading fluorescent emission of this isolated and compact molecular cloud in the Galactic center.                                                                                                                                                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Krivonos                      Roman                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Non-thermal X-rays from the Arches cluster region                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk348                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˝h?Zé⁄@Î˝i?Zé⁄60160026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ‰æKÂ␀␀ﬂÚHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk348                                            @(LÃÃÃÃÕ@?Ò¯†ê-‡@^Å≈÷8ÜY¿?␀h€ã¨q@0E'ª0@Î˝i?Zé⁄@Î˝wÕìrh60160026002@’”‚Ç@∏@‘ˆ˚ÁlãD@÷r˜˛\ë—@÷r˜˛\ë—SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ‰pãëB␀␀ﬂÚHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PSR_J0218p4232                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˝wÕìrh@Î˝x?Zé⁄30101030001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ‰I´Õ␀␀·aHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀fMillisecond pulsars (MSPs) are old neutron stars spun up by accretion from companion stars. The recent launch of the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope has greatly increased the MSP sample and identified a new subclass, known as Class II, MSPs that show aligned pulse profiles in different energy bands. This could represent a new radio emission mechanism in the outer magnetosphere. We suggest that the radio waves could inverse-Compton scatter with energetic particles to produce hard X-ray emission, and we developed a model to account for the high-energy emission. To directly test this model, we propose NuSTAR observations to measure the hard X-ray spectra of several Class II MSPs. The results will also offer predictions to the low-frequency radio spectra for a comparison with on-going studies.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ng                            C.-Y.                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HARD X-RAY EMISSION OF CLASS II MILLISECOND PULSARS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J0218p4232                                    @A?Ú‰éär@EE*ô0æ@ajÔ4÷°b¿1ßˆ+jÁ’@Qè≈Åo@Î˝x?Zé⁄@Î˝ëw␀°30101030002@‡Ì™ìtºj@‡„_xF‹@‚<ÃÃÃÕ@‚ ÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ„˙”†␀␀·aHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀fMillisecond pulsars (MSPs) are old neutron stars spun up by accretion from companion stars. The recent launch of the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope has greatly increased the MSP sample and identified a new subclass, known as Class II, MSPs that show aligned pulse profiles in different energy bands. This could represent a new radio emission mechanism in the outer magnetosphere. We suggest that the radio waves could inverse-Compton scatter with energetic particles to produce hard X-ray emission, and we developed a model to account for the high-energy emission. To directly test this model, we propose NuSTAR observations to measure the hard X-ray spectra of several Class II MSPs. The results will also offer predictions to the low-frequency radio spectra for a comparison with on-going studies.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ng                            C.-Y.                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HARD X-RAY EMISSION OF CLASS II MILLISECOND PULSARS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Arp_256                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˝ëw␀°@Î˝íw␀°60101069001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ„–n]␀␀·aHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙With the unrivaled 10-30 keV sensitivity provided by NuSTAR, we propose a snapshot program to observe a volume-limited sample of 13 luminous infrared galaxies spread across the galaxy major merger sequence. When combined with archival NuSTAR data, these observations stand to place the strongest constraints to date on the timing of SMBH growth during galaxy evolution, effectively probing the potential accretion signatures from even Compton-thick AGN (going where Chandra could not).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bauer                         Franz                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       WHEN IN THE MAJOR MERGER SEQUENCE IS (OBSCURED) BLACK HOLE GROWTH TRIGGERED?                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  