er position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCGp07m05m016                                     @?ÈÖoiD@Fl∆?@aÁ’fœB¿0ú‡uˆ˝@F1GÆz·@Î˛¥?Zé⁄@Î˛¬Õìrh60161089002@’F6≥g†˘@’7“ªò«„@÷†U+”√a@÷†U+”√aSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ›æìÈ?␀␀ﬂ˚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429TXS_0322p222                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Î˛¬Õìrh@Î˛√x>60101079001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ›óSÀ␀␀·hHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses,are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 3 most luminous MeV blazars detected by Fermi that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift and GROND to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 2 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_0322p222                                      @I≥6zêó@6l”ZÖáî@dp?º¿<+‡ﬁ“àŒ@^2àŒp:˚@Î˛√x>@Î˛‘?Zé⁄60101079002@’±	‘ï@’• tSéÛ@◊Là␀␀␀␀@◊QH␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ›R≈˘,␀␀·hHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses,are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 3 most luminous MeV blazars detected by Fermi that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift and GROND to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 2 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL         