e calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀·␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Data gap due to overwrite of on-board data storage                                                                      archived  202004291RXS_J195815d6m301119                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Îˇô?Zé⁄@ÎˇôÕìrh60101056001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ◊¡¥Ë␀␀·uHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‚We wish to exploit the unique capabilities of NuSTAR supported by simultaneous observations by the Swift and Fermi-LAT to search for the most powerful BL Lac objects. NuSTAR observations of a carefully chosen sample of high-peaked BL Lacs at 3-79keV will provide strong constraints on the high-energy portion of the electron distribution in the primary jet emitting region. Since these are the same electrons responsible for the very high energy (VHE, E>100 GeV) gamma-ray emission, it will allow testable predictions of the VHE emission. This will identify the best sources to observe with atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes for the goal of constraining the extragalactic background light (EBL) and the intergalactic magnetic field (IGMF).                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Becerra Gonzalez              Josefa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       SEARCHING FOR COSMOLOGICAL PROBES WITH NUSTAR                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161RXS_J195815d6m301119                             @r∏Æz·H¿>3tºj~˙@%°◊€Ùá˝¿:ìnóçP@tvÖáì›ò@ÎˇôÕìrh@ÎˇΩîØ‰060101056002@È—Óg8~@È√U>´6z@Î¶»»¥9X@Î®H»¥9XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ◊^Ë›}␀␀·uHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‚We wish to exploit the unique capabilities of NuSTAR supported by simultaneous observations by the Swift and Fermi-LAT to search for the most powerful BL Lac objects. NuSTAR observations of a carefully chosen sample of high-peaked BL Lacs at 3-79keV will provide strong constraints on the high-energy portion of the electron distribution in the primary jet emitting region. Since these are the same electrons responsible for the very high energy (VHE, E>100 GeV) gamma-ray emission, it will allow testable predictions of the VHE emission. This will identify the best sources to observe with atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes for the goal of constraining the extragalactic background light (EBL) and the intergalactic magnetic field (IGMF).                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Becerra Gonzalez              Josefa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       SEARCHING FOR COSMOLOGICAL PROBES WITH NUSTAR                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ18305065p0928414                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎˇΩîØ‰0@Îˇæ"Ë«æ60061285001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ◊2°ê␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ18305065p0928414                            @q[-w1è≈@"Î6zêó@C~≈m\˙≠@"a@N•@rÌ∞ ƒõ¶@Îˇæ"Ë«æ@ÎˇŒw␀°60061285002@÷/hÚ{≤ˇ@÷%ôV@◊≥–…Öo@◊µ–…ÖoSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ÷Ì:‘␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4258                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÎˇŒw␀°@ÎˇŒÕìrh60101046001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ÷≈6˛␀␀·±Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sGiven the unique constraints we have on the mass, distance, and inclination of the low luminosity active galactic nuclei (LLAGN) NGC 4258, and it's unique position as a bridge source between bright Seyferts (accreting at 10% of the Eddington luminosity) and likely Advection Dominated Accretion Flow (ADAF) systems (accreting at <10^-6 Eddington luminosity), we propose a 150 ksec NuSTAR observation of this source. We will study the historically variable narrow Fe line, and model the continuum spectrum with partial covering powerlaw models. We will search for evidence of spectral curvature in the hard X-rays. This will be the first observation with statistics sufficient to perform such a study.                                                                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE LOW LUMINOSITY AGN NGC 4258                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4258                                          @gÊö‘,@GßÑM;@aOxF‹]@Q.∏QÎÖ@hÏ	‘ï+@ÎˇŒÕìrh@Îˇı"Ë«æ60101046002@Íø‘=p£◊@Í≥®[¿7@Ï£Z«