expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ10447m6027                                    @d(:˚~ë␀¿N2ÎC,•@q˝6‚ÎCøÛùIQÇ©ì@c‘y=Ÿc@Ï;Õìrh@ÏX"Ë«æ30161003004@Ê≥8ÜYK@Ê°ñí£S@Ë:⁄QŒ@Ë9ÉtºjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ–r␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Galactic Legacy Survey                                                                                                  archived  20200429PHL1811                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏX"Ë«æ@ÏYw␀°60101004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁœ„ÔP␀␀·ÖHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)üQuasars exhibit X-ray emission proportional to their UV emission, and most X-ray weak objects are absorbed. The exception may be PHL 1811, a nearby quasar that is 25--100 times X-ray fainter than expected, but has a steep and variable X-ray spectrum suggesting a direct view of the central engine. It also has unusual UV emission line properties that are consistent with its X-ray weak SED. Searches for PHL 1811 analogs, chosen by their UV spectral properties, find objects that are X-ray weak but which have hard X-ray spectra, suggesting absorption. Previous X-ray observations of PHL 1811 extended only to 6 keV, so a highly absorbed component can‚Äôt be ruled out. We propose coordinated XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations to see whether PHL 1811 is intrinsically normal and highly absorbed.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Leighly                       Karen                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Unifying X-ray Weak Quasars: Is there a Highly Absorbed Component in PHL 1811?                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PHL1811                                           @tãŸb∂Æ¿"Ã<ûÏø±@GöΩ<64¿FVö‘,<ü@süÉ{J#:@ÏYw␀°@ÏÖîØ‰060101004002@Íº≈∏∫«@Í™´F
