allow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cen_X3                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏóÍ9Ö@Ïò?Zé⁄30101055001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁŒ«L˝X␀␀·ÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose a NuSTAR observation of the accreting pulsar Cen X-3 in order to perform a spectral analysis of the currently unexplained feature that has been seen ~10 keV in the spectra of a number of these types of sources. This would be the first detailed study of the feature. NuSTAR is currently the best instrument for the scientific objectives of this project because it allows high precision broadband spectral measurements, particularly at energy where the feature has been observed. Our objectives are to constrain the parameters of the feature by applying empirical continuum models and also determine how both the boradband spectrum and the feature can be described with newly implemented physical models.                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Marcu                         Diana                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CENTAURUS X-3: IMPROVING THE CONTINUUM MODELING OF ACCRETING PULSARS                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cen_X3                                            @eKáì›óˆ¿NMÃcÒA @r@$›/?⁄¡Ω•ù@c/ó$tSè@Ïò?Zé⁄@Ï¶±!´L30101055002@‘ÏﬂˆîF@’(ı¬è@ŸÚæxl"h@ŸÛ>xl"hSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁŒ2°ê␀␀·ÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose a NuSTAR observation of the accreting pulsar Cen X-3 in order to perform a spectral analysis of the currently unexplained feature that has been seen ~10 keV in the spectra of a number of these types of sources. This would be the first detailed study of the feature. NuSTAR is currently the best instrument for the scientific objectives of this project because it allows high precision broadband spectral measurements, particularly at energy where the feature has been observed. Our objectives are to constrain the parameters of the feature by applying empirical continuum models and also determine how both the boradband spectrum and the feature can be described with newly implemented physical models.                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Marcu                         Diana                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CENTAURUS X-3: IMPROVING THE CONTINUUM MODELING OF ACCRETING PULSARS                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO060mIG16                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¶±!´L@Ïßw␀°60101053001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁŒ
ÁY ␀␀·ÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀€A detailed study of low-redshift LIRGs is critical for our understanding of the cosmic evolution of galaxies and black holes, since LIRGs comprise the bulk of the cosmic far-infrared background and dominate the star-formation between 0.5 < z < 1. Among the 254 galaxies that are part of the Great Observatories All-Sky LIRG Survey (GOALS), there are a dozen highly obscured galaxies those with extremely deep 9.7-micron silicate features, implying A_V > 30-80 mags. We propose to observe 4 of these sources with NuSTAR to search for buried AGN, measure the hard X-ray spectrum, and characterize the the nature of the obscuring circum-nuclear, interstellar medium on scales of 10-100 pc.                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Armus                         Lee                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HEARTS OF DARKNESS: PROBING THE MOST OBSCURED LIRGS IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE WITH NUSTAR                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO060mIG16                                       @`¶Ù!-w¿QAkπå~(@q√¡Ω•¿.¶‹]càf@g∫«À)@Ïßw␀°@Ï¬"Ë«æ60101053002@‰sJI∫^5@‰fˇS˜ŒŸ@Â≈x√…ÓÃ@Â≈ÿ√…ÓÃSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÕ¶ï␀␀·ÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀€A detailed study of low-redshift LIRGs is critical for our understanding of the cosmic evolution of galaxies and black holes, since LIRGs comprise the bulk of the cosmic far-infrared background and dominate the star-formation between 0.5 < z < 1. Among the 254 galaxies that are part of the Great Observatories All-Sky LIRG Survey (GOALS), there are a dozen highly obscured galaxies those with extremely deep 9.7-micron silicate features, implying A_V > 30-80 mags. We propose to observe 4 of these sources with NuSTAR to search for buried AGN, measure the hard X-ray spectrum, and characterize the the nature of the obscuring circum-nuclear, interstellar medium on scales of 10-100 pc.                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Armus                         Lee                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HEARTS OF DARKNESS: PROBING THE MOST OBSCURED LIRGS IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE WITH NUSTAR                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_0446p11                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬"Ë«æ@Ï¬Í9Ö60101078001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÕyÓçÿ␀␀·ÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses,are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 3 most luminous MeV blazars detected by Fermi that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift and GROND to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 2 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_0446p11                                       @R[¿6„@&Øêñªò»@gkb∂Æ}¿4Â‹∆?@l␀—∑Y@Ï¬Í9Ö@Ï—îØ‰060101078002@‘6µ(@‘	:Ω<6@’ÜÁ]ÃcÒ@’Üg]ÃcÒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÕ6;ÏG␀␀·ÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses,are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 3 most luminous MeV blazars detected by Fermi that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift and GROND to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 2 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              GROND                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC2992                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï—îØ‰0@Ï“[ÃU˜60160371001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÕ§˙P␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC2992                                           @bNy=Ÿc¿,¢h	‘ï@o0–Â`Aâ@<πÖoiD@fZkP∞Ú|@Ï“[ÃU˜@Ï‡[ÃU˜60160371002@‘Owg†˘	@‘FmñRΩ<@’∏õHÀí@’∏õHÀíSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÃ∑èΩ@␀␀‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_0451m28                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï‡[ÃU˜@Ï·w␀°60101080001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÃêW;␀␀·ÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses,are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 3 most luminous MeV blazars detected by Fermi that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift and GROND to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 2 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_0451m28                                       @RVÌ(åÁ¿<$`™d¬¯@l°ä	ﬁ¿BèÏV’œ´@mÈéÛMj@Ï·w␀°@ÏÔîØ‰060101080002@”'˛\ë—@”R¡Ω•@‘