alaxies. A very important method of assessing the energetics of a particular source involves combining radio and X-ray observations to measure magnetic field strengths in the lobes using synchrotron and inverse-Compton observations. With soft X-ray observations this method is model-dependent because the electrons responsible for the inverse-Compton emission emit synchrotron radiation below the observable radio band. NuSTAR offers us the chance to subject the inverse-Compton model to a critical test and make the first model-independent measurement of field strength and field variation. Our target is the brightest inverse-Compton source in the sky, Pictor A.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Croston                       Judith                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PHYSICAL CONDITIONS IN THE LOBES OF A POWERFUL RADIO GALAXY                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Pictor_A                                          @Sˇì›óˆ+¿FÂqué"@osÌ˙C˛]¿AWÀí:)«@mI‡ﬁ“àŒ@ÏÔÍ9Ö@ÏC[ÃU˜60101047002@˙π±ƒ2 @˙™aa‰˜f@¸ëòì‡ﬂ@¸íàì‡ﬂSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÀÄë¢¥␀␀·ÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀òThe energetic impact of radio-loud AGN is widely accepted to have crucial effects on the growth of the most massive galaxies. A very important method of assessing the energetics of a particular source involves combining radio and X-ray observations to measure magnetic field strengths in the lobes using synchrotron and inverse-Compton observations. With soft X-ray observations this method is model-dependent because the electrons responsible for the inverse-Compton emission emit synchrotron radiation below the observable radio band. NuSTAR offers us the chance to subject the inverse-Compton model to a critical test and make the first model-independent measurement of field strength and field variation. Our target is the brightest inverse-Compton source in the sky, Pictor A.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Croston                       Judith                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       PHYSICAL CONDITIONS IN THE LOBES OF A POWERFUL RADIO GALAXY                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS0637m752                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏC[ÃU˜@ÏCÕìrh60160272001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÀB.E␀␀‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                       