=q␀␀·ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*"The nearby type 1.8 Seyfert galaxy IRAS 13197-1627 shows evidence for some of the strongest accretion disk reflection observed among local, Compton-thin AGN. This indicates an extreme accretion geometry in which the intrinsic continuum emission experiences strong gravitational light bending, resulting in an X-ray spectrum dominated by reflection from the inner accretion disk. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM and NuSTAR. This will enable us to robustly confirm the extreme reflection, and utilize the full potential of the latest models to measure the black hole spin and constrain the coronal geometry, self-consistently accounting for both the relative strength and the profile of the reflected emission.                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Extreme Hard Excess and Black Hole Spin of IRAS 13197-1627                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS13197m1627                                    @i©*0U¿0∏y=Ÿc@sîÈ·∞â†@Fœ¸π#¢ú@fú‡uˆ˝@Ïâ±!´L@Ï¡±!´L60101020002@Û*ªò«„@ÛÈU2a|@ÙÄÖ,<ûÌ@ÙÅï,<ûÌSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ≤ñH,␀␀·ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*"The nearby type 1.8 Seyfert galaxy IRAS 13197-1627 shows evidence for some of the strongest accretion disk reflection observed among local, Compton-thin AGN. This indicates an extreme accretion geometry in which the intrinsic continuum emission experiences strong gravitational light bending, resulting in an X-ray spectrum dominated by reflection from the inner accretion disk. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM and NuSTAR. This will enable us to robustly confirm the extreme reflection, and utilize the full potential of the latest models to measure the black hole spin and constrain the coronal geometry, self-consistently accounting for both the relative strength and the profile of the reflected emission.                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Extreme Hard Excess and Black Hole Spin of IRAS 13197-1627                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC4941                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¡±!´L@Ï¬w␀°60061236001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ≤b"""␀␀‡GHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in t