 mass X-ray binaries showing sporadic outbursts lasting only a few days, where most of the X-ray luminosity is concentrated in short (a few hours) X-ray flares reaching 1E36-1E37 erg/s. The driving mechanism is still highly debated, and involves models predicting magnetar-like neutron star magnetic fields (1E14 G) versus more typical values (1E12 G). IGRJ11215-5952 is the only SFXT with predictable outbursts, being periodic with a 165 day orbital period. This allows us to propose a XMM and NuSTAR joint observation (20 ks) to search for a cyclotron line and directly determine the NS magnetic field. This will enable to disentangle among the currently available models.                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sidoli                        Lara                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       IGR J 11215-5952: searching for cyclotron lines in a SFXT pulsar                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ11215m5952                                    @ePeîØO¿MÓêˇó$t@r<˜ŒŸá?Ú,<ûÏø±@kπVì@ÏÍ9Ö@Ï&±!´L30101008002@”÷_ßÜ¬'@”ŒÉ"–Â`@’[x‘˝Ù@’\Ax‘˝ÙSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ¢{‘Ñ␀␀·—Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*!Supergiant Fast X-ray Transient are a sub-class of high mass X-ray binaries showing sporadic outbursts lasting only a few days, where most of the X-ray luminosity is concentrated in short (a few hours) X-ray flares reaching 1E36-1E37 erg/s. The driving mechanism is still highly debated, and involves models predicting magnetar-like neutron star magnetic fields (1E14 G) versus more typical values (1E12 G). IGRJ11215-5952 is the only SFXT with predictable outbursts, being periodic with a 165 day orbital period. This allows us to propose a XMM and NuSTAR joint observation (20 ks) to search for a cyclotron line and directly determine the NS magnetic field. This will enable to disentangle among the currently available models.                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sidoli                        Lara                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       IGR J 11215-5952: searching for cyclotron lines in a SFXT pulsar                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Ton_396                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï&±!´L@Ï'Í9Ö90101021001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ¢SWõ‡␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                          