tardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_02_12_017                                     @PØ$tSéÛ¿(%áì›óˆ@iÍzêñº¿B‘"h	‘ï@téÿD–©@Ï+"Ë«æ@Ï:w␀°60101015002@”␀ÀÀí:*@“ˆÊ0U2a@‘N&lÙ!@‘PÊlÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁìFrâK␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       XMM and NuSTAR Observations of a New Population of Heavily Obscured AGN                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SN1006_NE_Limb                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï:w␀°@Ï;w␀°40110001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁìoÄí␀␀·‰Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛The presence of nonthermal synchrotron from radio to ~100keV in young shell-type SNRs is a strong evidence of CR acceleration by SNR blast wave. The existing radio/X-ray data are not sensitive to the hard X-ray tails of synchrotron spectrum, which is affected by many processes limiting the diffusive shock acceleration. NuSTAR's broad energy coverage and high angular resolution make it the best to study this hard X-ray tail via spatially resolved spectroscopy. We propose NuSTAR observations of the two nonthermal limbs of SN1006, a nearby clean CR accelerator, in order to perform spatially resolved spectral analysis combining radio, X-ray, GeV, and TeV data. We will distinguish different models limiting CR acceleration, search for high energy break and evidence of shock-cloud interaction.   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Li                            Jiangtao                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM A NEARBY COSMIC RAY ACCELERATOR                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN1006_NE_Limb                                    @l<†ê-‡¿D‰„º”ZÜ@tzæﬂ§@@-åV’œ™Œ@fÄ¢3úø@Ï;w␀°@Ï¬?Zé⁄40110001002A9ÿ åMA*˙^“àŒA	ÈòŒŸáA	ÎŒŸáSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁí6T2␀␀·‰Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˛The presence of nonthermal synchrotron from radio to ~100keV in young shell-type SNRs is a strong evidence of CR acceleration by SNR blast wave. The existing radio/X-ray data are not sensitive to the hard X-ray tails of synchrotron spectrum, which is affected by many processes limiting the diffusive shock acceleration. NuSTAR's broad energy coverage and high angular resolution make it the best to study this hard X-ray tail via spatially resolved spectroscopy. We propose NuSTAR observations of the two nonthermal limbs of SN1006, a nearby clean CR accelerator, in order to perform spatially resolved spectral analysis combining radio, X-ray, GeV, and TeV data. We will distinguish different models limiting CR acceleration, search for high energy break and evidence of shock-cloud interaction.   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Li                            Jiangtao                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM A NEARBY COSMIC RAY ACCELERATOR                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGRJ17091m3624                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬?Zé⁄@Ï¬±!´L80001041001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁëÊï␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"úThe combination of the fast EPIC-pn modes and broad-band coverage with RXTE proved to be an effective means of exploring accretion in bright Galactic black holes. XMM and NuSTAR provide an even more powerful combination. We propose to make one joint 40 ks observation of a new or known black hole transient during a bright, hard outburst phase. The goals of this program are to 1) measure the spin of the black hole via the Fe K-alpha line and broad-band disk reflection spectrum, 2) test an apparent anti-correlation between winds and jets in soft and hard states by making a very sensitive search for winds in a hard, intermediate, or very high state. This observation will be supported by a network of ground-based observatories and X-ray monitoring.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       The hard state of a transient black hole: is the accretion disk truncated?                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ17091m3624                                    @pöu%¿B2:)«yß@u◊º£n@Ov_Ÿ@c‚!-w2@Ï¬±!´L@Ï‚?Zé⁄80001041002@Â#-nóç@ÂÆ®¡T @Á±òÕûÉ‰@Á¥XÕûÉ‰SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁëh›|«␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"úThe combination of the fast EPIC-pn modes and broad-band coverage with RXTE proved to be an effective means of exploring accretion in bright Galactic black holes. XMM and NuSTAR provide an even more powerful combination. We propose to make one joint 40 ks observation of a new or known black hole transient during a bright, hard outburst phase. The goals of this program are to 1) measure the spin of the black hole via the Fe K-alpha line and broad-band disk reflection spectrum, 2) test an apparent anti-correlation between winds and jets in soft and hard states by making a very sensitive search for winds in a hard, intermediate, or very high state. This observation will be supported by a network of ground-based observatories and X-ray monitoring.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       The hard state of a transient black hole: is the accretion disk truncated?                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀ToO                         