of NuSTAR supported by simultaneous observations by the Swift and Fermi-LAT to search for the most powerful BL Lac objects. NuSTAR observations of a carefully chosen sample of high-peaked BL Lacs at 3-79keV will provide strong constraints on the high-energy portion of the electron distribution in the primary jet emitting region. Since these are the same electrons responsible for the very high energy (VHE, E>100 GeV) gamma-ray emission, it will allow testable predictions of the VHE emission. This will identify the best sources to observe with atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes for the goal of constraining the extragalactic background light (EBL) and the intergalactic magnetic field (IGMF).                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Becerra Gonzalez              Josefa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       SEARCHING FOR COSMOLOGICAL PROBES WITH NUSTAR                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PMN_J1936m4719                                    @rd3úæ‡¿G•<64@uÚØ∑È¿:÷ç∏ª@a!␀h€ã¨@Ï∑îØ‰0@Ï≈Õìrh60101057002@‘g⁄Ú‰è@‘XJ˝!ˇ.@’≥)®Xy>@’≤Ÿ®Xy>SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁàÀHpC␀␀·ıHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‚We wish to exploit the unique capabilities of NuSTAR supported by simultaneous observations by the Swift and Fermi-LAT to search for the most powerful BL Lac objects. NuSTAR observations of a carefully chosen sample of high-peaked BL Lacs at 3-79keV will provide strong constraints on the high-energy portion of the electron distribution in the primary jet emitting region. Since these are the same electrons responsible for the very high energy (VHE, E>100 GeV) gamma-ray emission, it will allow testable predictions of the VHE emission. This will identify the best sources to observe with atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes for the goal of constraining the extragalactic background light (EBL) and the intergalactic magnetic field (IGMF).                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Becerra Gonzalez              Josefa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       SEARCHING FOR COSMOLOGICAL PROBES WITH NUSTAR                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Abell_2163                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï≈Õìrh@Ï∆x>70101002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁà£◊
=␀␀·ıHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Since the first non-thermal detections of inverse Compton (IC) emission in galaxy clusters at hard X-ray energies, we have yet to unambiguously confirm IC in follow-up observations. Claims of large IC fluxes from the 10' extent of Abell 2163, a massive merging cluster at z=0.2, give it the best chance of confirming a previous IC detection with NuSTAR. Additionally, recently available deep XMM data indicate extreme temperature variations (10-20 keV), the hottest of which are likely due to shocks. However, the XMM spectra suffer from variable Galactic absorption across the cluster, which can be avoided with NuSTAR's harder energy band. We will definitively confirm or refute the previously estimated IC flux and constrain the high kT makeup of this dynamic cluster.                             Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       UNAMBIGUOUSLY DETECTING NON-THERMAL AND SHOCKED GAS IN ABELL 2163                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Abell_2163                                        @n~¨1'¿¢M“Ò©¸@ëÎÖ∏R@>ß»K]Ãd@cU2a|@Ï∆x>@Ï%?Zé⁄70101002002@¸{~˘€#@¸gfﬁ“àŒ@˛ûhÿD–@˛°jÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁáÔÄë£␀␀·ıHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Since the first non-thermal detections of inverse Compton (IC) emission in galaxy clusters at hard X-ray energies, we have yet to unambiguously confirm IC in follow-up observations. Claims of large IC fluxes from the 10' extent of Abell 2163, a massive merging cluster at z=0.2, give it the best chance of confirming a previous IC detection with NuSTAR. Additionally, recently available deep XMM data indicate extreme temperature variations (10-20 keV), the hottest of which are likely due to shocks. However, the XMM spectra suffer from variable Galactic absorption across the cluster, which can be avoided with NuSTAR's harder energy band. We will definitively confirm or refute the previously estimated IC flux and constrain the high kT makeup of this dynamic cluster.                             Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       UNAMBIGUOUSLY DETECTING NON-THERMAL AND SHOCKED GAS IN ABELL 2163                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SGR_0755m2933                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï%?Zé⁄@Ï&[ÃU˜80102103001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁá≠„ÔP␀␀·ÒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    	GMagnetars are highly magnetized young neutron stars that are powered by the decay of their enormous magnetic fields. Magnetars exhibit dramatic variability including major outbursts -- a hallmark of their emission. These outbursts allow us to probe the extreme physics at work in the Universe's largest known magnetic fields. Understanding the evolution of these outbursts will place stringent constraints on physical models of magnetars, including their crusts, cores, atmospheres, coronae, and magnetospheres. In this proposal, we request Chandra and NuSTAR Target-of-Opportunity observations of a major magnetar outburst in AO17 in order to put detailed physical models to the test, and as an anchor for multiwavelength follow-up.                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaspi                         Victoria M                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C17       Target-of-Oppurtunity Chandra Observations of Magnetars in Outburst                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_0755m2933                                     @]π∑X‚¿=èUõ=»@n√;dZ¨øÁQÇ©ì·@tP†'RTa@Ï&[ÃU˜@ÏNÍ9Ö80102103002@Íπı@N§©@Í©®`™d√@Ïöì˛\ë—@Ïù≥˛\ë—SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁáK0*z␀␀·ÒHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  	GMagnetars are highly magnetized young neutron stars that are powered by the decay of their enormous magnetic fields. Magnetars exhibit dramatic variability including major outbursts -- a hallmark of their emission. These outbursts allow us to probe the extreme physics at work in the Universe's largest known magnetic fields. Understanding the evolution of these outbursts will place stringent constraints on physical models of magnetars, including their crusts, cores, atmospheres, coronae, and magnetospheres. In this proposal, we request Chandra and NuSTAR Target-of-Opportunity observations of a major magnetar outburst in AO17 in order to put detailed physical models to the test, and as an anchor for multiwavelength follow-up.                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaspi                         Victoria M                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C17       Target-of-Oppurtunity Chandra Observations of Magnetars in Outburst                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀Source located on DET0/DET3 gap for both FPM                                                                            archived  20200429G54d1p0d3                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏNÍ9Ö@ÏP?Zé⁄40101006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁáf«~␀␀·˝Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*
A pulsar wind nebula (PWN) inside a supernova remnant (SNR) is an important laboratory for studying neutron star formation and high energy particle acceleration. This is often accomplished by fitting the observed properties of a PWN inside a SNR with a perhaps oversimplified evolutionary model for such source. Applying such a model to PWN G54.1+0.3 suggests a ~20 Solar Mass progenitor which exploded inside a low density medium and that leptons are accelerated to >1 PeV. In this proposal, we request the XMM and NuStar observations needed to not only test the SNR and hard X-ray properties predicted by our model, but determine its distance and where inside the PWN the highest energy leptons are produced.                                                                                          Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Gelfand                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Supernova Remnant and Hard X-ray Emission of PWN G54.1+0.3                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316G54d1p0d3                                         @rJYJÙÿ@2·$›/@K␀_ˆîF?ÿuˆ˝!ˇ.@cåØOÑM@ÏP?Zé⁄@Ïxx>40101006002@Í~‡ﬁ”@ÍmƒT`™e@Ïb'7K∆®@Ïcá7K∆®SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÜµ™Iî␀␀·˝Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*
A pulsar wind nebula (PWN) inside a supernova remnant (SNR) is an important laboratory for studying neutron star formation and high energy particle acceleration. This is often accomplished by fitting the observed properties of a PWN inside a SNR with a perhaps oversimplified evolutionary model for such source. Applying such a model to PWN G54.1+0.3 suggests a ~20 Solar Mass progenitor which exploded inside a low density medium and that leptons are accelerated to >1 PeV. In this proposal, we request the XMM and NuStar observations needed to not only test the SNR and hard X-ray properties predicted by our model, but determine its distance and where inside the PWN the highest energy leptons are produced.                                                                                          Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Gelfand                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The Supernova Remnant and Hard X-ray Emission of PWN G54.1+0.3                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ00341665m7905204                            @ ˇó$tT¿Sƒº£@s␀-V¿B¯6‚Î@O∫u%F
