                              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Abell_523                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïx>@Ï"Ë«æ70102001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÇµÛÌ␀␀·ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)KWe propose a joint XMM-NuSTAR observation of the cluster Abell 523. We recently found in this cluster a radio halo that is striking because it is in a low luminosity X-ray cluster, outside of the well established radio power-X-ray luminosity relation for radio halo clusters. This challenges the current scenario for radio halo formation. The joint XMM-NuSTAR observation will afford a detailed mapping of the surface brightness and temperature distribution of the thermal gas and will allow a detailed investigation of the Inverse Compton (IC) emission in one of the best targets to perform this search. In the case of a detection it will provide a measurement of the spatial distribution of the IC emission and a measurement of the magnetic field.                                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Gastaldello                   Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The giant radio halo in the low-luminosity X-ray cluster Abell 523           