 Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82_X2                                            @búìtºj@Qkã¨q≥@aÆ1&Èy@D3Ø∑È˘@2]B√…ÓÃ@ÏlÍ9Ö@ÏÅx>80202020006@›…H‘˝Û∂@›¥ êˇó$@ﬂœ±·∞â†@ﬂ“±·∞â†SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÅ≠RM␀␀·ZHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Abell_523                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÅx>@ÏÇ"Ë«æ70102001003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁÄÙ%Ì	␀␀·ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀)KWe propose a joint XMM-NuSTAR observation of the cluster Abell 523. We recently found in this cluster a radio halo that is striking because it is in a low luminosity X-ray cluster, outside of the well established radio power-X-ray luminosity relation for radio halo clusters. This challenges the current scenario for radio halo formation. The joint XMM-NuSTAR observation will afford a detailed mapping of the surface brightness and temperature distribution of the thermal gas and will allow a detailed investigation of the Inverse Compton (IC) emission in one of the best targets to perform this search. In the case of a detection it will provide a measurement of the spatial distribution of the IC emission and a measurement of the magnetic field.                                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Gastaldello                   Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       The giant radio halo in the low-luminosity X-ray cluster Abell 523                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Abell_523                                         @R∞ƒõ•„T@!ñ‚ÎC-@g·ã¨q≥¿4XeîØO@uIQÇ©ì@ÏÇ"Ë«æ@Ï–w␀°70102001004@˘!•T`™e@˘∏∫«@˙Ïπ8Ô4◊@˙Ô˘üUõ=SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁÄEò=␀␀·ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀)KWe propose a joint XMM-NuSTAR observation of the cluster Abell 523. We recently found in this cluster a radio halo that is striking because it is in a low luminosity X-ray cluster, outside of the well established radio power-X-ray luminosity relation for radio halo clusters. This challenges the current scenario for radio halo formation. The joint XMM-NuSTAR observation will afford a detailed mapping of the surface brightness and temperature distribution of the thermal gas and will allow a detailed investigation of the Inverse Compton (IC) emission in one of the best targets to perform this search. In the case of a detection it will provide a measurement of the spatial distribution of the IC emission and a measurement of the magnetic field.                                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Gastaldello                   Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                     