 in 2009, this magnetar exhibited a distinctive hard power-law in the ~10-100 keV band. Even at the present quiescent state (~0.4 mCrab), this source is still bright enough for NuSTAR to detect the hard X-rays. A good determination of its spectral index and intensity enables us to establish a broad-band spectral correlation with the pulsar poloidal field. We also search for evidence of free precession already suggested from the previous Suzaku observation. This will provide second evidence for a strong toroidal field inside magnetars.                                                                                                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Enoto                         Teruaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       QUIESCENT HARD X-RAYS OF THE FASTEST SPINING SGR/AXP 1E 1547.0-5408                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161E_1547d0m5408                                    @m∏€ã¨q¿K&ßÔù≤-@tro␀h€å?m}øHÀí@já+@Ï†?Zé⁄@Ïÿ[ÃU˜30101035002@Ù[R^û	@ÙQöÜYJı@ı∆˝!ˇ.@ı»˝!ˇ.SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁbfOD␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀rWe propose a 80 ks NuSTAR observation of the fast spinning (2.07 s) SGR/AXP 1E 1547.0-5408. During a transient outburst in 2009, this magnetar exhibited a distinctive hard power-law in the ~10-100 keV band. Even at the present quiescent state (~0.4 mCrab), this source is still bright enough for NuSTAR to detect the hard X-rays. A good determination of its spectral index and intensity enables us to establish a broad-band spectral correlation with the pulsar poloidal field. We also search for evidence of free precession already suggested from the previous Suzaku observation. This will provide second evidence for a strong toroidal field inside magnetars.                                                                                                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Enoto                         Teruaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       QUIESCENT HARD X-RAYS OF THE FASTEST SPINING SGR/AXP 1E 1547.0-5408                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M82_X2                                            @búOv_ÿÆ@QjÇ@∑Ä4@aÆá¸π#£@D5!ˇ.HÈ@Å£n.±ƒ@Ïÿ[ÃU˜@ÏŸ±!´L80202020007@mÏ_ˆîF@mË˘	kπå@o fffff@o fffffSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁb+Tì'␀␀·ZHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          