                                                archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïºw␀°@Ïºx>30102037003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ\,/5␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀âWe request two 20 ks observations of the well-known microquasar GRS 1915+105. GRS 1915+105 is extremely variable, and new exposures are guaranteed to expand upon the early-phase observation in the "low/hard" or"plateau" state. Additional observations can help to test our disk reflection-based spin constraint, as well as prior spin constraints in "high/soft" states based on the disk continuum. Recent work suggests that the continuum in the high/soft" state may be strongly affected by wind absorption; the improved resolution and sensitivity of NuSTAR over RXTE will be essential to making a robust test of the spectrum. Last, additional exposures can potentially detect Lense-Thirring precession through phase-resolved spectroscopy of the QPOs.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       A NU SPIN ON GRS 1915+105 WITH NUSTAR                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rÁØ∑È@%Ú{≤˛≈@F£◊
=p§ø∞U2a|⁄@`◊œ™ÕûÑ@Ïºx>@ÏÕ"Ë«æ30102037004@ΩU~Ov_Ÿ@æ⁄Óö@÷`sF‹]d@÷csF‹]dSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁZîJU∂␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀âWe request two 20 ks observations of the well-known microquasar GRS 1915+105. GRS 1915+105 is extremely variable, and new exposures are guaranteed to expand upon the early-phase observation in the "low/hard" or"plateau" state. Additional observations can help to test our disk reflection-based spin constraint, as well as prior spin constraints in "high/soft" states based on the disk continuum. Recent work suggests that the continuum in the high/soft" state may be strongly affected by wind absorption; the improved resolution and sensitivity of NuSTAR over RXTE will be essential to making a robust test of the spectrum. Last, additional exposures can potentially detect Lense-Thirring precession through phase-resolved spectroscopy of the QPOs.                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       A NU SPIN ON GRS 1915+105 WITH NUSTAR                                                                                   ␀␀d␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀(2/2) / data gap 1892s                                                                                                  archived  20200429PSR_J1813m1246                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÕ"Ë«æ@ÏÕîØ‰030101044001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁV©VŸ≤␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‘PSR J1813-1246 is a 48-ms rotation powered pulsar found by Fermi in gamma-rays and recently found to be pulsing in X-rays below 10 keV, using XMM. The X-ray pulses are advanced 90 degrees in phase relative to the gamma-rays, and the X-ray spectrum appears to be extremely hard. This phase offset is modeled by invoking a substantial physical separation of the X-ray and gamma-ray emitting regions. Further understanding and model refinement requires observations of the spectrum and light curve at energies above 10 keV. NuSTAR is uniquely qualifited to perform this observation, having the requisite sensitivity, energy coverage and time resolution. We propose a 50 ks observation, designed to address the puzzling nature of the source.                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Wood                          Kent                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE PUZZLING ROTATION-POWERED PULSAR PSR J1813-1246                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J1813m1246                                    @q ¿Éo¿)z‘,<ûÌ@1*u%F@ˆ+jÁ’@b}øHÃ@ÏÕîØ‰0@Ï¯?Zé⁄30101044002@ÎD$`™d√@Î0@E° ¡@ÌC¡≈÷8Ü@ÌFa≈÷8ÜSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁVﬁá∆Z␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‘PSR J1813-1246 is a 48-ms rotation powered pulsar found by Fermi in gamma-rays and recently found to be pulsing in X-rays below 10 keV, using XMM. The X-ray pulses are advanced 90 degrees in phase relative to the gamma-rays, and the X-ray spectrum appears to be extremely hard. This phase offset is modeled by invoking a substantial physical separation of the X-ray and gamma-ray emitting regions. Further understanding and model refinement requires observations of the spectrum and light curve at energies above 10 keV. NuSTAR is uniquely qualifited to perform this observation, having the requisite sensitivity, energy coverage and time resolution. We propose a 50 ks observation, designed to address the puzzling nature of the source.                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Wood                          Kent                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N01       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE PUZZLING ROTATION-POWERED PULSAR PSR J1813-1246                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SN2014C                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¯?Zé⁄@Ï˘w␀°40202013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁW<M^p␀␀‚“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR program that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow-up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R> 7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Continued NuSTAR Monitoring Of The Unprecedented Metamorphosis Of Sn2014C                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u4‰éäqﬁ@A6À˚µt@Wdµ‹∆?¿4∏öu%F@cz{≤˛≈m@Ï˘w␀°@ÏÍ9Ö40202013002@‰˙∆‘˝Û∂@‰Ú6u%F@Ê{à«À)@Ê~»«À)SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁWa_Û›␀␀‚“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR program that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow-up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R> 7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti     