_2785                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï&[ÃU˜@Ï&Í9Ö60201053001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁX¥%Ì␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∑We propose a 70 ks NuSTAR observation of NGC 2785, a Type 2 AGN hosted in a star-forming galaxy at z=0.009. The source was marginally detected (~3.5 sigma) at hard X-ray energies by a careful analysis of the 54-month Swift/BAT data. The intrinsic X-ray luminosity, derived from both the [OIII] emission line and the mid-IR AGN emission, is much higher than that measured from the 10 ks Swift/XRT pointed observation. The most likely explanation for these results and for the high BAT/XRT flux ratio is that the source is a heavily Compton-thick AGN. NuSTAR represents the most efficient way to shed definite light on this source.                                                                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vignali                       Cristian                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Ngc 2785, A Heavily Compton-Thick AGN Candidate In A Star-Forming Galaxy In The Backyard                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_2785                                          @aX¸PH@DtI∫^5?@fûµ‹∆?@EÿÉnóç@uËóçOﬂ;@Ï&Í9Ö@ÏYw␀°60201053002@ÒÆÕE° ¡@Ò¢6‚Î@Úﬁ‘ìu@ÚﬂÙìuSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁXEò=␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∑We propose a 70 ks NuSTAR observation of NGC 2785, a Type 2 AGN hosted in a star-forming galaxy at z=0.009. The source was marginally detected (~3.5 sigma) at hard X-ray energies by a careful analysis of the 54-month Swift/BAT data. The intrinsic X-ray luminosity, derived from both the [OIII] emission line and the mid-IR AGN emission, is much higher than that measured from the 10 ks Swift/XRT pointed observation. The most likely explanation for these results and for the high BAT/XRT flux ratio is that the source is a heavily Compton-thick AGN. NuSTAR represents the most efficient way to shed definite light on this source.                                                                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vignali                       Cristian                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Ngc 2785, A Heavily Compton-Thick AGN Candidate In A Star-Forming Galaxy In The Backyard                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SWIFT_J0357d5m6255                                @MÑÁØ∑È¿Okí:)«z@q@Ÿá+¿E≤nóçOﬂ@HŸ|⁄Q@ÏYw␀°@ÏZw␀°60201034001@1Óp:˚~ë@1ÊRΩ<6@3␀␀␀␀␀␀@3␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁX≠¸5␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429SWIFT_J0357d5m6255                                @MÑ9XbN¿Olm≈÷8Ü@qA␀—∑Y¿E≤M“Ò©¸@Hˆ¬&ÄùI@ÏZw␀°@Ïf[ÃU˜60201034002@ŸAGÆ{@Ÿ€,•zx@€∫ë?|Ìë@€∫ë?|ÌëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁXÿvT2␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Crab                                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïf[ÃU˜@Ïg?Zé⁄10202001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁYÈ>î␀␀‚ìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration 