                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PSR_J1023p0038                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏsÕìrh@Ïtx>30201005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁZSÀ␀␀‚+Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2ÜPSR J1023+0038 is a binary system that hosts a neutron star and a low-mass, main sequence companion star. It has been observed to switch on timescales of years between states as a radio millisecond pulsar and a LMXB. After its last switch to an X-ray state in 2013, the source has been the object of an extensive multi-wavelength monitoring, during which the switch between three flux X-ray modes (high, low and flare) on timescales of 10 s has been observed. Here we propose a model to account for these switches and ask for the first strictly simultaneous NuSTAR-EPIC-Swift-OM- VLT observation to probe our model using correlations and lags (a profitable tool to probe the emission mechanism), as well as establish differences among the power spectra of the multi-band light curves.                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Campana                       Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       PSR J1023+0038: a tool to investigate the accretion and ejection in LMXBs                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J1023p0038                                    @c},•zxl?‰!ñRΩ<6@nf<ûÏø±@F—≥–|Ñ∂@vÊö‘,=@Ïtx>@Ï¥±!´L30201005002@ıOzº£n@ıGÚ{≤ˇ@ˆ·÷ØOÑ@ˆ‰ØOÑSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁZO+ùe␀␀‚+Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀2ÜPSR J1023+0038 is a binary system that hosts a neutron star and a low-mass, main sequence companion star. It has been observed to switch on timescales of years between states as a radio millisecond pulsar and a LMXB. After its last switch to an X-ray state in 2013, the source has been the object of an extensive multi-wavelength monitoring, during which the switch between three flux X-ray modes (high, low and flare) on timescales of 10 s has been observed. Here we propose a model to account for these switches and ask for the first strictly simultaneous NuSTAR-EPIC-Swift-OM- VLT observation to probe our model using correlations and lags (a profitable tool to probe the emission mechanism), as well as establish differences among the power spectra of the multi-band light curves.                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Campana                       Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       PSR J1023+0038: a tool to investigate the accretion and ejection in LMXBs                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM VLT                                                                                             ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_2004m447                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¥±!´L@ÏµÕìrh80201024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁZÈ>ìÈ␀␀·mHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀9X-ray emission in excess of the intrinsic power law below 2 keV is a typical feature of radio-quiet active galactic nuclei (AGN). It has now been observed in a few sources of the newly-discovered class of radio-loud and gamma-ray emitting narrow-line Seyfert 1 (gamma-NLS1) galaxies. But the nature of this component in radio-loud AGN is not clear. Very recently, we found tentative evidence for the presence of a variable soft excess in the gamma-NLS1 galaxy PKS 2004-447 (Kreikenbohm et al. 2015, A&A, in press). Here, we propose a target-of-opportunity observation to study this peculiar component in this key source for the class of gamma-NLS1 galaxies. The unique joint NuSTAR-XMM-Newton program allows for the first reliable detection of the soft excess in this source.                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kreikenbohm                   Annika                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Nature Of The Soft Excess In The Radio And Gamma-Ray Loud Nls1 Pks 2004-447                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_2004m447                                      @r‡qué"¿FGÀ)^û@v4`Aâ7L¿?¨∆?@cq≤ïÍ@ÏµÕìrh@Ï⁄"Ë«æ80201024002@ËÃ-‡@Áıºöu%@Èπvv»¥9@È∫v»¥9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ[ïmõ␀␀·mHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀9X-ray emission in excess of the intrinsic power law below 2 keV is a typical feature of radio-quiet active galactic nuclei (AGN). It has now been observed in a few sources of the newly-discovered class of radio-loud and gamma-ray emitting narrow-line Seyfert 1 (gamma-NLS1) galaxies. But the nature of this component in radio-loud AGN is not clear. Very recently, we found tentative evidence for the presence of a variable soft excess in the gamma-NLS1 galaxy PKS 2004-447 (Kreikenbohm et al. 2015, A&A, in press). Here, we propose a target-of-opportunity observation to study this peculiar component in this key source for the class of gamma-NLS1 galaxies. The unique joint NuSTAR-XMM-Newton program allows for the first reliable detection of the soft excess in this source.                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kreikenbohm                   Annika                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Nature Of The Soft Excess In The Radio And Gamma-Ray Loud Nls1 Pks 2004-447                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429PKS0402m362                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï⁄"Ë«æ@Ï⁄Í9Ö60160179001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ[kÊ5€␀␀‡±Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS0402m362                                       @Ny„º”¿B$xG@m∏µ‹∆?¿HP ƒõ•„@Hù∏∫«À@Ï⁄Í9Ö@ÏÁ±!´L60160179002@“i!∞â†'@“\ß’fœB@”∏£I∫@”π£I∫SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ[åúA<␀␀‡±Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_1386                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÁ±!´L@ÏÁÍ9Ö60201024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ[»π␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We request a total of 105 ks of NuSTAR observing time in order to increase the exposures of two heavily obscured AGN, NGC 2960 (80 ks) and NGC 1386 (25 ks). These galaxies are sources of water megamaser emission from which robust black hole mass measurements have been made and comparison of Gamma and Eddington ratio can be performed where these quantities have been shown to be strongly correlated in unobscured AGN. However, these sources have far steeper Gamma values than any Eddington ratio would predict. Our proposed observations will reveal if this is an artifact of the low count nature of the existing data, or whether these sources are new and interesting outliers.                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brightman                     Murray                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Can The Growth Rates Of Heavily Obscured Supermassive Black Holes Be Estimated From Their X-Ray Spectral Indices?       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1386                                          @KÁØ∑È¿A¸ø±[W?@m∑nóçP¿KL/É{J@LœÜ¬&Äù@ÏÁÍ9Ö@Ï˙Õìrh60201024002@ŸŒ¢ÛMj@ŸºÏı¬è\@€§îæﬂ§@€•æﬂ§SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ[Î$ñ␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We request a total of 105 ks of NuSTAR observing time in order to increase the exposures of two heavily obscured AGN, NGC 2960 (80 ks) and NGC 1386 (25 ks). These galaxies are sources of water megamaser emission from which robust black hole mass measurements have been made and comparison of Gamma and Eddington ratio can be performed where these quantities have been shown to be strongly correlated in unobscured AGN. However, these sources have far steeper Gamma values than any Eddington ratio would predict. Our proposed observations will reveal if this is an artifact of the low count nature of the existing data, or whether these sources are new and interesting outliers.                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brightman                     Murray                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Can The Growth Rates Of Heavily Obscured Supermassive Black Holes Be Estimated From Their X-Ray Spectral Indices?       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429LMC_X1                                            @U3AÚ◊s¿Qnh	‘ï@qÉ∏QÎÖ¿?ä˚~êˇó@>Äç∏∫«@Ï˙Õìrh@Ï˚?Zé⁄30201029001@sßÔù≤-@sOÑM@tÉ33333@tÉ33333SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ\,_í∆␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ªWe propose a 40 ks NuSTAR observation of the black hole binary LMC X-1, of two persistently emitting stellar mass black holes that are close enough to allow high quality spectroscopic analysis. We will search for the origin of the (~ks) variability in the non-thermal continuum and thermal disk emission in the "high/soft" state of the source. We have also identified a variable absorption feature associated with ionized plasma. Previous observations with other satellites could not observe this feature or have limited observations of the source. Crucially, we do not yet have coverage over the full orbit with high quality, broadband X-ray data that NuSTAR will provide can simultaneously constrain the emission from the accretion disk, the non-thermal continuum, and the disk reflection spectrum.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Grefenstette                  Brian                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Hunting For The Source Of Variability In The Stellar Mass Black Hole Lmc X-1                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429LMC_X1                                            @U5'ª0¿QoE8Ô4◊@qÉY≥–|Ö¿?à}øHÄ@>ÎjÁ’fœ@Ï˚?Zé⁄@Ïx>30201029002@„£ÿõ•„T@„†2¥9X@ÂUzæﬂ§@ÂXZæﬂ§SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ\TJU∂␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ªWe propose a 40 ks NuSTAR observation of the black hole binary LMC X-1, of two persistently emitting stellar mass black holes that are close enough to allow high quality spectroscopic analysis. We will search for the origin of the (~ks) variability in the non-thermal continuum and thermal disk emission in the "high/soft" state of the source. We have also identified a variable absorption feature associated with ionized plasma. Previous observations with other satellites could not observe this feature or have limited observations of the source. Crucially, we do not yet have coverage over the full orbit with high quality, broadband X-ray data that NuSTAR will provide can simultaneously constrain the emission from the accretion disk, the non-thermal continuum, and the disk reflection spectrum.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Grefenstette                  Brian                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Hunting For The Source Of Variability In The Stellar Mass Black Hole Lmc X-1                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SWIFT_J2304d9p1220                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïx>@Ï[ÃU˜60201037001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ\¢É9E␀␀‚%Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SWIFT_J2304d9p1220                                @u§X‚e@(¥ÍJå@UÅ©˚Álã¿Eeóˆ+jË@a{„S˜ŒŸ@Ï[ÃU˜@Ï"Ë«æ60201037002@“{ä	ﬁ@—¯›≈÷8Ü@”1!®Xy>@”1!®Xy>SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ\¬ÿ-É␀␀‚%Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO509mG038                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï"Ë«æ@Ïx>60260010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ\¯!_Ù␀␀‡∏Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO509mG038                                       @iYUõ=»¿9o∑È˘r@sû=»K^@BXAâ7K«@tSqﬁi≠C@Ïx>@Ï/[ÃU˜60260010002@‘5∫^5?@‘Â@N•@’´·†'RT@’∞a†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ]ÅS—␀␀‡∏Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_7314                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï/[ÃU˜@Ï0x>60201031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ]RˆÑæ␀␀‚%Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀PNuSTAR s unprecedented energy coverage has been providing many black hole spin measurements in AGN. Most of them are at the high end of black hole spin, where strong relativistic effects are present. In NGC 7314, the iron line shape from spectroscopy and reverberation is narrow, suggesting a low black hole spin and possibly either a truncated disk or an illuminating source that is distant from the disk. The current low energy data (<  10 keV) do not constrain the reflection spectrum to specify this. Here, we propose to observe x for 100 ks with NuSTAR and 60 ks with XMM in order to measure both the relativistic broadening and reverberation. The two will allow us to localize the X-ray source and test for truncations. A truncated disk would have implications on accretion theory.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zoghbi                        Abderahmen                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Geometry Of The X-Ray Source In Ngc 7314 With Spectroscopy And Reverberation                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7314                                          @u/;dZ¨¿:»¥9X@:Ùµ‹∆?¿M»l"h	’@`:˛≈m\˚@Ï0x>@Ïy"Ë«æ60201031002@¯ÑdC,•@¯oWxF‹@˙DÙ”ZÖà@˙DD”ZÖàSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ]ÜZCÇ␀␀‚%Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀PNuSTAR s unprecedented energy coverage has been providing many black hole spin measurements in AGN. Most of them are at the high end of black hole spin, where strong relativistic effects are present. In NGC 7314, the iron line shape from spectroscopy and reverberation is narrow, suggesting a low black hole spin and possibly either a truncated disk or an illuminating source that is distant from the disk. The current low energy data (<  10 keV) do not constrain the reflection spectrum to specify this. Here, we propose to observe x for 100 ks with NuSTAR and 60 ks with XMM in order to measure both the relativistic broadening and reverberation. The two will allow us to localize the X-ray source and test for truncations. A truncated disk would have implications on accretion theory.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zoghbi                        Abderahmen                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Geometry Of The X-Ray Source In Ngc 7314 With Spectroscopy And Reverberation                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C445                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿