 three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SS_Cyg                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïàw␀°@ÏàîØ‰080202036001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ^bÃ
ü␀␀‚UHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀⁄We propose for a joint observation of SS~Cyg in its optical quiescence and outburst states with NuSTAR and XMM with the aim of having first robust detection of reflection component, commonly observed in binaries and AGNs. Presence of such component have been inferred via detection of Fe 6.4 keV line, however no direct detection till date. We will fit the phase averaged spectra to derive reflection amplitude. These measurements will help verify theoretical ideas like presence of central hole in disk, main contributing site for the reflection during the two states and also to study response of the system to varying accretion conditions.                                                                                                                                                              Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rana                          Vikram                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Probing Reflection In A Dwarf Nova Ss Cygni Using Joint NuSTAR And XMM Data                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SS_Cyg                                            @tZÎC,•@EÃ≤ïÈ·±@Võ’fœAÚ¿TØOÑM@a§}øHÄ@ÏàîØ‰0@Ï¨Õìrh80202036002@ËO]~êˇó@ËGÑÓcä@Í“àŒp@Íò“àŒpSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ^ãHpC␀␀‚UHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀⁄We propose for a joint observation of SS~Cyg in its optical quiescence and outburst states with NuSTAR and XMM with the aim of having first robust detection of reflection component, commonly observed in binaries and AGNs. Presence of such component have been inferred via detection of Fe 6.4 keV line, however no direct detection till date. We will fit the phase averaged spectra to derive reflection amplitude. These measurements will help verify theoretical ideas like presence of central hole in disk, main contributing site for the reflection during the two states and also to study response of the system to varying accretion conditions.                                                                                                                                                              Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rana                          Vikram                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Probing Reflection In A Dwarf Nova Ss Cygni Using Joint NuSTAR And XMM Data                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004294U2206p54                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¨Õìrh@Ï≠"Ë«æ30201015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁ^‰€ó␀␀‚+Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀4We propose to obtain, for the first time, a continuous, well exposed spectrum of the accreting magnetar candidate 4U2206+54, beyond 20 keV. This will allow to detect and characterise unam- biguously a previously claimed cyclotron line at 29 keV or disprove its existence definitely. Both outcomes are exciting. The first one would rule out the system as a magnetar. The very pronounced NS spin decay will need then a different explanation. This will certainly challenge current theories as only magnetobraking is thought to be effective enough. The second one, in turn, will support the magnetar scenario, helping to establish and characterize the newly proposed class of wind accreting magnetars.                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Torrejon                      Jose                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       4U2206+54: Is It Really An Accreting Magnetar?                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U2206p54                                         @t¿E8Ô4◊@KE¬è\(ˆ@Y!:í£SøÒ¿Énóç@bÅÒA [¿@Ï≠"Ë«æ@Ï’?Zé⁄30201015002@ÏùV√…ÓÃ@Ïòá+@Ó˚ëhr∞@Ó¸%ëhr∞SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁ_◊Ãl␀␀‚+Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀4We propose to obtain, for the first time, a continuous, well exposed spectrum of the accreting magnetar candidate 4U2206+54, beyond 20 keV. This will allow to detect and characterise unam- biguously a previously claimed cyclotron line at 29 keV or disprove its existence definitely. Both outcomes are exciting. The first one would rule out the system as a magnetar. The very pronounced NS spin decay will need then a different explanation. This will certainly challenge current theories as only magnetobraking is thought to be effective enough. The second one, in turn, will support the magnetar scenario, helping to establish and characterize the newly proposed class of wind accreting magnetars.                                          