◊@a≠ıY≥–}@D6äqﬁi≠@t£ËßÊõ@Ï⁄Í9Ö@Ï€îØ‰030202022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ%LSo‚␀␀‚ÜHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀äThe galaxy M82 harbors two of the most spectacular ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULXs), off-nuclear point sources exceeding the Eddington limit for a typical stellar remnant black hole. M82 X-1 reaches Lx> ~10^41 erg/s, and it's one of the best candidate intermediate mass black holes (IMBH). M82 X-2, reaching Lx> ~10^40 erg/s, was found to be powered by a pulsar, at 100 times the Eddington luminosity, by NuSTAR. This proposal complements an accepted Chandra program and aims at detecting these two sources in different spectral states. Thanks to the combined work of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will be able to disentangle the spectra of the two ULXs, unresolved in NuSTAR, but constrain the hard X-ray spectrum, invisible to Chandra, and investigate the nature of these two intriguing objects.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Timing And Spectral Studies Of The Ultraluminous X-Ray Sources In M82.                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82_ULXS                                          @bù+”√a@Qj£S&@aÆ<ûÏø±@D5Û∂E°À@tú◊s¸P@Ï€îØ‰0@ÏÒÕìrh30202022002@„©<64@„␀Õ,•zx@‰T:wökQ@‰T⁄wökQSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ$ˆ`∂␀␀‚ÜHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀äThe galaxy M82 harbors two of the most spectacular ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULXs), off-nuclear point sources exceeding the Eddington limit for a typical stellar remnant black hole. M82 X-1 reaches Lx> ~10^41 erg/s, and it's one of the best candidate intermediate mass black holes (IMBH). M82 X-2, reaching Lx> ~10^40 erg/s, was found to be powered by a pulsar, at 100 times the Eddington luminosity, by NuSTAR. This proposal complements an accepted Chandra program and aims at detecting these two sources in different spectral states. Thanks to the combined work of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will be able to disentangle the spectra of the two ULXs, unresolved in NuSTAR, but constrain the hard X-ray spectrum, invisible to Chandra, and investigate the nature of these two intriguing objects.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Timing And Spectral Studies Of The Ultraluminous X-Ray Sources In M82.                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ15120505m1046356                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÒÕìrh@ÏÚ±!´L60161610001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ$Õ-„Ô␀␀‡ÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ15120505m1046356                            @lÅ◊€Ùà¿%ñîFsÅÿ@u◊<ûÏø±@CöÕûÉ‰&@ƒµ‹∆?@ÏÚ±!´L@Ïw␀°60161610002@‘&Ω<6@‘|K]Ãd@’z$Œp:˚@’{§Œp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ$Å
ˇü␀␀‡ÕHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429EX_Hya                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïw␀°@ÏîØ‰030201016001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ$ZÌjí␀␀‚>Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀RWe propose to obtain the first hard X-ray imaging observation of the intermediate polar EX Hya, which will allow us to explore the high energy spectrum. Previous non-imaging observations did not allow to subtract the contamination of the close cluster of galaxies Abell 3528. The hard X-ray spectrum will be used to determine the shock temperature and the shock height, thus obtaining the white dwarf mass and reflection signatures from the magnetic accretion column. The proposed study has broad implications for our understanding of magnetic accretion and plasma cooling mechanisms.                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Orio                          Marina                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Structure Of The Magnetic Accretion Column In Intermediate Polars                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316EX_Hya                                            @h"uˆ˝!ˇ¿=EfœAÚ@rÓÓcä	@@◊˘rGE9@uh?º@ÏîØ‰0@Ï?Zé⁄30201016002@ÿI≈$›/@ÿ>Ëm≈÷9@⁄‘9Xb@⁄‘9XbSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ$
=q␀␀‚>Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀RWe propose to obtain the first hard X-ray imaging observation of the intermediate polar EX Hya, which will allow us to explore the high energy spectrum. Previous non-imaging observations did not allow to subtract the contamination of the close cluster of galaxies Abell 3528. The hard X-ray spectrum will be used to determine the shock temperature and the shock height, thus obtaining the white dwarf mass and reflection signatures from the magnetic accretion column. The proposed study has broad implications for our understanding of magnetic accretion and plasma cooling mechanisms.                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Orio                          Marina                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Structure Of The Magnetic Accretion Column In Intermediate Polars                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429J1155m0147                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï?Zé⁄@Ï±!´L60201055001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ#Ú“␀␀‚>Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÀLyman-alpha blobs (LABs) are luminous extended emission line regions at high redshift. Their ionization sources remain mostly mysterious, and obscured AGN are thought to be responsible in many cases. Recently, we have discovered LABs at redshifts z=0.3, existing 7 billion years later in the Universe than previously known. Our Chandra data show that the embedded AGN are very weak, and that they must have faded by 3-4 orders of magnitude in the last 10,000-100,000 years. Such transient AGN and episodic duty cycles naturally explain the apparent ionization deficits in LABs because of the delayed escape of the resonant Lyman-alpha photons. With short NUSTAR observations we will show that these AGN are indeed weak and not deeply obscured, solving a long-standing puzzle in galaxy evolution.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Schirmer                      Mischa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Probing The Faded AGN Of The Lowest Redshift Lyman-Alpha Blobs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316J1155m0147                                        @f]p£◊
=ø¸Ú‰éäqﬁ@q6{≤˛≈m@M/É{J#@vYHËßÁ@Ï±!´L@Ï#[ÃU˜60201055002@’Ÿbá+@’œ˛∂Æ}V@◊ãÓffff@◊çÆffffSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁ#ßN„␀␀‚>Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÀLyman-alpha blobs (LABs) are luminous extended emission line regions at high redshift. Their ionization sources remain mostly mysterious, and obscured AGN are thought to be responsible in many cases. Recently, we have discovered LABs at redshifts z=0.3, existing 7 billion years later in the Universe than previously known. Our Chandra data show that the embedded AGN are very weak, and that they must have faded by 3-4 orders of magnitude in the last 10,000-100,000 years. Such transient AGN and episodic duty cycles naturally explain the apparent ionization deficits in LABs because of the delayed escape of the resonant Lyman-alpha photons. With short NUSTAR observations we will show that these AGN are indeed weak and not deeply obscured, solving a long-standing puzzle in galaxy evolution.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Schirmer                      Mischa                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Probing The Faded AGN Of The Lowest Redshift Lyman-Alpha Blobs                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_349p2                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï#[ÃU˜@Ï$?Zé⁄30201026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ#klÿ␀␀‚>Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÆNeutron star low-mass X-ray binaries display significant spectral and timing variability, and get classified based their characteristic variability behavior. However, there is still not a clear picture as to what drives this variability. Timing properties suggest it may be due to changes in the radius of the inner accretion disk. Unfortunately, continuum modeling of neutron star LMXBs is especially degenerate, and thus hard to draw from. However, spectroscopy of the broad iron line and reflection spectrum provide a powerful tool to constrain the inner disk radius. Here, we propose an 80 ks observation of GX 349+2 to investigate spectral changes through the full set of spectral states. The broadband sensitivity make NuSTAR ideal for such a study.                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Cackett                       Edward                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       How Does The Accretion Disk Evolve With State In Gx 349+2?                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_349p2                                          @p^ûö¿B8$xG@uœ‘ï*ô@C…ÓÀ˚@n©*0U@Ï$?Zé⁄@Ï[±!´L30201026002@„î?Ë>B[@‰gˆïÈ·±@Ùè$D–©@ÙêîD–©SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁŸ˙∞º␀␀‚>Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÆNeutron star low-mass X-ray binaries display significant spectral and timing variability, and get classified based their characteristic variability behavior. However, there is still not a clear picture as to what drives this variability. Timing properties suggest it may be due to changes in the radius of the inner accretion disk. Unfortunately, continuum modeling of neutron star LMXBs is especially degenerate, and thus hard to draw from. However, spectroscopy of the broad iron line and reflection spectrum provide a powerful tool to constrain the inner disk radius. Here, we propose an 80 ks observation of GX 349+2 to investigate spectral changes through the full set of spectral states. The broadband sensitivity make NuSTAR ideal for such a study.                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Cackett                       Edward                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       How Does The Accretion Disk Evolve With State In Gx 349+2?                                                              ␀␀␀	␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO137_34                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[±!´L@Ï\w␀°60061272001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ¶«}â␀␀‡ÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                        