ar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429TON_S180                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏØÕìrh@Ï∞±!´L60201057001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁPg(ï␀␀‚AHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀—The origin of the hard X-ray excess in AGN is the subject of active debate. Recent studies have suggested the presence of a hard excess in a significant fraction of Type I AGN, possibly due to partial covering by Compton thick matter. This finding appears at odds with the standard AGN Unified Model, where such obscuration would only be predicted in type II AGN. Here we propose a joint 120 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observation of the luminous NLS1 galaxy TON S180, where a strong hard excess was reported in a previous Suzaku observation. With the proposed observations we will differentiate between a hard excess caused by a partially covering absorber, or by relativistically blurred reflection. We will also discriminate between competing scenarios of the energy dependent time lags.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Matzeu                        Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Solving The Origin Of The Hard X-Ray Excess In The Narrow Lined Seyfert 1,Ton S180                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TON_S180                                          @,¨ÃÃÃÃÕ¿6\cÒA \@a%càeîØ¿UKMjO@^ÿ…Öoi@Ï∞±!´L@Ï[ÃU˜60201057002@ˇû¡Ω•@˛˙ﬂŒ_A␀±6‚ÎA␀≤«6‚ÎSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁïŒ≤@␀␀‚AHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀—The origin of the hard X-ray excess in AGN is the subject of active debate. Recent studies have suggested the presence of a hard excess in a significant fraction of Type I AGN, possibly due to partial covering by Compton thick matter. This finding appears at odds with the standard AGN Unified Model, where such obscuration would only be predicted in type II AGN. Here we propose a joint 120 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observation of the luminous NLS1 galaxy TON S180, where a strong hard excess was reported in a previous Suzaku observation. With the proposed observations we will differentiate between a hard excess caused by a partially covering absorber, or by relativistically blurred reflection. We will also discriminate between competing scenarios of the energy dependent time lags.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Matzeu                        Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Solving The Origin Of The Hard X-Ray Excess In The Narrow Lined Seyfert 1,Ton S180                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429WR140                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[ÃU˜@Ï"Ë«æ30202002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏﬁTÑ]␀␀‚ﬂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2MThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable X-ray plasma. This nearly ``controlled'' collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016, a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and understand the X-ray Minimum.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Colliding Wind X-ray Emission from the Massive Binary WR140 around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR140                                             @s’fœAÚ@EÒΩ•ú‡@T7'ª/Ï@ˇ.HËß@Z9XbN@Ï"Ë«æ@Ï!îØ‰030202002002@“‹≠x‘˝Ù@“œ<©*0@‘®bM”@‘®bM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁø%å␀␀‚ﬂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀2MThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable X-ray plasma. This nearly ``controlled'' collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016, a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and understand the X-ray Minimum.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Colliding Wind X-ray Emission from the Massive Binary WR140 around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï!îØ‰0@Ï"w␀°30202033001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ√„-"␀␀‚hHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀«X-ray spectral/timing properties of BHXBs can provide information about black hole spin, strong gravity regime and accretion-ejection mechanism. But this information can be reliable only if broadband spectral components can be identified, systematics in relativistic line shape can be reduced and timing properties can be connected to spectral properties. Our proposed 80 ks of NuSTAR observations of BHXBs Cygnus X-1 and GRS 1915+105, and contemporaneous ASTROSAT observations using our guaranteed time, will for the first time achieve a spectral/timing characterization above 10 keV at a level similar to what could be achieved with joint observations with a CCD and RXTE/PCA below 10 keV. This proposal relies on the broadband sensitivity of NuSTAR and the uniquely large area of ASTROSAT/LAXPC.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bhattacharyya                 Sudip                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Broadband X-Ray Spectral And Timing Study Of Cygnus X-1 And Grs 1915+105                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rò«‚ÇA@%ı*ô0æ@F£•„S˜œø¶8ÜYJÙÒ@YIñRΩ<6@Ï"w␀°@Ï3"Ë«æ30202033002@∏÷ÇÎC-@∫kÛ9¿ÎÓ@÷Wë¿ÎÌ˙@÷\ë¿ÎÌ˙SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁh|e§␀␀‚hHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀«X-ray spectral/timing properties of BHXBs can provide information about black hole spin, strong gravity regime and accretion-ejection mechanism. But this information can be reliable only if broadband spectral components can be identified, systematics in relativistic line shape can be reduced and timing properties can be connected to spectral properties. Our proposed 80 ks of NuSTAR observations of BHXBs Cygnus X-1 and GRS 1915+105, and contemporaneous ASTROSAT observations using our guaranteed time, will for the first time achieve a spectral/timing characterization above 10 keV at a level similar to what could be achieved with joint observations with a CCD and RXTE/PCA below 10 keV. This proposal relies on the broadband sensitivity of NuSTAR and the uniquely large area of ASTROSAT/LAXPC.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bhattacharyya                 Sudip                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Broadband X-Ray Spectral And Timing Study Of Cygnus X-1 And Grs 1915+105                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              ASTROSAT                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SDSSJ104326d47p110524d2                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï3"Ë«æ@Ï4"Ë«æ60160406001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ<}Í␀␀‡ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ104326d47p110524d2                           @d´6zë@&(r∞ ƒú@mM´üUõ=@K„,•zxl@v9◊s¸@Ï4"Ë«æ@ÏBÕìrh60160406002@”ØF¬&Äù@”°ﬂZÖáî@’&”8}ø@’'S8}øSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁãëB␀␀‡ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ETA_Carinae                                       @d&_ÿ≠´ü¿Mÿ‘˝Û∂F@q˜hr∞ ≈ø„¥¢3úø@vPÚ‰éär@ÏBÕìrh@ÏCx>30201030001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ√ ˛›␀␀‚UHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀æEta Carinae is an extremely massive binary system whose strong winds produce a variety of high X-ray activities. Earlier INTEGRAL and Suzaku observations of the star suggested non-thermal emission from high energy particles above 10 keV, which might be related to the Fermi GeV gamma-ray source. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of the star near periastron in 2014 did not confirm this emission, but another joint observation in 2015 July at the orbital phase 0.17 detected a clear excess component above 17 keV. This result may suggest shock acceleration or extreme shock heating by the wind colliding shock, which is important for cosmic ray origin or stellar evolution theory. We propose a deep NuSTAR observation of the star near apastron to understand the nature of this component.      Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Monitoring The Extremely Hard Spectral Slope Of Eta Carinae Around Apastron                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ETA_Carinae                                       @d&À˚µt¿MŸ≠B√…Ô@q˜çOﬂ;dø„ ¿Énò@v]Œ_p@ÏCx>@Ïp[ÃU˜30201030002@ÚKÍJå@Ò˘û¡Ω•@ÛLŸí:)«@ÛN)í:)«SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏﬁNW˝␀␀‚UHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀æEta Carinae is an extremely massive binary system whose strong winds produce a variety of high X-ray activities. Earlier INTEGRAL and Suzaku observations of the star suggested non-thermal emission from high energy particles above 10 keV, which might be related to the Fermi GeV gamma-ray source. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of the star near periastron in 2014 did not confirm this emission, but another joint observation in 2015 July at the orbital phase 0.17 detected a clear excess component above 17 keV. This result may suggest shock acceleration or extreme shock heating by the wind colliding shock, which is important for cosmic ray origin or stellar evolution theory. We propose a deep NuSTAR observation of the star near apastron to understand the nature of this component.      Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                             