 per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429OQ208                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïü±!´L@Ï†?Zé⁄60201043001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïﬁ!¡␀␀␀‚UHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀gThis project will provide the first hard X-ray observations of the youngest (<  250 years old) known radio sources and establish their high energy properties. Theoretical models predict that young radio sources should be strong X-ray emitters, but they have never been observed above ~10 keV. The proposed NuSTAR observations of two young sources from a class of Compact Symmetric Objects will complement our existing Chandra and XMM data and allow us to put strong constraints on their intrinsic absorption, disentangle emission processes contributing to their SED and derive physical properties of radio sources at the time of the initial jet formation. Among the X-ray telescopes operating at energies > 10 keV, only NuSTAR has capabilities required to study these young radio sources.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Siemiginowska                 Aneta                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Pioneering Hard X-Ray Band Observations Of Young Radio Sources With NuSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316OQ208                                             @jw‡ﬁ“â@<r∞ ƒõ¶@EŸb∂Æ@R[˘rGE9@)æûö@Ï†?Zé⁄@Ï∆[ÃU˜60201043002@ËÁÜñªò»@Ë⁄Rxl"h@Í©ÓOv@Í≠ÓOvSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïﬁâìà'␀␀‚UHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀gThis project will provide the first hard X-ray observations of the youngest (<  250 years old) known radio sources and establish their high energy properties. Theoretical models predict that young radio sources should be strong X-ray emitters, but they have never been observed above ~10 keV. The proposed NuSTAR observations of two young sources from a class of Compact Symmetric Objects will complement our existing Chandra and XMM data and allow us to put strong constraints on their intrinsic absorption, disentangle emission processes contributing to their SED and derive physical properties of radio sources at the time of the initial jet formation. Among the X-ray telescopes operating at energies > 10 keV, only NuSTAR has capabilities required to study these young radio sources.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Siemiginowska                 Aneta                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Pioneering Hard X-Ray Band Observations Of Young Radio Sources With NuSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SNR_G327d1m1d1                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∆[ÃU˜@Ï«?Zé⁄40201010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›∞d8!`␀␀‚UHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÓAs a highly magnetic, rapidly rotating pulsar converts its spin-down energy into a wind of relativistic particles, a synchrotron-emitting pulsar wind nebula (PWN) is formed that can be observed across the electromagnetic spectrum. Since the PWN's relativistic particle population is eventually injected into the interstellar medium (ISM), constraining the nature of this particle spectrum and its evolution is particularly important for understanding the Galactic cosmic ray population and energy density. We propose NuSTAR observations of two evolved PWNe inside supernova remnants G327.1-1.1 and MSH 15-56, with the goal of constraining the properties of the PWNe and the particle injection spectra, and understanding the nature and evolution of the particle population that escapes into the ISM.  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Temim                         Tea                           NULL       