portunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Cygnus_X1                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïsx>@Ït±!´L30202032001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›°=p£◊␀␀„ÛHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀«X-ray spectral/timing properties of BHXBs can provide information about black hole spin, strong gravity regime and accretion-ejection mechanism. But this information can be reliable only if broadband spectral components can be identified, systematics in relativistic line shape can be reduced and timing properties can be connected to spectral properties. Our proposed 80 ks of NuSTAR observations of BHXBs Cygnus X-1 and GRS 1915+105, and contemporaneous ASTROSAT observations using our guaranteed time, will for the first time achieve a spectral/timing characterization above 10 keV at a level similar to what could be achieved with joint observations with a CCD and RXTE/PCA below 10 keV. This proposal relies on the broadband sensitivity of NuSTAR and the uniquely large area of ASTROSAT/LAXPC.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bhattacharyya                 Sudip                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Broadband X-Ray Spectral And Timing Study Of Cygnus X-1 And Grs 1915+105                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cygnus_X1                                         @r∏ÛMj@Aõº”ZÖà@QŒÏø±[W@	mëhr∞!@Ptï*ô1@Ït±!´L@ÏÉ[ÃU˜30202032002@ ␀5îØO@ “nÈx‘˛@’M˘£n.≤@’M˘£n.≤SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›†r°ê␀␀„ÛHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀«X-ray spectral/timing properties of BHXBs can provide information about black hole spin, strong gravity regime and accretion-ejection mechanism. But this information can be reliable only if broadband spectral components can be identified, systematics in relativistic line shape can be reduced and timing properties can be connected to spectral properties. Our proposed 80 ks of NuSTAR observations of BHXBs Cygnus X-1 and GRS 1915+105, and contemporaneous ASTROSAT observations using our guaranteed time, will for the first time achieve a spectral/timing characterization above 10 keV at a level similar to what could be achieved with joint observations with a CCD and RXTE/PCA below 10 keV. This proposal relies on the broadband sensitivity of NuSTAR and the uniquely large area of ASTROSAT/LAXPC.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bhattacharyya                 Sudip                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Broadband X-Ray Spectral And Timing Study Of Cygnus X-1 And Grs 1915+105                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Astrosat                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004292MASXJ19301380p3410495                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÉ[ÃU˜@ÏÉ±!´L60160713001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›†KÚXø␀␀‡ˆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison    