nts: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RBS1125                                           @gÜ¬&Äù@4%®Xy=Ÿ@pæ4÷°aÂ@Tl⁄QŒ@u|ØOÑM@Ï≠±!´L@ÏªÕìrh60061229002@”wÒcàeï@”iY˘rG@‘ÁS1è≈@‘Ëì1è≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›Ç>ìÈ?␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M82_ULXS                                          @bû=p£◊
@QhÿD–©@aÆÈx‘˝Ù@D9=Ÿb∑@p–9XbN@ÏªÕìrh@Ïºx>30202022005@`å~($@`1¯†ê.@a ÃÃÃÃÕ@a ÃÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›Ç´lŸ␀␀‚ÜHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀äThe galaxy M82 harbors two of the most spectacular ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULXs), off-nuclear point sources exceeding the Eddington limit for a typical stellar remnant black hole. M82 X-1 reaches Lx> ~10^41 erg/s, and it's one of the best candidate intermediate mass black holes (IMBH). M82 X-2, reaching Lx> ~10^40 erg/s, was found to be powered by a pulsar, at 100 times the Eddington luminosity, by NuSTAR. This proposal complements an accepted Chandra program and aims at detecting these two sources in different spectral states. Thanks to the combined work of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will be able to disentangle the spectra of the two ULXs, unresolved in NuSTAR, but constrain the hard X-ray spectrum, invisible to Chandra, and investigate the nature of these two intriguing objects.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Timing And Spectral Studies Of The Ultraluminous X-Ray Sources In M82.                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429M82_ULXS                                          @bû∫«À)@Qi‘ï*ô@aÆ@∑Ä4n@D8ßÊö‘@p∆¶µ(@Ïºx>@ÏΩîØ‰030202022006@†wÍd¬¯8@†l±ƒ2 X@°üôå~($@°üôå~($SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›ÅË≈6˛␀␀‚ÜHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀äThe galaxy M82 harbors two of the most 