Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀J§␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429IRAS_13224m3809                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï!Í9Ö@Ï!±!´L60202001013␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›{k$ñ␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀0ËThe Narrow-Line Seyfert 1 galaxy IRAS13224-3809 shows strong iron-K and L broad lines and high-frequency soft X-ray time lags indicating reflection from the innermost regions about a rapidly spinning black hole. It persistently varies with high amplitude changes every 2 to 3 days. The peak frequency and energy spectrum of the lags varies with flux, probably due to changes in size of the corona. We propose a Very Large Programme consisting of a 1.5Ms observation of this object, which will enable the full range of flux levels to be studied, yielding a dynamic picture of the changing height and size of the corona and transforming our understanding of how luminous black hole accretion operates.                                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15        Mapping the inner accretion flow: dynamic reverberation in IRAS 13224-3809                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_13224m3809                                   @i)©*0U2¿C5ÑM:ì@s_≥g†˘	@8+jÁ’g@2a|⁄@Ï!±!´L@Ï!j?Zé⁄60202001014A␀µÿ/É{JA␀¨o“ÛA‡Ãò_A·ÎÃò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›zU$…ƒ␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀0ËThe Narrow-Line Seyfert 1 galaxy IRAS13224-3809 shows strong iron-K and L broad lines and high-frequency soft X-ray time lags indicating reflection from the innermost regions about a rapidly spinning black hole. It persistently varies with high amplitude changes every 2 to 3 days. The peak frequency and energy spectrum of the lags varies with flux, probably due to changes in size of the corona. We propose a Very Large Programme consisting of a 1.5Ms observation of this object, which will enable the full range of flux levels to be studied, yielding a dynamic picture of the changing height and size of the corona and transforming our understanding of how luminous black hole accretion operates.                                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15        Mapping the inner accretion flow: dynamic reverberation in IRAS 13224-3809                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(7/7)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO323mG081                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï!j?Zé⁄@Ï!jx>60161526001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›<ª£uÛ␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO323mG081                                       @hòZ¨1¿D6RΩ<6@scÒA \@6r3úæ‡@"!â7K∆®@Ï!jx>@Ï!y"Ë«æ60161526002@’…•„Ω@’ª•∑X‚@◊9/˜ŒŸ@◊9/˜ŒŸSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›<|“ﬁ␀␀·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004294U_1538m522                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï!y"Ë«æ@Ï!yîØ‰030201028001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›<UÉ˚␀␀‚}Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∂We propose a NuSTAR observation of the high-mass X-ray binary 4U 1538-522, with the aim of constraining the evolution with time energy of its cyclotron resonance scattering feature (CRSF). With the discovery by Staubert et al. (2014) of a secular decrease in the CRSF energy of Her X-1, 4U 1538-522 is the most promising candidate for a second source exhibiting a similar trend, as the 2012 Suzaku observation of 4U 1538-522 finds a CRSF energy that is ~1.5 keV higher than the energy measured by RXTE between 1996 and 2004. A NuSTAR observation would determine conclusively whether this represents a secular trend or a temporary shift in the CRSF energy, in addition to obtaining the most precise measurement of the hard X-ray spectrum to date.                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hemphill                      Paul                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Is The Cyclotron Line Energy Of 4U 1538-522 Increasing Over Time?                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1538m522                                       @mqrGE8Ô¿J3J#9¿Ï@ttOv_ÿÆ@ì@N§®¡@uR‚e+‘@Ï!yîØ‰0@Ï!ô±!´L30201028002@Âfìã¨q@Âa#ôôôö@ÁA•ú‡@ÁA•ú‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›;¯	+␀␀‚}Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∂We propose a NuSTAR observation of the high-mass X-ray binary 4U 1538-522, with the aim of constraining the evolution with time energy of its cyclotron resonance scattering feature (CRSF). With the discovery by Staubert et al. (2014) of a secular decrease in the CRSF energy of Her X-1, 4U 1538-522 is the most promising candidate for a second source exhibiting a similar trend, as the 2012 Suzaku observation of 4U 1538-522 finds a CRSF energy that is ~1.5 keV higher than the energy measured by RXTE between 1996 and 2004. A NuSTAR observation would determine conclusively whether this represents a secular trend or a temporary shift in the CRSF energy, in addition to obtaining the most precise measurement of the hard X-ray spectrum to date.                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hemphill                      Paul                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Is The Cyclotron Line Energy Of 4U 1538-522 Increasing Over Time?                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SU_Lyn                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï!ô±!´L@Ï!õw␀°30201025001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›; Â␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀™Symbiotic stars, in which a white dwarf (WD) accretes from a red giant, produce some type Ia supernovae, provide a unique window into low-mass binary stellar evolution, and offer tests for theories of accretion and jets. But despite their importance, the Galactic population of symbiotics is wildly uncertain. We propose a 40 ks NuSTAR observation of a special source --- SU Lyn --- to reveal the properties of a hard-to-find but possibly dominant type of symbiotic. Because the WD in SU Lyn is powered by accretion alone rather than quasi-steady shell burning, it reveals itself more through hard X-rays than optical line emission. Characterizing its fundamental parameters and emission properties will help us craft the optimal search strategy for the elusive accretion-powered symbiotic stars.    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sokoloski                     Jennifer                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Su Lyn: A Hard X-Ray Bright Symbiotic Star Hiding In Plain Sight                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SU_Lyn                                            @Y2	ﬁ␀“@KºûÏø±[@d£n.≤@4√ÒA [¿@gêîFsÅÿ@Ï!õw␀°@Ï!∑x>30201025002@„˙Íúwök@„ÚsßÜ¬'@ÂZÏŸá+@Â[¨Ÿá+SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›;o7¿I␀␀‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀™Symbiotic stars, in which a white dwarf (WD) accretes from a red giant, produce some type Ia supernovae, provide a unique wi