minous X-ray sources (ULXs), off-nuclear point sources exceeding the Eddington limit for a typical stellar remnant black hole. M82 X-1 reaches Lx> ~10^41 erg/s, and it's one of the best candidate intermediate mass black holes (IMBH). M82 X-2, reaching Lx> ~10^40 erg/s, was found to be powered by a pulsar, at 100 times the Eddington luminosity, by NuSTAR. This proposal complements an accepted Chandra program and aims at detecting these two sources in different spectral states. Thanks to the combined work of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will be able to disentangle the spectra of the two ULXs, unresolved in NuSTAR, but constrain the hard X-ray spectrum, invisible to Chandra, and investigate the nature of these two intriguing objects.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Timing And Spectral Studies Of The Ultraluminous X-Ray Sources In M82.                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429M82_ULXS                                          @b†\ë—N<@Qi¿ÎÌ˙D@a≠‹∆?@D:ïÈ·∞ä@n|%ÆÊ1˘@Ï"kÕìrh@Ï"Ö[ÃU˜30202022010@Â©∆?@Âõµˇ.HÈ@Á(—s¸P@Á(Òs¸PSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›7vµI2␀␀‚ÜHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀äThe galaxy M82 harbors two of the most spectacular ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULXs), off-nuclear point sources exceeding the Eddington limit for a typical stellar remnant black hole. M82 X-1 reaches Lx> ~10^41 erg/s, and it's one of the best candidate intermediate mass black holes (IMBH). M82 X-2, reaching Lx> ~10^40 erg/s, was found to be powered by a pulsar, at 100 times the Eddington luminosity, by NuSTAR. This proposal complements an accepted Chandra program and aims at detecting these two sources in different spectral states. Thanks to the combined work of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will be able to disentangle the spectra of the two ULXs, unresolved in NuSTAR, but constrain the hard X-ray spectrum, invisible to Chandra, and investigate the nature of these two intriguing objects.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Timing And Spectral Studies Of The Ultraluminous X-Ray Sources In M82.                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Her_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï"Ö[ÃU˜@Ï"Üw␀°10202002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›6˛{£v␀␀·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                            