429IRASF_05189m2524                                  @Tp£◊
=¿9]IQÇ©ì@l{ä⁄πıZ¿>lãCïÅ@b5•„S˜œ@Ï$É±!´L@Ï$ÑÕìrh60201022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›-Œ ÜB␀␀‚öHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Recent Herschel observations have identified powerful large-scale molecular outflows in ultraluminous infrared galaxies, indicating that AGN feedback can indeed influence star formation and galaxy evolution. Theoretical models describing the formation of such large-scale outflows require a putative inner AGN accretion disk wind. We found the first evidence of such a wind in IRASF11119+3257, in the form of blue-shifted Fe XXV/XXVI absorption lines with velocity 0.25c. However, just one object does not allow to establish the validity of such mechanism. Here, we request simultaneous 150ks NuSTAR and 90ks XMM-Newton observations of the next most promising ULIRG, IRASF05189-2524, to characterize its disk wind and conclusively establish the mechanism of quasar mode feedback.                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tombesi                       Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Establishing The Quasar Mode Feedback With NuSTAR And XMM-Newton                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRASF_05189m2524                                  @TÊö‘,=¿9W1è≈Å@lz∆?¿>i„º”@ba(åÁ∞@Ï$ÑÕìrh@Ï$¸w␀°60201022002AÓ0SéÛMAﬁ<RT`™ALÌÕ5®XAN≠Õ5®XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›,ÛKx¸␀␀‚öHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Recent Herschel observations have identified powerful large-scale molecular outflows in ultraluminous infrared galaxies, indicating that AGN feedback can indeed influence star formation and galaxy evolution. Theoretical models describing the formation of such large-scale outflows require a putative inner AGN accretion disk wind. We found the first evidence of such a wind in IRASF11119+3257, in the form of blue-shifted Fe XXV/XXVI absorption lines with velocity 0.25c. However, just one object does not allow to establish the validity of such mechanism. Here, we request simultaneous 150ks NuSTAR and 90ks XMM-Newton observations of the next most promising ULIRG, IRASF05189-2524, to characterize its disk wind and conclusively establish the mechanism of quasar mode feedback.                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tombesi                       Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Establishing The Quasar Mode Feedback With NuSTAR And XMM-Newton                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429SMC_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï$¸w␀°@Ï$¸îØ‰030202004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›,®K⁄␀␀‚÷Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2yWe propose for a series of four 20 ks observations with XMM and NuSTAR of the binary X-ray pulsar SMC X-1. This source shows a long-period (40 60 d) superorbital cycle due to a warped, precessing accretion disk. This disk is heated by the pulsed hard X-ray beam from the neutron star, and observations of this thermal emission can provide a unique probe of the inner accretion flow near the magnetosphere. However this requires sensitivity and timing resolution across the full X-ray band (from below 1 keV to above 20 keV), and sampling of a full superorbital cycle. We will take advantage of XMM's excellent sensitivity and time resolution along with NuSTAR's exquisite high-energy coverage to yield the clearest picture to date of the inner accretion flow.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hickox                        Ryan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Probing the Warped Inner Accretion Flow in SMC X-1 with XMM and NuSTAR                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SMC_X1                                            @3`∑Ä4m∆¿R\K]ÃcÒ@r∆‚ÎC-¿EΩVì@j`[¿6„@Ï$¸îØ‰0@Ï%"Ë«æ30202004002@’Ôt4m≈÷@÷èÄ4m∆@Ÿ63ä	ﬁ@Ÿ93ä	ﬁSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›,9 ␀␀‚÷Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀2yWe propose for a series of four 20 ks observations with XMM and NuSTAR of the binary X-ray pulsar SMC X-1. This source shows a long-period (40 60 d) superorbital cycle due to a warped, precessing accretion disk. This disk is heated by the pulsed hard X-ray beam from the neutron star, and observations of this thermal emission can provide a unique probe of the inner accretion flow near the magnetosphere. However this requires sensitivity and timing resolution across the full X-ray band (from below 1 keV to above 20 keV), and sampling of a full superorbital cycle. We will take advantage of XMM's excellent sensitivity and time resolution along with NuSTAR's exquisite high-energy coverage to yield the clearest picture to date of the inner accretion flow.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hickox                        Ryan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Probing the Warped Inner Accretion Flow in SMC X-1 with XMM and NuSTAR                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_J2032p4127                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï%"Ë«æ@Ï%[ÃU˜90201036001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›,\(ˆ␀␀·„Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1   