ck holes and neutron stars is associated with two types of outflows that are mutually exclusive. At > E-2 of the Eddington limit, disk winds are detected through narrow X-ray absorption features, whereas at lower mass-accretion rates radio jets are seen. However, our AO1 NuSTAR observation of the neutron star low-mass X-ray binary IGR J17062-6143 revealed a ~3-4 sigma narrow absorption line at ~6.85 keV. This provides the very first evidence that disk winds can be launched at merely ~E-3 of Eddington, and may co-exist with a radio jet. We propose for a 75 ks NuSTAR observation and a supporting 70 ks XMM observation to solidify this measurement, which would change our current views of inflow-outflow coupling in accreting compact objects. Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       An Accretion Disk Wind At Low Eddington Rate                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J17062m6143                                   @p#9¿ÎÓ¿N‹˙¨ŸË>@tâ≠B√…Ô¿(uÈ·∞â†@uOÜYJÙÒ@Ï%ã±!´L@Ï%≥x>30201018002@BÉÌ˙C˛@9K‹]cà@Òcπ&ÄùI@ÒdÈ&ÄùISCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›)Z+<M␀␀‚§Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀\According to the current paradigm, accretion onto black holes and neutron stars is associated with two types of outflows that are mutually exclusive. At > E-2 of the Eddington limit, disk winds are detected through narrow X-ray absorption features, whereas at lower mass-accretion rates radio jets are seen. However, our AO1 NuSTAR observation of the neutron star low-mass X-ray binary IGR J17062-6143 revealed a ~3-4 sigma narrow absorption line at ~6.85 keV. This provides the very first evidence that disk winds can be launched at merely ~E-3 of Eddington, and may co-exist with a radio jet. We propose for a 75 ks NuSTAR observation and a supporting 70 ks XMM observation to solidify this measurement, which would change our current views of inflow-outflow coupling in accreting compact objects. Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       An Accretion Disk Wind At Low Eddington Rate                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PG_1535p547                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï%≥x>@Ï%¥x>60201023003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›)+Tì'␀␀‚§Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Previous X-ray observations of the nearby "complex" NLS1 PG1535+547 show evidence for some of the strongest reflection from the accretion disk observed among local, Compton-thin AGN. The unusually high reflection fraction indicates an extreme accretion geometry in which the intrinsic continuum emission experiences strong gravitational light bending, resulting in a broadband X-ray spectrum dominated by reflection from the inner accretion disk. However, PG1535+547 has never been observed in the hard X-ray band. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR in order to robustly confirm the extreme reflection, and utilize the full potential of the latest reflection models to measure the BH spin and constrain the accretion geometry.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Extreme Reflection In The Complex Narrow Line Seyfert 1 AGN PG 1535+547                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1535p547                                       @mDp:˚~ë@KDø±[W?@U¿[¿6„@Hπá+@ss÷°a‰˜@Ï%¥x>@Ï%Ïw␀°60201023004@ÙãÙÿE@ÙKä⁄πı@ı{5?|Ó@ı|65?|ÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›(©>ìÈ␀␀‚§Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Previous X-ray observations of the nearby "complex" NLS1 PG1535+547 show evidence for some of the strongest reflection from the accretion disk observed among local, Compton-thin AGN. The unusually high reflection fraction indicates an extreme accretion geometry in which the intrinsic continuum emission experiences strong gravitational light bending, resulting in a broadband X-ray spectrum dominated by reflection from the inner accretion disk. However, PG1535+547 has never been observed in the hard X-ray band. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR in order to robustly confirm the extreme reflection, and utilize the full potential of the latest reflection models to measure the BH spin and constrain the accretion geometry.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Extreme Reflection In The Complex Narrow Line Seyfert 1 AGN PG 1535+547                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004291RXSJ010713d0m113554                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï%Ïw␀°@Ï%Ì?Zé⁄60260004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›(u¶‡␀␀·9Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀