29COm0d4m0d22                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï&Yx>@Ï&Z"Ë«æ90201020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›&DDDD␀␀‡ÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316COm0d4m0d22                                       @p•∞Ú{≤ˇ¿=hy=Ÿc@vwjÁ’fœøêÂ`Aâ7L@u␀µsÍ≥@Ï&Z"Ë«æ@Ï&†îØ‰090201020002@ˆ‘¡V@ˆª˜º@¯±˜HÀí@¯¥ÁHÀíSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›%§±~K␀␀‡ÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20200429PKS1622m29                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï&†îØ‰0@Ï&†Í9Ö60160638001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›%kÊ5€␀␀·9Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS1622m29                                        @n–␀—∑Y¿=ﬁóçOﬂ;@u ØOÑM@+2 Wßá@u•º”ZÖà@Ï&†Í9Ö@Ï&Øx>60160638002@‘9´¸π#£@‘*‡r∞ ≈@’ö␀␀␀␀@’õ»␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›%&N û␀␀·9Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C_382                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï&Øx>@Ï&∞"Ë«æ60202015005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›$¸ñ/…␀␀‚øHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÁWe propose a joint NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of the broad-line radio galaxy (BLRG) 3C 382, consisting of 5x20 ks observations spaced by 10-15 days. 3C 382 is an ideal radio-loud source to obtain high sensitivity measurements over a broad (UV/hard X-rays) high-energy range, which will allow us to: 1) study the UV/X-ray spectrum of a BLRG with unprecedented accuracy and test physically motivated Comptonization models, 2) understand the origin of the puzzling reflection component, and 3) investigate the nature of the soft X-ray excess. These observations will allow for a direct comparison with radio-quiet Seyfert 1s, in particular with a similar, successful NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of NGC 4593.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ursini                        Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       NuSTAR/XMM Monitoring Of The Broad-Line Radio Galaxy 3C 382                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C_382                                            @qk¥9Xb@@Vµ'ª@NõàeîØO@1ì{J#9¡@u5“Ú‰è@Ï&∞"Ë«æ@Ï&æx>60202015006@‘UÌ≤-V@‘G@2 W®@’‘Y¶µ@’’Ÿ¶µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›$±˜®␀␀‚øHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÁWe propose a joint NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of the broad-line radio galaxy (BLRG) 3C 382, consisting of 5x20 ks observations spaced by 10-15 days. 3C 382 is an ideal radio-loud source to obtain high sensitivity measurements over a broad (UV/hard X-rays) high-energy range, which will allow us to: 1) study the UV/X-ray spectrum of a BLRG with unprecedented accuracy and test physically motivated Comptonization models, 2) understand the origin of the puzzling reflection component, and 3) investigate the nature of the soft X-ray excess. These observations will allow for a direct comparison with radio-quiet Seyfert 1s, in particular with a similar, successful NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of NGC 4593.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ursini                        Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       NuSTAR/XMM Monitoring Of The Broad-Line Radio Galaxy 3C 382                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(3/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429HESS_J1713m381                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï&æx>@Ï&ø"Ë«æ30201031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›$äûá∆␀␀‚ßHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∆The 3.82s AXP in the SNR CTB 37B is among the youngest and most energetic magnetars, with a spin-down power up to 7.4E34 erg/s. A recent NuSTAR observation reveals hard X-ray emission above 10 keV. We propose to measure its hard spectrum with sufficient accuracy to apply the Beloborodov e-/e+ outflow model and to characterize its > 10 keV pulsed emission, to study the emission geometry. We also propose to continue monitoring its spin-down rate, which varies by a factor of 2. The most energetic magnetars are SGRs, and a marked increase in their spin-down torque often precedes an outburst. By analogy with SGR/AXP 1E 1547.0-5408, the magnetar with the largest spin-down power, and most similar to the AXP in CTB 37B, we may be observing the behavior of an SGR before it outburst.                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Gotthelf                      Eric                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Energetic Magnetar In Hess J1713-381/Ctb 37B                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESS_J1713m381                                    @p'ﬁ␀—∑¿C†ê-‡@u»•„S˜œ?‚Ú‰éär@u\e+”√a@Ï&ø"Ë«æ@Ï&˘x>30201031002@Û-Ç–Â`B@ÚÛ+GE8Ô@Ùå¢3ú@Ùç≥¢3úSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›$␀¬.E␀␀‚ßHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∆The 3.82s AXP in the SNR CTB 37B is among the youngest and most energetic magnetars, with a spin-down power up to 7.4E34 erg/s. A recent NuSTAR observation reveals hard X-ray emission above 10 keV. We propose to measure its hard spectrum with sufficient accuracy to apply the Beloborodov e-/e+ outflow model and to characterize its > 10 keV pulsed emission, to study the emission geometry. We also propose to continue monitoring its spin-down rate, which varies by a factor of 2. The most energetic magnetars are SGRs, and a marked increase in their spin-down torque often precedes an outburst. By analogy with SGR/AXP 1E 1547.0-5408, the magnetar with the largest spin-down power, and most similar to the AXP in CTB 37B, we may be observing the behavior of an SGR before it outburst.                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Gotthelf                      Eric                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Energetic Magnetar In Hess J1713-381/Ctb 37B                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO_362mG18                                       @S˙‚ÎC-¿@Tø±[W?@mÄÅo␀h‹¿@W!-w1ê@d2°a‰˜f@Ï&˘x>@Ï&˙[ÃU˜60201046001@qte+”√a@qjb∂Æ}V@r†fœAÚ@r†fœAÚSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›#¬ß°Ú␀␀‚ÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀|We propose simultaneous 100 ks XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert 1.5 galaxy ESO 362-G18 for the purpose of making a detailed study of the spectrum of radiation reflected from the inner accretion disk. Our chief aim is to constrain both the spin of the black hole and the properties of the corona that illuminates the disk. The combination of NuSTAR's high-sensitivity in the hard X-ray band, the soft-band coverage of XMM-Newton, and our state-of-the-art relativistic reflection models will allow us to break degeneracies between different possible scenarios, and impose tight constraints on the accretion geometry and black hole spin of this bright AGN.                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Reflection Spectroscopy Of The Seyfert 1.5 Agn Eso 362-G18                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429ESO_362mG18                                       @S˚ÑM;¿@Ru%F@mª/ÏV÷¿@V8ÜYJı@dT"h	‘ï@Ï&˙[ÃU˜@Ï'E?Zé⁄60201046002@¯·	>BZÓ@¯À`:˚~ë@˙újF‹]@˙ú*F‹]SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›#bõ␀␀‚ÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀|We propose simultaneous 100 ks XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert 1.5 galaxy ESO 362-G18 for the purpose of making a detailed study of the spectrum of radiation reflected from the inner accretion disk. Our chief aim is to constrain both the spin of the black hole and the properties of the corona that illuminates the disk. The combination of NuSTAR's high-sensitivity in the hard X-ray band, the soft-band coverage of XMM-Newton, and our state-of-the-art relativistic reflection models will allow us to break degeneracies between different possible scenarios, and impose tight constraints on the accretion geometry and black hole spin of this bright AGN.                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Reflection Spectroscopy Of The Seyfert 1.5 Agn Eso 362-G18                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429Aql_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï'E?Zé⁄@Ï'F[ÃU˜80001043001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›"÷ê‡Ö␀␀‚∂Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀"pAt low luminosities, the X-ray spectra of neutron star (NS) LMXB transients often contain two components: one is due to thermal emission from the NS surface and is reasonably well-understood, while the other is a hard power-law of unknown origin. Recently, observations of Cen X-4 in quiescence with XMM-Newton and the Nuclear Spectroscopic Telescope Array (NuSTAR) have provided the first look at the broadband spectrum. The hard component is sharply cutoff above 10 keV and is consistent with a bremsstrahlung emission mechanism. We propose a joint XMM-Newton, NuSTAR, and Swift Target of Opportunity program to observe Aql X-1 in order to compare the broadband spectrum from this system to that of Cen X-4.                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Observing Aql X-1 to understand the hard X-ray emission from NS transients                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Aql_X1                                            @q¸’œ™Õü?‚2 WßÜ¬@A &ÄùIR¿æﬂ§?Â…@t }VlÙ@Ï'F[ÃU˜@Ï'~îØ‰080001043002@Òª)!-w2@Ò†s˝Û∂F@Ûˇ.HÈ@Ûëˇ.HÈSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›"W¸˜G␀␀‚∂Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀"pAt low luminosities, the X-ray spectra of neutron star (NS) LMXB transients often contain two components: one is due to thermal emission from the NS surface and is reasonably well-understood, while the other is a hard power-law of unknown origin. Recently, observations of Cen X-4 in quiescence with XMM-Newton and the Nuclear Spectroscopic Telescope Array (NuSTAR) have provided the first look at the broadband spectrum. The hard component is sharply cutoff above 10 keV and is consistent with a bremsstrahlung emission mechanism. We propose a joint XMM-Newton, NuSTAR, and Swift Target of Opportunity program to observe Aql X-1 in order to compare the broadband spectrum from this system to that of Cen X-4.                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀␀X13       Observing Aql X-1 to understand the hard X-ray emission from NS transients                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀ToO / Kp=6                                                                                                              archived  20200429IC_2497                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï'~îØ‰0@Ï'±!´L60201017001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›"#¶~¨␀␀‚ÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˘Until ~200 kyr ago IC 2497 hosted a powerful 1e46 erg/s quasar. The quasar has since shut down and its photoionisation echo is preserved in the AGN photoionised cloud known as Hannys Voorwerp, which provides us information about the past state of the quasar. We can therefore study AGN variability, and its effect on the host galaxy, on previously inaccessible timescales. Existing Chandra, XMM and Suzaku observations show that the present-day quasar luminosity is at least 2 and perhaps as much as 4 orders of magnitude lower than in the past. However, the Suzaku observations at 15-30 keV are uncertain. To measure the present-day nuclear luminosity of IC 2497 and therefore the true magnitude of the quasar shutdown we need deep hard X-ray observations. NuSTAR is the best instrument for this.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sartori                       Lia                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Sudden Death Of A Quasar: A NuSTAR Autopsy Of The Ic 2497 - Hanny'S Voorwerp System                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC_2497                                           @b)¨q≤ñ@A](åÁ∞@g¬a|⁄Q@HXy=Ÿc@gªò«‚ÇA@Ï'±!´L@Ï'õ?Zé⁄60201017002@„Ωa@O@‚ıZ¨ŸË>@‰Z*’œ™Œ@‰Zj’œ™ŒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›! m¸5␀␀‚ÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˘Until ~200 kyr ago IC 2497 hosted a powerful 1e46 erg/s quasar. The quasar has since shut down and its photoionisation echo is preserved in the AGN photoionised cloud known as Hannys Voorwerp, which provides us information about the past state of the quasar. We can therefore study AGN variability, and its effect on the host galaxy, on previously inaccessible timescales. Existing Chandra, XMM and Suzaku observations show that the present-day quasar luminosity is at least 2 and perhaps as much as 4 orders of magnitude lower than in the past. However, the Suzaku observations at 15-30 keV are uncertain. To measure the present-day nuclear luminosity of IC 2497 and therefore the true magnitude of the quasar shutdown we need deep hard X-ray observations. NuSTAR is the best instrument for this.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sartori                       Lia                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Sudden Death Of A Quasar: A NuSTAR Autopsy Of The Ic 2497 - Hanny'S Voorwerp System                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀