y is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀CVZ / Kp=5                                                                                                              archived  20200429SMC_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï'¿Í9Ö@Ï'¡?Zé⁄30202004005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï› ß°Òó␀␀‚÷Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2yWe propose for a series of four 20 ks observations with XMM and NuSTAR of the binary X-ray pulsar SMC X-1. This source shows a long-period (40 60 d) superorbital cycle due to a warped, precessing accretion disk. This disk is heated by the pulsed hard X-ray beam from the neutron star, and observations of this thermal emission can provide a unique probe of the inner accretion flow near the magnetosphere. However this requires sensitivity and timing resolution across the full X-ray band (from below 1 keV to above 20 keV), and sampling of a full superorbital cycle. We will take advantage of XMM's excellent sensitivity and time resolution along with NuSTAR's exquisite high-energy coverage to yield the clearest picture to date of the inner accretion flow.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hickox                        Ryan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Probing the Warped Inner Accretion Flow in SMC X-1 with XMM and NuSTAR                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SMC_X1                                            @3Y«y¶µ¿R]é!ñRΩ@r«„º”¿E∫≥g†˘	@m±ƒ2 X@Ï'¡?Zé⁄@Ï'–w␀°30202004006@”Ú4ØOÑ@”Ê7¸π#£@’{ÓkP∞Ú@’~ÆkP∞ÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï› cé8‰␀␀‚÷Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀2yWe propose for a series of four 20 ks observations with XMM and NuSTAR of the binary X-ray pulsar SMC X-1. This source shows a long-period (40 60 d) superorbital cycle due to a warped, precessing accretion disk. This disk is heated by the pulsed hard X-ray beam from the neutron star, and observations of this thermal emission can provide a unique probe of the inner accretion flow near the magnetosphere. However this requires sensitivity and timing resolution across the full X-ray band (from below 1 keV to above 20 keV), and sampling of a full superorbital cycle. We will take advantage of XMM's excellent sensitivity and time resolution along with NuSTAR's exquisite high-energy coverage to yield the clearest picture to date of the inner accretion flow.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hickox                        Ryan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Probing the Warped Inner Accretion Flow in SMC X-1 with XMM and NuSTAR                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(3/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429UDS_MOS031                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï'–w␀°@Ï'–Õìrh60111031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï› ;ÏGM␀␀·BHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    