                         ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SGR_1900p14                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï*5±!´L@Ï*6îØ‰030201013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï› ˇûÈ␀␀‚“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀The non-thermal hard X-rays of magnetars are a crucial key to understand their magnetic activities,or even a unique way to access the toroidal magnetic field hidden inside a stelar interior. A new signature of stellar free precision has been suggested from two sources 4U 0142+61 and 1E 1547.0-5408. This can be interpreted as a distortion of stellar shape due to the extremely strong toroidal field of magnetars. However, the results are only limited to Suzaku observations from a few sources. It is hence imperative to confirm whether such a signature does exists in other magnetars from NuSTAR observations. We propose a 120 ks observation of a prolific soft gamma repeater SGR 1900+14 which hard X-ray timing and spectral nature has yet to be investigated in detail.                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Enoto                         Teruaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Hard X-Ray Timing Behaviors Of Magnetar Activities: Search For Evidence Of Toroidal Field In Sgr 1900+14                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_1900p14                                       @qÏ†'RTa@"ù•ú‡v@Eq€"–Â`?Ì⁄QŒ_@t¬è\(ˆ@Ï*6îØ‰0@Ï*ê?Zé⁄30201013002@˝Ôrÿ≠´ü@˝Ÿ“|ÌëhA␀kªe+‘A␀l;e+‘SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›÷ê‡Ö␀␀‚“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀The non-thermal hard X-rays of magnetars are a crucial key to understand their magnetic activities,or even a unique way to access the toroidal magnetic field hidden inside a stelar interior. A new signature of stellar free precision has been suggested from two sources 4U 0142+61 and 1E 1547.0-5408. This can be interpreted as a distortion of stellar shape due to the extremely strong toroidal field of magnetars. However, the results are only limited to Suzaku observations from a few sources. It is hence imperative to confirm whether such a signature does exists in other magnetars from NuSTAR observations. We propose a 120 ks observation of a prolific soft gamma repeater SGR 1900+14 which hard X-ray timing and spectral nature has yet to be investigated in detail.                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Enoto                         Teruaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Hard X-Ray Timing Behaviors Of Magnetar Activities: Search For Evidence Of Toroidal Field In Sgr 1900+14                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_2004m447                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï*ê?Zé⁄@Ï*êÕìrh60201045001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›ïˇ=“␀␀‚“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀{Flat-spectrum radio-loud narrow line Seyfert 1 galaxies (F-RLNLS1s) define a newly discovered class of AGNs that appears to occupy a distinct locus in the parameter space of AGNs. They add a third dimension to the eigenvector-1 defined by black hole mass and accretion rate, with inclusion of BH spin. In models of scale-invariant disk-jet emission, radio emission defines the jet power while X-ray emission defines the accretion disk emission. The observed properties of F-RLNLS1s appear to defy this universality. The NuSTAR energy range of 3--79 keV is of particular interest as we expect it to reveal the physics of the ``disk-jet connection'' by probing accretion disk emission as well as relativistic jet emission. We propose NuSTAR observations of 3 F-RLNLS1s, 2 of which are gamma-ray loud.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mathur                        Smita                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Unexplored Parameter Space Of Flat-Spectrum Radio-Loud Narrow-Line Seyfert-1 Galaxies And The Disk-Jet Connection       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_2004m447                                      @rﬂ\(ı¬è¿FL(ı¬è\@v3åLò_¿?•œ™ÕûÑ@t<Å◊€Ùà@Ï*êÕìrh@Ï*πx>60201045002@Ìï¬¯7µ@ÌáO[W>´@Ô©oiDg@Ô´/<64SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›4%Ì	␀␀‚“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀{Flat-spectrum radio-loud narrow line Seyfert 1 galaxies (F-RLNLS1s) define a newly discovered class of AGNs that appears to occupy a distinct locus in the parameter space of AGNs. They add a third dimension to the eigenvector-1 defined by black hole mass and accretion rate, with inclusion of BH spin. In models of scale-invariant disk-jet emission, radio emission defines the jet power while X-ray emission defines the accretion disk emission. The observed properties of F-RLNLS1s appear to defy this universality. The NuSTAR energy range of 3--79 keV is of particular interest as we expect it to reveal the physics of the ``disk-jet connection'' by probing accretion disk emission as well as relativistic jet emission. We propose NuSTAR observations of 3 F-RLNLS1s, 2 of which are gamma-ray loud.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mathur                        Smita                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Unexplored Parameter Space Of Flat-Spectrum Radio-Loud Narrow-Line Seyfert-1 Galaxies And The Disk-Jet Connection       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SMC_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï*πx>@Ï*∫w␀°30202004007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›ö[»␀␀‚÷Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2yWe propose for a series of four 20 ks observations with XMM and NuSTAR of the binary X-ray pulsar SMC X-1. This source shows a long-period (40 60 d) superorbital cycle due to a warped, precessing accretion disk. This disk is heated by the pulsed hard X-ray beam from the neutron star, and observations of this thermal emission can provide a unique probe of the inner accretion flow near the magnetosphere. However this requires sensitivity and timing resolution across the full X-ray band (from below 1 keV to above 20 keV), and sampling of a full superorbital cycle. We will take advantage of XMM's excellent sensitivity and time resolution along with NuSTAR's exquisite high-energy coverage to yield the clearest picture to date of the inner accretion flow.                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hickox                        Ryan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Probing the Warped Inner Accretion Flow in SMC X-1 with XMM and NuSTAR                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                     