                NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SgrA_molecular_clouds                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï+1?Zé⁄@Ï+1±!´L40202001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›“4Vy␀␀·dHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SgrA_molecular_clouds                             @pß0æÌ)¿<·ÎÖ∏R@vF‹]cà?»F‹]cà@tÓÌ˙C˛]@Ï+1±!´L@Ï+öîØ‰040202001002Aj!®ı¬èARL™ô0æAŸ©◊s¸A⁄I◊s¸SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›Í™™´␀␀·dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀(1/2) Galactic Legacy Survey / Kp=5                                                                                     archived  20200429SADA_2016_305_52                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï+öîØ‰0@Ï+öÍ9Ö10201001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›ß“}(␀␀‚ Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1739m278                                      @pôﬁ␀—∑¿;∆Èx‘˝Ù?‡eîØOÑ?ˆAÚ◊s@u:^ûö@Ï+öÍ9Ö@Ï+≠Í9Ö80102101005@⁄c&¶L/É@⁄Eˆ_ÿ≠¨@‹ò‡␀␀␀␀@‹õ†␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›YP»@␀␀‚ Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  	ÅŸWe propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, INTEGRAL, JVLA/ATCA, Swift) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds before the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next ~2 months.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C17       An Integrated Approach to Winds, Jets, and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra INTEGRAL                                                                                    ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SN2014C                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï+≠Í9Ö@Ï+Æ±!´L40202013003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›1«r␀␀‚“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR program that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow-up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R> 7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Continued NuSTAR Monitoring Of The Unprecedented Metamorphosis Of Sn2014C                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u3‡qv@A0ßÊö‘@W_ÏV’œ´¿4ªÌ˙C˛]@uAûö@Ï+Æ±!´L@Ï+ÃîØ‰040202013004@„ıéïÈ·±@„Ëv($x@Â_(u%F@Âbu%FSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›ÿvT2␀␀‚“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR program that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow-up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R> 7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Continued NuSTAR Monitoring Of The Unprecedented Metamorphosis Of Sn2014C                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004291ES_0502p675                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï+ÃîØ‰0@Ï+Õ?Zé⁄60202026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›Ææc^␀␀„Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀'We propose NuSTAR observations, coordinated with spectral coverage from the infrared to very-high-energy (VHE) gamma rays, of the extreme high-synchrotron-peaked (HSP) blazar 1ES 0502+675. Extreme HSP blazars represent the apparent limiting case for particle acceleration in blazas surface magnetic field strength can be estimated from its spin-down properties, currently the best way of determining its initial spin period is by modeling the properties of the pulsar wind nebula (PWN) powered by its rotational energy. Our analysis of a new LOFAR observation of PWN G54.1+0.3, coupled with our recent modeling of this source, suggest the associated pulsar PSR J1930+1852 is born with a spin period of P 0 ~65 ms - which we will test with the requested NuSTAR observation.                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Willia