ation between white dwarf dynamical mass and accretion flow temperature in past work. The relation can potentially be used to estimate white dwarf masses, but notably fails for the two lowest mass white dwarfs in the sample. BZ UMa has the more reliable dynamical mass estimate of the two, and is thus the key source for testing the relation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Maccarone                     Thomas                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Mass-Temperature Relation In Accreting White Dwarfs                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZ_UMa                                            @`ÆóçOﬂ;@LÍZÓcä@c›oiDg@CU•ú‡v@d3+I∫@Ï,ﬁîØ‰0@Ï,w␀°30201019002@◊U`2 W®@◊Paˇ.HÈ@ÿ˚bM”@ÿ˛pbM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›2@y]␀␀‚ﬂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀jWe propose NuSTAR observations of the cataclysmic variable BZ UMa. This object is an outlier in the relation between white dwarf dynamical mass and accretion flow temperature in past work. The relation can potentially be used to estimate white dwarf masses, but notably fails for the two lowest mass white dwarfs in the sample. BZ UMa has the more reliable dynamical mass estimate of the two, and is thus the key source for testing the relation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Maccarone                     Thomas                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Mass-Temperature Relation In Accreting White Dwarfs                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀ Kp=5                                                                                                                   archived  20200429WR140                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï,w␀°@Ï,Õìrh30202002003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›
ûá∆␀␀‚ﬂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2MThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable X-ray plasma. This nearly ``controlled'' collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016, a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and understand the X-ray Minimum.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Colliding Wind X-ray Emission from the Massive Binary WR140 around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR140                                             @süUõ=@EÍa|⁄Q@T3©*0U2@Ê1¯†ê.@sîŒŸá+@Ï,Õìrh@Ï-±!´L30202002004@Èï≠Mj@Èá•øHÃ@Îä£/üæ@Îé#/üæSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›¶~¨/␀␀‚ﬂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀2MThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable X-ray plasma. This nearly ``controlled'' collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016, a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and understand the X-ray Minimum.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Colliding Wind X-ray Emission from the Massive Binary WR140 around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SMC_X3                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï-±!´L@Ï-±!´L90201041001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›x	+␀␀·qHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                        