␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS0558m504                                       @V8}øH¿I8‚e+‘@p<ûÏø±¿<ö¿Énò@jjßÔù≤-@Ï-Ò"Ë«æ@Ï.␀[ÃU˜60160254002@‘}‚æﬂ§@@‘q„X‚e@’‘zcä	@’‘zcä	SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›":gÎ␀␀·zHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429RT_Cru                                            @gò)«y¶µ¿P#/ÏV’–@r—␀—∑Yø˙r∞ ƒõ¶@^rúwökQ@Ï.␀[ÃU˜@Ï.␀Í9Ö30201023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›˚£uÛ␀␀‚ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ãBuilding on successful NuSTAR observations of other accreting white dwarfs, we propose to observe RT Cru and V648 Car - two hard X-ray bright symbiotic stars. Our primary goal is to determine the white dwarf mass and accretion rate. These quantities have important implications for the frequency of nova outbursts and therefore the long term evolution of the white dwarfs in these systems. Although in principle X-ray spectra of CVs and symbiotic stars encode information about the white dwarf mass, the effects of reflection from the white dwarf surface must be taken into account to fully realize this potential. Only NuSTAR has the sensitivity above 10 keV to detect and assess the importance of reflection while independently measuring the intrinsic spectral shape in our target sources.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nelson                        Thomas                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       A Reflection On Symbiotic Stars - Using Reflected X-Rays To Constrain Properties Of The White Dwarf And Accretion Disk  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RT_Cru                                            @gò8ÜYJı¿P"ïÈ·∞ä@r—:í£ø˙K∆ßÔù≤@^ô¶µ(@Ï.␀Í9Ö@Ï.#±!´L30201023002@ÏlT"h	’@ÏbΩ'ª/Ï@Óx±©*0U@Ó{±©*0USCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›ã¡Õ.␀␀‚ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ãBuilding on successful NuSTAR observations of other accreting white dwarfs, we propose to observe RT Cru and V648 Car - two hard X-ray bright symbiotic stars. Our primary goal is to determine the white dwarf mass and accretion rate. These quantities have important implications for the frequency of nova outbursts and therefore the long term evolution of the white dwarfs in these systems. Although in principle X-ray spectra of CVs and symbiotic stars encode information about the white dwarf mass, the effects of reflection from the white dwarf surface must be taken into account to fully realize this potential. Only NuSTAR has the sensitivity above 10 keV to detect and assess the importance of reflection while independently measuring the intrinsic spectral shape in our target sources.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nelson                        Thomas                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       A Reflection On Symbiotic Stars - Using Reflected X-Rays To Constrain Properties Of The White Dwarf And Accretion Disk  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MRK_926                                           @u¢±[W>´¿!pñªò«„@O“Ç@∑Ä4¿MU(åÁ∞@st‚ÎC-@Ï.#±!´L@Ï.$îØ‰060201029001@f!ó$tSè@f:)«yß@g®␀␀␀␀␀@g®␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›VŸ±ﬂ␀␀‚ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀AWe propose to observe Mrk 926 for 35 ks with XMM and 100 ks with NuSTAR to explore the Comptonization spectrum of this bright Seyfert 1 galaxy. Previous analyses of non-simultaneous observations have found a lower limit of the high energy cut-off of 510 keV, bringing it in disagreement by the temperature limit set by run-away pair production. With the proposed observations we will test, whether Mrk 926's spectrum is consistent with thermal Comptonization or not. If it is, we can study its Comptonization spectrum from the UV to the hard X-rays and would obtain key information on the properties of coronae typical for Seyferts. However, if we find that the temperature is inconsistent with thermal Comptonization, we can use this dataset to understand which other process could be responsible.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lohfink                       Anne                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Exploring The Corona Of Mrk 926                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429MRK_926                                           @u¢õ•„S¯¿!p£◊
=q@O—≠B√…Ô¿MT•zxl"@ss=Ÿb∑@Ï.$îØ‰0@Ï.s±!´L60201029002@˘Ìï¸π#£@˘›°∆?@˚„!4m≈÷@˚‰—4m≈÷SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›Æ,¿™␀␀‚ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀AWe propose to observe Mrk 926 for 35 ks with XMM and 100 ks with NuSTAR to explore the Comptonization spectrum of this bright Seyfert 1 galaxy. Previous analyses of non-simultaneous observations have found a lower limit of the high energy cut-off of 510 keV, bringing it in disagreement by the temperature limit set by run-away pair production. With the proposed observations we will test, whether Mrk 926's spectrum is consistent with thermal Comptonization or not. If it is, we can study its Comptonization spectrum from the UV to the hard X-rays and would obtain key information on the properties of coronae typical for Seyferts. However, if we find that the temperature is inconsistent with thermal Comptonization, we can use this dataset to understand which other process could be responsible.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lohfink                       Anne                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Exploring The Corona Of Mrk 926                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MAXIJ0911m635                                     @aAÆz·H¿P6v»¥9X@q°ÙÿD–¿&lãCïÅ@e•Å◊€Ùà@Ï.s±!´L@Ï.tîØ‰090201042001@AËoiDg8@A÷»¥9X@C@␀␀␀␀␀@C@␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›p[∞␀␀·ÅHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429MAXIJ0911m635                                     @aB1&Èx’¿P6a|⁄Q@q¢ìtº¿&iÖoiD@eÆYJÙÿ@Ï.tîØ‰0@Ï.áîØ‰090201042002@‡{ü˙C˛]@‡tÛú‡u˜@·¬zêó@·√RzêóSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›∫òv␀␀·ÅHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/2) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429CXO_J092418d2m314217                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï.áîØ‰0@Ï.àw␀°30201014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›Ù∑èΩ␀␀‚ÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀*In our systematic search for serendipitous sources (serendips) in the NuSTAR data, we have found a very bright (7.5 mCrab in the 2-10 keV band) serendip (J0923 s1) near the edge of one of the NuSTAR fields of view. The source has been previously detected in the soft X-ray band, but its nature is unknown. Based on its X-ray spectrum, its optical counterpart, and its long-term variability (as seen by MAXI), it must be Galactic, and, while some of its properties are suggestive of a magnetic Cataclysmic Variable, other properties cause problems with this interpretation. One interesting possibility is that it may be an accreting black hole with a high temperature disk. We propose NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations to determine the nature of J0923 s1.                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Determining The Nature Of The Brightest Serendipitous NuSTAR Source                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXO_J092418d2m314217                              @a£ökP∞Ú¿?≤úwökQ@p6ﬂ§?Â…@*dZ¨1@f—∑X‚@Ï.àw␀°@Ï.üw␀°30201014002@ﬂ • [¿@ﬂΩ#ﬂ;dZ@·7x“àŒp@·98“àŒpSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›ù3ıa␀␀‚ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀*In our systematic search for serendipitous sources (serendips) in the NuSTAR data, we have found a very bright (7.5 mCrab in the 2-10 keV band) serendip (J0923 s1) near the edge of one of the NuSTAR fields of view. The source has been previously detected in the soft X-ray band, but its nature is unknown. Based on its X-ray spectrum, its optical counterpart, and its long-term variability (as seen by MAXI), it must be Galactic, and, while some of its properties are suggestive of a magnetic Cataclysmic Variable, other properties cause problems with this interpretation. One interesting possibility is that it may be an accreting black hole with a high temperature disk. We propose NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations to determine the nature of J0923 s1.                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsic