@diÌ˙C˛]¿Lí-V@rµe+”√a@Ï/è[ÃU˜@Ï/≥?Zé⁄90201043002@ËÁ≈÷8Ü@Ë’xOv_Ÿ@Í•Û5®Xy@Íß≥5®XySCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›0*z␀␀·ÅHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SWIFT_J0128d9m6039                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï/≥?Zé⁄@Ï/≥Í9Ö60201035001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›␀ﬂ√Qä␀␀‚ÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SWIFT_J0128d9m6039                                @6GX‚e,¿NU‡qv@rne+”√¿KÂ?|Ìëh@r␀° ¿É@Ï/≥Í9Ö@Ï/√Õìrh60201035002@ÿf∫∞ ƒú@ÿZä6‚Î@⁄É!-w2@⁄É!-w2SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›␀ô‚jÛ␀␀‚ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429WR_140                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï/√Õìrh@Ï/ƒÕìrh30202027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï›␀q3ı␀␀„CHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≤The wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse-Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016 Dec., a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and answer questions about the abrupt X-ray flux decrease.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Colliding Wind X-Ray Emission From The Massive Binary Wr140 Around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR_140                                            @sºj~˘€@EÍ∂Æ}Vm@T3¸π#¢ú@„n.±ƒ3@rY"–Â`B@Ï/ƒÕìrh@Ï/”x>30202027002@’Súwök@’CËŸË>B@÷√Ñ—∑Y@÷√Ñ—∑YSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï›␀0º”␀␀„CHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≤The wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse-Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016 Dec., a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and answer questions about the abrupt X-ray flux decrease.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguch