¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï1T?Zé⁄@Ï1T±!´L30202035001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹˘FZCÇ␀␀‚ˇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÁOur goal is to test the nature of the compact object in NGC 300 X-1, one of only a trio of likely Wolf-Rayet, black hole systems using NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. As a primary challenge, we will test whether a neutron star model is compatible with the observations. If, as expected, we confirm that the compact primary must be a black hole, then we will analyze its spin as a function of mass and inclination. NuSTAR will firmly anchor the Compton component thereby allowing us to accurately disentangle the Compton and thermal emission. Without high-energy coverage, no meaningful constraint is possible. Accordingly, this science is uniquely feasible using NuSTAR and XMM-Newton in concert.                                                                                                                 Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Revealing The Compact Object In Ngc 300 X-1                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_300_X1                                        @+éÊ1¯†ê¿B‹I∫^5?@rΩC,•z¿S”a@N•@sQÇ©ì@Ï1T±!´L@Ï1œ[ÃU˜30202035002AÈ¢‚ÎCAﬁWOAÚAWŒLÃÃÕAZ∂LÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹¯j€6<␀␀‚ˇHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÁOur goal is to test the nature of the compact object in N