ose to the black hole that shield remote regions from ionising radiation. We witnessed such an event in NGC 5548 where 90% of the soft X-rays are blocked by a dense gas stream close to the BLR. Our joint observations with XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and HST/COS showed UV BAL lines associated with the X-ray absorption and allowed us to characterise this unique obscuration event completely. We propose to investigate a similar event in another Seyfert 1 using the same instruments. Swift monitoring will be used to find the event, which will be characterised by joint ToO observations with XMM-Newton (150 ks), HST/COS (4 orbits) and NuSTAR (50 ks).                                                                                                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaastra                       Jelle                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15        Shining light on obscured AGN outflows                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3783                                          @eŸ;dZ¨¿B›qué"@qÙîFsÅÿ@7␀™d¬¯8@fC,•z@Ï1Ó?Zé⁄@Ï1ˇ"Ë«æ80202006004@ÿ≈≠—N;Õ@ÿ∂I|⁄Q@⁄ë1ûÉ‰&@⁄ë1ûÉ‰&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹˜J∂Õè␀␀„Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀1AGN can be obscured by gas streams close to the black hole that shield remote regions from ionising radiation. We witnessed such an event in NGC 5548 where 90% of the soft X-rays are blocked by a dense gas stream close to the BLR. Our joint observations with XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and HST/COS showed UV BAL lines associated with the X-ray absorption and allowed us to characterise this unique obscuration event completely. We propose to investigate a similar event in another Seyfert 1 using the same instruments. Swift monitoring will be used to find the event, which will be characterised by joint ToO observations with XMM-Newton (150 ks), HST/COS (4 orbits) and NuSTAR (50 ks).                                                                                                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaastra                       Jelle                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15        Shining light on obscured AGN outflows                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_J2339m0533                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï1ˇ"Ë«æ@Ï2␀?Zé⁄30202020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹˜ m:␀␀„Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀nIn the past two years, three pulsar binaries have shown surprising back-and-forth transitions between an accreting low mass X-ray binary (LMXB) state and a non-accreting radio millisecond pulsar (MSP) state. These transitional MSPs (tMSPs) overturned the idea that accretion in an LMXB switches off permanently in a single occurence during pulsar `recycling', but it is unclear how/why the transitions happen. PSR J2339-0533 is a likely tMSP with spin and orbit parameters very similar to the known tMSPs and shows signs of intermittent accretion, orbital period and lightcurve variations. These signs suggest that PSR J2339-0533 may imminently transition into an LMXB. With 150-ks NuSTAR and 100-ks XMM observations, we propose to establish the pre-transition properties, critical for future work.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tendulkar                     Shriharsh                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Intra-Binary Shock And Energetics Of Transitional Binary Candidate Psr J2339 0533                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J2339m0533                                    @v.oiDg8¿ES&¡æ@T5„S˜ŒŸ¿O7_o“Û@swfœAÚ@Ï2␀?Zé⁄@Ï2~?Zé⁄30202020002A˙42a|AÁ£|⁄QAvWökP±AwØökP±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹ˆ4næc␀␀„Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀nIn the past two years, three pulsar binaries have shown surprising back-and-forth transitions between an accreting low mass X-ray binary (LMXB) state and a non-accreting radio millisecond pulsar (MSP) state. These transitional MSPs (tMSPs) overturned the idea that accretion in an LMXB switches off permanently in a single occurence during pulsar `recycling', but it is unclear how/why the transitions happen. PSR J2339-0533 is a likely tMSP with spin and orbit parameters very similar to the known tMSPs and shows signs of intermittent accretion, orbital period and lightcurve variations. These signs suggest that PSR J2339-0533 may imminently transition into an LMXB. With 150-ks NuSTAR and 100-ks XMM observations, we propose to establish the pre-transition properties, critical for future work.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tendulkar                     Shriharsh                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Intra-Binary Shock And Energetics Of Transitional Binary Candidate Psr J2339 0533                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429WR_140                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï2~?Zé⁄@Ï2~Í9Ö30202027003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹ıÏ¡l␀␀„CHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≤The wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse-Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016 Dec., a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and answer questions about the abrupt X-ray flux decrease.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Colliding Wind X-Ray Emission From The Massive Binary Wr140 Around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR_140                                            @sÌ(åÁ@EÍÁ’fœB@T4`™d¬¯@‹˙¨ŸË>@qX‚e@Ï2~Í9Ö@Ï2éÍ9Ö30202027004@◊4Æ§®¡U@◊)m/üæ@ÿø—è≈Å@ÿ¬è≈ÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹ıß“}(␀␀„CHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≤The wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse-Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016 Dec., a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and answer questions about the abrupt X-ray flux decrease.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Colliding Wind X-Ray Emission From The Massive Binary Wr140 Around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(2/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429GRS_1716m249                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï2éÍ9Ö@Ï2èÍ9Ö80201034001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹ı~¨/␀␀‚ZHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀îWe propose to undertake a 50 ks NuSTAR target-of-opportunity observation of a new or known black hole transient during a bright, hard outburst phase. The goals of this program are to 1) measure the spin of the black hole via the Fe K-alpha line and broadband disk reflection spectrum, 2) test an apparent anti-correlation between winds and jets in soft and hard states by making a sensitive search for winds in a hard state. A network of ground-based observatories, and X-ray monitoring will support this observation. The NuSTAR-only program proposed here is designed to complement our approved XMM-Newton+NuSTAR campaign (PI: Harrison) to cover sources that are not visible to XMM-Newton during outburst.                                                                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Bright Hard State Of A Transient Black Hole Binary - Is The Accretion Disk Truncated?                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1716m249                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï2èÍ9Ö@Ï2£w␀°80201034002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹ÙÛıax␀␀‚ZHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1         