 bright Swift source, Swift J0042.6+4112, that dominates M31's emission at E> 25 keV. We propose new simultaneous observations with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton one year later to (A) properly constrain the simultaneous 0.5-80 keV spectrum of this source with higher S/N and by removing ambiguities due to its significant E<  10 keV variability and (B) look for variability in its hard X-ray emission since AO1. We will also monitor ~30 X-ray binaries in the field, one of the richest local environments for old stellar populations outside the Galaxy, to provide better constraints on the nature of the resolved point sources (i.e., black hole binaries vs. neutron star binaries).              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Yukita                        Mihoko                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Hard X-Ray Pulsar Candidate That May Dominate M31 At E> 25 Kev: NuSTAR-XMM Observations Of The M31 Bulge            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SWIFT_J0042d6p4112                                @%?;dZ¨@DöT`™d√@^;Å◊€Ùà¿5∏r∞ ƒú@s¸PH@Ï5óÍM@Ï5ﬁ±9Ò50201001002@˘/∑uˆ˝"@˘õ„º”@˙ÚY5?|Ó@˙Û5?|ÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹Ê°ên␀␀„ Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÖDuring our AO1 NuSTAR observation of the M31 bulge, we identified for the first time the counterpart to the single bright Swift source, Swift J0042.6+4112, that dominates M31's emission at E> 25 keV. We propose new simultaneous observations with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton one year later to (A) properly constrain the simultaneous 0.5-80 keV spectrum of this source with higher S/N and by removing ambiguities due to its significant E<  10 keV variability and (B) look for variability in its hard X-ray emission since AO1. We will also monitor ~30 X-ray binaries in the field, one of the richest local environments for old stellar populations outside the Galaxy, to provide better constraints on the nature of the resolved point sources (i.e., black hole binaries vs. neutron star binaries).              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Yukita                        Mihoko                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Hard X-Ray Pulsar Candidate That May Dominate M31 At E> 25 Kev: NuSTAR-XMM Observations Of The M31 Bulge            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Solar flare C9.3 2017:021 0714Z                                                                                         archived  20200429NGC_3227                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï5ﬁ±9Ò@Ï5ﬂÕ´∏160202002013␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹Êe[f«␀␀„Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀1¨NGC 3227 has it all: rapid X-ray variability, a negative lag, variable Fe K emission and variable absorption. However, no observing campaign to date has been constructed to optimally use the timing and spectroscopic signatures of the reprocessor together to map the X-ray gas. We propose to combine 320 ks on XMM and 160 ks on Nustar to isolate reverberation signatures from the circumnuclear gas. Our proposed methodology will allow us to measure directly in the light curve the actual reverberation delay, the timescale of the delay and the shape of the "transfer function", which give an unambiguous model independent constraints on the reverberating gas. This gas is key to understanding the accretion process and the co-evolution of galaxies and their nuclear black holes.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Turner                        Tracey                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Mapping the X-ray reprocessor in NGC 3227                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3227                                          @c|åÁØ∏@3„ä	ﬁ@k/É{J#@K§„º”ZÜ@c¸£n.≤@Ï5ﬂÕ´∏1@Ï6èFj60202002014@Á>O	kπå@Á.·ˆîF@ÈH¿Éo@È»¿ÉoSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹Ê␀E…␀␀„Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀1¨NGC 3227 has it all: rapid X-ray variability, a negative lag, variable Fe K emission and variable absorption. However, no observing campaign to date has been constructed to optimally use the timing and spectroscopic signatures of the reprocessor together to map the X-ray gas. We propose to combine 320 ks on XMM and 160 ks on Nustar to isolate reverberation signatures from the circumnuclear gas. Our proposed methodology will allow us to measure directly in the light curve the actual reverberation delay, the timescale of the delay and the shape of the "transfer function", which give an unambiguous model independent constraints on the reverberating gas. This gas is key to understanding the accretion process and the co-evolution of galaxies and their nuclear black holes.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Turner                        Tracey                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Mapping the X-ray reprocessor in NGC 3227                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk813                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï6èFj@Ï6±9Ò60160583001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹ÂœWú␀␀·≤Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk813                                            @k#9¿ÎÓ@3◊1è≈Å@3Ω\˙¨ŸË@P¬¬&ÄùI@e•å~($@Ï6±9Ò@Ï6ÍM60160583002@◊¸fè\(ˆ@◊ÌÃcÒA@Ÿ›ËxG@Ÿ„®xGSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹ÂÉ ÁY␀␀·≤Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MRK_110                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï6ÍM@Ï6î»)¯60201025001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹ÂH—Y‚␀␀„-Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose to observe Mrk 110 with NuSTAR for 200 ksec. We will characterize the spectral properties including reflection fraction, coronal height, and high-energy cutoff in this radio-quiet AGN. The aim is to compare it to radio-loud AGN, such as 3C 120, in order to understand what are the primary differences in the corona that may drive variations in jet production. In particular, Mrk 110, has the same mass and mass accretion rate as 3C 120, yet its jet is suppressed by nearly three orders of magnitude in the radio band. Characterizing the coronal geometry, temperature, and spin will allow exploration as to other mechanisms that may drive the radio dichotomy.                                                                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    King                          Ashley                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Investigating The X-Ray Corona In The Agn Mrk 110                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRK_110                                           @a®˝Û∂E¢@J&ßÔù≤-@dû_ÿ≠´ü@F⁄QŒ@UNöu@Ï6î»)¯@Ï6®ÍM60201025002AáóÚ‰éäA@≤˛≈mAb—∏QÎÖAgq∏QÎÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹‰"≥ƒ÷␀␀„-Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose to observe Mrk 110 with NuSTAR for 200 ksec. We will characterize the spectral properties including reflection fraction, coronal height, and high-energy cutoff in this radio-quiet AGN. The aim is to compare it to radio-loud AGN, such as 3C 120, in order to understand what are the primary differences in the corona that may drive variations in jet production. In particular, Mrk 110, has the same mass and mass accretion rate as 3C 120, yet its jet is suppressed by nearly three orders of magnitude in the radio band. Characterizing the coronal geometry, temperature, and spin will allow exploration as to other mechanisms that may drive the radio dichotomy.                                                                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    King                          Ashley                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Investigating The X-Ray Corona In The Agn Mrk 110                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SADA_2017_028_53                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï6®ÍM@Ï6™èFj10201002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹„¬ß°Ú␀␀‚wHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1716m249                                      @p?ÑM;¿8ˇ.HËß?≠ä⁄πıY¥@∏}øHÄ@câS&¡æ@Ï6™èFj@Ï6÷Õ´∏180201034007@Â)GOﬂ;d@Â¢€—N;Õ@Ô/"QÎÖ@Ô/"QÎÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹·‘€¯j␀␀‚wHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀îWe propose to undertake a 50 ks NuSTAR target-of-opportunity observation of a new or known black hole transient during a bright, hard outburst phase. The goals of this program are to 1) measure the spin of the black hole via the Fe K-alpha line and broadband disk reflection spectrum, 2) test an apparent anti-correlation between winds and jets in soft and hard states by making a sensitive search for winds in a hard state. A network of ground-based observatories, and X-ray monitoring will support this observation. The NuSTAR-only program proposed here is designed to complement our approved XMM-Newton+NuSTAR campaign (PI: Harrison) to cover sources that are not visible to XMM-Newton during outburst.                                                                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Bright Hard State Of A Transient Black Hole Binary - Is The Accretion Disk Truncated?                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀SADA constraint violation / Solar flare C2.8 2017:028:2056Z                                                             archived  20200429IGRJ16181m5407                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï6÷Õ´∏1@Ï6◊?r‘£30161006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹·úØ˙␀␀·¿Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harriso