ey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SN2016jbu                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï97î»)¯@Ï98[‰õø80202029001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹÷´lÿ␀␀„≠Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀kContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR-XMM effort to, but it is unclear how/why the transitions happen. PSR J2339-0533 is a likely tMSP with spin and orbit parameters very similar to the known tMSPs and shows signs of intermittent accretion, orbital period and lightcurve variations. These signs suggest that PSR J2339-0533 may imminently transition into an LMXB. With 150-ks NuSTAR and 100-ks XMM observations, we propose to establish the pre-transition properties, critical for future woProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Detailed Mapping Of Extreme Mass Loss With Coordinated NuSTAR-XMM Observations                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2016jbu                                         @\à⁄QŒ¿Qd—∑X‚@qî¯†ê-‡¿5}w1è≈@qì8Ô4÷°@Ï98[‰õø@Ï9Rî»)¯80202029002@„®"⁄Q@„°•Uõ=@Â%g≤˛≈m@Â'≤˛≈mSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹÷VÒ˜®␀␀„≠Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀kContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR-XMM effort to, but it is unclear how/why the transitions happen. PSR J2339-0533 is a likely tMSP with spin and orbit parameters very similar to the known tMSPs and shows signs of intermittent accretion, orbital period and lightcurve variations. These signs suggest that PSR J2339-0533 may imminently transition into an LMXB. With 150-ks NuSTAR and 100-ks XMM observations, we propose to establish the pre-transition properties, critical for future woProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Detailed Mapping Of Extreme Mass Loss With Coordinated NuSTAR-XMM Observations                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429GRO_J1744m28                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï9Rî»)¯@Ï9S[‰õø80202027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹÷,wÿè␀␀„ûHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀^Cyclotron resonant scattering features (CRSFs or cyclotron lines) are the only direct way to measure the B-field close to the surface of an accreting neutron star and probe the physics in the accretion column. Here we proposed 3 ToO observations at fluxes of 20mCrab, 100mCrab, and 200mCrab of a known CRSF source in outburst to study its behavior as function of luminosity. NuSTAR is the only instrument to be able to measure the energy with the necessary precision and at the same time allows us to study the CRSF profile. These measurements will inform physical models of the line forming processes and constrain the accretion physics and geometry close to the neutron star. In a parallel proposal we ask to observe a CRSF candidate to discover new CRSF sources.                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Cyclotron Lines In Transient Pulsars I: Probing The B-Field                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRO_J1744m28                                      @p¢Æ}VlÙ¿<¥M:í£@vàŒp:˚?‡öu%@c"Ô4÷°b@Ï9S[‰õø@Ï9hxVb‹80202027002@‹%Æ=p£◊@‹=␀␀␀␀@ﬂÉHÀí@ﬂCHÀíSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹’–W;␀␀„ûHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀^Cyclotron resonant scattering features (CRSFs or cyclotron lines) are the only direct way to measure the B-field close to the surface of an accreting neutron star and probe the physics in the accretion column. Here we proposed 3 ToO observations at fluxes of 20mCrab, 100mCrab, and 200mCrab of a known CRSF source in outburst to study its behavior as function of luminosity. NuSTAR is the only instrument to be able to measure the energy with the necessary precision and at the same time allows us to study the CRSF profile. These measurements will inform physical models of the line forming processes and constrain the accretion physics and geometry close to the neutron star. In a parallel proposal we ask to observe a CRSF candidate to discover new CRSF sources.                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Cyclotron Lines In Transient Pulsars I: Probing The B-Field                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/3) ToO  