uring the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï9ƒÕ´∏1@Ï9≈?r‘£90202045001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹“óõ‡$␀␀·⁄Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rrGE8Ô@%Ï"h	‘ï@F£›óˆ+kø∂ö‘,<ûÌ@f†'RTa@Ï9≈?r‘£@Ï9÷èFj90202045002@À'EÑM;@ÀæÛéÛMj@’ÎxLò_@’Ì¯Lò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹—Ã;*␀␀·⁄Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2) DDT                                                                                                               archived  20200429WR_140                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï9÷èFj@Ï9÷î»)¯30202027007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹—§ïœ␀␀„CHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≤The wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse-Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016 Dec., a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and answer questions about the abrupt X-ray flux decrease.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Colliding Wind X-Ray Emission From The Massive Binary Wr140 Around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR_140                                            @sz·GÆ@EÏ%ÆÊ1˘@T5fœAÚ@–∞Ú{≤ˇ@jÒA [¿@Ï9÷î»)¯@Ï9ÁèFj30202027008@ÿÑF“àŒp@ÿsìtº@⁄3 ƒõ¶@⁄2H ƒõ¶SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹—Z§ô9␀␀„CHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≤The wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. Joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observation of WR 140 in AO14 discovered an extremely hard X-ray component, which can originate either from a kT ~13 keV plasma or inverse-Compton scattering. WR 140 will experience its periastron passage in 2016 Dec., a critical time when the WWC emission changes dramatically. We propose joint NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations of WR 140 at key phases around periastron in AO15, to determine the origin of this component and answer questions about the abrupt X-ray flux decrease.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Colliding Wind X-Ray Emission From The Massive Binary Wr140 Around Periastron                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀         