                            @Z#€Ùá¸π¿K¸9XbN@pü¯7¥¢4¿5Tº£@rıéäqﬁj@Ï:ö±9Ò@Ï:ú[‰õø10202003035@§Ái≠B√ @§⁄ŒVì@¶PåŸË>B@¶PåŸË>BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï:°ÏêWˇˇˇùHea_13Jan2017_V6.20_nustardas_06Dec16_v1.7.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             processed 20161207MRK_421                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï:ú[‰õø@Ï:ùî»)¯60202048005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹–äm¸5␀␀„nHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀âMulti-year and multi-frequency monitoring of the classical TeV blazars Mrk 421 and Mrk 501 is an important resource in the effort to understand the physics of relativistic jets. These objects are among the very small number of sources whose broadband SED can be characterized simultaneously by the currently existing instruments. We request NuSTAR observations as a part of two planned campaigns in 2016 and 2017 on these two objects. Only NuSTAR is sensitive enough to provide time-resolved hard X-ray spectroscopy on the short timescale over which the proposed targets vary. NuSTAR is therefore ideally suited to complement the UV and soft X-ray observations with Swift, and the VHE gamma-ray observations with MAGIC and VERITAS, in constraining the rapid variability of the targets' broadband SED.Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paneque                       David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Extensive Multi-Frequency Monitoring Of The Classical Tev Blazars Mrk 421 And Mrk 501                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRK_421                                           @d¬¬&ÄùI@Cµ‹∆?@fw€"–Â`@P3J#9¿Ï@R5?|Ìëh@Ï:ùî»)¯@Ï:ØÍM60202048006@◊XÇÌ(ç@◊Y˜e˝ã@Ÿ´¬zêó@ŸØ¬zêóSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹–<ñ/…␀␀„nHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀âMulti-year and multi-frequency monitoring of the classical TeV blazars Mrk 421 and Mrk 501 is an important resource in the effort to understand the physics of relativistic jets. These objects are among the very small number of sources whose broadband SED can be characterized simultaneously by the currently existing instruments. We request NuSTAR observations as a part of two planned campaigns in 2016 and 2017 on these two objects. Only NuSTAR is sensitive enough to provide time-resolved hard X-ray spectroscopy on the short timescale over which the proposed targets vary. NuSTAR is therefore ideally suited to complement the UV and soft X-ray observations with Swift, and the VHE gamma-ray observations with MAGIC and VERITAS, in constraining the rapid variability of the targets' broadband SED.Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paneque                       David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Extensive Multi-Frequency Monitoring Of The Classical Tev Blazars Mrk 421 And Mrk 501                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     MAGIC                                                                                               ␀␀␀(3/4) / Kp=5                                                                                                            archived  20200429SDSS_J084905d43p111450d9                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï:ØÍM@Ï:∞±9Ò60201060001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹–€¯j␀␀„EHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    XWDespite decades of searching, confirmed cases of dual AGNs are extremely rare, and most have been dis- covered serendipitously. Using the all-sky WISE survey, we identified a population of over one hundred advanced mergers that display mid-IR colors suggestive of AGNs despite having no signatures of activity in the optical. Expanding on our pilot program, which confirmed dual AGNs in 5 targets, we propose 1) longer exposure Chandra observations of 4 of the duals to confirm all tentative detections and obtain sufficient counts for a spectral analysis, and 2) NuSTAR observations of the one dual with sufficient counts for which our current Chandra observations show a prominent Fe K alpha line, consistent with a Compton-thick source.                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Satyapal                      S                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       WISE Discovery of the Largest Sample of Obscured Dual AGNs: A Chandra and NuSTAR Follow-up                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSS_J084905d43p111450d9                          @`àV’œ™Œ@&rΩ<64@j˚g†˘	l@>˘„º”@u:Àí:)«@Ï:∞±9Ò@Ï:∆#Ü60201060002@›Ë›ÑM;@›—§?Â…@ﬂˆvi≠Bƒ@ﬂ˜vi≠BƒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹œŒ ù∆␀␀„EHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  XWDespite decades of searching, confirmed cases of dual AGNs are extremely rare, and most have been dis- covered serendipitously. Using the all-sky WISE survey, we identified a population of over one hundred advanced mergers that display mid-IR colors suggestive of AGNs despite having no signatures of activity in the optical. Expanding on our pilot program, which confirmed dual AGNs in 5 targets, we propose 1) longer exposure Chandra observations of 4 of the duals to confirm all tentative detections and obtain sufficient counts for a spectral analysis, and 2) NuSTAR observations of the one dual with sufficient counts for which our current Chandra observations show a prominent Fe K alpha line, consistent with a Compton-thick source.                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars    