 the spiral galaxy contains a Compton-thick AGN in a luminosity regime where the AGN torus is predicted to vanish. Intense Fe Kalpha emission revealed by deep Chandra observations contradicts this prediction, but a deep NuSTAR observation will uncover the true covering factor of the torus through observation of the Compton hump. A ULX in M51a shows the properties of an IMBH in the low/hard state, but only the combined broadband coverage from Chandra and NuSTAR can confirm if the hard spectrum continues above 10 keV, indicative of an Eddington-limited accretor.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brightman                     M                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       A Deep broadband study of the diverse population of X-ray sources in M51 with Chandra and NuSTAR                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M51                                               @iMëhr∞!@GõCïÅ%@ZVQŒ_@Q!£n.±ƒ@XŸË>B[@Ï<îxVb‹@Ï<∂î»)¯60201062002@Á@˘€"—@Á␀èÀí:*@Ë•zŸá+@Ë®öŸá+SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹»Ÿ‚jÛ␀␀„THea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  YWe propose simultaneous 40-ks Chandra and 100-ks NuSTAR observations of the interacting galaxies of M51 that will shed light on a range of unique X-ray sources with diverse astrophysical implications. The nucleus of the spiral galaxy contains a Compton-thick AGN in a luminosity regime where the AGN torus is predicted to vanish. Intense Fe Kalpha emission revealed by deep Chandra observations contradicts this prediction, but a deep NuSTAR observation will uncover the true covering factor of the torus through observation of the Compton hump. A ULX in M51a shows the properties of an IMBH in the low/hard state, but only the combined broadband coverage from Chandra and NuSTAR can confirm if the hard spectrum continues above 10 keV, indicative of an Eddington-limited accretor.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brightman                     M                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       A Deep broadband study of the diverse population of X-ray sources in M51 with Chandra and NuSTAR                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M51                                               @iMÿ≠´üV@GûL/É{J@ZW␀h€ã¨@Q ìtºj@XÄ4m≈÷@Ï<∂î»)¯@Ï=.î»)¯60201062003AÁ°GE8ÔA‹ú∑È˘AKB£S&AM‚£S&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹«˛ìÈ?␀␀„THea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  YWe propose simultaneous 40-ks Chandra and 100-ks NuSTAR observations of the interacting galaxies of M51 that will shed light on a range of unique X-ray sources with diverse astrophysical implications. The nucleus of the spiral galaxy contains a Compton-thick AGN in a luminosity regime where the AGN torus is predicted to vanish. Intense Fe Kalpha emission revealed by deep Chandra observations contradicts this prediction, but a deep NuSTAR observation will uncover the true covering factor of the torus through observation of the Compton hump. A ULX in M51a shows the properties of an IMBH in the low/hard state, but only the combined broadband coverage from Chandra and NuSTAR can confirm if the hard spectrum continues above 10 keV, indicative of an Eddington-limited accretor.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brightman                     M                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       A Deep broadband study of the diverse population of X-ray sources in M51 with Chandra and NuSTAR                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Adjustment of desired location                                                                                          archived  20200429Sol_SADA_17080                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï=.î»)¯@Ï=/±9Ò20201008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹«±ﬂb:␀␀·ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will be able to observe solar hard X-ray sources 200 times fainter than our current capabilities allow. This increase in sensitivity will open the door to truly pioneering observations, providing diagnostics for solar flare and, in particular, for nanoflare observations. Almost as important is NuSTAR's advantage in dynamic range, the ability to see a faint source (e.g. coronal source at the reconnection point) at the same time as a brighter one (e.g. source at the magnetic footpoint of a flare).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀SOL       Solar Observat