 legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Extragalactic Legacy Survey / Kp=5                                                                                      archived  20200429PDS_456                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï=fèFj@Ï=g[‰õø60201020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹¬T %␀␀„nHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀PDS 456, the most luminous nearby AGN, is now recognised as the prototypical example of an ultra fast outflow. Indeed our 2013 XMM-Newton and NuSTAR campaign established the presence of a fast (0.3c), wide angle accretion disk wind, with the wind power sufficient to provide significant mechanical feedback into its host galaxy. Here we propose for a longer 180ks exposure of PDS456 with NuSTAR, which will reveal the true nature of the quasar at hard X-rays. The observations will also form part of a simultaneous multiwavelength campaign, encompassing approved XMM-Newton and HST/COS observations, as well as a likely PV phase observation with Astro-H. For the first time, we will obtain high resolution coverage of this important prototype disk wind over the entire UV to hard X-ray band.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Hard X-Ray View Of The Prototype Disk Wind Quasar Pds 456                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PDS_456                                           @paØOÑM¿,}øHÀí@$ú¨1&È@&∏«‚Ç@∏@czõ•„S¯@Ï=g[‰õø@Ï=ÿÕ´∏160201020002ANÊxl"hA6Ú1&ÈA®;qué"A®õqué"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‹¡zòvT␀␀„nHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀PDS 456, the most luminous nearby AGN, is now recognised as the prototypical example of an ultra fast outflow. Indeed our 2013 XMM-Newton and NuSTAR campaign established the presence of a fast (0.3c), wide angle accretion disk wind, with the wind power sufficient to provide significant mechanical feedback into its host galaxy. Here we propose for a longer 180ks exposure of PDS456 with NuSTAR, which will reveal the true nature of the quasar at hard X-rays. The observations will also form part of a simultaneous multiwavelength campaign, encompassing approved XMM-Newton and HST/COS observations, as well as a likely PV phase observation with Astro-H. For the first time, we will obtain high resolution coverage of this important prototype disk wind over the entire UV to hard X-ray band.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       The Hard X-Ray View Of The Prototype Disk Wind Quasar Pds 456                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM HST                                                                                             ␀␀␀ Kp=5                                                                                                                   archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï=ÿÕ´∏1@Ï=Ÿ?r‘£90202045003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‹¡#æƒu␀␀‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison     