ke (NL) Cataclysmic Variables (CVs) for a total of 100 ks with the NuSTAR observatory. NLs are high state CVs, with wind outflows and are laboratories to study the effects of high accretion rate on accretion physics and boundary layers. We observed these NLs with SWIFT and found virialized hot plasma emission, not expected in the high state of steady-state disks. They can be characterised as advective hot flows (ADAF-like). Such hot flows and advective heating of WDs are puzzling and important for CV accretion which may influence evolution, and nova explosions. NuSTAR will further our insights on the X-ray emission from these hardest NL sources utilizing the highest sensitivity in the widest energy band.                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Balman                        Solen                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Characterization Of The X-Ray Emission And Boundary Layers In Nonmagnetic Nova-Like Cataclysmic Variables               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429BZ_Cam                                            @XW§?Â…@QƒF‹]d@aÒ©*0U2@7®çπ@seÕûÉ‰&@ÏB[‰õø@ÏB$?r‘£30201010002@‚rS›óˆ+@‚fQÎÖ@„∂{•ú‡@„∏˚•ú‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄Õ‡TÙ>␀␀„ÇHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀We propose pointed observations of three nonmagnetic nova-like (NL) Cataclysmic Variables (CVs) for a total of 100 ks with the NuSTAR observatory. NLs are high state CVs, with wind outflows and are laboratories to study the effects of high accretion rate on accretion physics and boundary layers. We observed these NLs with SWIFT and found virialized hot plasma emission, not expected in the high state of steady-state disks. They can be characterised as advective hot flows (ADAF-like). Such hot flows and advective heating of WDs are puzzling and important for CV accretion which may influence evolution, and nova explosions. NuSTAR will further our insights on the X-ray emission from these hardest NL sources utilizing the highest sensitivity in the widest energy band.                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Balman                        Solen                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Characterization Of The X-Ray Emission And Boundary Layers In Nonmagnetic Nova-Like Cataclysmic Variables               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429TXS_1508p572                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏB$?r‘£@ÏB$Õ´∏160201013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄ÕµI2q␀␀„ÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀€MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses, are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift blazars ever detected by Fermi. We will complement the NuSTAR exposures with observations with Fermi, Swift, GROND and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class.                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Chasing The Most Powerful Blazars                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_1508p572                                      @lo;dZ¨@Lá¸π#¢ú@W[º”ZÖà@I≠≤-V@RúÂ`Aâ7@ÏB$Õ´∏1@ÏB@î»)¯60201013002@‚␀␀—∑@·ÛYn.±ƒ@„<ÒîØO@„>QîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄Õe*€6␀␀„ÇHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀€MeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses, are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration mechanism, the bulk Lorentz factor, and the emission process. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift blazars ever detected by Fermi. We will complement the NuSTAR exposures with observations with Fermi, Swift, GROND and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class.                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello               