 Mrk 421 and Mrk 501 is an important resource in the effort to understand the physics of relativistic jets. These objects are among the very small number of sources whose broadband SED can be characterized simultaneously by the currently existing instruments. We request NuSTAR observations as a part of two planned campaigns in 2016 and 2017 on these two objects. Only NuSTAR is sensitive enough to provide time-resolved hard X-ray spectroscopy on the short timescale over which the proposed targets vary. NuSTAR is therefore ideally suited to complement the UV and soft X-ray observations with Swift, and the VHE gamma-ray observations with MAGIC and VERITAS, in constraining the rapid variability of the targets' broadband SED.Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paneque                       David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Extensive Multi-Frequency Monitoring Of The Classical Tev Blazars Mrk 421 And Mrk 501                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRK_501                                           @oÆ,<ûÏ¿@C„6zêó@O—ƒ2 W®@C~’œ™Õü@S2GE8Ô5@ÏE=î»)¯@ÏEO±9Ò60202049004@◊G2BZÓc@◊7ôﬁ␀“@ÿÙD-‡@ÿ¯D-‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄∑(NÇ␀␀„†Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀âMulti-year and multi-frequency monitoring of the classical TeV blazars Mrk 421 and Mrk 501 is an important resource in the effort to understand the physics of relativistic jets. These objects are among the very small number of sources whose broadband SED can be characterized simultaneously by the currently existing instruments. We request NuSTAR observations as a part of two planned campaigns in 2016 and 2017 on these two objects. Only NuSTAR is sensitive enough to provide time-resolved hard X-ray spectroscopy on the short timescale over which the proposed targets vary. NuSTAR is therefore ideally suited to complement the UV and soft X-ray observations with Swift, and the VHE gamma-ray observations with MAGIC and VERITAS, in constraining the rapid variability of the targets' broadband SED.Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paneque                       David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Extensive Multi-Frequency Monitoring Of The Classical Tev Blazars Mrk 421 And Mrk 501                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              MAGIC VERITAS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_J1622m4950                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏEO±9Ò@ÏEPÍM80202051003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄∂˝@F␀␀„ïHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    KMagnetars are highly magnetized young neutron stars that are powered by the decay of their enormous magnetic fields. Magnetars exhibit dramatic variability including major outbursts - a hallmark of their emission. These outbursts allow us to probe the extreme physics at work in the Universe's largest known magnetic fields. Understanding the evolution of these outbursts will place stringent constraints on physical models of magnetars, including their crusts, cores, atmospheres, coronae, and magnetospheres. In this proposal, we request Chandra and NuSTAR Target-of-Opportunity observations of a major magnetar outburst in AO18 in order to put detailed physical models to the test, and as an anchor for multiwavelength follow-up.                                                                    Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaspi                         V M                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       Target-of-Oppurtunity Chandra Observations of Magnetars in Outburst                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J1622m4950                                    @n∑'ª0¿HÏ”ZÖáî@t€ÎC,•?¨C,•zxl@m˚"–Â`B@ÏEPÍM@ÏEÇèFj80202051004@Ò$›/@˜ˇ∞ ƒú@Ú,IökP±@Ú-ökP±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄∂è+ùe␀␀„ïHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  KMagnetars are highly magnetized young neutron stars that are powered by the decay of their enormous magnetic fields. Magnetars exhibit dramatic variability including major outbursts - a hallmark of their emission. These outbursts allow us to probe the extreme physics at work in the Universe's largest known magnetic fields. Understanding the evolution of these outbursts will place stringent constraints on physical models of magnetars, including their crusts, cores, atmospheres, coronae, and magnetospheres. In this proposal, we request Chandra and NuSTAR Target-of-Opportunity observations of a major magnetar outburst in AO18 in order to put detailed physical models to the test, and as an anchor for multiwavelength follow-up.                                                                    Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaspi                         V M                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       Target-of-Oppurtunity Chandra Observations of Magnetars in Outburst                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429CXO_J164710d2m455216                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏEÇèFj@ÏEÇ[‰õø80201050001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄∂\Î$␀␀„óHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXO_J164710d2m455216                              @ozè\(ı√¿F—∑X‚@u6ˇó$tTø—}øHÀí@l‡ﬁ“â@ÏEÇ[‰õø@ÏEê±9Ò80201050002@“±÷Ov`@“¶≠Œ_@‘M©ôôôö@‘QÈôôôöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄∂Ø˙␀␀„óHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429SWIFT_J2015d2p2526                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏEê±9Ò@ÏEë±9Ò60201032001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄µÒ~K␀␀„†Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SWIFT_J2015d2p2526                                @r˚ˇó$tT@9jπıY≥–@P7ÏV’œ´¿pç∏∫«@^8q≤ïÍ@ÏEë±9Ò@ÏE§±9Ò60201032002@€x”È˘r@€b)„S˜œ@›UC3333@›VC3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄µí}'“␀␀„†Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀UHeavily obscured accretion is believed to represent an extremely important phase in the growth of supermassive black holes, and to play an important role in shaping the observed spectrum of the Cosmic X-ray Background. So far however, only a small number of Compton-thick AGN are known. Our group recently carried out a detailed study of ~830 Swift/BAT AGN, finding CT absorption in 55 objects. We propose here to observe 28 local CT AGN with NuSTAR, with the aim of accurately constraining their column density and luminosity. This will allow us to sample the low and high-luminosity bins of CT accretion, as well as the NH> 5e24 cm^-2 range. With this we will study the luminosity-function of CT AGN, and the evolution of CT material with the physical characteristics of the accreting system.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Compton-Thick Accretion In The Local Universe With NuSTAR                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Kp=6                                                                                                                    archived  20200429SN2016jbu                                         @\Å≥–|Ñ∂¿Qb‚ÎC-@qîGE8Ô5¿5É9¿ÎÌ˙@3RàŒp:˚@ÏE§±9Ò@ÏE•Õ´∏180202029003@o5nóçP@o ,<ûÏ¿@qTÃcÒA @qTÃcÒA SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄µ9çvµ␀␀„≠Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀kContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR-XMM effort to, but it is unclear how/why the transitions happen. PSR J2339-0533 is a likely tMSP with spin and orbit parameters very similar to the known tMSPs and shows signs of intermittent accretion, orbital period and lightcurve variations. These signs suggest that PSR J2339-0533 may imminently transition into an LMXB. With 150-ks NuSTAR and 100-ks XMM observations, we propose to establish the pre-transition properties, critical for future woProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Detailed Mapping Of Extreme Mass Loss With Coordinated NuSTAR-XMM Observations                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429SN2016jbu                                         @\Ä|Ñµ‹∆¿Qbﬁ␀—∑@qî<64¿5Ñ…Öoi@3™d¬¯8@ÏE•Õ´∏1@ÏE #Ü80202029004@Û¡nÃcÒA@Û≥·ffff@ıDu%F@ıŸAÚ◊SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄¥∫O§˙␀␀„≠Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀kContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR-XMM effort to, but it is unclear how/why the transitions happen. PSR J2339-0533 is a likely tMSP with spin and orbit parameters very similar to the known tMSPs and shows signs of intermittent accretion, orbital period and lightcurve variations. These signs suggest that PSR J2339-0533 may imminently transition into an LMXB. With 150-ks NuSTAR and 100-ks XMM observations, we propose to establish the pre-transition properties, critical for future woProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N02       Detailed Mapping Of Extreme Mass Loss With Coordinated NuSTAR-XMM Observations                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(2/2) ToO / Kp=7                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS1329m049                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏE #Ü@ÏE ÍM60160541001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄¥äm¸5␀␀‚GHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars   