 limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2017eaw                                         @sKêñªô@N⁄QŒ@WÏìtºj@'rº£n@]QΩ•ú‡@ÏF*#Ü@ÏFKèFj90302004004@ÁP¯†ê-‡@ÁE8ºj~˙@ËÌ=E8Ô5@ËÒΩE8Ô5SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄≤Î$ñ␀␀‚9Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2) ToO                                                                                                               archived  202004291E_161348m5055                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏFKèFj@ÏFL?r‘£30301017001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄≤ªrÍb␀␀„¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀\1E 161348-5055 (1E 161348), the source at the center of the supernova remnant RCW103, has defied any easy classification since its discovery. Because of its puzzling behavior (a long periodicity of 6.67 hr and a strong variability) it is challenging to interpret it within the standard neutron-star scenarios. On June 2016, 1E 161348 emitted a magnetar-like millisecond burst of hard X-rays accompanied with a factor ~ 100 brightening in the persistent soft X-ray emission. This recent episode suggests that 1E 161348 is an isolated magnetar with an incredibly slow spin period. We ask for a new 70 ks observation with NuSTAR to characterize the nature and phenomenology of this unique source while it recovers from its recent outburst.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Borghese                      Alice                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE FIRST HARD X-RAY OBSERVATION OF THE PUZZLING SOURCE 1E 161348-5055 TOWARDS THE QUIESCENT STATE                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161E_161348m5055                                    @néI∫^5¿Iàl"h	’@tƒ¯†ê-‡øŒp:˚~ë␀@pÑµ‹∆?@ÏFL?r‘£@ÏF|Õ´∏130301017002@ué!ñ@Ô¯—HÀí@Ò&ä	@Ò(Oä	SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄≤N ÜB␀␀„¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀\1E 161348-5055 (1E 161348), the source at the center of the supernova remnant RCW103, has defied any easy classification since its discovery. Because of its puzzling behavior (a long periodicity of 6.67 hr and a strong variability) it is challenging to interpret it within the standard neutron-star scenarios. On June 2016, 1E 161348 emitted a magnetar-like millisecond burst of hard X-rays accompanied with a factor ~ 100 brightening in the persistent soft X-ray emission. This recent episode suggests that 1E 161348 is an isolated magnetar with an incredibly slow spin period. We ask for a new 70 ks observation with NuSTAR to characterize the nature and phenomenology of this unique source while it recovers from its recent outburst.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Borghese                      Alice                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE FIRST HARD X-RAY OBSERVATION OF THE PUZZLING SOURCE 1E 161348-5055 TOWARDS THE QUIESCENT STATE                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292HWC_J1928p178                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏF|Õ´∏1@ÏF}ÍM30362002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄≤ˆÖ␀␀‚9Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162HWC_J1928p178                                    @rB´6zë@1ÀÖ∏QÏ@Ji∑X‚?—ÖoiDg@[^Ov_Ÿ@ÏF}ÍM@ÏF∆[‰õø30362002002@ˆyÊö‘@ˆkËŒp:˚@¯'#–|Ñ∂@¯+£–|Ñ∂SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄±Çw`␀␀‚9Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀GLS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429WR_140                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏF∆[‰õø@ÏF∆Õ´∏130302023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄±Eœt␀␀‰¸Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÿThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric, massive-binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at various, shock densities and on particle acceleration. The campaign observation of WR 140 with NuSTAR and XMM around periastron in 2016 provided an interesting variation of the extremely hard X-ray component, which may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV photons by particles accelerated at the WWC region. We propose two joint NuSTAR and XMM observations of WR 140 in AO3 to monitor the variation of the extremely hard X-ray component during the flux decline after periastron.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY VARIATION OF WR140 AFTER PERIASTRON                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR_140                                            @sŸá+@EÂ`Aâ7@T6WßÜ¬'@˛($x@[≠“Ò©˚Á@ÏF∆Õ´∏1@ÏF⁄Õ´∏130302023002@Ÿ[Mué!ñ@ŸKïOﬂ;d@€4ñÖáìﬁ@€8ñÖáìﬁSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄∞˛{£v␀␀‰¸Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÿThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric, massive-binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at various, shock densities and on particle acceleration. The campaign observation of WR 140 with NuSTAR and XMM around periastron in 2016 provided an interesting variation of the extremely hard X-ray component, which may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV photons by particles accelerated at the WWC region. We propose two joint NuSTAR and XMM observations of WR 140 in AO3 to monitor the variation of the extremely hard X-ray component during the flux decline after periastron.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY VARIATION OF WR140 AFTER PERIASTRON                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PKS_J1220p0203                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏF⁄Õ´∏1@ÏF€Õ´∏160301001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄∞‘Ù>3␀␀„∫Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9ÄPKS J1220+0203 is the only AGN with a relativistic jet, whose X-ray spectrum is reflection dominated and boasts a broad Iron line emission with equivalent width of 1.2 keV. Because of the jet inclination angle and the lack of obscuration, we believe this emission arises in the inner regions of the accretion disk and it is relativistically blurred. As such it provides a unique opportunity to probe the geometry and state of the accretion flow near the black hole and the black hole spin. We propose to acquire a high signal-to-noise spectrum with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR to study the accretion flow and measure the black hole spin in this very interesting object.                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The Unique Case of PKS J1220+0203: a jetted AGN with a broad Iron Line                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_J1220p0203                                    @g ≤ïÈ·±@␀^␀—∑Y@q∆àŒp:˚@OÏj~˘€#@vr)«y¶µ@ÏF€Õ´∏1@ÏGÕ´∏160301001002@Ë™ôY≥–}@ËùÊ‚ÎC@Í|f.±ƒ3@Í¶.±ƒ3SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄∞rÍaŸ␀␀„∫Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9ÄPKS J1220+0203 is the only AGN with a relativistic jet, whose X-ray spectrum is reflection dominated and boasts a broad Iron line emission with equivalent width of 1.2 keV. Because of the jet inclination angle and the lack of obscuration, we believe this emission arises in the inner regions of the accretion disk and it is relativistically blurred. As such it provides a unique opportunity to probe the geometry and state of the accretion flow near the black hole and the black hole spin. We propose to acquire a high signal-to-noise spectrum with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR to study the accretion flow and measure t