ived  20200429NGC_1313_X1                                       @HÀÍ≥g†˘¿Pùëhr∞!@q∂QÇ©ì¿FQ#¢úwö@W¿(ı¬è\@ÏGŸî»)¯@ÏG⁄xVb‹30302016001@}≥ ƒõ•„@}¶C,•zx@°3úæ‡@°3úæ‡SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄¨NÅ¥Ë␀␀‰dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀IWe request 5x75ks NuSTAR observations of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) NGC1313 X-1 (375ks in total), to be coordinated with the very large XMM-Newton (750ks) and Chandra (500ks) follow-up programs our team has been awarded to study its extreme (~0.2-0.25c) outflow. The addition of NuSTAR observations to these programs will allow us to perform multi-epoch broadband spectroscopy, robustly determining the continuum and thus allowing us to better study the ionization structure and variability of this outflow, as well as undertaking broadband spectral variability studies for comparison with other ULXs. NGC1313 X-1 is the only source for which this is currently possible, so it is vital we take advantage of this unique opportunity.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTROSCOPY OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE NGC1313 X-1                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC_1313_X1                                       @H∆µ'ª¿PùS&¡æ@q∂b∂Æ}¿FSI∫^@WÏ–©*0@ÏG⁄xVb‹@ÏH?r‘£30302016002@ˆ˝∞˘€"—@ˆÎæ1è≈@¯ëBÆz·@¯ë≤Æz·SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄´ﬁá∆Z␀␀‰dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀IWe request 5x75ks NuSTAR observations of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) NGC1313 X-1 (375ks in total), to be coordinated with the very large XMM-Newton (750ks) and Chandra (500ks) follow-up programs our team has been awarded to study its extreme (~0.2-0.25c) outflow. The addition of NuSTAR observations to these programs will allow us to perform multi-epoch broadband spectroscopy, robustly determining the continuum and thus allowing us to better study the ionization structure and variability of this outflow, as well as undertaking broadband spectral variability studies for comparison with other ULXs. NGC1313 X-1 is the only source for which this is currently possible, so it is vital we take advantage of this unique opportunity.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTROSCOPY OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE NGC1313 X-1                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SN2014C                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏH?r‘£@ÏH?r‘£40302002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄´•ïn␀␀‰¸Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˝Mass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CONTINUED NUSTAR MONITORING OF THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C AT T>1000 DAYS SINCE EXPLOSION                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u4Å$›/@A7¥¢3ú@WcÀ)^û¿4¥2 Wßá@_¢ÄùIQÉ@ÏH?r‘£@ÏH%±9Ò40302002002@‰®àŸá+@‰ô˘E8Ô5@ÊÄÍJå@Ê‡ÍJåSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄´G}àÍ␀␀‰¸Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˝Mass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CONTINUED NUSTAR MONITORING OF THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C AT T>1000 DAYS SINCE EXPLOSION                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429CXO_J164710d2m455216                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏH%±9Ò@ÏH&ÍM80201050003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄´JU∂␀␀„≠Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXO_J164710d2m455216                              @oy≤˛≈m]¿FÙÿD–@u6iDg8ø—È·∞â†'@qIøHÀí@ÏH&ÍM@ÏH8èFj80201050004@◊wQË>B[@◊^ˇ\(ı√@Ÿ|ÓÉ‰%Ø@Ÿ}.É‰%ØSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄™¬sK␀␀„≠Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429AM0224m283                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏH8èFj@ÏH8ÍM60363002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄™í≈˘,␀␀‚NHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                 