ission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Moon-CXB test                                                                                                           processed 20161207IGRJ11215m5952                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏH™î»)¯@ÏH´î»)¯30301010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄°ÔÄë£␀␀„ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients are massive X-ray binaries showing rare, sporadic and short outbursts that cannot be simply produced by Bondi-Hoyle accretion of supergiant wind onto the neutron star. At present, two competing scenarii have been proposed, involving magnetar-like neutron star magnetic fields (1e14 G) versus more typical values (1e12 G). IGRJ11215-5952 is the only SFXT with predictable outbursts, occurring every 165 days. This allows us to propose a NuSTAR observation (40 ks) during next outburst, to confirm the hint of a cyclotron line we found at 17 keV during a previous shorter NuSTAR pointing. This confirmation is of paramount importance to disentangle among the competing scenarii.                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sidoli                        Lara                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CYCLOTRON LINES IN THE SFXT PULSAR IGRJ11215-5952                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ11215m5952                                    @eKkπå~(¿MÔæv»¥9@r;∫«À)?Ò®Xy=Ÿ@v1÷°a‰˜@ÏH´î»)¯@ÏHÀ±9Ò30301010002@ÂX√.HËß@ÂRõ=»@Ê˘X◊s¸@Ê˝x◊s¸SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄°âìà'␀␀„ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients are massive X-ray binaries showing rare, sporadic and short outbursts that cannot be simply produced by Bondi-Hoyle accretion of supergiant wind onto the neutron star. At present, two competing scenarii have been proposed, involving magnetar-like neutron star magnetic fields (1e14 G) versus more typical values (1e12 G). IGRJ11215-5952 is the only SFXT with predictable outbursts, occurring every 165 days. This allows us to propose a NuSTAR observation (40 ks) during next outburst, to confirm the hint of a cyclotron line we found at 17 keV during a previous shorter NuSTAR pointing. This confirmation is of paramount importance to disentangle among the competing scenarii.                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sidoli                        Lara                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CYCLOTRON LINES IN THE SFXT PULSAR IGRJ11215-5952                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429S82X_002                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏHÀ±9Ò@ÏHÃ±9Ò60366002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄°ZCÇ␀␀‚NHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316S82X_002                                          @v
ˆîFtø‰Â`Aâ7L@T∏V’œ™Œ¿Lé»¥9X@`\Ov`@ÏHÃ±9Ò@ÏHÍM60366002002@È^5?}@ÈìàeîØ@ÍÒnh	‘ï@ÍÚh	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄†¸ç␀␀‚NHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ELS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SDSS_2201                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏHÍM@ÏHÒ[‰õø60369003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄†Ã"‰P␀␀„ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÂWe identified a sample of low-z galaxies with extended AGN photoionised clouds indicative of a powerful AGN, the Voorwerpjes (VP). Far-IR and X-ray data suggest that the current luminosity of some of these AGN is lower than what expected from the VP. This may signify that the AGN dropped in luminosity in the last 10-100 kyr, but the clouds remain lit up because of the travel time of the ionising photons. NuSTAR observations are the best probe of the current AGN state. We propose to observe 4 VP related galaxies with no hard X-ray data available, completing the investigation of the shut down AGN candidates sample. We will probe if the AGN are shutting down, and the magnitude of the drop in luminosity. With this sample we will study AGN variability on previously unreachable timescales.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sartori                       Lia                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE LIFECYCLE OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES ON 10-100 KYR TIMESCALES                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSS_2201                                         @t¶ÃÃÃÃÕ@'≈º£n/@Q°„º”¿@ôrGE8Ô@^ëVì@ÏHÒ[‰õø@ÏIèFj60369003002@ËÛ†ûö@Ë›êuˆ˝"@ÍØØˇ.HÈ@Í∞oˇ.HÈSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄†oP␀␀„ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÂWe identified a sample of low-z galaxies with extended AGN photoionised clouds indicative of a powerful AGN, the Voorwerpjes (VP). Far-IR and X-ray data suggest that the current luminosity of some of these AGN is lower than what expected from the VP. This may signify that the AGN dropped in luminosity in the last 10-100 kyr, but the clouds remain lit up because of the travel time of the ionising photons. NuSTAR observations are the best probe of the current AGN state. We propose to observe 4 VP related galaxies with no hard X-ray data available, completing the investigation of the shut down AGN candidates sample. We will probe if the AGN are shutting down, and the magnitude of the drop in luminosity. With this sample we will study AGN variability on previously unreachable timescales.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sartori                       Lia                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE LIFECYCLE OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES ON 10-100 KYR TIMESCALES                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏIèFj@ÏIî»)¯30302018001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄†<∆ª[␀␀„˛Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀âWe propose 2 25 ks NuSTAR observations of the enigmatic microquasar GRS 1915+105 designed to study its accretion instabilities, ionized winds, Comptonization and reflection processes in coordination with our existing Chandra GTO program. With the ability to track the ionizing radiation field, wind absorption lines, and the behavior of the disk and corona simultaneously, NuSTAR is ideal for probing connections between inflows, outflows, and radiation with Chandra. Our previous work on winds and GRS 1915+105 with Chandra, Suzaku, and RXTE has provided numerous discoveries about the physics of accretion and ejection around stellar mass black holes. With this proposal, we aim to grow a legacy dataset and further our understanding of the extreme accretion physics of this remarkable source.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRAVITY, VARIABILITY, AND WINDS IN GRS 1915+105 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rwökP±@%˙QŒ_@F£qué!ñøñªò«‚ÇA@V␀®¡T…Ü@ÏIî»)¯@ÏI*xVb‹30302018002@—sju%F@—˜xG@›ÂÃÃÃÃÕ@›ÁÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄ü'∫7_␀␀„˛Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀âWe propose 2 25 ks NuSTAR observations of the enigmatic microquasar GRS 1915+105 designed to study its accretion instabilities, ionized winds, Comptonization and reflection processes in coordination with our existing Chandra GTO program. With the ability to track the ionizing radiation field, wind absorption lines, and the behavior of the disk and corona simultaneously, NuSTAR is ideal for probing connections between inflows, outflows, and radiation with Chandra. Our previous work on winds and GRS 1915+105 with Chandra, Suzaku, and RXTE has provided numerous discoveries about the physics of accretion and ejection around stellar mass black holes. With this proposal, we aim to grow a legacy dataset and further our understanding of the extreme accretion physics of this remarkable source.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀