                              ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ELS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC_4968                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏIH#Ü@ÏIH[‰õø60302006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄lN‚Ã␀␀‰ÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀NGC 4968, a nearby Seyfert 2 galaxy, has an extreme Fe K-alpha equivalent width (EW = ~2.5 keV), a hallmark of Compton-thick obscuration. Hints from the Chandra spectrum suggest this obscuration has a spherical covering geometry, which could possibly be fed by circumnuclear star formation. We request 2 20 ks NuSTAR observations, spaced >= 6 months apart, to measure the column density and geometry of the X-ray reprocessor, calculate the intrinsic X-ray luminosity and compare that with the mid-infrared emission, and to search for variability similar to that observed in NGC 1068, the prototypical Compton-thick AGN which also has an extreme Fe K-alpha EW. Evidence of variability would provide insight into the physics of the central engine and perhaps distance to the X-ray reprocessor.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lamassa                       Stephanie                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MODELING THE OBSCURATION AROUND EXTREME AGN NGC 4968                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4968                                          @hóÿ≠´üV¿7≤–Â`Aâ@s4	‘ï+@Cíêñªô@v2a|⁄@ÏIH[‰õø@ÏIW#Ü60302006002@‘à⁄¿Éo@‘{uÛ∂E¢@’€l–©*@’€l–©*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄ln]␀␀‰ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀NGC 4968, a nearby Seyfert 2 galaxy, has an extreme Fe K-alpha equivalent width (EW = ~2.5 keV), a hallmark of Compton-thick obscuration. Hints from the Chandra spectrum suggest this obscuration has a spherical covering geometry, which could possibly be fed by circumnuclear star formation. We request 2 20 ks NuSTAR observations, spaced >= 6 months apart, to measure the column density and geometry of the X-ray reprocessor, calculate the intrinsic X-ray luminosity and compare that with the mid-infrared emission, and to search for variability similar to that observed in NGC 1068, the prototypical Compton-thick AGN which also has an extreme Fe K-alpha EW. Evidence of variability would provide insight into the physics of the central engine and perhaps distance to the X-ray reprocessor.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lamassa                       Stephanie                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MODELING THE OBSCURATION AROUND EXTREME AGN NGC 4968                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004293C273                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏIW#Ü@ÏIWî»)¯10302020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄kÁY =␀␀‚NHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C273                                             @gh£n.≤@␀S˜ŒŸá@r∑È˘r@Pˇó$tT@v]â†'RT@ÏIWî»)¯@ÏIs#Ü10302020002@·H≈‹∆?@·@•Öoi@‚¶†'RT@‚®†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄kè’Ö·␀␀‚NHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra XMM INTEGRAL NICER Swift                                                                    ␀␀␀␀IACHEC cross calibration                                                                                                archived  20200429J1222p0403                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏIs#Ü@ÏIs[‰õø60301018001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄kb‰Pg␀␀„√Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀zGamma-ray Narrow Line Seyfert 1 s (NLS1) are a new class of AGN detected at GeV energies by Fermi which extend the domain of relativistic jets sources down to lower black hole masses. We have 8m Gemini near-IR spectra of some objects and estimated black hole masses from their Balmer and Paschen line profiles. We also now have VLT time (2017). We propose NuSTAR (with standard short SWIFT) observations of 3 gamma-ray NLS1s to build broad band SEDs which we will fit with our newly developed models for the jet and disc components. We will use the results to determine the relationship between the gamma-ray NLS1s, and the similarly high Eddington ratio flat spectrum gamma-ray radio quasars to determine how the jet properties relate to black hole mass.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ward                          Martin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       HARD X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF GAMMA-RAY DETECTED SEYFERTS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                