 area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO344mG016                                       @t€?Â…¿Cb√…ÓÀ˚@qﬁi≠Bƒ¿Kû≈m\˙≠@câ_o“Û@ÏI¨?r‘£@ÏIΩèFj60361017002@ÿHÛ–|Ñ∂@ÿ8ÀiDg8@Ÿ˜–£n/@Ÿ˚£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄i˙òvT␀␀‚VHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ELS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC_7793_P13                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏIΩèFj@ÏIΩxVb‹30302015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄i—Aú¢␀␀„–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀BFollowing a series of remarkable recent discoveries, we now know that some of the most luminous members of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) population are actually powered by apparently super-Eddington neutron star accretors. Three such systems are now known: M82 X-2, NGC7793 P13, and NGC5907 ULX1. Here we propose 2x75ks NuSTAR observations of P13 in order to extend our approved XMM-Newton program and track the evolution of the pulse period and the spectral properties of P13 across a full cycle of the 64d period exhibited by this source, and test its proposed orbital nature. Out of the three ULX pulsars currently known, the low absorption and lack of source confusion make P13 a key laboratory for such systems.                                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TRACKING THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7793_P13                                      @vwìtºj¿@Lπ#¢úx@z«À)_¿S>_ÿ≠´ü@as‡qv@ÏIΩxVb‹@ÏIÛÕ´∏130302015002@ÚîZ≥g†˘@ÚÑ5ä	@Ûﬂ˘4÷°b@Û·‘h	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄iZ§ô9␀␀„–Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀BFollowing a series of remarkable recent discoveries, we now know that some of the most luminous members of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) population are actually powered by apparently super-Eddington neutron star accretors. Three such systems are now known: M82 X-2, NGC7793 P13, and NGC5907 ULX1. Here we propose 2x75ks NuSTAR observations of P13 in order to extend our approved XMM-Newton program and track the evolution of the pulse period and the spectral properties of P13 across a full cycle of the 64d period exhibited by this source, and test its proposed orbital nature. Out of the three ULX pulsars currently known, the low absorption and lack of source confusion make P13 a key laboratory for such systems.                                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TRACKING THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PNE_G343                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏIÛÕ´∏1@ÏIÙ±9Ò30301024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄i#¶~¨␀␀„ Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀·Cataclysmic variables (CVs) are the most abundant class of X-ray binary system. We have recently discovered a new CV (intermediate polar) that is consistent with the position of the planetary nebula HaTr5. The discovery of a compact binary system in a planetary nebula comes with the promise of insight into common envelope physics and SN Ia progenitors if the binary mass is greater than Chandrasekhar. Herein, we propose to take advantage of the superior hard X-ray sensitivity of NuSTAR to measure the temperature of the accretion shock onto and constrain the mass of the white dwarf in this intriguing binary system.                                                                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Reynolds                      Mark                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF AN NEW IP ASSOCIATED WITH A PNE                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PNE_G343                                          @oÏ¨1'¿Eï(åÁ∞@ur>´6zø‡ßÊö‘,@rªöu%F@ÏIÙ±9Ò@ÏJ#Ü30301024002@‚RC,•z