rograms selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ02420381p0510061                            @DDõ•„S¯@”√a@O@d–6‚Î¿H˝Û∂E¢@`§ÍJå@ÏKz[‰õø@ÏKâ[‰õø60363003002@”U˚”√a@”I¬YJÙÒ@‘™s/ÏV÷@‘¨≥/ÏV÷SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄[=—ª␀␀‚cHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Solar Flare M2.3 2017:195:0107Z                                                                                         archived  20200429CXO_J164710d2m455216                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏKâ[‰õø@ÏKäxVb‹80201050005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄[T´lŸ␀␀„≈Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXO_J164710d2m455216                              @oy'ª/Ï¿FÙFsÅ◊‹@u6T…Öoø—Åo␀h‹@sú“Ò©˚Á@ÏKäxVb‹@ÏKöÕ´∏180201050006@◊`\∞Ú{≥@◊BÄ∞Ú{≥@ŸSº1&È@ŸTº1&ÈSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄[x˚‘␀␀„≈Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(3/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429NGC5907_ULX1                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏKöÕ´∏1@ÏKõÕ´∏130302004003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄Z·ŸP»␀␀„⁄Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀:NGC 5907 ULX-1 is the most luminous of the 3 ULXs known to be accreting pulsars. At 17.1 Mpc, its X-ray isotropic luminosity peaks at 1e41 erg/s, 500 times the Eddington limit for a NS. Understanding the physics of this extreme object, for a long time prototypical of IMBHs, which defeats simple theoretical explanations, is interesting per se and essential for population studies and searches for pulsation in other ULXs. The most effective strategy is to repeatedly observe it with XMM and NuSTAR with a carefully devised schedule. Their synergy will provide us also with orbital- and spin-phase-resolved broad-band X-ray spectroscopy, essential for a better understanding of this object, in the context of other extreme ULXs.                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Belfiore                      Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The orbit and spectra of the brightest accreting pulsar: NGC 5907 ULX-1                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                 