ty during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1716m249                                      @p>À)^û¿9}øHÃ@v´üUõ=@«À)^û@uˆ˝!ˇ.@ÏMP#Ü@ÏMü#Ü90301007002@ı ËåLò@ıˆ
=p§@˙J?Á’fœ@˙NœÁ’fœSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄S-:‘␀␀‚rHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC_1068                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏMü#Ü@ÏM†[‰õø60302003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄RÎÊ5€␀␀‰°Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀«We propose four 50 ks NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC 1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2s. A flux excess above 20 keV was discovered by NuSTAR in the August 2014 observation, with respect to observations performed 20 months earlier, and 6 months later. This variability is ascribed to Compton-thick material (with column density NH>2.5 10^24 cm^-2 ) moving temporarily out from our line of sight, allowing the nuclear radiation to pierce through the circumnuclear absorbing material. NuSTAR is the only telescope in orbit that permits a search for such column density variations in this energy band. This monitoring program aims to estimate the distance of the variable absorber, to check if it is consistent with the pc-scale torus.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       LOCATING THE ABSORBING STRUCTURE IN NGC 1068 VIA HARD X-RAY MONITORING                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1068                                          @DW>´6z?åæﬂ§?Ê@e|˜e˝ä€¿Jzxl"h@a<ûÏø±@ÏM†[‰õø@ÏMƒî»)¯60302003002@ËgfÀí:@ËQ'¨q≥@Í"åŒp:˚@Í#LŒp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄Râ‹YÅ␀␀‰°Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀«We propose four 50 ks NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC 1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2s. A flux excess above 20 keV was discovered by NuSTAR in the August 2014 observation, with respect to observations performed 20 months earlier, and 6 months later. This variability is ascribed to Compton-thick material (with column density NH>2.5 10^24 cm^-2 ) moving temporarily out from our line of sight, allowing the nuclear radiation to pierce through the circumnuclear absorbing material. NuSTAR is the only telescope in orbit that permits a search for such column density variations in this energy band. This monitoring program aims to estimate the distance of the variable absorber, to check if it is consistent with the pc-scale torus.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       LOCATING THE ABSORBING STRUCTURE IN NGC 1068 VIA HARD X-RAY MONITORING                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_1358                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏMƒî»)¯@ÏMƒÍM60301026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄RY±ﬂb␀␀„ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∫The X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation two candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month volume-limited (z<0.03) sample. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge of the selection function of CT-AGN, therefore helping us in determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN and their contribution to the CXB. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       COMPTON THICK AGN: THE PHYSICS BEHIND THE OBSCURATION                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1358                                          @J∂§®¡T ¿B&ÄùIR@gœ‡ﬁ“â¿FﬁŸá+@`Ë∞Ú{≤ˇ@ÏMƒÍM@ÏMËÕ´∏160301026002@Ëj$H@ËP®qv@Í4Ñº£@Í5MüUõ=SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄QˆÂ‘ƒ␀␀„ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∫The X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation two candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month volume-limited (z<0.03) sample. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge of the selection function of CT-AGN, therefore helping us in determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN and their contribution to the CXB. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       COMPTON THICK AGN: THE PHYSICS BEHIND THE OBSCURATION                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M33_MOS03                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏMËÕ´∏1@ÏMÈ[‰õø50310003002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄Q∆A˝π␀␀‚yHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Normal galaxies                                             ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M33_MOS03                                         @7ñœAÚ◊@>›5®Xy>@`ØC,•z¿?;Ì˙C˛]@`&»¥9X@ÏMÈ[‰õø@ÏN1ÍM50310003003@¯≈ué!ñ@¯±'jÁ’g@˙|≤⁄QŒ@˙}r⁄QŒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄Q6˛å␀␀‚yHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Normal galaxies                                             ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429BOL_135                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏN1ÍM@ÏN2?r‘£30365002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄P˙”†␀␀„ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ﬂX-ray binaries (XBs) in nearby galaxies have been studied for decades. However, their nature largely remains illusive, mainly due to the lack of hard X-ray coverage. With the extraordinary sensitivity of NuSTAR in hard X-rays, we propose a small NuSTAR survey of four luminous hard X-ray sources (Lx>2e38 erg/s) in M31. The observations will allow us to differentiate between black-hole XBs in the hard state and luminous neutron star (NS) low-mass XBs (LMXBs) by measuring the spectral shape in hard X-rays. The program is important for understanding the origin and evolution of LMXBs. It also allows us to identify supercritically accreting NSs and provides a valuable test for our recently discovered simple, yet effective, source identification method in the Chandra band.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lin                           Dacheng                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR SURVEY OF BRIGHT X-RAY SOURCES IN M32                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BOL_135                                           @%qN;Õ5®@D«HÀí:@^AÖoiD¿5_\(ı¬è@]¢‚ÎC-@ÏN2?r‘£@ÏN@±9Ò30365002002@’)aú‡u˜@’J3úø@÷Çç∏ª@÷Çç∏ªSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄P∏öºﬂ␀␀„ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ﬂX-ray binaries (XBs) in nearby galaxies have been studied for decades. However, their nature largely remains illusive, mainly due to the lack of hard X-ray coverage. With the extraordinary sensitivity of NuSTAR in hard X-rays, we propose a small NuSTAR survey of four luminous hard X-ray sources (Lx>2e38 erg/s) in M31. The observations will allow us to differentiate between black-hole XBs in the hard state and luminous neutron star (NS) low-mass XBs (LMXBs) by measuring the spectral shape in hard X-rays. The program is important for understanding the origin and evolution of LMXBs. It also allows us to identify supercritically accreting NSs and provides a valuable test for our recently discovered simple, yet effective, source identification method in the Chandra band.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lin                           Dacheng                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR SURVEY OF BRIGHT X-RAY SOURCES IN M32                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429S52116p81                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏN@±9Ò@ÏNA?r‘£60061303001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄PèC„-␀␀‚ÖHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316S52116p81                                         @sÂ“àŒp;@TÜ£S&@\¸ùIQÇ™@6Nz·GÆ@P◊Õ5®Xy@ÏNA?r‘£@ÏNLÍM60061303002@“èqué"@“ƒ«‚ÇA@”gnóçP@”hûnóçPSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄PNô˙±␀␀‚ÖHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀in CVZ                                                                                                                  archived  20200429Her_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏNLÍM@ÏNMxVb‹30302012001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄P&fff␀␀‰¡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀·We propose to continue the study of the long-term evolution of the cyclotron line energy Ecyc in Her X-1. The long-term decay of Ecyc between 1993 and 2012, was confirmed by NuSTAR observations, firmly establishing a decay by ~5 keV over the last 20 yrs. However, the last observing campaign in 2016, coordinated between NuSTAR and INTEGRAL has provided evidence that the decay has ended, in fact hinting at a possible increase, similar to the increase observed between 1990 and 1993. We had speculated that a new increase might happen, once Ecyc had reached a low level similar to the time at the discovery. We therefore propose to continue the monitoring of Her X-1 during Cycle-3 with two observations of 30 ks each: the first as soon as possible, the second half a year later.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Staubert                      Ruediger                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MONITORING THE CYCLTRON LINEE ENERGY IN HERCULES X-1: FOLLOW THE LONG-TERM VARIABILITY                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Her_X1                                            @oÕa‰˜e˛@A™0U2a|@M˘rGE@B◊À)^û@Ö∏QÎÖ@ÏNMxVb‹@ÏNf±9Ò30302012002@€Çp€ã¨q@€±∫∏QÎÖ@‡–pìu@‡—–ìuSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄Oå
ûà␀␀‰¡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀·We propose to continue the study of the long-term evolution of the cyclotron line energy Ecyc in Her X-1. The long-term decay of Ecyc between 1993 and 2012, was confirmed by NuSTAR observations, firmly establishing a decay by ~5 keV over the last 20 yrs. However, the last observing campaign in 2016, coordinated between NuSTAR and INTEGRAL has provided evidence that the decay has ended, in fact hinting at a possible increase, similar to the increase observed between 1990 and 1993. We had speculated that a new increase might happen, once Ecyc had reached a low level similar to the time at the discovery. We therefore propose to continue the monitoring of Her X-1 during Cycle-3 with two observations of 30 ks each: the first as soon as possible, the second half a year later.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Staubert                      Ruediger                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MONITORING THE CYCLTRON LINEE ENERGY IN HERCULES X-1: FOLLOW THE LONG-TERM VARIABILITY                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SwiftJ181723d1m164300                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏNf±9Ò@ÏNg[‰õø90301009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄O^á∆Z␀␀‚yHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SwiftJ181723d1m164300                             @q%GÆz·¿0æŸá+@,(>BZÓcø¥®¡T…Ö@uNDg8~@ÏNg[‰õø@ÏNÇ±9Ò90301009002@·ëÓg8~@·g~h	‘ï@‚œ␀£n/@‚–‡£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄OpB¿␀␀‚yHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                         