±9Ò60161224001@j—®Xy=Ÿ@j≤j~˘€#@lzh	‘ï@lzh	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄A+Ê5€␀␀‚ìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429CGCG307m007                                       @SôjOv@Pü∑È˘r@b K]ÃcÒ@/(åÁØ∏@d-#¢úwö@ÏQ
±9Ò@ÏQ#Ü60161224002@‹∞ö{≤˛≈@‹ù?>´6z@ﬁ©($x@ﬁ≠($xSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄@„uÛ␀␀‚ìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_1068                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏQ#Ü@ÏQÕ´∏160302003003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄@∫…Â␀␀‰°Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀«We propose four 50 ks NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC 1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2s. A flux excess above 20 keV was discovered by NuSTAR in the August 2014 observation, with respect to observations performed 20 months earlier, and 6 months later. This variability is ascribed to Compton-thick material (with column density NH>2.5 10^24 cm^-2 ) moving temporarily out from our line of sight, allowing the nuclear radiation to pierce through the circumnuclear absorbing material. NuSTAR is the only telescope in orbit that permits a search for such column density variations in this energy band. This monitoring program aims to estimate the distance of the variable absorber, to check if it is consistent with the pc-scale torus.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       LOCATING THE ABSORBING STRUCTURE IN NGC 1068 VIA HARD X-RAY MONITORING                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1068                                          @DW†˘	k∫?ínóçOﬂ;@e|Ócä	¿J–©*0@a˘ ¿Éo@ÏQÕ´∏1@ÏQDÕ´∏160302003004@È®°»K^@Èì0	ﬁ@Îé‡«À)@Îë‡«À)SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄@U$…ƒ␀␀‰°Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀«We propose four 50 ks NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC 1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2s. A flux excess above 20 keV was discovered by NuSTAR in the August 2014 observation, with respect to observations performed 20 months earlier, and 6 months later. This variability is ascribed to Compton-thick material (with column density NH>2.5 10^24 cm^-2 ) moving temporarily out from our line of sight, allowing the nuclear radiation to pierce through the circumnuclear absorbing material. NuSTAR is the only telescope in orbit that permits a search for such column density variations in this energy band. This monitoring program aims to estimate the distance of the variable absorber, to check if it is consistent with the pc-scale torus.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       LOCATING THE ABSORBING STRUCTURE IN NGC 1068 VIA HARD X-RAY MONITORING                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/4)                                                                                                                   archived  202004294U_1702m43                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏQDÕ´∏1@ÏQEÍM30363005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄@!Òñë␀␀‰Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛We propose to observe the neutron star low-mass X-ray binaries and Atoll sources GX 3+1, 4U 0614+09, 4U 1735-44, 4U 1746-37, and 4U 1702-43, for 20 ks each. Our primary goal is to increase the number of neutron stars with clear, robust indications of relativistic reflection from the inner disk. These observations will also address the radial extent of the inner disk in Atoll sources, which likely accrete at a low Eddington fraction similar to the black hole low/hard state. Our final goal is to find examples of disks that extend very close to stellar surfaces in order to obtain limits on neutron star radii.              