                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004292MASXJ06230765m6436211                            @WÙ`™d¬¯¿P%:í£@q$Vìu¿;NÊ1¯†ê@a∞j~˘€#@ÏS[î»)¯@ÏSrî»)¯60160263002@‰m2¡T…Ü@‰Z›AÚ◊@Êuˆ˝"@Êﬁuˆ˝"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄¯	+␀␀‚©Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀ Kp=6                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_1313_X1                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏSrî»)¯@ÏSrÍM30302016007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄…b¸ñ␀␀‰dHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀IWe request 5x75ks NuSTAR observations of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) NGC1313 X-1 (375ks in total), to be coordinated with the very large XMM-Newton (750ks) and Chandra (500ks) follow-up programs our team has been awarded to study its extreme (~0.2-0.25c) outflow. The addition of NuSTAR observations to these programs will allow us to perform multi-epoch broadband spectroscopy, robustly determining the continuum and thus allowing us to better study the ionization structure and variability of this outflow, as well as undertaking broadband spectral variability studies for comparison with other ULXs. NGC1313 X-1 is the only source for which this is currently possible, so it is vital we take advantage of this unique opportunity.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTROSCOPY OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE NGC1313 X-1                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1313_X1                                       @H“h	‘ï¿PùÊö‘,=@q∂Œ_p¿FN¬&ÄùI@g;7K∆ß@ÏSrÍM@ÏS§ÍM30302016008@Ú	Û3úø@Ò˝A?|Ìë@ÛkõS&@Ûl+S&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄Eœt␀␀‰dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀IWe request 5x75ks NuSTAR observations of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) NGC1313 X-1 (375ks in total), to be coordinated with the very large XMM-Newton (750ks) and Chandra (500ks) follow-up programs our team has been awarded to study its extreme (~0.2-0.25c) outflow. The addition of NuSTAR observations to these programs will allow us to perform multi-epoch broadband spectroscopy, robustly determining the continuum and thus allowing us to better study the ionization structure and variability of this outflow, as well as undertaking broadband spectral variability studies for comparison with other ULXs. NGC1313 X-1 is the only source for which this is currently possible, so it is vital we take advantage of this unique opportunity.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTROSCOPY OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE NGC1313 X-1                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(4/5) / Kp=6                                                                                                            archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏS§ÍM@ÏS•xVb‹80302309003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄
ÁY ␀␀‰:Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in April 2017, we take a great step forward. NICER will be to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they will produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER to exploit these groundbreaking capabilities.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1535m571                                    @m8∫«À)¿LüÔù≤-@t9"h	‘ïøÔ/ÏV’œ´@␀¿∑Ä4m∆@ÏS•xVb‹@ÏS©î»)¯80302309004@ísÑµ‹∆?@ì◊Bè\(ˆ@ª‡ø˘rGE@ª‡ø˘rGESCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄x	+␀␀‰:Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scatterin