ervatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4993                                          @h≠|Ìëhs¿7d…Öoi@sAQŒ_@C≤åLò_@8âJÙÿE@ÏT.ÍM@ÏTYî»)¯90361003009@≤2ë—N;Õ@≤-üæv»¥@≥Æ@'RTa@≥Æ@'RTaSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ˝TÑ]␀␀‚∞Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Monitoring                                                                                                              archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏTYî»)¯@ÏTZxVb‹30302020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ˝!/hL␀␀‰ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≠In recent years, the study of the correlated X-ray/OIR fast variability in black-hole transients (BHTs) is showing more and more promising results. GRS 1915+105 was one of the first BHTs to be studied with a multi-wavelength approach, showing the first strong evidences for a disk-jet connection. As of today, only few X-ray-OIR simultaneous observations have been reported of this source. We therefore propose to participate with NuSTAR to an already approved multi-wavelength observing campaign to study the X-ray-IR fast variability, together with XMM-Newton and VLT, in order to cover the hard X-ray band. NuSTAR sensibility in the hard X-rays will be crucial to study the inflow-outflow connection without the accretion disc contamination.                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRS 1915+105 MULTI-WAVELENGTH FAST VARIABILITY                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @r[¿6„@%‘ÿD–@Fö√…ÓÀ˚ø©¶µ'ª@u%S˜ŒŸ@ÏTZxVb‹@ÏTkèFj30302020002@ª¢‡ê-‡@Ω1köu@◊7év»¥9@◊:Œv»¥9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ˚ÿ¶ﬂ√␀␀‰ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≠In recent years, the study of the correlated X-ray/OIR fast variability in black-hole transients (BHTs) is showing more and more promising results. GRS 1915+105 was one of the first BHTs to be studied with a multi-wavelength approach, showing the first strong evidences for a disk-jet connection. As of today, only few X-ray-OIR simultaneous observations have been reported of this source. We therefore propose to participate with NuSTAR to an already approved multi-wavelength observing campaign to study the X-ray-IR fast variability, together with XMM-Newton and VLT, in order to cover the hard X-ray band. NuSTAR sensibility in the hard X-rays will be crucial to study the inflow-outflow connection without the accretion disc contamination.                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRS 1915+105 MULTI-WAVELENGTH FAST VARIABILITY                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM VLT INTEGRAL                                                                                    ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_B1821m24                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏTkèFj@ÏTkxVb‹10202005003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ˚≠:‘␀␀ÂvHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316