lity in black-hole transients (BHTs) is showing more and more promising results. GRS 1915+105 was one of the first BHTs to be studied with a multi-wavelength approach, showing the first strong evidences for a disk-jet connection. As of today, only few X-ray-OIR simultaneous observations have been reported of this source. We therefore propose to participate with NuSTAR to an already approved multi-wavelength observing campaign to study the X-ray-IR fast variability, together with XMM-Newton and VLT, in order to cover the hard X-ray band. NuSTAR sensibility in the hard X-rays will be crucial to study the inflow-outflow connection without the accretion disc contamination.                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRS 1915+105 MULTI-WAVELENGTH FAST VARIABILITY                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rtSéÛM@%‘÷°a‰˜@Fõ¸PHø¨]càeîØ@tpn.±ƒ3@ÏV‹xVb‹@ÏVÎ#Ü30302020004@¥Ò√n.±ƒ@∂»>BZÓ@”˜”3333@”¸3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÁn,¿™␀␀‰ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≠In recent years, the study of the correlated X-ray/OIR fast variability in black-hole transients (BHTs) is showing more and more promising results. GRS 1915+105 was one of the first BHTs to be studied with a multi-wavelength approach, showing the first strong evidences for a disk-jet connection. As of today, only few X-ray-OIR simultaneous observations have been reported of this source. We therefore propose to participate with NuSTAR to an already approved multi-wavelength observing campaign to study the X-ray-IR fast variability, together with XMM-Newton and VLT, in order to cover the hard X-ray band. NuSTAR sensibility in the hard X-rays will be crucial to study the inflow-outflow connection without the accretion disc contamination.                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRS 1915+105 MULTI-WAVELENGTH FAST VARIABILITY                                                                          ␀␀µ␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM VLT Astrosat ALMA                                                                               ␀␀␀(2/4) / Kp=5                                                                                                            archived  202004292MASXJ18560128p1538059                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏVÎ#Ü@ÏVÎxVb‹60160701001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÁBsK␀␀‚ºHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                     