  Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀Kp=6                                                                                                                    archived  20200429UGC06728                                          @f_o“Û@SÎS˜ŒŸ@_§«‚Ç@∏@BpÂ`Aâ7@k ¶L/É{@ÏV˙èFj@ÏV˙Õ´∏160376007001@Äºoù≤-@Ä∫è≈Åo@Å␀␀␀␀␀@Å␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÊ»π␀␀‚ºHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429UGC06728                                          @fıY≥–}@SÍ‘,<ûÌ@_§∫«À)@Bq¯†ê-‡@kΩoiDg@ÏV˙Õ´∏1@ÏW#Ü60376007002@Ï[í<ûÏ¿@ÏK©‰%ÆÊ@ÓõÄ␀␀␀␀@Óü@␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÊjIìà␀␀‚ºHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀ELS (CVZ) / Kp=5                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_1144                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏW#Ü@ÏW#Ü60368001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÊ9Óçÿ␀␀‰1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‡We propose to use a complete, volume-limited sample of AGN to obtain an accurate measurement of the column density distribution of AGN in the local universe. At the same time, we will measure the volume emissivity of hard X-ray emission in the local universe, which can be an important component in ionization and feedback. The survey is modest, and reasonable exposure times have been estimated with the aid of the Swift/BAT 105-month catalog. NuSTAR has only observed a few sources from this sample, but we need only 420 ks to observe the remaining sources in a quasi-uniform manner.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF OBSCURED AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1144                                          @EÈË>BZÓøƒûÏø±[W@eˆÿD–¿I>´6z@eÍ¢3úø@ÏW#Ü@ÏW$xVb‹60368001002@‘9M÷8ÜY@‘*3dZ¨@’Ÿ±†'RT@’ÿq†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÂﬁ–ó¥␀␀‰1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡We propose to use a complete, volume-limited sample of AGN to obtain an accurate measurement of the column density distribution of AGN in the local universe. At the same time, we will measure the volume emissivity of hard X-ray emission in the local universe, which can be an important component in ionization and feedback. The survey is modest, and reasonable exposure times have been estimated with the aid of the Swift/BAT 105-month catalog. NuSTAR has only observed a few sources from this sample, but we need only 420 ks to observe the remaining sources in a quasi-uniform manner.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF OBSCURED AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO_103m35                                        @qxÀ)^û¿P\D–©*@tõÍJå¿6˚)^û	@uGÅo␀i@ÏW$xVb‹@ÏW%xVb‹60301004001@)-V@(œ'ª/ÏW@*Û33333@*Û33333SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÂ™Iìà␀␀‰1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‰What determines the coronal temperature in AGN is much debated. One plausible possibility is the Pair Thermostat, where heating drives the temperature up to the point at which electron-positron pairs are produced in significant quantity to control further temperature rise. Hybrid plasmas, in which most pairs are produced by a small nonthermal fraction, can lead to still lower coronal temperatures. We propose to use NuSTAR to observe 3 hard X-ray bright AGN with low temperatures (30-40 keV) measured by INTEGRAL in order to test this hypothesis. NuSTAR will give snapshot spectra of the objects, correct for relativistic reflection and be at much higher precision than Integral.                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE PAIR CONSTRAINT ON CORONAL TEMPERATURES IN SEYFERT 1 GALAXIES                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429ESO_103m35                                        @qwÓÀ˚µ¿P]˘rGE@töÀí:)«¿6˚"–Â`B@uK6‚Î@ÏW%xVb‹@ÏW>±9Ò60301004002@Âg0?Â…@ÂZˆê-‡@Á”ÁlãD@Á”ÁlãDSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÂGˆÂ’␀␀‰1Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‰What determines the coronal temperature in AGN is much debated. One plausible possibility is the Pair Thermostat, where heating drives the temperature up to the point at which electron-positron pairs are produced in significant quantity to control further temperature rise. Hybrid plasmas, in which most pairs are produced by a small nonthermal fraction, can lead to still lower coronal temperatures. We propose to use NuSTAR to observe 3 hard X-ray bright AGN with low temperatures (30-40 keV) measured by INTEGRAL in order to test this hypothesis. NuSTAR will give snapshot spectra of the objects, correct for relativistic reflection and be at much higher precision than Integral.                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE PAIR CONSTRAINT ON CORONAL TEMPERATURES IN SEYFERT 1 GALAXIES                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429AX_J1841d0m0536                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏW>±9Ò@ÏW?[‰õø30301026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÂ©&N␀␀‰>Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‚The detection of cyclotron resonance absorption features in transient accreting HMXBs, also known as Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXT) is a powerful tool to unveil the processes driving their accretion modes. Our previous discoveries of cyclotron lines in the transients SAX J17544-2619 and IGR J16418-4532 with NuSTAR have motivated us to further study the role of magnetic fields in the extreme variability of these objects. For this purpose we ask to study two additional HMXB systems, selected by the available sample of SFXTs, for a a total exposure of 100~ks to be executed in AO3. These sources, SAX J1818.6-1703 and AX J1841.0-0536 show a dynamic range of variability of about 10^3 and may show bright flaring activity during a single exposure of 50ks.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Natalucci                     Lorenzo                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE MAGNETIC FIELDS OF NEUTRON STARS IN SUPERGIANT FAST X-RAY TRANSIENTS                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AX_J1841d0m0536                                   @qÉùIQÇ™¿ÇI∫^5@:õW>´6zø¢Ω<64@tcûÏø±[@ÏW?[‰õø@ÏWg±9Ò30301026002@ÍÑÒ≤ïÍ@Íu5ÓÀ˚@Ï≥ÊlÙ!@Ï∑ÊlÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ‰úÎ$␀␀‰>Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‚The detection of cyclotron resonance absorption features in transient accreting HMXBs, also known as Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXT) is a powerful tool to unveil the processes driving their accretion modes. Our previous discoveries of cyclotron lines in the transients SAX J17544-2619 and IGR J16418-4532 with NuSTAR have motivated us to further study the role of magnetic fields in the extreme variability of these objects. For this purpose we ask to study two additional HMXB systems, selected by the available sample of SFXTs, for a a total exposure of 100~ks to be executed in AO3. These sources, SAX J1818.6-1703 and AX J1841.0-0536 show a dynamic range of variability of about 10^3 and may show bright flaring activity during a single exposure of 50ks.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Natalucci                     Lorenzo                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE MAGNETIC FIELDS OF NEUTRON STARS IN SUPERGIANT FAST X-RAY TRANSIENTS                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292E1739d1m1210                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏWg±9Ò@ÏWhèFj60160670001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ‰`$h≠␀␀‚∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162E1739d1m1210                                     @pó5?|Ìë¿(qhr∞ ≈@+ëA [¿@#@ÎÌ˙C˛@t†Ø∑È@ÏWhèFj@ÏWv±9Ò60160670002@‘›l¬¯7µ@‘œ÷°aÂ@÷ml-‡@÷ml-‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ‰ˆÑæ␀␀‚∆Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_3p1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏWv±9Ò@ÏWwèFj30363001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ„Ê~¨/␀␀‰3Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛We propose to observe the neutron star low-mass X-ray binaries and Atoll sources GX 3+1, 4U 0614+09, 4U 1735-44, 4U 1746-37, and 4U 1702-43, for 20 ks each. Our primary goal is to increase the number of neutron stars with clear, robust indications of relativistic reflection from the inner disk. These observations will also address the radial extent of the inner disk in Atoll sources, which likely accrete at a low Eddington fraction similar to the black hole low/hard state. Our final goal is to find examples of disks that extend very close to stellar surfaces in order to obtain limits on neutron star radii.                                                                                                                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NEUTRON STARS AND THEIR INNERMOST ACCRETION FLOWS                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_3p1                                            @pØ<64¿:óÔù≤-@*ô0æ?!-w1ê@u?º@ÏWwèFj@ÏWÖÕ´∏130363001002@ ∞◊€Ùà@ÀL-ä⁄πı@‘√e¶µ@‘∆•¶µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ„5¬è\␀␀‰3Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˛We propose to observe the neutron star low-mass X-ray binaries and Atoll sources GX 3+1, 4U 0614+09, 4U 1735-44, 4U 1746-37, and 4U 1702-43, for 20 ks each. Our primary goal is to increase the number of neutron stars with clear, robust indications of relativistic reflection from the inner disk. These observations will also address the radial extent of the inner disk in Atoll sources, which likely accrete at a low Eddington fraction similar to the black hole low/hard state. Our final goal is to find examples of disks that extend very close to stellar surfaces in order to obtain limits on neutron star radii.                                                                                                                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NEUTRON STARS AND THEIR INNERMOST ACCRETION FLOWS                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PSR_J1826m1334                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏWÖÕ´∏1@ÏWÜ#Ü30364004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ„pB¿␀␀‰>Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀⁄Fermi has detected over 200 gamma-ray pulsars, but only a handful fall in the soft gamma-ray (emission peaking below 1 GeV) category. These tend to be single-pulsed, young, and extremely energetic, the archetypal example being PSR B1509-58. With the greatly improved sensitivity of Pass 8, Fermi is expected to detect increasing numbers of soft gamma-ray pulsars. We propose NuSTAR observations of 5 soft gamma-ray pulsar candidates. The unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR in the hard X-ray energy range, combined with existing soft X-ray and gamma-ray data (e.g. Chandra, XMM, and Fermi) will enable us to test and constrain models of high-energy pulsar emission over a range of parameters (e.g. viewing geometries, spind-down parameters).                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Parkinson                     Pablo                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF SOFT GAMMA-RAY PULSARS                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J1826m1334                                    @qHs¸PH¿+8ÜYJÙÒ@1◊¡Ω•ùøﬁæﬂ§?Â…@tÉxF‹]@ÏWÜ#Ü@ÏW¨î»)¯30364004002@ÈP∞Œp:˚@È|”◊€Ùà@Îôvg8~@Îöˆg8~SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ‚ó¥%Ì␀␀‰>Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀⁄Fermi has detected over 200 gamma-ray pulsars, but only a handful fall in the soft gamma-ray (emission peaking below 1 GeV) category. These tend to be single-pulsed, young, and extremely energetic, the archetypal example being PSR B1509-58. With the greatly improved sensitivity of Pass 8, Fermi is expected to detect increasing numbers of soft gamma-ray pulsars. We propose NuSTAR observations of 5 soft gamma-ray pulsar candidates. The unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR in the hard X-ray energy range, combined with existing soft X-ray and gamma-ray data (e.g. Chandra, XMM, and Fermi) will enable us to test and constrain models of high-energy pulsar emission over a range of parameters (e.g. viewing geometries, spind-down parameters).                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Parkinson                     Pablo                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF SOFT GAMMA-RAY PULSARS                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429QSO_J0509p0541                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏW¨î»)¯@ÏW≠ÍM90301618003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ‚b"""␀␀‚ÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316QSO_J0509p0541                                    @SXãCïÅ@ÂÆÊ1¯°@hiC,•z¿3Õ<64@d^Â`Aâ7@ÏW≠ÍM@ÏWºxVb‹90301618004@‘Ÿûö@‘…"ªò«„@÷rq†'RT@÷rq†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ‚P»@␀␀‚ÕHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Mrk_231                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏWºxVb‹@ÏWΩ[‰õø80302608001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ·Óç◊Ã␀␀‰3Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÄWe propose for two 80 ks TOO observations of Mrk 231 with NuSTAR. We aim at obtaining two high quality NuSTAR X-ray spectra, one during a quiescent radio state, the other during a bright radio flare. Results from our previous radio and X-ray campaign show that low radio flux states are associated with the appearance of an X-ray ultra fast outflow (UFO) that is not detected during radio flares. This suggests that winds and jets could be mutually exclusive, as is the case in black hole X-ray binaries. We aim at confirming this behaviour with better statistics and at a different epoch to exclude chance coincidence. The resulting intermittent nature of UFOs in AGN, and the associated jet-wind anti-correlation will have an important impact on wind-driven AGN feedback models.                    Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Miniutti                      Giovanni                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       EXPLORING THE JET-WIND CONNECTION IN MRK 231                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / 292:21:32:58 idiagnostics_off                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_231                                           @hCT…Öo@Lm„S˜ŒŸ@^q!ˇ.HÈ@Nëhr∞!@mI∫^5@ÏWΩ[‰õø@ÏWÛÕ´∏180302608002@ÙîıY≥–@Û¸MÜYJı@ıg_Œ_p@ıh‚ö‘,=SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ·m≥cø␀␀‰3Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÄWe propose for two 80 ks TOO observations of Mrk 231 with NuSTAR. We aim at obtaining two high quality NuSTAR X-ray spectra, one during a quiescent radio state, the other during a bright radio flare. Results from our previous radio and X-ray campaign show that low radio flux states are associated with the appearance of an X-ray ultra fast outflow (UFO) that is not detected during radio flares. This suggests that winds and jets could be mutually exclusive, as is the case in black hole X-ray binaries. We aim at confirming this behaviour with better statistics and at a different epoch to exclude chance coincidence. The resulting intermittent nature of UFOs in AGN, and the associated jet-wind anti-correlation will have an important impact on wind-driven AGN feedback models.                    Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Miniutti                      Giovanni                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       EXPLORING THE JET-WIND CONNECTION IN MRK 231                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀(1/2) ToO / Solar Flare 293:2310 M1.1                                                                                   archived  20200429PKS_0625m354                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏWÛÕ´∏1@ÏWÙÕ´∏160301007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ·4üIı␀␀‰3Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose to study one of the four FRI radio galaxies with detected TeV emission, PKS 0625-354. It hosts a billion solar mass black hole emitting at 0.2% Eddington luminosity, generally associated with a radiatively inefficient accretion flow (RIAF). It has been studied in radio to TeV bands, suggesting that the TeV and X-ray emission comes from a jet. However, it is also possible that the RIAF can contribute significantly to the X-rays. Unfortunately, hard X-ray spectrum above 10 keV is missing, making it hard to distinguish the X-ray emission model. With the excellent sensitivity of NuSTAR, we will study the nature of the hard X-rays from this rare TeV FRI radio galaxy and will provide constraints on hard X-ray emission models: do they have a jet or an accretion flow origin?            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Wong                          Ka-Wah                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NATURE OF HARD X-RAYS FROM A TEV EMITTING RADIO GALAXY                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_0625m354                                      @X4!-w2¿AºFsÅ◊‹@nläqﬁi≠¿4¨q≤ñ@eÍtSéÛM@ÏWÙÕ´∏1@ÏXxVb‹60301007002@ÈhLò_@ÈO®_ÿ≠¨@Î#Û9¿ÎÓ@Î#Û9¿ÎÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ‡ÃSo‚␀␀‰3Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose to study one of the four FRI radio galaxies with detected TeV emission, PKS 0625-354. It hosts a billion solar mass black hole emitting at 0.2% Eddington luminosity, generally associated with a radiatively inefficient accretion flow (RIAF). It has been studied in radio to TeV bands, suggesting that the TeV and X-ray emission comes from a jet. However, it is also possible that the RIAF can contribute significantly to the X-rays. Unfortunately, hard X-ray spectrum above 10 keV is missing, making it hard to distinguish the X-ray emission model. With the excellent sensitivity of NuSTAR, we will study the nature of the hard X-rays from this rare TeV FRI radio galaxy and will provide constraints on hard X-ray emission models: do they have a jet or an accretion flow origin?            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Wong                          Ka-Wah                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NATURE OF HARD X-RAYS FROM A TEV EMITTING RADIO GALAXY                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571                                    @m864Í¿Lúéäqﬁj@t9C˛\ë—øÓVìtº@=èiDg8@ÏXxVb‹@ÏX?r‘£80302309013␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ‡z∞º␀␀‰:Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in April 2017, we take a great step forward. NICER will be to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they will produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER to exploit these groundbreaking capabilities.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1535m571                                    @m8?º¿Lúõ•„S¯@t9E8Ô4◊øÓ[¿6‚Î@=öÓcä	@ÏX?r‘£@ÏX$±9Ò80302309014@´≈␀™d¬¯@≠P:˚~ë@«ö˚~ë␀@«ú˚~ë␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸﬂìo·©␀␀‰:Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in April 2017, we take a great step forward. NICER will be to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they will produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER to exploit these groundbreaking capabilities.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(5/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571_SLPA030                            @l”Ú‰éär¿L'§?Â…@t*¡T…Ö?Ëµ‹∆?@@û´6zë@ÏX$±9Ò@ÏX*±9Ò80302312001@¬ú€Ùá˝@¬à1=Ÿc@ƒ≈±[W?@ƒ≈±[W?SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸﬁÌ„ÔP␀␀‰:Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in April 2017, we take a great step forward. NICER will be to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they will produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER to exploit these groundbreaking capabilities.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                      