nitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             processed 20170817Luna_17296_Pos15                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏX,K}s+@ÏX,U”)ó10312015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏX-ìo·©ˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             processed 20150316PSR_J2032p4127                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏX/?r‘£@ÏX0èFj30302002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ…ß°Ú␀␀‰mHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9?PSR J2032+4127 is a Fermi and radio pulsar in the Cygnus OB2 association, powering a TeV pulsar wind nebula. It is in a highly eccentric 48 year orbit around a Be star. We propose for time-critical spectroscopic monitoring at periastron to look for the expected interaction of the pulsar with the wind and/or disk of the Be star. XMM-Newton is also able to test for pulsations at the 143 ms spin period that would be the signature of accretion onto the neutron star. These observations are important for comparison with PSR B1259-63, the prototype and only other Be/gamma-ray binary with a known pulsar, as well as to help understand gamma-ray binaries that have compact objects of uncertain type that may be neutron stars even though their pulsations have not been detected.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Observing PSR J2032+4127, a Be Gamma-ray Binary, at Periastron                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J2032p4127                                    @s@ôôôôö@D∑!-w1ê@T*0U2a?ÒIQÇ©ì@t√‰˜e˝ã@ÏX0èFj@ÏXLî»)¯30302002002@‚zn7¥¢4@‚l␀<ûÏ¿@„⁄{˚µt@„ﬁ˚µtSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ»q5yæ␀␀‰mHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9?PSR J2032+4127 is a Fermi and radio pulsar in the Cygnus OB2 association, powering a TeV pulsar wind nebula. It is in a highly eccentric 48 year orbit around a Be star. We propose for time-critical spectroscopic monitoring at periastron to look for the expected interaction of the pulsar with the wind and/or disk of the Be star. XMM-Newton is also able to test for pulsations at the 143 ms spin period that would be the signature of accretion onto the neutron star. These observations are important for comparison with PSR B1259-63, the prototype and only other Be/gamma-ray binary with a known pulsar, as well as to help understand gamma-ray binaries that have compact objects of uncertain type that may be neutron stars even though their pulsations have not been detected.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Observing PSR J2032+4127, a Be Gamma-ray Binary, at Periastron                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_3718                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏXLî»)¯@ÏXM[‰õø60301031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ»l¡␀␀‰DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÍThe accretion flow in AGN changes its structure when the accretion rate drops. High power AGN are softer when brighter while low-luminosity sources do the opposite. The transition point is not well known, largely because of the imprecise determination of the spectral slope, whose dynamic range is very small. What happens to the accretion flow at low rates is also not well understood, either a RIAF or a jet are possible. In this proposal we target three low accretion rate AGN to measure their spectral slope, and confirm or reject spectral curvature, which would point to a RIAF. The targets belong to a flux limited sample covering a wide range in accretion rates. They fill the mid-low L/L_Edd range, crucial to follow the evolution of the X-ray emission at the turning point.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Arevalo                       Patricia                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TRACKING ACCRETION POWER INTO THE LOW LUMINOSITY REGIME                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3718                                          @e¶ﬁ“àŒp@JálãCïÅ@bb:)«yß@Nò«‚ÇA@i‰∑Ä4m∆@ÏXM[‰õø@ÏX]?r‘£60301031002@◊ÙgS˜ŒŸ@◊Âé∏QÎÖ@ŸíMj@Ÿî€MjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ«w_1Ø␀␀‰DHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÍThe accretion flow in AGN changes its structure when the accretion rate drops. High power AGN are softer when brighter while low-luminosity sources do the opposite. The transition point is not well known, largely because of the imprecise determination of the spectral slope, whose dynamic range is very small. What happens to the accretion flow at low rates is also not well understood, either a RIAF or a jet are possible. In this proposal we target three low accretion rate AGN to measure their spectral slope, and confirm or reject spectral curvature, which would point to a RIAF. The targets belong to a flux limited sample covering a wide range in accretion rates. They fill the mid-low L/L_Edd range, crucial to follow the evolution of the X-ray emission at the turning point.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Arevalo                       Patricia                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TRACKING ACCRETION POWER INTO THE LOW LUMINOSITY REGIME                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571_SADA_17295                         @n=´üUõ=¿L.HËß@trÿ≠´üV¿m(åÁ∞@5πrGE8Ô@ÏX]?r‘£@ÏX^±9Ò80302311001@_*ô0æ@__ÿ≠´ü@`§ÕûÉ‰&@`§ÕûÉ‰&SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ∆ËNÇ␀␀‰0Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in April 2017, we take a great step forward. NICER will be to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they will produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER to exploit these groundbreaking capabilities.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571                                    @m8 ƒõ•„¿Lú2 Wßá@t9F
¶L0øÓ<ûÏø±[@?ÓOv_ÿÆ@ÏX^±9Ò@ÏXbÍM80402302009@õ•—N;Õ@ù ÓÀ˚µ@∂)%˝ä⁄∫@∂/%˝ä⁄∫SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ≈*z␀␀‰0Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  (ZWe propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, INTEGRAL, JVLA/ATCA, Swift, XMM, Gemini) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds during and after the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next 2-3 months. Our spectral and timing constraints will provide precise probes of the accretion geometry and accretion/ejection physics.                                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       An Integrated Approach to Winds/ Jets/ and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571_SLPA030                            @l”Êö‘,=¿L'˘rGE9@t*∏∫«À?ËßÔù≤-@A≤u%F@ÏXbÍM@ÏXkî»)¯80302312002@…*“üæv…@»‰Ì®Xy>@À›lV’œ´@À›lV’œ´SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ≈Otnæ␀␀‰0Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, spectral resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of black hole accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in April 2017, we take a great step forward. NICER will be to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they will produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER to exploit these groundbreaking capabilities.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Straylight observation to reduce data volume / Kp=5                                                                     archived  20200429MAXI_J1535m571                                    @m7Ì(åÁ¿Lõ…ÓÀ˚@t9BZÓc øÓç∏ª@@˝!ˇ.I@ÏXkî»)¯@ÏXrèFj80402302010@™ù®K]Ãd@¨O'Êö‘@ƒÌü€Ùá˝@ƒÌü€Ùá˝SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸƒ6˛å␀␀‰0Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  (ZWe propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, INTEGRAL, JVLA/ATCA, Swift, XMM, Gemini) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds during and after the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next 2-3 months. Our spectral and timing constraints will provide precise probes of the accretion geometry and accretion/ejection physics.                                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       An Integrated Approach to Winds/ Jets/ and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429GX_339m4                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏXrèFj@ÏXr[‰õø80302304003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ√·GÆ␀␀‰ôHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀X-ray reflection spectroscopy is one of the most effective means of probing the strong-field region near an event horizon. Modeling a reflection spectrum, one can constrain the spin of a black hole, the degree of truncation of the inner disk, the inclination of the system, and other key parameters. We request 80 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger four observations of the black hole binary GX 339-4 during the rise of its next outburst. Our principal goal is to track the radius of the inner edge of the accretion disk in the hard state as a function of luminosity, as we have done before using RXTE data. Using NuSTAR advanced capabilities, we aim to resolve the gross discrepancies in published measurements of the inner disk radius.                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE EVOLUTION OF THE ACCRETION DISK IN GX 339-4                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_339m4                                          @oÙ_ˆîF¿Hf˝!ˇ.I@u,ùIQÇ™¿w1è≈Å@,1&Èy@ÏXr[‰õø@ÏXÄÕ´∏180302304004@—èïhr∞!@—ú'Æz·@‘æÉ‰%Ø@‘~É‰%ØSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ√N≤@y␀␀‰ôHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀X-ray reflection spectroscopy is one of the most effective means of probing the strong-field region near an event horizon. Modeling a reflection spectrum, one can constrain the spin of a black hole, the degree of truncation of the inner disk, the inclination of the system, and other key parameters. We request 80 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger four observations of the black hole binary GX 339-4 during the rise of its next outburst. Our principal goal is to track the radius of the inner edge of the accretion disk in the hard state as a function of luminosity, as we have done before using RXTE data. Using NuSTAR advanced capabilities, we aim to resolve the gross discrepancies in published measurements of the inner disk radius.                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE EVOLUTION OF THE ACCRETION DISK IN GX 339-4                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429MAXI_J1621m501_SADA_17299                         @o›‘ï*ô¿Iå~($@uÀ˚µt¿˘ıY≥–}@6E° ¿É@ÏXÄÕ´∏1@ÏXÅèFj90301323001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ¬ÓÓÓÔ␀␀‚“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀