s may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC7793_P13                                       @vwy¶µ¿@T≤ïÈ·±@}VlÙ!¿S<ìtºj@qı4m≈÷9@ÏY2#Ü@ÏYXxVb‹90301326002@ÈZõƒ2 X@ÈIX%ÆÊ2@Î#Hºj~˙@Î%®ºj~˙SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸºx„é9␀␀‚“Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429ESO_116m18                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏYXxVb‹@ÏYYèFj60301027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸªì'␀␀‰DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∫The X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation two candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month volume-limited (z<0.03) sample. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge of the selection function of CT-AGN, therefore helping us in determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN and their contribution to the CXB. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       COMPTON THICK AGN: THE PHYSICS BEHIND THE OBSCURATION                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_116m18                                        @I£I∫^¿N_≈Åo␀@qD\(ı¬è¿GŒRΩ<6@l›∂E° ¡@ÏYYèFj@ÏY{±9Ò60301027002@Ê∞Öáìﬁ@Âˆ␀h€ã¨@Áá⁄H@Áá˙HSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ∫ÄÚπ÷␀␀‰DHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∫The X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation two candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month volume-limited (z<0.03) sample. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge of the selection function of CT-AGN, therefore helping us in determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN and their contribution to the CXB. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       COMPTON THICK AGN: THE PHYSICS BEHIND THE OBSCURATION                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_339m4_SADA_17306                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏY{±9Ò@ÏY|xVb‹80302305001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸeP¯ÀH␀␀‰ôHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀X-ray reflection spectroscopy is one of the most effective means of probing the strong-field region near an event horizon. Modeling a reflection spectrum, one can constrain the spin of a black hole, the degree of truncation of the inner disk, the inclination of the system, and other key parameters. We request 80 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger four observations of the black hole binary GX 339-4 during the rise of its next outburst. Our principal goal is to track the radius of the inner edge of the accretion disk in the hard state as a function of luminosity, as we have done before using RXTE data. Using NuSTAR advanced capabilities, we aim to resolve the gross discrepancies in published measurements of the inner disk radius.                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE EVOLUTION OF THE ACCRETION DISK IN GX 339-4                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_339m4                                          @oÙßÊö‘¿Hf1¯†ê.@u,ø±[W?¿xF‹]d@!S›óˆ+k@ÏY|xVb‹@ÏYåèFj80302304005@“zD2 W®@“~îm≈÷9@‘⁄~\ë—N@‘€˛\ë—NSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸdÏ®d ␀␀‰ôHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀X-ray reflection spectroscopy is one of the most effective means of probing the strong-field region near an event horizon. Modeling a reflection spectrum, one can constrain the spin of a black hole, the degree of truncation of the inner disk, the inclination of the system, and other key parameters. We request 80 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger four observations of the black hole binary GX 339-4 during the rise of its next outburst. Our principal goal is to track the radius of the inner edge of the accretion disk in the hard state as a function of luminosity, as we have done before using RXTE data. Using NuSTAR advanced capabilities, we aim to resolve the gross discrepancies in published measurements of the inner disk radius.                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE EVOLUTION OF THE ACCRETION DISK IN GX 339-4                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429NGC_3718                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏYåèFj@ÏYç[‰õø60301031007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸdª‘Ñ␀␀‰DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÍThe accretion flow in AGN changes its structure when the accretion rate drops. High power AGN are softer when brighter while low-luminosity sources do the opposite. The transition point is not well known, largely because of the imprecise determination of the spectral slope, whose dynamic range is very small. What happens to the accretion flow at low rates is also not well understood, either a RIAF or a jet are possible. In this proposal we target three low accretion rate AGN to measure their spectral slope, and confirm or reject spectral curvature, which would point to a RIAF. The targets belong to a flux limited sample covering a wide range in accretion rates. They fill the mid-low L/L_Edd range, crucial to follow the evolution of the X-ray emission at the turning point.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Arevalo                       Patricia                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TRACKING ACCRETION POWER INTO THE LOW LUMINOSITY REGIME                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3718                                          @e¶⁄πıY¥@JáÚ‰éär@bbLò_@N&¡Ω•@hÏbM“Ò™@ÏYç[‰õø@ÏY∂?r‘£60301031008@Î◊$Ô4÷°@Î¿:u%F@Ì¿äËßÁ@ÌøjËßÁSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸdK0*z␀␀‰DHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÍThe accretion flow in AGN changes its structure when the accretion rate drops. High power AGN are softer when brighter while low-luminosity sources do the opposite. The transition point is not well known, largely because of the imprecise determination of the spectral slope, whose dynamic range is very small. What happens to the accretion flow at low rates is also not well understood, either a RIAF or a jet are possible. In this proposal we target three low accretion rate AGN to measure their spectral slope, and confirm or reject spectral curvature, which would point to a RIAF. The targets belong to a flux limited sample covering a wide range in accretion rates. They fill the mid-low L/L_Edd range, crucial to follow the evolution of the X-ray emission at the turning point.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Arevalo                       Patricia                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TRACKING ACCRETION POWER INTO THE LOW LUMINOSITY REGIME                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏY∂?r‘£@ÏY∑èFj30302019001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸd9D•␀␀ÂYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @rπ4m≈÷9@Aî˝Û∂E¢@QÃæﬂ§@@	)^ûö@sºßÔù≤-@ÏY∑èFj@ÏY≈±9Ò30302019002@¬\4q≤ñ@√Cnóç@“–S+J@“”S+JSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸc	J∂Œ␀␀ÂYHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_J2032p4127                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏY≈±9Ò@ÏY∆èFj90302321001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸb÷`TÙ␀␀‚ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J2032p4127                                    @s@y¶µ@D∂¬&ÄùI@TÀ)^û?ÒVìtº@túæﬂ§@ÏY∆èFj@ÏY„î»)¯90302321002@„rR≤ïÈ‚@„cAÚ◊s@‰◊≥,•zx@‰Ÿ≥,•zxSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸbo·®≈␀␀‚ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2) DDT                                                                                                               archived  20200429NGC_1068                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏY„î»)¯@ÏY‰±9Ò60302003005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸb-Çÿ.␀␀‰°Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀«We propose four 50 ks NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC 1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2s. A flux excess above 20 keV was discovered by NuSTAR in the August 2014 observation, with respect to observations performed 20 months earlier, and 6 months later. This variability is ascribed to Compton-thick material (with column density NH>2.5 10^24 cm^-2 ) moving temporarily out from our line of sight, allowing the nuclear radiation to pierce through the circumnuclear absorbing material. NuSTAR is the only telescope in orbit that permits a search for such column density variations in this energy band. This monitoring program aims to estimate the distance of the variable absorber, to check if it is consistent with the pc-scale torus.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       LOCATING THE ABSORBING STRUCTURE IN NGC 1068 VIA HARD X-RAY MONITORING                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 arc