urvey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C345                                             @oX%ÆÊ1˘@C‰µ‹∆?@O∫{≤˛≈m@DÜ´6zë@pÍ#¢úwö@ÏZÇî»)¯@ÏZìÕ´∏160160647002@◊⁄b∂Æ}@◊√5a‰˜f@Ÿl>Ç@∑Ä@ŸkæÇ@∑ÄSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸKü1Æ◊␀␀‚ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M31_Field_E                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏZìÕ´∏1@ÏZîî»)¯50312002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸKmÀ©á␀␀‰HHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÚThe sensitivity and spatial resolving power of NuSTAR have allowed the study of individual neutron stars and black holes in other galaxies at hard energies for the first time, but they are nearly all more luminous than the population of sources probed in detail in the Milky Way. With a NuSTAR legacy survey of M31, we have finally detected sources at these lower luminosities, allowing a direct comparison of M31's neutron star and black hole accretors to the Milky Way's in the crucial window above 10 keV. We propose 4 additional pointings of M31 to double the number of >10^37 erg/s detected at hard energies to ensure the shape of the luminosity function is more robust to low number fluctuations. Also, we will classify the brightest sources, which is only possible with hard band data.        Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TOWARD A BETTER CENSUS OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN THE DISK OF M32                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_Field_E                                       @'‘˝Û∂F@E␀À)^û@^l”ZÖáî¿4Ò|⁄Q@bÎC,•@ÏZîî»)¯@ÏZ∏?r‘£50312002002@ÈOîFsÇ@È6Åä	@Î]†˘	l@Îù†˘	lSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸK≤°ê␀␀‰HHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÚThe sensitivity and spatial resolving power of NuSTAR have allowed the study of individual neutron stars and black holes in other galaxies at hard energies for the first time, but they are nearly all more luminous than the population of sources probed in detail in the Milky Way. With a NuSTAR legacy survey of M31, we have finally detected sources at these lower luminosities, allowing a direct comparison of M31's neutron star and black hole accretors to the Milky Way's in the crucial window above 10 keV. We propose 4 additional pointings of M31 to double the number of >10^37 erg/s detected at hard energies to ensure the shape of the luminosity function is more robust to low number fluctuations. Also, we will classify the brightest sources, which is only possible with hard band data.        Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TOWARD A BETTER CENSUS OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN THE DISK OF M32                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PSR_J2032p4127                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏZ∏?r‘£@ÏZ∏Õ´∏130302002003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸJ«ïŒ≤␀␀‰mHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9?PSR J2032+4127 is a Fermi and radio pulsar in the Cygnus OB2 association, powering a TeV pulsar wind nebula. It is in a highly eccentric 48 year orbit around a Be star. We propose for time-critical spectroscopic monitoring at periastron to look for the expected interaction of the pulsar with the wind and/or disk of the Be star. XMM-Newton is also able to test for pulsations at the 143 ms spin period that would be the signature of accretion onto the neutron star. These observations are important for comparison with PSR B1259-63, the prototype and only other Be/gamma-ray binary with a known pulsar, as well as to help understand gamma-ray binaries that have compact objects of uncertain type that may be neutron stars even though their pulsations have not been detected.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Observing PSR J2032+4127, a Be Gamma-ray Binary, at Periastron                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J2032p4127                                    @s@óˆ+jË@D∑4÷°aÂ@T/üæw?ÒK∆ßÔù≤@s•®Xy=Ÿ@ÏZ∏Õ´∏1@ÏZ÷xVb‹30302002004@‰⁄%±[W?@‰«∂ÜYJı@ÊI˙C˛]@ÊIø˙C˛]SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸJiX␀␀‰mHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9?PSR J2032+4127 is a Fermi and radio pulsar in the Cygnus OB2 association, powering a TeV pulsar wind nebula. It is in a highly eccentric 48 year orbit around a Be star. We propose for time-critical spectroscopic monitoring at periastron to look for the expected interaction of the pulsar with the wind and/or disk of the Be star. XMM-Newton is also able to test for pulsations at the 143 ms spin period that would be the signature of accretion onto the neutron star. These observations are important for comparison with PSR B1259-63, the prototype and only other Be/gamma-ray binary with a known pulsar, as well as to help understand gamma-ray binaries that have compact objects of uncertain type that may be neutron stars even though their pulsations have not been detected.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Observing PSR J2032+4127, a Be Gamma-ray Binary, at Periastron                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429M31_Field_F                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏZ÷xVb‹@ÏZ◊èFj50312003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸJ3cæƒ␀␀‰KHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÚThe sensitivity and spatial resolving power of NuSTAR have allowed the study of individual neutron stars and black holes in other galaxies at hard energies for the first time, but they are nearly all more luminous than the population of sources probed in detail in the Milky Way. With a NuSTAR legacy survey of M31, we have finally detected sources at these lower luminosities, allowing a direct comparison of M31's neutron star and black hole accretors to the Milky Way's in the crucial window above 10 keV. We propose 4 additional pointings of M31 to double the number of >10^37 erg/s detected at hard energies to ensure the shape of the luminosity function is more robust to low number fluctuations. Also, we will classify the brightest sources, which is only possible with hard band data.        Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TOWARD A BETTER CENSUS OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN THE DISK OF M33                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_Field_F                                       @&dM:í£@DÊ‹]càf@^Zqﬁi≠C¿5#n.±ƒ3@vqÊ1¯†ê@ÏZ◊èFj@ÏZ˘#Ü50312003002@ÁË∏q≤ñ@Á“jO@ÈéÚqﬁi≠@Èê2qﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸIÕé˚␀␀‰KHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÚThe sensitivity and spatial resolving power of NuSTAR have allowed the study of individual neutron stars and black holes in other galaxies at hard energies for the first time, but they are nearly all more luminous than the population of sources probed in detail in the Milky Way. With a NuSTAR legacy survey of M31, we have finally detected sources at these lower luminosities, allowing a direct comparison of M31's neutron star and black hole accretors to the Milky Way's in the crucial window above 10 keV. We propose 4 additional pointings of M31 to double the number of >10^37 erg/s detected at hard energies to ensure the shape of the luminosity function is more robust to low number fluctuations. Also, we will classify the brightest sources, which is only possible with hard band data.        Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TOWARD A BETTER CENSUS OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN THE DISK OF M33                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M31_Field_G                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏZ˘#Ü@ÏZ˘[‰õø50312004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸIî€¯j␀␀‰SHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÚThe sensitivity and spatial resolving power of NuSTAR have allowed the study of individual neutron stars and black holes in other galaxies at hard energies for the first time, but they are nearly all more luminous than the population of sources probed in detail in the Milky Way. With a NuSTAR legacy survey of M31, we have finally detected sources at these lower luminosities, allowing a direct comparison of M31's neutron star and black hole accretors to the Milky Way's in the crucial window above 10 keV. We propose 4 additional pointings of M31 to double the number of >10^37 erg/s detected at hard energies to ensure the shape of the luminosity function is more robust to low number fluctuations. Also, we will classify the brightest sources, which is only possible with hard band data.        Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TOWARD A BETTER CENSUS OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN THE DISK OF M34                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_Field_G                                       @&˘#¢úwö@DÁÑM;@^iOﬂ;dZ¿5$®¡T…Ü@v_z·GÆ@ÏZ˘[‰õø@Ï[#Ü50312004002@Áÿ2ı¬è\@Á¿O≠B√ @ÈÜÅ®Xy>@ÈáA®Xy>SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸI!"É␀␀‰SHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÚThe sensitivity and spatial resolving power of NuSTAR have allowed the study of individual neutron stars and black holes in other galaxies at hard energies for the first time, but they are nearly all more luminous than the population of sources probed in detail in the Milky Way. With a NuSTAR legacy survey of M31, we have finally detected sources at these lower luminosities, allowing a direct comparison of M31's neutron star and black hole accretors to the Milky Way's in the crucial window above 10 keV. We propose 4 additional pointings of M31 to double the number of >10^37 erg/s detected at hard energies to ensure the shape of the luminosity function is more robust to low number fluctuations. Also, we will classify the brightest sources, which is only possible with hard band data.        Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TOWARD A BETTER CENSUS OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN THE DISK OF M34                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk248                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[#Ü@Ï[èFj60363006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸHÔ4∏␀␀‚ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             