 the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ELS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429PKS2145p06                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[EÍM@Ï[F±9Ò60160768001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸGﬁ†#␀␀‚ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS2145p06                                        @tp+I∫@¡Ú◊s@O≤&ÄùIR¿@˚a@N•@s¬@N§®¡@Ï[F±9Ò@Ï[UèFj60160768002@‘ÿ´tºj@‘«|64@÷4‹ÃÃÃÕ@÷4‹ÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸGû>ı␀␀␀‚ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_00521m7054                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[UèFj@Ï[UÕ´∏160301029005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸGiX␀␀‰VHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀◊Previous X-ray observations of the Seyfert 2 galaxy IRAS00521 show evidence for some of the strongest reflection from the accretion disk observed among local, Compton-thin AGN. The unusually high reflection fraction indicates an extreme accretion geometry in which the intrinsic continuum emission experiences strong gravitational light bending, resulting in a broadband X-ray spectrum dominated by reflection from the inner accretion disk. However, IRAS00521 has never been observed in the hard X-ray band. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM (175ks) and NuSTAR (300ks). The quality of the data will enable us to robustly confirm the strong reflection, and utilize the full potential of the latest models to measure the black hole spin.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       STRONG DISK REFLECTION IN THE SEYFERT 2 AGN IRAS 00521-7054                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_00521m7054                                   @*˛“àŒp;¿Q™´6zë@rÎ8}øH¿G/'ª/ÏW@q°¢3úø@Ï[UÕ´∏1@Ï[ÖÕ´∏160301029006@Ú*ÆßÜ¬'@Ú íw1è≈@Ûk◊öu@ÛmGöuSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸFÎùdÅ␀␀‰VHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀◊Previous X-ray observations of the Seyfert 2 galaxy IRAS00521 show evidence for some of the strongest reflection from the accretion disk observed among local, Compton-thin AGN. The unusually high reflection fraction indicates an extreme accretion geometry in which the intrinsic continuum emission experiences strong gravitational light bending, resulting in a broadband X-ray spectrum dominated by reflection from the inner accretion disk. However, IRAS00521 has never been observed in the hard X-ray band. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM (175ks) and NuSTAR (300ks). The quality of the data will enable us to robustly confirm the strong reflection, and utilize the full potential of the latest models to measure the black hole spin.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       STRONG DISK REFLECTION IN THE SEYFERT 2 AGN IRAS 00521-7054                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀CVZ                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SN2017gas                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[ÖÕ´∏1@Ï[ÜÍM80202052001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸF¥Vxõ␀␀‰DHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    KContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program is designed to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. The overarching goal is to constrain the nature of the physical process responsible for mass loss in evolved massive stars.                Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      R                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       Mapping extreme mass loss from evolved massive stars with coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR observations                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2017gas                                         @sQŒ_@M›/üæ@W*cä	@(˘¶µ(@s-ÃÃÃÃÕ@Ï[ÜÍM@Ï[™èFj80202052002@Í ¨6‚Î@ÍRLò_@Î‹îŒp:˚@Î‹¥Œp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸFUmõ␀␀‰DHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  KContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program is designed to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. The overarching goal is to constrain the nature of the physical process responsible for mass loss in evolved massive stars.                Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      R                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       Mapping extreme mass loss from evolved massive stars with coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR observations                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NUSTARJ1206p4957                                  @f≥»K]Ãd@H˚nóçP@aô—N;Õ6@P\ÎÌ˙C˛@hK:í£S@Ï[™èFj@Ï[™±9Ò60301012001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸFi–␀␀‰VHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀;Through a joint NuSTAR+XMM/Chandra spectroscopic analysis we have obtained the column density (Nh) distribution for a sample of 63 bright hard-band detected AGN selected in the NuSTAR survey fields. This is the most sensitive Compton Thick (CT) AGN search to date from a blind >10 keV sample. The two faintest candidates for being the most obscured AGN in the sample lack low-energy data and have Nh upper/lower limits consistent with being heavily obscured/CT. We are proposing a joint NuSTAR+XMM observation of these sources in order to complete the CT census. This will effectively double the observed fraction of CT AGN in our sample and will give us the opportunity to challenge X-ray Background population synthesis models directly aiming at the major contributors to the 20-30 keV peak.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zappacosta                    Luca                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CONSTRAINING THE NON-LOCAL COMPTON THICK AGN FRACTION IN THE NUSTAR 8-24 KEV BAND                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NUSTARJ1206p4957                                  @f≤‡uˆ˝"@H˙ÙÿD–@aõÊö‘,@P\z·GÆ@h@WßÜ¬'@Ï[™±9Ò@Ï[œ?r‘£60301012002@Ëªﬂá+@ËÆL£S&@ÍÖÅº”ZÜ@Íä!º”ZÜSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸE∏!_Ù␀␀‰VHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀;Through a joint NuSTAR+XMM/Chandra spectroscopic analysis we have obtained the column density (Nh) distribution for a sample of 63 bright hard-band detected AGN selected in the NuSTAR survey fields. This is the most sensitive Compton Thick (CT) AGN search to date from a blind >10 keV sample. The two faintest candidates for being the most obscured AGN in the sample lack low-energy data and have Nh upper/lower limits consistent with being heavily obscured/CT. We are proposing a joint NuSTAR+XMM observation of these sources in order to complete the CT census. This will effectively double the observed fraction of CT AGN in our sample and will give us the opportunity to challenge X-ray Background population synthesis models directly aiming at the major contributors to the 20-30 keV peak.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zappacosta                    Luca                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CONSTRAINING THE NON-LOCAL COMPTON THICK AGN FRACTION IN THE NUSTAR 8-24 KEV BAND                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429PSR_J2032p4127                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï[œ?r‘£@Ï[–èFj90302321003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸEÄ¬.E␀␀‚ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison             