                     ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC2712                                           @`‹˘	kπå@Fye+”√a@eÖáì›ò@Dhı¬è\)@c1‚Ç@∑Ä@Ï]^xVb‹@Ï]m[‰õø60161342002@—Ki=Ÿc@—HïDg8@“ü>´6z@“§[>´6zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ<úA;ã␀␀‚˜Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PSR_J2032p4127                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï]m[‰õø@Ï]n#Ü30302002005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ<s|ç␀␀‰mHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9?PSR J2032+4127 is a Fermi and radio pulsar in the Cygnus OB2 association, powering a TeV pulsar wind nebula. It is in a highly eccentric 48 year orbit around a Be star. We propose for time-critical spectroscopic monitoring at periastron to look for the expected interaction of the pulsar with the wind and/or disk of the Be star. XMM-Newton is also able to test for pulsations at the 143 ms spin period that would be the signature of accretion onto the neutron star. These observations are important for comparison with PSR B1259-63, the prototype and only other Be/gamma-ray binary with a known pulsar, as well as to help understand gamma-ray binaries that have compact objects of uncertain type that may be neutron stars even though their pulsations have not been detected.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Observing PSR J2032+4127, a Be Gamma-ray Binary, at Periastron                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J2032p4127                                    @s@…ÓÀ˚@D∂i≠B√ @T5®Xy>?Ò∏QÎÖ@r{P∞Ú{≥@Ï]n#Ü@Ï]åèFj30302002006@‰Gz¡Ω•@‰8·S&@Â∫d¬¯8@Âª∆d¬¯8SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ<i␀␀‰mHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9?PSR J2032+4127 is a Fermi and radio pulsar in the Cygnus OB2 association, powering a TeV pulsar wind nebula. It is in a highly eccentric 48 year orbit around a Be star. We propose for time-critical spectroscopic monitoring at periastron to look for the expected interaction of the pulsar with the wind and/or disk of the Be star. XMM-Newton is also able to test for pulsations at the 143 ms spin period that would be the signature of accretion onto the neutron star. These observations are important for comparison with PSR B1259-63, the prototype and only other Be/gamma-ray binary with a known pulsar, as well as to help understand gamma-ray binaries that have compact objects of uncertain type that may be neutron stars even though their pulsations have not been detected.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Halpern                       Jules                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Observing PSR J2032+4127, a Be Gamma-ray Binary, at Periastron                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(3/3) / Kp=5                                                                                                            archived  20200429LEDA100168                                        @nH4m≈÷9@U@ßÊö‘@]¢sÅ◊€ı@>:πıY≥–@mk*0U2a@Ï]åèFj@Ï]åî»)¯60160631001@{≠÷8ÜYK@{≤sÅ◊€ı@–␀␀␀␀␀@–␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ;Âïn␀␀‚ÙHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429LEDA100168                                        @nC@N§©@UAjOv@]£»K]Ãd@>;ò«‚ÇA@md0U2a|@Ï]åî»)¯@Ï]ñ#Ü60160631002@◊°úåÁ∞@◊çÖ	kπå@Ÿ/¿␀␀␀␀@Ÿ/¿␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ;ßN„␀␀‚ÙHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ CVZ                                                                                                                    archived  20200429NGC_3516                                          @dÿõ•„S¯@R%Êö‘,=@`ß>´6z@E"£S&@d¿SéÛMj@Ï]ñ#Ü@Ï]ñxVb‹60302016001@e4üæv»¥@e≤ïÈ‚@fékP∞Ú|@fékP∞Ú|SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ;{£uÛ␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC_3516                                          @d◊ˇ.HËß@R%•ú‡v@`ßã¨q≥@E"¨ŸË>B@d∫Ÿá+@Ï]ñxVb‹@Ï]ß[‰õø60302016002@‹∑ä	@‹åé@ﬁ+ÏV’–@ﬁÎÏV’–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ;+¡Õ␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429UGC_8621                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï]ß[‰õø@Ï]ßÕ´∏160368003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ:¯Çw␀␀‰hHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‡We propose to use a complete, volume-limited sample of AGN to obtain an accurate measurement of the column density distribution of AGN in the local universe. At the same time, we will measure the volume emissivity of hard X-ray emission in the local universe, which can be an important component in ionization and feedback. The survey is modest, and reasonable exposure times have been estimated with the aid of the Swift/BAT 105-month catalog. NuSTAR has only observed a few sources from this sample, but we need only 420 ks to observe the remaining sources in a quasi-uniform manner.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF OBSCURED AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC_8621                                          @ié‰éäqﬁ@Cî]càeï@UÎµsÍ≥@R¶!ñRΩ<@h⁄’œ™Õü@Ï]ßÕ´∏1@Ï]≤ÍM60368003002@Ãi¬º£@ÃU+Ö∏R@ŒÉ›ÑM;@ŒÜ›ÑM;SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ:π ␀␀‰hHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡We propose to use a complete, volume-limited sample of AGN to obtain an accurate measurement of the column density distribution of AGN in the local universe. At the same time, we will measure the volume emissivity of hard X-ray emission in the local universe, which can be an important component in ionization and feedback. The survey is modest, and reasonable exposure times have been estimated with the aid of the Swift/BAT 105-month catalog. NuSTAR has only observed a few sources from this sample, but we need only 420 ks to observe the remaining sources in a quasi-uniform manner.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF OBSCURED AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Swift_J0243d6p6124                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï]≤ÍM@Ï]≥±9Ò90302319007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÁj+Ê5€␀␀‚ÙHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J0243d6p6124                                @Dj{≤˛≈m@Nµ®Xy=Ÿ@`˘;dZ¨?ÙDg8}ø@4)^û	@Ï]≥±9Ò@Ï]æ±9Ò90302319008@±ÏÚ©ì·@≥GÜ¬&Å@–JPH@–I,PHSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÁjg∫7_␀␀‚ÙHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀”␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ToO / data gap 211s                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC_3982                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï]æ±9Ò@Ï]ø[‰õø60375001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ9ˇ>␀␀‰]Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˘To determine the population of heavily obscured AGN, we have searched the complete 12um Seyfert Galaxy sample for likely Compton-thick candidates. In it we identified 10 Seyfert 2's as candidates, with XMM 2-10keV flux at least 10 times weaker than predicted from their [OIII] line fluxes, and also not detected by BAT (except for one, weakly). NuSTAR observations of five of the candidates confirms that four are currently accreting at a much higher rate than XMM found. For a statistically robust measure of the volume density and other properties of Compton-thick AGN in the local Universe, we propose NuSTAR observations of the remaining 5 candidates to see how strongly their MBHs are accreting, if at all. Supplemented with spectropolarimetry at Keck, we will test torus unification models.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Malkan                        Matthew                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       OBSCURED ACCRETING MASSIVE BLACK HOLES: SURVEY OF THE COMPLETE SAMPLE OF X-RAY WEAK 12UM AGN                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3982                                          @feÆz·H@Kíe+”√@a\‘,<ûÌ@NA [¿@fw–|Ñµ›@Ï]ø[‰õø@Ï]÷î»)¯60375001002@‡PPy=Ÿ@‡G\[¿7@·x¯Ÿá+@·y¯Ÿá+SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ8™€6<␀␀‰]Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˘To determine the population of heavily obscured AGN, we have searched the complete 12um Seyfert Galaxy sample for likely Compton-thick candidates. In it we identified 10 Seyfert 2's as candidates, with XMM 2-10keV flux at least 10 times weaker than predicted from their [OIII] line fluxes, and also not detected by BAT (except for one, weakly). NuSTAR observations of five of the candidates confirms that four are currently accreting at a much higher rate than XMM found. For a statistically robust measure of the volume density and other properties of Compton-thick AGN in the local Universe, we propose NuSTAR observations of the remaining 5 candidates to see how strongly their MBHs are accreting, if at all. Supplemented with spectropolarimetry at Keck, we will test torus unification models.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Malkan                        Matthew                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       OBSCURED ACCRETING MASSIVE BLACK HOLES: SURVEY OF THE COMPLETE SAMPLE OF X-RAY WEAK 12UM AGN                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_3516                                          @dŸ*0U2a@R%÷8ÜYK@`ßº@E#¸PH@dÇT`™d√@Ï]÷î»)¯@Ï]÷ÍM60302016003@gÚ‘,<ûÌ@g’`Aâ7L@ivv»¥9X@ivv»¥9XSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸnz·␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC_3516                                          @d◊Ö∏QÏ@R%ÑM:ì@`ß¡Ω•ù@E"ïÈ·∞ä@dy‰%ÆÊ2@Ï]÷ÍM@Ï]Í±9Ò60302016004@ﬂÙ;CïÅ@ﬂ‰Ÿóˆ+k@·@ÁØ∏@·`ÁØ∏SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ‚jÛ␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC0449                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï]Í±9Ò@Ï]ÎxVb‹60360002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸﬁá∆Z␀␀‚˛Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC0449                                           @3éäqﬁj@@àoiDg8@`¶L/É{¿=°ﬁi≠Bƒ@t≥"–Â`B@Ï]ÎxVb‹@Ï^xVb‹60360002002@ﬂÔ⁄QŒ@ﬂ◊0z·GÆ@·3ÆÊ1˘@·ÆÊ1˘SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸzi␀␀‚˛Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ELS NuLANDS                                                                                                             archived  20200429NGC_1313_X1                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï^xVb‹@Ï^[‰õø30302016009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ6Â‘ƒ␀␀‰dHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀IWe request 5x75ks NuSTAR observations of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) NGC1313 X-1 (375ks in total), to be coordinated with the very large XMM-Newton (750ks) and Chandra (500ks) follow-up programs our team has been awarded to study its extreme (~0.2-0.25c) outflow. The addition of NuSTAR observations to these programs will allow us to perform multi-epoch broadband spectroscopy, robustly determining the continuum and thus allowing us to better study the ionization structure and variability of this outflow, as well as undertaking broadband spectral variability studies for comparison with other ULXs. NGC1313 X-1 is the only source for which this is currently possible, so it is vital we take advantage of this unique opportunity.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTROSCOPY OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE NGC1313 X-1                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1313_X1                                       @HÕzxl"h¿P†„º”ZÜ@q∑„º”¿FL<ûÏø±@p÷‹]càf@Ï^[‰õø@Ï^>Õ´∏130302016010@ÛZ÷jO@ÛO–|Ñ∂@Ù≥√·∞â†@Ùµc·∞â†SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ¶«}â␀␀‰dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀IWe request 5x75ks NuSTAR observations of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) NGC1313 X-1 (375ks in total), to be coordinated with the very large XMM-Newton (750ks) and Chandra (500ks) follow-up programs our team has been awarded to study its extreme (~0.2-0.25c) outflow. The addition of NuSTAR observations to these programs will allow us to perform multi-epoch broadband spectroscopy, robustly determining the continuum and thus allowing us to better study the ionization structure and variability of this outflow, as well as undertaking broadband spectral variability studies for comparison with other ULXs. NGC1313 X-1 is the only source for which this is currently possible, so it is vital we take advantage of this unique opportunity.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTROSCOPY OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE NGC1313 X-1                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                             