¶@dVìu@Ï^>Õ´∏1@Ï^@#Ü60302016005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸhK⁄␀␀‰xHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3516                                          @dŸüUõ=@R&1¯†ê.@`¶ WßÜ¬@E"«À)_@d‚ÎC-@Ï^@#Ü@Ï^SÕ´∏160302016006@‡ÍL/É{@‡7‹∆?@·6Q•ú‡@·7—•ú‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÉ˚␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429III_Zw_2                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï^SÕ´∏1@Ï^T±9Ò60301014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ‚≥ƒ÷␀␀‰dHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀?In this proposal, we seek to test the new magnetic flux paradigm for AGN jet production, which claims that the magnetic flux threading the black hole is the dominant factor determining the wide distribution of observed jet powers. We propose to constrain the black hole spin values of two radio intermediate quasars by using joint NuSTAR-XMM observations, and compare them to the jet magnetic flux measurements obtained via radio observations with the Very Long Baseline Array. The jet production efficiency in these sources is lower compared to powerful radio-loud quasars and we aim to find out, if this is due to their low black hole spin value, or them having failed to accumulate enough magnetic flux.                                                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koljonen                      Karri                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TESTING THE MAGNETIC FLUX PARADIGM WITH RADIO INTERMEDIATE QUASARS                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316III_Zw_2                                          @o␀h€å@%Á8}øH@Z¶_ÿ≠´ü¿IWä	ﬁ@sôÜ¬&Äù@Ï^T±9Ò@Ï^ßÍM60301014002@ˆ‡/üæ@ˆ–µsÎ@¯¶j±ƒ2 @¯™®Ô4÷°SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ%§8!␀␀‰dHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀?In this proposal, we seek to test the new magnetic flux paradigm for AGN jet production, which claims that the magnetic flux threading the black hole is the dominant factor determining the wide distribution of observed jet powers. We propose to constrain the black hole spin values of two radio intermediate quasars by using joint NuSTAR-XMM observations, and compare them to the jet magnetic flux measurements obtained via radio observations with the Very Long Baseline Array. The jet production efficiency in these sources is lower compared to powerful radio-loud quasars and we aim to find out, if this is due to their low black hole spin value, or them having failed to accumulate enough magnetic flux.                                                                                              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koljonen                      Karri                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       TESTING THE MAGNETIC FLUX PARADIGM WITH RADIO INTERMEDIATE QUASARS                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HESS_J0632p057                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï^ßÍM@Ï^©èFj30362001003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ·_Û›␀␀‚˛Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESS_J0632p057                                    @XëQÇ©ì@@ùIQÇ™@i±óˆ+jËø˘∂Æ}VlÙ@i†ËßÊõ@Ï^©èFj@Ï^Õ#Ü30362001004@Ë=Å∞â†'@Ë+Ãõ•„T@ÈÍ␀—∑@ÈÍ–␀—∑SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸx˚‘␀␀‚˛Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              VERITAS                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO500_34                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï^Õ#Ü@Ï^ÕÕ´∏160061100001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸHπ␀␀‚˛Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO500_34    