¶L@”ÜkP∞Ú@”xÍJåSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ¸5ß␀␀‚˛Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_3621                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï^⁄[‰õø@Ï^⁄±9Ò60371002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÕ-„Ô␀␀‰kHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ùWe propose for a total of 150 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations to characterise the intrinsic power and obscuring properties of four of the nearest active galactic nuclei (AGN) to us. We are carrying out the first complete and direct measurement of the column density (N_H) distribution of AGN in a local volume-limited (D < 15 Mpc) AGN survey using primarily NuSTAR. Prior to our program, only 50% of the AGN had reliable obscuration measurements. We have recently improved this to 75%, and found a Compton-thick AGN fraction of at least 30%. This is higher than that directly measured in flux-limited AGN samples. NuSTAR observations of these four targets will be crucial in bringing the local N_H distribution of AGN to 100% completeness.                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Annuar                        Ady                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A COMPLETE CENSUS OF THE COMPTON-THICK AGN POPULATION IN OUR COSMIC BACKYARD                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3621                                          @e3º¿@fÛMj@qê<ûÏø±@:"GE8Ô5@ftSéÛM@Ï^⁄±9Ò@Ï^èFj60371002002@ﬁÍ_ÿ≠¨@›Ú˚Álã@‡öq≤ñ@‡öq≤ñSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸx!_Ù␀␀‰kHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ùWe propose for a total of 150 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations to characterise the intrinsic power and obscuring properties of four of the nearest active galactic nuclei (AGN) to us. We are carrying out the first complete and direct measurement of the column density (N_H) distribution of AGN in a local volume-limited (D < 15 Mpc) AGN survey using primarily NuSTAR. Prior to our program, only 50% of the AGN had reliable obscuration measurements. We have recently improved this to 75%, and found a Compton-thick AGN fraction of at least 30%. This is higher than that directly measured in flux-limited AGN samples. NuSTAR observations of these four targets will be crucial in bringing the local N_H distribution of AGN to 100% completeness.                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Annuar                        Ady                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A COMPLETE CENSUS OF THE COMPTON-THICK AGN POPULATION IN OUR COSMIC BACKYARD                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HE_1143m1810                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï^èFj@Ï^[‰õø60302002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸLúA<␀␀‰vHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¡We propose a joint NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of the high accretion rate Seyfert 1 HE 1143-1810 (L/Ledd>0.5), consisting of 5x20 ks observations spaced by 2-3 days. The source is an ideal candidate to obtain high sensitivity measurements over a broad (UV/hard X-rays) high-energy spectrum, which will allow us to: 1) study the UV/X-ray spectrum with the highest possible accuracy and test physically motivated Comptonization models, 2) investigate the nature of the soft X-ray excess, and 3) put firm conclusions on the reflection component. These observations will allow for a direct comparison with different sources, in particular with a similar, successful NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of NGC 4593 (L/Ledd<0.15).                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE NUSTAR-XMM MONITORING OF THE HIGH ACCRETION RATE SEYFERT 1 HE1143-1810                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HE_1143m1810                                      @fh€ã¨q¿2q@N§©@qô^ûö@D‡-‡q@fÊö‘,@Ï^[‰õø@Ï^˛Õ´∏160302002002@‘woiDg@‘jÚ˛≈m]@’Ï`£n/@’Ô`£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ∆ZD␀␀‰vHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀¡We propose a joint NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of the high accretion rate Seyfert 1 HE 1143-1810 (L/Ledd>0.5), consisting of 5x20 ks observations spaced by 2-3 days. The source is an ideal candidate to obtain high sensitivity measurements over a broad (UV/hard X-rays) high-energy spectrum, which will allow us to: 1) study the UV/X-ray spectrum with the highest possible accuracy and test physically motivated Comptonization models, 2) investigate the nature of the soft X-ray excess, and 3) put firm conclusions on the reflection component. These observations will allow for a direct comparison with different sources, in particular with a similar, successful NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of NGC 4593 (L/Ledd<0.15).                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE NUSTAR-XMM MONITORING OF THE HIGH ACCRETION RATE SEYFERT 1 HE1143-1810                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Eta_Carinae                                       @d*h	‘ï¿M◊æv»¥9@q¯"h	‘ïø‚oiDg8@f)àeîØO@Ï^˛Õ´∏1@Ï^ˇ[‰õø30301022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ⁄ºﬂ␀␀‰kHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀»The massive stellar binary system Eta Carinae drives strong shocks by the collision of winds from two stars. Our NuSTAR observations of Eta Carinae in 2015 and 2016 detected an extremely hard X-ray component over the thermal emission above 15 keV. The apparent absence of this component during the 2-10 keV X-ray minimum around periastron indicates that the source is located very close to the wind colliding region. This component may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV by accelerated non-thermal particles and may be related to the Fermi GeV gamma-ray source. We propose a NuSTAR observation of the star in AO3 to monitor its variation when the gamma-ray emission above 10 GeV is expected to increase, and thus establish a crucial correlation between the two ranges.           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       ON THE PATH OF UNDERSTANDING THE EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY COMPONENT OF ETA CARINAE                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                           