¶L0@Dﬂó$tSè@fX≈m\˙≠@Ï_Ú#Ü@Ï`ÍM60302002010@’⁄û8ÜYK@’ÀG™ÕûÑ@◊]ÿÍJå@◊_òÍJåSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÆÓÓÔ␀␀‰vHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀¡We propose a joint NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of the high accretion rate Seyfert 1 HE 1143-1810 (L/Ledd>0.5), consisting of 5x20 ks observations spaced by 2-3 days. The source is an ideal candidate to obtain high sensitivity measurements over a broad (UV/hard X-rays) high-energy spectrum, which will allow us to: 1) study the UV/X-ray spectrum with the highest possible accuracy and test physically motivated Comptonization models, 2) investigate the nature of the soft X-ray excess, and 3) put firm conclusions on the reflection component. These observations will allow for a direct comparison with different sources, in particular with a similar, successful NuSTAR/XMM monitoring of NGC 4593 (L/Ledd<0.15).                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE NUSTAR-XMM MONITORING OF THE HIGH ACCRETION RATE SEYFERT 1 HE1143-1810                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(5/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO_121mG6                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï`ÍM@Ï`î»)¯60201063001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÉi–7␀␀‰nHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    Y!We propose for Chandra and NuSTAR observations of two local AGNs to characterise their obscuring properties. We are using Chandra and NuSTAR to form the first complete measurement of the column density (N H) distribution of AGN at D<15 Mpc. Even at this distance the distribution was only 50% complete. We have recently improved this, and found a Compton-thick (CT) AGN fraction of >35%. We also found that Chandra resolution is key in resolving the AGN from off-nuclear X-ray sources. When combined with NuSTAR, this allow us to accurately characterise the broadband spectrum of the AGN, and identify it as CT. These new observations will provide Chandra data for all D<15Mpc AGNs and boost up the N H distribution up to 85% complete. This will be fully completed with future NuSTAR observations.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Annuar                        A                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       Towards A Complete Census of the Compton-thick AGN Population in our Cosmic Backyard                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_121mG6                                        @V˙ßÔù≤-¿NÈ ¿Éo@pÔ◊s¸P¿<—:í£S@n–á¸π#£@Ï`î»)¯@Ï`&?r‘£60201063002@Ëv+J@Ëe´Ç©ì@Í ﬁwökQ@Í!˛wökQSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ$ÄÚ∫␀␀‰nHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  Y!We propose for Chandra and NuSTAR observations of two local AGNs to characterise their obscuring properties. We are using Chandra and NuSTAR to form the first complete measurement of the column density (N H) distribution of AGN at D<15 Mpc. Even at this distance the distribution was only 50% complete. We have recently improved this, and found a Compton-thick (CT) AGN fraction of >35%. We also found that Chandra resolution is key in resolving the AGN from off-nuclear X-ray sources. When combined with NuSTAR, this allow us to accurately characterise the broadband spectrum of the AGN, and identify it as CT. These new observations will provide Chandra data for all D<15Mpc AGNs and boost up the N H distribution up to 85% complete. This will be fully completed with future NuSTAR observations.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Annuar                        A                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       Towards A Complete Census of the Compton-thick AGN Population in our Cosmic Backyard                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_3516                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï`&?r‘£@Ï`'xVb‹60302016007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÛ¨ê␀␀‰xHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3516                                          @d◊Ω•ú‡@R&öu@`ßxF‹]@E!∫^5?}@b„º”@Ï`'xVb‹@Ï`5xVb‹60302016008@’ƒä∞ ƒú@’∂∫␀—∑@◊K8£n/@◊N∏£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ∑®	␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2a/2)                                                                                                                  archived  202004293C_111                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï`5xVb‹@Ï`6#Ü60202061001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸè§˙P␀␀‰uHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    X∑The discovery of X-ray emission from AGN jets is a touchstone of the Chandra mission. Their X-ray emission processes have become the source of much debate, with implications for both jet physics and cluster feedback models. 3C111 has an extraordinary 2-arcminute long jet that is seen in X-ray, near-IR and radio. At least 8 knots, plus both approaching and receding hotspots and lobes, are seen in the Chandra image. We request additional Chandra, HST and NuSTAR observations, to follow up on possible evidence of variability in two knots, pin down the knots' X-ray spectrum and SED, detect the jet above 10 keV, and study the morphology and X-ray spectrum of the extended lobes. These observations will place the tightest constraints yet on the physics of this fascinating system.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Perlman                       E S                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       The 3C111 Jet: X-ray Variability, Spectrum & Broadband SED                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C_111                                            @P$º£@B˛RΩ<6@d1‹∆?¿"¸PH@vOv`@Ï`6#Ü@Ï`DÍM60202061002@‘“„æv»¥@‘ΩXõ•„T@÷Xi≠Bƒ@÷Xi≠BƒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸEOC„␀␀‰uHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  X∑The discovery of X-ray emission from AGN jets is a touchstone of the Chandra mission. Their X-ray emission processes have become the source of much debate, with implications for both jet physics and cluster feedback models. 3C111 has an extraordinary 2-arcminute long jet that is seen in X-ray, near-IR and radio. At least 8 knots, plus both approaching and receding hotspots and lobes, are seen in the Chandra image. We request additional Chandra, HST and NuSTAR observations, to follow up on possible evidence of variability in two knots, pin down the knots' X-ray spectrum and SED, detect the jet above 10 keV, and study the morphology and X-ray spectrum of the extended lobes. These observations will place the tightest constraints yet on the physics of this fascinating system.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Perlman                       E S                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       The 3C111 Jet: X-ray Variability, Spectrum & Broadband SED                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1a/2)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429UGC_7342                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï`DÍM@Ï`EÍM60369004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ[«ﬂ␀␀‰xHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÂWe identified a sample of low-z galaxies with extended AGN photoionised clouds indicative of a powerful AGN, the Voorwerpjes (VP). Far-IR and X-ray data suggest that the current luminosity of some of these AGN is lower than what expected from the VP. This may signify that the AGN dropped in luminosity in the last 10-100 kyr, but the clouds remain lit up because of the travel time of the ionising photons. NuSTAR observations are the best probe of the current AGN state. We propose to observe 4 VP related galaxies with no hard X-ray data available, completing the investigation of the shut down AGN candidates sample. We will probe if the AGN are shutting down, and the magnitude of the drop in luminosity. With this sample we will study AGN variability on previously unreachable timescales.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sartori                       Lia                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE LIFECYCLE OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES ON 10-100 KYR TIMESCALES                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC_7342                                          @g]ÃcÒA@=CàeîØO@hJìtº@TçŸb∂Æ@eÕ
