 NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3516                                          @d÷{≤˛≈m@R%è≈Åo@`®¡Ω•@E!€"–Â`@a|Aâ7K«@Ï`¶#Ü@Ï`ªî»)¯60302016012@‡GÍ,<ûÌ@‡<√4÷°b@·dñe+‘@·g6e+‘SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ,/5␀␀‰xHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LWe propose two 90-ks NuSTAR observations of NGC 3516 with a cadence of three weeks, to be executed contemporaneously with the already-scheduled 90-ks Chandra/LETGS observations in 2017. The NuSTAR data will enable tracking of broadband X-ray spectral and flux changes in this notoriously variable source and will also facilitate accurate modeling of its AGN continuum. This will significantly augment the science return of the Chandra observations, allowing more accurate and precise measurements of the properties of a new obscuring wind from this AGN as well as providing important insight into the driving mechanism(s) of AGN winds and their role in AGN/galaxy coevolution. The NuSTAR data will also yield the most accurate probe of the corona and reflection spectrum in this source.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE OBSCURING WIND, DISK AND CORONA IN NGC 3516                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2c/2)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429B1422p231                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï`ªî»)¯@Ï`º#Ü60301020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸøÁ∫␀␀‰|Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀}We propose NuSTAR observations of two among the X-ray brightest (F_0.5-10 > 5 10^-13 ergs s^-1 cm^-2, L_2-10 > 10^45 ergs s^-1) radio-quiet QSOs at z > 1.8, complemented by a joint, short XMM-Newton exposure for one of them. Our total time request is 250ks with NuSTAR and 30ks with XMM-Newton. By looking at rest-frame energies up to ~ 200-300 keV, our goal is to measure unambiguously the presence of spectral cut-offs at rest-frame energies as high as E_c=500 keV. This is not possible for targets in the local Universe, where NuSTAR is just sensitive to E_c < 250 keV. Our sample is unique and will allow unprecedented measurements of E_c in distant AGN with such X-ray luminosities. Our program will prove for the first time whether there is a physical limit to the temperature of AGN           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanzuisi                      Giorgio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE HIGH-ENERGY CUT-OFF IN HIGH-Z QSOS: A FIRST CUT                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316B1422p231                                         @kL/É{J@6Aâ7K«@:Ò&Èx‘˛@Q*Èx‘˝Ù@g8ÎÌ˙C˛@Ï`º#Ü@Ïa#Ü60301020002@¯ª#⁄πıZ@¯¶'RT`™@˙àÙá¸π@˙àØÙá¸πSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸTz·H␀␀‰|Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀}We propose NuSTAR observations of two among the X-ray brightest (F_0.5-10 > 5 10^-13 ergs s^-1 cm^-2, L_2-10 > 10^45 ergs s^-1) radio-quiet QSOs at z > 1.8, complemented by a joint, short XMM-Newton exposure for one of them. Our total time request is 250ks with NuSTAR and 30ks with XMM-Newton. By looking at rest-frame energies up to ~ 200-300 keV, our goal is to measure unambiguously the presence of spectral cut-offs at rest-frame energies as high as E_c=500 keV. This is not possible for targets in the local Universe, where NuSTAR is just sensitive to E_c < 250 keV. Our sample is unique and will allow unprecedented measurements of E_c in distant AGN with such X-ray luminosities. Our program will prove for the first time whether there is a physical limit to the temperature of AGN           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanzuisi                      Giorgio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE HIGH-ENERGY CUT-OFF IN HIGH-Z QSOS: A FIRST CUT                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PG1100p772                                        @dƒ`™d¬¯@SAoiDg@`Kì›óˆ+@C2∂Æ}Vm@`∑2a|⁄@Ïa#Ü@ÏaÕ´∏160463031001@Äy}!ˇ.I@Äeò«‚ÇA@ÅÄ␀␀␀␀␀@ÅÄ␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ
ÛîJV␀␀„#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429PG1100p772                                        @d¬ç∏∫«@S@ò_ˆî@`LwökP±@C4,<ûÏ¿@`ÆtSéÛM@ÏaÕ´∏1@Ïa±9Ò60463031002@”V™ç∏∫«@”GÃ}øH@‘ü≈-w1ê@‘£Ö-w1êSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ
ß“}(␀␀„#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ CVZ                                                                                                                    archived  20200429IGR_J21247p5058                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïa±9Ò@Ïa?r‘£60301005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ
a¡␀␀␀‰yHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‰What determines the coronal temperature in AGN is much debated. One plausible possibility is the Pair Thermostat, where heating drives the temperature up to the point at which electron-positron pairs are produced in significant quantity to control further temperature rise. Hybrid plasmas, in which most pairs are produced by a small nonthermal fraction, can lead to still lower coronal temperatures. We propose to use NuSTAR to observe 3 hard X-ray bright AGN with low temperatures (30-40 keV) measured by INTEGRAL in order to test this hypothesis. NuSTAR will give snapshot spectra of the objects, correct for relativistic reflection and be at much higher precision than Integral.                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE PAIR CONSTRAINT ON CORONAL TEMPERATURES IN SEYFERT 1 GALAXIES                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J21247p5058                                   @tú‡uˆ˝@IwÄ4m≈÷@WJ±ƒ2 X?ÿ%ÆÊ1¯°@qp»K]Ãd@Ïa?r‘£@Ïa4Õ´∏160301005002@„≤DAâ7L@„£„º”@Â'Ÿò«‚Ç@Â'yò«‚ÇSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ	ı¶‡␀␀‰yHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‰What determines the coronal temperature in AGN is much debated. One plausible possibility is the Pair Thermostat, where heating drives the temperature up to the point at which electron-positron pairs are produced in significant quantity to control further temperature rise. Hybrid plasmas, in which most pairs are produced by a small nonthermal fraction, can lead to still lower coronal temperatures. We propose to use NuSTAR to observe 3 hard X-ray bright AGN with low temperatures (30-40 keV) measured by INTEGRAL in order to test this hypothesis. NuSTAR will give snapshot spectra of the objects, correct for relativistic reflection and be at much higher precision than Integral.                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE PAIR CONSTRAINT ON CORONAL TEMPERATURES IN SEYFERT 1 GALAXIES                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_0123p25                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïa4Õ´∏1@Ïa5[‰õø60367001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ	øF*␀␀‰áHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≈We propose to observe 4 blazars at z>2, detected in the gamma-rays by Fermi/LAT and in the soft X-rays, but not yet observed at energies larger than 10 keV. They are among the most powerful blazars and they host black holes whose masses exceed 1e9 Msun. The total requested time of 80 ks is sufficient to establish flux and slope of their X-ray continuum in order to estimate their total electromagnetic output and peak frequency, combined with the Fermi gamma-ray data. This will shed light on the jet-accretion relation, on the controversy about the blazar sequence at its high power extreme, and about the formation epoch of supermassive black holes in jetted quasars.                                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ghisellini                    Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE MOST POWERFUL GAMMA RAY LOUD BLAZARS                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_0123p25                                       @5´~êˇó$@9ıîØOÑ@`êE° ¿É¿B/º@sÀ$tSéÛ@Ïa5[‰õø@ÏaD#Ü60367001002@”k>˚~ë␀@”]mœ™Õü@‘Á;J#9¡@‘È;J#9¡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ	rqÓ␀␀‰áHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≈We propose to observe 4 blazars at z>2, detected in the gamma-rays by Fermi/LAT and in the soft X-rays, but not yet observed at energies larger than 10 keV. They are among the most powerful blazars and they host black holes whose masses exceed 1e9 Msun. The total requested time of 80 ks is sufficient to establish flux and slope of their X-ray continuum in order to estimate their total electromagnetic output and peak frequency, combined with the Fermi gamma-ray data. This will shed light on the jet-accretion relation, on the controversy about the blazar sequence at its high power extreme, and about the formation epoch of supermassive black holes in jetted quasars.                                                                 