018, a `changing-look' AGN changing optical type to a Type 1.9 after 30 stable years of being a Type 1 (McElroy et al. 2016, Husemann et al. 2016). After dropping by an order of magnitude over the last two years, the optical continuum is now again on the rise, hinting at a return to increased accretion activity. We are monitoring Mrk 1018's ongoing extreme variability from the radio through X-ray, but new NuSTAR observation is critically needed to probe the innermost regions surrounding the accretion disk in order to further constrain scenarios that might account for these dramatic, rapid, and cross-spectrum changes.                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Powell                        Meredith                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT IS CAUSING THE NEW, EXTREME VARIABILITY IN MRK 1018?                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_1018                                          @?éVìuø‘oiDg8@cÒôôôôö¿Lˆá+J@stä⁄πıZ@Ïaqî»)¯@Ïaëî»)¯60301022002@⁄jàõ•„T@⁄U—a‰˜f@‹ZÜêˇó$@‹^Üêˇó$SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ⁄[«ﬂ␀␀‰ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀êWe propose a 40 ksec observation of Mrk 1018, a `changing-look' AGN changing optical type to a Type 1.9 after 30 stable years of being a Type 1 (McElroy et al. 2016, Husemann et al. 2016). After dropping by an order of magnitude over the last two years, the optical continuum is now again on the rise, hinting at a return to increased accretion activity. We are monitoring Mrk 1018's ongoing extreme variability from the radio through X-ray, but new NuSTAR observation is critically needed to probe the innermost regions surrounding the accretion disk in order to further constrain scenarios that might account for these dramatic, rapid, and cross-spectrum changes.                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Powell                        Meredith                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT IS CAUSING THE NEW, EXTREME VARIABILITY IN MRK 1018?                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429UGC_01214                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïaëî»)¯@ÏaëÍM60360004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ©áeC␀␀„#Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                   