d based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀IP survey                                                                                                               archived  20200429NGC_5104                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏbLÕ´∏1@ÏbM±9Ò60370003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸm≥cø␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀vStars are widely understood to form from molecular gas, so knowledge of the molecular ISM is a key ingredient in understanding galaxy formation. Emission from the HCN molecule has been invoked both as a tracer of dense gas and of embedded AGN. We a pursuing a multi-wavelength campaign to understand the ISM and nuclear activity in local IR lumionus galaxies. We have identified starburst galaxies with HCN enhancements (relative to emission from HCO+) similar to those seen in AGN, suggesting they may host Compton-thick AGN. We reqeust NuSTAR observations of four targets to detect or put stringent upper limits (~1% of Lbol) on buried AGN. These data will be used to improve our understanding of the excitation of dense molecular gas tracers and test this proposed method of finding buried AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       DO COMPTON-THICK AGN LURK IN HCN-ENHANCED LIRGS?                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_5104                                          @iu%F?÷’œ™ÕûÑ@sÌ>BZÓc@O5∏∫«À@fUõ=»@ÏbM±9Ò@Ïb\±9Ò60370003002@‘6’©˚Ám@‘(ŒπıY¥@’£ŒlÙ!@’§élÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ!/hL␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀vStars are widely understood to form from molecular gas, so knowledge of the molecular ISM is a key ingredient in understanding galaxy formation. Emission from the HCN molecule has been invoked both as a tracer of dense gas and of embedded AGN. We a pursuing a multi-wavelength campaign to understand the ISM and nuclear activity in local IR lumionus galaxies. We have identified starburst galaxies with HCN enhancements (relative to emission from HCO+) similar to those seen in AGN, suggesting they may host Compton-thick AGN. We reqeust NuSTAR observations of four targets to detect or put stringent upper limits (~1% of Lbol) on buried AGN. These data will be used to improve our understanding of the excitation of dense molecular gas tracers and test this proposed method of finding buried AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       DO COMPTON-THICK AGN LURK IN HCN-ENHANCED LIRGS?                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429BOL_82                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïb\±9Ò@Ïb]Õ´∏130365001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸÙßA␀␀‰áHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ﬂX-ray binaries (XBs) in nearby galaxies have been studied for decades. However, their nature largely remains illusive, mainly due to the lack of hard X-ray coverage. With the extraordinary sensitivity of NuSTAR in hard X-rays, we propose a small NuSTAR survey of four luminous hard X-ray sources (Lx>2e38 erg/s) in M31. The observations will allow us to differentiate between black-hole XBs in the hard state and luminous neutron star (NS) low-mass XBs (LMXBs) by measuring the spectral shape in hard X-rays. The program is important for understanding the origin and evolution of LMXBs. It also allows us to identify supercritically accreting NSs and provides a valuable test for our recently discovered simple, yet effective, source identification method in the Chandra band.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lin                           Dacheng                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR SURVEY OF BRIGHT X-RAY SOURCES IN M31                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BOL_82                                            @%0æÌ)@D~Vìu@^6L/É{J¿5Ò@N§©@sSVlÙ!@Ïb]Õ´∏1@Ïb{xVb‹30365001002@‰∏Ç@∑Ä@‰©Üñªò»@Ê#pu%F@Ê&Pu%FSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸéô˙±␀␀‰áHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ﬂX-ray binaries (XBs) in nearby galaxies have been studied for decades. However, their nature largely remains illusive, mainly due to the lack of hard X-ray coverage. With the extraordinary sensitivity of NuSTAR in hard X-rays, we propose a small NuSTAR survey of four luminous hard X-ray sources (Lx>2e38 erg/s) in M31. The observations will allow us to differentiate between black-hole XBs in the hard state and luminous neutron star (NS) low-mass XBs (LMXBs) by measuring the spectral shape in hard X-rays. The program is important for understanding the origin and evolution of LMXBs. It also allows us to identify supercritically accreting NSs and provides a valuable test for our recently discovered simple, yet effective, source identification method in the Chandra band.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lin                           Dacheng                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR SURVEY OF BRIGHT X-RAY SOURCES IN M31                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429BZQ_J0122p0310                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïb{xVb‹@Ïb{ÍM60373002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸJˇûÈ␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀œMeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration & emission mechanisms, the bulk Lorentz factor. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift that have ever been detected at gamma-rays and that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift, and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZQ_J0122p0310                                    @4ÄæÌ(ç@	<£n.≤@a,ÎC,•¿M|ÁØ∑È@séb∂Æ}@Ïb{ÍM@Ïbì?r‘£60373002002@ﬁò,<ûÌ@›ˇ5Oﬂ;d@‡!§ÕûÉ‰@‡$$ÕûÉ‰SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸÔò◊k␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀œMeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration & emission mechanisms, the bulk Lorentz factor. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift that have ever been detected at gamma-rays and that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift, and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429Mrk1148                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïbì?r‘£@Ïbìî»)¯60160028001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸΩÍ␀¬␀␀„#Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk1148                                           @)ÿD–©@1i„º”@^∞ï*ô1¿F«|Ìëhs@saÉnóç@Ïbìî»)¯@Ïb§#Ü60160028002@’ë≈qué@’Ü≠4ÍK@◊=À1è≈@◊B1è≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸmjíe␀␀„#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO350mIG038                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïb§#Ü@Ïb§±9Ò60374008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ6˛å␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO350mIG038                                      @"gÊö‘,¿@…&Èx‘˛@têœ™ÕûÑ¿T©◊€Ùá˝@tPm\˙¨⁄@Ïb§±9Ò@Ïb¥î»)¯60374008002@÷{“lÙ!@÷k≈˘rG@◊˚\ìtº@◊˝úìtºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ␀‰˙O•␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PSR_J2022p3842                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïb¥î»)¯@ÏbµxVb‹30364002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ␀Ææc^␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀⁄Fermi has detected over 200 gamma-ray pulsars, but only a handful fall in the soft gamma-ray (emission peaking below 1 GeV) category. These tend to be single-pulsed, young, and extremely energetic, the archetypal example being PSR B1509-58. With the greatly improved sensitivity of Pass 8, Fermi is expected to detect increasing numbers of soft gamma-ray pulsars. We propose NuSTAR observations of 5 soft gamma-ray pulsar candidates. The unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR in the hard X-ray energy range, combined with existing soft X-ray and gamma-ray data (e.g. Chandra, XMM, and Fermi) will enable us to test and constrain models of high-energy pulsar emission over a range of parameters (e.g. viewing geometries, spind-down parameters).                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Parkinson                     Pablo                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF SOFT GAMMA-RAY PULSARS                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J2022p3842                                    @s∏QÎÖ@CUÈ·∞â†@S/Õ5®Xy?Ì(åÁ@oôÙá¸π$@ÏbµxVb‹@Ïb…Õ´∏130364002002@€X($x@€@á¸π$@›IŒ_@›LŒ_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ␀Pó¥&␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀⁄Fermi has detected over 200 gamma-ray pulsars, but only a handful fall in the soft gamma-ray (emission peaking below 1 GeV) category. These tend to be single-pulsed, young, and extremely energetic, the archetypal example being PSR B1509-58. With the greatly improved sensitivity of Pass 8, Fermi is expected to detect increasing numbers of soft gamma-ray pulsars. We propose NuSTAR observations of 5 soft gamma-ray pulsar candidates. The unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR in the hard X-ray energy range, combined with existing soft X-ray and gamma-ray data (e.g. Chandra, XMM, and Fermi) will enable us to test and constrain models of high-energy pulsar emission over a range of parameters (e.g. viewing geometries, spind-down parameters).                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Parkinson                     Pablo                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF SOFT GAMMA-RAY PULSARS                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_5695                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïb…Õ´∏1@Ïb xVb‹60368004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿˇ˙òvT␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‡We propose to use a complete, volume-limited sample of AGN to obtain an accurate measurement of the column density distribution of AGN in the local universe. At the same time, we will measure the volume emissivity of hard X-ray emission in the local universe, which can be an important component in ionization and feedback. The survey is modest, and reasonable exposure times have been estimated with the aid of the Swift/BAT 105-month catalog. NuSTAR has only observed a few sources from this sample, but we need only 420 ks to observe the remaining sources in a quasi-uniform manner.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF OBSCURED AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_5695                                          @kkˆ+jÁ’@BJkP∞Ú|@OY¿ÎÌ˙D@Ps;dZ¨@f5&Èx‘˛@Ïb xVb‹@ÏbËèFj60368004002@‰Qe‡ﬁ”@‰Cä˝!ˇ.@ÂπHº£@Â∫»º£SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿˇj1M¿␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡We propose to use a complete, volume-limited sample of AGN to obtain an accurate measurement of the column density distribution of AGN in the local universe. At the same time, we will measure the volume emissivity of hard X-ray emission in the local universe, which can be an important component in ionization and feedback. The survey is modest, and reasonable exposure times have been estimated with the aid of the Swift/BAT 105-month catalog. NuSTAR has only observed a few sources from this sample, but we need only 420 ks to observe the remaining sources in a quasi-uniform manner.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF OBSCURED AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRASF15250p3608                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏbËèFj@ÏbË[‰õø60374009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿˇ"É9E␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRASF15250p3608                                   @l¯æÌ(ç@A˝È·∞â†@L˘ué!ñS@LRT`™e@g1&Èy@ÏbË[‰õø@ÏbˆÕ´∏160374009002@”¡Ç¨1@”µ›Lò_@’1ézêó@’4zêóSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˛◊"É9␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRASF12112p0305                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏbˆÕ´∏1@Ïb˜[‰õø60374005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˛´$ñ␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRASF12112p0305                                   @fÓı¬è\)@£9¿ÎÌ˙@qáK]ÃcÒ@P¯7¥¢4@f≥g†˘	@Ïb˜[‰õø@Ïc#Ü60374005002@“ˇÅ‹∆?@“Ôﬂí:)«@‘J»£n/@‘J£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˛Yôôö␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429FBQS_J1644p2619                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïc#Ü@ÏcÕ´∏160301017001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˛◊Ãl␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀zGamma-ray Narrow Line Seyfert 1 s (NLS1) are a new class of AGN detected at GeV energies by Fermi which extend the domain of relativistic jets sources down to lower black hole masses. We have 8m Gemini near-IR spectra of some objects and estimated black hole masses from their Balmer and Paschen line profiles. We also now have VLT time (2017). We propose NuSTAR (with standard short SWIFT) observations of 3 gamma-ray NLS1s to build broad band SEDs which we will fit with our newly developed models for the jet and disc components. We will use the results to determine the relationship between the gamma-ray NLS1s, and the similarly high Eddington ratio flat spectrum gamma-ray radio quasars to determine how the jet properties relate to black hole mass.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ward                          Martin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       HARD X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF GAMMA-RAY DETECTED SEYFERTS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316FBQS_J1644p2619                                   @ofÀí:*@:S¸PH@GQŒ_@CEÌ(åÁ@h2QŒ_@ÏcÕ´∏1@Ïc-#Ü60301017002@ÈüÆqv@Èãæ•zxl@Î{=¯†ê.@Î|¯†ê.SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˝ë¢≥≈␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀zGamma-ray Narrow Line Seyfert 1 s (NLS1) are a new class of AGN detected at GeV energies by Fermi which extend the domain of relativistic jets sources down to lower black hole masses. We have 8m Gemini near-IR spectra of some objects and estimated black hole masses from their Balmer and Paschen line profiles. We also now have VLT time (2017). We propose NuSTAR (with standard short SWIFT) observations of 3 gamma-ray NLS1s to build broad band SEDs which we will fit with our newly developed models for the jet and disc components. We will use the results to determine the relationship between the gamma-ray NLS1s, and the similarly high Eddington ratio flat spectrum gamma-ray radio quasars to determine how the jet properties relate to black hole mass.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ward                          Martin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       HARD X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF GAMMA-RAY DETECTED SEYFERTS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ16531506p2349431                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïc-#Ü@Ïc-[‰õø60160654001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˝S Ü␀␀„#Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ16531506p2349431                            @o™˛≈m\˚@7”a@N•@EÌ(åÁ∞@Aˇ†˘	k∫@h.Ú{≤˛≈@Ïc-[‰õø@Ïc@èFj60160654002@⁄¸‡D–©@⁄Ëé’œ™Œ@‹”{ã¨q@‹‘;ã¨qSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ¸ÍaŸQ␀␀„#Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NUSTARJ150645p0346d2                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïc@èFj@Ïc@xVb‹60301023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ¸èÌÀ™␀␀‰êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀´Through our survey of all serendipitous sources detected with NuSTAR, we have found one source (J1506) which stands out in having the most extreme hard X-ray spectrum. The existing data suggest that J1506 is a Compton-thick (CT) AGN. There is currently a sparsity of such discoveries in the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys. In determining the nature of J1506 we are limited by: (1) the shallow depth of the off-axis (10.8ks) NuSTAR coverage, and (2) the lack of sensitive soft X-ray (<8keV) coverage. Here, we propose a deeper and broader-energy investigation using on-axis NuSTAR and XMM observations of 60ks and 30ks duration. These will dramatically improve the broad-band (~0.5-50keV) spectral constraints, detect iron line emission, and place stringent constraints on a merging companion galaxy.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lansbury                      George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE HARDEST X-RAY SOURCE FOUND IN THE NUSTAR SERENDIPITOUS SURVEY: A DEEPER, BROADER LOOK                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NUSTARJ150645p0346d2                              @lV˘	kπå@C…ÓÀ˚@{µsÍ≥@I,Ìëhr∞@e˘$tSéÛ@Ïc@xVb‹@ÏcnÍM60301023002@Ó¿mÃcÒA@Ó£íz·GÆ@~≠†ê-‡@~≠†ê-‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ¸õ‡$␀␀‰êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀´Through our survey of all serendipitous sources detected with NuSTAR, we have found one source (J1506) which stands out in having the most extreme hard X-ray spectrum. The existing data suggest that J1506 is a Compton-thick (CT) AGN. There is currently a sparsity of such discoveries in the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys. In determining the nature of J1506 we are limited by: (1) the shallow depth of the off-axis (10.8ks) NuSTAR coverage, and (2) the lack of sensitive soft X-ray (<8keV) coverage. Here, we propose a deeper and broader-energy investigation using on-axis NuSTAR and XMM observations of 60ks and 30ks duration. These will dramatically improve the broad-band (~0.5-50keV) spectral constraints, detect iron line emission, and place stringent constraints on a merging companion galaxy.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lansbury                      George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE HARDEST X-RAY SOURCE FOUND IN THE NUSTAR SERENDIPITOUS SURVEY: A DEEPER, BROADER LOOK                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C266                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏcnÍM@Ïcoî»)¯60372001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˚mjíe␀␀‰ôHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀•A key unknown is how much of the accretion power in the universe is visible versus obscured. Our Chandra survey of luminous, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that radio galaxies (RGs) have X-ray flux 10-1000 times lower than quasars of the same radio luminosity. The Unification hypothesis explains this as obscuration of the RGs with half of them being Compton thick (CT). However, the potential CT sources show relatively soft Chandra spectra, suggesting low obscuration and therefore low intrinsic luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, and 25% are CT. The alternative is the existence of a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. We propose NuSTAR observations of six CT candidate high-redshift 3CRR radio galaxies to distinguish between these possibilities.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS?                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C266                                             @fæﬂ§@@HÊcä	@bw”√a@@OÚ¨ŸË>B@aJu%F
¶@Ïcoî»)¯@Ïcç#Ü60372001002@‰øWË>B[@‰≥(|Ñµ›@Ê+=©˚Ám@Ê,}©˚ÁmSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˙–Ø˘Ô␀␀‰ôHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀•A key unknown is how much of the accretion power in the universe is visible versus obscured. Our Chandra survey of luminous, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that radio galaxies (RGs) have X-ray flux 10-1000 times lower than quasars of the same radio luminosity. The Unification hypothesis explains this as obscuration of the RGs with half of them being Compton thick (CT). However, the potential CT sources show relatively soft Chandra spectra, suggesting low obscuration and therefore low intrinsic luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, and 25% are CT. The alternative is the existence of a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. We propose NuSTAR observations of six CT candidate high-redshift 3CRR radio galaxies to distinguish between these possibilities.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS?                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Rho_Oph                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïcç#Ü@ÏcéèFj90301002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˙üIÙü␀␀„,Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Rho_Oph                                           @n⁄+jÁ’g¿8§g8}ø@vÙá¸π$@0“-V@d!Dg8~@ÏcéèFj@Ïc≤èFj90301002002@ÈG_o“Û@È.Ma‰˜f@ÎbÄùIR@Î£MjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˙/·®≈␀␀„,Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20200429NGC_5765B                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïc≤èFj@Ïc≤xVb‹60301025001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˘˙˘çw␀␀‰ôHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀±We propose to observe NGC5765b with NuSTAR for 50 ks. NGC5765b is a recently discovered water disk megamaser, and candidate Compton-thick AGN in a close merger. In the X-ray band, only a short CHANDRA observation is available. The NuSTAR high-quality spectrum will constrain the X-ray spectral parameters of the source (e.g. photon index Gamma, column density NH, intrinsic luminosity Lx). Combining the latter with the precise BH mass derived from the maser kinematics, we will also obtain an accurate estimate of the Eddington ratio, LEdd.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Masini                        Alberto                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       DISK MEGAMASERS IN LATE MERGERS. A CASE STUDY: NGC5765B                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_5765B                                         @k◊´üUõ=@ÉnóçP?€Ü¬&ÄùI@KÏV’œ´@e»ÿD–@Ïc≤xVb‹@Ïc÷xVb‹60301025002@ËùÃ‡uˆ˝@Ëâg␀h€å@ÍfG>´6z@ÍgG>´6zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˘èΩ@␀␀‰ôHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀±We propose to observe NGC5765b with NuSTAR for 50 ks. NGC5765b is a recently discovered water disk megamaser, and candidate Compton-thick AGN in a close merger. In the X-ray band, only a short CHANDRA observation is available. The NuSTAR high-quality spectrum will constrain the X-ray spectral parameters of the source (e.g. photon index Gamma, column density NH, intrinsic luminosity Lx). Combining the latter with the precise BH mass derived from the maser kinematics, we will also obtain an accurate estimate of the Eddington ratio, LEdd.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Masini                        Alberto                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       DISK MEGAMASERS IN LATE MERGERS. A CASE STUDY: NGC5765B                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SDSS_1510                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïc÷xVb‹@Ïc÷±9Ò60369001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ˘[∞[␀␀‰ôHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÂWe identified a sample of low-z galaxies with extended AGN photoionised clouds indicative of a powerful AGN, the Voorwerpjes (VP). Far-IR and X-ray data suggest that the current luminosity of some of these AGN is lower than what expected from the VP. This may signify that the AGN dropped in luminosity in the last 10-100 kyr, but the clouds remain lit up because of the travel time of the ionising photons. NuSTAR observations are the best probe of the current AGN state. We propose to observe 4 VP related galaxies with no hard X-ray data available, completing the investigation of the shut down AGN candidates sample. We will probe if the AGN are shutting down, and the magnitude of the drop in luminosity. With this sample we will study AGN variability on previously unreachable timescales.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sartori                       Lia                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE LIFECYCLE OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES ON 10-100 KYR TIMESCALES                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                 