o those seen in AGN, suggesting they may host Compton-thick AGN. We reqeust NuSTAR observations of four targets to detect or put stringent upper limits (~1% of Lbol) on buried AGN. These data will be used to improve our understanding of the excitation of dense molecular gas tracers and test this proposed method of finding buried AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       DO COMPTON-THICK AGN LURK IN HCN-ENHANCED LIRGS?                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_F06076m2139                                  @W¥9Xb¿5µÆÊ1¯°@lâ˝ä⁄πı¿2µ° ¿É@r;_ˆîF@Ïdh?r‘£@Ïdx?r‘£60370004002@÷ìg'ª0@÷Ç^!ñRΩ@ÿ+3333@ÿ+3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÛ¨wÿè␀␀‰ôHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀vStars are widely understood to form from molecular gas, so knowledge of the molecular ISM is a key ingredient in understanding galaxy formation. Emission from the HCN molecule has been invoked both as a tracer of dense gas and of embedded AGN. We a pursuing a multi-wavelength campaign to understand the ISM and nuclear activity in local IR lumionus galaxies. We have identified starburst galaxies with HCN enhancements (relative to emission from HCO+) similar to those seen in AGN, suggesting they may host Compton-thick AGN. We reqeust NuSTAR observations of four targets to detect or put stringent upper limits (~1% of Lbol) on buried AGN. These data will be used to improve our understanding of the excitation of dense molecular gas tracers and test this proposed method of finding buried AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       DO COMPTON-THICK AGN LURK IN HCN-ENHANCED LIRGS?                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IC1623B                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïdx?r‘£@ÏdxÍM60374003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÛ~¨/␀␀‰úHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC1623B                                           @0Ì/üæw¿1áÈ˘rG@bŸË>B[¿Sˆ⁄πıY¥@t/D–©*@ÏdxÍM@Ïdäî»)¯60374003002@‘ÕßØ∑È@‘ºeﬁi≠C@÷jHN§®¡@÷n»N§®¡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÛ(≈6˛␀␀‰úHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_339m4                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïdäî»)¯@Ïdãî»)¯80302304006␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÚ¸“ﬁ␀␀‰ôHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀X-ray reflection spectroscopy is one of the most effective means of probing the strong-field region near an event horizon. Modeling a reflection spectrum, one can constrain the spin of a black hole, the degree of truncation of the inner disk, the inclination of the system, and other key parameters. We request 80 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger four observations of the black hole binary GX 339-4 during the rise of its next outburst. Our principal goal is to track the radius of the inner edge of the accretion disk in the hard state as a function of luminosity, as we have done before using RXTE data. Using NuSTAR advanced capabilities, we aim to resolve the gross discrepancies in published measurements of the inner disk radius.                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE EVOLUTION OF THE ACCRETION DISK IN GX 339-4                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_339m4                                          @o˜ò«‚ÇA¿Ha [¿7@u-Ω•ú‡¿Öáì›óˆ@aõÍ≥g†˘@Ïdãî»)¯@Ïd†±9Ò80302304007@‹I≤ïÈ‚@‹6ésÅ◊‹@ﬁ£v