ut are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2010da                                          @+wŒŸá+¿B€∂E° ¡@r¡–Â`Aâ¿S”.HËß@u"j~˘€#@Ïd°î»)¯@Ïd xVb‹90401005002@ÈÕà°a‰˜@ÈΩtwökQ@Î°è˜ŒŸ@Î£Ô˜ŒŸSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÚ†ŒQ␀␀„,Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  202004296dFJ2234555m843453                                @u&àŒp:˚¿U$öu%F@s(^5?|Ó¿?r∂Æ}Vm@C—†'RTa@Ïd xVb‹@ÏdÀèFj60464199001@uêº@uq!-w1ê@v‡␀␀␀␀␀@v‡␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÒ“4Vy␀␀„4Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004296dFJ2234555m843453                                @u&§®¡T ¿U$D–©*@s(jÁ’fœ¿?s‰%ÆÊ2@CÏ"h	‘ï@ÏdÀèFj@Ïd◊î»)¯60464199002@⁄F«Í≥g°@⁄6øW>´6@‹£˜ŒŸ@‹#˜ŒŸSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÒêg(ï␀␀„4Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ CVZ                                                                                                                    archived  20200429IC860                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïd◊î»)¯@Ïdÿî»)¯60301024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÒd…ƒ␀␀‰üHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ØIC860 is a nearby IR-luminous early-type spiral with a unique set of properties: it is in the early stages of transition without clear signs of the triggering event for the transition, showing optical signatures of shocks and hosting an AGN-driven neutral wind and a compact core of molecular gas. New Hubble observations will trace the mass distribution in the neutral outflow. We propose to observe IC860 using NuSTAR for 70ks to measure the X-ray properties of its moderately to heavily obscured AGN and thereby gauge the relative mechanical and radiative powers applied by the AGN during this process, yielding insights into the role of AGN in driving outflows in transitioning galaxies.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanz                          Lauranne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MEASURING AGN LUMINOSITY IN THE EARLY STAGES OF TRANSITIONING IN IC860                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC860                                             @hÿ¯7¥¢4@8§®¡T…Ü@"pâ†'RT@U“Ò©˚Á@cˇ*ô0æ@Ïdÿî»)¯@Ïe±9Ò60301024002@Ò≤6‚Î@Ò£"#¢úx@ÚÏˇoiD@ÚÌœoiDSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿBøË␀␀‰üHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ØIC860 is a nearby IR-luminous early-type spiral with a unique set of properties: it is in the early stages of transition without clear signs of the triggering event for the transition, showing optical signatures of shocks and hosting an AGN-driven neutral wind and a compact core of molecular gas. New Hubble observations will trace the mass distribution in the neutral outflow. We propose to observe IC860 using NuSTAR for 70ks to measure the X-ray properties of its moderately to heavily obscured AGN and thereby gauge the relative mechanical and radiative powers applied by the AGN during this process, yielding insights into the role of AGN in driving outflows in transitioning galaxies.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanz                          Lauranne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MEASURING AGN LUMINOSITY IN THE EARLY STAGES OF TRANSITIONING IN IC860                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C294                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïe±9Ò@ÏeèFj60372002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿΩåµ␀␀‰úHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀•A key unknown is how much of the accretion power in the universe is visible versus obscured. Our Chandra survey of luminous, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that radio galaxies (RGs) have X-ray flux 10-1000 times lower than quasars of the same radio luminosity. The Unification hypothesis explains this as obscuration of the RGs with half of them being Compton thick (CT). However, the potential CT sources show relatively soft Chandra spectra, suggesting low obscuration and therefore low intrinsic luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, and 25% are CT. The alternative is the existence of a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. We propose NuSTAR observations of six CT candidate high-redshift 3CRR radio galaxies to distinguish between these possibilities.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS?                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C294                                             @jvlÙ!-@AúwökQ@NŒcä	@RàŒp:˚@dêˇó$t@ÏeèFj@Ïe)Õ´∏160372002002@‰,‰ÛMj@‰`Aâ7@ÂâãN;Õ6@Âã+N;Õ6SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿk$ñ␀␀‰úHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀•A key unknown is how much of the accretion power in the universe is visible versus obscured. Our Chandra survey of luminous, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that radio galaxies (RGs) have X-ray flux 10-1000 times lower than quasars of the same radio luminosity. The Unification hypothesis explains this as obscuration of the RGs with half of them being Compton thick (CT). However, the potential CT sources show relatively soft Chandra spectra, suggesting low obscuration and therefore low intrinsic luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, and 25% are CT. The alternative is the existence of a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. We propose NuSTAR observations of six CT candidate high-redshift 3CRR radio galaxies to distinguish between these possibilities.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS?                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004291RXSJ075813d2p835637                              @]—÷8ÜYK@T˝XbM”@`0v»¥9X@<è∑È˘r@FÀÙá¸π$@Ïe)Õ´∏1@Ïe*xVb‹60464061001@ÄóHé@Äá7Ä4m∆@Å†␀␀␀␀␀@Å†␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ9 ␀␀„4Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004291RXSJ075813d2p835637                              @]ÿZ¨1@T¸éäqﬁj@`0–©*0@<ìä	ﬁ@F∂˘€"–Â@Ïe*xVb‹@Ïe5ÍM60464061002@—ÚÙÂ`Aâ@—‰≥s¸P@”)␀,<ûÌ@”*Ä,<ûÌSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÔÙßA␀␀„4Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ CVZ                                                                                                                    archived  20200429SDSS_J082247d6p22414                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïe5ÍM@Ïe6î»)¯60301009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÔŒ≤@y␀␀‰úHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of two green pea galaxies (GPs) with an exposure of 20 ksec for each to detect hard X-ray emission as a direct AGN sign. GPs offer valuable opportunity to study the supermassive black hole (SMBH) growth in an early stage of the galaxy formation. Our goal is to investigate whether the AGN resides in such an immature galaxy. Observables ever obtained suggest that our targets can host a buried AGN. Weak optical high excitation lines and the absence of broad lines do not necessarily mean the absence of an AGN. As an alternative idea, these facts could suggest that the AGN is highly obscured if any. Also, the high X-ray luminosity to star formation rate ratios infer the presence. Thus, detecting less extincted hard X-rays is important to finally unveil the AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kawamuro                      Taiki                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       FIRST DETECTION OF BURIED ACTIVE GALACTIC NUCLEI IN EARLY STAGE OF GALAXY FORMATION: GREEN PEA GALAXIES                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSS_J082247d6p22414                              @_k_o“Û@6Øo“ÚÂ@i:)«yß@=i¶µ(@üæv»¥@Ïe6î»)¯@ÏeFxVb‹60301009002@’¥@QÎÖ@’û‚p:˚@◊?)-w1ê@◊@)-w1êSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÔÖgâ¨␀␀‰úHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of two green pea galaxies (GPs) with an exposure of 20 ksec for each to detect hard X-ray emission as a direct AGN sign. GPs offer valuable opportunity to study the supermassive black hole (SMBH) growth in an early stage of the galaxy formation. Our goal is to investigate whether the AGN resides in such an immature galaxy. Observables ever obtained suggest that our targets can host a buried AGN. Weak optical high excitation lines and the absence of broad lines do not necessarily mean the absence of an AGN. As an alternative idea, these facts could suggest that the AGN is highly obscured if any. Also, the high X-ray luminosity to star formation rate ratios infer the presence. Thus, detecting less extincted hard X-rays is important to finally unveil the AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kawamuro                      Taiki                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       FIRST DETECTION OF BURIED ACTIVE GALACTIC NUCLEI IN EARLY STAGE OF GALAXY FORMATION: GREEN PEA GALAXIES                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_1068                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏeFxVb‹@ÏeG?r‘£60302003007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÔTz·H␀␀‰°Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀«We propose four 50 ks NuSTAR observations of the Seyfert galaxy NGC 1068, the archetypal and one of the brightest Compton-thick Seyfert 2s. A flux excess above 20 keV was discovered by NuSTAR in the August 2014 observation, with respect to observations performed 20 months earlier, and 6 months later. This variability is ascribed to Compton-thick material (with column density NH>2.5 10^24 cm^-2 ) moving temporarily out from our line of sight, allowing the nuclear radiation to pierce through the circumnuclear absorbing material. NuSTAR is the only telescope in orbit that permits a search for such column density variations in this energy band. This monitoring program aims to estimate the distance of the variable absorber, to check if it is consistent with the pc-scale torus.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       LOCATING THE ABSORBING STRUCTURE IN NGC 1068 VIA HARD X-RAY MONITORING                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316N