ghout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab_bkgd_PA330                                   @T…◊€Ùá˝@5áì›óˆ+@gLò_˜¿çOﬂ;dZ@t©fffff@Ïeé?r‘£@ÏeúxVb‹10402003002@“»$4m≈÷@“∑dÌëhs@‘%–é@‘&–éSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÏGeC!␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             archived  20200429SGR_0526m66                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏeúxVb‹@Ïeù?r‘£30301014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÏÅ¥Ë␀␀‰°Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÒUnlike most magnetars the soft Gamma-ray repeater (SGR) 0526-66 has been in a quiescent state for decades, and is projected within the boundary of a supernova remnant (SNR N49). This object represents a rare opportunity for the study of the long-term cooling and structural evolution of a magnetar after its conspicuous outbursts. The nature of the progenitor star to create a magnetar may also be studied directly through the host supernova remnant, if this SGR is physically associated with SNR N49. The origin of the X-ray emission in the quiescent state, a key component for such studies, is elusive. NuSTAR is a unique opportunity to reveal the spectral nature of 0526-66's X-ray emission, and thus we request a 50 ks NuSTAR observation of the quiescent state 0526-66.                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Park                          Sangwook                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       SPECTRAL NATURE OF THE QUIESCENT X-RAYS FROM SGR 0526-66                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_0526m66                                       @T_˛\ë—N¿PÜfffff@qB!-w2¿@ü_o“Û@r◊MjO@Ïeù?r‘£@Ïeπ[‰õø30301014002@Êˆ˝¿ÎÌ˙@ÊÊÿ($x@ËÜ$:)«z@ËÜd:)«zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÎ´lÿ␀␀‰°Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÒUnlike most magnetars the soft Gamma-ray repeater (SGR) 0526-66 has been in a quiescent state for decades, and is projected within the boundary of a supernova remnant (SNR N49). This object represents a rare opportunity for the study of the long-term cooling and structural evolution of a magnetar after its conspicuous outbursts. The nature of the progenitor star to create a magnetar may also be studied directly through the host supernova remnant, if this SGR is physically associated with SNR N49. The origin of the X-ray emission in the quiescent state, a key component for such studies, is elusive. NuSTAR is a unique opportunity to reveal the spectral nature of 0526-66's X-ray emission, and thus we request a 50 ks NuSTAR observation of the quiescent state 0526-66.                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Park                          Sangwook                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       SPECTRAL NATURE OF THE QUIESCENT X-RAYS FROM SGR 0526-66                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïeπ[‰õø@Ïe∫±9Ò30302019003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÎ\q«␀␀ÂYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @rπ‹∆?@AóŒŸá+@QÕ˙C˛\í@	ñªò«„@kËßÊõ@Ïe∫±9Ò@Ïe…[‰õø30302019004@»“ı¬è\@…wÀí:*@◊$•qué@◊$•quéSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÍ,ÿÔ±␀␀ÂYHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429IGR_J08390m4833                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïe…[‰õø@Ïe ±9Ò30460025001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÈ¸ñ/…␀␀„:Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J08390m4833                                   @`7{J#9¡¿HEp£◊
