35-44, 4U 1746-37, and 4U 1702-43, for 20 ks each. Our primary goal is to increase the number of neutron stars with clear, robust indications of relativistic reflection from the inner disk. These observations will also address the radial extent of the inner disk in Atoll sources, which likely accrete at a low Eddington fraction similar to the black hole low/hard state. Our final goal is to find examples of disks that extend very close to stellar surfaces in order to obtain limits on neutron star radii.                                                                                                                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NEUTRON STARS AND THEIR INNERMOST ACCRETION FLOWS                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1746m37                                        @pπ\(ı¬è¿BÉ–|Ñµ›@vº¿6E° ¿É@b+®Xy=Ÿ@ÏeÙ[‰õø@Ïf±9Ò30363004002@”jà⁄Q@”b^¨1@’MŸË>B@’OŸË>BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿË÷H,␀␀‰™Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˛We propose to observe the neutron star low-mass X-ray binaries and Atoll sources GX 3+1, 4U 0614+09, 4U 1735-44, 4U 1746-37, and 4U 1702-43, for 20 ks each. Our primary goal is to increase the number of neutron stars with clear, robust indications of relativistic reflection from the inner disk. These observations will also address the radial extent of the inner disk in Atoll sources, which likely accrete at a low Eddington fraction similar to the black hole low/hard state. Our final goal is to find examples of disks that extend very close to stellar surfaces in order to obtain limits on neutron star radii.                                                                                                                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NEUTRON STARS AND THEIR INNERMOST ACCRETION FLOWS                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429CGCG229m015                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïf±9Ò@Ïfî»)¯60160705001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿË©–6ù␀␀„AHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CGCG229m015                                       @qÊcä	@E:&ÄùIR@RA∞â†'R@/,~($x@ifz·GÆ@Ïfî»)¯@ÏfèFj60160705002@’yÏ≠´üV@’n“àŒp@◊(9Xb@◊(9XbSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿË[‡$i␀␀„AHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_J1559p0304                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏfèFj@Ïfî»)¯60373003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿË1«r␀␀‰ÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀œMeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration & emission mechanisms, the bulk Lorentz factor. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift that have ever been detected at gamma-rays and that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift, and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_J1559p0304                                    @m¸ﬁ␀—∑@ø|Ìëhs@*,ãCïÅ@C§"h	‘ï@eh	‘ï@Ïfî»)¯@Ïf=èFj60373003002@ÍuÀ)_@È˙¸␀␀␀␀@ÎÒ∆g8~@ÎÚfg8~SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÁ¡Ñ\ä␀␀‰ÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀œMeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration & emission mechanisms, the bulk Lorentz factor. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift that have ever been detected at gamma-rays and that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift, and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Ophiuchus_Cluster_2                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïf=èFj@Ïf=xVb‹70301003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÁWõ‡$␀␀‰™Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÃThe Ophiuchus cluster is the most massive galaxy cluster within 200 Mpc and it hosts a radio minihalo in its sloshing cool core, which one theoretical model predicts is the result of a minor merger, although the merging subcluster has never been clearly identified in any minihalo cluster. The minihalo is also thought to be the source of INTEGRAL-detected IC emission from the cluster. NuSTAR's hard band sensitivity and spatial resolving power makes it ideal for directly detecting the merging subcluster, for which there are hints at lower X-ray energies, but need confirmation due to complications with foreground absorption. It is also ideal for confirming the IC detection, which is critical given how controversial such detections have been in the past.                                        Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE MERGER-MINIHALO CONNECTION IN THE OPHIUCHUS CLUSTER: A SMOKING GUN?                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Ophiuchus_Cluster_2                               @p"∏QÎÖ¿7}»K]Ã?ÿ£n.±ƒ@"¡|⁄Q@c÷ÕûÉ‰&@Ïf=xVb‹@ÏfùxVb‹70301003002@˝±xK]Ãd@˝ô!-wA␀Ö÷ìA␀Áoù≤-SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÊUû&Ø␀␀‰™Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÃThe Ophiuchus cluster is the most massive galaxy cluster within 200 Mpc and it hosts a radio minihalo in its sloshing cool core, which one theoretical model predicts is the result of a minor merger, although the merging subcluster has never been clearly identified in any minihalo cluster. The minihalo is also thought to be the source of INTEGRAL-detected IC emission from the cluster. NuSTAR's hard band sensitivity and spatial resolving power makes it ideal for directly detecting the merging subcluster, for which there are hints at lower X-ray energies, but need confirmation due to complications with foreground absorption. It is also ideal for confirming the IC detection, which is critical given how controversial such detections have been in the past.                                        Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE MERGER-MINIHALO CONNECTION IN THE OPHIUCHUS CLUSTER: A SMOKING GUN?                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IGR_J14536m5522                                   @kÔF
