nd Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀IP sample                                                                                                               archived  20200429Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“SéÛMj¿EW“Ú‰è@urQÎÖ∏?–xF‹]@c5áì›óˆ@ÏfæÕ´∏1@Ïfø?r‘£90401307001@~|@∑Ä4n@~^Énóç@Åp␀␀␀␀␀@Åp␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÂlÒ5z␀␀„:Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / arrive unocculted                                                                                             archived  20200429Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“åLò_¿EWÀ)^û@urqﬁi≠C?–ç∏∫«@c:zêñº@Ïfø?r‘£@Ïf€Õ´∏190401307002@‡G#†˘	l@‡8«ﬂ;dZ@‚ÍèAÚ◊@‚ÍØAÚ◊SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ‰”à'q␀␀„:Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Serpens_X1                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïf€Õ´∏1@Ïf‹[‰õø30301021001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ‰p*z␀␀‰™Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≥Numerous properties make Serpens X-1 the best candidate for constraints on the inner accretion flow and neutron star radius via disk reflection spectroscopy. We propose a joint NuSTAR (40 ks) and NICER observation of Serpens X-1, in order to obtain the best possible limits on the neutron star radius, the best measurements of the inner accretion flow, and to explore changes in the ionization of the disk with radius. The extraordinary sensitivity of NICER at and below 1 keV will be able to reveal relativistic Ne X and Fe L lines predicted by the prior NuSTAR exposure of Serpens X-1. We will maximize the return of these observations by utilizing new disk reflection models, optimized for neutron star X-ray binaries.                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NICER NUSTAR LOOK AT SERPENS X-1                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Serpens_X1                                        @qÄMjO@-˙C˛\í@BS&¬@
#9¿ÎÓ@eî7¥¢3ú@Ïf‹[‰õø@Ïf˚ÍM30301021002@ﬁˇöu@ﬁáÓÀ˚@ÂHÀí@ÂÄ_HÀíSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ„:∞º␀␀‰™Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≥Numerous properties make Serpens X-1 the best candidate for constraints on the inner accretion flow and neutron star radius via disk reflection spectroscopy. We propose a joint NuSTAR (40 ks) and NICER observation of Serpens X-1, in order to obtain the best possible limits on the neutron star radius, the best measurements of the inner accretion flow, and to explore changes in the ionization of the disk with radius. The extraordinary sensitivity of NICER at and below 1 keV will be able to reveal relativistic Ne X and Fe L lines predicted by the prior NuSTAR exposure of Serpens X-1. We will maximize the return of these observations by utilizing new disk reflection models, optimized for neutron star X-ray binaries.                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NICER NUSTAR LOOK AT SERPENS X-1                                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429AR_Sco                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïf˚ÍM@Ïf¸xVb‹30301027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ„␀ë¢¥␀␀‰ÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀„A recent detection of pulsations with the 1.97 min period, modulated by the binary period of 3.56 hr, in the radio-IR-optical-UV synchrotron emission from the white dwarf (WD) - M-star binary AR Sco was the first discovery of a WD pulsar powered by WD spindown. Although it is clear that the detected emission is produced by relativistic particles, the acceleration mechanism and the sites of emission remain uncertain. To distinguish between different models for the WD-pulsar - M-star binary, X-ray observations are particularly useful, especially at energies above 10 keV. We propose a NuSTAR observation supplemented by a short XMM-Newton observation to investigate the X-ray spectrum and pulsations of AR Sco and infer a realistic model of this unique object.                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pavlov                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       AR SCO: THE FIRST WHITE DWARF PULSAR                                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AR_Sco                                            @nØC,•z¿6ﬁ˘€"–Â@v~($x@2ÍÁ’fœB@dy≠B√…Ô@Ïf¸xVb‹@Ïge[‰õø30301027002A␀p[>Ì)A␀`@»ËßAûπ—∑YAü1—∑YSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ‚8	+␀␀‰ÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀„A recent detection of pulsations with the 1.97 min period, modulated by the binary period of 3.56 hr, in the radio-IR-optical-UV synchrotron emission from the white dwarf (WD) - M-star binary AR Sco was the first discovery of a WD pulsar powered by WD spindown. Although it is clear that the detected emission is produced by relativistic particles, the acceleration mechanism and the sites of emission remain uncertain. To distinguish between different models for the WD-pulsar - M-star binary, X-ray observations are particularly useful, especially at energies above 10 keV. We propose a NuSTAR observation supplemented by a short XMM-Newton observation to investigate the X-ray spectrum and pulsations of AR Sco and infer a realistic model of this unique object.                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pavlov                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       AR SCO: THE FIRST WHITE DWARF PULSAR                                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429SAX_J1818d6m1703                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïge[‰õø@ÏgeÕ´∏130301025001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ·„◊
=␀␀‰ÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‚The detection of cyclotron resonance absorption features in transient accreting HMXBs, also known as Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXT) is a powerful tool to unveil the processes driving their accretion modes. Our previous discoveries of cyclotron lines in the transients SAX J17544-2619 and IGR J16418-4532 with NuSTAR have motivated us to further study the role of magnetic fields in the extreme variability of these objects. For this purpose we ask to study two additional HMXB systems, selected by the available sample of SFXTs, for a a total exposure of 100~ks to be executed in AO3. These sources, SAX J1818.6-1703 and AX J1841.0-0536 show a dynamic range of variability of about 10^3 and may show bright flaring activity during a single exposure of 50ks.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Natalucci                     Lorenzo                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE MAGNETIC FIELDS OF NEUTRON STARS IN SUPERGIANT FAST X-RAY TRANSIENTS                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SAX_J1818d6m1703                                  @q+␀—∑Y¿1oiDg@+˙kP∞Ú|ø‡Ì(åÁ@c∫^5?}@ÏgeÕ´∏1@Ïgê[‰õø30301025002@Í# cä	@ÍåU2a|@Ï1'6zë@Ï26zëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ·a_Û›␀␀‰ÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‚The detection of cyclotron resonance absorption features in transient accreting HMXBs, also known as Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXT) is a powerful tool to unveil the processes driving their accretion modes. Our previous discoveries of cyclotron lines in the transients SAX J17544-2619 and IGR J16418-4532 with NuSTAR have motivated us to further study the role of magnetic fields in the extreme variability of these objects. For this purpose we ask to study two additional HMXB systems, selected by the available sample of SFXTs, for a a total exposure of 100~ks to be executed in AO3. These sources, SAX J1818.6-1703 and AX J1841.0-0536 show a dynamic range of variability of about 10^3 and may show bright flaring activity during a single exposure of 50ks.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Natalucci                     Lorenzo                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE MAGNETIC FIELDS OF NEUTRON STARS IN SUPERGIANT FAST X-RAY TRANSIENTS                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  202004291RXS_J21334d1p510725                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïgê[‰õø@Ïgë#Ü30460001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ·+lÿ␀␀„AHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161RXS_J21334d1p510725                              @t7rGE8Ô@IçÃcÒA @Wïx‘˝Û∂øﬂ∑È˘rG@l©¡Ω•ù@Ïgë#Ü@Ïg°Õ´∏130460001002@Ÿñ§1&È@ŸÄ:E° ¡@€[,„º”[@€[,„º”[SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ‡”–¯À␀␀„AHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  