GLS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Swift_J1658d2m4242                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïg√xVb‹@Ïgƒ?r‘£80301301001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿﬂ⁄ºﬂ␀␀‰»Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9fThe combination of the fast EPIC-pn modes and broad-band coverage with RXTE was an effective means of exploring accretion in bright Galactic black holes. XMM-Newton and NuSTAR provide an even more powerful combination. We propose one joint 40 ks observation of a new or known black hole transient during a bright, hard outburst. The goals of this program are to 1) measure the inner accretion disk radius of the black hole via disk reflection spectrum and infer the black hole spin, 2) test an apparent anti-correlation between winds and jets in soft and hard states by making a very sensitive search for winds in a hard state. This observation will be supported by a network of ground-based observatories and X-ray monitoring and is in continuation of our successful program in AO 13 15.            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The hard state of a transient black hole: is the accretion disk truncated?                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“¨1&È¿EW!-w1ê@urwökP±?œ∑È˘rG@cúá¸π#£@Ïgƒ?r‘£@Ïg·î»)¯80301301002@ﬁÃ/qué"@ﬂn£S&@‰ˆ–1&È@‰ı1&ÈSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿﬁq5yæ␀␀‰»Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9fThe combination of the fast EPIC-pn modes and broad-band coverage with RXTE was an effective means of exploring accretion in bright Galactic black holes. XMM-Newton and NuSTAR provide an even more powerful combination. We propose one joint 40 ks observation of a new or known black hole transient during a bright, hard outburst. The goals of this program are to 1) measure the inner accretion disk radius of the black hole via disk reflection spectrum and infer the black hole spin, 2) test an apparent anti-correlation between winds and jets in soft and hard states by making a very sensitive search for winds in a hard state. This observation will be supported by a network of ground-based observatories and X-ray monitoring and is in continuation of our successful program in AO 13 15.            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The hard state of a transient black hole: is the accretion disk truncated?                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Her_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïg·î»)¯@Ïg‚[‰õø30302012003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿﬁ.Es␀␀‰¡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀·We propose to continue the study of the long-term evolution of the cyclotron line energy Ecyc in Her X-1. The long-term decay of Ecyc between 1993 and 2012, was confirmed by NuSTAR observations, firmly establishing a decay by ~5 keV over the last 20 yrs. However, the last observing campaign in 2016, coordinated between NuSTAR and INTEGRAL has provided evidence that the decay has ended, in fact hinting at a possible increase, similar to the increase observed between 1990 and 1993. We had speculated that a new increase might happen, once Ecyc had reached a low level similar to the time at the discovery. We therefore propose to continue the monitoring of Her X-1 during Cycle-3 with two observations of 30 ks each: the first as soon as possible, the second half a year later.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Staubert                      Ruediger                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MONITORING THE CYCLTRON LINEE ENERGY IN HERCULES X-1: FOLLOW THE LONG-TERM VARIABILITY                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Her_X1                                            @oœdZ¨@AÆö‘,<ü@MÏV’œ´@B“=p£◊
@dïÌ(åÁ@Ïg‚[‰õø@Ïg˘?r‘£30302012004@⁄d–⁄Q@⁄ãò«‚ÇA@ﬂΩÄæﬂ§@ﬂ¡¿æﬂ§SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ›dPg)␀␀‰¡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀·We propose to continue the study of the long-term evolution of the cyclotron line energy Ecyc in Her X-1. The long-term decay of Ecyc between 1993 and 2012, was confirmed by NuSTAR observations, firmly establishing a decay by ~5 keV over the last 20 yrs. However, the last observing campaign in 2016, coordinated between NuSTAR and INTEGRAL has provided evidence that the decay has ended, in fact hinting at a possible increase, similar to the increase observed between 1990 and 1993. We had speculated that a new increase might happen, once Ecyc had reached a low level similar to the time at the discovery. We therefore propose to continue the monitoring of Her X-1 during Cycle-3 with two observations of 30 ks each: the first as soon as possible, the second half a year later.                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Staubert                      Ruediger                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MONITORING THE CYCLTRON LINEE ENERGY IN HERCULES X-1: FOLLOW THE LONG-TERM VARIABILITY                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              INTEGRAL                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429CXO_J164710d2m455216                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïg˘?r‘£@Ïg˙èFj80201050007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ›04V␀␀‰¨Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXO_J164710d2m455216                              @oz2 Wßá¿FÌY≥–|Ö@u7"–Â`BøŒîFsÅ◊‹@c‡?º@Ïg˙èFj@Ïh±9Ò80201050008@„d±ò«‚Ç@„Wı [¿@Â#Òúæ‡@Â#ëúæ‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ‹¿E…␀␀‰¨Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  J¿The discovery of transient magnetars has opened a new perspective in the field confirming that a relatively large number of members of this class has not been discovered yet, and suggesting that others would manifest themselves in the future through outbursts. This proposal is aimed at gathering new insights on the physics of magnetars through the study of their outbursts. In particular, we are asking for 85ks of Chandra time divided in 4 observations throughout the outburst from a known or still unknown magnetar candidate, as well as 85ks of NuSTAR time to monitor the hard X-ray emission during the outburst decay.                                                                                                                                                                                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           N                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       PROMPT STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS WITH CHANDRA AND NUSTAR                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(4/4) / Kp=5                                                                                                            archived  20200429Swift_J1658d2m4242                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïh±9Ò@ÏhÍM80302302001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ‹<e§8␀␀‰ÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9ÕBlack hole transients (BHT) are the only class of black holes (BH) which samples a wide range of accretion rates and accretion states during their month to year long outbursts. BHT classically show two types of outflows: collimated radio-jets ubiquitous during the canonical hard state and highly ionised X-ray winds during the soft state. We propose a combined XMM+NuSTAR monitoring campaign of an high inclination BHT, to shed light on the evolution of the wind while the source transits through the intermediate states.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Tracing the evolution of BH winds through the intermediate states                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“©ì‡ﬂ¿EWº@urxF‹]?œ≈Åo␀i@c∫(ı¬è\@ÏhÍM@Ïh/[‰õø80302302002@◊v6)«yß@◊©ˇÉ{J#@‡*∑5®Xy@‡)˜5®XySCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ€,¿©Ë␀␀‰ÌHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9ÕBlack hole transients (BHT) are the only class of black holes (BH) which samples a wide range of accretion rates and accretion states during their month to year long outbursts. BHT classically show two types of outflows: collimated radio-jets ubiquitous during the canonical hard state and highly ionised X-ray winds during the soft state. We propose a combined XMM+NuSTAR monitoring campaign of an high inclination BHT, to shed light on the evolution of the wind while the source transits through the intermediate states.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Tracing the evolution of BH winds through the intermediate states                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(1/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429MAXI_J1813m095                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïh/[‰õø@Ïh/Õ´∏180402303001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ⁄Óz·␀␀‰∞Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    (àThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J F                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1813m095                                    @qì›óˆ+¿#À)^û@4%ÆÊ1˘@úC,•zx@cÿ©ì‡ﬂ@Ïh/Õ´∏1@Ïh>xVb‹80402303002@‘0~($@‘øò«‚Ç@÷S‰–©*@÷S‰–©*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ⁄–ó¥␀␀‰∞Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  (àThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J F                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429W44                                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïh>xVb‹@Ïh>Õ´∏140301003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿŸ∞*z␀␀‰ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀@We propose a 50-ks NuSTAR observation of a hard X-ray emission in the supernova remnant W44 recently discovered by Suzaku. The Suzaku XIS spectrum of the emission is described by a power law with a photon index of 1.5. The hard spectrum is consistent with nonthermal bremsstrahlung either by electrons or protons. If this scenario is the case, we can probe low-energy particles accelerated in W44, which cannot be done in any other wavelengths. In the hard X-ray band, the power-law component becomes dominant over the thermal emission. Therefore, a NuSTAR observation is essential to determine the spectral slope and flux of the hard component and to disentangle possible emission mechanisms.                                                                                                           Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Tanaka                        Takaaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR STUDY OF A HARD X-RAY EMISSION DISCOVERED IN THE SUPERNOVA REMNANT W44                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316W44                                               @qøN;Õ5®?ı WßÜ¬'@ABd¬¯7µøƒ”ZÖáìﬁ@dzÀ)^û@Ïh>Õ´∏1@ÏhfxVb‹40301003002@Í(<ìtº@Íc]ÃcÒ@Î˚B}øH@Î¸B}øHSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿŸD+˛|␀␀‰ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀@We propose a 50-ks NuSTAR observation of a hard X-ray emission in the supernova remnant W44 recently discovered by Suzaku. The Suzaku XIS spectrum of the emission is described by a power law with a photon index of 1.5. The hard spectrum is consistent with nonthermal bremsstrahlung either by electrons or protons. If this scenario is the case, we can probe low-energy particles accelerated in W44, which cannot be done in any other wavelengths. In the hard X-ray band, the power-law component becomes dominant over the thermal emission. Therefore, a NuSTAR observation is essential to determine the spectral slope and flux of the hard component and to disentangle possible emission mechanisms.                                                                                                           Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Tanaka                        Takaaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR STUDY OF A HARD X-RAY EMISSION DISCOVERED IN THE SUPERNOVA REMNANT W44                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk_461                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏhfxVb‹@Ïhg?r‘£60465007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿŸ†m:␀␀„NHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_461                                           @i⁄õ•„S¯@A ƒõ•„@P‹‡uˆ˝"@S␀␀␀␀␀@`bπıY≥–@Ïhg?r‘£@Ïh|î»)¯60465007002@‹‘ÍL/É{@‹ø}-w1ê@ﬁÊç/üæ@ﬁËM/üæSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿÿ≥Kx¸␀␀„NHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ELS NuLANDS / idiagnostics_on 2018:061:16:43:13Z                                                                        archived  20200429IGR_J19294p1816                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïh|î»)¯@Ïh}?r‘£90401306001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ¢wFÎÊ␀␀„UHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J19294p1816                                   @rH:í£S@2Q&Èx‘˛@J∑ª/ÏV÷?Œ≈m\˙¨⁄@e˚o␀h€å@Ïh}?r‘£@ÏhöÕ´∏190401306002@„’Ëé@„∆ñªò»@Âks=»K@Âk3=»KSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ†Õ4␀␀„UHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20200429IRASF19297m0406                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏhöÕ´∏1@Ïhõ?r‘£60374007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ†ù≠RM␀␀‰ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                             