14_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9ÕBlack hole transients (BHT) are the only class of black holes (BH) which samples a wide range of accretion rates and accretion states during their month to year long outbursts. BHT classically show two types of outflows: collimated radio-jets ubiquitous during the canonical hard state and highly ionised X-ray winds during the soft state. We propose a combined XMM+NuSTAR monitoring campaign of an high inclination BHT, to shed light on the evolution of the wind while the source transits through the intermediate states.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Tracing the evolution of BH winds through the intermediate states                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“üæv»¥¿EW'ª/ÏW@ursÍ≥g°?œŒŸá+@cÓOv_Ÿ@Ïh™èFj@ÏhæÍM80302302004@ÿ|˚OÑM@ÿ±9«y¶µ@‡´—ú‡u˜@‡´—ú‡u˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿû£-!¡␀␀‰ÌHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9ÕBlack hole transients (BHT) are the only class of black holes (BH) which samples a wide range of accretion rates and accretion states during their month to year long outbursts. BHT classically show two types of outflows: collimated radio-jets ubiquitous during the canonical hard state and highly ionised X-ray winds during the soft state. We propose a combined XMM+NuSTAR monitoring campaign of an high inclination BHT, to shed light on the evolution of the wind while the source transits through the intermediate states.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Tracing the evolution of BH winds through the intermediate states                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Mrk_1018_SADA_17062                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏhæÍM@Ïh¿èFj60301122001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿûuI2q␀␀‰ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀êWe propose a 40 ksec observation of Mrk 1018, a `changing-look' AGN changing optical type to a Type 1.9 after 30 stable years of being a Type 1 (McElroy et al. 2016, Husemann et al. 2016). After dropping by an order of magnitude over the last two years, the optical continuum is now again on the rise, hinting at a return to increased accretion activity. We are monitoring Mrk 1018's ongoing extreme variability from the radio through X-ray, but new NuSTAR observation is critically needed to probe the innermost regions surrounding the accretion disk in order to further constrain scenarios that might account for these dramatic, rapid, and cross-spectrum changes.                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Powell                        Meredith                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT IS CAUSING THE NEW, EXTREME VARIABILITY IN MRK 1018?                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_1018                                          @?ã˚µsÎø”ÁlãCïÅ@c›/üæ¿LˆBZÓc @t]À˚µt@Ïh¿èFj@Ïh·±9Ò60301022003@Â5T·GÆ@Â%¡Ω•ù@ÊÈ¥¢3ú@ÊÏy¥¢3úSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿûß°Ú␀␀‰ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀êWe propose a 40 ksec observation of Mrk 1018, a `changing-look' AGN changing optical type to a Type 1.9 after 30 stable years of being a Type 1 (McElroy et al. 2016, Husemann et al. 2016). After dropping by an order of magnitude over the last two years, the optical continuum is now again on the rise, hinting at a return to increased accretion activity. We are monitoring Mrk 1018's ongoing extreme variability from the radio through X-ray, but new NuSTAR observation is critically needed to probe the innermost regions surrounding the accretion disk in order to further constrain scenarios that might account for these dramatic, rapid, and cross-spectrum changes.                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Powell                        Meredith                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT IS CAUSING THE NEW, EXTREME VARIABILITY IN MRK 1018?                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO148mIG002                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïh·±9Ò@Ïh‚xVb‹60374006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿù”Wõ‡␀␀‰ªHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO148mIG002                                      @uŒuˆ˝!ˇ¿MÉàeîØO@t=æﬂ§?Ê¿JÒ∫^5?}@N°4ÍJå@Ïh‚xVb‹@ÏhÚxVb‹60374006002@⁄i‡7¥¢4@⁄RWS&@‹V9†'RT@‹V9†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿùÄë¢¥␀␀‰ªHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀EGS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429MAXI_J1813m095                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏhÚxVb‹@ÏhÛ?r‘£80402303003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿùX^i␀␀‰∑Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    (àThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J F                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1813m095                                    @qóçOﬂ;¿#œAÚ◊@4`™d¬¯@õ¿6‚Î@c•è≈Åo@ÏhÛ?r‘£@ÏiÕ´∏180402303004@”Ûˇ†˘	l@”ÓYm\˙≠@÷1M≠B√ @÷1M≠B√ SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿú˙·GÆ␀␀‰∑Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  (àThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J F                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/3) ToO / idiagnostics_off 2018:065:20:53:43Z                                                                         archived  20200429SWIFTJ0522d3p0744                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏiÕ´∏1@Ïi#Ü60464041001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿú–Ø˘Ô␀␀„NHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SWIFTJ0522d3p0744                                 @T#ÿ≠´üV@ÿ*ô0æ@hfﬂ§?Â…¿0ìtº@tö∞â†'R@Ïi#Ü@ÏiÍM60464041002@”ŒNsÅ◊‹@”Ω‡™d¬¯@’%F^5?}@’&Ü^5?}SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿúé ù∆␀␀„NHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429RX_J1347d5m1145                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏiÍM@Ïi#Ü70301001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿúaên␀␀‰‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀)We propose to observe a merging galaxy cluster RX J1347.5-1145 at z=0.451 with NuSTAR to 1) distinguish between thermal and non-thermal origins of the excess hard component detected by Suzaku and 2) identify its peak position in hard X-rays. If the measured spectrum follows a power-law, we will achieve either the first detection of the non-thermal hard X-ray emission from galaxy clusters or the thermal emission in excess of 25 keV. If it is thermal, we will significantly improve both accuracy of gas temperature and limits on the magnetic field strength in this distant cluster. NuSTAR's hard X-ray spectroscopy and imaging capability are crucial for this study.                    