tic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429UGC_10120                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏjÒ±9Ò@ÏjÚ?r‘£60361013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿçtœ÷␀␀„bHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC_10120                                         @m˘ó$tSè@Aáä	ﬁ@LWßÜ¬'@H≈“Ò©˚Á@`√Uõ=»@ÏjÚ?r‘£@ÏkÍM60361013002@ŸmOÑM@ŸU!:í£@€∞-‡@€p-‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿå∫gÍ√␀␀„bHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ELS NuLANDS                                                                                                             archived  20200429SDSS_J074936d7p33371                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏkÍM@ÏkÕ´∏160301008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿååkµ™␀␀‰ÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of two green pea galaxies (GPs) with an exposure of 20 ksec for each to detect hard X-ray emission as a direct AGN sign. GPs offer valuable opportunity to study the supermassive black hole (SMBH) growth in an early stage of the galaxy formation. Our goal is to investigate whether the AGN resides in such an immature galaxy. Observables ever obtained suggest that our targets can host a buried AGN. Weak optical high excitation lines and the absence of broad lines do not necessarily mean the absence of an AGN. As an alternative idea, these facts could suggest that the AGN is highly obscured if any. Also, the high X-ray luminosity to star formation rate ratios infer the presence. Thus, detecting less extincted hard X-rays is important to finally unveil the AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kawamuro                      Taiki                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       FIRST DETECTION OF BURIED ACTIVE GALACTIC NUCLEI IN EARLY STAGE OF GALAXY FORMATION: GREEN PEA GALAXIES                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSS_J074936d7p33371                              @]WS˜ŒŸ@@ÕS&¡æ@gR*ô0æ@9¬{≤˛≈m@uﬂä	ﬁ@ÏkÕ´∏1@Ïk[‰õø60301008002@“_9≤-V@“Mk§?Â…@”í„Mj@”í„MjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿåJm¸5␀␀‰ÕHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of two green pea galaxies (GPs) with an exposure of 20 ksec for each to detect hard X-ray emission as a direct AGN sign. GPs offer valuable opportunity to study the supermassive black hole (SMBH) growth in an early stage of the galaxy formation. Our goal is to investigate whether the AGN resides in such an immature galaxy. Observables ever obtained suggest that our targets can host a buried AGN. Weak optical high excitation lines and the absence of broad lines do not necessarily mean the absence of an AGN. As an alternative idea, these facts could suggest that the AGN is highly obscured if any. Also, the high X-ray luminosity to star formation rate ratios infer the presence. Thus, detecting less extincted hard X-rays is important to finally unveil the AGN.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kawamuro                      Taiki                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       FIRST DETECTION OF BURIED ACTIVE GALACTIC NUCLEI IN EARLY STAGE OF GALAXY FORMATION: GREEN PEA GALAXIES                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS_J1939m1002                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïk[‰õø@Ïkî»)¯60373004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿå`TÙ␀␀‰ÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀œMeV blazars, that have larger-than-average jet powers, accretion luminosities and black hole masses are best studied in the hard X-ray domain where they display extremely hard X-ray continua. Observations in this domain probe the acceleration & emission mechanisms, the bulk Lorentz factor. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the 4 highest-redshift that have ever been detected at gamma-rays and that do not have any observation between 10 keV and 100 MeV. We will complement the NuSTAR exposure with observations with Fermi, Swift, and SARA to obtain an high-quality simultaneous SED for all these sources. Moreover, optical spectroscopy will be performed for 3 targets lacking a kinematic measurement of the black hole mass. All this will greatly enhance our knowledge of this elusive source class. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316