ÿÖÀ‰±␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀INERTIAL only, straylight, unocculted during slew                                                                       archived  202004291RXSJ184642d2p842506                              @q†€Ùá¸π@UeîØO@]*⁄πıY¥@:˛öu@c¸=p£◊
@Ïkó[‰õø@ÏkóÍM60464157001@w◊ç∏∫«@wµ33333@yP␀␀␀␀␀@yP␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÖòø%å␀␀„sHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004291RXSJ184642d2p842506                              @qüè\(ı√@UÙá¸π$@]*Vìu@;␀©*0U@c¯@∑Ä4n@ÏkóÍM@Ïkü?r‘£60464157002@œB2Ò©˚Á@œ"ƒï*ô@–≠sa@O@–≠sa@OSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÖ[‡$i␀␀„sHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ CVZ                                                                                                                    archived  20200429Swift_J1658d2m4242                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïkü?r‘£@Ïk†?r‘£80302302009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÖ+ùdÅ␀␀‰ÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀9ÕBlack hole transients (BHT) are the only class of black holes (BH) which samples a wide range of accretion rates and accretion states during their month to year long outbursts. BHT classically show two types of outflows: collimated radio-jets ubiquitous during the canonical hard state and highly ionised X-ray winds during the soft state. We propose a combined XMM+NuSTAR monitoring campaign of an high inclination BHT, to shed light on the evolution of the wind while the source transits through the intermediate states.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Tracing the evolution of BH winds through the intermediate states                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“ÁØ∑È¿EWAÚ◊s@urÅ◊€Ùà?œüæv»¥@d˚F‹]cà@Ïk†?r‘£@Ïkπî»)¯80302302010@‹kÄ©*0@‹âË$x@·Qòêñº@·SòêñºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÑÅ
ˇü␀␀‰ÌHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀9ÕBlack hole transients (BHT) are the only class of black holes (BH) which samples a wide range of accretion rates and accretion states during their month to year long outbursts. BHT classically show two types of outflows: collimated radio-jets ubiquitous during the canonical hard state and highly ionised X-ray winds during the soft state. We propose a combined XMM+NuSTAR monitoring campaign of an high inclination BHT, to shed light on the evolution of the wind while the source transits through the intermediate states.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       Tracing the evolution of BH winds through the intermediate states                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(5/5) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429CGCG448m020E                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïkπî»)¯@Ïk∫[‰õø60374002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÑEgâ¨␀␀‰ŸHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CGCG448m020E                                      @s¶Lò_@1$SéÛMj@O‰£n.≤¿1Ùõ•„S¯@d\{≤˛≈m@Ïk∫[‰õø@Ïk—?r‘£60374002002@›JQqué@›1äqﬁj@ﬂlñRΩ<@ﬂo—ñRΩ<SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿÉÎÊ5€␀␀‰ŸHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       PROBING THE GROWTH, OBSCURATION AND FEEDBACK OF SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES IN THE FINAL STAGES OF GALAXY MERGERS          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO148mIG002                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïk—?r‘£@Ïk“#Ü60374006003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÉ¿⁄t␀␀‰”Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊMajor gas-rich galaxy mergers play a significant role in galaxy evolution and SMBH growth. However, we still do not know exactly when and how in the last stages of the merger sequence the observed dramatic SMBH growth phase and its feedback on the environment start. Thanks to its unprecedented E>10 keV sensitivity and imaging capabilities, NuSTAR can strongly contribute to answer these questions. We propose to carry out ten observations of galaxies in the final stages of galaxy mergers which are known to host an AGN. The NuSTAR data will allow to infer the intrinsic luminosity and amount of obscuration in these AGN, and to understand whether feedback removes part of the obscuring material around coalescence.                     