ys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ17476m2253                                    @pÆ]càeï¿6⁄Á’fœB@8Ô4÷°b@Û¸PH@ct-V@Ïm+xVb‹@Ïm>[‰õø60463061002@Ÿ|W'ª0@Ÿe
Ú‰è@€]énóçP@€_nóçPSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿk6`∂␀␀„sHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïm>[‰õø@Ïm>Õ´∏130302020005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿjÕû'␀␀‰ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≠In recent years, the study of the correlated X-ray/OIR fast variability in black-hole transients (BHTs) is showing more and more promising results. GRS 1915+105 was one of the first BHTs to be studied with a multi-wavelength approach, showing the first strong evidences for a disk-jet connection. As of today, only few X-ray-OIR simultaneous observations have been reported of this source. We therefore propose to participate with NuSTAR to an already approved multi-wavelength observing campaign to study the X-ray-IR fast variability, together with XMM-Newton and VLT, in order to cover the hard X-ray band. NuSTAR sensibility in the hard X-rays will be crucial to study the inflow-outflow connection without the accretion disc contamination.                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRS 1915+105 MULTI-WAVELENGTH FAST VARIABILITY                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @r	kπå~@%Ô\(ı¬è@F¢3úæ‡ø´~êˇó$t@bY†'RTa@Ïm>Õ´∏1@ÏmOxVb‹30302020006@∆˚jÒ©˚Á@«†,ï*ô@’SS4÷°b@’XS4÷°bSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿi«∆ZD␀␀‰ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≠In recent years, the study of the correlated X-ray/OIR fast variability in black-hole transients (BHTs) is showing more and more promising results. GRS 1915+105 was one of the first BHTs to be studied with a multi-wavelength approach, showing the first strong evidences for a disk-jet connection. As of today, only few X-ray-OIR simultaneous observations have been reported of this source. We therefore propose to participate with NuSTAR to an already approved multi-wavelength observing campaign to study the X-ray-IR fast variability, together with XMM-Newton and VLT, in order to cover the hard X-ray band. NuSTAR sensibility in the hard X-rays will be crucial to study the inflow-outflow connection without the accretion disc contamination.                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       GRS 1915+105 MULTI-WAVELENGTH FAST VARIABILITY                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM VLT                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(3/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Sgr_B2                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏmOxVb‹@ÏmPèFj40401001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿi\Î$␀␀„sHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                     