agnetic field of the X-ray pulsar 2A 1822-371 and thus give insight into the geometry of this source. With this NuStar observation we will be able to confirm if this pulsar has a high magnetic field as observed in similar sources or if it has a lower magnetic field.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nielsen                       Ann-Sofie                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       OBSERVATION OF POSSIBLE CYCLOTRON RESONANCE SCATTERING FEATURE IN THE X-RAY PULSAR 2A 1822-371                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162A_1822m371                                       @qGä	ﬁ¿Bä¯7¥¢4@vL&¡Ω•¿&+˚µsÎ@c˙2 Wßá@Ïo3?r‘£@ÏoH?r‘£30301009002@€˜±ˆ˝!ˇ@€ˆÕè≈Å@ﬂXa|⁄@ﬂWNa|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿX‚R≠≥␀␀‰˜Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ΩWe propose to conduct a 30 ks observation with NuStar, which would give new insight into the strength of the magnetic field of the X-ray pulsar 2A 1822-371 and thus give insight into the geometry of this source. With this NuStar observation we will be able to confirm if this pulsar has a high magnetic field as observed in similar sources or if it has a lower magnetic field.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nielsen                       Ann-Sofie                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       OBSERVATION OF POSSIBLE CYCLOTRON RESONANCE SCATTERING FEATURE IN THE X-RAY PULSAR 2A 1822-371                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429TXS_0458m020_SADA_18116                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏoH?r‘£@ÏoIxVb‹60367013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿX∂`∂␀␀‰ÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≈We propose to observe 4 blazars at z>2, detected in the gamma-rays by Fermi/LAT and in the soft X-rays, but not yet observed at energies larger than 10 keV. They are among the most powerful blazars and they host black holes whose masses exceed 1e9 Msun. The total requested time of 80 ks is sufficient to establish flux and slope of their X-ray continuum in order to estimate their total electromagnetic output and peak frequency, combined with the Fermi gamma-ray data. This will shed light on the jet-accretion relation, on the controversy about the blazar sequence at its high power extreme, and about the formation epoch of supermassive black holes in jetted quasars.                                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ghisellini                    Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE MOST POWERFUL GAMMA RAY LOUD BLAZARS                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_0458m020                                      @R—≠B√…ÔøˇÌ˙C˛\í@i+µsÍ≥¿9à¥9Xb@v>b∂Æ}V@ÏoIxVb‹@ÏoZ±9Ò60367003001@‘/ösÅ◊‹@‘#±≤ïÍ@’∂‰ÁØ∏@’∫$ÁØ∏SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿX\YÅT␀␀‰ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≈We propose to observe 4 blazars at z>2, detected in the gamma-rays by Fermi/LAT and in the soft X-rays, but not yet observed at energies larger than 10 keV. They are among the most powerful blazars and they host black holes whose masses exceed 1e9 Msun. The total requested time of 80 ks is sufficient to establish flux and slope of their X-ray continuum in order to estimate their total electromagnetic output and peak frequency, combined with the Fermi gamma-ray data. This will shed light on the jet-accretion relation, on the controversy about the blazar sequence at its high power extreme, and about the formation epoch of supermassive black holes in jetted quasars.                                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ghisellini                    Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       THE MOST POWERFUL GAMMA RAY LOUD BLAZARS                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4968                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏoZ±9Ò@Ïo[±9Ò60302006003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿX/4∏␀␀‰ÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀NGC 4968, a nearby Seyfert 2 galaxy, has an extreme Fe K-alpha equivalent width (EW = ~2.5 keV), a hallmark of Compton-thick obscuration. Hints from the Chandra spectrum suggest this obscuration has a spherical covering geometry, which could possibly be fed by circumnuclear star formation. We request 2 20 ks NuSTAR observations, spaced >= 6 months apart, to measure the column density and geometry of the X-ray reprocessor, calculate the intrinsic X-ray luminosity and compare that with the mid-infrared emission, and to search for variability similar to that observed in NGC 1068, the prototypical Compton-thick AGN which also has an extreme Fe K-alpha EW. Evidence of variability would provide insight into the physics of the central engine and perhaps distance to the X-ray reprocessor.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lamassa                       Stephanie                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MODELING THE OBSCURATION AROUND EXTREME AGN NGC 4968                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4968                                          @hò±ƒ2 X¿7≤ WßÜ¬@s4JåLò@Cí£S&@s3÷8ÜYK@Ïo[±9Ò@Ïon[‰õø60302006004@”÷ÃcÒA@”∆gÿ≠´ü@’@®uˆ˝"@’AËuˆ˝"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿW›dÄÛ␀␀‰ÔHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀NGC 4968, a nearby Seyfert 2 galaxy, has an extreme Fe K-alpha equivalent width (EW = ~2.5 keV), a hallmark of Compton-thick obscuration. Hints from the Chandra spectrum suggest this obscuration has a spherical covering geometry, which could possibly be fed by circumnuclear star formation. We request 2 20 ks NuSTAR observations, spaced >= 6 months apart, to measure the column density and geometry of the X-ray reprocessor, calculate the intrinsic X-ray luminosity and compare that with the mid-infrared emission, and to search for variability similar to that observed in NGC 1068, the prototypical Compton-thick AGN which also has an extreme Fe K-alpha EW. Evidence of variability would provide insight into the physics of the central engine and perhaps distance to the X-ray reprocessor.         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lamassa                       Stephanie                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       MODELING THE OBSCURATION AROUND EXTREME AGN NGC 4968                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429WKK6092                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïon[‰õø@ÏooèFj60160632001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿW1Æ÷©␀␀„êHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WKK6092                                           @n`GE8Ô5¿NP	‘ï+@tQºj~˘€¿\wökP±@jüæv»¥@ÏooèFj@Ïo}ÍM60160632002@’â	-w1ê@’y◊
=q@÷ınJåM@÷ınJåMSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿV“≈˘,␀␀„êHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Swift_J1658d2m4242                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïo}ÍM@Ïo~[‰õø90401317001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿV®π␀␀„ÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1658d2m4242                                @o“è\(ı√¿EV’œ™Õü@urxl"h
?–©*0U2@f„a@N•@Ïo~[‰õø@Ïoì?r‘£90401317002@€”@•zxl@€⁄∏4m≈÷@ﬂæÅ•ú‡@ﬂø¡•ú‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿV3|ç␀␀„ÇHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429WR_140                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïoì?r‘£@Ïoî#Ü30302023003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿV	ƒπ␀␀‰¸Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÿThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric, massive-binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at various, shock densities and on particle acceleration. The campaign observation of WR 140 with NuSTAR and XMM around periastron in 2016 provided an interesting variation of the extremely hard X-ray component, which may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV photons by particles accelerated at the WWC region. We propose two joint NuSTAR and XMM observations of WR 140 in AO3 to monitor the variation of the extremely hard X-ray component during the flux decline after periastron.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY VARIATION OF WR140 AFTER PERIASTRON                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR_140                                            @sJåLò@E|Ñµ‹∆@T6ÄùIQÉ@Ó±ƒ2 X@aÁˇ.HËß@Ïoî#Ü@Ïoµ#Ü30302023004@ÊΩëÖoi@Ê™øπå~(@ËR-†˘	l@ËSm†˘	lSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿU©Ë|f␀␀‰¸Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÿThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric, massive-binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at various, shock densities and on particle acceleration. The campaign observation of WR 140 with NuSTAR and XMM around periastron in 2016 provided an interesting variation of the extremely hard X-ray component, which may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV photons by particles accelerated at the WWC region. We propose two joint NuSTAR and XMM observations of WR 140 in AO3 to monitor the variation of the extremely hard X-ray component during the flux decline after periastron.                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY VARIATION OF WR140 AFTER PERIASTRON                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PSR_J1617m5055                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïoµ#Ü@Ïo∂#Ü30301013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿUgâ´Œ␀␀‰¸Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ËObservations of hard X-ray emission from rotation-powered pulsars are crucial for understanding the physics of pulsar magnetospheres. However, only a handful of brightest pulsars have been observed at these energies, and most of them have not been investigated in detail. To probe the magnetosphere of the young, energetic PSR J1617-5055, whose properties are drastically different from those of the best-investigated Crab pulsar, we propose a NuSTAR observation for phase-resolved spectroscopy and energy-resolved timing of pulsar radiation. Being bright in hard X-rays, this pulsar has a very underluminous pulsar wind nebula, which greatly facilitates NuSTAR data analysis.                                                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pavlov                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       SPECTRAL AND TIMING PROPERTIES OF THE YOUNG, ENERGETIC PSR J1617-5055 IN HARD X-RAYS                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J1617m5055                                    @nç»K]Ã¿Isúæﬂ§@t∆p£◊
