od yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CONTINUED NUSTAR MONITORING OF THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C AT T>1000 DAYS SINCE EXPLOSION                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u4ŒŸá+@A5Û∂E°À@WbΩ<64¿4π#¢úwö@cpN§®¡U@ÏpUî»)¯@Ïpr[‰õø40302002004@„¢ç∏ª@„ë∂Œp:˚@Â;>´6z@Â[>´6zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿO~2“␀␀‰¸Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˝Mass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar death.               Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       CONTINUED NUSTAR MONITORING OF THE UNPRECEDENTED METAMORPHOSIS OF SN2014C AT T>1000 DAYS SINCE EXPLOSION                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429CXOU_J173324d6m332321                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïpr[‰õø@Ïps?r‘£90401321001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿON ù∆␀␀„äHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  