=@‹◊ãÁlãD@‹ﬂÁlãDSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ=Qr(4␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀⁄Fermi has detected over 200 gamma-ray pulsars, but only a handful fall in the soft gamma-ray (emission peaking below 1 GeV) category. These tend to be single-pulsed, young, and extremely energetic, the archetypal example being PSR B1509-58. With the greatly improved sensitivity of Pass 8, Fermi is expected to detect increasing numbers of soft gamma-ray pulsars. We propose NuSTAR observations of 5 soft gamma-ray pulsar candidates. The unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR in the hard X-ray energy range, combined with existing soft X-ray and gamma-ray data (e.g. Chandra, XMM, and Fermi) will enable us to test and constrain models of high-energy pulsar emission over a range of parameters (e.g. viewing geometries, spind-down parameters).                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Parkinson                     Pablo                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF SOFT GAMMA-RAY PULSARS                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïs*#Ü@Ïs+[‰õø30302019009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ<“d‚
␀␀ÂYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @rπP∞Ú{≥@Aûcä	@Qœ›óˆ+k@	xF‹]d@]"-V@Ïs+[‰õø@Ïs:?r‘£30302019010@¿
˘|⁄Q@¿ÓÉÍ≥g°@÷H}øH@÷»}øHSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ;Çß°Ú␀␀ÂYHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(5/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO_317mG041                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïs:?r‘£@Ïs;î»)¯60468002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ;Dtœ÷␀␀ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ùThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation three candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month survey. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to assess if these sources are in fact CT-AGN. We will also constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN and their contribution to the CXB.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       HEAVILY OBSCURED AGN: THE PHYSICS BEHIND THE OBSCURATION                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_317mG041                                      @c∫}øHÄ¿EF‹]cà@qL_o“ @+3¸PH@uœ¶µ(@Ïs;î»)¯@ÏsRÍM60468002002@ﬁf	ﬁ@›ÁÅF
¶L@‡çökP±@‡≠ökP±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ:ÏÒ5z␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ùThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation three candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month survey. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to assess if these sources are in fact CT-AGN. We will also constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN and their contribution to the CXB.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       HEAVILY OBSCURED AGN: THE PHYSICS BEHIND THE OBSCURATION                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GRS_1734m292                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏsRÍM@ÏsSÍM60301010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ:∫·GÆ␀␀ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀(We propose an observation of the Seyfert 1 galaxy GRS 1734-292 with NuSTAR, 25 ks long. Our primary aim is to search for changes in the variable coronal environment of this source, which has recently shown one of the lowest high-energy cutoff in the NuSTAR era (Ec=53+11-8 keV: Tortosa et al., 2016). GRS 1734-292 is the perfect candidate for this goal: it is a bright Seyfert 1 galaxy which showed a variable primary continuum (both in spectral shape and in flux), on time scales of years. The comparison between past data and the hereby proposed observation will allow us to infer, with high accuracy, a variation in the physical parameters of the corona surrounding the accretion disk in GRS 1734-292.                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       INVESTIGATING THE VARIABLE CORONAL ENVIRONMENT OF THE SEYFERT 1 GALAXY GRS 1734-292                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1734m292                                      @pÜMjO¿=~˘€"—@vlÿD–©?˘ç∏∫«@e›„S˜ŒŸ@ÏsSÍM@ÏsièFj60301010002@Ÿi*0U2@ŸYLK]Ãd@€s#/ÏV÷@€s£/ÏV÷SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ:h|e§␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀(We propose an observation of the Seyfert 1 galaxy GRS 1734-292 with NuSTAR, 25 ks long. Our primary aim is to search for changes in the variable coronal environment of this source, which has recently shown one of the lowest high-energy cutoff in the NuSTAR era (Ec=53+11-8 keV: Tortosa et al., 2016). GRS 1734-292 is the perfect candidate for this goal: it is a bright Seyfert 1 galaxy which showed a variable primary continuum (both in spectral shape and in flux), on time scales of years. The comparison between past data and the hereby proposed observation will allow us to infer, with high accuracy, a variation in the physical parameters of the corona surrounding the accretion disk in GRS 1734-292.                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       INVESTIGATING THE VARIABLE CORONAL ENVIRONMENT OF THE SEYFERT 1 GALAXY GRS 1734-292                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Crab_SLPA335                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏsièFj@Ïsj[‰õø10402008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ:;B^—␀␀Á¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab_SLPA335                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïsj[‰õø@ÏstxVb‹10402008002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ9æƒt–␀␀Á¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀	G␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Straylight observation                                                                                                  archived  20150316Sol_18149_AR2712_POS1                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏstÕ´∏1@ÏsvÍM80410201001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ8¬ˇ>␀␀„∂Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ΩOur goal is to investigate the solar coronal heating problem, specifically the role of small solar flares in explaining how the Sun s corona is consistently heated to millions of degrees. To this aim, we will observe solar active regions with long enough integrations to detect faint signatures of small flares and will quantify their contributions to the heating of these particular regions. We will also combine NuSTAR measurements with those from novel solar-observing telescopes operating the extreme ultraviolet X-ray range to obtain the most sensitive measurements to date of flare heating of the corona. With these coordinated observations we will search for the weak high-temperature (>5MK) and non- thermal emission expected from impulsive energy release models.                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Glesener                      Lindsay                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       INVESTIGATION OF IMPULSIVE HEATING OF ACTIVE REGIONS IN THE SOLAR CORONA                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                           