e NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429RXS_J1131m1231                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏtpèFj@ÏtqèFj60401001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ4>‹∫ò␀␀Â*Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀@£We propose to perform a simultaneous observation with XMM-Newton (130 ks) and NuSTAR (75 ks) of the lensed quasar RX J1131-1231. The flux magnification makes it possible to study in detail the central regions of a quasar at moderate redshift (z=0.658). The existing X-ray data reveal the presence of strong reflection features in RX J1131-1231, suggesting a rapid rotation of the central black hole. The new broadband observations proposed here will probe for the first time the hard X-ray emission of the source, allowing us to witness the behaviour of the broadband X-ray spectrum in response to the frequent microlensing events. RX J1131-1231 provides the unique opportunity of measuring the spin of the black hole employing two independent methods: reflection and microlensing.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nardini                       Emanuele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X17       X-ray reflection and microlensing in the lensed quasar RX J1131-1231                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RXS_J1131m1231                                    @eû⁄QŒ¿)µsÍ≥@q ÙÿD–@FÌVì@v9•zxl"@ÏtqèFj@ÏtÆÍM60401001002@ÚÓ™7K∆®@Ú‚]ºj~˙@ÙV{QÇ©ì@ÙXkQÇ©ìSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ3©áeC␀␀Â*Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀@£We propose to perform a simultaneous observation with XMM-Newton (130 ks) and NuSTAR (75 ks) of the lensed quasar RX J1131-1231. The flux magnification makes it possible to study in detail the central regions of a quasar at moderate redshift (z=0.658). The existing X-ray data reveal the presence of strong reflection features in RX J1131-1231, suggesting a rapid rotation of the central black hole. The new broadband observations proposed here will probe for the first time the hard X-ray emission of the source, allowing us to witness the behaviour of the broadband X-ray spectrum in response to the frequent microlensing events. RX J1131-1231 provides the unique opportunity of measuring the spin of the black hole employing two independent methods: reflection and microlensing.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Nardini                       Emanuele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X17       X-ray reflection and microlensing in the lensed quasar RX J1131-1231                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏtÆÍM@Ït∞èFj80401312001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ3n¶ï␀␀‰mHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‹We propose a 30 ks ToO, contemporaneous with a pre-approved 30 ks NICER ToO, to observe a low frequency quasi-periodic oscillation (QPO) from the accreting black hole (BH) GRS 1915+105. We will use QPO phase-resolved spectroscopy to test for QPO phase dependent changes in the shape of the reflection spectrum and disc temperature indicative of relativistic precession. The unique combination of NICER and NuSTAR will allow the first joint analysis of disc, iron line and reflection hump modulations. We will trigger from MAXI, plus Swift/XRT pointings, and aim to supplement our core science goals with multi-wavelength observations.                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ingram                        Adam                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       TESTING RELATIVISTIC PRECESSION AROUND THE ACCRETING BLACK HOLE IN GRS 1915+105 WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rqﬁi≠C@%Ù!-w2@F°Œ_pøëú‡uˆ˝"@ZﬂX‚e@Ït∞èFj@Ït«[‰õø80401312002@ŸçüMj@Ÿ„ÙÜYJı@‡Û:g8~@‡Û∫g8~SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ2¨êW␀␀‰mHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‹We propose a 30 ks ToO, contemporaneous with a pre-approved 30 ks NICER ToO, to observe a low frequency quasi-periodic oscillation (QPO) from the accreting black hole (BH) GRS 1915+105. We will use QPO phase-resolved spectroscopy to test for QPO phase dependent changes in the shape of the reflection spectrum and disc temperature indicative of relativistic precession. The unique combination of NICER and NuSTAR will allow the first joint analysis of disc, iron line and reflection hump modulations. We will trigger from MAXI, plus Swift/XRT pointings, and aim to supplement our core science goals with multi-wavelength observations.                                                                                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Ingram                        Adam                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       TESTING RELATIVISTIC PRECESSION AROUND THE ACCRETING BLACK HOLE IN GRS 1915+105 WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Ark_564                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ït«[‰õø@Ït«ÍM60401031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ2}X^␀␀Â⁄Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀—Ark 564 is a luminous accreting supermassive black hole that has revealed several extraordinary qualities in soft X-rays: it is the brightest NLS1 from 0.3-10 keV, is one of the most highly variable AGN, has a very steep spectrum and strong soft excess. In NuSTAR AO1, we were awarded 200 ks to study this source above 20 keV for the first time, and discovered that Ark 564 is as exceptional in hard X-rays as it is in soft. Ark 564 has a coronal temperature of 15 2 keV, one of the lowest ever observed, and also shows an iron K line complex that is so highly ionised that it resembles more closely a black hole binary than an AGN. We now propose for an additional 500 ks of NuSTAR observations, simultaneous with 200 ks of XMM-Newton time and 100 ks of NICER time (through NICER Baseline Mission   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE COOLEST CORONA IN THE EDDINGTON-LIMITED AGN ARK 564                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Ark_564                                           @uJ”ZÖáî@=√¢úwök@V¸bM“Ò™¿9KI∫^@`X·GÆ{@Ït«ÍM@Ït‚ëF˘60401031002@‚ô=≈Åo@‚Öÿ◊s¸@„ÊÅú‡u˜@„Ê·ú‡u˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ2"‰Pg␀␀Â⁄Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀—Ark 564 is a luminous accreting supermassive black hole that has revealed several extraordinary qualities in soft X-rays: it is the brightest NLS1 from 0.3-10 keV, is one of the most highly variable AGN, has a very steep spectrum and strong soft excess. In NuSTAR AO1, we were awarded 200 ks to study this source above 20 keV for the first time, and discovered that Ark 564 is as exceptional in hard X-rays as it is in soft. Ark 564 has a coronal temperature of 15 2 keV, one of the lowest ever observed, and also shows an iron K line complex that is so highly ionised that it resembles more closely a black hole binary than an AGN. We now propose for an additional 500 ks of NuSTAR observations, simultaneous with 200 ks of XMM-Newton time and 100 ks of NICER time (through NICER Baseline Mission   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE COOLEST CORONA IN THE EDDINGTON-LIMITED AGN ARK 564                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ACS_contingency_20180610                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ït‚ëF˘@ÏuÑ±9Ò00401005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï≠<6;ÏGˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Non-Pointing data                                           ␀␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Spacecraft ACS anomaly / Instrument powered off                                                                         observed  201503163C273                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏuÑ±9Ò@ÏuÜèFj10402020003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ>t%Ì	␀␀„∂Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C273                                             @ghêñªô@␀E÷8ÜYK@rS˜ŒŸ@P¬¯7¥¢@vm:)«yß@ÏuÜèFj@ÏuîÕ´∏110402020004@‘Ø∆«À)@‘ü4–©*@÷"D÷°aÂ@÷"D÷°aÂSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ1´µ™J␀␀„∂Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             archived  20200429Elias_29                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏuîÕ´∏1@ÏuïxVb‹30301001005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ1É„-"␀␀Â.Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀8ÉClass~I and II YSOs are bright sources of soft (< 10 keV) X-rays, but very little is known of their hard (> 10 keV) X-ray emission and of their inter-relation. Those YSOs can exhibit emission in the Fe Kalpha line at 6.4 keV. Its origin and site of production is still controversial, e.g., being due to flare reverberation or accelerated particles hitting the corona or the surrounding inner part of the circumstellar disk. Time resolved spectroscopy made possible with the proposed 300 ks long joint simultaneous XMM-EPIC and Nustar observations of Elias29, in the nearby (120 pc) rho Oph SFR, and of the other YSOs in the Nustar FOV will allow us to investigate, for the first time, these open questions.                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sciortino                     Salvatore                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The hard X-ray emission of Class I-II YSOs and the origin of the 6.4 keV Fe line                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Elias_29                                          @nÿı¬è\)¿8¶á+J@vx‘˝Û∂@0◊_o“Û@tÍ4ÍJå@ÏuïxVb‹@Ïuﬁ#Ü30301001006@¯˜8ÍJå@¯‰dhr∞!@˙¿¿kP∞Ú@˙¡∞kP∞ÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ0Ò5yæ␀␀Â.Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀8ÉClass~I and II YSOs are bright sources of soft (< 10 keV) X-rays, but very little is known of their hard (> 10 keV) X-ray emission and of their inter-relation. Those YSOs can exhibit emission in the Fe Kalpha line at 6.4 keV. Its origin and site of production is still controversial, e.g., being due to flare reverberation or accelerated particles hitting the corona or the surrounding inner part of the circumstellar disk. Time resolved spectroscopy made possible with the proposed 300 ks long joint simultaneous XMM-EPIC and Nustar observations of Elias29, in the nearby (120 pc) rho Oph SFR, and of the other YSOs in the Nustar FOV will allow us to investigate, for the first time, these open questions.                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Sciortino                     Salvatore                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The hard X-ray emission of Class I-II YSOs and the origin of the 6.4 keV Fe line                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                          