   @Ïÿ0G}àÍ␀␀„∫Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Calibration / Kp=5                                                                                                      archived  20200429RCW_86_SW                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏuˇÕ´∏1@Ïv␀±9Ò40301005003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ0Y‚k␀␀Â.Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ˆThat SNRs accelerate particles to CR energies is no longer in doubt, but there are many questions yet to be answered. We still lack detailed knowledge about the efficiency of the process of particle acceleration at SNR shocks, the effect of CR production on the evolution of SNRs, and the properties of magnetic fields in these systems and how these are amplified. In order to address these outstanding issues, we propose to observe the SW region of SNR RCW 86 with NuSTAR, with a 200 ks pointing (shown in Fig. 1). This will allow us to: (1) determine the morphology of the non-thermal X-ray emission in this region in the NuSTAR band, and contrast it to observations with XMM-Newton and Chandra, (2) constrain the spectral shape of the electron population that underlies the non-thermal emission.  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Castro                        Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A HARD (X-RAY) LOOK AT THE SW REGION OF SNR RCW 86                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RCW_86_SW                                         @kâøHÀí¿OWÆz·H@s∞êˇó$t¿|π#¢úx@s"—N;Õ6@Ïv␀±9Ò@Ïv,ÍM40301005004@ÒÕëwökQ@Ò∏z‰˜e˛@Ûì3úø@Û”3úøSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ/ûá∆Z␀␀Â.Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ˆThat SNRs accelerate particles to CR energies is no longer in doubt, but there are many questions yet to be answered. We still lack detailed knowledge about the efficiency of the process of particle acceleration at SNR shocks, the effect of CR production on the evolution of SNRs, and the properties of magnetic fields in these systems and how these are amplified. In order to address these outstanding issues, we propose to observe the SW region of SNR RCW 86 with NuSTAR, with a 200 ks pointing (shown in Fig. 1). This will allow us to: (1) determine the morphology of the non-thermal X-ray emission in this region in the NuSTAR band, and contrast it to observations with XMM-Newton and Chandra, (2) constrain the spectral shape of the electron population that underlies the non-thermal emission.  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Castro                        Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A HARD (X-RAY) LOOK AT THE SW REGION OF SNR RCW 86                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429CXOU_J110926d4m650224                             @dË‚e+‘¿PCó$tSè@rDŒp:˚¿Òué!ñS@v,µsÍ≥@Ïv,ÍM@Ïv-[‰õø80101101001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ/jíd‚␀␀Â:Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀*íThe XMM discovery of a ms pulsar swinging between an accretion-powered (X-ray)and a rotation-powered (radio) pulsar state demonstrated that transitions between the two states can be observed over timescales of a few weeks. We propose an XMM/NuStar ToO observation of 60 ks aimed at studying the accretion state of transitional ms pulsars, detecting X-ray accretion powered pulsations, and characterizing its variability over three decades in energy. Candidates are restricted to black widows and redbacks, systems in an evolutionary phase that allows state transitions. Enlarging the number of systems in this transitional phase is crucial to test binary evolution theories, and to study the disk-field interaction over a large range of mass accretion rates.                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Papitto                       Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Hunting for transitional millisecond pulsars with XMM-Newton and NuStar                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CXOU_J110926d4m650224                             @dÈ«y¶µ¿PDV’œ™Œ@rEÌ(åÁ¿˜ŒŸá+@v8—N;Õ6@Ïv-[‰õø@ÏvXèFj80101101002@©≤Lò_@õ—qv@ÒœîÅo␀@Ò–Åo␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ.»ààâ␀␀Â:Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀*íThe XMM discovery of a ms pulsar swinging between an accretion-powered (X-ray)and a rotation-powered (radio) pulsar state demonstrated that transitions between the two states can be observed over timescales of a few weeks. We propose an XMM/NuStar ToO observation of 60 ks aimed at studying the accretion state of transitional ms pulsars, detecting X-ray accretion powered pulsations, and characterizing its variability over three decades in energy. Candidates are restricted to black widows and redbacks, systems in an evolutionary phase that allows state transitions. Enlarging the number of systems in this transitional phase is crucial to test binary evolution theories, and to study the disk-field interaction over a large range of mass accretion rates.                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Papitto                       Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X14       Hunting for transitional millisecond pulsars with XMM-Newton and NuStar                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM ATCA                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429HD_93129A                                         @d…„Ω¿M»Aâ7K«@qÙL/É{Jø·Û∂E° ¡@Ä—∑X‚@ÏvXèFj@ÏvX[‰õø30402001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ/Al¡␀␀ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    $˘¯Colliding-wind binaries are expected to be non-thermal (NT) X-ray sources; however, up to date none has been firmly confirmed as such. With a total mass of 120 Mo, an eccentricity of 0.9, and an orbital period of 120 yr, HD 93129A is one of the most extreme massive binaries in our Galaxy, and it will go through periastron passage in 2018. During that time, the intensity of the wind-collision region (WCR) will greatly enhance, making this a unique opportunity to determine the properties of the shocked plasma and the level of NT hard X-ray emission. This event is a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity which would provide an observational breakthrough in our understanding of shock physics, the hydrodynamics of the WCR, and mechanisms of particle acceleration and magnetic field amplification.       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    del Palacio                   S                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       A unique opportunity to observe the extreme massive binary HD 93129A near periastron passage                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HD_93129A                                         @d!ñRΩ<¿M»|Ñµ‹∆@qÙcàeîØø·ÎÖ∏QÏ@≠Vì@ÏvX[‰õø@ÏviÕ´∏130402001002@⁄Úç‰˜e˛@⁄ÿz}VlÙ@‹Ÿ·õ=»@‹⁄aõ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ.rXø&␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  $˘¯Colliding-wind binaries are expected to be non-thermal (NT) X-ray sources; however, up to date none has been firmly confirmed as such. With a total mass of 120 Mo, an eccentricity of 0.9, and an orbital period of 120 yr, HD 93129A is one of the most extreme massive binaries in our Galaxy, and it will go through periastron passage in 2018. During that time, the intensity of the wind-collision region (WCR) will greatly enhance, making this a unique opportunity to determine the properties of the shocked plasma and the level of NT hard X-ray emission. This event is a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity which would provide an observational breakthrough in our understanding of shock physics, the hydrodynamics of the WCR, and mechanisms of particle acceleration and magnetic field amplification.       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    del Palacio                   S                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C19       A unique opportunity to observe the extreme massive binary HD 93129A near periastron passage                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Fairall_51                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏviÕ´∏1@Ïvj[‰õø60402014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ.E∏R␀␀ÊûHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose an observation of the particularly intriguing polar-scattered Seyfert 1 Fairall 51 with NuSTAR (120 ks), divided in two periods of 60 ks separated by about 8 months, to be coordinated with our two Guaranteed Time Observations (GTO) with Chandra/HETG (120 ks each, so 240 ks of total exposure). Timing and spectral analyses of these joint observations will put constraints on the characteristics of reflection from both the accretion disk near the black hole and from more distant material, on the presence and origin of a possible broad iron line, on the spin of the black hole, on the inclination angle, on the physical properties of the hot corona, on the structure and properties of the warm absorbers, and on the origin of the potential soft excess.                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boissay Malaquin              Rozenn                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       INVESTIGATION ON FAIRALL 51 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA/HETG                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Fairall_51                                        @qîﬁ“àŒp¿O/À)^û@t“⁄QŒ¿6Òƒ2 W®@lÕN;Õ5®@Ïvj[‰õø@Ïvñ#Ü60402014002@ÔuOÑM@ÓÈZ"h	’@ïœ0U2a@ó0U2aSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ-“LúA␀␀ÊûHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose an observation of the particularly intriguing polar-scattered Seyfert 1 Fairall 51 with NuSTAR (12