Ïÿ$`<Æv␀␀„◊Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C273                                             @gg·∞â†'@␀IÖoiD@r¨1'@PÃÃÃÃÕ@vSÌ(åÁ@Ïw˜#Ü@ÏxèFj10402020006@„Ø•Õ5®X@„ûÅÌ(åÁ@Â0ÍÿD–@Â2ÍÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ#º”␀␀„◊Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra XMM INTEGRAL                                                                                ␀␀␀␀IACHEC cross calibration                                                                                                archived  20200429SWIFT_J2127d4p5654                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏxèFj@ÏxÍM60402008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ#∑www␀␀ÂÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∏Recent data have led to a breakthrough in AGN studies through the detection of relativistic reverberation. These echoes are caused by the delayed response of the reflected emission to a primary illuminating source, providing a powerful tool to probe regions close to the black hole in an unprecedented manner. The properties of the echoes are constant on days timescale, but change over the years. The characteristic timescale of the change, which would suggest a geometry change, is unknown. Here, we propose to observe SW J2127.4 for 350 ks to address this question. With several exposures separated by weeks to months, we will constrain this characteristic timescale and probe changes in the geometry of the system, providing unprecedented details about the disk-corona at the horizon scales.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zoghbi                        Abderahmen                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       PROBING THE CHANGING REVERBERATION ECHOES                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SWIFT_J2127d4p5654                                @tª/ÏV÷@LzÙÿD–@Xlºj~˘€@Ü
¶L/É@X~äqﬁi≠@ÏxÍM@ÏxJÕ´∏160402008002@ÒeoÅ$›@ÒRÅ˙¨ŸË@Ú≥∏dZ¨@Ú∂xdZ¨SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ#¸˜G␀␀ÂÎHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∏Recent data have led to a breakthrough in AGN studies through the detection of relativistic reverberation. These echoes are caused by the delayed response of the reflected emission to a primary illuminating source, providing a powerful tool to probe regions close to the black hole in an unprecedented manner. The properties of the echoes are constant on days timescale, but change over the years. The characteristic timescale of the change, which would suggest a geometry change, is unknown. Here, we propose to observe SW J2127.4 for 350 ks to address this question. With several exposures separated by weeks to months, we will constrain this characteristic timescale and probe changes in the geometry of the system, providing unprecedented details about the disk-corona at the horizon scales.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zoghbi                        Abderahmen                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       PROBING THE CHANGING REVERBERATION ECHOES                                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀(1/5) / Kp=5                                                                                                            archived  20200429MAXI_J1820p070                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏxJÕ´∏1@ÏxKxVb‹90401309022␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ"œWú␀␀„–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1820p070                                    @q0•zxl"@ƒõ•„S¯@A›óˆ+jË@$Àí:)«z@FT≤ïÈ·±@ÏxKxVb‹@ÏxYÍM90401309023@ºdœ˘rGE@æHë—N;Õ@’ƒÀÈ˘r@’≈ãÈ˘rSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ!o…b˝␀␀„–Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory i