ssed or even absent primary continuum. As the continuum disappears weak spectral features like relativistic iron lines or narrow soft X-ray emission lines from ionized plasmas become highly significant and their parameters can be determined. Therefore deep minimum states offer unique possibilities to investigate in detail the physics of the reprocessed components in AGN, including the immediate vicinity of the supermassive black hole. Applying our experience (several deep minimum observations) we propose 2 triggered 10ks XMM snapshot, one 80ks XMM follow-up simultaneous with one 80ks NuSTAR one 2-orbit HST observation of an AGN in deep minimum state. We will identify deep minimum states mainly based on Swift and XMM-NewtonProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Catching AGN in Deep Minimum States to Unveil Their Core Environment                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mkn_335                                           ?˘a‰˜e˝ã@4>cä	@[º£n¿D∑OÑM@_æOv_Ÿ@Ïx∑[‰õø@Ïxı[‰õø80201001002@ÙK]Ãd@Û˝@␀␀␀␀@ı~∞Œp:˚@ıŒp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿY %+␀␀ÂôHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀0‚The deep minimum state of AGNs is characterized by a strongly suppressed or even absent primary continuum. As the continuum disappears weak spectral features like relativistic iron lines or narrow soft X-ray emission lines from ionized plasmas become highly significant and their parameters can be determined. Therefore deep minimum states offer unique possibilities to investigate in detail the physics of the reprocessed components in AGN, including the immediate vicinity of the supermassive black hole. Applying our experience (several deep minimum observations) we propose 2 triggered 10ks XMM snapshot, one 80ks XMM follow-up simultaneous with one 80ks NuSTAR one 2-orbit HST observation of an AGN in deep minimum state. We will identify deep minimum states mainly based on Swift and XMM-NewtonProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Catching AGN in Deep Minimum States to Unveil Their Core Environment                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429XY_Ari                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïxı[‰õø@ÏxıÕ´∏130460006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÓæc^␀␀„–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316XY_Ari                                            @F»K]Ã@3w¡Ω•ù@c·S˜ŒŸ¿ATãCïÅ@a[sÍ≥g°@ÏxıÕ´∏1@Ïx˚ÍM30460006002@¬ˆ1¯†ê@¬Ì˙C˛]@√Cl÷°aÂ@√Cl÷°aÂSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿôi␀␀„–Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀GLS IP sample. End early due to a fast ToO                                                                              archived  202004294U_1608m52                                        @neä	ﬁ¿J;˛\ë—N@t¨M“Ò©¸øÊÉnóçP@s¶„º”[@Ïx˚ÍM@Ïx˝èFj80401321001@<Íô0æÌ@<ﬁﬂ§?Â…@?␀␀␀␀␀␀@?␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿl®d ␀␀‰áHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀AIt is commonly assumed that the accretion disk in low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXBs) moves significantly away from the black hole or neutron star when the X-ray luminosity decays below ~E-2 of the Eddington limit (Ledd). However, there is virtually no observational data to test this key aspect of accretion disk physics. Using a single 60-ks NuSTAR ToO observation and 11 ks of Swift/XRT monitoring, we propose to exploit the proximity and strong reflection features of the neutron star LMXB 4U 1608-52 to measure the location of its inner disk at a luminosity of ~E-3 Ledd. Comparing this with our previous NuSTAR observation obtained at E-2 Ledd, where we found the disk very close to the neutron star, we can directly test the disk truncation paradigm.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       TESTING DISK TRUNCATION FOR ACCRETING NEUTRON STARS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004294U_1608m52                                        @neé!ñRΩ¿J;dZ¨@t¨\(ı¬èøÊh€ã¨q@sÆÏV’œ´@Ïx˝èFj@Ïy'±9Ò80401321002@Í=±ó$tT@Í'–ö@Ïeûi≠Bƒ@Ïj~i≠BƒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ˙…Â␀␀‰áHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀AIt is commonly assumed that the accretion disk in low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXBs) moves significantly away from the black hole or neutron star when the X-ray luminosity decays below ~E-2 of the Eddington limit (Ledd). However, there is virtually no observational data to test this key aspect of accretion disk physics. Using a single 60-ks NuSTAR ToO observation and 11 ks of Swift/XRT monitoring, we propose to exploit the proximity and strong reflection features of the neutron star LMXB 4U 1608-52 to measure the location of its inner disk at a luminosity of ~E-3 Ledd. Comparing this with our previous NuSTAR observation obtained at E-2 Ledd, where we found the disk very close to the neutron star, we can directly test the disk truncation paradigm.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       TESTING DISK TRUNCATION FOR ACCRETING NEUTRON STARS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429AT2018cow          