fter 30 stable years of being a Type 1 (McElroy et al. 2016, Husemann et al. 2016). After dropping by an order of magnitude over the last two years, the optical continuum is now again on the rise, hinting at a return to increased accretion activity. We are monitoring Mrk 1018's ongoing extreme variability from the radio through X-ray, but new NuSTAR observation is critically needed to probe the innermost regions surrounding the accretion disk in order to further constrain scenarios that might account for these dramatic, rapid, and cross-spectrum changes.                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Powell                        Meredith                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WHAT IS CAUSING THE NEW, EXTREME VARIABILITY IN MRK 1018?                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_1018                                          @?í˛≈m\˚ø–Àí:)«@cè\(ı√¿LÓûö@`™äqﬁi≠@Ïyõ#Ü@Ïy∏ÍM60301022005@‰nHÌ(ç@‰Q„[W>´@ÂÂ À˚µ@ÂÂ`À˚µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ*1M¿␀␀ÂDHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀êWe propose a 40 ksec observation of Mrk 1018, a `changing-look' AGN changing optical type to a Type 1.9 after 30 stable years of being a Type 1 (McElroy et al. 2016, Husemann et al. 2016). After dropping by an order of magnitude over the last two years, the optical continuum is now again on the rise, hinting at a return to increased accretion activity. We are monitoring Mrk 1018