 NULL                                                        ␀N03       NEUTRON STARS AND THEIR INNERMOST ACCRETION FLOWS                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk1383                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï|ã[‰õø@Ï|åèFj60061254001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ-:‘␀␀„ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk1383                                           @k(1&Èy?Ù9¿ÎÌ˙D@uœqué@K™‘,<ûÌ@uﬂnóçP@Ï|åèFj@Ï|£?r‘£60061254002@ﬂ∏ñÀ˚µ@ﬂêA„S˜œ@·dp:˚@·	Dp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ’É˚␀␀„ÏHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï|£?r‘£@Ï|§èFj30302019011␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ§ïœ␀␀ÂYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @r∏ßÔù≤-@AòŒp:˚@QÃ[¿6„@	Ñµ‹∆?@C~Ov_Ÿ@Ï|§èFj@Ï|¥?r‘£30302019012@«´iú‡u˜@»≥n!ñRΩ@÷∫ﬁzêó@÷Ω^zêóSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ¨¿©Ë␀␀ÂYHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀òUnderstanding the nature of the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both instruments handle bright sources without suffering from pileup effects. NICER is carrying out a monitoring program on Cyg X-1, the black-hole source for which disk and reflection components are most strongly intermingled, and with a disk that is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. We ask for 6x20 ks NuSTAR observations of Cyg X-1 to be carried out at any time. NICER will work with NuSTAR to coordinate these observations and the NICER allotment is already approved.                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       A NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT COMPTONIZATION, REFLECTION, AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(6/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429GC_Positive_Lat                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï|¥?r‘£@Ï|µèFj40410001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÿÇˇ>␀␀Â_Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀•A compelling dark-matter candidate is the keV-mass sterile neutrino, which can be tested by X-ray line signals from their radiative decays. NuSTAR observations have been used to place strong constraints on their properties. For example, the proposing team exploited archival NuSTAR observations of the Galactic Center, using 0-bounce photons to search for diffuse emission from dark matter. Here we propose dedicated NuSTAR observations (100 ks) just off the Galactic plane. Relative to our previous work, there is only a slight reduction in the dark-matter signal, while there is a large reduction in the background and attendant systematic uncertainties. We project that these observations, analyzed by our proven team, will lead to unprecedented ensitivity to sterile neutrino dark matter.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Perez                         Kerstin                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       OPTIMIZING SENSITIVITY TO STERILE NEUTRINO DARK MATTER IN THE GALACTIC CENTER                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GC_Positive_Lat                                   @o®_o“ ¿:•®Xy=Ÿ@v1¢úwök@&(€ã¨q@u2πå~($@Ï|µèFj@Ï|€?r‘£40410001002@Í Ì(åÁ@Í©rAâ7L@ÏØRh	‘ï@Ï∞“h	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÿL;*␀␀Â_Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀•A compelling dark-matter candidate is the keV-mass sterile neutrino, which can be tested by X-ray line signals from their radiative decays. NuSTAR observations have been used to place strong constraints on their properties. For example, the proposing team exploited archival NuSTAR observations of the Galactic Center, using 0-bounce photons to search for diffuse emission from dark matter. Here we propose dedicated NuSTAR observations (100 ks) just off the Galactic plane. Relative to our previous work, there is only a slight reduction in the dark-matter signal, while there is a large reduction in the background and attendant systematic uncertainties. We project that these observations, analyzed by our proven team, will lead to unprecedented ensitivity to sterile neutrino dark matter.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Perez                         Kerstin                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       OPTIMIZING SENSITIVITY TO STERILE NEUTRINO DARK MATTER IN THE GALACTIC CENTER                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_340p0                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï|€?r‘£@Ï|€î»)¯30302030001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ
≈∞[␀␀ÂeHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙The properties of super-Eddington accretion flows onto neutron stars (NS) remain poorly understood. Recent NuSTAR observations of the super-Eddington Z source GX 5-1 suggest that significant changes occur in the reflection and wind properties at the highest luminosity end of NS LMXBs. Here we propose to observe GX 340+0, a close twin to GX 5-1, with NuSTAR and NICER, to obtain broadband spectra of unprecedented quality. These spectra will allow us to study the evolution of the source's continuum components and emission/absorption lines. Our main goal is to investigate 1) to what extent GX 340+0 shows the same divergent behavior as seen in GX 5-1 and 2) how its wind and reflection properties change along its Z track. We request three 25 ks NuSTAR observations, to be spaced ~1 day apart.    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Homan                         Jeroen                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N03       WINDS AND REFLECTION FEATURES AT THE SUPPER-EDDINGTON LUMINOSITIES                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_340p0                                          @oneîØO¿F—£n.±ƒ@u7¡Ω•?∏ËßÊö‘@tªE° ¿É@Ï|€î»)¯@Ï|ÓxVb‹30302030002@—¢ßÔù≤@—w”È˘r@‹=ôµsÍ≥@‹AµsÍ≥SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ	πu0Ì␀␀ÂeHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˙The properties of super-Eddington accretion flows onto neutron stars (NS) remain poorly understood. Recent NuSTAR observations of the super-Eddington Z source GX 5-1 suggest that signi