dying active galactic nculeus (AGN) candidate Arp 187. The scientific goal is to constrain the X-ray luminosity of Arp 187 down to low-luminosity regime with < 10^42 erg/s. Our ALMA and VLA images reveal the radio-jet lobe. The optical spectrum also indicates a clear AGN signature originated from the narrow line region with 1 kpc. However, Arp 187 shows neither AGN torus nor radio-jet core emission. From these contradictory results, Arp 187 hosts either an obscured AGN or a dying AGN which dropped its luminosity rapidly over >100 times within 10^4 yrs. The broadband NuSTAR spectra above 10 keV are crucial to break the degeneracy between the obscuration and the intrinsic AGN luminosity drops.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ichikawa                      Kohei                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       HOW RAPID DOES AGN DIE? UNVEILING THE DRASTIC AGN LUMINOSITY DROP WITHIN 10^4 YEARS                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Arp_187                                           @S‚Ç@∑Ä¿$oAÚ◊s@jAÇ©ì·¿<qhr∞ ≈@ej˘	kπå@ÏÑøî»)¯@ÏÑ˚[‰õø60401010002@Û˘ºûö@Û‡b\(ı√@ıãF@N•@ıãÊ@N•SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï◊Sà'q␀␀ÂüHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≈We propose the NuSTAR 3-70 keV observation of a nearby dying active galactic nculeus (AGN) candidate Arp 187. The scientific goal is to constrain the X-ray luminosity of Arp 187 down to low-luminosity regime with < 10^42 erg/s. Our ALMA and VLA images reveal the radio-jet lobe. The optical spectrum also indicates a clear AGN signature originated from the narrow line region with 1 kpc. However, Arp 187 shows neither AGN torus nor radio-jet core emission. From these contradictory results, Arp 187 hosts either an obscured AGN or a dying AGN which dropped its luminosity rapidly over >100 times within 10^4 yrs. The broadband NuSTAR spectra above 10 keV are crucial to break the degeneracy between the obscuration and the intrinsic AGN luminosity drops.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ichikawa                      Kohei                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       HOW RAPID DOES AGN DIE? UNVEILING THE DRASTIC AGN LUMINOSITY DROP WITHIN 10^4 YEARS                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429TXS_0506p056                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÑ˚[‰õø@ÏÑ˚±9Ò90402637001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï◊§ô9␀␀‰/Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                          