rious Dust Obscured Galaxy (DOG). DOGs have been considered as a subset of high-redshift (z~2) ULIRGs and a crucial population for understanding the co-evolution of galaxies and SMBHs. However, the properties of BHs in DOGs have been poorly understood observationally due to the absence of high-sensitivity hard X-ray telescopes. Recently, we discovered a peculiar DOG (ULIRG) whose spectroscopic redshift (z=0.89) is much lower than the typical redshift of DOGs (z~2) and has an extremely red color. Its extreme MIR color and its SED imply the presence of heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. The observation proposed here will allow to shed light on the BH properties for ULIRG with extremely red color.                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Toba                          Yoshiki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       REVEALING THE BLACK HOLE IN THE DUST OBSCURED GALAXY WITH EXTREME RED COLOR                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ0825p3002                                    @_íæﬂ§?Ê@>‡ﬁ“â@h+I∫@@sÅ◊€ı@e(˝!ˇ.I@ÏÖ3Õ´∏1@ÏÖ8î»)¯60401012002@∫eMw1è≈@∫HúwökQ@ºn$g8~@ºp§g8~SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷‚óõ‡$␀␀ÊVHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀–We propose the hard X-ray observation with NuSTAR for a curious Dust Obscured Galaxy (DOG). DOGs have been considered as a subset of high-redshift (z~2) ULIRGs and a crucial population for understanding the co-evolution of galaxies and SMBHs. However, the properties of BHs in DOGs have been poorly understood observationally due to the absence of high-sensitivity hard X-ray telescopes. Recently, we discovered a peculiar DOG (ULIRG) whose spectroscopic redshift (z=0.89) is much lower than the typical redshift of DOGs (z~2) and has an extremely red color. Its extreme MIR color and its SED imply the presence of heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. The observation proposed here will allow to shed light on the BH properties for ULIRG with extremely red color.                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Toba                          Yoshiki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       REVEALING THE BLACK HOLE IN THE DUST OBSCURED GALAXY WITH EXTREME RED COLOR                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Truncated due to ToO                                                                                                    archived  20200429SGR_1806m20                                       @q
=p£◊¿4zd¬¯7µ@#óX‚e,øõW>´6z@t¡ÿ≠´üV@ÏÖ8î»)¯@ÏÖ9Õ´∏180402320001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷‚rqÓ␀␀ÂeHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀3We propose NuSTAR ToO observations (total 80 ks) of magnetar outbursts coordinated with the new soft X-ray timing instrument NICER. The evolution of the pulse profile and spectrum over the decay phase of a magnetar outburst provide crucial insight into the magnetic energy dissipation process in magnetars. Simultaneous coverage of the NICER (0.2-12 keV) and NuSTAR (3-79 keV) bands is essential for understanding the soft-thermal surface radiation and non-thermal magnetospheric emission. With the unprecedented sensitivity in the soft X-ray band of NICER, a pre-approved ToO can constrain the thermal evolution in the soft X-ray band. The addition of NuSTAR will allow simultaneous tracking of the hard X-ray flux -- a crucial element to understand the physics of magnetars.                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Enoto                         Teruaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       MAGNETIC ENERGY DISSIPATION IN MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS OBSERVED BY NUSTAR AND NICER                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_1806m20                                       @q/ÏV’–¿4rM“Ò©¸@#ß†˘	k∫øìZÖáì›ò@tèÔ4÷°b@ÏÖ9Õ´∏1@ÏÖZÍM80402320002@„}£ [¿@„_Ü^5?}@Â(öu@Â*/öuSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷‚
%*€␀␀ÂeHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀3We propose NuSTAR ToO observations (total 80 ks) of magnetar outbursts coordinated with the new soft X-ray timing instrument NICER. The evolution of the pulse profile and spectrum over the decay phase of a magnetar outburst provide crucial insight into the magnetic energy dissipation process in magnetars. Simultaneous coverage of the NICER (0.2-12 keV) and NuSTAR (3-79 keV) bands is essential for understanding the soft-thermal surface radiation and non-thermal magnetospheric emission. With the unprecedented sensitivity in the soft X-ray band of NICER, a pre-approved ToO can constrain the thermal evolution in the soft X-ray band. The addition of NuSTAR will allow simultaneous tracking of the hard X-ray flux -- a crucial element to understand the physics of magnetars.                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Enoto                         Teruaki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       MAGNETIC ENERGY DISSIPATION IN MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS OBSERVED BY NUSTAR AND NICER                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429PKS0442m28                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÖZÍM@ÏÖ[ÍM60160205001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷·€óS␀␀‰?Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS0442m28                                        @QÃÂ`Aâ7¿<%é!ñRΩ@lè!ˇ.HÈ¿C~ç∏∫«@gÖ.HËß@ÏÖ[ÍM@ÏÖl[‰õø60160205002@÷lPH@’Ë8}ø@◊”·#¢úx@◊”°#¢úxSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷·ê¯ÀH␀␀‰?Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429S82X_010                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÖl[‰õø@ÏÖm#Ü60366010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷·h3îJ␀␀‰8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316S82X_010                                          @0ú≤ïÈ·±øÿy=Ÿb∑@`W€Ùá¸π¿Oëo␀h€å@qø0U2a|@ÏÖm#Ü@ÏÖóxVb‹60366010002@Ïj*xl"h@ÏH.÷°aÂ@ÓöfE° ¡@ÓöÊE° ¡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷‡ı␀a␀␀‰8Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ELS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429ESO_157mG023                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÖóxVb‹@ÏÖò?r‘£60469005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷‡∆ZCÇ␀␀ÂüHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀