␀␀Â⁄Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀—Ark 564 is a luminous accreting supermassive black hole that has revealed several extraordinary qualities in soft X-rays: it is the brightest NLS1 from 0.3-10 keV, is one of the most highly variable AGN, has a very steep spectrum and strong soft excess. In NuSTAR AO1, we were awarded 200 ks to study this source above 20 keV for the first time, and discovered that Ark 564 is as exceptional in hard X-rays as it is in soft. Ark 564 has a coronal temperature of 15 2 keV, one of the lowest ever observed, and also shows an iron K line complex that is so highly ionised that it resembles more closely a black hole binary than an AGN. We now propose for an additional 500 ks of NuSTAR observations, simultaneous with 200 ks of XMM-Newton time and 100 ks of NICER time (through NICER Baseline Mission   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE COOLEST CORONA IN THE EDDINGTON-LIMITED AGN ARK 564                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Ark_564                                           @uJr∞ ƒú@=≤–Â`Aâ@V¯≤ïÈ·±¿9Vá+J@sπ
