ntified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ06411806p3249313                            @YJåLò@@iÚ◊s@f¡Ö∏QÏ@(;¿6‚Î@Oƒõ•„T@Ïé|Õ´∏1@Ïéç?r‘£60061071002@—ÊoiD@—”Œ_o“@”R¢v»¥9@”X"v»¥9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷∏tß␀␀‰~Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀Chandra CAT                                                                                                             archived  20200429X_Persei                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïéç?r‘£@ÏéçÍM30401033001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷j^i␀␀ÂÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∆We propose a 60 ks NuSTAR observation of the low luminosity accretion-powered pulsar X Persei. Several previous studies have shown that this source has both a softer power law spectral component (3-20 keV) and also a hard X-ray spectral component ($>$ 30 keV). Work by Coburn et al. who studied RXTE data, and Maitra et al., who studied Suzaku data, concluded there is a possible cyclotron resonant scattering feature in the range 29-39 keV. However, Doroshenko et al. have challenged this conclusion in their study of INTEGRAL data. Our NuSTAR observation will settle key questions for this source: 1) does the X-ray spectrum really have a soft and hard component, and, 2) is there a cyclotron resonant scattering feature anywhere in the NuSTAR energy band.                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Wolff                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE BE BINARY SYSTEM X PERSEI                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316X_Persei                                          @Mh_ˆîF@?GE8Ô5@d]•ú‡v¿1dÅo␀h‹@t˛ÿD–©@ÏéçÍM@Ïé∫ÍM30401033002@Ï|8?Â…@ÏjùÍ≥g°@Ô∂¶{≤˛≈@Ôπ∆{≤˛≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷iz˘çw␀␀ÂÎHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀∆We propose a 60 ks NuSTAR observation of the low luminosity accretion-powered pulsar X Persei. Several previous studies have shown that this source has both a softer power law spectral component (3-20 keV) and also a hard X-ray spectral component ($>$ 30 keV). Work by Coburn et al. who studied RXTE data, and Maitra et al., who studied Suzaku data, concluded there is a possible cyclotron resonant scattering feature in the range 29-39 keV. However, Doroshenko et al. have challenged this conclusion in their study of INTEGRAL data. Our NuSTAR observation will settle key questions for this source: 1) does the X-ray spectrum really have a soft and hard component, and, 2) is there a cyclotron resonant scattering feature anywhere in the NuSTAR energy band.                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Wolff                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE BE BINARY SYSTEM X PERSEI                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429XTE_J1810m197                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïé∫ÍM@Ïéº#Ü80202013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷i>c]≠␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2^The magnetar candidates (comprising AXPs and SGRS) are a small class of neutron stars mainly powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity, via short X-ray bursts, large giant flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Observations performed during, or immediately after such periods yield the largest amount of information on magnetars' emission, allowing one to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a Joint XMM (85ks) and NuSTAR (170ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insight on magnetars crust, field configuration, magnetospheres, and evolution.                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           Nanda                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Prompt XMM-Newton and NuSTAR study of magnetar outbursts                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316XTE_J1810m197                                     @\|ÁØ∑È¿4∑y¶µ@mÖ‰%ÆÊ2øÀjÁ’fœB@H§≤ïÈ·±@Ïéº#Ü@Ïé⁄èFj80202013002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷h·/hL␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀2^The magnetar candidates (comprising AXPs and SGRS) are a small class of neutron stars mainly powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity, via short X-ray bursts, large giant flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Observations performed during, or immediately after such periods yield the largest amount of information on magnetars' emission, allowing one to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a Joint XMM (85ks) and NuSTAR (170ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insight on magnetars crust, field configuration, magnetospheres, and evolution.                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           Nanda                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X15       Prompt XMM-Newton and NuSTAR study of magnetar outbursts                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(1/4) ToO / Sun proximity / no CHU4 astrometry                                                                          archived  201503162MASXJ06262702p0727287                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïé⁄èFj@Ïé€?r‘£60161267001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷h´µ™J␀␀‰~Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ06262702p0727287                            @X)*0U2a@æ˘€"–Â@ik&Èx‘˛¿É{J#9¡@p‚◊€Ùá˝@Ïé€?r‘£@ÏéÎ±9Ò60161267002@—Ì!ôôôö@—∆®ê-‡@”`˘õ=»@”_˘õ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷h_√Qä␀␀‰~Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀Chandra CAT                                                                                                             archived  202004293C_111                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏéÎ±9Ò@ÏéÏxVb‹60202061005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷h(ı¬è␀␀ÂËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    X∑The discovery of X-ray emission from AGN jets is a touchstone of the Chandra mission. Their X-ray emission processes have become the source of much debate, with implications for both jet physics and cluster feedback models. 3C111 has an extraordinary 2-arcminute long jet that is seen in X-ray, near-IR and radio. At least 8 knots, plus both approaching and receding hotspots and lobes, are seen in the Chandra image. We request additional Chandra, HST and NuSTAR observations, to follow up on possible evidence of variability in two knots, pin down the knots' X-ray spectrum and SED, detect the jet above 10 keV, and study the morphology and X-ray spectrum of the extended lobes. These observations will place the tightest constraints yet on the physics of this fascinating system.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Perlman                       E S                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C18       The 3C111 Jet: X-ray Variability, Spectrum & Broadband SED                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C_111                                            @P#æv»¥9@Bˇª/ÏV÷@d1S&¬¿"!Ω•ú‡@u¨◊
