o structures than the X-ray unobscured sources with the same 5 GHz radio power. This has far reaching implications for the initial evolution of AGN radio jets and AGN/galaxy co-evolution. Confirming and exploring this result requires hard X-ray observations (>10 keV). Among the current X-ray telescopes operating at energies >10 keV, only NuSTAR has capabilities required to detect and study our targets.                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Sobolewska                    Malgorzata                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       COMPTON THICK ENVIRONMENT OF YOUNG RADIO SOURCES CONSTRAINED WITH NUSTAR                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_1511p0518                                     @l~ÿD–©@FsÅ◊€ı@$tSéÛ@I+C,•z@f¶Xy=Ÿ@Ïèrî»)¯@Ïè§Õ´∏160401024002@Òﬂ:˚~ë@ˇîé@ÚLÃ?|Ìë@ÚN•„S¯SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷d–g(ï␀␀Â¯Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀}We propose to observe with NuSTAR two young radio sources: PKS 2021+614 and PKS 1511+0518 (300-400 years old, linear radio size 7-16 pc). Our ongoing studies of young radio sources indicate that the X-ray obscured sources (including the two proposed targets) have smaller radio structures than the X-ray unobscured sources with the same 5 GHz radio power. This has far reaching implications for the initial evolution of AGN radio jets and AGN/galaxy co-evolution. Confirming and exploring this result requires hard X-ray observations (>10 keV). Among t