=␀␀Ê	Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀=We propose to perform a unique NuSTAR/XMM monitoring with 5 repeated observations of 50 ks spaced by a 2-3 days of the extreme Radio-Quiet Narrow line Seyfert 1 galaxy Mrk 359. This will permit 1) to correctly disentangle the different spectral components present in this energy band, and 2) to reveal their complex interdependences and variability behavior. This study will allow us i) to constrain the physical parameters of the Comptonizing coronae responsible for the bulk of the X-ray emission ; ii) to investigate the nature of the soft X-ray excess and differentiate between multiple origins; iii) to put firm conclusions on the distance, nature and geometry of the reflecting material(s). Such monitorings appear as the best strategy to reach these goals.                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Petrucci                      Pierre-Olivier                NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       NUSTAR/XMM MONITORING OF THE EXTREME NARROW LINE SEYFERT MRK 359                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(5/5)                                                                                                                   archived  202004291RXSJ174645d6p131844                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïí°ÍM@Ïí¢ÍM60464144001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷I2π÷H␀␀‰ùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161RXSJ174645d6p131844                              @p´Åo␀h‹@*¶¬&ÄùI@BÏ%ÆÊ1˘@4n_o“ @gm≈÷8Ü@Ïí¢ÍM@Ïí≥[‰õø60464144002@”IiJÙÿ@”)÷°aÂ@‘≈	≠B√ @‘∆I≠B√ SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷HÍ␀¬.␀␀‰ùHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀Chandra CAT                                                                                                             archived  20200429Swift_J1728d9m3613                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïí≥[‰õø@Ïí¥èFj90501303001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷Hø=—ª␀␀‰ùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              