␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷:Œ ÜB␀␀‰≤Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASSJ17485512m3254521                            @p¥Lò_˜¿@py=Ÿc@vNqué¿í£S&@b¿}øHÄ@ÏîtÍM@ÏîÑèFj60160677002@’JY•ú‡@’,˛.±ƒ3@÷¡˙¨ŸË@÷¡à˙¨ŸËSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷:VÑ␀␀‰≤Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429LMC_X3                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏîÑèFj@ÏîÑÕ´∏130402035001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷:W¥%Ì␀␀Ê¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀”LMC X-3 is unique among stellar black holes in that it is a transient system (varying by orders of magnitude in intensity), and yet it is also persistently bright and nearly always in a very soft, thermal state. By virtue of its extreme thermal dominance, it is the ideal system with which to examine the structure of the thermal disk. NICER and NuSTAR offer a uniquely powerful capability in this respect to empirically establish the structure of slim disks.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LMC X-3 AS A WINDOW INTO ACCRETION-DISK STRUCTURE WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316LMC_X3                                            @U/S&¬¿P’fœAÚ@q$xG¿@ïÅ$›@sFC,•zx@ÏîÑÕ´∏1@ÏîìxVb‹30402035002@’HJìu@’5ìlãCñ@◊∂zêó@◊∂zêóSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷:‚Ã␀␀Ê¢Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀”LMC X-3 is unique among stellar black holes in that it is a transient system (varying by orders of magnitude in intensity), and yet it is also persistently bright and nearly always in a very soft, thermal state. By virtue of its extreme thermal dominance, it is the ideal system with which to examine the structure of the thermal disk. NICER and NuSTAR offer a uniquely powerful capability in this respect to empirically establish the structure of slim disks.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LMC X-3 AS A WINDOW INTO ACCRETION-DISK STRUCTURE WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429IRAS20247m7542                                    @s9sÍ≥g°¿R·:í£S@sÁÙÿD–¿@¬¯7¥¢@VïOﬂ;dZ@ÏîìxVb‹@ÏîìÍM60160739001@süHé@sê-‡@t␀␀␀␀␀@t␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷9‡ù≈ò␀␀‰≤Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429IRAS20247m7542                                    @s9dZ¨¿R·ú‡u˜@sËI∫^¿@ºj~˘€@V£Â…‰@ÏîìÍM@Ïî£xVb‹60160739002@·««y¶µ@·îÚ‰è@‚OÄ␀␀␀␀@‚Q␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷9îJU∂␀␀‰≤Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429GX_9p1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïî£xVb‹@Ïî§#Ü30401016001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷9io{␀␀Ê"Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀?The strong connection between the X-ray and radio properties of low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXBs) reflects a direct link between the accretion inflow and outflow. Most of our current knowledge on this is based on LMXBs in hard X-ray states. However, neutron star LMXBs in soft X-ray states reveal intriguing differences in their radio properties that can provide new information about the destruction of jets. We propose a single NuSTAR observation of each of the two neutron star LMXBs GX 9+9 (40 ks) and GX 9+1 (20 ks), which persistently accrete in a soft state. Through broad-band and reflection modeling we aim to explain their very weak radio emission compared to other neutron stars that accrete at similar X-ray luminosities.                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LINKING JET SUPPRESSION TO ACCRETION GEOMETRY                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_9p1                                            @pÊ≤ïÈ·±¿4|ÁØ∑È@!˛öu?ˆº£n/@cum\˙¨⁄@Ïî§#Ü@Ïî≤±9Ò30401016002@ƒïïú‡v@≈|ØAÚ◊@’Å° ¿É@’A° ¿ÉSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷8ozÄ␀␀Ê"Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀?The strong connection between the X-ray and radio properties of low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXBs) reflects a direct link between the accretion inflow and outflow. Most of our current knowledge on this is based on LMXBs in hard X-ray states. However, neutron star LMXBs in soft X-ray states reveal intriguing differences in their radio properties that can provide new information about the destruction of jets. We propose a single NuSTAR observation of each of the two neutron star LMXBs GX 9+9 (40 ks) and GX 9+1 (20 ks), which persistently accrete in a soft state. Through broad-band and reflection modeling we aim to explain their very weak radio emission compared to other neutron stars that accrete at similar X-ray luminosities.                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LINKING JET SUPPRESSION TO ACCRETION GEOMETRY                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_6397                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïî≤±9Ò@Ïî≥#Ü30401022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷8H?∑/␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Globular clusters (GCs) are efficient sites for the formation of compact binary systems through close stellar encounters. Cataclysmic variables (CVs) in globular clusters exhibit vastly different properties to their Galactic field counterparts, leading to the suggestion that the majority of CVs in GCs are formed as products of these encounters. The unusual properties of GC CVs are thought to be evidence of belonging to the magnetic class of CVs. The X-ray spectrum of magnetic CVs is dominated by a high temperature (>10keV) thermal plasma component, requiring telescopes with hard X-ray capabilities to measure. We propose a 100ks observation of the CVs in NGC 6397 with NuSTAR, allowing us to constrain the nature of a GC CV population for the first time.                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Shaw                          Aarran                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE NATURE OF THE CATACLYSMIC VARIABLE POPULATION IN THE GLOBULAR CLUSTER NGC 6397                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_6397                                          @pìo“ÚÂ¿J—≤ïÈ‚@u!™d¬¯8¿'Éä	ﬁ@a‡ê-‡@Ïî≥#Ü@Ïî˘Õ´∏130401022002@˙"◊∞ ƒú@˘ı¶º@˚Ò÷‰%ÆÊ@˚Ú<‰%ÆÊSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷7≠Çÿ.␀␀ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Globular clusters (GCs) are efficient sites for the formation of compact binary systems through close stellar encounters. Cataclysmic variables (CVs) in globular clusters exhibit vastly different properties to their Galactic field counterparts, leading to the suggestion that the majority of CVs in GCs are formed as products of these encounters. The unusual properties of GC CVs are thought to be evidence of belonging to the magnetic class of CVs. The X-ray spectrum of magnetic CVs is dominated by a high temperature (>10keV) thermal plasma component, requiring telescopes with hard X-ray capabilities to measure. We propose a 100ks observation of the CVs in NGC 6397 with NuSTAR, allowing us to constrain the nature of a GC CV population for the first time.                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Shaw                          Aarran                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE NATURE OF THE CATACLYSMIC VARIABLE POPULATION IN THE GLOBULAR CLUSTER NGC 6397                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ02560264m1629159                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïî˘Õ´∏1@Ïî˙±9Ò60362027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷7sƒ’Á␀␀‰≤Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ02560264m1629159                            @E˛_o“ ¿0Öõ=»K@h‰⁄πıY¥¿M∆sÅ◊€ı@tö–Â`Aâ@Ïî˙±9Ò@Ïï[‰õø60362027002@„iGˇ.HÈ@„M9Ì˙C˛@‰◊1A [¿@‰Ÿ—A [¿SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷7Œ≤@␀␀‰≤Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀NuLANDS                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC_1106                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïï[‰õø@ÏïèFj60469002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷6Ëî´m␀␀Ê$Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1106                                          @ESCïÅ%@D—0æÌ)@b/üæv…¿0öu%F@srQÇ©ì@ÏïèFj@Ïï*[‰õø60469002002@“LÑ6‚Î@“3w1è≈@”®±ûÉ‰&@”´±ûÉ‰&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷6¢¸ñ0␀␀Ê$Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SDSSJ1652                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïï*[‰õø@Ïï+[‰õø60401028001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷6yD•[␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ We discovered a population of powerful L>1e47 erg/s red quasars at z=2-3 which show unprecedented signatures of fast v>3000 km/s outflows in their [OIII]5007A lines. These objects may be signposts of the 'blow-out' phase of quasar feedback onto quasar host galaxy, at the epoch when quasars may have made the strongest impact on galaxy formation. Here we propose NuStar+XMM observations of one of these objects, a target from our approved JWST Early Release Science program. These data will allow us to determine the physical properties of the corona and the intrinsic accretion luminosity; constrain wind driving mechanisms; and provide a 'gold standard' spectrum for studies of the obscured high-redshift quasar population, which remains the single most elusive key target of X-ray astronomy.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zakamska                      Nadia                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF AN OBSCURED QUASAR WITH OUTFLOWS AT PEAK GALAXY FORMATION EPOCH                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ1652                                         @o°AÚ◊s@1ÅT…Öo@B7€Ùá¸π@Aﬁi≠Bƒ@dêd¬¯7µ@Ïï+[‰õø@Ïï«±9Ò60401028002A
∞Q~˘€#A
Ñùh€ã¨Aï˚Ω•ùAó+Ω•ùSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷5f«}â␀␀ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ We discovered a population of powerful L>1e47 erg/s red quasars at z=2-3 which show unprecedented signatures of fast v>3000 km/s outflows in their [OIII]5007A lines. These objects may be signposts of the 'blow-out' phase of quasar feedback onto quasar host galaxy, at the epoch when quasars may have made the strongest impact on galaxy formation. Here we propose NuStar+XMM observations of one of these objects, a target from our approved JWST Early Release Science program. These data will allow us to determine the physical properties of the corona and the intrinsic accretion luminosity; constrain wind driving mechanisms; and provide a 'gold standard' spectrum for studies of the obscured high-redshift quasar population, which remains the single most elusive key target of X-ray astronomy.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zakamska                      Nadia                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF AN OBSCURED QUASAR WITH OUTFLOWS AT PEAK GALAXY FORMATION EPOCH                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429Her_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïï«±9Ò@Ïï»èFj30402034005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷5ß@⁄␀␀Ê7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÀWe propose a series of four 20 ks observations with NuSTAR and XMM of the Galactic binary X-ray pulsar Hercules X-1, which has a 35 day superorbital period caused by a warped, precessing accretion disk. The hard beam from the neutron star heats this disk and the corresponding reprocessed, thermal emission provides a unique opportunity for observation of magnetic-dominated accretion. Our proposed analysis requires good spectral and timing resolution across the full energy band (<1 keV to >20 keV) in order to constrain both the soft emission from the disk and the pulsar beam. For the first time, we will track a complete precession of Her X-1's accretion disk with full hard X-ray coverage to gain the clearest picture to date of the structure and kinematics of the magnetized accretion flow.   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       A DEFINITIVE INVESTIGATION OF THE MAGNETIZED ACCRETION FLOW IN THE X-RAY PULSAR HER X-1 WITH NUSTAR AND XMM             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Her_X1                                            @oœ/üæw@AØiDg8@MÿD–©@B‘£n.≤@deﬁ␀—∑@Ïï»èFj@ÏïÿÍM30402034006@÷{ÛHÀí@÷a≈^û	@ÿäc9¿ÎÓ@ÿã„9¿ÎÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷4≈»†Œ␀␀Ê7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÀWe propose a series of four 20 ks observations with NuSTAR and XMM of the Galactic binary X-ray pulsar Hercules X-1, which has a 35 day superorbital period caused by a warped, precessing accretion disk. The hard beam from the neutron star heats this disk and the corresponding reprocessed, thermal emission provides a unique opportunity for observation of magnetic-dominated accretion. Our proposed analysis requires good spectral and timing resolution across the full energy band (<1 keV to >20 keV) in order to constrain both the soft emission from the disk and the pulsar beam. For the first time, we will track a complete precession of Her X-1's accretion disk with full hard X-ray coverage to gain the clearest picture to date of the structure and kinematics of the magnetized accretion flow.   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       A DEFINITIVE INVESTIGATION OF THE MAGNETIZED ACCRETION FLOW IN THE X-RAY PULSAR HER X-1 WITH NUSTAR AND XMM             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM HXMT                                                                                            ␀␀␀(3/4) / Kp=5                                                                                                            archived  202004292XMMJ185348d4m010229                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏïÿÍM@ÏïŸxVb‹60463067001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷4û&Ø8␀␀‰πHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                              