¶L0@”Æ◊⁄QŒ@’FÊh	‘ï@’G&h	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷)eC ˇ␀␀‰ Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004294U_1957p11                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïólî»)¯@Ïóm[‰õø30402011003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷)<“ﬁ␀␀ÊtHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÕWe propose three, 35 ksec observations of 4U 1957+11, potentially the most rapidly spinning Galactic black hole. With the existing 50 ksec observation, we plan to perform a simultaneous fit to the spectra using modern disk atmosphere models, where we attempt to describe the spectra with a common mass, distance, spin, and system inclination. These results will be compared to and used to 'normalize' a similar fit to existing RXTE spectra. The posterior probably estimates from these fits will be combined with theoretical models of disk color correction factors to make a prediction of the likely range of the black hole system parameters, and help verify that it is indeed a maximally spinning black hole.                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING NUSTAR TO ASSESS THE MASS, SPIN, DISTANCE, AND FE LINE OF 4U 1957+11                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1957p11                                        @ræØ∑È@'t¢3úø@Iñç∏ª¿"[ò«‚ÇA@d«hr∞ ≈@Ïóm[‰õø@Ïóà[‰õø30402011004@‡^gP∞Ú|@‡ZS®Xy>@‚v5†'RT@‚xu†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷(≈∏R␀␀ÊtHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÕWe propose three, 35 ksec observations of 4U 1957+11, potentially the most rapidly spinning Galactic black hole. With the existing 50 ksec observation, we plan to perform a simultaneous fit to the spectra using modern disk atmosphere models, where we attempt to describe the spectra with a common mass, distance, spin, and system inclination. These results will be compared to and used to 'normalize' a similar fit to existing RXTE spectra. The posterior probably estimates from these fits will be combined with theoretical models of disk color correction factors to make a prediction of the likely range of the black hole system parameters, and help verify that it is indeed a maximally spinning black hole.                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING NUSTAR TO ASSESS THE MASS, SPIN, DISTANCE, AND FE LINE OF 4U 1957+11                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Her_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïóà[‰õø@ÏóàÍM30402034007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷(òvT2␀␀Ê7Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÀWe propose a series of four 20 ks observations with NuSTAR and XMM of the Galactic binary X-ray pulsar Hercules X-1, which has a 35 day superorbital period caused by a warped, precessing accretion disk. The hard beam from the neutron star heats this disk and the corresponding reprocessed, thermal emission provides a unique opportunity for observation of magnetic-dominated accretion. Our proposed analysis requires good spectral and timing resolution across the full energy band (<1 keV to >20 keV) in order to constrain both the soft emission from the disk and the pulsar beam. For the first time, we will track a complete precession of Her X-1's accretion disk with full hard X-ray coverage to gain the clearest picture to date of the structure and kinematics of the magnetized accretion flow.   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       A DEFINITIVE INVESTIGATION OF THE MAGNETIZED ACCRETION FLOW IN THE X-RAY PULSAR HER X-1 WITH NUSTAR AND XMM             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Her_X1                                            @oœrGE8Ô@A∞€ã¨q@M–Â`Aâ@B”a@N•@bÊ1&Èx’@ÏóàÍM@ÏóòèFj30402034008@–KÃ:˚~ë@–O•*ô1@“ﬁñp:˚@“‚p:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷(DΩ°/␀␀Ê7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÀWe propose a series of four 20 ks observations with NuSTAR and XMM of the Galactic binary X-ray pulsar Hercules X-1, which has a 35 day superorbital period caused by a warped, precessing accretion disk. The hard beam from the neutron star heats this disk and the corresponding reprocessed, thermal emission provides a unique opportunity for observation of magnetic-dominated accretion. Our proposed analysis requires good spectral and timing resolution across the full energy band (<1 keV to >20 keV) in order to constrain both the soft emission from the disk and the pulsar beam. For the first time, we will track a complete precession of Her X-1's accretion disk with full hard X-ray coverage to gain the clearest picture to date of the structure and kinematics of the magnetized accretion flow.   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL