gions. We will also combine NuSTAR measurements with those from novel solar-observing telescopes operating the extreme ultraviolet X-ray range to obtain the most sensitive measurements to date of flare heating of the corona. With these coordinated observations we will search for the weak high-temperature (>5MK) and non- thermal emission expected from impulsive energy release models.                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Glesener                      Lindsay                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       INVESTIGATION OF IMPULSIVE HEATING OF ACTIVE REGIONS IN THE SOLAR CORONA                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA rally INERTIAL only                                                                                                archived  201503166dFJ1949458m794523                                @ròﬂ;dZ¿SÓ+jÁ’g@s•F‹]d¿=!4ÍJå@bX~($x@ÏõP#Ü@ÏõQ#Ü60464168001@ÇÒOAÚ◊@Ç—‚∂Æ}V@Ñ4ÏÙ!-@Ñ4ÏÙ!-SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷^á∆Z␀␀‰·Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                