 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO374mG025                                       @b⁄¯7¥¢4¿B∏}øHÄ@p◊ç∏∫«@,§Z¨1@s‰„º”ZÜ@Ïõïî»)¯@Ïõ•ÍM60160384002@’=ÑùIQÉ@’!Hõ•„T@÷◊C/ÏV÷@÷ÿ√/ÏV÷SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€Î¸M^p␀␀‰ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SDSSJ0825p3002                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïõ•ÍM@Ïõ¶±9Ò60401012003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷j1M¿␀␀ÊVHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀–We propose the hard X-ray observation with NuSTAR for a curious Dust Obscured Galaxy (DOG). DOGs have been considered as a subset of high-redshift (z~2) ULIRGs and a crucial population for understanding the co-evolution of galaxies and SMBHs. However, the properties of BHs in DOGs have been poorly understood observationally due to the absence of high-sensitivity hard X-ray telescopes. Recently, we discovered a peculiar DOG (ULIRG) whose spectroscopic redshift (z=0.89) is much lower than the typical redshift of DOGs (z~2) and has an extremely red color. Its extreme MIR color and its SED imply the presence of heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. The observation proposed here will allow to shed light on the BH properties for ULIRG with extremely red color.                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Toba                          Yoshiki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       REVEALING THE BLACK HOLE IN THE DUST OBSCURED GALAXY WITH EXTREME RED COLOR                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ0825p3002                                    @_é±ƒ2 X@>ç∏∫«@h*ô0æ@@á¸π#£@u”3úæ‡@Ïõ¶±9Ò@ÏõÂÕ´∏160401012004@Ù∞©=»K@Ùãèxl"h@ˆ(£@N•@ˆ)@N•SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€ÏR}'“␀␀ÊVHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀–We propose the hard X-ray observation with NuSTAR for a curious Dust Obscured Galaxy (DOG). DOGs have been considered as a subset of high-redshift (z~2) ULIRGs and a crucial population for understanding the co-evolution of galaxies and SMBHs. However, the properties of BHs in DOGs have been poorly understood observationally due to the absence of high-sensitivity hard X-ray telescopes. Recently, we discovered a peculiar DOG (ULIRG) whose spectroscopic redshift (z=0.89) is much lower than the typical redshift of DOGs (z~2) and has an extremely red color. Its extreme MIR color and its SED imply the presence of heavily obscured, Compton-thick AGN. The observation proposed here will allow to shed light on the BH properties for ULIRG with extremely red color.                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Toba                          Yoshiki                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       REVEALING THE BLACK HOLE IN THE DUST OBSCURED GALAXY WITH EXTREME RED COLOR                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀idiagnostics_on 2019:107:1843Z                                                                                          archived  20200429LMC_X3                                            @U*§®¡T ¿P5?|Ìë@q≤˛≈m]¿@ökP∞Ú@úC,•zx@ÏõÂÕ´∏1@ÏõÊ±9Ò30402035003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷§8!`␀␀Ê¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀”LMC X-3 is unique among stellar black holes in that it is a transient system (varying by orders of magnitude in intensity), and yet it is also persistently bright and nearly always in a very soft, thermal state. By virtue of its extreme thermal dominance, it is the ideal system with which to examine the structure of the thermal disk. NICER and NuSTAR offer a uniquely powerful capability in this respect to empirically establish the structure of slim disks.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LMC X-3 AS A WINDOW INTO ACCRETION-DISK STRUCTURE WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  2