=p§@‘§™Ç@∑Ä@÷zÎÌ˙D@÷z‘ÎÌ˙DSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷ΩàÈ†␀␀‰ÛHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO_374mG044                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïú[‰õø@ÏúÕ´∏160469004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷ñÉö␀␀ÊaHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_374mG044                                      @c*9XbN¿BT`™d√@p„tºj~˙@0ßlãCïÅ@tÌ(åÁ@ÏúÕ´∏1@ÏúèFj60469004002@“ˇöÒ©˚Á@“Ï≥πå~(@‘}T›/†@‘~›/†SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷SWõ‡␀␀ÊaHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429APM_08279p5255                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏúèFj@ÏúÍM60401017001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷*¬s␀␀ÊaHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀+We propose NuSTAR observations of two bright (FX>5 10^-13 cgs, LX>10^46 erg/s) radio-quiet QSOs at z>2, plus short XMM exposures for each of them: our total time request is 320k NuSTAR and 70ks XMM. By looking at rest-frame energies up to 300 keV, our goal is to measure unambiguously the presence of spectral cut-offs at rest-frame energies up to E_c=500 keV. This is not possible for local targets, where NuSTAR is just sensitive to E_c<250 keV. The two brightest sources of a sample of four have been successfully observed in Cycle 3. These observations allowed to measure for the first time E_c in distant, luminous QSOs. The proposed observations will allow to build a a small but unique sample of high-z, high-LX QSOs with accurate E_c measurements, to test current coronal models.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanzuisi                      Giorgio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE HIGH-ENERGY CUT-OFF IN HIGH-Z/HIGH-L QSO                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316APM_08279p5255                                    @_¯F‹]d@J]ˆ˝!ˇ.@d∂≠´üUõ@AˇÉ{J#:@u—ô0æÌ@ÏúÍM@Ïú_?r‘£60401017002@ˆ—‹h€ã¨@ˆ™° ¿É@¯îÒ®Xy>@¯ï1®Xy>SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷Ä⁄t␀␀ÊaHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀+We propose NuSTAR observations of two bright (FX>5 10^-13 cgs, LX>10^46 erg/s) radio-quiet QSOs at z>2, plus short XMM exposures for each of them: our total time request is 320k NuSTAR and 70ks XMM. By looking at rest-frame energies up to 300 keV, our goal is to measure unambiguously the presence of spectral cut-offs at rest-frame energies up to E_c=500 keV. This is not possible for local targets, where NuSTAR is just sensitive to E_c<250 keV. The two brightest sources of a sample of four have been successfully observed in Cycle 3. These observations allowed to measure for the first time E_c in distant, luminous QSOs. The proposed observations will allow to build a a small but unique sample of high-z, high-LX QSOs with accurate E_c measurements, to test current coronal models.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lanzuisi                      Giorgio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE HIGH-ENERGY CUT-OFF IN HIGH-Z/HIGH-L QSO                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004294U_1909p07                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïú_?r‘£@Ïú`?r‘£30402026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷9çvµ␀␀ÊaHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÜFive wind-accreting supergiant high-mass X-ray binaries are now known to exhibit superorbital periods. Unlike systems that possess persistent accretion disks, the cause of this modulation is unknown, although several models have been proposed. We were awarded NuSTAR time on one of these systems (IGR J16493-4348) in Cycle 1 to compare superorbital maximum and minimum. Surprisingly, we see differences from an RXTE study of the superorbital modulation in 2S 0114+650. We now request NuSTAR observations at superorbital maximum and minimum of the three other known members of this class so we can determine global modulation properties and disentangle the effects of inclination angle and orbital period. Determining the superorbital mechanism may yield insights into supergiant wind properties.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Corbet                        Robin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       PROPERTIES & CAUSES OF SUPERORBITAL PERIODS IN SUPERGIANT X-RAY BINARIES                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                  