mass X-ray binaries (LMXBs) reflects a direct link between the accretion inflow and outflow. Most of our current knowledge on this is based on LMXBs in hard X-ray states. However, neutron star LMXBs in soft X-ray states reveal intriguing differences in their radio properties that can provide new information about the destruction of jets. We propose a single NuSTAR observation of each of the two neutron star LMXBs GX 9+9 (40 ks) and GX 9+1 (20 ks), which persistently accrete in a soft state. Through broad-band and reflection modeling we aim to explain their very weak radio emission compared to other neutron stars that accrete at similar X-ray luminosities.                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Degenaar                      Nathalie                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LINKING JET SUPPRESSION TO ACCRETION GEOMETRY                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_9p9                                            @po0æÌ)¿0Èx‘˝Û∂@ ›}øHÃ@"êU2a|@bŸS&¡æ@Ïùlî»)¯@Ïùå[‰õø30401015002@›Ü␀ÍJå@›ÙO]ÃcÒ@‰cÅ∂E°À@‰d°∂E°ÀSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’˘Ûıax␀␀ÊaHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀?The strong connection between the X-ray and radio properties of low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXBs) reflects a direct link between the accretion inflow and outflow. Most of our current knowledge on this is based on LMXBs in hard X-ray states. However, neutron star LMXBs in soft X-ray states reveal intriguing differences in their radio properties 