s per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316FBQSJ1159p2914                                    @f{4ÍJå@=;6zêó@h„‰%ÆÊ2@SáÈ˘rG@ ,•zxl@Ï†U?r‘£@Ï†cÍM60463037002@–ú÷©ì·@–ÜH€ã¨q@—C,•z@—ÚHC,•zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Ëœ+ùe␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MCG_03_58_007                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï†cÍM@Ï†e#Ü60502004003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ë§8!`␀␀Ê¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀H7We propose a monitoring campaign of 4x60 ks simultaneous XMM & 4x40 ks NuSTAR observations of MCG-03-58-007, which is a powerful and variable disk wind. A Suzaku observation unveiled the presence of a high ionization, high Nh and fast (v~ 0.1c) wind launched from within a few 100Rg from the black hole. A follow-up 130ks simultaneous XMM/NuSTAR observation confirmed the presence of the wind and showed a Nh~1e24cm-2 variation in a day, which we ascribe to an increase of the Nh of the wind. Such dramatic variability places MCG-03-58-007 among the unique objects, and makes it an ideal AGN to map the inner disk-wind. With the new observations we will: probe the origin of the Nh variations, reveal the structure of the wind and determine how the wind responds to the continuum.                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Monitoring the extremely variable fast disk wind in MCG-03-58-007                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_03_58_007                                     @ufì‡ﬁ”¿3=øHÀí@DÊ\ë—N<¿Nhâ†'RT@`aä⁄πıZ@Ï†e#Ü@Ï†É#Ü60502004004@‰kÊ¡Ω•@‰F±˝ä⁄∫@ÂŸÕ¶µ@Â€¶µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ËGÆ{␀␀Ê¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀H7We propose a monitoring campaign of 4x60 ks simultaneous XMM & 4x40 ks NuSTAR observations of MCG-03-58-007, which is a powerful and variable disk wind. A Suzaku observation unveiled the presence of a high ionization, high Nh and fast (v~ 0.1c) wind launched from within a few 100Rg from the black hole. A follow-up 130ks simultaneous XMM/NuSTAR observation confirmed the presence of the wind and showed a Nh~1e24cm-2 variation in a day, which we ascribe to an increase of the Nh of the wind. Such dramatic variability places MCG-03-58-007 among the unique objects, and makes it an ideal AGN to map the inner disk-wind. With the new observations we will: probe the origin of the Nh variations, reveal the structure of the wind and determine how the wind responds to the continuum.                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Monitoring the extremely variable fast disk wind in MCG-03-58-007                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429LMC_X3                                            @U*#9¿ÎÓ¿P…Öoi@qHËßÁ¿@:˚~ë␀@EYΩ•ú‡@Ï†É#Ü@Ï†ÉÍM30402035005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ë©&N␀␀Ê¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀”LMC X-3 is unique among stellar black holes in that it is a transient system (varying by orders of magnitude in intensity), and yet it is also persistently bright and nearly always in a very soft, thermal state. By virtue of its extreme thermal dominance, it is the ideal system with which to examine the structure of the thermal disk. NICER and NuSTAR offer a uniquely powerful capability in this respect to empirically establish the structure of slim disks.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LMC X-3 AS A WINDOW INTO ACCRETION-DISK STRUCTURE WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316LMC_X3                                            @U*+jÁ’g¿PVì@q\˙¨ŸË¿@*ô0æ@E}•ú‡v@Ï†ÉÍM@Ï†íÍM30402035006@÷êx%ÆÊ2@÷Ä∂Ê1¯°@Ÿæa|⁄@Ÿ>a|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Á∫gÍ√␀␀Ê¢Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀”LMC X-3 is unique among stellar black holes in that it is a transient system (varying by orders of magnitude in intensity), and yet it is also persistently bright and nearly always in a very soft, thermal state. By virtue of its extreme thermal dominance, it is the ideal system with which to examine the structure of the thermal disk. NICER and NuSTAR offer a uniquely powerful capability in this respect to empirically establish the structure of slim disks.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       LMC X-3 AS A WINDOW INTO ACCRETION-DISK STRUCTURE WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/4)                                                                                                                   archived  202004293C_397                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï†íÍM@Ï†îèFj40401003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Áâb¸ñ␀␀ÊÉHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÓWe propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of 3C 397, a Type Ia supernova remnant (SNR) likely originating from a near-Chandrasekhar-mass white dwarf in a single-degenerate progenitor system. Suzaku observations of this SNR detected strong emission of the Fe-peak elements (Cr, Mn, Fe, Ni) and indicated remarkably high mass ratios of Mn/Fe and Ni/Fe. Under the auspices of this proposal, we will reveal the spatial distribution of these elements to constrain the properties of the progenitor and the supernova explosion mechanism. NuSTAR is particularly suitable for this study, thanks to the highest sensitivity to Ni K-alpha emission at 7.6 keV among currently operating X-ray missions. XMM-Newton provides a perfect complement to NuSTAR, by enabling clear detection of Cr and Mn lines.             Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Yamaguchi                     Hiroya                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE SUPERNOVA REMNANT 3C 397: CONSTRAINING PHYSICS OF TYPE IA SUPERNOVAE IN THE SINGLE-DEGENERATE SCENARIO              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C_397                                            @qÌÈx‘˝Ù@◊s¸PH@DÖ8Ô4÷°ø±∑X‚e@]–`™d¬¯@Ï†îèFj@Ï†Ú[‰õø40401003002A␀(lÙ!A␀␀¡,ŸË>AGÛhr∞!AH{hr∞!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÊŒQ)W␀␀ÊÉHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÓWe propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of 3C 397, a Type Ia supernova remnant (SNR) likely originating from a near-Chandrasekhar-mass white dwarf in a single-degenerate progenitor system. Suzaku observations of this SNR detected strong emission of the Fe-peak elements (Cr, Mn, Fe, Ni) and indicated remarkably high mass ratios of Mn/Fe and Ni/Fe. Under the auspices of this proposal, we will reveal the spatial distribution of these elements to constrain the properties of the progenitor and the supernova explosion mechanism. NuSTAR is particularly suitable for this study, thanks to the highest sensitivity to Ni K-alpha emission at 7.6 keV among currently operating X-ray missions. XMM-Newton provides a perfect complement to NuSTAR, by enabling clear detection of Cr and Mn lines.             Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Yamaguchi                     Hiroya                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE SUPERNOVA REMNANT 3C 397: CONSTRAINING PHYSICS OF TYPE IA SUPERNOVAE IN THE SINGLE-DEGENERATE SCENARIO              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MCG_03_58_007                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï†Ú[‰õø@Ï†ÛèFj60502004005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ÊÇsK␀␀Ê¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀H7We propose a monitoring campaign of 4x60 ks simultaneous XMM & 4x40 ks NuSTAR observations of MCG-03-58-007, which is a powerful and variable disk wind. A Suzaku observation unveiled the presence of a high ionization, high Nh and fast (v~ 0.1c) wind launched from within a few 100Rg from the black hole. A follow-up 130ks simultaneous XMM/NuSTAR observation confirmed the presence of the wind and showed a Nh~1e24cm-2 variation in a day, which we ascribe to an increase of the Nh of the wind. Such dramatic variability places MCG-03-58-007 among the unique objects, and makes it an ideal AGN to map the inner disk-wind. With the new observations we will: probe the origin of the Nh variations, reveal the structure of the wind and determine how the wind responds to the continuum.                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Monitoring the extremely variable fast disk wind in MCG-03-58-007                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_03_58_007                                     @ufìtºj¿3<ŸË>B[@DÁ8}øH¿NheîØO@`|2 Wßá@Ï†ÛèFj@Ï°ÍM60502004006@„1”¡Ω•@„{8}ø@‰Ç1è≈@‰Çã1è≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Ê)VŸ≤␀␀Ê¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀H7We propose a monitoring campaign of 4x60 ks simultaneous XMM & 4x40 ks NuSTAR observations of MCG-03-58-007, which is a powerful and variable disk wind. A Suzaku observation unveiled the presence of a high ionization, high Nh and fast (v~ 0.1c) wind launched from within a few 100Rg from the black hole. A follow-up 130ks simultaneous XMM/NuSTAR observation confirmed the presence of the wind and showed a Nh~1e24cm-2 variation in a day, which we ascribe to an increase of the Nh of the wind. Such dramatic variability places MCG-03-58-007 among the unique objects, and makes it an ideal AGN t