cy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163XMM_J182855d9m095413                             @qS±ƒ2 X¿#∫C˛\ë—@5we˝ä⁄∫?Âì‡ﬁ“â@_Ô∆ßÔù≤@Ï°Wî»)¯@Ï°tÍM30360004002@‰à˘ä⁄πı@‰`’Ø∑È@ÊÿÀ)^û@ÊòÀ)^ûSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’‰
ˇü␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀GLS       Galactic Plane Legacy Surveys                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀GLS                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SN2014C                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï°tÍM@Ï°uî»)¯40502001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’„“ïmõ␀␀ÊrHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    8œcMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful Chandra-NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request Chandra-NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar deathNon-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      R                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       Continued Chandra-NuSTAR monitoring of the unprecedented metamorphosis of SN2014C at t>1500 days since explosion        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u4π#¢úx@A9ú‡uˆ˝@Wb»¥9X¿4≤3úæ‡@`ÙÒ©˚Ám@Ï°uî»)¯@Ï°ìÍM40502001002@‰B^Ì(ç@‰ aÔù≤-@Â®g8~@Â©Zg8~SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’„wèΩ@␀␀ÊrHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  8œcMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful Chandra-NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request Chandra-NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar deathNon-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      R                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       Continued Chandra-NuSTAR monitoring of the unprecedented metamorphosis of SN2014C at t>1500 days since explosion        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429V834_Cen                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï°ìÍM@Ï°ï?r‘£90501327001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’„I+<␀␀ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             