 NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              INTEGRAL                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  202004294U_1957p11                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï°⁄?r‘£@Ï°⁄î»)¯30502007003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’·–6ù␀␀ÂHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1957p11                                        @rΩp£◊
=@'yá+@IïûÉ‰%Ø¿"I«y¶µ@]Ü°a‰˜f@Ï°⁄î»)¯@Ï°È[‰õø30502007004@”ïSC,•@”ñ`ÿD–@÷U”ZÖà@÷V\”ZÖàSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’·v#¶␀␀ÂHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_1566                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï°È[‰õø@Ï°Í[‰õø80502606001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’·LSo‚␀␀Ê°Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀HpRecent XMM-Newton observations of Seyfert galaxies establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as a poorly explored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn: GSN 069 is most likely a high Eddington-ratio true Seyfert 2 galaxy with no broad line region and no X-ray continuum. NGC 1566, HE1136-2304 and NGC 2617 are rare changing look AGNs with the latter possibly showing absorption by in-falling matter. We propose two 80ks simultaneous XMMNewton/NuSTAR observations accompanied by optical spectroscopy of the next suited AGN outburst(s). They will allow tracing accretion physics, spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime.                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1566                                          @P<≤ïÈ·±¿KsÌ˙C˛]@pÖ¡Ω•ù¿EΩLò_˜@RVlÙ!@Ï°Í[‰õø@Ï¢î»)¯80502606002@Îı∑ﬁ␀“@Î…cä	@Ì˚˛©*0@Ìˇﬁ©*0SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’‡‹äÂ␀␀Ê°Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀HpRecent XMM-Newton observations of Seyfert galaxies establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as a poorly explored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn: GSN 069 is most likely a high Eddington-ratio true Seyfert 2 galaxy with no broad line region and no X-ray continuum. NGC 1566, HE1136-2304 and NGC 2617 are rare changing look AGNs with the latter possibly showing absorption by in-falling matter. We propose two 80ks simultaneous XMMNewton/NuSTAR observations accompanied by optical spectroscopy of the next suited AGN outburst(s). They will allow tracing accretion physics, spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime.                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Swift_J184418d3m023304                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¢î»)¯@Ï¢î»)¯30360003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’‡®›|«␀␀Â)Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. The goals of the Galactic Legacy Survey are to expose the hidden Milky Way and reveal the wide range of astrophysics that creates high-energy emission. The program will continue NuSTAR studies of flares from Sgr A*, HMXB in a range of environments, and diffuse emission toward the Galactic Center, including the Galactic ridge emission.                                                                                                             