tified so far. Such systems may exist preferentially in BAL quasars, where it is challenging to ascribe the observed X-ray weakness to absorption vs. intrinsic X-ray weakness. Via stacking analysis, our exploratory 21-32 ks NuSTAR observations have demonstrated the unique capability of NuSTAR to discover a significant fraction of intrinsically X-ray weak AGNs among BAL quasars. Thus we propose to obtain 90 and 100 ks additional observations for PG 1254 and PG 1001, respectively, to identify intrinsically X-ray weak AGNs individually and constrain the fraction of such objects among the luminous BAL quasar population.                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brandt                        William                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       IDENTIFYING INTRINSICALLY X-RAY WEAK AGNS WITH NUSTAR                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1254p047                                       @hG"–Â`B@æ\ë—N<@swökP±@Pÿ†ê-‡?¸'RT`™e@Ï¢C?r‘£@Ï¢Ü[‰õø60401013002@ˆá˘dZ¨@ˆc ª/ÏW@¯/Izxl"@¯0Izxl"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ﬂ-Çÿ␀␀ÊíHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀›Luminous X-ray emission is considered a universal property of AGNs. There are few intrinsically X-ray weak AGNs reliably identified so far. Such systems may exist preferentially in BAL quasars, where it is challenging to ascribe the observed X-ray weakness to absorption vs. intrinsic X-ray weakness. Via stacking analysis, our exploratory 21-32 ks NuSTAR observations have demonstrated the unique capability of NuSTAR to discover a significant fraction of intrinsically X-ray weak AGNs among BAL quasars. Thus we propose to obtain 90 and 100 ks additional observations for PG 1254 and PG 1001, respectively, to identify intrinsically X-ray weak AGNs individually and constrain the fraction of such objects among the luminous BAL quasar population.                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brandt                        William                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       IDENTIFYING INTRINSICALLY X-RAY WEAK AGNS WITH NUSTAR                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀ Kp=5                                                                                                                   archived  20200429FAIRALL_0265                                      @ZM“Ò©¸¿PcI∫^@q?B√…ÓÃ¿8ÁØ∑È@D;©*0U2@Ï¢Ü[‰õø@Ï¢á?r‘£60360009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ﬁ›››ﬁ␀␀Â2Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                       