¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¢∞#Ü@Ï¢±#Ü30401019001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’›‰˙O•␀␀ÊéHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀UWe propose a 80 ks observation of the accreting X-ray pulsar, OAO 1657-415, in the brightest phase of its orbital cycle (phase 0.65-0.75), with a major aim of investigating the much debated cyclotron line at ~36 keV with NuSTAR, which provides unprecedented sensitivity for such studies. Its large effective area will permit detailed spin phase-resolved spectral analysis and possible variations of CRSF energy with luminosity, for the first time for OAO 1657-415, helping us understand accretion physics/geometry. The magnetic field strength will also allow us to investigate the contribution of magnetic/centrifugal gating to dips/flares. Through deep spectral studies, we will characterize the frequent flares. Intensity/energy-resolved pulse profiles will map hard X-ray beaming and clumpy winds.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pradhan                       Pragati                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       UNVEILING THE ACCRETION PHYSICS AND GEOMETRY IN OAO 1657-415 WITH NUSTAR                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316OAO_1657m415                                      @oÁã¨q≥¿D÷
¶L/É@uÉ–|Ñµ›?‡!-w1ê@p2}VlÙ@Ï¢±#Ü@Ï¢Í±9Ò30401019002@Ú@Çﬁ␀—∑@Ú>ÁßÔù≤@Ù␀¿ì›óˆ@Ùpì›óˆSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’›Bw`␀␀ÊéHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀UWe propose a 80 ks observation of the accreting X-ray pulsar, OAO 1657-415, in the brightest phase of its orbital cycle (phase 0.65-0.75), with a major aim of investigating the much debated cyclotron line at ~36 keV with NuSTAR, which provides unprecedented sensitivity for such studies. Its large effective area will permit detailed spin phase-resolved spectral analysis and possible variations of CRSF energy with luminosity, for the first time for OAO 1657-415, helping us understand accretion physics/geometry. The magnetic field strength will also allow us to investigate the contribution of magnetic/centrifugal gating to dips/flares. Through deep spectral studies, we will characterize the frequent flares. Intensity/energy-resolved pulse profiles will map hard X-ray beaming and clumpy winds.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pradhan                       Pragati                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       UNVEILING THE ACCRETION PHYSICS AND GEOMETRY IN OAO 1657-415 WITH NUSTAR                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MCG_03_58_007                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¢Í±9Ò@Ï¢Îî»)¯60502004007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’›'qf␀␀Ê¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀H7We propose a monitoring campaign of 4x60 ks simultaneous XMM & 4x40 ks NuSTAR observations of MCG-03-58-007, which is a powerful and variable disk wind. A Suzaku observation unveiled the presence of a high ionization, high Nh and fast (v~ 0.1c) wind launched from within a few 100Rg from the black hole. A follow-up 130ks simultaneous XMM/NuSTAR observation confirmed the presence of the wind and showed a Nh~1e24cm-2 variation in a day, which we ascribe to an increase of the Nh of the wind. Such dramatic variability places MCG-03-58-007 among the unique objects, and makes it an ideal AGN to map the inner disk-wind. With the new observations we will: probe the origin of the Nh variations, reveal the structure of the wind and determine how the wind responds to the continuum.                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Monitoring the extremely variable fast disk wind in MCG-03-58-007                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_03_58_007                                     @uf®¡T…Ü¿3=5®Xy>@DÁX‚e,¿Ni	kπå~@`ﬁ‚e+‘@Ï¢Îî»)¯@Ï£?r‘£60502004008@‰vüGÆ{@‰QÜ¬&Å@Âı¯ÍJå@Â˜xÍJåSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’‹§±~K␀␀Ê¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀H7We propose a monitoring campaign of 4x60 ks simultaneous XMM & 4x40 ks NuSTAR observations of MCG-03-58-007, which is a powerful and variable disk wind. A Suzaku observation unveiled the presence of a high ionization, high Nh and fast (v~ 0.1c) wind launched from within a few 100Rg from the black hole. A follow-up 130ks simultaneous XMM/NuSTAR observation confirmed the presence of the wind and showed a Nh~1e24cm-2 variation in a day, which we ascribe to an increase of the Nh of the wind. Such dramatic variability places MCG-03-58-007 among the unique objects, and makes it an ideal AGN to map the inner disk-wind. With the new observations we will: probe the origin of the Nh variations, reveal the structure of the wind and determine how the wind responds to the continuum.                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Braito                        Valentina                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Monitoring the extremely variable fast disk wind in MCG-03-58-007                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PGC049418                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï£?r‘£@Ï£[‰õø60061248001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’‹q3ı␀␀Â2Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PGC049418                                         @jS&¡æ¿BÁÔù≤-@s¬ﬂ;dZ@7ê ƒõ•„@t“ò_ˆî@Ï£[‰õø@Ï£#Ü60061248002@‘∆.±ƒ3@”Í  Wßá@’X<‘˝Û∂@’X<‘˝Û∂SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’‹1ﬂb:␀␀Â2Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_2788A                                         @`ÚOv_Ÿ¿QàŒp:˚@q¡>BZÓc¿,OiDg8@0ä¨1'@Ï£#Ü@Ï£±9Ò60469001001@wîºj~˘€@wrüUõ=@y2 Wßá@y2 WßáSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’‹ ÁY␀␀ÊéHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC_2788A                                         @`ÚàŒp:˚¿Q'ª/Ï@q¡e˝ä⁄∫¿,P-‡q@0÷ç∏ª@Ï£±9Ò@Ï£+î»)¯60469001002@⁄ÓHæﬂ§@⁄ªw‰%ÆÊ@‹‹N;Õ6@‹‹¡N;Õ6SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’€æKÂ␀␀ÊéHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20