A sub-sample of bright ULXs (Lx~(1-5)e40 erg/s) shows peculiar powerlaw like spectra in the 0.3-10 keV band, with no high energy turn-off and short-term variability at the level of ~10%. These sources might host Intermediate Mass Black Holes (IMBHs) in a hard state, although their apparently flat spectra may be the effect of low counting statistics. However, hard states with low energy spectral cut-off can indicate the presence of super-Eddington accreting neutron stars or stellar mass BHs. Here we propose simultaneous, deep XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of three best candidates to find evidence either of IMBHs, accreting sub-Eddington and with hard powerlaw spectra and high short term variability, or of the characteristic spectral shape of the super-Eddington accretion.              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pintore                       Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       UNVEILING BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN ULXs                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162XMMJ123551d7p275604                              @gùÈ˘rG@;Í«À)_@hÀfœAÚ@Uç5®Xy>?‚¡T…Öo@Ï£|±9Ò@Ï£≈[‰õø30501004002@˜)á+@˜æ˚Álã@¯À∞sÍ≥h@¯Œ†sÍ≥hSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Ÿ>¨/␀␀Ê°Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀I
A sub-sample of bright ULXs (Lx~(1-5)e40 erg/s) shows peculiar powerlaw like spectra in the 0.3-10 keV band, with no high energy turn-off and short-term variability at the level of ~10%. These sources might host Intermediate Mass Black Holes (IMBHs) in a hard state, although their apparently flat spectra may be the effect of low counting statistics. However, hard states with low energy spectral cut-off can indicate the presence of super-Eddington accreting neutron stars or stellar mass BHs. Here we propose simultaneous, deep XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of three best candidates to find evidence either of IMBHs, accreting sub-Eddington and with hard powerlaw spectra and high short term variability, or of the characteristic spectral shape of the super-Eddington accretion.              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pintore                       Fabio                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       UNVEILING BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN ULXs                                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SDSSJ1306p0735                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï£≈[‰õø@Ï£≈±9Ò60467003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ÿ␀Úπ÷␀␀ÊïHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀YDespite decades of searching, observationally confirmed cases of dual AGNs are extremely rare, and most have been discovered serendipitously. Using the all-sky WISE survey, we identified a population of over 200 interacting galaxies that display extreme red mid-IR colors thus far exclusively associated in extragalactic sources with powerful AGNs, despite showing no signatures of activity in the optical. We were recently awarded 340ks of Chandra time to observe the 15 brightest MIR-selected dual AGN candidates with separations of a few kpc, of which 8 display two nuclear X-ray point sources consistent with dual AGNs.We propose follow-up NuSTAR observations of our sample to constrain the intrinsic absorption of the systems and determine the AGN bolometric luminosity.                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Satyapal                      Shobita                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       WISE DISCOVERY OF THE LARGEST SAMPLE OF OBSCURED DUAL AGNS: A NUSTAR FOLLOW-UP                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ1306p0735                                    @hñº£n@KxF‹]@sò2a|⁄@QêLò_?ÌY≥–|Ñ∂@Ï£≈±9Ò@Ï£Î±9Ò60467003002@ËBñ[¿7@Ë¢Æ}Vm@È˛íÊ1¯°@Í2Ê1¯°SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÿú“ﬁ?␀␀ÊïHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀YDespite decades of searching, observationally confirmed cases of dual AGNs are extremely rare, and most have been discovered serendipitously. Using the all-sky WISE survey, we identified a population of over 200 interacting galaxies that display extreme red mid-IR colors thus far exclusively associated in extragalactic sources with powerful AGNs, despite showing no signatures of activity in the optical. We were recently awarded 340ks of Chandra time to observe the 15 brightest MIR-selected dual AGN candidates with separations of a few kpc, of which 8 display two nuclear X-ray point sources consistent with dual AGNs.We propose follow-up NuSTAR observations of our sample to constrain the intrinsic absorption of the systems and determine the AGN bolometric luminosity.                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Satyapal                      Shobita                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       WISE DISCOVERY OF THE LARGEST SAMPLE OF OBSCURED DUAL AGNS: A NUSTAR FOLLOW-UP                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MAXI_J1348m630                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï£Î±9Ò@Ï£ÏxVb‹80502304001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ÿ]L;*␀␀ÊéHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    7IŸThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors 