TAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA rally                                                                                                              archived  20200429Her_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï§*èFj@Ï§+èFj30402009003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’÷Øô␀␀ÊïHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¥We propose to continue the study of the long-term evolution of the cyclotron line energy in Her X-1. The long-term decay of Ecyc discovered in data taken between 1993 and 2012, was confirmed by a precise measurement by NuSTAR in August 2015, firmly establishing a decay by 5 keV over the last 20 yrs. However, the last observing campaign in 2016, had provided evidence that the decay has ended. This was confirmed by the latest NuSTAR observation in August 2017. It is now the question whether we will (at some time) see a new increase, similar to the one that was observed between 1990 and 1993. It is likely that we see a change in the structure of the local B-field. We propose to continue the monitoring of Her X-1 during Cycle-4 with two observations of 30 ks each (about half a year apart).    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Staubert                      Ruediger                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       MONITORING THE CYCLOTRON LINE ENERGY IN HERCULES X-1: IS THERE A TURN-UP AFTER THE LONG DECAY?                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Her_X1                                            @oÕ∏QÎÖ@A¨SéÛMj@M©*0U2@B⁄-V@G%“Ò©˚Á@Ï§+èFj@Ï§BèFj30402009004@⁄Ä9fœAÚ@⁄´˜lãCñ@‡aó'ª/Ï@‡cW'ª/ÏSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’’:gÍ√␀␀ÊïHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀¥We propose to continue the study of the long-term evolution of the cyclotron line energy in Her X-1. The long-term decay of Ecyc discovered in data taken between 1993 and 2012, was confirmed by a precise measurement by NuSTAR in August 2015, firmly establishing a decay by 5 keV over the last 20 yrs. However, the last observing campaign in 2016, had provided evidence that the decay has ended. This was confirmed by the latest NuSTAR observation in August 2017. It is now the question whether we will (at some time) see a new increase, similar to the one that was observed between 1990 and 1993. It is likely that we see a change in the structure of the local B-field. We propose to continue the monitoring of Her X-1 during Cycle-4 with two observations of 30 ks each (about half a year apart).    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Staubert                      Ruediger                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       MONITORING THE CYCLOTRON LINE ENERGY IN HERCULES X-1: IS THERE A TURN-UP AFTER THE LONG DECAY?                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              INTEGRAL                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC5907_ULX1                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï§BèFj@Ï§BxVb‹30302004005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’æé˚␀␀Ê¿Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀:NGC 5907 ULX-1 is the most luminous of the 3 ULXs known to be accreting pulsars. At 17.1 Mpc, its X-ray isotropic luminosity peaks at 1e41 erg/s, 500 times the Eddington limit for a NS. Understanding the physics of this extreme object, for a long time prototypical of IMBHs, which defeats simple theoretical explanations, is interesting per se and essential for population studies and searches for pulsation in other ULXs. The most effective strategy is to repeatedly observe it with XMM and NuSTAR with a carefully devised schedule. Their synergy will provide us also with orbital- and spin-phase-resolved broad-band X-ray spectroscopy, essential for a better understanding of this object, in the context of other extreme ULXs.                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Belfiore                      Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The orbit and spectra of the brightest accreting pulsar: NGC 5907 ULX-1                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC5907_ULX1                                      @lùœ™ÕûÑ@L&
¶L/É@VÌ ¿Éo@IíJåLò@8ÏŸË>B[@Ï§BxVb‹@Ï§hî»)¯30302004006@ËΩ˜º”ZÜ@Ëí”∑X‚@ÍàZë—N<@ÍãZë—N<SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’æ;ÏGM␀␀Ê¿Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀:NGC 5907 ULX-1 is the most luminous of the 3 ULXs known to be accreting pulsars. At 17.1 Mpc, its X-ray isotropic luminosity peaks at 1e41 erg/s, 500 times the Eddington limit for a NS. Understanding the physics of this extreme object, for a long time prototypical of IMBHs, which defeats simple theoretical explanations, is interesting per se and essential for population studies and searches for pulsation in other ULXs. The most effective strategy is to repeatedly observe it with XMM and NuSTAR with a carefully devised schedule. Their synergy will provide us also with orbital- and spin-phase-resolved broad-band X-ray spectroscopy, essential for a better understanding of this object, in the context of other extreme ULXs.                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Belfiore                      Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X16       The orbit and spectra of the brightest accreting pulsar: NGC 5907 ULX-1                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(3/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Fairall_51                                        @qîy¶µ¿O/À)^û@t—˝Û∂E¢¿6ÏV’œ™Œ@m0êñªô@Ï§hî»)¯@Ï§iÕ´∏160402014003@t,¨1'@t∫^5?}@uÄ␀␀␀␀␀@uÄ␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’æöºﬂ␀␀ÊûHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose an observation of the particularly intriguing polar-scattered Seyfert 1 Fairall 51 with NuSTAR (120 ks), divided in two periods of 60 ks separated by about 8 months, to be coordinated with our two Guaranteed Time Observations (GTO) with Chandra/HETG (120 ks each, so 240 ks of total exposure). Timing and spectral analyses of these joint observations will put constraints on the characteristics of reflection from both the accretion disk near the black hole and from more distant material, on the presence and origin of a possible broad iron line, on the spin of the black hole, on the inclination angle, on the physical properties of the hot corona, on the structure and properties of the warm absorbers, and on the origin of the potential soft excess.                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boissay Malaquin              Rozenn                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       INVESTIGATION ON FAIRALL 51 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA/HETG                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429Fairall_51                                        @qî”ZÖáî¿O/iDg8@t—˝Û∂E¢¿6ÔˆîFt@m?&Èx‘˛@Ï§iÕ´∏1@Ï§Õ´∏160402014004@‹Ù‘˝Û∂@‹ÀU*ô1@ﬁ¸ÿD–@ﬁˇXÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’æÃ
ûà␀␀ÊûHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose an observation of the particularly intriguing polar-scattered Seyfert 1 Fairall 51 with NuSTAR (120 ks), divided in two periods of 60 ks separated by about 8 months, to be coordinated with our two Guaranteed Time Observations (GTO) with Chandra/HETG (120 ks each, so 240 ks of total exposure). Timing and spectral analyses of these joint observations will put constraints on the characteristics of reflection from both the accretion disk near the black hole and from more distant material, on the presence and origin of a possible broad iron line, on the spin of the black hole, on the inclination angle, on the physical properties of the hot corona, on the structure and properties of the warm absorbers, and on the origin of the potential soft excess.                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boissay Malaquin              Rozenn                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       INVESTIGATION ON FAIRALL 51 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA/HETG                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2a/2)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429ESO_506mG027                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï§Õ´∏1@Ï§Äî»)¯60469006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’øõ‡$␀␀Ê§Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_506mG027                                      @g∂•zxl"¿;Uzxl"h@r≥a@N•@A≈îØOÑ@vÉnóç@Ï§Äî»)¯@Ï§è#Ü60469006002@“C!ú‡v@“#∞ƒõ•„@”ôµ≥–|Ö@”ú5≥–|ÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ø@Úπ÷␀␀Ê§Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀±We propose Nustar observations of 15 candidate Compton-thick (CT) AGN using a new metric, the spectral curvature (SC) above 10 keV, to identify CT AGN in low-quality Swift BAT X-ray data from the deepest 105-month all sky maps. Our initial survey of the 70-month data found that the majority of SC selected AGN are indeed CT (7/9). The proposed sensitive, broadband X-ray observations will allow us to confirm their reflection-dominated nature, study the iron line morphology, and constrain the absorbing column and reflection. The shape of the X-ray background requires a significant population of heavily obscured AGN and this flux-limited sample will improve our understanding of this important population by providing detailed study of some of the nearest examples.                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       USING THE SPECTRAL CURVATURE TO FIND COMPTON-THICK AGN AMONG THE FAINTEST SWIFT BAT SOURCES                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MAXI_J1348m630                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï§è#Ü@Ï§è±9Ò80502304003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’øÉi–7␀␀ÊìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    7IŸThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1348m630                                    @i·	kπå~¿O®£◊
=q@sQ‡qvøIQÇ©ì@t^éäqﬁj@Ï§è±9Ò@Ï§û±9Ò80502304004@Œ˜Ú‰éä@ŒŒ˚˚µt@◊ú>kP∞Ú@◊ü~kP∞ÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ø´ùdÅ␀␀ÊìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  7IŸThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429FGC_0061                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï§û±9Ò@Ï§ü±9Ò60361020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’¿ozÄ␀␀Â>Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and AGN obscuration. We will continue to pursue a multi-layer survey strategy, expanding the number of deep fields, and increasing the area of shallow observations through serendipitous sources.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀A    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀ELS       Extragalactic Legacy Surveys                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316FGC_0061                                          @!_ä	ﬁøx*ô0æÌ@\Re+”√¿OSMjO@`cC,•z@Ï§ü±9Ò@Ï§øèFj60361020002@‰·ù}øHÄ@‰º¿}VlÙ@ÊWÎé!ñS@ÊXÎé!ñSSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’¿ü´¬␀␀Â>Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀NuSTAR will continue both Galactic and Extragalactic legacy Surveys during the extended mission with programs selected based on community input. One of the principal goals of the NuSTAR extragalactic surveys is to constrain the high-energy X-ray luminosity function and trace its evolution as a function of redshift and A