uring the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SgrAstar                                          @p¶´6zë¿=öu%@v|Énóç?≈»K]Ãd@s˜Œp:˚@Ï•º±9Ò@Ï¶#Ü90501330002@˘Ä9C˛\í@˘AUõ=@˚äı°a‰˜@˚åö‘ï+SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’À€óS␀␀Â7Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429MAXI_J1348m630                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¶#Ü@Ï¶±9Ò80502304005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ãâìà'␀␀ÊûHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    7IŸThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1348m630                                    @i·HÀí:¿Oß†˘	k∫@sQÙÿD–ø,•zxl"@uN;Õ5®@Ï¶±9Ò@Ï¶[‰õø80502304006@–«¶8ÜYK@—ba|⁄@◊vÙ‘˝Û∂@◊z4‘˝Û∂SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Ã≤°ê␀␀ÊûHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  7IŸThe assumption that a black hole's accretion disk extends inwards to the ISCO is on firm footing for soft spectral states, but has been challenged for hard spectral states where it is often argued that the accretion flow is truncated far from the horizon. This is of critical importance because black-hole spin is measured on the basis of this assumption. The direct detection (or absence) of thermal disk emission associated with a disk extending to the ISCO is the smoking-gun test to rule truncation in or out for the bright hard state. Using a self-consistent spectral model on data taken in the bright hard state while taking advantage of the complementary coverage and capabilities of Chandra and NuSTAR, we will achieve a definitive test of the truncation paradigm.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       A Test of Black-Hole Disk Truncation: Thermal Disk Emission in the Bright Hard State                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_1377                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¶[‰õø@Ï¶#Ü60501054001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ÕY8Çw␀␀ÊÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀£The evolution of low-mass galaxies may follow a different path to quiescence than more massive galaxies, particular with regards to the prevalence of stellar versus active galactic nuclei (AGN) feedback. It is only recently that we have begun to detect AGN activity in dwarf galaxies. NGC 1377, a local post-starburst lenticular with a young molecular outflow, will provide useful insights into AGN feedback in low-mass galaxies. ALMA observations of molecular gas indicate a likely Compton-thick obscuration, explaining the marginal nuclear X-ray detection in previous Chandra observations. We propose to observe NGC 1377 for 60 ks to detect and characterize the X-ray emission and intrinsic luminosity of this heavily obscured AGN and shed light on its relation to the molecular outflow.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    LANZ                          LAURANNE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NGC 1377: A LOW-MASS GALAXY WITH AGN FEEDBACK?                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1377                                          @Ké!ñRΩ¿4‹æﬂ§@@jîcÒA \¿JAÚ◊s@]zµ'ª@Ï¶#Ü@Ï¶Fî»)¯60501054002@Ì#p‘˝Û∂@ÏÓ8„º”[@ÔÔ¯†ê.@Ô O¯†ê.SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÕãëAù␀␀ÊÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀£The evolution of low-mass galaxies may follow a different path to quiescence than more massive galaxies, particular with regards to the prevalence of stellar versus active galactic nuclei (AGN) feedback. It is only recently that we have begun to detect AGN activity in dwarf galaxies. NGC 1377, a local post-starburst lenticular with a young molecular outflow, will provide useful insights into AGN feedback in low-mass galaxies. ALMA observations of molecular gas indicate a likely Compton-thick obscuration, explaining the marginal nuclear X-ray detection in previous Chandra observations. We propose to observe NGC 1377 for 60 ks to detect and characterize the X-ray emission and intrinsic luminosity of this heavily obscured AGN and shed light on its relation to the molecular outflow.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    LANZ                          LAURANNE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NGC 1377: A LOW-MASS GALAXY WITH AGN FEEDBACK?                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀ Kp=5                                                                                                                   archived  20200429M51                                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¶Fî»)¯@Ï¶GÍM60501023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ŒÑ\ä␀␀ÊÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose joint observations with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton of the M51 galaxies that will provide the best simultaneous, high-time resolution, and sensitive broadband X-ray spectral dataset on the sources within them yet. The joint dataset will yield the first high-quality broadband spectrum of at least one neutron-star-powered ultraluminous X-ray source in the galaxy, possibly two. This will allow a detailed spectral decomposition and sensitive searches for cyclotron lines. The timing resolution of XMM-Newton will allow us to detect pulsations, yielding an improved orbital solution for one ULX, and perhaps detecting them for the first time in one of the many others in the galaxies.                                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BRIGHTMAN                     MURRAY                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A BROADBAND X-RAY SPECTRAL STUDY OF TWO NEUTRON-STAR-POWERED ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCES IN M51                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M51                                               @iNîFsÅÿ@GôJÙÿE@ZTŒp:˚@Q!¿ÎÌ˙D@uÈçOﬂ;d@Ï¶GÍM@Ï¶¡ÍM60501023002A¶€¿7A~¥ˇó$AÛ$›A7öüæwSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ŒO+ùe␀␀ÊÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                