Ns.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316KUG1208p386                                       @f‘¡T…Ö@C'±[W>´@c√lãCïÅ@RÌË>BZÓ@u?iDg8@Ï¶¬?r‘£@Ï¶Ÿ[‰õø60061225002@ﬂ'ïÅ%@ﬁ b&ÄùI@‡áeîØO@‡áeîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’œVŸ±ﬂ␀␀ÂEHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C_264                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¶Ÿ[‰õø@Ï¶Ÿ±9Ò60501016001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’œ´Ö∏␀␀ÊÆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀∂We propose to study one of the few FRI radio galaxies with detected TeV emission, 3C 264. It hosts a supermassive black hole emitting at 7x10^-5 Eddington luminosity, generally associated with a radiatively inefficient accretion flow (RIAF). Multiwavelength studies suggest that the X-ray emission comes from a jet. However, it is also possible that the RIAF can contribute significantly to the X-rays. Unfortunately, hard X-ray spectrum above 10 keV is missing, making it hard to distinguish the X-ray emission model. With the excellent sensitivity of NuSTAR, we will study the nature of the hard X-rays from this rare TeV FRI radio galaxy and will distinguish whether the hard X-ray emission comes from a jet or an accretion flow.                                                                    Active galaxies and Qu