                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG1149m110                                        @f?º¿&«Êö‘,@qÄh€ã¨q@Hp_ˆîF@	ôôôôôö@Ïßáî»)¯@Ïßî?r‘£60160458002@–ÿÙ™d¬¯@–æ—w1è≈@—¯∆Ç@∑Ä@—¯∆Ç@∑ÄSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’”Xéô˚␀␀ÂLHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO511_G030                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïßî?r‘£@Ïßî±9Ò60502035001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’º∏	+␀␀Ê∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀@We propose a nustar/xmm monitoring composed by 5x30ks observations spaced by 3-4 days of the bright Radio-Quiet Seyfert 1 ESO511-G030. Its spectrum is characterised by a relativistic Fe Kalpha line (significance >5sigma) and a strong soft excess, thus making ESO511-G030 the ideal candidate for testing the warm Comptonisation origin of soft excess in AGN. In fact, warm corona has been proved to be a viable physical model in sources without a relativistic reflection component, but it has never been properly tested in a source hosting a relativistic iron line where the soft excess is reproduced via relativistic reflection. The proposed monitoring will allow us to test the general validity of the warm Comptonisation by breaking its spectral degeneracy with relativistic reflection.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MIDDEI                        RICCARDO                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       ESO511-G030: THE ULTIMATE TEST BENCH FOR THE TWO-CORONA MODEL                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO511_G030                                       @j⁄	‘ï+¿:¨⁄QŒ@t_^ûö@@0ÜYJÙÒ@uÙM:í£@Ïßî±9Ò@Ïß´±9Ò60502035002@ﬂcÑF‹]@ﬂ6˚úæ‡@‡’≈úæ‡@‡÷eúæ‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’º_Û›␀␀Ê∆Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀@We propose a nustar/xmm monitoring composed by 5x30ks observations spaced by 3-4 days of the bright Radio-Quiet Seyfert 1 ESO511-G030. Its spectrum is characterised by a relativistic Fe Kalpha line (significance >5sigma) and a strong soft excess, thus making ESO511-G030 the ideal candidate for testing the warm Comptonisation origin of soft excess in AGN. In fact, warm corona has been proved to be a viable physical model in sources without a relativistic reflection component, but it has never been properly tested in a source hosting a relativistic iron line where the soft excess is reproduced via relativistic reflection. The proposed monitoring will allow us to test the general validity of the warm Comptonisation by breaking its spectral degeneracy with relativistic reflection.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MIDDEI                        RICCARDO                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       ESO511-G030: THE ULTIMATE TEST BENCH FOR THE TWO-CORONA MODEL                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429HD110432                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïß´±9Ò@Ïß¨#Ü30501001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’º4%Ì	␀␀ÊƒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀HlWe propose to conduct a spectral and photometric characterization of the gamma Cas-like star HD 110432 from a broad energy range. The aims are to confront the multiple-discrete plasma and the cooling plasma scenarios, and look for signs of reflection of X-rays from a cold material. In addition, the investigation will allow us to check for presence of flare-like events in high energies and for extended drop-off time interval in the hard flux. This investigation requires a simultaneous campaign with the XMM-Newton (15 ks) and NuSTAR (100 ks) satelites. The ultimate goal is to contribute to solve one of the bigger X-ray mysteries in massive stars: the origin of the X-rays of gamma Cas stars; magnetic Be stars or accreting white dwarfs?                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lopes de Oliveira             Raimundo                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       HD110432: the most extreme case amongst gamma-Cas X-ray stars                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HD110432                                          @g‘‡uˆ˝"¿Oä&ÄùIR@r‹“Ò©˚Áø¿qué"?¸»K]Ãd@Ïß¨#Ü@ÏßÓ?r‘£30501001002@˘˝H
=p§@˘”3lãCñ@˚—$≈Åo@˚”#¯7¥¢SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ª¶¯	␀␀ÊƒHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀HlWe propose to conduct a spectral and photometric characterization of the gamma Cas-like star HD 110432 from a broad energy range. The aims are to confront the multiple-discrete plasma and the cooling plasma scenarios, and look for signs of reflection of X-rays from a cold material. In addition, the investigation will allow us to check for presence of flare-like events in high energies and for extended drop-off time interval in the hard flux. This investigation requires a simultaneous campaign with the XMM-Newton (15 ks) and NuSTAR (100 ks) satelites. The ultimate goal is to contribute to solve one of the bigger X-ray mysteries in massive stars: the origin of the X-rays of gamma Cas stars; magnetic Be stars or accreting white dwarfs?                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Lopes de Oliveira             Raimundo                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       HD110432: the most extreme case amongst gamma-Cas X-ray stars                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004291E_1145d1m6141                                    @f¶µ(¿N˙=p£◊
@ru<ûÏø±?¨‡uˆ˝!ˇ@,˜¥¢3ú@ÏßÓ?r‘£@ÏßÓî»)¯30501002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ªlGL˝␀␀ÊƒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀HÑWe propose to observe the high-mass X-ray binary (HMXB) 1E 1145.1-6141 simultaneously with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR for 40ks each. This will be the first sensitive broad-band observations of this little studied source. The system consists of a neutron star and a B2 supergiant, which is one of the latest stellar types found in a HMXB. Our goal is to investigate the structure of the accreted matter and the stellar wind and search for a cyclotron resonant scattering feature to constrain the magnetic field. We will describe the broadband X-ray spectrum with state-of-the-art physical models to constrain the physical conditions close to the neutron star and perform pulse phase-resolved analysis to constraint the emission geometry.                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Wind structure and accretion physics in the rarely studied HMXB 1E 1145.1-6141                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161E_1145d1m6141                                    @f´üUõ=¿N¸º£@ruLò_˜?•œ™ÕûÉ‰@-Õ‡qv@ÏßÓî»)¯@Ï®	ÍM30501002002@Âôñ¡Ω•@ÂÄ>⁄πıZ@Áão␀h‹@Áço␀h‹SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ª†ŒQ␀␀ÊƒHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀HÑWe propose to observe the high-mass X-ray binary (HMXB) 1E 1145.1-6141 simultaneously with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR for 40ks each. This will be the first sensitive broad-band observations of this little studied source. The system consists of a neutron star and a B2 supergiant, which is one of the latest stellar types found in a HMXB. Our goal is to investigate the structure of the accreted matter and the stellar wind and search for a cyclotron resonant scattering feature to constrain the magnetic field. We will describe the broadband X-ray spectrum with state-of-the-art physical models to constrain the physical conditions close to the neutron star and perform pulse phase-resolved analysis to constraint the emission geometry.                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Wind structure and accretion physics in the rarely studied HMXB 1E 1145.1-6141                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4151                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï®	ÍM@Ï®
Õ´∏160502017001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’∫⁄ºﬂ␀␀ÁuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4151                                          @f”S˜ŒŸ@C±JÙÿE@cp\ë—N<@R∑|Ìëhs@t≤V’œ™Œ@Ï®
