atories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     ART-XC                                                                                              ␀␀␀Cross calibration / Kp=5                                                                                                archived  20200429Swift_J1858d6m0814                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï©óÍM@Ï©ò±9Ò90501333001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’∞ˆÂ‘ƒ␀␀ÂWHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1858d6m0814                                @q…‚Ç@∑Ä¿ ÜÈx‘˝Ù@:6‚ÎC-¿`ê-‡@v •„S˜œ@Ï©ò±9Ò@Ï©´[‰õø90501333002@’f:˚~ë@’OZ˛≈m]@◊Iã6zë@◊OK6zëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’∞´˛{£␀␀ÂWHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              HST NICER                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï©´[‰õø@Ï©¨èFj80502335001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’∞Ü£␀␀ÊuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀§Understanding the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge in black hole systems. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both components are very strong for Cyg X-1, but the disk is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. This proposal is a continuation of a previously approved NuSTAR Cycle 3 program on Cyg X-1 using the uniquely powerful capability of combined continuum and reflection modeling provided with both instruments together. By chance, our first program covered the soft state only. We propose to expand and target hard states and request 3x20 ks NuSTAR (3x4 ks NICER) observations of Cyg X-1 for triggered monitoring of hard states.        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    STEINER                       JAMES                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A CONTINUED NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT REFLECTION AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @r∏ñªò«„@Aô¯†ê-‡@QÃV’œ™Œ@	ûÏø±[W@Fy^ûö@Ï©¨èFj@Ï©ªî»)¯80502335002@ =√W>´6@À+⁄£S&@÷ˆ|ìtº@÷˜<ìtºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Øñ/…c␀␀ÊuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀§Understanding the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge in black hole systems. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both components are very strong for Cyg X-1, but the disk is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. This proposal is a continuation of a previously approved NuSTAR Cycle 3 program on Cyg X-1 using the uniquely powerful capability of combined continuum and reflection modeling provided with both instruments together. By chance, our first program covered the soft state only. We propose to expand and target hard states and request 3x20 ks NuSTAR (3x4 ks NICER) observations of Cyg X-1 for triggered monitoring of hard states.        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    STEINER                       JAMES                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A CONTINUED NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT REFLECTION AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429M82_X2                                            @b°Ùá¸π$@QhÀ)^û@a≠¸π#¢ú@D:6‚ÎC@p6)^û	@Ï©ªî»)¯@Ï©º?r‘£30502020003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Øl®d ␀␀Ê∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82_X2                                            @b†Ov`@QiVlÙ!@aÆ1&Èx’@D7v_ÿ≠¨@p%xl"h
@Ï©º?r‘£@Ï©ÓèFj30502020004@ı~y0æÌ@ı_n„º”[@ˆ˛¿ökP±@˜␀␀ökP±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÆÍÛ|␀␀Ê∆Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_1566                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï©ÓèFj@Ï©Ô?r‘£60501031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Æ¥œ’Ü␀␀Ê”Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀.We propose a deep 200 ks NuSTAR observations of the nearby (z = 0.005) repeat changing-look AGN NGC 1566 as it returns to quiescence, simultaneous with a 10 ks XMM-Newton exposure, to complete the X-ray picture of this unique source. With this observation, we will follow the properties of the X-ray corona as the source transitions back to a Seyfert 2, combining this with Swift monitoring to track the evolution of the accretion disk.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PARKER                        MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       UNDERSTANDING THE REPEAT CHANGING-LOOK AGN NGC 1566                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_1566                                          @P@Åo␀h‹¿Ks≤˛≈m]@pÖÖáì›ò¿Eπ [¿7@`|ƒõ•„T@Ï©Ô?r‘£@Ï™èFj60501031002@Ï≈JœAÚ@Ïé”e˝ä€@Ó¡·o“Û@Ó¡o“ÛSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÆK`∂␀␀Ê”Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀.We propose a deep 200 ks NuSTAR observations of the nearby (z = 0.005) repeat changing-look AGN NGC 1566 as it returns to quiescence, simultaneous with a 10 ks XMM-Newton exposure, to complete the X-ray picture of this unique source. With this observation, we will follow the properties of the X-ray corona as the source transitions back to a Seyfert 2, combining this with Swift monitoring to track the evolution of the accretion disk.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PARKER                        MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       UNDERSTANDING THE REPEAT CHANGING-LOOK AGN NGC 1566                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO511_G030                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï™èFj@Ï™ÍM60502035009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Æ[«ﬂ␀␀Ê∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀@We propose a nustar/xmm monitoring composed by 5x30ks observations spaced by 3-4 days of the bright Radio-Quiet Seyfert 1 ESO511-G030. Its spectrum is characterised by a relativistic Fe Kalpha line (significance >5sigma) and a strong soft excess, thus making ESO511-G030 the ideal candidate for testing the warm Comptonisation origin of soft excess in AGN. In fact, warm corona has been proved to be a viable physical model in sources without a relativistic reflection component, but it has never been properly tested in a source hosting a relativistic iron line where the soft excess is reproduced via relativistic reflection. The proposed monitoring will allow us to test the general validity of the warm Comptonisation by breaking its spectral degeneracy with relativistic reflection.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MIDDEI                        RICCARDO                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       ESO511-G030: THE ULTIMATE TEST BENCH FOR THE TWO-CORONA MODEL                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO511_G030                                       @j⁄JåLò¿:©JÙÿE@t_ì‡ﬁ”@@1ué!ñS@v1l"h	’@Ï™ÍM@Ï™.[‰õø60502035010@‹z	ﬁ@‹NB+jÁ’@ﬁó…ôôôö@ﬁùâôôôöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’≠Õé˚␀␀Ê∆Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀@We propose a nustar/xmm monitoring composed by 5x30ks observations spaced by 3-4 days of the bright Radio-Quiet Seyfert 1 ESO511-G030. Its spectrum is characterised by a relativistic Fe Kalpha line (significance >5sigma) and a strong soft excess, thus making ESO511-G030 the ideal candidate for testing the warm Comptonisation origin of soft excess in AGN. In fact, warm corona has been proved to be a viable physical model in sources without a relativistic reflection component, but it has never been properly tested in a source hosting a relativistic iron line where the soft excess is reproduced via relativistic reflection. The proposed monitoring will allow us to test the general validity of the warm Comptonisation by breaking its spectral degeneracy with relativistic reflection.             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MIDDEI                        RI