prised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ18470283m7831494                            @qùP∞Ú{≥¿S§¢3úø@s∫ãCïÅ¿:3S&¬@r“Ú‰è@Ï™ÈxVb‹@Ï™ıÕ´∏160160699002@’␀Lë—N<@‘ﬂMe+”√@÷YS'ª0@÷YS'ª0SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’™%ãÚY␀␀ÂvHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293FGL_J1405d4m6119                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï™ıÕ´∏1@Ï™ˆ?r‘£30502015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’©˛¨/␀␀Ê‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We have discovered a new member of the rare and poorly understood class of high-mass gamma-ray binaries: 3FGL J1405.4-6119. The system shows gamma-ray, radio and X-ray modulation on its 13.7 day period, with different patterns at each waveband. The existing X-ray data, while clearly showing the period, are insufficient to perform phase-resolved spectroscopy, which is needed to investigate the physics driving the emission and its orbital variability.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    CORBET                        ROBIN                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PHYSICAL PROCESSES IN THE NEW GAMMA-RAY BINARY 3FGL J1405.4-6119                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163FGL_J1405d4m6119                                 @jh_o“ ¿N©kπå~(@sxC,•z?€ïÅ$›/@ıY≥–|Ö@Ï™ˆ?r‘£@Ï´&èFj30502015002@ÓÉ§?Â…@ÌÔhÂ`Aâ@&öu%@(TÕ5®XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’©Äa#␀␀Ê‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We have discovered a new member of the rare and poorly understood class of high-mass gamma-ray binaries: 3FGL J1405.4-6119. The system shows gamma-ray, radio and X-ray modulation on its 13.7 day period, with different patterns at each waveband. The existing X-ray data, while clearly showing the period, are insufficient to perform phase-resolved spectroscopy, which is needed to investigate the physics driving the emission and its orbital variability.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    CORBET                        ROBIN                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PHYSICAL PROCESSES IN THE NEW GAMMA-RAY BINARY 3FGL J1405.4-6119                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_1566                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï´&èFj@Ï´&±9Ò60501031003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’©LSo‚␀␀Ê”Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀.We propose a deep 200 ks NuSTAR observations of the nearby (z = 0.005) repeat changing-look AGN NGC 1566 as it returns to quiescence, simultaneous with a 10 ks XMM-Newton exposure, to complete the X-ray picture of this unique source. With this observation, we will follow the properties of the X-ray corona as the source transitions back to a Seyfert 2, combining this with Swift monitoring to track the evolution of the accretion disk.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PARKER                        MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       UNDERSTANDING THE REPEAT CHANGING-LOOK AGN NGC 1566                                                                     ␀␀