observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / shield event rejection off                                                                                    archived  20150316Crab_bkgd_PA150                                   @U)qué"@6É˛\ë—N@gÚ‰éär¿”˜ŒŸá@b¢Xy=Ÿ@Ï¨∫î»)¯@Ï¨ ÍM10502009003@◊EéÀ˚µ@◊%õàeîØ@Ÿ˛qﬁi≠@Ÿ˛qﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’üË3îJ␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Blank-sky background calibration / Kp=4-6 / shield event rejection off                                                  archived  20200429Mrk_590                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¨ ÍM@Ï¨ÀxVb‹80502630001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’üæƒt–␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÑAfter a 10-year hiatus Mrk590 is rekindling its nuclear activity! Our Swift monitoring strongly suggests that the AGN is likely to turn on again soon. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document, for the first time ever, the onset of AGN activity when it occurs since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are fueled. We ask to monitor Mrk590 bi-weekly with Swift and based thereon trigger a joint 3-visit program with NuSTAR and Swift. We aim to establish at which accretion luminosity the expected emission features of a standard accretion disk appear as the AGN turns on. This program is an important part of a broad-scoped, multi-wavelength study of this remarkable AGN.                                                                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    VESTERGAARD                   MARIANNE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING AND TOO OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: CATCHING THE MONSTER IN THE ACT!                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_590                                           @@“πıY≥–øÁQÇ©ì·@dfXy=Ÿ¿LèAÚ◊s@b∫«À)@Ï¨ÀxVb‹@Ï¨˛?r‘£80502630002@°µ=Ÿc@É⁄,<ûÌ@Ò–¢dZ¨@Ò“≤dZ¨SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ü?ûË›␀␀ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÑAfter a 10-year hiatus Mrk590 is rekindling its nuclear activity! Our Swift monitoring strongly suggests that the AGN is likely to turn on again soon. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document, for the first time ever, the onset of AGN activity when it occurs since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are fueled. We ask to monitor Mrk590 bi-weekly with Swift and based thereon trigger a joint 3-visit program with NuSTAR and Swift. We aim to establish at which accretion luminosity the expected emission features of a standard accretion disk appear as the AGN turns on. This program is an important part of a broad-scoped, multi-wavelength study of this remarkable AGN.                                                                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    VESTERGAARD                   MARIANNE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING AND TOO OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: CATCHING THE MONSTER IN THE ACT!                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀(1/3) ToO / Kp=4-6                                                                                                      archived  20200429Sol_19245_SADA                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¨˛?r‘£@Ï¨ˇS’p"80501001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ü
œX␀␀ÂvHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ We propose to take the ultimate NuSTAR observations of the quiet Sun during the minimum of the solar cycle. These observations, collected at close to 100% livetime, will be used for the deepest-ever search for X-ray bremsstrahlung signatures to investigate the key question of coronal heating, and they will additionally allow us to search for an axion conversion signal. With solar activity being currently at a minimum and activity expected to pick up again later in 2020 or latest in 2021, the proposed observations are time-critical.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    KRUCKER                       SAM                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF QUIETEST X-RAY SUN: CORONAL HEATING AND AXIONS                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    archived  20150316ACS_contingency_19244                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¨ˇS’p"@Ï≠Ä[‰õø00501001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÆ’û&ØˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Non-Pointing data                                           ␀␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀!˚␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ACS anomaly / instrument powered off                                                                                    observed  20150316Cen_A_FPMB                                        @i*Lò_¿EÉ¸PH@sTlãCïÅ@3äÙÿD–@4å≤ïÈ·±@Ï≠ÄÍM@Ï≠óîØ‰000501005002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀@ﬁπ\ç∏ª␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀@‡Ÿê`™d√SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ûßqfˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Su