=q␀␀Ê+Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ıContrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR-XMM effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program has the immediate goal to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion.                                                                                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       DETAILED MAPPING OF EXTREME MASS LOSS WITH COORDINATED NUSTAR-XMM OBSERVATIONS                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Cen_X4                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï≠‹î»)¯@Ï≠›?r‘£30502024003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ù%Ì	{␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cen_X4                                            @l|⁄Q¿?∞uˆ˝!ˇ@t¿µsÍ≥@8ﬁ␀—∑@v[m≈÷8Ü@Ï≠›?r‘£@ÏÆ[‰õø30502024004@ÁDPÙ!-@Á(·±[W?@Ë˙∆eîØO@Ë˚ÜeîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’úΩÍ␀¬␀␀ÈHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀ ␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀(2/10)                                                                                                                  archived  202004294U_1957p11                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÆ[‰õø@ÏÆ#Ü30502007007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’öfOD␀␀ÂáHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1957p11                                        @rΩS&¬@'[Ùá¸π$@Içëhr∞!¿"Mqué"@uÛùIQÇ™@ÏÆ#Ü@ÏÆÍM30502007008@√æ`¸PH@√Œn‚ÎC@«-≥–|Ö@«1≥–|ÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’öF␀␀ÂáHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀B␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀(4/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Cyg_X2                                            @ta◊€Ùá˝@C'ä	ﬁ@U∆ÛMj¿&ÖÅo␀i@A¿Énò@ÏÆxVb‹@ÏÆ #Ü80511301002@∆X©*0@∆ˇí·GÆ@’∏ˇêñªô@’∏ˇêñªôSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ôÃk∂␀␀Â}Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀SThe best laboratory for strong-field gravity is the inner 100s of kilometers around compact objects in low-mass X-ray binaries. The X-ray emission varies rapidly, notably in quasi-periodic oscillations (QPOs), which may be produced by general relativistic effects. Low-frequency QPOs from black holes and neutron stars share similar temporal and spectral properties, indicating that they have similar emission mechanisms, even though neutron stars have a surface and strong magnetic field in addition to general relativistic effects. With these proposed observations, we will use phase-resolved spectroscopy of two types of low-frequency QPOs to constrain and compare the emission mechanisms, to better understand accretion flows in the strong gravity regime.                                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Stevens                       Abigail                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       COMPARING EMISSION MECHANISMS OF LOW-FREQUENCY QPOS IN BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS                                    ␀␀Ù␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004292MASXJ21355399p4728217                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÆ #Ü@ÏÆ xVb‹60160761001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’òÔ·®≈␀␀ÂvHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ21355399p4728217                            @t>∑X‚@Gªò«‚ÇA@W∏QÎÖ¿‰%ÆÊ2@A“Ò©˚Á@ÏÆ xVb‹@ÏÆ.ÍM60160761002@“C‚3úø@“)Zá+@”£␀