s from the Swift-BAT and INTEGRAL-ISGRI surveys. We know little about these sources other than a crude X-ray position and flux, but they are good candidates for being high-redshift blazars or Compton-thick AGN that appear missing from those surveys. Our scientific objectives are: to obtain an improved X-ray position that will narrow down the list of possible counterparts in the optical/infrared bands; and create X-ray spectra to combine with the OIR data to generate their broadband spectral energy distribution. The targets are too faint to be detected by BAT or ISGRI alone, but they are easily detected by NuSTAR with minimal exposure times.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BODAGHEE                      ARASH                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR BRINGS INTO FOCUS FAINT AGN CANDIDATES FROM THE SWIFT-INTEGRAL X-RAY SURVEY                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J20469p6512                                   @sz∞â†'R@PLSéÛMj@Y(¬¯7¥¢@+‹]càf@:åPH@ÏÆ/?r‘£@ÏÆB[‰õø60501052002@›ù =p£◊@›l€ì›óˆ@ﬂèv!ñRΩ@ﬂê6!ñRΩSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ò5␀a␀␀ÊÙHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀xNuSTAR observations are requested for three unclassified hard X-ray sources that were detected by merging mosaic images from the Swift-BAT and INTEGRAL-ISGRI surveys. We know little about these sources other than a crude X-ray position and flux, but they are good candidates for being high-redshift blazars or Compton-thick AGN that appear missing from those surveys. Our scientific objectives are: to obtain an improved X-ray position that will narrow down the list of possible counterparts in the optical/infrared bands; and create X-ray spectra to combine with the OIR data to generate their broadband spectral energy distribution. The targets are too faint to be detected by BAT or ISGRI alone, but they are easily detected by NuSTAR with minimal exposure times.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BODAGHEE                      ARASH                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR BRINGS INTO FOCUS FAINT AGN CANDIDATES FROM THE SWIFT-INTEGRAL X-RAY SURVEY                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cyg_X2                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÆB[‰õø@ÏÆBÕ´∏180511301003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’òﬁ†␀␀Â}Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀SThe best laboratory for strong-field gravity is the inner 100s of kilometers around compact objects in low-mass X-ray binaries. The X-ray emission varies rapidly, notably in quasi-periodic oscillations (QPOs), which may be produced by general relativistic effects. Low-frequency QPOs from black holes and neutron stars share similar temporal and spectral properties, indicating that they have similar emission mechanisms, even though neutron stars have a surface and strong magnetic field in addition to general relativistic effects. With these proposed observations, we will use phase-resolved spectroscopy of two types of low-frequency QPOs to constrain and compare the emission mechanisms, to better understand accretion flows in the strong gravity regime.                                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Stevens                       Abigail                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       COMPARING EMISSION MECHANISMS OF LOW-FREQUENCY QPOS IN BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X2                                            @ta‡qv@C'Ä4m≈÷@U«Ø∑È¿&Ö° ¿É@?ˆö‘,<ü@ÏÆBÕ´∏1@ÏÆSî»)¯80511301004@»ŒrGE9@…©é_o“@÷èx⁄Q@÷í8⁄QSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ó#uÛ␀␀Â}Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀SThe best laboratory for strong-field gravity is the inner 100s of kilometers around compact objects in low-mass X-ray binaries. The X-ray emission varies rapidly, notably in quasi-periodic oscillations (QPOs), which may be produced by general relativistic effects. Low-frequency QPOs from black holes and neutron stars share similar temporal and spectral properties, indicating that they have similar emission mechanisms, even though neutron stars have a surface and strong magnetic field in addition to general relativistic effects. With these proposed observations, we will use phase-resolved spectroscopy of two types of low-frequency QPOs to constrain and compare the emission mechanisms, to better understand accretion flows in the strong gravity regime.                                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Stevens                       Abigail                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       COMPARING EMISSION MECHANISMS OF LOW-FREQUENCY QPOS IN BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Bkgd_19255                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÆSî»)¯@ÏÆTxVb‹10501012001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ñ˘÷H␀␀Â}Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                 