pportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÆrÕ´∏1@ÏÆt#Ü30502008005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ñ ÊñÚ␀␀ÂáHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀0GRS 1915+105 is a black hole binary displaying unique variability, strong winds, jets, and BH spin. The source was extensively monitored with RXTE; NICER provides improved constraints on the disk and lines owing to its soft X-ray sensitivity and resolution. Even after two decades of monitoring, critical questions remain unanswered. What is the relationship between winds, jets, and rapid variability in this system? NICER is the only mission capable of building a legacy catalog of observations to answer this question, but a broader sample is required. We propose 35 weekly exposures of 2.7 ks in Cycle 1, enabling the first measurement of intra-state wind variability, a more nuanced view of accretion/ejection physics around black holes, and a legacy archive of variability in GRS 1915+105.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       A NICER CATALOG OF WINDS AND VARIABILITY IN GRS 1915+105                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @rëhr∞!@%÷4ÍK@Fôˇ.HËßø•Lò_ˆî@uÑ-w1è≈@ÏÆt#Ü@ÏÆÜÍM30502008006@◊*◊&¡æ@◊2 W®@Ÿäòæﬂ§@Ÿçòæﬂ§SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ïÕû'␀␀ÂáHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀0GRS 1915+105 is a black hole binary displaying unique variability, strong winds, jets, and BH spin. The source was extensively monitored with RXTE; NICER provides improved constraints on the disk and lines owing to its soft X-ray sensitivity and resolution. Even after two decades of monitoring, critical questions remain unanswered. What is the relationship between winds, jets, and rapid variability in this system? NICER is the only mission capable of building a legacy catalog of observations to answer this question, but a broader sample is required. We propose 35 weekly exposures of 2.7 ks in Cycle 1, enabling the first measurement of intra-state wind variability, a more nuanced view of accretion/ejection physics around black holes, and a legacy archive of variability in GRS 1915+105.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       A NICER CATALOG OF WINDS AND VARIABILITY IN GRS 1915+105                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429BL_Lac                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÆÜÍM@ÏÆáxVb‹60501024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ï¢Ã
ü␀␀ÊÛHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose to monitor BL Lacertae with NuSTAR at 3-70 keV for 350 ks over 5 consecutive days during the 2019/09/11-2019/10/07 period when the source will be in the FOV of TESS. The hard X-ray and optical fluxes are variable on timescales of hours. The observations will allow us to determine delays between variations at different energies, relative locations of emission regions, and parameters of turbulence involved in photon production at different frequencies. We propose also 50 measurements of 1.5 ks each of the source with the Swift XRT and UVOT during these 5 days to sample variability continuously from hard X-ray to optical bands and to compare mechanisms and origins of hard and soft X-ray emission.                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    JORSTAD                       SVETLANA                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       HARD X-RAY COMPONENT OF BL LACERTAE                                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BL_Lac                                            @t™*ô0æ@E"ïÈ·∞ä@WcÒA \¿$—Ç©ì·@@øo“ÚÂ@ÏÆáxVb‹@ÏØ(èFj60501024002A	ÁPÍOA	Ω´6zA¿Ú‰¬¯8Aƒ≤‰¬¯8SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’îêW;␀␀ÊÛHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose to monitor BL Lacertae with NuSTAR at 3-70 keV for 350 ks over 5 consecutive days during the 2019/09/11-2019/10/07 period when the source will be in the FOV of TESS. The hard X-ray and optical fluxes are variable on timescales of hours. The observations will allow us to determine delays between variations at different energies, relative locations of emission regions, and parameters of turbulence involved in photon production at different frequencies. We propose also 50 measurements of 1.5 ks each of the source with the Swift XRT and UVOT during these 5 days to sample variability continuously from hard X-ray to optical bands and to compare mechanisms and origins of hard and soft X-ray emission.                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    JORSTAD                       SVETLANA                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       HARD X-RAY COMPONENT OF BL LACERTAE                                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              TESS Swift                                                                                          ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏØ(èFj@ÏØ(xVb‹80502335003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’î2°ê␀␀ÊuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀§Understanding the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge in black hole systems. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both components are very strong for Cyg X-1, but the disk is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. This proposal is a continuation of a previously approved NuSTAR Cycle 3 program on Cyg X-1 using the uniquely powerful capability of combined continuum and reflection modeling provided with both instruments together. By chance, our first program covered the soft state only. We propose to expand and target hard states and request 3x20 ks NuSTAR (3x4 ks NICER) observations of Cyg X-1 for triggered monitoring of hard states.        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    STEINER                       JAMES                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A CONTINUED NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT REFLECTION AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @rπ¡Ω•@Añ1¯†ê.@QÀí:)«z@	RàŒp:˚@vI˜ŒŸá@ÏØ(xVb‹@ÏØ6ÍM80502335004@∆àØlãCñ@«]ŒÌ(ç@÷(#;dZ@÷)#;dZSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ì?n]L␀␀ÊuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀§Understanding the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge in black hole systems. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both components are very strong for Cyg X-1, but the disk is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. This proposal is a continuation of a previously approved NuSTAR Cycle 3 program on Cyg X-1 using the uniquely powerful capability of combined continuum and reflection modeling provided with both instruments together. By chance, our first program covered the soft state only. We propose to expand and target hard states and request 3x20 ks NuSTAR (3x4 ks NICER) observations of Cyg X-1 for triggered monitoring of hard states.        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    STEINER                       JAMES                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A CONTINUED NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT REFLECTION AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429EXO_1846m031                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏØ6ÍM@ÏØ7xVb‹80502303005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ì∂lx␀␀ÊÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    7I¡We propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, JVLA/ATCA, NICER, Swift, Gemini) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds during and after the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next 2-3 months. Our spectral and timing constraints will provide precise probes of the accretion geometry and accretion/ejection physics.                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       An Integrated Approach to Winds, Jets, and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316EXO_1846m031                                      @q•S&¬¿√a@N•@=Àqué!ñøÊ›/üæw@tÙÎÖ∏R@ÏØ7xVb‹@ÏØJèFj80502303006@–xÈ—N;Õ@–√7ökP±@ÿ	‘”ZÖà@ÿT”ZÖàSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ífï␀␀ÊÌHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  7I¡We propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, JVLA/ATCA, NICER, Swift, Gemini) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds during and after the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next 2-3 months. Our spectral and timing constraints will provide precise probes of the accretion geometry and accretion/ejection physics.                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       An Integrated Approach to Winds, Jets, and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀