al and pulsed evolution of XTE J1810-197, measuring its surface thermal hot spots and magnetic field configuration, to compare with the original outburst at a similar epoch.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    GOTTHELF                      ERIC                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING THE 2018 X-RAY OUTBURST OF XTE J1810-197                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429Mrk_622                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∞A±9Ò@Ï∞BÍM60561042001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’éçøÜ£␀␀Ê˚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_622                                           @^~BZÓc @CÉW>´6z@f≥Õ5®Xy@>´"–Â`B@eª;dZ¨@Ï∞BÍM@Ï∞ièFj60561042002@ÍtÚ-‡@ÍKcÀ)_@ÏLøÀí:*@ÏLﬂÀí:*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’é%º}Í␀␀Ê˚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                     XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20200429Sco_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∞ièFj@Ï∞j?r‘£30502010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’çBøË␀␀ÁNHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀æNeutron stars (NSs) are the most compact objects with a surface in the Universe. The elusive equation of state (EoS) sets the radius for a NS of a given mass, therefore determining the mass and radius of a NS can, in-turn, be used to place limits on the EoS. Sco X-1 is a NS low-mass X-ray binary and the brightest X-ray source in the night sky. X-ray spectroscopic studies can place limits of the radial extent of the NS and track changes within the innermost accretion region. We request 60 ks of exposure of Sco X-1 broken up into 3x20 ks observations. The previously determined limits on NS mass and inclination of this system from optical and radio studies makes Sco X-1 a prime target for demonstrating the utility of reflection studies to provide limits on the EoS.                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    LUDLAM                        JON                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       AN ENHANCED NUSTAR VIEW OF SCORPIUS X-1                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Sco_X1                                            @nûGE8Ô5¿/Y¿ÎÌ˙D@vnZÓcä@8–|Ñµ›@uH≈Åo␀@Ï∞j?r‘£@Ï∞yèFj30502010002@áﬁ…„Ω@âÁ‚Ç@∑Ä@”ä∆i≠Bƒ@”çFi≠BƒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’åÜªZ•␀␀ÁNHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀æNeutron stars (NSs) are the most compact objects with a surface in the Universe. The elusive equation of state (EoS) sets the radius for a NS of a given mass, therefore determining the mass and radius of a NS can, in-turn, be used to place limits on the EoS. Sco X-1 is a NS low-mass X-ray binary and the brightest X-ray source in the night sky. X-ray spectroscopic studies can place limits of the radial extent of the NS and track changes within the innermost accretion region. We request 60 ks of exposure of Sco X-1 broken up into 3x20 ks observations. The previously determined limits on NS mass and inclination of this system from optical and radio studies makes Sco X-1 a prime target for demonstrating the utility of reflection studies to provide limits on the EoS.                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    LUDLAM                        JON                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       AN ENHANCED NUSTAR VIEW OF SCORPIUS X-1                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(1/2) / data gaps 1295s                                                                                                 archived  20200429TXS_0506p056                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∞yèFj@Ï∞z[‰õø60502053003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’å[¯j1␀␀ÁπHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ùOn Sept 22, 2017, the IceCube observatory reported the detection of the high-energy astrophysical neutrino candidate event IC170922A. A large multiwavelength observation campaign of the neutrino location involving led to the detection of enhanced broadband EM emission from the blazar TXS 0506+056. This detection represents the first evidence for an electromagnetic counterpart to the high-energy astrophysical neutrinos detected by IceCube. The soft-to-hard X-ray emission from the source provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the source SED. We here propose an ambitious observational program of TXS 0506+056 involving NuSTAR, Swift, VERITAS and Fermi.                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    SANTANDER                     J_MARCOS                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PROBING THE HADRONIC EMISSION FROM THE CANDIDATE NEUTRINO BLAZAR TXS 0506+056                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_0506p056                                      @SXuˆ˝!ˇ@Ë¡T…Ö@hhô0æÌ¿3”9¿ÎÌ˙@clàŒp:˚@Ï∞z[‰õø@Ï∞ëÕ´∏160502053004@ﬂ:tÚ{≤ˇ@ﬂz·GÆ@‡∆_B√…Ô@‡«B√…ÔSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’å-„Ô␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ùOn Sept 22, 2017, the IceCube observatory reported the detection of the high-energy astrophysical neutrino candidate event IC170922A. A large multiwavelength observation campaign of the neutrino location involving led to the detection of enhanced broadband EM emission from the blazar TXS 0506+056. This detection represents the first evidence for an electromagnetic counterpart to the high-energy astrophysical neutrinos detected by IceCube. The soft-to-hard X-ray emission from the source provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the source SED. We here propose an ambitious observational program of TXS 0506+056 involving NuSTAR, Swift, VERITAS and Fermi.                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    SANTANDER                     J_MARCOS                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PROBING THE HADRONIC EMISSION FROM THE CANDIDATE NEUTRINO BLAZAR TXS 0506+056                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              VERITAS                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS01                                    @pMæv»¥9@PsRT`™e@WÙ¬¯7¥¢@@≈Ì(åÁ@sÛZÓcä@Ï∞ëÕ´∏1@Ï∞í±9Ò60511001001@t‹\ë—N<@tªkP∞Ú|@v&ÃÃÃÃÕ@v&ÃÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ã›ïÑ␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS01                                    @pM‹∆?@Puqué"@WˆŸá+@@≈Åo␀i@s„ı¬è\)@Ï∞í±9Ò@Ï∞Ω?r‘£60511001002@Ú;)ÑM;@Ú‹Ùá¸π@Û}¨ïÈ·±@Û}‹ïÈ·±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ãxQÎÖ␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429XTE_J1739m285                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∞Ω?r‘£@Ï∞æ#Ü90501343001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ãEOC„␀␀Â¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                    