R by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS03                                    @pJ@N§©@Pu8Ô4÷°@W¯Lò_@@—A [¿@s√ïÈ·∞ä@Ï±	#Ü@Ï±3#Ü60511003002@œÏÑµ‹∆@∞±M“Ò™@ÒÎ»!-w2@ÒÏ!-w2SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’â$ô8Ç␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀4␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀housekeeping data gap                                                                                                   archived  20200429MCGp10m17m061                                     @f€ã¨q@MzÕûÉ‰&@aG@N§©@Lp£◊
=@lä¡Ω•@Ï±3#Ü@Ï±3î»)¯60161452001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’àÛ›∞␀␀Â¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCGp10m17m061                                     @f6‚Î@M|ï*ô1@aGT…Öo@L&¡Ω•@lÖÈ·∞â†@Ï±3î»)¯@Ï±BxVb‹60161452002@’ª[‡ﬁ“â@’ï©˘rG@◊9ê«‚ÇA@◊9–«‚ÇASCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’àØP␀␀Â¢Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mkn_848                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï±BxVb‹@Ï±BÍM60501033001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’àÜ£‹␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀4Galactic outflows are now commonly observed in starburst and active galactic nuclei (AGN) host galaxies. Yet, there is no clear consensus on their physical driving mechanism(s), and whether the observed outflows are powered by stellar winds and supernovae explosions or by AGN winds. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the starburst Mkn 848, a merging system at z = 0.04 showing powerful kpc-scale multi-phase outflows. The properties of these outflows are compatible with the presence of winds driven by AGN, but available observations are strongly limited by absorption and prevent a direct identification of AGN emission. Our proposal is designed to study the 3-30 keV emission of this target, by looking for a direct evidence of the presence of an highly-obscured AGN.                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PERNA                         MICHELE                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       CLASH IN MKN 848. AGN- OR STARBURST-DRIVEN OUTFLOWS?                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mkn_848                                           @l∞ÎÌ˙C˛@EZÎC,•@Qù“Ò©˚Á@LL!-w2@rh	‘ï@Ï±BÍM@Ï±Ä[‰õø60501033002@Ù;≥∑Ä4n@ÙG∏QÎÖ@ı¶a|⁄@ı•ra|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’áÛKx¸␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀4Galactic outflows are now commonly observed in starburst and active galactic nuclei (AGN) host galaxies. Yet, there is no clear consensus on their physical driving mechanism(s), and whether the observed outflows are powered by stellar winds and supernovae explosions or by AGN winds. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the starburst Mkn 848, a merging system at z = 0.04 showing powerful kpc-scale multi-phase outflows. The properties of these outflows are compatible with the presence of winds driven by AGN, but available observations are strongly limited by absorption and prevent a direct identification of AGN 