       Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ARP151                                            @enwökP±@K0ç∏∫«@bc(åÁ∞@M2#9¿ÎÓ@kr¯7¥¢4@Ï±ÄÍM@Ï±è[‰õø60160430002@◊°oiD@÷ÔÓ_o“@ÿ¶ûlÙ!@ÿßlÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’áx!_Ù␀␀Â¢Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_09104p4109                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï±è[‰õø@Ï±èÕ´∏160502055001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’áPr␀␀ÁËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀©We propose three 40-ks observations of IRAS 09104+4109, a heavily obscured highly luminous quasar. Our target is uniquely accessible because of its relatively low redshift in comparison to similarly luminous obscured quasars found only at higher redshift. With the proposed observations we will be able to 1) characterize potential variability above 10 keV, and 2) build up the photon statistics to model its X-ray spectrum with physically motivated models for the obscuring structure usually called the torus. With modeling of the torus applied to the wealth of available infrared data, we will be able to make some of the first direct comparisons of the constraints implied from both of these spectral windows.                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BALOKOVIC                     MISLAV                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       STRUCTURE OF THE OBSCURER IN THE HEAVILY OBSCURED LUMINOUS QUASAR IRAS 09104+4109                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_09104p4109                                   @aO˙C˛\í@Dz@∑Ä4n@fú	‘ï+@E∏∞Ú{≤ˇ@f›$tSéÛ@Ï±èÕ´∏1@Ï±¨ÍM60502055002@„ËjÃÃÃÕ@„«üUõ=@ÂJŸˆîF@ÂKˆîFSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÜÚ“␀␀ÁËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀©We propose three 40-ks observations of IRAS 09104+4109, a heavily obscured highly luminous quasar. Our target is uniquely accessible because of its relatively low redshift in comparison to similarly luminous obscured quasars found only at higher redshift. With the proposed observations we will be able to 1) characterize potential variability above 10 keV, and 2) build up the photon statistics to model its X-ray spectrum with physically motivated models for the obscuring structure usually called the torus. With modeling of the torus applied to the wealth of available infrared data, we will be able to make some of the first direct comparisons of the constraints implied from both of these spectral windows.                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BALOKOVIC                     MISLAV                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       STRUCTURE OF THE OBSCURER IN THE HEAVILY OBSCURED LUMINOUS QUASAR IRAS 09104+4109                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO426_G002                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï±¨ÍM@Ï±≠±9Ò60561040001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ü√ ˛›␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO426_G002                                       @W˝◊€Ùá˝¿@Hé@m˚6zêó¿3õCïÅ%@dïb∂Æ}@Ï±≠±9Ò@Ï±‘ÍM60561040002@Èù∏§®¡U@Èp%≈÷8Ü@Îj3?|Ìë@Îm?|ÌëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÜYi␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_7552                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï±‘ÍM@Ï±’±9Ò50501001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ü#uÛ␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀õLuminous infrared galaxies (LIRGs; LIR > 10^11 Lsol) have been shown to have significant deficits of X-ray emission compared with that expected from the local LX-SFR correlation. The most likely scenarios are that the XRB population is either (1) missing as a result of the populations being relatively young and/or of high metallicity - factors that influence the formation rates - or (2) buried under nearly Compton-thick obscuring column densities in these high-intensity star-forming regions. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the nearest LIRG that definitively does not contain an AGN, NGC 7552, and decisively determine whether a buried population of XRBs is present.                                                                                                                               Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    LEHMER                        BRET                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       BURIED ALIVE? EXPLAINING THE LARGE DEFICIT OF HMXBS IN THE CIRCUMNUCLEAR STARBURST RING OF NGC 7552 AND LIRGS           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7552                                          @u—!-w1ê¿ENêˇó$t@u¡^ûö¿PA+”√a@qoUõ=»@Ï±’±9Ò@Ï≤Xî»)¯50501001002AOû%zxlA)ÙÛMjAÛÌa|⁄Aı%a|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÖCQän␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀õLuminous infrared galaxies (LIRGs; LIR > 10^11 Lsol) have been shown to have significant deficits of X-ray emission compared with that expected from the local LX-SFR correlation. The most likely scenarios are that the XRB population is either (1) missing as a result of the populations being relatively young and/or of high metallicity - factors that influence the formation rates - or (2) buried under nearly Compton-thick obscuring column densities in these high-intensity star-forming regions. We propose to observe with NuSTAR the nearest LIRG that definitively does not contain an AGN, NGC 7552, and decisively determine whether a buried population of XRBs is present.                                                                                                                               Normal galaxies       