                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              e-MERLIN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429PSR_J1838m0655                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï≤©±9Ò@Ï≤™èFj30501013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ÉUˇ=“␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀–Of the hundreds of rotation-powered pulsars with detected PWNe, three stand out by their extremely under-luminous nebulae relative to their spin-down power. PSR J1617-5055, PSR J2022+3842, and PSR J1838-0655 display remarkably similar properties, (1) a PWN to PSR flux ratio of <0.1 in the 2-10 keV band, (2) anomalously flat (Gamma < 1) Chandra spectra, (3) highly modulated pulsations (60-100%), and (4) lack of a Fermi pulsed signal. We propose a NuSTAR and XMM broadband X-ray observation of PSR J1838-0655, the brightest in the NuSTAR band. By studying this rare class of pulsars we hope to address their exceptionalism, key to more fully understanding the emission mechanism of pulsars.                                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    GOTTHELF                      ERIC                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       ENERGETIC PULSARS WITH UNDER-LUMINOUS WIND NEBULAE: UNLOCKING THE PULSAR EMISSION MECHANISM                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J1838m0655                                    @qwÕûÉ‰&¿‡uˆ˝!ˇ@9N§®¡U?ôôôôôôö@t`Ø∑È˘@Ï≤™èFj@Ï≤˝xVb‹30501013002@˚ª_ÄùIR@˚ãqêñªô@˝ªï*ô@˝ºï*ôSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Ç´˛{£␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀–Of the hundreds of rotation-powered pulsars with detected PWNe, three stand out by their extremely under-luminous nebulae relative to their spin-down power. PSR J1617-5055, PSR J2022+3842, and PSR J1838-0655 display remarkably similar properties, (1) a PWN to PSR flux ratio of <0.1 in the 2-10 keV band, (2) anomalously flat (Gamma < 1) Chandra spectra, (3) highly modulated pulsations (60-100%), and (4) lack of a Fermi pulsed signal. We propose a NuSTAR and XMM broadband X-ray observation of PSR J1838-0655, the brightest in the NuSTAR band. By studying this rare class of pulsars we hope to address their exceptionalism, key to more fully understanding the emission mechanism of pulsars.                                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    GOTTHELF                      ERIC                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       ENERGETIC PULSARS WITH UNDER-LUMINOUS WIND NEBULAE: UNLOCKING THE PULSAR EMISSION MECHANISM                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429EXO_1846m031                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï≤˝xVb‹@Ï≤˝±9Ò80502303007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ÇkTì'␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    7I¡We propose a large multiwavelength campaign (120 ks Chandra HETGS, NuSTAR, JVLA/ATCA, NICER, Swift, Gemini) on a black hole transient to study the influence of ionized winds on relativistic jets and state transitions. With a reimagined observing strategy based on new results on integrated RMS variability and a decade of radio/X-ray monitoring, we will search for winds during and after the state transition to test their influence on and track their coevolution with the disk and the jet over the next 2-3 months. Our spectral and timing constraints will provide precise probes of the accretion geometry and accretion/ejection physics.                                                                                                                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       J                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       An Integrated Approach to Winds, Jets, and State Transitions                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR        