                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Å∆ZCÇ␀␀Â¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1957p11                                        @rΩ]càeï@'ZQŒ_@IéîFsÅÿ¿"W>´6z@tU≤-V@Ï≥#Ü@Ï≥±9Ò30502007010@”.Ob∂Æ}@”!–ñªò»@’ø34÷°b@’øs4÷°bSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ÅrqÓ␀␀Â¢Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(5/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Swift_J1845d7m0037                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï≥±9Ò@Ï≥ #Ü90501347001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ÅK‰±␀␀Â¢Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                     