al of 100 ks. Detected in gamma-rays, these sources are some of the most powerful of their class, but crucially lack of X-ray measurements. We propose to observe it in the X-ray with NuSTAR, which will allow us to accurately measure the rising part of the inverse Compton (IC) spectrum, expected to be very hard, up to 70 keV. Together with the LAT data, the X-rays will be fundamental to pinpointing the IC peak, determining the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCOTULLI                    LEA                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZQ_J0733p0456                                    @\`õ•„S¯@◊
