(50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk279                                            @j
g8}ø@QS]ÃcÒA@\«S&¬@Gd˜e˝ä€@mûÏø±[@Ï¥,èFj@Ï¥:èFj60160562002@⁄¢–…Öo@⁄pÉ¸π#£@‹ÇÈú‡u˜@‹É©ú‡u˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’|–6ù␀␀ÂÆHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk382                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¥:èFj@Ï¥:î»)¯60501008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’|£]≠R␀␀Á?Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀INSuper-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN ( Edd = 15 to Edd = 427) at z <~ 0.1 by simultaneously observing five of them with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. We use the best sample available, SEAMBHs, which contain objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable. The broad-band observations will allow to shed light on a large number of fundamental issues, such as the behaviour of the cutoff energy, of the reprocessed radiation, of the iron complex and of the soft excess at extreme Eddington ratios.                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       The first systematic study of extreme accretion with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk382                                            @]∏xF‹@CöÓcä	@föcÒA \@<\⁄QŒ@dBÿD–@Ï¥:î»)¯@Ï¥a±9Ò60501008002@Èêm†˘	l@Èf¢xl"h@ÎRzı¬è\@ÎTöı¬è\SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’|3333␀␀Á?Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀INSuper-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN ( Edd = 15 to Edd = 427) at z <~ 0.1 by simultaneously observing five of them with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. We use the best sample available, SEAMBHs, which contain objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable. The broad-band observations will allow to shed light on a large number of fundamental issues, such as the behaviour of the cutoff energy, of the reprocessed radiation, of the iron complex and of the soft excess at extreme Eddington ratios.                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       The first systematic study of extreme accretion with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429AT2019rwd                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¥a±9Ò@Ï¥b±9Ò80502515001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’{¯„é9␀␀ÊÜHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ˆWe propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent NuSTAR discovery of hard X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first hard X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    COPPEJANS                     DEANNE                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF FAST AND BLUE STELLAR EXPLOSIONS                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2019rwd                                         @m\˙¨ŸË@5⁄QŒ@[Ç
¶L/É¿Diﬁ␀—∑@ub∂Æ}Vm@Ï¥b±9Ò@Ï¥êÕ´∏180502515002@Ï√hr∞!@Ïåˇnóç@Ó‰Ùﬁ␀—∑@ÓËﬁ␀—∑SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’{}Í␀¬␀␀ÊÜHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ˆWe propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent NuSTAR discovery of hard X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first hard X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    COPPEJANS                     DEANNE                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF FAST AND BLUE STELLAR EXPLOSIONS                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀ (1/2)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429PKS_2004m447                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¥êÕ´∏1@Ï¥ëî»)¯90501649001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’{>KÂ␀␀Â∑Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of op