¶L0SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’vÕû'␀␀Á!Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_23226m3843                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏµY?r‘£@ÏµYî»)¯80502607001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’vú∫òv␀␀ÁAHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀HpRecent XMM-Newton observations of Seyfert galaxies establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as a poorly explored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn: GSN 069 is most likely a high Eddington-ratio true Seyfert 2 galaxy with no broad line region and no X-ray continuum. NGC 1566, HE1136-2304 and NGC 2617 are rare changing look AGNs with the latter possibly showing absorption by in-falling matter. We propose two 80ks simultaneous XMMNewton/NuSTAR observations accompanied by optical spectroscopy of the next suited AGN outburst(s). They will allow tracing accretion physics, spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime.                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_23226m3843                                   @uıƒ2 W®¿C>_o“ @v3^5?|Ó¿Q ÿD–@rÜb∂Æ}@ÏµYî»)¯@ÏµÖî»)¯80502607002@Í≈3¿6„@Íñ«∏∫«@ÏΩxYJÙÒ@Ï¿π&¡æSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’v/±4␀␀ÁAHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀HpRecent XMM-Newton observations of Seyfert galaxies establish outbursts of radio-quiet AGNs as a poorly explored discovery space for AGN physics with an enormous potential to learn: GSN 069 is most likely a high Eddington-ratio true Seyfert 2 galaxy with no broad line region and no X-ray continuum. NGC 1566, HE1136-2304 and NGC 2617 are rare changing look AGNs with the latter possibly showing absorption by in-falling matter. We propose two 80ks simultaneous XMMNewton/NuSTAR observations accompanied by optical spectroscopy of the next suited AGN outburst(s). They will allow tracing accretion physics, spectral complexity connected with dramatic emission-line changes and possibly elusive tidal disruption events probing an even more extreme accretion regime.                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Schartel                      Norbert                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Outbursts of radio-quiet AGN                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429EXO_1846m031                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏµÖî»)¯@ÏµÜ?r‘£90501350001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’u¸“ﬁ␀␀Â∑Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316EXO_1846m031                                      @q§oiDg¿œﬂ;dZ@=…x‘˝Û∂øÊœAÚ◊s@sƒ/üæw@ÏµÜ?r‘£@Ïµ¶±9Ò90501350002@‚óH±ƒ2 @‚å∑RT`™@ÂS8Ô4◊@ÂTó8Ô4◊SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ua®≈7␀␀Â∑Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra ATCA                                                                                        ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  202004293C_223                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïµ¶±9Ò@ÏµßÕ´∏160501020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’u2π÷H␀␀Á*Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÛA 2001 XMM spectrum of radio-loud AGN 3C 223 revealed that the line-of-sight column density is significantly different from the average global column density. Two qualities make 3C 223 remarkable: the implied obscuration geometry is a Compton-thick (>1.7 x 10^24 cm^-2) ring of gas embedded in a global Compon-thin (1.4 x 10^23 cm^-2) medium, and it is one of the very few radio loud Compton-thick AGN candidates known. We request a joint 45 ks NuSTAR obervation and 40 ks XMM observation to confirm 3C 223 as the first bona fide Compton thick radio galaxy, search for absorption variability between the 2001 observation and proposed dataset, and accurately measure the line-of-sight column density which is unconstrained with the XMM data, requiring NuSTAR coverage.                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    LAMASSA                       STEPHANIE                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       INVESTIGATING THE COMPLEX OBSCURATION AROUND THE COMPTON-THICK QUASAR CANDIDATE 3C 223                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C_223                                            @b }øHÄ@Aˆäqﬁi≠@gáGÆz·@H:úwökQ@eæŸË>B[@ÏµßÕ´∏1@Ïµ î»)¯60501020002@Á≠Kﬂ;dZ@ÁÇd4m≈÷@ÈYñlÙ!@ÈYˆlÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’t‘ì'␀␀Á*Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÛA 2001 XMM spectrum of radio-loud AGN 3C 223 revealed that the line-of-sight column density is significantly different from the average global column density. Two qualities make 3C 223 remarkable: the implied obscuration geometry is a Compton-thick (>1.7 x 10^24 cm^-2) ring of gas embedded in a global Compon-thin (1.4 x 10^23 cm^-2) medium, and it is one of the very few radio loud Compton-thick AGN candidates known. We request a joint 45 ks NuSTAR obervation and 40 ks XMM observation to confirm 3C 223 as the first bona fide Compton thick radio galaxy, search for absorption variability between the 2001 observation and proposed dataset, and accurately measure the line-of-sight column density which is unconstrained with the XMM data, requiring NuSTAR coverage.                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    LAMASSA                       STEPHANIE                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       INVESTIGATING THE COMPLEX OBSCURATION AROUND THE COMPTON-THICK QUASAR CANDIDATE 3C 223                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429BOL_45                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïµ î»)¯@ÏµÀxVb‹30560002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’t•‘√≥␀␀Á$Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀zX-ray binaries (XBs) in nearby galaxies have been studied for decades. However, their nature largely remains illusive, mainly due to the lack of hard X-ray coverage. With the extraordinary sensitivity of NuSTAR in hard X-rays, we propose to observe two luminous hard X-ray sources (Lx>2e38 erg/s) in M31 to complete the survey of such objects. The observations will allow us to differentiate between black-hole XBs in the hard state and luminous neutron star (NS) low-mass XBs (LMXBs) by measuring the spectral shape in hard X-rays. The program is important for understanding the origin and evolution of LMXBs. It also allows us to identify supercritically accreting NSs and provides a valuable test for our recently discovered simple, yet effective, source identification method in the Chandra band.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    LIN                           DACHENG                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR SURVEY OF BRIGHT X-RAY SOURCES IN M31                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BOL_45                                            @$»ßÊö‘@D∆¡Ω•@^/!-w1ê¿5a©˚Álã@␀{ÁlãCñ@ÏµÀxVb‹@ÏµÎî»)¯30560002002@‰Ú
ÁØ∏@‰«òE° ¡@Êgnj~˘€@ÊhÓj~˘€SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’t9,_ì␀␀Á$Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀zX-ray binaries (XBs) in nearby galaxies have been studied for decades. However, their nature largely remains illusive, mainly due to the lack of hard X-ray coverage. With the extraordinary sensitivity of NuSTAR in hard X-rays, we propose to observe two luminous hard X-ray sources (Lx>2e38 erg/s) in M31 to complete the survey of such objects. The observations will allow us to differentiate between black-hole XBs in the hard state and luminous neutron star (NS) low-mass XBs (LMXBs) by measuring the spectral shape in hard X-rays. The program is important for understanding the origin and evolution of LMXBs. It also allows us to identify supercritically accreting NSs and provides a valuable test for our recently discovered simple, yet effective, source identification method in the Chandra band.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    LIN                           DACHENG                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR SURVEY OF BRIGHT X-RAY SOURCES IN M31                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4151                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏµÎî»)¯@ÏµÏxVb‹60502017003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’tHpC␀␀ÁuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4151                                          @f÷çπ@C¥ãCïÅ@ch£n.≤@R∏ŸË>B[@h·ÆÊ1¯°@ÏµÏxVb‹@Ï∂±9Ò60502017004@Â[G“Ò©¸@Â@f9Xb@ÁP‘êñº@ÁPîêñºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’sô˙∞º␀␀ÁuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Cyg_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂±9Ò@Ï∂xVb‹80502335005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’sg°Òó␀␀ÊuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀§Understanding the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge in black hole systems. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both components are very strong for Cyg X-1, but the disk is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. This proposal is a continuation of a previously approved NuSTAR Cycle 3 program on Cyg X-1 using the uniquely powerful capability of combined continuum and reflection modeling provided with both instruments together. By chance, our first program covered the soft state only. We propose to expand and target hard states and request 3x20 ks NuSTAR (3x4 ks NICER) observations of Cyg X-1 for triggered monitoring of hard states.        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    STEINER                       JAMES                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A CONTINUED NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT REFLECTION AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cyg_X1                                            @rπÇ@∑Ä4@Aïóˆ+jË@QÃÍJå@	(Xy=Ÿ@sQ´6zë@Ï∂xVb‹@Ï∂[‰õø80502335006@«D˛Ê1¯°@«Î(4m≈÷@”V!ñRΩ<@”W·ñRΩ<SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’r¶ñÒ¯␀␀ÊuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀§Understanding the corona and disk-coronal interaction is a forefront challenge in black hole systems. While NuSTAR is the leading instrument at probing the high-energy spectral reflection component, NICER provides a complementary view of the thermal accretion disk. Both components are very strong for Cyg X-1, but the disk is cool and out of reach for NuSTAR alone. This proposal is a continuation of a previously approved NuSTAR Cycle 3 program on Cyg X-1 using the uniquely powerful capability of combined continuum and reflection modeling provided with both instruments together. By chance, our first program covered the soft state only. We propose to expand and target hard states and request 3x20 ks NuSTAR (3x4 ks NICER) observations of Cyg X-1 for triggered monitoring of hard states.        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    STEINER                       JAMES                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A CONTINUED NUSTAR & NICER LOOK AT REFLECTION AND THERMAL EMISSION IN CYGNUS X-1                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429MAXI_J0637m430                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂[‰õø@Ï∂î»)¯80502324003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’r|ﬂ#␀␀Á˙Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J0637m430                                    @X…càeîØ¿El®¡T…Ü@on©ì‡ﬂ¿4∫WßÜ¬'@hâkπå~(@Ï∂î»)¯@Ï∂N[‰õø80502324004@âòr∞ ≈@ÅF*ô0æ@Ú}‚≤ïÍ@Ú}í≤ïÍSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’qŒW˝␀␀Á˙Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/10)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429NGC_3227                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂N[‰õø@Ï∂O#Ü80502609001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’qôi␀␀ÁWHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀I“AGN can be obscured by gas streams close to the black hole that shield remote regions from ionising radiation. We witnessed such an event in NGC 5548 and NGC 3783 where 90% of the soft X-rays are blocked by a dense gas stream close to the BLR. Our joint observations with XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and HST/COS showed UV broad absorption lines associated with the X-ray absorption and allowed us to characterise this unique obscuration event completely. We propose to investigate a similar event in another Seyfert 1 using the same instruments. Swift monitoring (98 ks) will be used to find the event, which will be characterized by joint ToO observations with XMM-Newton (150 ks), HST/COS (4 orbits) and NuSTAR (50 ks).                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaastra                       Jelle                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Shining light on obscuring outflows in AGN                                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3227                                          @c}A [¿@3‚ïÈ·∞ä@k™ÕûÉ‰@K§¢3úø@f&ï*ô1@Ï∂O#Ü@Ï∂d?r‘£80502609002@‹éu%F@€Î° ¿É@ﬁ&yøHÃ@ﬁ)˘øHÃSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’qFZCÇ␀␀ÁWHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀I“AGN can be obscured by gas streams close to the black hole that shield remote regions from ionising radiation. We witnessed such an event in NGC 5548 and NGC 3783 where 90% of the soft X-rays are blocked by a dense gas stream close to the BLR. Our joint observations with XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and HST/COS showed UV broad absorption lines associated with the X-ray absorption and allowed us to characterise this unique obscuration event completely. We propose to investigate a similar event in another Seyfert 1 using the same instruments. Swift monitoring (98 ks) will be used to find the event, which will be characterized by joint ToO observations with XMM-Newton (150 ks), HST/COS (4 orbits) and NuSTAR (50 ks).                                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Kaastra                       Jelle                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Shining light on obscuring outflows in AGN                                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM HST                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429Mrk_110                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂d?r‘£@Ï∂d±9Ò60502022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’q∫òv␀␀ÁπHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛We propose to probe the disc-corona system and determine the BH spin of the bright bare AGN Mrk 110. Our ``2 in 1'' spectral analysis method is based on the global energetics of the disc-corona system and accounts for the contribution of relativistic reflection and the coronal extension. It has been successfully tested on the moderate accretion rate bright bare AGN, Ark120, where for the first time we tightly constrained its BH spin, and revealed that the corona radius decreases with increasing flux. Here, we propose to apply this method to Mrk 110 in order to probe the corona-disc system at higher accretion rate (~0.3--0.4) and a much lower reverberation BH mass (2.0x10^7 M_odot), thanks to two simultaneous NuSTAR (2x80ks) and XMM-Newton (2x43ks) observations separated by a few months.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PORQUET                       DELPHINE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A BROAD BAND VIEW OF THE DISC-CORONA SYSTEM OF MRK 110                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_110                                           @a™1&Èx’@J( ƒõ•„@dùzxl"h@F~êˇó$@d∆u%F
