y=Ÿ@ˆ∞
y=ŸSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’pî√≤¢␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˛We propose to probe the disc-corona system and determine the BH spin of the bright bare AGN Mrk 110. Our ``2 in 1'' spectral analysis method is based on the global energetics of the disc-corona system and accounts for the contribution of relativistic reflection and the coronal extension. It has been successfully tested on the moderate accretion rate bright bare AGN, Ark120, where for the first time we tightly constrained its BH spin, and revealed that the corona radius decreases with increasing flux. Here, we propose to apply this method to Mrk 110 in order to probe the corona-disc system at higher accretion rate (~0.3--0.4) and a much lower reverberation BH mass (2.0x10^7 M_odot), thanks to two simultaneous NuSTAR (2x80ks) and XMM-Newton (2x43ks) observations separated by a few months.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PORQUET                       DELPHINE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A BROAD BAND VIEW OF THE DISC-CORONA SYSTEM OF MRK 110                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429UGC12282                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂°?r‘£@Ï∂¢èFj60160812001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’p]|∆ª␀␀ÂøHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC12282                                          @uã.HËß@Dr√…ÓÀ˚@Y3–|Ñµ›¿1&fffff@t’êˇó$t@Ï∂¢èFj@Ï∂¥±9Ò60160812002@€‰iS&¬@€´Ü($x@›Æ±ñRΩ<@›Æ±ñRΩ<SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’pÑ]␀␀ÂøHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_7793_P13                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂¥±9Ò@Ï∂µxVb‹50401003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’oÎTì'␀␀Á2Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀@The study of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULXs) has been revolutionized by the detection of pulsations from three objects, indicating that they contain neutron stars that radiate apparent luminosities of up to 500 L_Edd. They therefore pose an immense challenge to our understanding of accretion physics. In order to study the soft emission from these objects, we have been approved a 60 ks eROSITA observation of the pulsating ULX NGC 7793-P13. We ask for a simultaneous 40 ks NuSTAR observation, with the aim of constraining the spectral and timing behavior of the source and providing crucial cross-calibration information.                                                                                                                                                                              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wilms                         Joern                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       SIMULTANEOUS NUSTAR-EROSITA OBSERVATIONS OF NGC 7793-P13                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7793_P13                                      @vw@N§®¡¿@U´üUõ=@gRT`™e¿S:$›/†@rõ±[W>´@Ï∂µxVb‹@Ï∂‘ÍM50401003002@Âœ˘rGE@‰˜»u%F@Êüä˝!ˇ.@Ê†Í˝!ˇ.SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’oèC„-␀␀Á2Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀@The study of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULXs) has been revolutionized by the detection of pulsations from three objects, indicating that they contain neutron stars that radiate apparent luminosities of up to 500 L_Edd. They therefore pose an immense challenge to our understanding of accretion physics. In order to study the soft emission from these objects, we have been approved a 60 ks eROSITA observation of the pulsating ULX NGC 7793-P13. We ask for a simultaneous 40 ks NuSTAR observation, with the aim of constraining the spectral and timing behavior of the source and providing crucial cross-calibration information.                                                                                                                                                                              Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Wilms                         Joern                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       SIMULTANEOUS NUSTAR-EROSITA OBSERVATIONS OF NGC 7793-P13                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              SRG                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Maffei_1                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∂‘ÍM@Ï∂’ÍM50401005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’oZˆÖ␀␀ÁCHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀≤As NuSTAR enters its sixth year of operations, it has yet to perform a deep observation of an elliptical galaxy to study the low-mass X-ray binary (LMXB) population associated with older stellar ages. Maffei 1 is the nearest giant elliptical galaxy at 3.4 Mpc and as such provides a unique environment to investigate the accreting black hole (BH) and neutron star (NS) populations in the sensitive E>10 keV bandpass. Due to its proximity, it is the only elliptical where the resolved X-ray binary (XRB) population can be studied at hard X-ray energies until a next generation hard X-ray telescope is flown. We request a 200 ks NuSTAR observation of the giant elliptical galaxy Maffei 1 to study the formation channels of NS and BH populations both in globular cluster (GC) and field environments.    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Vulic                         Neven                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE GIANT ELLIPTICAL GALAXY HIDING IN OUR BACKYARD: THE RESOLVED X-RAY BINARY POPULATION IN MAFFEI 1                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Maffei_1                                          @Cîºj~˘€@M“u%F@`˜Ø∑È˘øÊ°a‰˜e˛@D2úwökQ@Ï∂’ÍM@Ï∑=#Ü50401005002Aú•„TAuÆıY≥–AÀj~˘€AÀWj~˘€SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’n¢R≠≥␀␀ÁCHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀≤As NuSTAR enters its sixth year of operations, it has yet to perform a deep observation of an elliptical galaxy to study the low-mass X-ray binary (LMXB) population associated with older stellar ages. Maffei 1 is the nearest giant elliptical galaxy at 3.4 Mpc and as such provides a unique environment to investigate the accreting black hole (BH) and neutron star (NS) populations in the sensitive E>10 keV bandpass. Due to its proximity, it is the only elliptical where the resolved X-ray binary (XRB) population can be studied at hard X-ray energies until a next generation hard X-ray telescope is flown. We request a 200 ks NuSTAR observation of the giant elliptical galaxy Maffei 1 to study the formation channels of NS and BH populations both in globular cluster (GC) and field environments.    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Vulic                         Neven                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       THE GIANT ELLIPTICAL GALAXY HIDING IN OUR BACKYARD: THE RESOLVED X-RAY BINARY POPULATION IN MAFFEI 1                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_F12397p3333                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∑=#Ü@Ï∑=ÍM60501007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’nY‚jÛ␀␀ÁEHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀INSuper-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN ( Edd = 15 to Edd = 427) at z <~ 0.1 by simultaneously observing five of them with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. We use the best sample available, SEAMBHs, which contain objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable. The broad-band observations will allow to shed light on a large number of fundamental issues, such as the behaviour of the cutoff energy, of the reprocessed radiation, of the iron complex and of the soft excess at extreme Eddington ratios.                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       The first systematic study of extreme accretion with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_F12397p3333                                  @g”Êö‘,@@§Z¨1@a©É{J#:@TÿcÒA \@hìiDg8@Ï∑=ÍM@Ï∑aÕ´∏160501007002@Á»ßT…Ö@ÁöcÇ©ì@ÈqOÑM@ÈoÀOÑMSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’mÚ°ê␀␀ÁEHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀INSuper-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN ( Edd = 15 to Edd = 427) at z <~ 0.1 by simultaneously observing five of them with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR. We use the best sample available, SEAMBHs, which contain objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable. The broad-band observations will allow to shed light on a large number of fundamental issues, such as the behaviour of the cutoff energy, of the reprocessed radiation, of the iron complex and of the soft excess at extreme Eddington ratios.                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       The first systematic study of extreme accretion with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASS_J104222p164115                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∑aÕ´∏1@Ï∑b?r‘£60501032001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’m¡Ñ\ä␀␀Á3Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀1Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a modest, coordinated X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM (25ks) and NuSTAR (50ks). These exposures will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of this system, and to constrain the geometry of the absorber itself.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    WALTON                        DOMINIC                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       IS J1042+1641 THE FIRST EXAMPLE OF A GRAVITATIONALLY LENSED, COMPTON-THICK QUASAR?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASS_J104222p164115                              @dÌ˙C˛]@0µF
¶L0@l#‡ﬁ“â@M!£n.±ƒ@f'N;Õ5®@Ï∑b?r‘£@Ï∑á±9Ò60501032002@Ë˛ûí£S@ËŒ5÷8ÜY@Í√≠úæ‡@Íƒmúæ‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’mX◊kU␀␀Á3Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀1Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a modest, coordinated X-ray observation of this remarkable source with XMM (25ks) and NuSTAR (50ks). These exposures will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of this system, and to constrain the geometry of the absorber itself.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    WALTON                        DOMINIC                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       IS J1042+1641 THE FIRST EXAMPLE OF A GRAVITATIONALLY LENSED, COMPTON-THICK QUASAR?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MAXI_J0637m430                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∑á±9Ò@Ï∑àxVb‹80502324005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’m'∫7_␀␀Á˙Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J0637m430                                    @X…m\˙¨⁄¿Em+”√a@onÀ˚µt¿4∫åLò_@iÒïÅ$›@Ï∑àxVb‹@Ï∑™?r‘£80502324006@ÁæA‡ﬁ“â@Á£é¢3ú@È‰–«À)@ÈÊP«À)SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’l¥ßA␀␀Á˙Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/10) ToO                                                                                                              archived  20200429RXJ0209d6m7427                                    @@;P∞Ú{≥¿RùoiDg@rÅzxl"h¿D¡ıY≥–}@p˙*ô0æ@Ï∑™?r‘£@Ï∑™Õ´∏190502352001@~e+”√a@}ÊnóçOﬂ@Ä®␀␀␀␀␀@Ä®␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’l|˜FÏ␀␀Â«Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429RXJ0209d6m7427                                    @@:üæv»¥¿RûúwökQ@rÅò_ˆî¿D¿À)^û@p˚ÃÃÃÃÕ@Ï∑™Õ´∏1@Ï∑∏ÍM90502352002@’Ï0Z¨@’‹'üUõ=@ÿyº¢3ú@ÿz|¢3úSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’l$h¨Ò␀␀Â«Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO CVZ                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NGC_55_POS1                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∑∏ÍM@Ï∑π[‰õø50510001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’kˆ`∂␀␀Á5Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ôWith NuSTAR, it is possible for the first time to determine the compact object type and accretion state of large samples of X-ray binaries (XRBs) in nearby galaxies. This classification, based on hard X-ray luminosity and hardness ratios, can provide valuable insights into how XRB properties vary with variables such as metallicity (Z), age, and specific star formation rate (sSFR). Vulic et al. (2018) classified 90 XRBs in nearby galaxies observed by NuSTAR, but the sample contains few galaxies with low-Z or low sSFR. Furthermore, variability studies of XRB populations have been limited to M31. We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of the NGC 55 to expand studies of the compact objects, accretion states, and variability of XRBs to lower luminosities in a low-Z galaxy.                    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    FORNASINI                     FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A NUSTAR-XMM SURVEY OF NGC 55: A HOLISTIC VIEW OF A LOW METALLICITY X-RAY BINARY POPULATION                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_55_POS1                                       @üæv»¥¿Cõ∂E° ¡@tÃw1è≈¿RÊ¨1'@r£ÛMj@Ï∑π[‰õø@Ï∑›î»)¯50510001002@Èƒ:&ÄùI@Èöªò«„@Îô»eîØO@ÎöHeîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’kîz·H␀␀Á5Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ôWith NuSTAR, it is possible for the first time to determine the compact object type and accretion state of large samples of X-ray binaries (XRBs) in nearby galaxies. This classification, based on hard X-ray luminosity and hardness ratios, can provide valuable insights into how XRB properties vary with variables such as metallicity (Z), age, and specific star formation rate (sSFR). Vulic et al. (2018) classified 90 XRBs in nearby galaxies observed by NuSTAR, but the sample contains few galaxies with low-Z or low sSFR. Furthermore, variability studies of XRB populations have been limited to M31. We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of the NGC 55 to expand studies of the compact objects, accretion states, and variability of XRBs to lower luminosities in a low-Z galaxy.                    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    FORNASINI                     FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A NUSTAR-XMM SURVEY OF NGC 55: A HOLISTIC VIEW OF A LOW METALLICITY X-RAY BINARY POPULATION                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MGC_07_03_007                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∑›î»)¯@Ï∑›ÍM60561039001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ke‘√≥␀␀Á5Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MGC_07_03_007                                     @0`ÿD–©¿EÜ¬&Äù@r[’œ™Õü¿R¢ WßÜ¬@r⁄QŒ@Ï∑›ÍM@Ï∏èFj60561039002@Í∂±Ì(åÁ@Íà¢ô0æ@Ïò˙^5?}@Ïö:^5?}SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’k}àÍ␀␀Á5Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429TXS_0506p056                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∏èFj@Ï∏Õ´∏160502053005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’jÿE»°␀␀ÁπHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ùOn Sept 22, 2017, the IceCube observatory reported the detection of the high-energy astrophysical neutrino candidate event IC170922A. A large multiwavelength observation campaign of the neutrino location involving led to the detection of enhanced broadband EM emission from the blazar TXS 0506+056. This detection represents the first evidence for an electromagnetic counterpart to the high-energy astrophysical neutrinos detected by IceCube. The soft-to-hard X-ray emission from the source provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the source SED. We here propose an ambitious observational program of TXS 0506+056 involving NuSTAR, Swift, VERITAS and Fermi.                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    SANTANDER                     J_MARCOS                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PROBING THE HADRONIC EMISSION FROM THE CANDIDATE NEUTRINO BLAZAR TXS 0506+056                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_0506p056                                      @SX„º”ZÜ@œAÚ◊s@hip£◊
=¿3’*ô0æ@j•ú‡v@Ï∏Õ´∏1@Ï∏[‰õø60502053006@”Ò.Öáìﬁ@” å~($@’q£n/@’t£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’jê»?∑␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ùOn Sept 22, 2017, the IceCube observatory reported the detection of the high-energy astrophysical neutrino candidate event IC170922A. A large multiwavelength observation campaign of the neutrino location involving led to the detection of enhanced broadband EM emission from the blazar TXS 0506+056. This detection represents the first evidence for an electromagnetic counterpart to the high-energy astrophysical neutrinos detected by IceCube. The soft-to-hard X-ray emission from the source provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the source SED. We here propose an ambitious observational program of TXS 0506+056 involving NuSTAR, Swift, VERITAS and Fermi.                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    SANTANDER                     J_MARCOS                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PROBING THE HADRONIC EMISSION FROM THE CANDIDATE NEUTRINO BLAZAR TXS 0506+056                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift VERITAS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429CGCG_475_040                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∏[‰õø@Ï∏xVb‹60561047001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’jcé8‰␀␀Á=Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CGCG_475_040                                      @uØ␀h€ã¨@6™ÕûÉ‰&@W[úæﬂ§¿AX‚e,@s‚J#9¿Ï@Ï∏xVb‹@Ï∏;[‰õø60561047002@Î<eÕ5®X@Î!-w2@Ì6M≤-V@Ì7ç≤-VSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’i˝πu1␀␀Á=Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004294U_1957p11                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∏;[‰õø@Ï∏;ÍM30502007011␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’i œX␀␀Â«Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1957p11                                        @rΩêˇó$t@'ZQŒ_@Ièb∂Æ}V¿"\π#¢úx@r⁄é!ñRΩ@Ï∏;ÍM@Ï∏J#Ü30502007012@‘å~($@”Û©ÎÖ∏@÷…Öo@÷…ÖoSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’inEs␀␀Â«Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˙4U 1957+11 is one of the few persistently active black hole candidates. Although we do not know its mass, distance, or inclination, the fact that it is apparently always in a thermal-dominated state with extremely high disk temperature, yet very low normalization, suggests a very rapidly rotating black hole. Furthermore, it has an extremely low column, allowing an extremely good view of the soft X-ray spectrum accessible to NICER. Its high disk temperature also places the peak of the disk spectrum in the bandpass of both NICER and NuSTAR. Thus, it becomes an ideal test bed of modern disk atmosphere models. We propose a series of joint NICER/NuSTAR observations that we will analyze as a whole, in order to measure this system's unknown black hole parameters.                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R01       4U 1957+11: A RAPIDLY SPINNING BLACK HOLE IN (THE HALO OF) THE GALAXY?                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429W50_SADA_19334                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∏J#Ü@Ï∏Jî»)¯40510004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’iA#Eh␀␀ÁºHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀HQRecently, very high energy TeV gamma-rays have been detected from the extended source W50/SS433 using the High-Altitude Water Cherenkov Observatory (HAWC). Multi-wavelength observations suggest that the extended lobes of W50 are being impacted by the SS433 jets tens of parsecs away. X-ray emission is found to fill the radio lobe out to the radio 'ears', softening away from the black hole and with evidence of hard X-rays in the innermost regions pointing to an extreme accelerator. We propose a joint XMM/NuSTAR observation of the HAWC emission, coincident with the inner eastern lobe of W50. This broadband X-ray study will, for the first time, resolve and constrain both the non-thermal and thermal X-ray emission, providing a key insight into the nature of this Galactic PeVatron.              Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Safi-Harb                     Samar                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Probing a PeV Accelerator: Origin of Very High-Energy Gamma Rays from W50                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ 