o targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Crab_deepbkgd                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïπœ±9Ò@Ïπ–xVb‹10502013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’cDçû␀␀Á¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab_deepbkgd                                     @R¿l"h	’@*À)^û@gn≤ïÈ·±¿1’®Xy=Ÿ@q/ÏV’–@Ïπ–xVb‹@Ïπ›î»)¯10502013002@—Ào|Ìëh@—©Œ~˘€#@“¸´3333@“˝+3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’b˛2“␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Calibration / High OPT0_T3 temperature / observation truncated                                                          archived  20200429NGC_7793_P13                                      @vw¸PH¿@Rh	‘ï@ﬂÿ≠´üV¿S:E° ¿É@sc+I∫@Ïπ›î»)¯@Ïπﬁ[‰õø30502019001@zãB√…ÓÃ@zY◊€Ùá˝@|?33333@|?33333SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’b≥Ìk␀␀Á^Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LNGC7793 P13 is part of the small group of ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), i.e., neutron stars that accrete orders of magnitude above their Eddington limit. It is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations, making it an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known, allowing us to determine the complete orbital ephemeris through pulse timing. Here we propose two NuSTAR observations of P13 to follow-up on our successful campaigns in AO 3 and AO 4. We aim to track the evolution of the pulse period and study possible glitches. We will improve the orbital ephemeris, characterise the timing noise, and investigate a tentative super-orbital period on the order of 1500d.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1 