olar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429ESO565_G019                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∫LÍM@Ï∫MxVb‹60561043001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’`,wÿè␀␀ÁCHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO565_G019                                       @aˆÕûÉ‰&¿5Áe˝ä⁄∫@o¥Ù!-w@5°$›/@g≤ WßÜ¬@Ï∫MxVb‹@Ï∫s[‰õø60561043002@Ë†ÒIQÇ™@Ën¿Éo@Í`DæÌ)@Í_ƒæÌ)SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’_û∏QÏ␀␀ÁCHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429RX_J1131m1231                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∫s[‰õø@Ï∫sÕ´∏160502021003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’_`"‰␀␀Ë†Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÙThe origin of the X-ray reflection component of AGN has been debated for more than two decades. We propose to measure the size the reflection hump with microlensing for the first time to the X-ray brightest lensed quasar, RXJ1131-1231. This will provide a crucial independent test of the emerging paradigm for the reflection model for Type I AGN, where the reflection is dominated by regions close to the black hole with strong relativistic and light bending effects. This will further constrain the environment immediately around the black hole, which is essential to accurately measure black hole spins and understand the cosmic X-ray background.                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    DAI                           XINYU                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MICROLENSING SIZE OF AGN REFLECTION HUMP                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RX_J1131m1231                                     @eüØ∑È˘¿)$›/†@q!9XbN@FÙg8}ø@f>!ñRΩ<@Ï∫sÕ´∏1@Ï∫Ö±9Ò60502021004@◊U…‰@÷÷º”ZÜ@ÿåîŒp:˚@ÿçTŒp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’_+ù␀␀Ë†Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÙThe origin of the X-ray reflection component of AGN has been debated for more than two decades. We propose to measure the size the reflection hump with microlensing for the first time to the X-ray brightest lensed quasar, RXJ1131-1231. This will provide a crucial independent test of the emerging paradigm for the reflection model for Type I AGN, where the reflection is dominated by regions close to the black hole with strong relativistic and light bending effects. This will further constrain the environment immediately around the black hole, which is essential to accurately measure black hole spins and understand the cosmic X-ray background.                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    DAI                           XINYU                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MICROLENSING SIZE OF AGN REFLECTION HUMP                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429RX_J0134d2m4258                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∫Ö±9Ò@Ï∫Ü±9Ò60501005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’^€∞[␀␀Á[Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀H‘The mechanism of launching and powering relativistic jets in AGN is still not understood, but must link to the accretion flow. Evidence for this comes from the clear change in blazar jet properties at L/LEdd~0.01, probably due to the accretion flow transition from a geometrically thick ADAF to a thin disc. There should be another transition at L/LEdd >~ 1, where the accretion disc should puff up, but this is harder to study as few radio-loud AGN are super-Eddington. RX J0134.2-4258 is a robust super-Eddington NLS1 with L/LEdd~11, the highest of all radio-loud AGN known. Therefore, we propose a joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR program to study the accretion disc, corona and jet mechanism in this new radio-loud super-Eddington regime.                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Jin                           Chichuan                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Exploring the Disc-Jet Properties in the Most Super-Eddington Radio-Loud NLS1                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RX_J0134d2m4258                                   @7ëˇ.HËß¿EÅ[W>´6@qY&Èx‘˛¿Q˘ﬁ␀—∑@r¬8Ô4÷°@Ï∫Ü±9Ò@Ï∫—xVb‹60501005002@¯@„Ω@˜Ù/çπ@˘‘N|⁄Q@˘◊û|⁄QSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’^+˛|␀␀Á[Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀H‘The mechanism of launching and powering relativistic jets in AGN is still not understood, but must link to the accretion flow. Evidence for this comes from the clear change in blazar jet properties at L/LEdd~0.01, probably due to the accretion flow transition from a geometrically thick ADAF to a thin disc. There should be another transition at L/LEdd >~ 1, where the accretion disc should puff up, but this is harder to study as few radio-loud AGN are super-Eddington. RX J0134.2-4258 is a robust super-Eddington NLS1 with L/LEdd~11, the highest of all radio-loud AGN known. Therefore, we propose a joint XMM-Newton/NuSTAR program to study the accretion disc, corona and jet mechanism in this new radio-loud super-Eddington regime.                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Jin                           Chichuan                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       Exploring the Disc-Jet Properties in the Most Super-Eddington Radio-Loud NLS1                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HB89_0403m132                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∫—xVb‹@Ï∫“èFj60160181001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’]ï∂lx␀␀Â„Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HB89_0403m132                                     @NµVlÙ!¿*S√a@O@i∑¸π#¢ú¿En_o“ @qöô0æÌ@Ï∫“èFj@Ï∫‚Õ´∏160160181002@‘◊µkπå~@‘∞ò‚e,@÷Où≠B√ @÷RlLò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’]Mé˚␀␀Â„Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HESS_J0632p057                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∫‚Õ´∏1@Ï∫„xVb‹30502017001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’]%º}Í␀␀ÁìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀8HESS J0632+057 is one of the rare, six binary systems detected above 100 GeV. Besides its peculiar double-peak X-ray lightcurve, HESS J0632+057 is unique among the TeV binaries since the effects of the pulsar wind interacting with the stellar wind and Be disk are observable throughout its 315 day orbit in the X-ray and TeV bands. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR + VERITAS observations of the secondary X-ray peak (whose origin is still unknown), including the periastron and pulsar-disk interaction passage. These observations, combined with our wind-collision shock model, will probe the enhanced shock acceleration at periastron, determine the orbital solution and offer a rare opportunity of studying relativistic hydrodynamics on how the pulsar wind interacts with the dense circumstellar disk. Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    MORI                          KAYA                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       X-RAY AND TEV OBSERVATIONS OF HESS J0632+057'S PULSAR WIND COLLIDING INTO THE STELLAR WIND AND CIRCUMSTELLAR DISK       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESS_J0632p057                                    @Xë\˙¨ŸË@:«À)_@i±T…Öoø˙¨1&È@kÀîØOÑ@Ï∫„xVb‹@ÏªxVb‹30502017002@‰ˇ•ûÉ‰&@‰“á‚Ç@∏@ÊÜÔ;dZ@ÊÜ;dZSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’\ =p§␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀8HESS J0632+057 is one of the rare, six binary systems detected above 100 GeV. Besides its peculiar double-peak X-ray lightcurve, HESS J0632+057 is unique among the TeV binaries since the effects of the pulsar wind interacting with the stellar wind and Be disk are observable throughout its 315 day orbit in the X-ray and TeV bands. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR + VERITAS observations of the secondary X-ray peak (whose origin is still unknown), including the periastron and pulsar-disk interaction passage. These observations, combined with our wind-collision shock model, will probe the enhanced shock acceleration at periastron, determine the orbital solution and offer a rare opportunity of studying relativistic hydrodynamics on how the pulsar wind interacts with the dense circumstellar disk. Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    MORI                          KAYA                          NULL                          NULL                                           