␀␀ÁQHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4151                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïª.xVb‹@Ïª/#Ü60502017005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’[¸“ﬁ␀␀ÁuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4151                                          @f’A [¿@C∑Uõ=»@chM“Ò©¸@R∂ÙÿD–@e©∞â†'R@Ïª/#Ü@ÏªF[‰õø60502017006@ﬂ§ 	‘ï@ﬂx)á+@·EájÁ’g@·F	—N;ÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’[ü#E␀␀ÁuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC_4388                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏªF[‰õø@ÏªFÕ´∏160501018001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’[p*z␀␀ÁQHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀‡We propose to observe the Seyfert 2 galaxy NGC 4388 simultaneously with Nustar and XMM-Newton for 50 ks and 60 ks, respectively, in order to obtain conclusive evidence on the physical nature of the circumnuclear matter in this source. In 2013 NuSTAR revealed a very weak Compton hump and a very large iron Ka line EW for a source with weak or no reflection, suggesting that the reflecting matter is the same Compton-thin matter responsible for the absorption. Exploiting NuSTAR and XMM complementary capabilities, the requested simultaneous observations will enable us to probe the nature of the absorbing and reflecting matter in NGC 4388 obtaining simultaneously for the first time both a robust measurement of the Compton hump and a detailed study of the physical properties of the iron Ka line.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZAINO                         ALESSANDRA                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       IS THE CIRCUMNUCLEAR ABSORBING AND REFLECTING MATTER THE SAME IN NGC 4388?                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4388                                          @gOqué"@)^␀—∑Y@qc©˚Álã@Ró˚µsÎ@f=(åÁ∞@ÏªFÕ´∏1@ÏªjÕ´∏160501018002@Ëñ˙çπ@Ëg=è≈Å@ÍÄ£<64@ÍÄÉ<64SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’[gâ¨␀␀ÁQHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀‡We propose to observe the Seyfert 2 galaxy NGC 4388 simultaneously with Nustar and XMM-Newton for 50 ks and 60 ks, respectively, in order to obtain conclusive evidence on the physical nature of the circumnuclear matter in this source. In 2013 NuSTAR revealed a very weak Compton hump and a very large iron Ka line EW for a source with weak or no reflection, suggesting that the reflecting matter is the same Compton-thin matter responsible for the absorption. Exploiting NuSTAR and XMM complementary capabilities, the requested simultaneous observations will enable us to probe the nature of the absorbing and reflecting matter in NGC 4388 obtaining simultaneously for the first time both a robust measurement of the Compton hump and a detailed study of the physical properties of the iron Ka line.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZAINO                         ALESSANDRA                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       IS THE CIRCUMNUCLEAR ABSORBING AND REFLECTING MATTER THE SAME IN NGC 4388?                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429KUG1141p371                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏªjÕ´∏1@Ïªk?r‘£60160449001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Z”†m:␀␀Â„Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀S