ysics - the source populations that comprise the Galactic ridge X-ray emission (GRXE). Broad-band X-ray spectroscopy of six nearby polars (highly magnetized cataclysmic variables) with modest exposure (20 ksec per source) will test our hypothesis that the GRXE is dominated by a large number of polars whose mean white dwarf mass is consistent with those of intermediate polars and CVs (observed by SDSS) at 0.8-0.9 solar mass.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hailey                        Charles                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       NUSTAR OBSERVATION OF POLARS - PROBING THE X-RAY POINT SOURCE POPULATION IN THE GALACTIC RIDGE                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ST_LMi                                            @dÕÚ◊s@9#›óˆ+k@jkã¨q≥@PÅ0æÌ)@dŸ™ÕûÉ‰@ÏºW?r‘£@ÏºfèFj30461001002@’+®-‡@’␀R4Í@÷üa|⁄@÷üa|⁄SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’NÇß°Ú␀␀ÁgHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀SThe proposed NuSTAR observations are the first crucial steps to resolve one of the long-standing problems in high-energy astrophysics - the source populations that comprise the Galactic ridge X-ray emission (GRXE). Broad-band X-ray spectroscopy of six nearby polars (highly magnetized cataclysmic variables) with modest exposure (20 ksec per source) will test our hypothesis that the GRXE is dominated by a large number of polars whose mean white dwarf mass is consistent with those of intermediate polars and CVs (observed by SDSS) at 0.8-0.9 solar mass.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hailey                        Charles                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N04       NUSTAR OBSERVATION OF POLARS - PROBING THE X-RAY POINT SOURCE POPULATION IN THE GALACTIC RIDGE                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS04                                    @pHìtºj@Pze+”√@W˛àŒp:˚@@÷.±ƒ2 @k¯ÿD–©@ÏºfèFj@Ïºf±9Ò60511004001@wÈä	ﬁ@w±˙¨ŸË>@yr␀h€ã¨@yr␀h€ã¨SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’NSÈ>î␀␀ÁZHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS04                                    @pG¸π#¢ú@Pz«À)_@Wˇ_o“Û@@÷Vìu@lÀ)^û@Ïºf±9Ò@Ïºëî»)¯60511004002@ˆZéæv»¥@ˆ.Ω<6@˜·πıY¥@˜‚ÇπıY¥SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’M‰ïœ␀␀ÁZHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                         