omising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Cen_X4                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏºÊ±9Ò@ÏºÁî»)¯30502024005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’L\äÂ␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cen_X4                                            @lÓÀ˚µ¿?£ÒA [¿@t¡WßÜ¬'@8¥¢3ú@d2¡Ω•@ÏºÁî»)¯@ÏΩ#Ü30502024006@ÈPÌ@N§©@È
Ç@∑Ä@Î'≤i≠Bƒ@Î'Úi≠BƒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’KÎÖ∏␀␀ÈHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/10)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429NGC_7793_P13                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏΩ#Ü@ÏΩ#Ü30502019003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’K∫i␀␀Á^Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀LNGC7793 P13 is part of the small group of ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), i.e., neutron stars that accrete orders of magnitude above their Eddington limit. It is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations, making it an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known, allowing us to determine the complete orbital ephemeris through pulse timing. Here we propose two NuSTAR observations of P13 to follow-up on our successful campaigns in AO 3 and AO 4. We aim to track the evolution of the pulse period and study possible glitches. We will improve the orbital ephemeris, characterise the timing noise, and investigate a tentative super-orbital period on the order of 1500d.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    FUERST                        FELIX                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       TRACKING THE SPIN OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7793_P13                                      @vwá+¿@SÌ˙C˛]@©≠B√…Ô¿S9/üæw@tI5®Xy>@ÏΩ#Ü@ÏΩ9[‰õø30502019004@È°»£n/@È~"¨ŸË>@ÎwT—∑Y@Îy‘—∑YSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’KW‰±~␀␀Á^Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LNGC7793 P13 is part of the small group of ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), i.e., neutron stars that accrete orders of magnitude above their Eddington limit. It is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations, making it an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known, allowing us to determine the complete orbital ephemeris through pulse timing. Here we propose two NuSTAR observations of P13 to follow-up on our successful campaigns in AO 3 and AO 4. We aim to track the evolution of the pulse period and study possible glitches. We will improve the orbital ephemeris, characterise the timing noise, and investigate a tentative super-orbital period on the order of 1500d.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    FUERST                        FELIX                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       TRACKING THE SPIN OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC7465                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏΩ9[‰õø@ÏΩ9ÍM60160815001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’K(3îJ␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC7465                                           @uò„º”[@/€/ÏV’–@UÌa‰˜e˛¿C±	kπå~@r∆Å◊€Ùà@ÏΩ9ÍM@ÏΩI#Ü60160815002@‘}…rGE9@‘UˇF‹]d@’Ï`Ù!-@’Ï‡Ù!-SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’J”È>î␀␀ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_4151                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏΩI#Ü@ÏΩJ#Ü60502017007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’J™z␀␀ÁuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4151                                          @f’[W>´6@C∏∞Ú{≤ˇ@cg»K]Ãd@Rµ‹∆?@d'#¢úwö@ÏΩJ#Ü@ÏΩaèFj60502017008@›ﬁolãCñ@›≤Lç∏ª@‡2ˆu%F@‡5÷u%FSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’JL¥á␀␀ÁuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(4/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429BZB_J0955p3551                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿