n hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4151                                          @f‘ÃÃÃÃÕ@Cπ¿ÎÌ˙D@chr∞ ƒú@R¥ËßÊõ@b¬p:˚~ë@ÏæËî»)¯@Ïæ˝±9Ò60502017012@‹.∂√…ÓÃ@‹ æﬂ§@@ﬁù<>BZÓ@ﬁüÏ>BZÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’D`$h≠␀␀ÁuHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀»We propose a monitoring program of NGC 4151 to track the coronal temperature variations as the source flux changes, and at the same time track how the narrow Fe K line and the Compton hump respond to those flux changes. The pair-dominated model for the corona has a testable prediction for the dependence of cutoff energy on flux and spectral index, while a Compton-thick hypothesis for the narrow Fe Line predicts a correlation between the line flux, the Compton hump and the continuum flux. We will test these predictions and advance our understanding of the corona and the reflector producing the strong Fe K line.                                                                                                                                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    ZOGHBI                        ABDERAHMEN                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE CHANGING CORONA AND DISTANT REFLECTOR IN NGC 4151                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(6/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429PG_1426p015                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïæ˝±9Ò@Ïæ˛?r‘£60501049001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’D5¶‡␀␀ÁoHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀USpin measurements for high-mass active galactic nuclei are currently very sparse, but are critical for testing correlations between mass and spin hinted at in the current sample of AGN with spin constraints, and testing theoretical models of supermassive black hole growth. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of PG1426+015, which has the largest reverberation-mapped mass to date (log[Mbh/Msun] = 9.01 (+0.11,-0.16)), with XMM-Newton (100ks) and NuSTAR (100ks). The quality of the data will enable us to robustly search for relativistically broadened iron emission, an unambigous signature of reflection from the innermost accretion disc, and take an important first step towards constrining the spin for this key source.                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    WALTON                        DOMINIC                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       SEARCHING FOR RELATIVISTIC DISC REFLECTION IN THE HIGH MASS AGN PG1426+015                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1426p015                                       @k)ù≤-V?ÙÛMjO@u–åLò_@KÆ+jÁ’g@e¥πıY≥–@Ïæ˛?r‘£@ÏøI[‰õø60501049002@˜m7§®¡U@˜<„Ω@˘:ŸË>B@˘ŸË>BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Cóõ‡$␀␀ÁoHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀USpin measurements for high-mass active galactic nuclei are currently very sparse, but are critical for testing correlations between mass and spin hinted at in the current sample of AGN with spin constraints, and testing theoretical models of supermassive black hole growth. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of PG1426+015, which has the largest reverberation-mapped mass to date (log[Mbh/Msun] = 9.01 (+0.11,-0.16)), with XMM-Newton (100ks) and NuSTAR (100ks). The quality of the data will enable us to robustly search for relativistically broadened iron emission, an unambigous signature of reflection from the innermost accretion disc, and take an important first step towards constrining the spin for this key source.                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    WALTON                        DOMINIC                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       SEARCHING FOR RELATIVISTIC DISC REFLECTION IN THE HIGH MASS AGN PG1426+015                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PKS0326m288                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏøI[‰õø@ÏøJî»)¯60160155001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’CY <Æ␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS0326m288                                       @J'ª/Ï¿<ªí:)«z@l!é!ñRΩ¿KÃFsÅ◊‹@st†˘	k∫@ÏøJî»)¯@ÏøY#Ü60160155002@’z˝áì›ò@’RcüUõ=@÷›p:˚@÷›p:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’CkTì␀␀ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429TXS_0506p056                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏøY#Ü@ÏøY±9Ò60502053007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’BÔP␀␀ÁπHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ùOn Sept 22, 2017, the IceCube observatory reported the detection of the high-energy astrophysical neutrino candidate event IC170922A. A large multiwavelength observation campaign of the neutrino location involving led to the detection of enhanced broadband EM emission from the blazar TXS 0506+056. This detection represents the first evidence for an electromagnetic counterpart to the high-energy astrophysical neutrinos detected by IceCube. The soft-to-hard X-ray emission from the source provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the source SED. We here propose an ambitious observational program of TXS 0506+056 involving NuSTAR, Swift, VERITAS and Fermi.                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    SANTANDER                     J_MARCOS                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PROBING THE HADRONIC EMISSION FROM THE CANDIDATE NEUTRINO BLAZAR TXS 0506+056                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_0506p056                                      @SVsÅ◊€ı@ôb∂Æ}@hjçπ¿3Ás¸PH@s≤÷°a‰˜@ÏøY±9Ò@Ïøj±9Ò60502053008@”ˆîFt@“ÒOs¸P@‘∑S8}ø@‘∫ì8}øSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’B®d‹␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ùOn Sept 22, 2017, the IceCube observatory reported the detection of the high-energy astrophysical neutrino candidate event IC170922A. A large multiwavelength observation campaign of the neutrino location involving led to the detection of enhanced broadband EM emission from the blazar TXS 0506+056. This detection represents the first evidence for an electromagnetic counterpart to the high-energy astrophysical neutrinos detected by IceCube. The soft-to-hard X-ray emission from the source provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the source SED. We here propose an ambitious observational program of TXS 0506+056 involving NuSTAR, Swift, VERITAS and Fermi.                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    SANTANDER                     J_MARCOS                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       PROBING THE HADRONIC EMISSION FROM THE CANDIDATE NEUTRINO BLAZAR TXS 0506+056                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift VERITAS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀(4/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO323_G032                                       @h+≈÷8ÜY¿DŒÊ1¯†ê@rÒ›/üæ@5]:í£@g,Ñµ‹∆?@Ïøj±9Ò@Ïøk±9Ò60561045001@\ó≤˛≈m]@\f^5?|Ó@^Ä␀␀␀␀␀@^Ä␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Bx9•º␀␀Á}Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429ESO323_G032                                       @h+∂E° ¡¿DŒzêñº@rÒ◊
=p§@5]Ÿb∂Æ@g7hr∞ ≈@Ïøk±9Ò@Ïøèî»)¯60561045002@ÁeÕŒŸá@Á3çŒ_@È¥␀␀␀␀@È␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’B Ü␀␀Á}Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429RX_J1131m1231                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïøèî»)¯@ÏøêèFj60502021005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’A„-!¡␀␀Ë†Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÙThe origin of the X-ray reflection component of AGN has been debated for more than two decades. We propose to measure the size the reflection hump with microlensing for the first time to the X-ray brightest lensed quasar, RXJ1131-1231. This will provide a crucial independent test of the emerging paradigm for the reflection model for Type I AGN, where the reflection is dominated by regions close to the black hole with strong relativistic and light bending effects. This will further constrain the environment immediately around the black hole, which is essential to accurately measure black hole spins and understand the cosmic X-ray background.                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    DAI                           XINYU                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MICROLENSING SIZE OF AGN REFLECTION HUMP                                                                                ␀␀¯␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RX_J1131m1231                                     @eü‰˜e˝ã¿)8Ô4÷°@q!ç∏∫«@FÙÁØ∑È@g¡£n.±ƒ@ÏøêèFj@Ïø†±9Ò60502021006@‘Cá•„S¯@‘ÇÎC-@’îVT`™e@’îVT`™eSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’A†ÊñÚ␀␀Ë†Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ÙThe origin of the X-ray reflection component of AGN has been debated for more than two decades. We propose to measure the size the reflection hump with microlensing for the first time to the X-ray brightest lensed quasar, RXJ1131-1231. This will provide a crucial independent test of the emerging paradigm for the reflection model for Type I AGN, where the reflection is dominated by regions close to the black hole with strong relativistic and light bending effects. This will further constrain the environment immediately around the black hole, which is essential to accurately measure black hole spins and understand the cosmic X-ray background.                                                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    DAI                           XINYU                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MICROLENSING SIZE OF AGN REFLECTION HUMP                                                                                ␀␀W␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(3/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429MR2251m178                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïø†±9Ò@Ïø°ÍM90601604001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’AuI2q␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       