=qSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’;a_Û›␀␀Á˙Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(5/10) ToO                                                                                                              archived  202004292MASS_J104222p164115                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¿8î»)¯@Ï¿9î»)¯60601001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’;"É9E␀␀ÁpHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    K›Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with Chandra and NuSTAR (50+50ks). This will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of J1042+1641, and also obtain the first high-resolution image of this system.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        D                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Is J1042+1641 the First Example of a Gravitationally Lensed, Compton-Thick Quasar?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASS_J104222p164115                              @d∑È˘r@0π^ûö@l!b∂Æ}V@M â†'RT@cﬂ'ª/Ï@Ï¿9î»)¯@Ï¿>#Ü60601001002@∑âj¿Éo@∑ZRÒ©˚Á@π~z·H@π~z·HSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’:BøË␀␀ÁpHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  K›Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with Chandra and NuSTAR (50+50ks). This will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of J1042+1641, and also obtain the first high-resolution image of this system.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        D                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Is J1042+1641 the First Example of a Gravitationally Lensed, Compton-Thick Quasar?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1a/3)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429Conj_2020033_SAA90                                @iiµsÍ≥¿I	‘ï+@sY	kπå~@(ß_o“Û@f«ïÅ$›@Ï¿>#Ü@Ï¿?¥qE02020033001@ä§?Â…@â„Á8}ø@ã∞␀␀␀␀␀@ã∞␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï¿E$Pg)ˇˇˇùHea_22Oct2018_V6.25_nustardas_06Jul17_v1.8.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                  