                                                       ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 201503162MASS_J104222p164115                              @dßÜ¬&Å@0∑$tSéÛ@l!—N;Õ6@M€Ùá¸π@c‡ø±[W?@Ï¿?Õ´∏1@Ï¿@î»)¯60601001003@kØ_o“Û@kwOÑM@o`␀␀␀␀␀@o`␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’lêW␀␀ÁpHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  K›Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with Chandra and NuSTAR (50+50ks). This will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of J1042+1641, and also obtain the first high-resolution image of this system.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        D                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Is J1042+1641 the First Example of a Gravitationally Lensed, Compton-Thick Quasar?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202004292MASS_J104222p164115                              @d∑È˘r@0∑»K]Ãd@l!∫^5?}@M 4m