                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MAXI_J0637m430                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¡;ÍM@Ï¡<ÍM80502324011␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’TJU∂␀␀Á˙Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J0637m430                                    @X«X‚e¿Et?Â…@op9XbN¿4∆»¥9X@r6Ùá¸π$@Ï¡<ÍM@Ï¡c[‰õø80502324012@Ëáºj~˙@Ë]àŒp;@ÍY␀£n/@Í[ £n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ãëB␀␀Á˙Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(6/9)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429ESO323_G032                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¡c[‰õø@Ï¡dèFj60561045003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’¿H—Z␀␀Á}Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO323_G032                                       @h+˚ÁlãD¿DŒÀ˚µt@rÒÚ‰éär@5]5®Xy>@h]tºj~˙@Ï¡dèFj@Ï¡Öî»)¯60561045004@Ë}ß§?Â…@ËM¸%ÆÊ2@Í;)å~($@Í<	å~($SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’]ïÑ␀␀Á}Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASS_J104222p164115                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¡Öî»)¯@Ï¡Ü[‰õø60601001007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’.ÓÓÔ␀␀ÁpHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    K›Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with Chandra and NuSTAR (50+50ks). This will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of J1042+1641, and also obtain the first high-resolution image of this system.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        D                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Is J1042+1641 the First Example of a Gravitationally Lensed, Compton-Thick Quasar?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASS_J104222p164115                              @d«‚Ç@∏@0∏$xG@l ÿD–@MÅo␀i@bn.±ƒ3@Ï¡Ü[‰õø@Ï¡îÍM60601001008@‘Í/F‹]d@‘æ≥X‚@÷Z∏ ƒõ¶@÷[∏ ƒõ¶SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Îµ™J␀␀ÁpHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  K›Previous X-ray observations of the quadruply lensed quasar J1042+1641 (z=2.517) with Swift XRT reveal evidence for strong obscuration, unusual among the lensed quasar population. However, the current data are not sufficient to robustly determine the level of the absorption. Both Compton-thick and Compton-thin solutions provide equivalent fits, meaning J1042+1641 is potentially the first example of a gravitationally lensed, Compton-thick quasar. We request a coordinated broadband X-ray observation of this remarkable source with Chandra and NuSTAR (50+50ks). This will enable us to robustly determine the nature of the obscuration (Compton-thick vs Compton-thin), a key step towards determining the intrinsic power of J1042+1641, and also obtain the first high-resolution image of this system.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Walton                        D                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Is J1042+1641 the First Example of a Gravitationally Lensed, Compton-Thick Quasar?                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SDSS_J1433p4006                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¡îÍM@Ï¡ïî»)¯90601606001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’ƒtœ÷␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSS_J1433p4006                                   @kP ƒõ•„@Dú‡uˆ˝@Q◊•„S˜œ@POUõ=»@c%*0U2a@Ï¡ïî»)¯@Ï¡πÕ´∏190601606002@ÈSÉs¸P@È!åÎÌ˙D@Î"◊s¸@Î"◊s¸SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’i–6ù␀␀ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429G0d13m0d11                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¡πÕ´∏1@Ï¡∫î»)¯40501005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’*Iìà␀␀ÁÖHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀A unique phenomenon in the Galactic center region is the existence of numerous radio and X-ray filaments. The origin and formation of of the filaments have been a long-standing question. NuSTAR has detected four X-ray filaments so far. Only one of them, G0.13-0.11, has not been identified due to limited statistics. Based on its location and morphology, this filament is most likely to be a pulsar wind nebular or a magnetic structure fed by a pulsar. However, the current available data is not sufficient for us to test our hypothesis. Therefore, we propose for a deep on-axis observation for this filament, which will allow us to reveal its nature. The new data will extend our understanding on how filaments are powered, and could lead to discovery of the first pulsar in the Galactic center.    Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    ZHANG                         SHUO                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       UNVEILING THE NATURE OF A GALACTIC CENTER X-RAY FILAMENT G0.13-0.11: PULSAR WIND NEBULAR OR MAGNETIC STRUCTURE?         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316G0d13m0d11                                        @p©ËßÊõ¿<Ÿx‘˝Û∂?†∞Ú{≤˛≈?¿}øHÃ@cWπå~($@Ï¡∫î»)¯@Ï¬î»)¯40501005002@¯-¢‰˜e˛@¯+Óä	ﬁ@˙8´oiD@˙;oiDSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’yÓçÿ␀␀ÁÖHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀A unique phenomenon in the Galactic center region is the existence of numerous radio and X-ray filaments. The origin and formation of of the filaments have been a long-standing question. NuSTAR has detected four X-ray filaments so far. Only one of them, G0.13-0.11, has not been identified due to limited statistics. Based on its location and morphology, this filament is most likely to be a pulsar wind nebular or a magnetic structure fed by a pulsar. However, the current available data is not sufficient for us to test our hypothesis. Therefore, we propose for a deep on-axis observation for this filament, which will allow us to reveal its nature. The new data will extend our understanding on how filaments are powered, and could lead to discovery of the first pulsar in the Galactic center.    Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    ZHANG                         SHUO                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       UNVEILING THE NATURE OF A GALACTIC CENTER X-RAY FILAMENT G0.13-0.11: PULSAR WIND NEBULAR OR MAGNETIC STRUCTURE?         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004292MASXJ04332716m5843346                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬î»)¯@Ï¬xVb‹60160195001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’<Æuí␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ04332716m5843346                            @Q!ñRΩ<¿Ma=Ÿb∑@pÀÖ∏QÏ¿DVÈx‘˝Ù@tN§®¡U@Ï¬xVb‹@Ï¬èFj60160195002@◊:±F
¶L@◊,«‚ÇA@ÿπ±õ=»@ÿπ±õ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’ˇ%ãÚ␀␀ÊHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_04416p1215                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬èFj@Ï¬±9Ò60560026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’÷Ÿ±ﬂ␀␀ÁáHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Super-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. However, among all accretion modes, this phase is still the least understood one, and it is still largely debated what are the physical properties of the accretion flow and of the X-ray source at very high accretion rates. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN at z < 0.1 by simultaneously observing three sources with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. These three AGN have the highest Eddington ratios in the SEAMBHs sample, which contains exclusively objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable.                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    RICCI                         CLAUDIO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE FIRST SYSTEMATIC BROAD-BAND X-RAY STUDY OF EXTREME ACCRETION ONTO SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES WITH NUSTAR AND XMM      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_04416p1215                                   @Q∆îFsÅÿ@(•zxl"h@g7XbM”¿5\cÒA \@tN_o“Û@Ï¬±9Ò@Ï¬K#Ü60560026002@ÒàË◊s@Òe7õ•„T@Ú∆'5?|Ó@Ú∆˜5?|ÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’^W:…␀␀ÁáHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Super-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. However, among all accretion modes, this phase is still the least understood one, and it is still largely debated what are the physical properties of the accretion flow and of the X-ray source at very high accretion rates. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN at z < 0.1 by simultaneously observing three sources with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. These three AGN have the highest Eddington ratios in the SEAMBHs sample, which contains exclusively objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable.                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    RICCI                         CLAUDIO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE FIRST SYSTEMATIC BROAD-BAND X-RAY STUDY OF EXTREME ACCRETION ONTO SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES WITH NUSTAR AND XMM      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429XTE_J1739m285                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬K#Ü@Ï¬L[‰õø90601307001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’&¯	␀␀ÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           