␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’	&¯	␀␀Ê8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ00004876m0709117                            ?ÀÅ◊€Ùá˝¿´πå~($@V:è\(ı√¿P´|Ìëhs@tBé!ñRΩ@Ï¬üèFj@Ï¬¢î»)¯60161001004@uvAÚ◊s@uH∏QÎÖ@v·≠B√…Ô@v·≠B√…ÔSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’˙·GÆ␀␀Ê8Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ Solar dogleg out                                                                                                       archived  20200429HESS_J0632p057                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬¢î»)¯@Ï¬£xVb‹30502017003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’÷Ò˜®␀␀ÁìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀8HESS J0632+057 is one of the rare, six binary systems detected above 100 GeV. Besides its peculiar double-peak X-ray lightcurve, HESS J0632+057 is unique among the TeV binaries since the effects of the pulsar wind interacting with the stellar wind and Be disk are observable throughout its 315 day orbit in the X-ray and TeV bands. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR + VERITAS observations of the secondary X-ray peak (whose origin is still unknown), including the periastron and pulsar-disk interaction passage. These observations, combined with our wind-collision shock model, will probe the enhanced shock acceleration at periastron, determine the orbital solution and offer a rare opportunity of studying relativistic hydrodynamics on how the pulsar wind interacts with the dense circumstellar disk. Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    MORI                          KAYA                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       X-RAY AND TEV OBSERVATIONS OF HESS J0632+057'S PULSAR WIND COLLIDING INTO THE STELLAR WIND AND CIRCUMSTELLAR DISK       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HESS_J0632p057                                    @XèÉ{J#:@Â`Aâ7@i±Ÿb∂Æø˚:í£S@t_+I∫@Ï¬£xVb‹@Ï¬…±9Ò30502017004@È)∑X‚@È∫≈»K^@ÎÃıfœAÚ@ÎŒ’fœAÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’v;ÏG␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀8HESS J0632+057 is one of the rare, six binary systems detected above 100 GeV. Besides its peculiar double-peak X-ray lightcurve, HESS J0632+057 is unique among the TeV binaries since the effects of the pulsar wind interacting with the stellar wind and Be disk are observable throughout its 315 day orbit in the X-ray and TeV bands. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR + VERITAS observations of the secondary X-ray peak (whose origin is still unknown), including the periastron and pulsar-disk interaction passage. These observations, combined with our wind-collision shock model, will probe the enhanced shock acceleration at periastron, determine the orbital solution and offer a rare opportunity of studying relativistic hydrodynamics on how the pulsar wind interacts with the dense circumstellar disk. Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    MORI                          KAYA                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       X-RAY AND TEV OBSERVATIONS OF HESS J0632+057'S PULSAR WIND COLLIDING INTO THE STELLAR WIND AND CIRCUMSTELLAR DISK       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              VERITAS                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429LEDA_478026                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï¬…±9Ò@Ï¬ ?r‘£60561041001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’G4∑ê␀␀ÁìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316LEDA_478026                                       @ZãCïÅ¿HÆ∏QÎÖ@p31&Èy¿3ë—N;Õ6@r©ÎC,•@Ï¬ ?r‘£@Ï¬Óî»)¯60561041002@Î˙ﬁ“àŒ@ÍÌS˚µt@Ì#E8Ô5@ÌÉE8Ô5SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’Âº}Í␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation the last 10 candidate CT-AGN the BAT 100-month survey still lacking of NuSTAR data. With this proposal, each CT-AGN candidate of this sample will have a NuSTAR observation, thus enabling the best characterization to date of CT-AGN. Thanks to the excellent statistics (both in terms of spectral counts and sample size) we will study the sample physical properties, and put tighter constraints on the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the nearby Universe and on their contribution to the CXB.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    MARCHESI                      STEFANO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE COMPTON THICK AGN LEGACY PROJECT: A COMPLETE SAMPLE OF NUSTAR-OBSERVED NEARBY COMPTON THICK AGN                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                              