   ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM HXMT INTEGRAL                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀Deep background Calibration                                                                                             archived  20200429GX_339m4                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï√ìÕ´∏1@Ï√îÍM80502325003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’Ô…b˝␀␀Á	Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀vWe propose a series of 4 Target of Opportunity (ToO) observations with NuSTAR and NICER for any of the five following recurrent black hole binaries: GX 339-4, H1743-322, 4U 1630-47, XTE J1550-564, or XTE 1752-223. We aim to obtain high signal-to-noise spectra of these sources at different accretion states as they go into outburst. Our principal goal is to use X-ray reflection spectroscopy to track the evolution of the inner edge of the accretion disk as a function of luminosity; estimate the spin of the black hole; and measure the accretion disk inclination, density, ionization and iron abundance. These observations will provide crucial constraints to support a large-scale data analysis program for these sources.                                                                              Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    GARCIA                        JAVIER                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE PROPERTIES AND EVOLUTION OF ACCRETION DISKS IN BLACK HOLE BINARIES                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_339m4                                          @o˜T…Öo¿H`¡T…Ö@u-É‰%ÆÊ¿iDg8~@cê¨1&È@Ï√îÍM@Ï√¢ÍM80502325004@””òm≈÷9@”Ã~Ócä@÷f@£n/@÷e¿£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’óÉö\␀␀Á	Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀vWe propose a series of 4 Target of Opportunity (ToO) observations with NuSTAR and NICER for any of the five following recurrent black hole binaries: GX 339-4, H1743-322, 4U 1630-47, XTE J1550-564, or XTE 1752-223. We aim to obtain high signal-to-noise spectra of these sources at different accretion states as they go into outburst. Our principal goal is to use X-ray reflection spectroscopy to track the evolution of the inner edge of the accretion disk as a function of luminosity; estimate the spin of the black hole; and measure the accretion disk inclination, density, ionization and iron abundance. These observations will provide crucial constraints to support a large-scale data analysis program for these sources.                                                                              Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    GARCIA                        JAVIER                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE PROPERTIES AND EVOLUTION OF ACCRETION DISKS IN BLACK HOLE BINARIES                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS07                                    @pHL/É{J@Pq [¿7@WÛ–|Ñµ›@@ÿAâ7K«@d•ÖoiD@Ï√¢ÍM@Ï√£ÍM60511007001@oA@N§®¡@nÛ!ˇ.HÈ@p°ù≤-V@p°ù≤-VSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’k$ñ␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS07                                    @pH¨1'@PpßÊö‘@WÛOÑM@@ÿˇó$tT@d¢y=Ÿc@Ï√£ÍM@Ï√ xVb‹60511007002@-∂—∑Y@°a‰˜@ÒMÇŸá+@ÒOrŸá+SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’˚rÍ␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS08                                    @pK™ÕûÉ‰@Poe˝ä⁄∫@W∆?@@Õ“Ò©˚Á@d°∞â†'R@Ï√ xVb‹@Ï√Û?r‘£60511008001@ÒCrá+@Ò%ôÆÊ1˘@Ú|B®¡T @Úí®¡T SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’åSo‚␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NEP_DTDF_MOS09                                    @pN˝ä⁄πı@PoZÖáìﬁ@WÔ√a@O@@√öu%@d†Énóç@Ï√Û?r‘£@Ïƒî»)¯60511009001@…˜Ov_Ÿ@´y*ô1@ÒÅãCïÅ@ÒÚãCïÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’¶ﬂ√␀␀ÁìHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀HThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the north ecliptic pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. We propose to perform a 585 ks NuSTAR survey in this field, reaching a sensitivity comparable to the deepest NuSTAR extragalactic surveys, detecting 20-30 sources in 3-24 keV, to focus on: X-ray variability (3-8 keV monitoring and the first 8-24 keV studies!), the true obscured AGN fraction, 0.5-24 keV spectroscopy combining NuSTAR and Chandra, and the bright NLSy1 in the field.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    CIVANO                        FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Mrk_493                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïƒî»)¯@ÏƒèFj60560027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’␀ÈX␀␀ÁüHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Super-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. However, among all accretion modes, this phase is still the least understood one, and it is still largely debated what are the physical properties of the accretion flow and of the X-ray source at very high accretion rates. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN at z < 0.1 by simultaneously observing three sources with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. These three AGN have the highest Eddington ratios in the SEAMBHs sample, which contains exclusively objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable.                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    RICCI                         CLAUDIO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE FIRST SYSTEMATIC BROAD-BAND X-RAY STUDY OF EXTREME ACCRETION ONTO SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES WITH NUSTAR AND XMM      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_493                                           @m˘é!ñRΩ@AáökP∞Ú@Lqué!ñ@H«Ôù≤-@bª±[W>´@ÏƒèFj@ÏƒJÕ´∏160560027002@Ó¥*0U2@Ó}¥cä	@sbﬁ␀—∑@u≤ﬁ␀—∑SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’␀w¿H—␀␀ÁüHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Super-Eddington accretion periods are expected to be extremely important to explain the fast growth of the first supermassive black holes. However, among all accretion modes, this phase is still the least understood one, and it is still largely debated what are the physical properties of the accretion flow and of the X-ray source at very high accretion rates. We propose here a systematic broad-band X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN at z < 0.1 by simultaneously observing three sources with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. These three AGN have the highest Eddington ratios in the SEAMBHs sample, which contains exclusively objects with black hole masses estimated from reverberation mapping, making the Eddington ratio estimates extremely reliable.                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    RICCI                         CLAUDIO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE FIRST SYSTEMATIC BROAD-BAND X-RAY STUDY OF EXTREME ACCRETION ONTO SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES WITH NUSTAR AND XMM      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429Swift_J1858d6m0814                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏƒJÕ´∏1@ÏƒKxVb‹90601308001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’␀Bw`␀␀Ê8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1858d6m0814                                @q ∞â†'R¿ h1&Èx’@:HÜYJÙÒ¿fÈx‘˝Ù@cÄq≤ïÍ@ÏƒKxVb‹@Ïƒ_èFj90601308002@◊4ÄÿD–@◊«™ÕûÑ@⁄ˆv»¥9@⁄6v»¥9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘ˇ‹(ı√␀␀Ê8Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429MAXI_J0637m430                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïƒ_èFj@Ïƒ`èFj80502324013␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ˇØhK⁄␀␀Á˙Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J0637m430                                    @X≈Dg8~¿EsS&¬@oo∞â†'R¿4ÀµsÍ≥@s«¬è\(ˆ@Ïƒ`èFj@ÏƒêÕ´∏180502324014@ÔÏl’œ™Œ@Ô∞≠∫^5?@Ò€-w1ê@Òõ-w1êSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘ˇ8j1N␀␀Á˙Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(8/10)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429IRAS_09104p4109                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏƒêÕ´∏1@Ïƒë±9Ò60502055003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ˇ„-"␀␀ÁËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀©We propose three 40-ks observations of IRAS 09104+4109, a heavily obscured highly luminous quasar. Our target is uniquely accessible because of its relatively low redshift in comparison to similarly luminous obscured quasars found only at higher redshift. With the proposed observations we will be able to 1) characterize potential variability above 10 keV, and 2) build up the photon statistics to model its X-ray spectrum with physically motivated models for the obscuring structure usually called the torus. With modeling of the torus applied to the wealth of available infrared data, we will be able to make some of the first direct comparisons of the constraints implied from both of these spectral windows.                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BALOKOVIC                     MISLAV                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       STRUCTURE OF THE OBSCURER IN THE HEAVILY OBSCURED LUMINOUS QUASAR IRAS 09104+4109                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_09104p4109                                   @aLè\(ı√@DvVìu@fù+”√a@E¨…Öoi@2‹æﬂ§@@Ïƒë±9Ò@Ïƒ±xVb‹60502055004@‰õ9ˆ˝!ˇ@‰põ∏∫«@Ê]£n.≤@Ê£n.≤SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘˛©áeC␀␀ÁËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀©We propose three 40-ks observations of IRAS 09104+4109, a heavily obscured highly luminous quasar. Our target is uniquely accessible because of its relatively low redshift in comparison to similarly luminous obscured quasars found only at higher redshift. With the proposed observations we will be able to 1) characterize potential variability above 10 keV, and 2) build up the photon statistics to model its X-ray spectrum with physically motivated models for the obscuring structure usually called the torus. With modeling of the torus applied to the wealth of available infrared data, we will be able to make some of the first direct comparisons of the constraints implied from both of these spectral windows.                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BALOKOVIC                     MISLAV                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       STRUCTURE OF THE OBSCURER IN THE HEAVILY OBSCURED LUMINOUS QUASAR IRAS 09104+4109                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  202004292MASXJ20180125m5539312                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïƒ±xVb‹@Ïƒ≤Õ´∏160061296001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘˛{*␀␀Ê8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ20180125m5539312                            @s4ÍK¿K–uˆ˝!ˇ@ucóçOﬂ;¿@˘Œ_o“@]_&¡Ω•@Ïƒ≤Õ´∏1@Ïƒ√xVb‹60061296002@⁄r#“ÚÂ@⁄:ÓÄùIR@‹!òı¬è\@‹!òı¬è\SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘˛$…ƒ␀␀Ê8Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M82_X2                                            @böVìu@Qk»K]Ãd@aÆi≠B√ @D-Åo␀i@Di˘rGE@Ïƒ√xVb‹@Ïƒƒ±9Ò30502021001@bµÈ˘rG@bñC,•zx@cˆ”ZÖáî@cˆ”ZÖáîSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘˝ÛÌk␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429M82_X2                                            @bõ”√a@@QjÒ©˚Ám@aÆxl"h
@D/Æz·H@D®Xy=Ÿ@Ïƒƒ±9Ò@Ï≈?r‘£30502021002@Ù Icä	@Ù†≠K]Ãd@ˆA∆Åo␀@ˆDÜÅo␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘˝dô8Ç␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC5683                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï≈?r‘£@Ï≈±9Ò60160589001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘˝-	{B␀␀Ê8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised o