  Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C380                                             @qVú‡uˆ˝@HcàeîØO@SNd¬¯7µ@7íÇ@∑Ä4@cÓåLò_@Ï∆[‰õø@Ï∆&ÍM60160690002@”&q4ÍK@”êéäqﬁ@‘Üî–©*@‘á–©*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€˜Íz␀␀ÊLHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M82_X2                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï∆&ÍM@Ï∆'xVb‹30502021003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï€¯%s¨ê␀␀ÁπHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82_X2                                            @bõ”√a@@Qjæﬂ§?Ê@aÆìtºj@D/È˘rG@>sZÖáìﬁ@Ï∆'xVb‹@Ï∆a?r‘£30502021004@Û/ˆy¶µ@Û∆3333@ÙÄ	‰˜e˛@ÙÅ‰˜e˛SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€¯[NÅµ␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀≤␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(2/2) HK gap 421s / data gap 1805s                                                                                      archived  202004292MASXJ15504152m0353175                            @mµÚ‰éär¿—ÎÖ∏R@␀h€ã¨q@BÜ°a‰˜f@cw:í£S@Ï∆b#Ü@Ï∆Ü#Ü90601603002@Ájmáì›ò@Á6Uˇ.HÈ@ÈR~($@Èr~($SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘ıÉö␀␀ÊLHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2       