LL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429Swift_J1818d0m1607                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï«‚xVb‹@Ï«‚±9Ò80402308003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ŸÇˇ␀␀ÁîHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀A∑Magnetars are a small class of neutron stars mainly powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity, via large flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Magnetar activity has been recently observed also in other pulsar classes. Observations performed during, and immediately after such periods yield the largest amount of information on this magnetic emission, allowing one to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a Joint XMM (85ks) and NuSTAR (170ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insight on the crust and field configuration of sources showing magnetar outbursts.                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           Nanda                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X17       Prompt XMM-Newton and NuSTAR study of magnetar outbursts                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1818d0m1607                                @q(D–©*¿0QŒ_@-j⁄πıY¥?∫Ov_Ÿ@bÍõ•„S¯@Ï«‚±9Ò@Ï»#Ü80402308004@Ï‹ıtºj@Ï˜°VlÙ@ÔÜsHÀí@ÔâÛHÀíSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘ÿå¥á␀␀ÁîHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀A∑Magnetars are a small class of neutron stars mainly powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity, via large flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Magnetar activity has been recently observed also in other pulsar classes. Observations performed during, and immediately after such periods yield the largest amount of information on this magnetic emission, allowing one to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a Joint XMM (85ks) and NuSTAR (170ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insight on the crust and field configuration of sources showing magnetar outbursts.                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           Nanda                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X17       Prompt XMM-Newton and NuSTAR study of magnetar outbursts                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429Mrk_110                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï»#Ü@Ï»?r‘£60502022003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ÿWÃk∂␀␀ÁπHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛We propose to probe the disc-corona system and determine the BH spin of the bright bare AGN Mrk 110. Our ``2 in 1'' spectral analysis method is based on the global energetics of the disc-corona system and accounts for the contribution of relativistic reflection and the coronal extension. It has been successfully tested on the moderate accretion rate bright bare AGN, Ark120, where for the first time we tightly constrained its BH spin, and revealed that the corona radius decreases with increasing flux. Here, we propose to apply this method to Mrk 110 in order to probe the corona-disc system at higher accretion rate (~0.3--0.4) and a much lower reverberation BH mass (2.0x10^7 M_odot), thanks to two simultaneous NuSTAR (2x80ks) and XMM-Newton (2x43ks) observations separated by a few months.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PORQUET                       DELPHINE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A BROAD BAND VIEW OF THE DISC-CORONA SYSTEM OF MRK 110                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_110                                           @a®ñªò«„@J!◊€Ùá˝@dü‘ï*ô@FökP∞Ú@È≠B√…Ô@Ï»?r‘£@Ï»QÍM60502022004@ı¶#cä	@ıteúwök@˜+∂ÄùIR@˜,vÄùIRSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘◊Ÿi␀␀ÁπHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀˛We propose to probe the disc-corona system and determine the BH spin of the bright bare AGN Mrk 110. Our ``2 in 1'' spectral analysis method is based on the global energetics of the disc-corona system and accounts for the contribution of relativistic reflection and the coronal extension. It has been successfully tested on the moderate accretion rate bright bare AGN, Ark120, where for the first time we tightly constrained its BH spin, and revealed that the corona radius decreases with increasing flux. Here, we propose to apply this method to Mrk 110 in order to probe the corona-disc system at higher accretion rate (~0.3--0.4) and a much lower reverberation BH mass (2.0x10^7 M_odot), thanks to two simultaneous NuSTAR (2x80ks) and XMM-Newton (2x43ks) observations separated by a few months.   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    PORQUET                       DELPHINE                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A BROAD BAND VIEW OF THE DISC-CORONA SYSTEM OF MRK 110                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429LMC_X3                                            @U*ìtº¿PåLò_@q–|Ñµ›¿@-‡q@vRLò_@Ï»QÍM@Ï»RÍM10601308001@#xQÎÖ∏@#MVì@$¿␀␀␀␀␀@$¿␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘◊î√≤¢␀␀ÊLHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observato