¶@“DC,•z@”Âqﬁi≠@”ÈFqﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘∂ë∫˘ç␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             archived  20200429Crab_PA330_GR35                                   @TÊv»¥9X@5k¿6‚Î@g˛≈m\˚¿z)«y¶µ@tôÀ˚µt@Ï á±9Ò@Ï ñî»)¯10610035001@‘˘©è≈Å@‘œ†~($@÷]»Lò_@÷]»Lò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘∂@a#␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Calibration                                                                                                             archived  20200429MAXI_J0637m430                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï ñî»)¯@Ï ó?r‘£80502324015␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘¢Dtœ÷␀␀Á˙Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J0637m430                                    @X¬ô0æÌ¿Epê-‡@onΩ<64¿4–ˇó$tT?˜öu%@Ï ó?r‘£@Ï π#Ü80502324016@Á/∂␀␀␀␀@ÁE˚µt@Ë”Ä–Â`B@Ë’Ä–Â`BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘°Ì„ÔP␀␀Á˙Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀]Much of our understanding of the properties of accretion disks, relativistic jets, and the regions of strong gravity near black holes (BHs) come from observations of BH X-ray binaries. NuSTAR has made significant contributions to these studies especially through improved measurements of reflection components that have allowed us to access the inner disk and constrain the spins of BHs. Recently, comparisons between BH spins in X-ray binaries and in binary BH mergers have further increased the level of interest in BH spins, and a main focus of this proposal is to improve assessments of the uncertainties in BH spin measurements. We propose to do this by monitoring a bright BH transient with NuSTAR to systematically quantify the effect of the changing continuum on the BH spin measurement.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    TOMSICK                       JOHN                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MONITORING A BRIGHT OUTBURST FROM A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(9/10)                                                                                                                  archived  20200429NGC_4190_ULX1                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï π#Ü@Ï ∫èFj30601009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘°º5ß␀␀ÁÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀IWe propose to observe NGC 4190 ULX-1, one of the last remaining nearby ULXs without broad- band X-ray data and an excellent target for both NICER and NuSTAR due to its persistently high flux (> 10^&#8722;12 ergs/s/cm2) and isolation from other X-ray sources. Quasi-simultaneous observations for 50 ks with NICER and 80 ks with NuSTAR will allow us to constrain the broadband spectrum of the source and to search for pulsations and other timing features in both the NICER and NuSTAR data, in one of the best available opportunities to grow the small sample of ULXs with sufficient data for in-depth timing analysis.                                                                                                                                                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Earnshaw                      Hannah                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R02       A BROADBAND STUDY OF A NEARBY ULX IN NGC 4190                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4190_ULX1                                     @fÏï*ô1@BOX‚e,@d#w1è≈@SV⁄πıY¥@;ﬂ'ª/ÏW@Ï ∫èFj@Ï ÛxVb‹30601009002@ÒÁÙI∫^@Ò¡ØH@Û2|£S&@Û5l£S&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘°H'qf␀␀ÁÕHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀IWe propose to observe NGC 4190 ULX-1, one of the last remaining nearby ULXs without broad- band X-ray data and an excellent target for both NICER and NuSTAR due to its persistently high flux (> 10^&#8722;12 ergs/s/cm2) and isolation from other X-ray sources. Quasi-simultaneous observations for 50 ks with NICER and 80 ks with NuSTAR will allow us to constrain the broadband spectrum of the source and to search for pulsations and other timing features in both the NICER and NuSTAR data, in one of the best available opportunities to grow the small sample of ULXs with sufficient data for in-depth timing analysis.                                                                                                                                                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Earnshaw                      Hannah                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R02       A BROADBAND STUDY OF A NEARBY ULX IN NGC 4190                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC_7090_ULX2                                     @tA£n.±ƒ¿KBu%F@uU$›/†¿FïÑM:ì@`v®¡T…Ü@Ï ÛxVb‹@Ï ÙÕ´∏180501321001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘°W˝␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Following a series of remarkable recent discoveries, we now know that some of the most luminous members of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) population are actually powered by highly super-Eddington neutron stars. Five such systems are now known, but based on the long-term variability characteristics exhibited by these sources, we have compiled a sample of 19 additional ULX pulsar candidates from the broader ULX population. Here we propose a co-ordinated target of opportunity observation with XMM (100ks) and NuSTAR (100ks) of one of the most promising of these candidates, NGC7090 ULX, when the source is bright. Our main aims are to search for pulsations in order to test our prediction that this is another ULX pulsar, and constrain the broadband spectrum.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    WALTON                        DOMINIC                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE HUNT FOR NEW PULSAR ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCES: NGC7090 ULX                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7090_ULX2                                     @tAŒ_o“¿K@Œp:˚@uUN;Õ5®¿Fñûö@`äI∫^5@Ï ÙÕ´∏1@ÏÀ8ÍM80501321002@˘‹eéäqﬁ@˘†E`Aâ7@˚ù5¨1'@˚ù%¨1'SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘†êó¥&␀␀ÁHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀Following a series of remarkable recent discoveries, we now know that some of the most luminous members of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) population are actually powered by highly super-Eddington neutron stars. Five such systems are now known, but based on the long-term variability characteristics exhibited by these sources, we have compiled a sample of 19 additional ULX pulsar candidates from the broader ULX population. Here we propose a co-ordinated target of opportunity observation with XMM (100ks) and NuSTAR (100ks) of one of the most promising of these candidates, NGC7090 ULX, when the source is bright. Our main aims are to search for pulsations in order to test our prediction that this is another ULX pulsar, and constrain the broadband spectrum.                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    WALTON                        DOMINIC                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       THE HUNT FOR NEW PULSAR ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCES: NGC7090 ULX                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SN2014C                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÀ8ÍM@ÏÀ9±9Ò40502001003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘†S–¯À␀␀ÁºHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    8œcMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful Chandra-NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request Chandra-NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar deathNon-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      R                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       Continued Chandra-NuSTAR monitoring of the unprecedented metamorphosis of SN2014C at t>1500 days since explosion        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2014C                                           @u4„S˜ŒŸ@A8çπ@Wb¨1'¿4µ…„Ω@cÀ!-w1ê@ÏÀ9±9Ò@ÏÀ_î»)¯40502001004@Íyã¯†ê.@ÍEÄÿD–@ÏKÓcä@ÏK∞ÓcäSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘üÙnæc␀␀ÁºHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  8œcMass loss in evolved massive stars is one of the least understood yet fundamental aspects of stellar evolution. HOW and WHEN do massive stars lose their H-envelopes? This central question motivates this proposal. We request a continuation of our successful Chandra-NuSTAR campaign that led to the first detection of a H-poor extragalactic SN in the hard X-rays. We request Chandra-NuSTAR observations to map the unique situation of the interaction of the H-stripped SN2014C with a H-rich shell ejected by its progenitor star, as part of our approved gamma-ray to radio follow up. Our goal is to complete the mapping of the density profile of the H-rich material at R>7d17 cm to determine its location and total mass, and hence the mass-loss history of the progenitor in the years before stellar deathNon-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Margutti                      R                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C20       Continued Chandra-NuSTAR monitoring of the unprecedented metamorphosis of SN2014C at t>1500 days since explosion        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429SGR1935p2154                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÀ_î»)¯@ÏÀ`#Ü80602313001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ü¿©Ë|␀␀Á±Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀SMagnetars are a small class of neutron stars believed to be powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity in the form of short X-ray bursts, giant flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Multi-band observations of these transient events yield the largest amount of information on magnetars emission, allowing to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a joint NICER (50 ks) and NuSTAR (110 ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insights on magnetars surface and crust, magnetic field configuration and magnetospheres.                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Borghese                      Alice                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R02       PROMPT NICER AND NUSTAR STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR1935p2154                                      @r[ﬁ␀—∑@5Ô!-w1ê@LíÙÿD–?Ôm\˙¨ŸË@`øä	ﬁ@ÏÀ`#Ü@ÏÀ{[‰õø80602313002@‚ ´ [¿@·ˇëS&@„íç=Ÿc@„ìM=ŸcSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘ük¡Õ␀␀Á±Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀SMagnetars are a small class of neutron stars believed to be powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity in the form of short X-ray bursts, giant flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Multi-band observations of these transient events yield the largest amount of information on magnetars emission, allowing to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a joint NICER (50 ks) and NuSTAR (110 ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insights on magnetars surface and crust, magnetic field configuration and magnetospheres.                                                                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Borghese                      Alice                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R02       PROMPT NICER AND NUSTAR STUDY OF MAGNETAR OUTBURSTS                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20200429Swift_J1818d0m1607                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÀ{[‰õø@ÏÀ{ÍM80402308005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ü<ç␀␀ÁîHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀A∑Magnetars are a small class of neutron stars mainly powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity, via large flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Magnetar activity has been recently observed also in other pulsar classes. Observations performed during, and immediately after such periods yield the largest amount of information on this magnetic emission, allowing one to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a Joint XMM (85ks) and NuSTAR (170ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insight on the crust and field configuration of sources showing magnetar outbursts.                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           Nanda                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X17       Prompt XMM-Newton and NuSTAR study of magnetar outbursts                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Swift_J1818d0m1607                                @q(⁄QŒ¿0$xG@-lV’œ™Œ?ΩIQÇ©ì@b`F‹]d@ÏÀ{ÍM@ÏÀõ?r‘£80402308006@‰õ¸4m≈÷@‰™˘≤-V@Êu–°a‰˜@Êvê°a‰˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘û·"É␀␀ÁîHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀A∑Magnetars are a small class of neutron stars mainly powered by their huge magnetic fields. They go through long stretches of quiescence, interrupted by periods of activity, via large flares and yearly-timescale X-ray outbursts. Magnetar activity has been recently observed also in other pulsar classes. Observations performed during, and immediately after such periods yield the largest amount of information on this magnetic emission, allowing one to test the theoretical models on a variety of phenomena and source states. We propose a Joint XMM (85ks) and NuSTAR (170ks) ToO program (1 trigger of 4 observations over a few months) aimed at gathering new physical insight on the crust and field configuration of sources showing magnetar outbursts.                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Rea                           Nanda                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X17       Prompt XMM-Newton and NuSTAR study of magnetar outbursts                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(3/4)                                                                                                                   archived  202004291ES_1927p654                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÀõ?r‘£@ÏÀúèFj60502034003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘û∞[∞␀␀ÁÕHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀>Our group recently caught a local AGN, 1ES 1927+654, during an optical changing-look event. Our X-ray monitoring campaign has shown an extremely peculiar X-ray emission, with a supersoft X-ray spectrum, dominated by a blackbody component, with only a weak contribution from the power-law, which suggests that the X-ray corona was destroyed during the optical-UV transient event. Our observations have finally shown a recent hardening of the X-ray emission, indicative of a re-creation of the X-ray corona. We propose here two 45ks NuSTAR observations of this intriguing source, simultaneous with two XMM-Newton observations that we were recently awarded, to monitor its spectral behaviour, and in particular to study the reappearance of the hard component and the evolution of the cutoff.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    RICCI                         CLAUDIO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DESTRUCTION AND RECREATION OF THE X-RAY CORONA IN A CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161ES_1927p654                                      @r=Ÿb∂Æ@Pf√…ÓÀ˚@X>˘€"–Â@4ı`Aâ7L@`@4ÍK@ÏÀúèFj@ÏÀ∫±9Ò60502034004@Ê_ê—∑Y@Ê2ø:í£@ÁÂZïÈ·±@ÁÂÚïÈ·±SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘û_b:h␀␀ÁÕHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀>Our group recently caught a local AGN, 1ES 1927+654, during an optical changing-look event. Our X-ray monitoring campaign has shown an extremely peculiar X-ray emission, with a supersoft X-ray spectrum, dominated by a blackbody component, with only a weak contribution from the power-law, which suggests that the X-ray corona was destroyed during the optical-UV transient event. Our observations have finally shown a recent hardening of the X-ray emission, indicative of a re-creation of the X-ray corona. We propose here two 45ks NuSTAR observations of this intriguing source, simultaneous with two XMM-Newton observations that we were recently awarded, to monitor its spectral behaviour, and in particular to study the reappearance of the hard component and the evolution of the cutoff.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    RICCI                         CLAUDIO                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       DESTRUCTION AND RECREATION OF THE X-RAY CORONA IN A CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429OJ287                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÀ∫±9Ò@ÏÀªxVb‹90601616001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘û.ç◊Ã␀␀ÊkHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316OJ287                                             @`µÆz·H@4E° ¿É@i‘z·GÆ@A»}øHÄ@u‚êˇó$t@ÏÀªxVb‹@ÏÀ“±9Ò90601616002@‹–Õˆ˝!ˇ@‹ïzxl"@ﬁ„ﬁBZÓc@ﬁÁ^BZÓcSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‘ù◊¥%Ì␀␀ÊkHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429RX_J1131m1231                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÀ“±9Ò@ÏÀ”?r‘£60502021007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‘ù•‘√≥␀␀Ë†Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÙThe origin of the X-ray reflection component of AGN has been debated for more than two decades. We propose to measure the size the reflection hump with microlensing for the first time to the X-ray brightest lensed quasar, RXJ1131-1231. This will provide a crucial independent test of the emerging paradigm for the reflection model for Type I AGN, where the reflection is dominated by regions close to the black hole with strong relativistic and light bending effects. This will further constrain the environment immediately around the black hole, which is essential to accurately measure black hole spins and understand the cosmic X-ray background.                                                                                                                                                     