binaries (XRBs) in nearby galaxies. This classification, based on hard X-ray luminosity and hardness ratios, can provide valuable insights into how XRB properties vary with variables such as metallicity (Z), age, and specific star formation rate (sSFR). Vulic et al. (2018) classified 90 XRBs in nearby galaxies observed by NuSTAR, but the sample contains few galaxies with low-Z or low sSFR. Furthermore, variability studies of XRB populations have been limited to M31. We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of the NGC 55 to expand studies of the compact objects, accretion states, and variability of XRBs to lower luminosities in a low-Z galaxy.                    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    FORNASINI                     FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A NUSTAR-XMM SURVEY OF NGC 55: A HOLISTIC VIEW OF A LOW METALLICITY X-RAY BINARY POPULATION                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_55_POS3                                       @£S&¿CùF
¶L0@tÃ{J#9¡¿R‰µ‹∆?@[{É{J#:@ÏÃú#Ü@ÏÃ¿[‰õø50510003002@Á—Ücä	@Á°•)^û@ÈÆä6‚Î@È±*6‚ÎSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÕ6Öò=␀␀Á‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ôWith NuSTAR, it is possible for the first time to determine the compact object type and accretion state of large samples of X-ray binaries (XRBs) in nearby galaxies. This classification, based on hard X-ray luminosity and hardness ratios, can provide valuable insights into how XRB properties vary with variables such as metallicity (Z), age, and specific star formation rate (sSFR). Vulic et al. (2018) classified 90 XRBs in nearby galaxies observed by NuSTAR, but the sample contains few galaxies with low-Z or low sSFR. Furthermore, variability studies of XRB populations have been limited to M31. We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of the NGC 55 to expand studies of the compact objects, accretion states, and variability of XRBs to lower luminosities in a low-Z galaxy.                    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    FORNASINI                     FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A NUSTAR-XMM SURVEY OF NGC 55: A HOLISTIC VIEW OF A LOW METALLICITY X-RAY BINARY POPULATION                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429KUG1141p371                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÃ¿[‰õø@ÏÃ¡±9Ò90601618001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÃ„∫˘ç␀␀ÊsHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316KUG1141p371                                       @f‘,<ûÌ@Bnªò«‚Ç@e…´üUõ=@R$g8}ø@'·ñRΩ<6@ÏÃ¡±9Ò@ÏÃﬁèFj90601618002@‚‘1≤˛≈m@‚´≥ [¿@‰AÄ¶L/É@‰A@¶L/ÉSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÃ‚ÛîJV␀␀ÊsHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200429SN1987A                                           @TÛ&¡Ω•¿QP÷°a‰˜@q{bM“Ò™¿?Ú{≤˛≈m@@ª∂E° ¡@ÏÃﬁèFj@ÏÃﬂèFj40501004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÃ‚≈œt␀␀ÁËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Being the closest observed supernova (SN) in more than four centuries, SN 1987A provides unique opportunities to study the evolution of a SN into a SN remnant (SNR). We propose a 275 ks NuSTAR observation to investigate the ongoing evolution of SN 1987A. The gamma-ray flux detected by Fermi, which is produced by relativistic particles, has increased by at least a factor of 2 over the past years. Hard X-ray observations constrain properties of the relativistic particles and provide insight into cosmic ray acceleration in SNRs. The observations also probe the CSM structure and mass loss history of the progenitor. SN 1987A has been monitored in practically all other wavebands and hard X-ray observations are an important complement and offer unique perspectives of the remnant.                 Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    ALP                           DENNIS                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       HARD X-RAY EVOLUTION OF SN 1987A                                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN1987A                                           @TÒ$›/¿QQué!ñS@q{ò_ˆî¿?ı%F
