ut the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab_PA330_GR15                                   @TÁ•„S˜œ@5øÉ{J#:@gd¬¯7µ¿∏F‹]d@tõI∫^5@ÏÕNÕ´∏1@ÏÕ]xVb‹10610015003@xØŒp:˚@xyI∫^5?@{\␀␀␀␀␀@{\␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€˝ÁY =␀␀Á¶Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀û␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Calibration / no CHU4 / data gap 1182s                                                                                  archived  20200429Crab_PA330_GR30                                   @TÁ©*0U2@5}≈÷8ÜY@ga@N•¿Dõ•„S¯@tö?|Ìëh@ÏÕ]xVb‹@ÏÕmxVb‹10610030002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€˛1~K␀␀Á¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀–␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀Calibration / no CHU4 / data gap 720s                                                                                   archived  20150316NGC_55_POS4                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÕmxVb‹@ÏÕnî»)¯50510004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÕºËπ␀␀Á‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ôWith NuSTAR, it is possible for the first time to determine the compact object type and accretion state of large samples of X-ray binaries (XRBs) in nearby galaxies. This classification, based on hard X-ray luminosity and hardness ratios, can provide valuable insights into how XRB properties vary with variables such as metallicity (Z), age, and specific star formation rate (sSFR). Vulic et al. (2018) classified 90 XRBs in nearby galaxies observed by NuSTAR, but the sample contains few galaxies with low-Z or low sSFR. Furthermore, variability studies of XRB populations have been limited to M31. We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of the NGC 55 to expand studies of the compact objects, accretion states, and variability of XRBs to lower luminosities in a low-Z galaxy.                    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    FORNASINI                     FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A NUSTAR-XMM SURVEY OF NGC 55: A HOLISTIC VIEW OF A LOW METALLICITY X-RAY BINARY POPULATION                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_55_POS4                                       @déäqﬁj¿Cñûö@t÷ÎÖ∏R¿R·fœAÚ@\áØ∑È@ÏÕnî»)¯@ÏÕèÍM50510004002@‡ÿ:l"h
@‡∑YÄ4m∆@‚0]’fœB@‚2]’fœBSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÕÃ·x9¶␀␀Á‡Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ôWith NuSTAR, it is possible for the first time to determine the compact object type and accretion state of large samples of X-ray binaries (XRBs) in nearby galaxies. This classification, based on hard X-ray luminosity and hardness ratios, can provide valuable insights into how XRB properties vary with variables such as metallicity (Z), age, and specific star formation rate (sSFR). Vulic et al. (2018) classified 90 XRBs in nearby galaxies observed by NuSTAR, but the sample contains few galaxies with low-Z or low sSFR. Furthermore, variability studies of XRB populations have been limited to M31. We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of the NGC 55 to expand studies of the compact objects, accretion states, and variability of XRBs to lower luminosities in a low-Z galaxy.                    Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    FORNASINI                     FRANCESCA                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       A NUSTAR-XMM SURVEY OF NGC 55: A HOLISTIC VIEW OF A LOW METALLICITY X-RAY BINARY POPULATION                             ␀␀Vñ␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀data gap 22166s                                                                                                         archived  20200429MACS_J0717_SADA_20140                             @\˝å~($@=˘e+”√a@gæºj~˘€@7iDg8@u ˆ˝!ˇ.@ÏÕèÍM@ÏÕë?r‘£70501011001@wÈ˘rG@.óçOﬂ;@␀␀␀␀␀␀@␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÕÃØ˘Óé␀␀ÁËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ØThe most energetic events in the Universe since the Big Bang have been the merging of 2 galaxy clusters. In rare cases, such as with MACSJ0717, 4 subclusters have been observed to be undergoing a merger. This produces a complex merger structure with very hot (T > 20 keV) gas, only observed in clusters containing shocks with high Mach number shocks (M > 2) such as the Bullet Cluster and A665. While Chandra measurements have been performed on this cluster, the constraints placed on this hot gas are inconclusive. NuSTARS ability to probe into the hard X-ray band can provide better constraints on the temperature, as well as provide an opportunity to constrain potential Inverse Compton scattering that may occur for the reacceleration of electrons by the shock.                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    ROJAS                         RANDALL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MAPPING THE TEMPERATURE STRUCTURE OF THE COMPLEX HOT MERGING GALAXY CLUSTER MACS J0717                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA set up                                                                                                   archived  20200429MACS_J0717d5p3745                                 @[V4ÍK@B÷}VlÙ@fÜº£@4¿â†'RT@tçÓÀ˚µ@ÏÕë?r‘£@ÏŒ	xVb‹70501001001AÛ√,•zA»∑π¿ÎÓAd.¬√…ÔAd¥\]càSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŒ/6Õé˚␀␀ÁËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ØThe most energetic events in the Universe since the Big Bang have been the merging of 2 galaxy clusters. In rare cases, such as with MACSJ0717, 4 subclusters have been observed to be undergoing a merger. This produces a complex merger structure with very hot (T > 20 keV) gas, only observed in clusters containing shocks with high Mach number shocks (M > 2) such as the Bullet Cluster and A665. While Chandra measurements have been performed on this cluster, the constraints placed on this hot gas are inconclusive. NuSTARS ability to probe into the hard X-ray band can provide better constraints on the temperature, as well as provide an opportunity to constrain potential Inverse Compton scattering that may occur for the reacceleration of electrons by the shock.                                   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    ROJAS                         RANDALL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MAPPING THE TEMPERATURE STRUCTURE OF THE COMPLEX HOT MERGING GALAXY CLUSTER MACS J0717                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀PID 05295 INERTIAL only                                                                                                 archived  20200429GQCom                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏŒ	xVb‹@ÏŒ
#Ü60160469003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŒ.¨ÿÔ±␀␀Ê{Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GQCom                                             @f£æv»¥9@;„¢úwök@iØDg8~@S’Lò_˜@ [dZ¨@ÏŒ
