 three unclassified hard X-ray sources that were detected by merging mosaic images from the Swift-BAT and INTEGRAL-ISGRI surveys. We know little about these sources other than a crude X-ray position and flux, but they are good candidates for being high-redshift blazars or Compton-thick AGN that appear missing from those surveys. Our scientific objectives are: to obtain an improved X-ray position that will narrow down the list of possible counterparts in the optical/infrared bands; and create X-ray spectra to combine with the OIR data to generate their broadband spectral energy distribution. The targets are too faint to be detected by BAT or ISGRI alone, but they are easily detected by NuSTAR with minimal exposure times.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BODAGHEE                      ARASH                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR BRINGS INTO FOCUS FAINT AGN CANDIDATES FROM THE SWIFT-INTEGRAL X-RAY SURVEY                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J12346p5238                                   @gî;Õ5®X@JPâ†'RT@`$p:˚~ë@Püæv…@%Ï•zxl"@Ïœ	±9Ò@Ïœ#Ü60501050002@Ÿ†ê-‡@ÿ‹=JÙÿ@⁄¥¸C,•z@⁄µºC,•zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€ˇ´lŸ␀␀ÁËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀xNuSTAR observations are requested for three unclassified hard X-ray sources that were detected by merging mosaic images from the Swift-BAT and INTEGRAL-ISGRI surveys. We know little about these sources other than a crude X-ray position and flux, but they are good candidates for being high-redshift blazars or Compton-thick AGN that appear missing from those surveys. Our scientific objectives are: to obtain an improved X-ray position that will narrow down the list of possible counterparts in the optical/infrared bands; and create X-ray spectra to combine with the OIR data to generate their broadband spectral energy distribution. The targets are too faint to be detected by BAT or ISGRI alone, but they are easily detected by NuSTAR with minimal exposure times.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    BODAGHEE                      ARASH                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       NUSTAR BRINGS INTO FOCUS FAINT AGN CANDIDATES FROM THE SWIFT-INTEGRAL X-RAY SURVEY                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429M82_X2                                            @bõ%F
¶L@Qhı¬è\)@aØ∂E° ¡@D14ÍJå@tŒ≈Åo␀@Ïœ#Ü@Ïœî»)¯30502022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï€ˇUû&Ø␀␀Á¯Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82_X2                                            @bú∑Ä4m∆@Qi|⁄Q@aØØ∑È@D2a|⁄Q@t¬ˆîFsÇ@Ïœî»)¯@ÏœR±9Ò30502022002@ıëºVì@ıd†¶µ@˜aA [¿@˜aA [¿SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï€ˇã‰±␀␀Á¯Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀`M82 X-2 is the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), a class of ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX) whose accretor is a neutron star. The extreme luminosity of ULXs suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion, and this is confirmed by the detection of strong relativistic outflows in many of these systems. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star. The predicted mass transfer is so extreme that it should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing, and measure accurately (or set an upper limit to) the mass transfer.                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    BACHETTI                      MATTEO                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       MEASURING THE MASS TRANSFER IN THE PULSATING ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCE M82 X-2                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202004298C1849p670                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏœR±9Ò@ÏœS#Ü60463066001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏœlW˝␀␀ÊÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503168C1849p670                                        @q§Aâ7K«@P«ÁlãCñ@X_àeîØO@9N§®¡U@WB:)«yß@ÏœS#Ü@Ïœe[‰õø60463066002@ŸQ?ù≤-@Ÿ V@€ÿD–@€\ÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏœÅÕé˚␀␀ÊÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429PG1216p069                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïœe[‰õø@Ïœf#Ü60661007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏœÅ®›|«␀␀ÁÒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀úA Large Program (1.58 Ms) has been granted in XMM-Newton AO18 and 27 orbits have been awarded in HST Cycle 27 to study Supermassive Black Hole Winds in X-rays (SUBWAYS), designed to provide direct support on feedback models by deriving constraints on the energetics and duty cycle of Ultra-Fast Outflows (UFOs) in Active Galactic Nuclei. We ask now for NuSTAR observations of the 20 SUBWAYS sources (630 ks) without archival NuSTAR data. The availability of NuSTAR data above 10 keV is crucial for the objectives of SUBWAYS, and will maximise the outcome of this ambitious Large Program. The proposed observations represent a fundamental step to maintain NuSTAR science upfront during the next decade in the physically motivated investigation of multi-phase outflows.                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BUILDING SUBWAYS: THE NUSTAR LINE                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG1216p069                                        @gÍ≥g†˘@z¨ŸË>B@qÑâ†'RT@QI∫^5??Îv_ÿ≠´ü@Ïœf#Ü@Ïœ}[‰õø60661007002@ﬂ3Í¨ŸË>@ﬁÒ}å~($@‡¨öç∏∫«@‡≠∫ç∏∫«SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏœÅèΩ@␀␀ÁÒHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀úA Large Program (1.58 Ms) has been granted in XMM-Newton AO18 and 27 orbits have been awarded in HST Cycle 27 to study Supermassive Black Hole Winds in X-rays (SUBWAYS), designed to provide direct support on feedback models by deriving constraints on the energetics and duty cycle of Ultra-Fast Outflows (UFOs) in Active Galactic Nuclei. We ask now for NuSTAR observations of the 20 SUBWAYS sources (630 ks) without archival NuSTAR data. The availability of NuSTAR data above 10 keV is crucial for the objectives of SUBWAYS, and will maximise the outcome of this ambitious Large Program. The proposed observations represent a fundamental step to maintain NuSTAR science upfront during the next decade in the physically motivated investigation of multi-phase outflows.                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BUILDING SUBWAYS: THE NUSTAR LINE                                                                               