ed  20150316HB89m1354p195                                     @j'[W>´6@3NÏø±[W@!ñÆ}VlÙ@RS’fœAÚ@18D–©*@Ïœ}Õ´∏1@Ïœê#Ü60463043002@’AOW>´6@’œØ∑È@÷ﬂf¬&Äù@÷Â&¬&ÄùSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïœ°À‰±␀␀ÊÑHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MCG_p04_22_042                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïœê#Ü@ÏœêÕ´∏160602018001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïœ°¶fff␀␀ÈSHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MCG +04-22-042 is a Seyfert 1.2 AGN which shows evidence of significant hard X-ray variability. Curiously, previous analyses of the Swift BAT and a short NuSTAR observation suggest that the source shows increased variability at high energies (above 20keV) than at lower energies. Archival data also suggest that the spectrum appears harder when the source is brighter. These behaviours have been observed in radio loud AGN, but are extremely atypical for radio quiet, type-1 AGN. We propose to monitor the hard X-ray flux of this source using 3x40ks observations of the source throughout AO6. NuSTAR can uniquely provide the opportunity to study the source on short and long timescales across a wide energy range,and permit the search for spectral changes associated with the variability.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHARACTERISING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF THE SEYFERT 1.2 GALAXY MCG +04-22-042                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_p04_22_042                                    @aú∞ ƒõ¶@6‚–Â`Aâ@iªÄ4m≈÷@EhHé@u≈À)^û@ÏœêÕ´∏1@Ïœ∞±9Ò60602018002@‰˙ÿD–@‰Œi∑È˘@Êu€≥–|Ö@Êw{≥–|ÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïœ¬+˛|␀␀ÈSHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀MCG +04-22-042 is a Seyfert 1.2 AGN which shows evidence of significant hard X-ray variability. Curiously, previous analyses of the Swift BAT and a short NuSTAR observation suggest that the source shows increased variability at high energies (ab