   @Ï–Y"‰Pg␀␀ÊãHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀Solar dogleg                                                                                                            archived  20150316Sol_20158_SADA                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ïœÿî»)¯@ÏœŸèFj20601001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏœÔŒQ)W␀␀ÊãHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sThe goal of this proposal is to take advantage of opportunities for co-observation between NuSTAR and the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) during six of the PSP perihelia ocurring during NuSTAR GO Cycles 6 & 7. These Earth-facing perihelia provide compelling opportunities to combine NuSTAR s capability for highly sensitive remote X-ray observation with PSP s powerful suite of in-situ instruments. By arranging these co-observations, the capacity of NuSTAR and PSP will be used in tandem to examine connectivity between the corona and the inner heliosphere. Additionally, solar observations performed at these times will allow the NuSTAR observatory to continue to contribute to the rich, multi-instrument solar datasets achieved when the heliophysics community unites in support of each PSP perihelia.    Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Duncan                        Jessie                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       COORDINATED NUSTAR OBSERVATION IN SUPPORT OF SIX EARTH-SIDE PARKER SOLAR PROBE PERIHELIA                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    archived  20150316Sol_20158_AR12764                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏœŸèFj@Ïœ⁄#b$©20611001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï–Xÿ¶ﬂ√␀␀ÊãHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sThe goal of this proposal is to take advantage of opportunities for co-observation between NuSTAR and the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) during six of the PSP perihelia ocurring during NuSTAR GO Cycles 6 & 7. These Earth-facing perihelia provide compelling opportunities to combine NuSTAR s capability for highly sensitive remote X-ray observation with PSP s powerful suite of in-situ instruments. By arranging these co-observations, the capacity of NuSTAR an