                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Frederick                     Sara                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X19       sfrederick@astro.umd.edu                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC6921_MCG04                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï—è±9Ò@Ï—ê±9Ò60662001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï—˜ÏÿÔ±␀␀Ë∏Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC6921_MCG04                                     @s1º£n/@9≈ˆ˝!ˇ.@P∂Ç@∑Ä4¿õã¨q≥@ZÃÃÃÃÕ@Ï—ê±9Ò@Ï—¢?r‘£60662001002@◊√¥ı¬è\@◊ããÌ˙C˛@ŸWÙ…Öo@ŸWÙ…ÖoSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï—˜‘ì'␀␀Ë∏Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200429NGC3079                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï—¢?r‘£@Ï—£#Ü60662004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï—˜Øò◊k␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC3079                                           @bŒûÏø±[@K‘64Í@c∫˘€"–Â@H«y¶µ@t7éäqﬁj@Ï—£#Ü@Ï—±±9Ò60662004002@ÿ¶L/É@◊Õ&ÄùI@Ÿän_ÿ≠¨@Ÿän_ÿ≠¨SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï—˜òø%å␀␀ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC3094                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï—±±9Ò@Ï—≤?r‘£60668001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï—˜vT2␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀mTo tackle some of the biggest ongoing questions regarding AGN activity and feedback, we are constructing a complete census of local Seyfert-luminosity AGN. The Local AGN Survey (LASr) is a multi-tiered selection of AGN designed to reveal all AGN in the local Universe, even in the presence of heavy obscuration. As a proof-of-concept, we propose 3x100ks NuSTAR observations of 3 previously-unknown Compton-thick AGN candidates to quantify the efficiency of our selection. Extensive simulations show that the requested exposure is sufficient to confirm the presence or lack of a heavily obscured AGN to high significance in each. The observations will test the robustness of the LASr selection and pave the way towards achieving it's primary goal: mitigating obscuration biases of AGN selection.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boorman                       Peter                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       VALIDATING A NEW AGN SELECTION AS A MEANS TO CONSTRUCT THE LOCAL CENSUS OF AGN                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC3094                                           @b +jÁ’g@/ÇZÓcä@kyo␀h€å@HhU2a|@v␀V’œ™Œ@Ï—≤?r‘£@Ï“î»)¯60668001002@¯ùnX‚@¯l‡ﬁ“â@˙q˚∞ ƒú@˙u+∞ ƒúSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï“.%[f«␀␀ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀mTo tackle some of the biggest ongoing questions regarding AGN activity and feedback, we are constructing a complete census of local Seyfert-luminosity AGN. The Local AGN Survey (LASr) is a multi-tiered selection of AGN designed to reveal all AGN in the local Universe, even in the presence of heavy obscuration. As a proof-of-concept, we propose 3x100ks NuSTAR observations of 3 previously-unknown Compton-thick AGN candidates to quantify the efficiency of our selection. Extensive simulations show that the requested exposure is sufficient to confirm the presence or lack of a heavily obscured AGN to high significance in each. The observations will test the robustness of the LASr selection and pave the way towards achieving it's primary goal: mitigating obscuration biases of AGN selection.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boorman                       Peter                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       VALIDATING A NEW AGN SELECTION AS A MEANS TO CONSTRUCT THE LOCAL CENSUS OF AGN                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429SN2012au                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï“î»)¯@Ï“#Ü90601521001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï“- œX␀␀ÊïHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2012au                                          @h5ª/ÏV÷¿$ä	ﬁ␀“@r˝nóçOﬂ@JZh	‘ï@vHâ7K∆®@Ï“#Ü@Ï“[‰õø90601521002@◊2[¿7@◊H⁄Q@ÿ±°¬è\)@ÿ±°¬è\)SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï“-≥|ç␀␀ÊïHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                     