                                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC6921_MCG04                                     @s1åÁØ∏@9¬&ÄùIR@PµVlÙ!¿õã¨q≥@V‘K]ÃcÒ@Ï”M?r‘£@Ï”\èFj60662001004@‘DgÊö‘@‘PM:ì@’¢ékP∞Ú@’•ŒkP∞ÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï”cà—Y‚␀␀Ë∏Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  202004292MASX_J02051994m023                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï”\èFj@Ï”\Õ´∏160601026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï”cfï␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀LThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation two candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month survey. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge of the selection function of CT-AGN, therefore helping us in determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local Universe.          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       COMPTON THICK AGN WITH NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASX_J02051994m023                               @?WE8Ô4◊¿~($x@d/Êö‘,¿MÛ9¿ÎÌ˙@`;µsÍ≥@Ï”\Õ´∏1@Ï”v?r‘£60601026002@ﬁ'ÏZ¨@›ﬂ¸π#£@‡8åbM”@‡8ÏbM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï”â£¶~¨␀␀ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀LThe X-ray spectral analysis of Compton thick (CT-, i.e., having intrinsic obscuration NH>1E24 cm^-2) active galactic nuclei (AGN) is key to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. We propose to target with a combined NuSTAR/XMM-Newton observation two candidate CT-AGN selected from the BAT 100-month survey. Thanks to the excellent count statistics, we will be able to constrain several spectral parameters (e.g., relative importance of the scattered component versus the direct one, geometry of the obscuring torus, equivalent width of the Iron emission line). This analysis will refine our knowledge of the selection function of CT-AGN, therefore helping us in determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local Universe.          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ajello                        Marco                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       COMPTON THICK AGN WITH NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429ESO317mG041                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï”v?r‘£@Ï”w?r‘£60161395001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï”âv`∂␀␀Ê†Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO317mG041                                       @cπº¿EbM“Ú@qK(åÁ∞@+7ökP∞Ú@0Ö®Xy=Ÿ@Ï”w?r‘£@Ï”ÜèFj60161395002@’|R≥g†˘@’QLtSéÛ@÷˙É+J@÷˚√+JSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï”©ﬂ#E␀␀Ê†Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202004293C273                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï”ÜèFj@Ï”Üî»)¯10602606001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï”©∫…Â␀␀Ê†Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations       