 time is sensitive to the underlying cosmology. Cluster cosmology requires accurate mass measurements, which generally depend on temperature measurements. Unfortunately, the main sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. The hard band sensitivity of NuSTAR, however, has the potential to resolve this discrepancy and reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose deep observations of 4 low redshift, relaxed clusters, with extensive XMM and Chandra (and future XRISM) data, where both calibration and physical differences can be assessed in the simplest possible systems.                                      Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ACCURATE GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: A LEGACY DATASET WITH CHANDRA, XMM, AND XRISM                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ABELL_1795                                        @iÊJåLò@:ñsÅ◊€ı@Ab∂Æ}@SZﬁ␀—∑@v*◊s¸P@Ï‘≥?r‘£@Ï‘˛[‰õø70660003002@¯ù‡9¿ÎÓ@¯h†$›/@˙k(uˆ˝"@˙mXuˆ˝"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’)–6ù␀␀ËHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀›Galaxy clusters are the most massive virialized objects in the universe, and their growth over cosmic time is sensitive to the underlying cosmology. Cluster cosmology requires accurate mass measurements, which generally depend on temperature measurements. Unfortunately, the main sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. The hard band sensitivity of NuSTAR, however, has the potential to resolve this discrepancy and reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose deep observations of 4 low redshift, relaxed clusters, with extensive XMM and Chandra (and future XRISM) data, where both calibration and physical differences can be assessed in the simplest possible systems.                                      Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ACCURATE GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: A LEGACY DATASET WITH CHANDRA, XMM, AND XRISM                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MGAB_V207                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï‘˛[‰õø@Ï‘ˇxVb‹80601317001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï’◊¸˜G␀␀Ê∏Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose a 60ks observation for one new GeV bright nova to explore the particle acceleration and gamma-ray production mechanisms. We will constrain the particle acceleration efficiency by comparing thermal X-ray to GeV luminosity and search for predicted non-thermal X-rays. Understanding shocks in novae is relevant for other shock-powered transients including Type IIn supernovae, tidal disruption events and stellar mergers. NuSTAR is the only instrument capable of detecting X-rays from novae simultaneously with the GeV emission (the lower energy X-rays accessible to other focusing instruments are hidden by the nova shell at these times). The observations should be conducted now to take advantage of the simultaneous operations with Fermi/LAT.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sokolovsky                    Kirill                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       Understanding the gamma-ray production mechanism in nova shocks                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MGAB_V207                                         @M—GÆz·¿K^RΩ<6@pó0U2a|¿G9ué!ñS@]V≥g†˘	@Ï‘ˇxVb‹@Ï’.[‰õø80601317002@i5Lò_@>≤º@Òè∆bM”@ÒèVbM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï’B %+␀␀Ê∏Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose a 60ks observation for one new GeV bright nova to explore the particle acceleration and gamma-ray production mechanisms. We will constrain the particle acceleration efficiency by comparing thermal X-ray to GeV luminosity and search for predicted non-thermal X-rays. Understanding shocks in novae is relevant for other shock-powered transients including Type IIn supernovae, tidal disruption events and stellar mergers. NuSTAR is the only instrument capable of detecting X-rays from novae simultaneously with the GeV emission (the lower energy X-rays accessible to other focusing instruments are hidden by the nova shell at these times). The observations should be conducted now to take advantage of the simultaneous operations with Fermi/LAT.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sokolovsky                    Kirill                        NU