␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷Â Á␀␀ÊøHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RXJ1756d4p5235                                    @p–«À)_@JK9¿ÎÌ˙@Th	‘ï@=}ä⁄πıZ@7ì©*0U2@Ï÷√Õ´∏1@Ï÷‘xVb‹60464145002@Ÿ]*0U2@ÿÕ∑b∂Æ}@⁄≠]¿ÎÌ˙@⁄≠ù¿ÎÌ˙SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï÷‰˙Ä0å␀␀ÊøHea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429IRAS_F17207m0014                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï÷‘xVb‹@Ï÷’èFj60667001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï÷‰◊:…␀␀Ë,Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀OUltraluminous infrared galaxies (ULIRGs; LIR > 10^12 Lsun) are the most intrinsically luminous galaxies in the low-redshift Universe, yet the power source of ULIRGs (AGN vs starburst) is still debated. We propose NuSTAR observations of 7 local ULIRGs to, 1) establish the first complete, unbiased sample with which to assess (at E>10 keV) the role of BH accretion in powering these luminous systems, and 2) resolve significant disagreements in the AGN luminosities and obscuring columns for the individual systems. These observations will be used to perform detailed studies of individual systems, linking the AGN properties observed outflows, and determine the global importance of AGN activity at the highest galaxy luminosities.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       GAZING INTO THE INFERNO: EMBEDDED AGN IN LOCAL ULIRGS                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_F17207m0014                                  @pL…„Ωø”∂E° ¿É@6ó$tSè@3ú•zxl"@vKD–©*@Ï÷’èFj@Ï÷ÊÍM60667001002@‘C…&Èx’@‘âîØO@’ÓÄ‚e,@’Ô¿‚e,SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï◊ú¢S␀␀Ë,Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀OUltraluminous infrared galaxies (ULIRGs; LIR > 10^12 Lsun) are the most intrinsically luminous galaxies in the low-redshift Universe, yet the power source of ULIRGs (AGN vs starburst) is still debated. We propose NuSTAR observations of 7 local ULIRGs to, 1) establish the first complete, unbiased sample with which to assess (at E>10 keV) the role of BH accretion in powering these luminous systems, and 2) resolve significant disagreements in the AGN luminosities and obscuring columns for the individual systems. These observations will be used to perform detailed studies of individual systems, linking the AGN properties observed outflows, and determine the global importance of AGN activity at the highest galaxy luminosities.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       GAZING INTO THE INFERNO: EMBEDDED AGN IN LOCAL ULIRGS                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429NGC3894                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï÷ÊÍM@Ï÷Á±9Ò60601019001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï◊›Øô␀␀Ë,Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of NGC6328 (z=0.014) and NGC3894 (z=0.0108), the only two CSOs(young radio sources) which are also confirmed gamma-ray (>0.1 GeV) emitters by Fermi-LAT. The joint modeling of the broadband X-ray and gamma-ray bands will be an asset to establish the nature of the X-ray emission and disentangle the non-thermal radiative output from the accretion related components. This is key to understand the initial conditions of the growing source, its following evolution, and the nature of the feedback with the host galaxy. Among the current X-ray telescopes operating at energies >10 keV, only NuSTAR has the capabilities required to detect and study our targets.                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Migliori                      Giulia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BEYOND THE 10 KEV WALL: UNVEILING THE HIGH-ENERGY OUTPUT OF YOUNG RADIO SOURCES WITH NUSTAR AND FERMI                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC3894                                           @f%æv»¥9@M∞À)^û@a)Õ5®Xy@KËˇó$tT@r«¨ŸË>B@Ï÷Á±9Ò@Ï◊èFj60601019002@ÚÁ(càeï@Ú∫Vº@Ù6¢1è≈@Ù6r1è≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï◊%x	+␀␀Ë,Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of NGC6328 (z=0.014) and NGC3894 (z=0.0108), the only two CSOs(young radio sources) which are also confirmed gamma-ray (>0.1 GeV) emitters by Fermi-LAT. The joint modeling of the broadband X-ray and gamma-ray bands will be an asset to establish the nature of the X-ray emission and disentangle the non-thermal radiative output from the accretion related components. This is key to understand the initial conditions of the growing source, its following evolution, and the nature of the feedback with the host galaxy. Among the current X-ray telescopes operating at energies >10 keV, only NuSTAR has the capabilities required to detect and study our targets.                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Migliori                      Giulia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BEYOND THE 10 KEV WALL: UNVEILING THE HIGH-ENERGY OUTPUT OF YOUNG RADIO SOURCES WITH NUSTAR AND FERMI                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429MARKARIAN_279                                     @j˛\ë—N@QNåLò_@\À4÷°aÂ@GpùIQÇ™@tY'ª/ÏW@Ï◊èFj@Ï◊[‰õø60601011001@Oã9¿ÎÌ˙@Oa [¿7@P≈^ûö@P≈^ûöSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï◊%/hK⁄␀␀Ë3Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀ªRelativistic reflection from the inner disk is often controversial, since extremely skewed features could also be due to complex continua or complex absorption. Clearly double-peaked Fe K lines are more robust, as they can only be shaped by relativistic Doppler shifts; however, these line profiles are extremely rare. Analysis of a very recent NuSTAR snapshot observation of the Seyfert-1 Mrk 279 reveals such a line profile. It likely traces a signficant evolution in the inner accretion flow, and may signal that the disk is truncated. We request 300 ks of exposure on Mrk 279 during NuSTAR Cycle 6, to study the nature and evolution of the accretion flow in Mrk 279 and to place relativistic disk reflection on firmer ground.                                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       A DOUBLE-PEAKED FE K LINE AND TRUNCATED DISK IN MRK 279                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200429MARKARIAN_279                                     @j◊
