on and NuSTAR observations of a luminous, optically-selected, non-jetted quasar at z=0.821. Our main goal is to measure the high-energy cutoff in its X-ray spectrum, hence constraining, in the framework of Comptonization models, its coronal temperature and optical depth. This will allow us to test whether the same physical conditions hold in quasars at luminosities and masses 100 times higher than those probed in so far in the nearby Universe, exploring the unknown X-ray properties of the quasar population at intermediate redshifts (z = 0.1-1), constituting half the age of the Universe.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kammoun                       Elias                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       THE CORONAL PROPERTIES OF AN EXCEPTIONALLY LUMINOUS QUASAR AT Z = 0.821                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HS_1630p2355                                      @oüæv…@7œ§?Â…@E4ÍJå@D@Énóç@vAÚ◊s@Ï◊«î»)¯@ÏÿÍM60601012002@¯}„º”@¯∂óiDg8@˙£d¬¯8@˙£ö˛≈m]SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ïÿ#≈ò␀␀Ë‚Hea_10Apr2020_V6.27.1_nustardas_01Apr20_v1.9.2                                  ␀␀…High luminosity quasars are unique laboratories to test our understanding of X-ray coronae in AGN. We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of a luminous, optically-selected, non-jetted quasar at z=0.821. Our main goal is to measure the high-energy cutoff in its X-ray spectrum, hence constraining, in the framework of Comptonization models, its coronal temperature and optical depth. This will allow us to test whether the same physical conditions hold in quasars at luminosities and masses 100 times higher than those probed in so far in the nearby Universe, exploring the unknown X-ray properties of the quasar population at intermediate redshifts (z = 0.1-1), constituting half the age of the Universe.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kammoun                       Elias                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       THE CORONAL PROPERTIES OF AN EXCEPTIONALLY LUMINOUS QUASAR AT Z = 0.821                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200429HB89_1334m127                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÿÍM@ÏÿxVb‹60160547001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ïÿ"ø=—ª␀␀Ê∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                              