binary system eta Carinae have provided definitive evidence for extremely hard, non-thermal X-ray emission. This emission probably originates from the inverse-Compton (IC) of seed stellar photons by GeV electrons produced by the wind-wind colliding (WWC) shock acceleration. The NuSTAR monitoring observations exhibit a continuous decline of the non-thermal flux ratio to the thermal flux through a binary orbit after the recovery, which was unexpected in the standard WWC shock acceleration models. This ratio is expected to be at a maximum during the next NuSTAR cycle. We propose 3 NuSTAR observations of eta Carinae to understand how the non-thermal emission recovers from the periastron decay and connects to the steady decline toward apastron.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CATCHING THE PEAK OF NON-THERMAL EMISSION FROM ETA CARINAE                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Eta_Carinae                                       @d&Ov`¿M”Ùá¸π$@qˆûÏø±[ø‚eîØOÑ@Lv_ÿ≠¨@ÏŸê#Ü@ÏŸ»#Ü30602030004@¯ÙŸkπå~@¯¿° ¿É@˙“8‘,<ü@˙”X‘,<üSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏŸ„¿©Ë|␀␀Ë€Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀_NuSTAR observations of the massive stellar binary system eta Carinae have provided definitive evidence for extremely hard, non-thermal X-ray emission. This emission probably originates from the inverse-Compton (IC) of seed stellar photons by GeV electrons produced by the wind-wind colliding (WWC) shock acceleration. The NuSTAR monitoring observations exhibit a continuous decline of the non-thermal flux ratio to the thermal flux through a binary orbit after the recovery, which was unexpected in the standard WWC shock acceleration models. This ratio is expected to be at a maximum during the next NuSTAR cycle. We propose 3 NuSTAR observations of eta Carinae to understand how the non-thermal emission recovers from the periastron decay and connects to the steady decline toward apastron.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CATCHING THE PEAK OF NON-THERMAL EMISSION FROM ETA CARINAE                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20200811NGC6328                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏŸ»#Ü@ÏŸ»±9Ò60601020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏŸ„yu0Ì␀␀ËAHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of NGC6328 (z=0.014) and NGC3894 (z=0.0108), the only two CSOs(young radio sources) which are also confirmed gamma-ray (>0.1 GeV) emitters by Fermi-LAT. The joint modeling of the broadband X-ray and gamma-ray bands will be an asset to establish the nature of the X-ray emission and disentangle the non-thermal radiative output from the accretion related components. This is key to understand the initial conditions of the growing source, its following evolution, and the nature of the feedback with the host galaxy. Among the current X-ray telescopes operating at energies >10 keV, only NuSTAR has the capabilities required to detect and study our targets.                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Migliori                      Giulia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BEYOND THE 10 KEV WALL: UNVEILING THE HIGH-ENERGY OUTPUT OF YOUNG RADIO SOURCES WITH NUSTAR AND FERMI                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC6328                                           @pMÙá¸π$¿PC=»K^@tm64Í¿/KÌ˙C˛]@tHb∂Æ}V@ÏŸ»±9Ò@ÏŸÛî»)¯60601020002@–xe+”√@¨î…‰@ÒıFÀ˚µ@ÒˆˆÀ˚µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï⁄MvµI␀␀ËAHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀We propose NuSTAR observations of NGC6328 (z=0.014) and NGC3894 (z=0.0108), the only two CSOs(young radio sources) which are also confirmed gamma-ray (>0.1 GeV) emitters by Fermi-LAT. The joint modeling of the broadband X-ray and gamma-ray bands will be an asset to establish the nature of the X-ray emission and disentangle the non-thermal radiative output from the accretion related components. This is key to understand the initial conditions of the growing source, its following evolution, and the nature of the feedback with the host galaxy. Among the current X-ray telescopes operating at energies >10 keV, only NuSTAR has the capabilities required to detect and study our targets.                                                                                                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Migliori                      Giulia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BEYOND THE 10 KEV WALL: UNVEILING THE HIGH-ENERGY OUTPUT OF YOUNG RADIO SOURCES WITH NUSTAR AND FERMI                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811IC1816                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏŸÛî»)¯@ÏŸÙ[‰õø60061023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï⁄à'q␀␀Ê€Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                 