@Ï‚Õ´∏1@Ï‚aèFj80601309001@Û◊«y¶µ@Û™wÿD–@ıUÆúwök@ıX.úwökSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‚É{ªªº␀␀ÁœHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀NWe propose a ToO observation of Galactic X-ray transients aimed at discovering accreting millisecond X-ray pulsars. NuSTAR and NICER represent ideal observatories capable of fulfilling such goal, which will help increasing the knowledge of the evolutionary paths followed by NS, and possibly lead to breakthrough discoveries of extremely quickly rotating objects. We propose a 40 ks observations of newly discovered X-ray transients. Criteria set to maximise the probability of discovering new sources of this class, based on the X-ray flux (> 7 and 10 mCrab, in 2-10 and 20-40 keV energy bands, respectively), the spectral shape (a power law with index 1.5-2.5), and the absence of an alternative identification, will be used to trigger the proposed observations among newly discovered transients.  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sanna                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       Revealing accreting millisecond X-ray pulsars with simultaneous NuSTAR and NICER observations.                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  202008112MASXJ17085915p2153082                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï‚aèFj@Ï‚a±9Ò60160659001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‚É.uí␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ17085915p2153082                            @p‹∆?@5◊8}øH@EucàeîØ@?¸V’œ™Œ@rdúwökQ@Ï‚a±9Ò@Ï‚p?r‘£60160659002@‘Û¬è\(ˆ@‘¡`ñªò»@÷JÑU2a|@÷JÑU2a|SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‚É <Æ␀␀ÁHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              HST                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811WR140                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï‚p?r‘£@Ï‚pÕ´∏130601031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‚Ç˘çvµ␀␀ËáHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀fThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. A series of NuSTAR observations of WR140 around periastron in 2016 has provided an interesting variation of the extremely hard X-ray component, which may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV photons by particles accelerated at the WWC region. We propose one NuSTAR observation of WR140 around apastron to measure the intensity of the extremely hard X-ray component.                                                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM WR140 AROUND APASTRON                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WR140                                             @sè≈Åo@EË∫«À)@T1S&¡æ@Ó}VlÙ@tsÅ◊€ı@Ï‚pÕ´∏1@Ï‚¶?r‘£30601031002@Òe•àeîØ@Ò:ã«À)@Ú¢)˙¨ŸË@Ú¢…˙¨ŸËSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‚ƒöÌjí␀␀ËáHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀fThe wind-wind collision (WWC) in eccentric massive binary systems produces predictably variable shock-heated X-ray plasma. This collision provides an ideal laboratory for shock astrophysics, providing key constraints on how gas thermalizes at variable density and on particle acceleration. A series of NuSTAR observations of WR140 around periastron in 2016 has provided an interesting variation of the extremely hard X-ray component, which may originate from inverse-Compton scattering of stellar UV photons by particles accelerated at the WWC region. We propose one NuSTAR observation of WR140 around apastron to measure the intensity of the extremely hard X-ray component.                                                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Hamaguchi                     Kenji                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       EXTREMELY HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM WR140 AROUND APASTRON                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811AT2020xnd                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï‚¶?r‘£@Ï‚¶Õ´∏180701407001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‚ƒ[‡$i␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    WS`We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent discovery of X-rays with unprecedented properties from the FBOTs AT2018cow and CSS161010, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C22       Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of Rapid, Luminous, and Blue Stellar Explosions                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2020xnd                                         @tÔÎÌ˙C˛¿¡Ω•ù@M·©˚Álã¿G&?º@s≈L/É{J@Ï‚¶Õ´∏1@Ï‚ºÍM80701407002@‹Q¡¿ÎÌ˙@‹4U2a|@ﬁG¸π#£@ﬁGø¸π#£SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‚ƒDDDD␀␀ÁHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    WS`We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent discovery of X-rays with unprecedented properties from the FBOTs AT2018cow and CSS161010, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C22       Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of Rapid, Luminous, and Blue Stellar Explosions                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20200811GX_5m1_SADA_20309                                 @qOßÊö‘¿9‹∆?@}»K]Ã¿X≠´üUõ@tÃµsÍ≥@Ï‚ºÍM@Ï‚Ωî»)¯30602101001@Jÿ—∑X‚@JêÂ`Aâ7@LƒÌëhr∞@LƒÌëhr∞SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‚ƒ#E␀␀ËÉHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    FãıDust is a major component of ISM absorption. Chandra-HETG spectroscopy has revealed detailed signatures of this dust, specifically near the Si edge as seen in spectra of bright X-ray binary systems behind large neutral columns. Understanding these signatures helps reveal the composition and structure of dust in the ISM. This is complicated by the fact that some fraction of this absorption and dust may be local to the binary system, and thus the dust signatures will respond to the local radiative environment. Here we propose to revisit one such system, GX 5-1, with simultaneous Chandra-HETG/NuSTAR observations. Chandra-HETG will provide detailed spectroscopic descriptions of the dust signatures, while NuSTAR will provide descriptions of the hard X-ray continua affecting the dust.           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         M                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Distinguishing between Circumbinary and Interstellar Medium Dust Signatures in GX 5-1                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200811GX_5m1                                            @p„ﬂ§?Â…¿9À˚µt@àŒp:˚øËlÙ!-w@tıì›óˆ+@Ï‚Ωî»)¯@Ï‚Ã#Ü30602001007@æ~π7K∆®@¿9¯†ê.@’ì4m≈÷9@’í¥m≈÷9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï‚Ên¶ï␀␀ËÉHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    FãıDust is a major component of ISM absorption. Chandra-HETG spectroscopy has revealed detailed signatures of this dust, specifically near the Si edge as seen in spectra of bright X-ray binary systems behind large neutral columns. Understanding these signatures helps reveal the composition and structure of dust in the ISM. This is complicated by the fact that some fraction of this absorption and dust may be local to the binary system, and thus the dust signatures will respond to the local radiative environment. Here we propose to revisit one such system, GX 5-1, with simultaneous Chandra-HETG/NuSTAR observations. Chandra-HETG will provide detailed spectroscopic descriptions of the dust signatures, while NuSTAR will provide descriptions of the hard X-ray continua affecting the dust.           Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Nowak                         M                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Distinguishing between Circumbinary and Interstellar Medium Dust Signatures in GX 5-1                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀idiagnostics                                                                                                            archived  202008112MASXJ17431735p6250207                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï‚Ã#Ü@Ï‚ÃÍM60061281001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï‚ÊI‹YÅ␀␀Á!Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ17431735p6250207                            @pùz·GÆ@Ofzêñº@WMjO@?©„º”@qÙò«‚ÇA@Ï‚ÃÍM@Ï‚‹Õ´∏160061281002@÷†´o“Û@÷_9p£◊
