                                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC7479                                           @u£x‘˝Û∂@(ç‡qv@UvΩ<64¿Ei ¿Éo@t1QÎÖ∏@Ï‚˛ÍM@Ï„èFj60061316002@◊dπ#¢ú@÷‡_ˆî@ÿúwïÅ%@ÿúwïÅ%SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„"øÜ£␀␀Á!Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202008113C368                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï„èFj@Ï„±9Ò60665003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï„!ÏGL˝␀␀ËéHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀3The total accretion luminosity in the Universe is a key parameter for cosmology, but despite decades of study, the fraction hidden behind obscuring material is unknown. A Chandra survey of luminous, high-redshift, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that narrow-line radio galaxies (NLRGs) have 10-1000 times lower X-ray luminosities than quasars of the same radio luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, 25% are Compton-Thick (CT), and typical AGN surveys miss much of the accretion luminosity. The alternative hypothesis is a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. Either scenario is important for cosmology, and we propose NuSTAR observations of three CT candidates to distinguish between them.                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS ?                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C368                                             @pÙ7¥¢3ú@%¯∫«À)@B«»K]Ãd@.⁄QŒ_@s	ŸË>B[@Ï„±9Ò@Ï„8èFj60665003002@Ë)ÀüUõ=@ÁÙ%«y¶µ@ÈÔ(?Â…@ÈÒ(?Â…SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„OOtnæ␀␀ËéHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀3The total accretion luminosity in the Universe is a key parameter for cosmology, but despite decades of study, the fraction hidden behind obscuring material is unknown. A Chandra survey of luminous, high-redshift, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that narrow-line radio galaxies (NLRGs) have 10-1000 times lower X-ray luminosities than quasars of the same radio luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, 25% are Compton-Thick (CT), and typical AGN surveys miss much of the accretion luminosity. The alternative hypothesis is a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. Either scenario is important for cosmology, and we propose NuSTAR observations of three CT candidates to distinguish between them.                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS ?                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811Sct_X1                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï„8èFj@Ï„8[‰õø30601002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï„OÈ>î␀␀ËìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    FåSymbiotic X-ray binaries (SyXBs) are rare low mass X-ray binaries hosting a red giant and a neutron star (NS). Observations suggest that the NS is young and strongly magnetized, a fact that is difficult to reconcile with the several Gyr age of the system (to allow evolution to the red giant phase). Measuring cyclotron lines in SyXBs thus challenges theories of the NS magnetic field decay or demonstrate that highly magnetized white dwarfs exist and can collapse to strongly magnetized NS. A cyclotron line has been recently discovered from a SyXB, thus calling for a systematic search in other sources of this class. We propose a 30 ks-long observation with Chandra+NuSTAR of the faint SyXB Sct X-1, which faintness requires low background and high sensitivity instruments.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bozzo                         E                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Hunting for cyclotron lines in the faint symbiotic X-ray binary Sct X-1 with Chandra and NuSTAR                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Sct_X1                                            @qm\(ı¬è¿ö‘,<ûÌ@8'e˝ä⁄∫?”&¡Ω•@sÌ	‘ï+@Ï„8[‰õø@Ï„Oî»)¯30601002002@ﬂº[™ÕûÑ@ﬂxˇàeîØ@‡˚´üUõ@‡˚ß´üUõSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„bnuí␀␀ËìHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    FåSymbiotic X-ray binaries (SyXBs) are rare low mass X-ray binaries hosting a red giant and a neutron star (NS). Observations suggest that the NS is young and strongly magnetized, a fact that is difficult to reconcile with the several Gyr age of the system (to allow evolution to the red giant phase). Measuring cyclotron lines in SyXBs thus challenges theories of the NS magnetic field decay or demonstrate that highly magnetized white dwarfs exist and can collapse to strongly magnetized NS. A cyclotron line has been recently discovered from a SyXB, thus calling for a systematic search in other sources of this class. We propose a 30 ks-long observation with Chandra+NuSTAR of the faint SyXB Sct X-1, which faintness requires low background and high sensitivity instruments.                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bozzo                         E                             NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C21       Hunting for cyclotron lines in the faint symbiotic X-ray binary Sct X-1 with Chandra and NuSTAR                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811CD_Ind                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï„Oî»)¯@Ï„P?r‘£30601018001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï„bDtœ÷␀␀ËéHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÍBroadband X-ray spectra of polars and intermediate polars consist of multitemperature thermal plasma component,its reflection from white dwarf & 6.4 keV Fe fluorescence emission line. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR-XMM observation of CD Ind (an asynchronous polar) and Paloma (a special IP evolving from IP to polar)for the first time to robustly detect the reflection hump and resolve the 6.4 keV Fe fluorescence line. These observations will help us in having an idea of the geometry of accretion process. Also, we will determine the shock temperature to estimate the mass of WD and perform emission line spectroscopy to determine the temperature and density profile of the post-shock region. For both the sources, we request 50 ks of NuSTAR (Total 100 ks) & 25 ks of XMM observation (Total 50 ks).Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Dutta                         Anirban                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       INVESTIGATING COMPTON REFLECTION FOR TWO ASYNCHRONOUS MAGNETIC CVS CD IND AND PALOMA USING JOINT NUSTAR XMM OBSERVATION ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CD_Ind                                            @sÌ¯7¥¢4¿M\–©*0@u6‚Î¿Dò‚e+‘@t#≈Åo␀@Ï„P?r‘£@Ï„t[‰õø30601018002@Îî(óçOﬂ@Î_°•ú‡@ÌÖ-rGE9@ÌárGE9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„áq«r␀␀ËéHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀ÍBroadband X-ray spectra of polars and intermediate polars consist of multitemperature thermal plasma component,its reflection from white dwarf & 6.4 keV Fe fluorescence emission line. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR-XMM observation of CD Ind (an asynchronous polar) and Paloma (a special IP evolving from IP to polar)for the first time to robustly detect the reflection hump and resolve the 6.4 keV Fe fluorescence line. These observations will help us in having an idea of the geometry of accretion process. Also, we will determine the shock temperature to estimate the mass of WD and perform emission line spectroscopy to determine the temperature and density profile of the post-shock region. For both the sources, we request 50 ks of NuSTAR (Total 100 ks) & 25 ks of XMM observation (Total 50 ks).Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Dutta                         Anirban                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       INVESTIGATING COMPTON REFLECTION FOR TWO ASYNCHRONOUS MAGNETIC CVS CD IND AND PALOMA USING JOINT NUSTAR XMM OBSERVATION ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811AT2020xnd                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï„t[‰õø@Ï„tÍM80701407003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï„á?=—ª␀␀ÁHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    WS`We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent discovery of X-rays with unprecedented properties from the FBOTs AT2018cow and CSS161010, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C22       Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of Rapid, Luminous, and Blue Stellar Explosions                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2020xnd                                         @tÔµ'ª¿#¢úwök@MﬂOÑM¿G%VlÙ!@sµ¿Énò@Ï„tÍM@Ï„âÕ´∏180701407004@‹yÅŒ_p@‹1R˘€"—@ﬁè…ôôôö@ﬁèâôôôöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï„•Ì¸5␀␀ÁHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    WS`We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent discovery of X-rays with unprecedented properties from the FBOTs AT2018cow and CSS161010, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C22       Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of Rapid, Luminous, and Blue Stellar Explosions                                             ␀