   @ÏËPkùdÅ␀␀Ë∏Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀óMarkarian 817 is a Seyfert-1 AGN that has been observed to increase in X-ray flux by 1.5 orders of magnitude over decades. The conditions and geometry at work in the inner accretion flow in this source have not been studied in detail, though they may hold important clues to changing-look AGN, broad absorption line quasars (BALQSOs), and massive black hole accretion in general. Aspects of the UV and X-ray light curves from this source also raise fundamental questions. We propose to observe Mrk 817 for 120 ks in Cycle 6, to better understand the relationship between the corona and the disk, whether or not the inner disk is visible, and the role of geometry in shaping radiation and mechanical feedback in this important source.                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MRK 817: A KEY TO AGN EVOLUTION AND ACCRETION GEOMETRY                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200811MARKARIAN_817                                     @keF‹]cà@Me}øHÃ@Yç∏∫«@J∏␀␀␀␀␀@i!€Ùá¸π@ÏË&î»)¯@ÏËÅ±9Ò60601007002A␀Ñ":í£A␀^.f1¯°A™ç©ì·A´GC,•zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏË£Ω°/h␀␀Ë∏Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀óMarkarian 817 is a Seyfert-1 AGN that has been observed to increase in X-ray flux by 1.5 orders of magnitude over decades. The conditions and geometry at work in the inner accretion flow in this source have not been studied in detail, though they may hold important clues to changing-look AGN, broad absorption line quasars (BALQSOs), and massive black hole accretion in general. Aspects of the UV and X-ray light curves from this source also raise fundamental questions. We propose to observe Mrk 817 for 120 ks in Cycle 6, to better understand the relationship between the corona and the disk, whether or not the inner disk is visible, and the role of geometry in shaping radiation and mechanical feedback in this important source.                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MRK 817: A KEY TO AGN EVOLUTION AND ACCRETION GEOMETRY                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811UGC3478                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏËÅ±9Ò@ÏËÇ?r‘£60061068001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏË£\q«␀␀ÁKHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC3478                                           @XáAÚ◊s@OŸˇ.HËß@bÈŒ_o“@5ÂoiDg@S4ßÊö‘@ÏËÇ?r‘£@ÏËëÕ´∏160061068002@’+˜6zë@‘˘ñßÔù≤@÷ôlÍJå@÷õlÍJåSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏË£F”†m␀␀ÁKHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811NGC6921_MCG04                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏËëÕ´∏1@ÏËíî»)¯60662001005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏË£%§8!␀␀Ë∏Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC6921_MCG04                                     @s25?|Ìë@9¨ÁØ∑È@P≤M“Ò©¸¿Î6zêó@qµÄùIQÉ@ÏËíî»)¯@ÏË£[‰õø60662001006@‘Ãö($x@‘ïç◊€Ùà@÷hà␀␀␀␀@÷hà␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏË√e*€6␀␀Ë∏Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀¡For more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. The peak variability periods also match unveiling events found with NuSTAR. We propose a volume limited variability study of the nearest and brightest CT AGN that have only been observed with NuSTAR a single time. These observations will test the frequency of variability in CT AGN, double the available NuSTAR data for CTs with multiple observations, and test whether the >10 keV variability is related to changes in column density or accretion luminosity.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       MIRROR, MIRROR, ON THE WALL: CONSTRAINING REFLECTION IN THE BRIGHTEST COMPTON THICK AGN ABOVE 10 KEV                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                            