                       ␀␀úA Large Program (1.58 Ms) has been granted in XMM-Newton AO18 and 27 orbits have been awarded in HST Cycle 27 to study Supermassive Black Hole Winds in X-rays (SUBWAYS), designed to provide direct support on feedback models by deriving constraints on the energetics and duty cycle of Ultra-Fast Outflows (UFOs) in Active Galactic Nuclei. We ask now for NuSTAR observations of the 20 SUBWAYS sources (630 ks) without archival NuSTAR data. The availability of NuSTAR data above 10 keV is crucial for the objectives of SUBWAYS, and will maximise the outcome of this ambitious Large Program. The proposed observations represent a fundamental step to maintain NuSTAR science upfront during the next decade in the physically motivated investigation of multi-phase outflows.                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BUILDING SUBWAYS: THE NUSTAR LINE                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG1435m067                                        @ktöu¿◊
=p£◊@u}&Èx‘˛@G≥…ÓÀ˚@f44ÍK@ÏÈ0ÍM@ÏÈA#Ü60661005002@‘ù?Â…@‘p∞Ú{≥@÷‹Ñµ‹∆@÷úÑµ‹∆SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÈd¬ß°Ú␀␀Ë∆Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀úA Large Program (1.58 Ms) has been granted in XMM-Newton AO18 and 27 orbits have been awarded in HST Cycle 27 to study Supermassive Black Hole Winds in X-rays (SUBWAYS), designed to provide direct support on feedback models by deriving constraints on the energetics and duty cycle of Ultra-Fast Outflows (UFOs) in Active Galactic Nuclei. We ask now for NuSTAR observations of the 20 SUBWAYS sources (630 ks) without archival NuSTAR data. The availability of NuSTAR data above 10 keV is crucial for the objectives of SUBWAYS, and will maximise the outcome of this ambitious Large Program. The proposed observations represent a fundamental step to maintain NuSTAR science upfront during the next decade in the physically motivated investigation of multi-phase outflows.                                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bianchi                       Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BUILDING SUBWAYS: THE NUSTAR LINE                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811GX_301m2                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÈA#Ü@ÏÈAÕ´∏130601013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÈdü#E␀␀Ë∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀£GX 301-2 is a known Cyclotron Resonant Scattering Feature (CRSF) source but was only recently shown to exhibit not only one but two CRSFs at ~35 keV and ~50 keV. The non-harmonic spacing of the CRSFs and their different pulse-phase evolution make the system particularly interesting. Fuerst et al. (2018) suggest a scenario, where both CRSFs are formed at different locations inside one accretion column. We propose an 80 ks observation at a luminosity five times higher than used by Fuerst et al. (2018) to directly test their hypothesis that the observed CRSF behavior can be explained with a single accretion column. We will measure energy shifts and the pulse-phase evolution of both CRSFs to within 6% and compare our results to predictions of the single column model for higher luminosities.   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ballhausen                    Ralf                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       HOW MANY ACCRETION COLUMNS DO WE SEE IN GX 301-2?                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_301m2                                          @gX‚e+‘¿O^ßÔù≤-@r¿-‡q?π*0U2a|@d≤˝!ˇ.I@ÏÈAÕ´∏1@ÏÈ~ÍM30601013002@ÁÆ$îFsÇ@ÁÚMCïÅ@ÔÊCe+”√@ÔÁË1¯†êSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÈÇ®3îJ␀␀Ë∆Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀£GX 301-2 is a known Cyclotron Resonant Scattering Feature (CRSF) source but was only recently shown to exhibit not only one but two CRSFs at ~35 keV and ~50 keV. The non-harmonic spacing of the CRSFs and their different pulse-phase evolution make the system particularly interesting. Fuerst et al. (2018) suggest a scenario, where both CRSFs are formed at different locations inside one accretion column. We propose an 80 ks observation at a luminosity five times higher than used by Fuerst et al. (2018) to directly test their hypothesis that the observed CRSF behavior can be explained with a single accretion column. We will measure energy shifts and the pulse-phase evolution of both CRSFs to within 6% and compare our results to predictions of the single column model for higher luminosities.   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ballhausen                    Ralf                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       HOW MANY ACCRETION COLUMNS DO WE SEE IN GX 301-2?                                                                       ␀␀¬␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811PKS_0537m286                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÈ~ÍM@ÏÈî»)¯80601602001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÈÇbÉ9E␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose a 100 ks ToO observation of a high-redshift (z>=3) blazar during a gamma-ray flare detected by Fermi/LAT. Only 12 high-redshift blazars have been detected in gamma-rays. These objects are the most luminous and massive objects in the Universe, which allow to study the formation of active galactic nuclei in the early Universe. Because the high-energy hump of their SED peaks in the MeV range, X-ray and gamma-ray data is needed to constrain the hump's shape. We propose to get high-sensitivity X-ray data during enhanced gamma-ray activity of a blazar at high redshift. The data will be complemented by triggered radio observations. The resulting multiwavelength data set will yield important knowledge on the behaviour of flaring MeV blazars for future MeV telescopes.                    Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Gokus                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       Gamma-ray flares from supermassive black holes at high redshift                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_0537m286                                      @U@åÁØ∏¿<≥˛\ë—N@m'RT`™¿;qué!ñS@pOÑM:ì@ÏÈî»)¯@ÏÈ [‰õø80601602002@˜¥T…Ö@˜Ä[Ov@˘fõô0æ@˘h€ô0æSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÈ„{rÍb␀␀ËHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀We propose a 100 ks ToO observation of a high-redshift (z>=3) blazar during a gamma-ray flare detected by Fermi/LAT. Only 12 high-redshift blazars have been detected in gamma-rays. These objects are the most luminous and massive objects in the Universe, which allow to study the formation of active galactic nuclei in the early Universe. Because the high-energy hump of their SED peaks in the MeV range, X-ray and gamma-ray data is needed to constrain the hump's shape. We propose to get high-sensitivity X-ray data during enhanced gamma-ray activity of a blazar at high redshift. The data will be complemented by triggered radio observations. The resulting multiwavelength data set will yield important knowledge on the behaviour of flaring MeV blazars for future MeV telescopes.                    Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Gokus                         Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       Gamma-ray flares from supermassive black holes at high redshift                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200811MAXI_J1848m015                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÈ [‰õø@ÏÈÀ±9Ò90601341001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÈ„-RLú␀␀ÁYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1848m015                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÈÀ±9Ò@ÏÈÂ±9Ò90601341002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÍû–ó¥␀␀ÁYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ToO / No CHU4                                                                                                           archived  20150316M82                                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÈÂ±9Ò@ÏÈÊî»)¯30602028001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÍu⁄’%␀␀Ë‘Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀EThe galaxy M82 harbors two notable ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX): M82 X-2, the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), and M82 X-1, a IMBH candidate. The extreme luminosity of ULXs, and the observation of strong winds from these sources, suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star, and should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing in M82 X-2, and study in detail the quasi-periodic oscillations from both ULXs.                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BROADBAND TIMING OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCES IN M82: ORBITAL DECAY, QPOS, AND MORE.                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82                                               @bü	kπå~@QnOv`@a´Û∂E°À@D.êˇó$t@]˛ìtº@ÏÈÊî»)¯@ÏÍ?r‘£30602028002@4‘D–©@˘<64@ÒVı‹]cà@ÒY•‹]càSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÍ#\äÂ␀␀Ë‘Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀EThe galaxy M82 harbors two notable ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX): M82 X-2, the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), and M82 X-1, a IMBH candidate. The extreme luminosity of ULXs, and the observation of strong winds from these sources, suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star, and should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing in M82 X-2, and study in detail the quasi-periodic oscillations from both ULXs.                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BROADBAND TIMING OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCES IN M82: ORBITAL DECAY, QPOS, AND MORE.                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20200811VIIZw742                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÍ?r‘£@ÏÍ±9Ò60160676003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÍ##uÛ␀␀ÁYHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316VIIZw742                                          @p≠üUõ=@Q%†'RTa@X≤v»¥9X@>ÌjOv@l¶º£n@ÏÍ±9Ò@ÏÍ#î»)¯60160676004@ﬁ®U„Ω@ﬁ`Mˆ˝!ˇ@‡W∫^5?}@‡W⁄^5?}SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÍdπ␀␀ÁYHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811PG0026p129                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÍ#î»)¯@ÏÍ$[‰õø60663003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÍc‡ŒQ)␀␀Ë∆Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˝Hereby we propose an X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN by observing three of them with NuSTAR : PG 0953+414 (70ks), NGC 4748 (80ks) and PG0026+129 (140ks). These object are part of the best sample available, SEAMBHs, in which all the objects have the black hole masses estimated by reverberation mapping. The use of the mass estimated by reverberation mapping makes the estimation of the Eddington ratio extremely reliable. These sources are the ones of this sample with the highest 2-10 keV flux that have not been observed by NuSTAR . The aim of these observations is to better understand the behavior of the primary power-law and the reprocessed radiation in the regime of extreme accretion, as well as the property of soft-excess and/or the outflows.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tortosa                       Alessia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       X-RAY STUDY OF EXTREME ACCRETION ONTO SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES WITH NUSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG0026p129                                        @$›/üæ@*vá+J@\ä˚~êˇó¿H≠œ™ÕûÑ@sg·∞â†'@ÏÍ$[‰õø@ÏÍè±9Ò60663003002A˝p=•ùA”gΩ•ùAC∏+üUõAD¯+üUõSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÍ¢%ïn␀␀Ë∆Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀˝Hereby we propose an X-ray study of super-Eddington AGN by observing three of them with NuSTAR : PG 0953+414 (70ks), NGC 4748 (80ks) and PG0026+129 (140ks). These object are part of the best sample available, SEAMBHs, in which all the objects have the black hole masses estimated by reverberation mapping. The use of the mass estimated by reverberation mapping makes the estimation of the Eddington ratio extremely reliable. These sources are the ones of this sample with the highest 2-10 keV flux that have not been observed by NuSTAR . The aim of these observations is to better understand the behavior of the primary power-law and the reprocessed radiation in the regime of extreme accretion, as well as the property of soft-excess and/or the outflows.                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tortosa                       Alessia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       X-RAY STUDY OF EXTREME ACCRETION ONTO SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLES WITH NUSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811Cen_X4                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÍè±9Ò@ÏÍëèFj30502024013␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÍ°ª£uÛ␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                         