ude, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SS_Cyg                                            @t[nóçOﬂ@E≈<64@Vó∆ßÔù≤¿ä⁄πıY¥@p$Aâ7K«@ÏÌz[‰õø@ÏÌçÕ´∏190701304002@ŸªV’œ´@ŸôéÇ@∑Ä@‹≤Ë›/†@‹≥(›/†SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÌ•-RLú␀␀ÁuHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200811SRGA_J124402d9m632235                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÌçÕ´∏1@ÏÌè#Ü80660301001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÌ•W¸˜␀␀Ë*Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÈThe eRosita X-ray survey started last year is expected to discover several millions of new sources and double the number of known Galactic X-ray binaries (XRBs). This will help to clarify several long-standing questions related to numbers, origin, and evolution of XRBs and massive stars in general, and accretion physics in low-luminosity XRBs. To fully realise this potential, follow-up observations in X-ray and other bands are, however, essential. Here we propose to start the exploration of this new dataset with NuSTAR follow-up of up to four select High mass X-ray binary (HMXB) candidates discovered by eRosita within current Cycle 6 period with the aim to confirm identifications, and study their broadband X-ray properties in detail.                                                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       Follow-up of X-ray binaries discovered by eROSITA                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SRGA_J124402d9m632235                             @g„¬è\(ˆ¿OØˆîFt@r‡„º”øŸQÇ©ì·@gÿ1¯†ê.@ÏÌè#Ü@ÏÌ±#Ü80660301002@Íbö7¥¢4@Í&D¿Éo@Ï=4ÎC-@Ï>‘ÎC-SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÌ√2Ó␀␀Ë*Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀ÈThe eRosita X-ray survey started last year is expected to discover several millions of new sources and double the number of known Galactic X-ray binaries (XRBs). This will help to clarify several long-standing questions related to numbers, origin, and evolution of XRBs and massive stars in general, and accretion physics in low-luminosity XRBs. To fully realise this potential, follow-up observations in X-ray and other bands are, however, essential. Here we propose to start the exploration of this new dataset with NuSTAR follow-up of up to four select High mass X-ray binary (HMXB) candidates discovered by eRosita within current Cycle 6 period with the aim to confirm identifications, and study their broadband X-ray properties in detail.                                                         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Doroshenko                    Victor                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       Follow-up of X-ray binaries discovered by eROSITA                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200811HS_1630p2355                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÌ±#Ü@ÏÌ≤èFj60601012003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÌ¬„ÔP␀␀Ë‚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀…High luminosity quasars are unique laboratories to test our understanding of X-ray coronae in AGN. We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of a luminous, optically-selected, non-jetted quasar at z=0.821. Our main goal is to measure the high-energy cutoff in its X-ray spectrum, hence constraining, in the framework of Comptonization models, its coronal temperature and optical depth. This will allow us to test whether the same physical conditions hold in quasars at luminosities and masses 100 times higher than those probed in so far in the nearby Universe, exploring the unknown X-ray properties of the quasar population at intermediate redshifts (z = 0.1-1), constituting half the age of the Universe.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kammoun                       Elias                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       THE CORONAL PROPERTIES OF AN EXCEPTIONALLY LUMINOUS QUASAR AT Z = 0.821                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                           