4m≈÷9@7◊_o“Û@EOv`@D7Ôù≤-@fsáì›óˆ@ÏÌ≤èFj@ÏÌ˛Õ´∏160601012004@¯…Ãúæ‡@¯ç≈E° ¡@˙êÜSéÛM@˙ë6SéÛMSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÓ~c]≠␀␀Ë‚Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀…High luminosity quasars are unique laboratories to test our understanding of X-ray coronae in AGN. We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of a luminous, optically-selected, non-jetted quasar at z=0.821. Our main goal is to measure the high-energy cutoff in its X-ray spectrum, hence constraining, in the framework of Comptonization models, its coronal temperature and optical depth. This will allow us to test whether the same physical conditions hold in quasars at luminosities and masses 100 times higher than those probed in so far in the nearby Universe, exploring the unknown X-ray properties of the quasar population at intermediate redshifts (z = 0.1-1), constituting half the age of the Universe.                                                                                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kammoun                       Elias                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       THE CORONAL PROPERTIES OF AN EXCEPTIONALLY LUMINOUS QUASAR AT Z = 0.821                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202008112MASSJ15213967p0337292                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÌ˛Õ´∏1@ÏÌˇ?r‘£60160615001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÓ-Çÿ.␀␀ÁuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASSJ15213967p0337292                            @lŒ WßÜ¬@*d¬¯7µ@¨q≤ñ@GÆ¬&ÄùI@ee$›/@ÏÌˇ?r‘£@ÏÓxVb‹60160615002@’_ò_˜@’*[W>´6@÷Òﬁqﬁi≠@÷Ûﬁqﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÓ$r(␀␀ÁuHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811RBS_1055                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÓxVb‹@ÏÓèFj60601015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÓ#‰h¨Ò␀␀ËÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÎAn observation of the luminous quasar RBS 1055 is requested, for a net observing time of 250 ks. We aim at studying the yet poorly investigated emission of this object in the hard X-rays, which exhibits very interesting spectral features in the existing XMM and Swift-BAT data. Recent XMM observations of RBS 1055 show an intense Fe Ka emission line, one of the few ever detected at these luminosities. This is the most promising candidate for studying the Compton reflection parameters in very luminous quasars. Furthermore, the proposed NuSTAR observation will lead to a very accurate measurement of a high energy curvature of the primary continuum, which have been obtained so far only in three other sources with redshift z>0.4.                                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marinucci                     Andrea                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       HARD X-RAY SPECTRAL FEATURES OF THE LUMINOUS QUASAR RBS 1055                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RBS_1055                                          @f~ WßÜ¬¿3˘·∞â†'@qÂƒ2 W®@Dß‚Ç@∑Ä@h8fffff@ÏÓèFj@ÏÓ_?r‘£60601015002@˙äZª/ÏW@˙K´˚~ë␀@¸`«ûÉ‰&@¸b⁄kP∞ÚSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÓd6Â‘ƒ␀␀ËÈHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀ÎAn observation of the luminous quasar RBS 1055 is requested, for a net observing time of 250 ks. We aim at studying the yet poorly investigated emission of this object in the hard X-rays, which exhibits very interesting spectral features in the existing XMM and Swift-BAT data. Recent XMM observations of RBS 1055 show an intense Fe Ka emission line, one of the few ever detected at these luminosities. This is the most promising candidate for studying the Compton reflection parameters in very luminous quasars. Furthermore, the proposed NuSTAR observation will lead to a very accurate measurement of a high energy curvature of the primary continuum, which have been obtained so far only in three other sources with redshift z>0.4.                                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀