	ﬁSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔ*1M¿␀␀Á|Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811IRAS_F23365p3604                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÓÔÕ´∏1@ÏÓ[‰õø60667002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔ	2q␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀OUltraluminous infrared galaxies (ULIRGs; LIR > 10^12 Lsun) are the most intrinsically luminous galaxies in the low-redshift Universe, yet the power source of ULIRGs (AGN vs starburst) is still debated. We propose NuSTAR observations of 7 local ULIRGs to, 1) establish the first complete, unbiased sample with which to assess (at E>10 keV) the role of BH accretion in powering these luminous systems, and 2) resolve significant disagreements in the AGN luminosities and obscuring columns for the individual systems. These observations will be used to perform detailed studies of individual systems, linking the AGN properties observed outflows, and determine the global importance of AGN activity at the highest galaxy luminosities.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       GAZING INTO THE INFERNO: EMBEDDED AGN IN LOCAL ULIRGS                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_F23365p3604                                  @v,"h	‘ï@B&±ƒ2 X@ZÆa|⁄Q¿8]:í£@qsOv_ÿÆ@ÏÓ[‰õø@ÏÔèFj60667002002@Íü≠
=p§@ÍYπX‚e@ÏzbÀ˚µ@ÏzÀ˚µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔ#BøÁ∫␀␀ËHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀OUltraluminous infrared galaxies (ULIRGs; LIR > 10^12 Lsun) are the most intrinsically luminous galaxies in the low-redshift Universe, yet the power source of ULIRGs (AGN vs starburst) is still debated. We propose NuSTAR observations of 7 local ULIRGs to, 1) establish the first complete, unbiased sample with which to assess (at E>10 keV) the role of BH accretion in powering these luminous systems, and 2) resolve significant disagreements in the AGN luminosities and obscuring columns for the individual systems. These observations will be used to perform detailed studies of individual systems, linking the AGN properties observed outflows, and determine the global importance of AGN activity at the highest galaxy luminosities.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Privon                        George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       GAZING INTO THE INFERNO: EMBEDDED AGN IN LOCAL ULIRGS                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀PID 06223                                                                                                               archived  20200811Deep_background_NEP                               @p·N§®¡U@P£nóçP@XüUõ=@=«e˝ä⁄∫@gÓìtºj@ÏÔèFj@ÏÔî»)¯10701001001@s(Ov_Ÿ@r˛zxl"h@tÅ5®Xy>@tÅ5®Xy>SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔ#
œX␀␀Á¶Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20200811Deep_background_NEP                               @p‡1&Èx’@P£X‚e@XßÜ¬&Å@=ŒÉ‰%ÆÊ@g‡ˇó$tT@ÏÔî»)¯@ÏÔ9èFj10701001002@ÔÜIe+”√@ÔH”Ø∑È@ˇ'ı¬è\@Ò␀Wı¬è\SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔEªrÍb␀␀Á¶Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Deep background calibration / idiagnostics                                                                              archived  20200811TXS_0506p056                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔ9èFj@ÏÔ9ÍM60602004011␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔEà†ŒQ␀␀Ë8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀¥<There exist compelling evidence for a correlation of the high energy IceCube neutrino event IC-170922A and the blazar TXS 0506+056 observed in its flaring state by instruments spanning the whole electromagnetic spectrum. This object is up to date our best candidate for a neutrino and cosmic ray emitting source. We propose regular deep observations of the multi-messenger blazar TXS0506+056 to be preformed together by Swift, NuSTAR and MAGIC, and other MAGIC MWL partners. We ask for 22 5ks Swift observations (110ks in total) and 6 25ks NuSTAR observations (150 ks in total). The proposed Swift and NuSTAR observations are essential to characterise the X-ray emission of this source, which in turn is crucial to understand and distinguish the leptonic and hadronic processes at play.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Satalecka                     Konstancja                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀S16       OBSERVATIONS OF THE MULTIMESSENGER BLAZAR TXS 0506+056 WITH SWIFT, NUSTAR AND MAGIC                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_0506p056                                      @SU@N§©@†qué@hiS&¡æ¿3ÓVìu@t&h€ã¨q@ÏÔ9ÍM@ÏÔMèFj60602004012@Ÿ7}Å$›@ÿ˛J#9¡@€§Ú{≤ˇ@€§Ú{≤ˇSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔd•‘√≥␀␀Ë8Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀¥<There exist compelling evidence for a correlation of the high energy IceCube neutrino event IC-170922A and the blazar TXS 0506+056 observed in its flaring state by instruments spanning the whole electromagnetic spectrum. This object is up to date our best candidate for a neutrino and cosmic ray emitting source. We propose regular deep observations of the multi-messenger blazar TXS0506+056 to be preformed together by Swift, NuSTAR and MAGIC, and other MAGIC MWL partners. We ask for 22 5ks Swift observations (110ks in total) and 6 25ks NuSTAR observations (150 ks in total). The proposed Swift and NuSTAR observations are essential to characterise the X-ray emission of this source, which in turn is crucial to understand and distinguish the leptonic and hadronic processes at play.              Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Satalecka                     Konstancja                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀S16       OBSERVATIONS OF THE MULTIMESSENGER BLAZAR TXS 0506+056 WITH SWIFT, NUSTAR AND MAGIC                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift MAGIC                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀(6/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20200811XTE_J1859p226                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔMèFj@ÏÔN#Ü90701305001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔdÇ.E␀␀ÁÉHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316XTE_J1859p226                                     @qÀÑM:ì@6ßs¸PH@J¯ÿD–©@!z)«y¶µ@gœÈ˘rG@ÏÔN#Ü@ÏÔmÍM90701305002@‰:Ì(ç@„””CïÅ@ÂrùˆîF@ÂsΩˆîFSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔÑ!_Û›␀␀ÁÉHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20200811NGC454E                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔmÍM@ÏÔo#Ü60760003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔÉÔhK⁄␀␀ËÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ZaçWe propose a survey of 7 highly obscured Active Galactic Nuclei (logNh>23) to find or set limits at the 10% level on their extended hard (2-7 keV) continuum and Fe-K emission. The Chandra discovery that 10-30% of the 2-7 keV continuum from high Nh AGN can be extended on scales from ~100 pc up to ~2 kpc affects the structure of the obscuring torus determined from NuSTAR spectra, and demonstrates the AGN impact on the host galaxy, i.e. AGN feedback. By completing a sample of 20 BASS high Nh AGN we can look for predictors of hard extent; their feedback duty cycle may also be estimated on ~1000 year timescales.                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Elvis                         Martin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C22       THE TORUS AND THE HOST -2: Extended Hard Emission in Heavily Obscured AGN                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC454E                                           @2Ç:)«yß¿K¥/É{J#@rà;Õ5®X¿N´=»K^?ˆ¢3úæ‡@ÏÔo#Ü@ÏÔxVb‹60760003002@ÿ ÁÜ¬&Å@ÿóÎI∫@⁄x