sition), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  2015031687GB011040d5p250307                               @2Súæﬂ§@9ES&¡æ@`˘€"–Â¿Bº®¡T…Ü@r›Ì˙C˛]@ÏÔÄ[‰õø@ÏÔè#Ü60160045002@’˙ö‘,=@‘”µÌ(åÁ@÷j˚4÷°b@÷k˚4÷°bSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔ≠hı¬è␀␀ÁÉHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811BZQ_J0805p6144                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔè#Ü@ÏÔèÕ´∏160601032001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔ≠Hπ␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀hWe request to observe 2 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 50 ks. Detected in gamma-rays and by Swift-BAT, these sources are some of the most powerful of their class. We propose to observe it in the X-ray with NuSTAR, which will allow us to accurately measure the rising part of the inverse Compton (IC) spectrum up to 70 keV, expected to be very hard. Together with the LAT data, NuSTAR data will be fundamental to pinpointing the IC peak, determining the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZQ_J0805p6144                                    @^MÎÖ∏R@NﬁÈx‘˝Ù@c_}øHÃ@@
è\(ı√@=}˙C˛\í@ÏÔèÕ´∏1@ÏÔ©?r‘£60601032002@‚4µ∆ßÔû@‚È8}ø@„rEŒ_@„sÖŒ_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔƒ~¨/␀␀ËHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀hWe request to observe 2 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 50 ks. Detected in gamma-rays and by Swift-BAT, these sources are some of the most powerful of their class. We propose to observe it in the X-ray with NuSTAR, which will allow us to accurately measure the rising part of the inverse Compton (IC) spectrum up to 70 keV, expected to be very hard. Together with the LAT data, NuSTAR data will be fundamental to pinpointing the IC peak, determining the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHASING THE MOST POWERFUL BLAZARS                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              SARA Fermi Swift                                                                                    ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202008114U_1538m52                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔ©?r‘£@ÏÔ™xVb‹30602024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔƒT√≤¢␀␀Ë˜Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀%The puzzling variety of super-orbital modulations seen in the long term light-curves of Supergiant High Mass X-ray binaries (sgHMXBs), opens up new questions on the stellar wind interaction with a highly magnetised neutron star. We propose a joint 40 ks NuSTAR + 10 ks NICER observation of the persistent sgHMXB 4U 1538-52 at its super-orbital maximum and minimum to conclusively understand the mechanisms driving these flux modulations. The results of energy resolved pulsation studies, pulse phase resolved spectroscopic study of photon indices, absorption column density and cyclotron lines will be used to differentiate or constrain the various models explaining these super-orbital modulations.                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Islam                         Nazma                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       UNRAVELLING THE ENIGMATIC SUPER-ORBITAL MODULATION OBSERVED IN 4U 1538-52                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1538m52                                        @muÖoiD¿J.öu@tu}øHÄ@’œ™ÕûÑ@d´J#9¿Ï@ÏÔ™xVb‹@ÏÔª[‰õø30602024002@’L„º”[@’(ÀUõ=@◊ñ ⁄Q@◊ó†⁄QSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔƒ9÷H␀␀Ë˜Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀%The puzzling variety of super-orbital modulations seen in the long term light-curves of Supergiant High Mass X-ray binaries (sgHMXBs), opens up new questions on the stellar wind interaction with a highly magnetised neutron star. We propose a joint 40 ks NuSTAR + 10 ks NICER observation of the persistent sgHMXB 4U 1538-52 at its super-orbital maximum and minimum to conclusively understand the mechanisms driving these flux modulations. The results of energy resolved pulsation studies, pulse phase resolved spectroscopic study of photon indices, absorption column density and cyclotron lines will be used to differentiate or constrain the various models explaining these super-orbital modulations.                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Islam                         Nazma                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       UNRAVELLING THE ENIGMATIC SUPER-ORBITAL MODULATION OBSERVED IN 4U 1538-52                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/2) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20200811IGR_J17391m3021                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔª[‰õø@ÏÔªÕ´∏130601023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔƒkTì␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXTs) are a subset of HMXBs showing sporadic outbursts that've fascinated X-ray community and many theories attempt to explain their erratic behaviour.The most direct way to disentangle these theories is to estimate magnetic field of SFXTs through cyclotron resonant scattering features (CRSFs).We propose first NuSTAR observation of SFXT IGR J17391-3021 for 40ks during its outburst at periastron to confirm hints of CRSF at 30keV seen by INTEGRAL and Suzaku. We will also study hard X-ray properties of IGR J17391-3021 in the context of other SFXTs and superslow pulsars.In an unlikely event of quiescence at periastron, we will characterize the quiescence spectrum of IGR J17391-3021 with greatest accuracy yet made possible by large area of FPMs.               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pradhan                       Pragati                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHARACTERIZING THE HARD X-RAY SPECTRUM OF SFXT IGR J17391-3021 WITH NUSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J17391m3021                                   @pçe˝ä⁄∫¿>Oÿ≠´üV@v_v»¥9X?ÂÔù≤-V@c∫^5?}@ÏÔªÕ´∏1@ÏÔ€?r‘£30601023002@‰rª}øHÄ@‰C¡ä	@Â˘müUõ=@Â¯ÌüUõ=SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÔ‰ã⁄˜␀␀ËHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀#Supergiant Fast X-ray Transients (SFXTs) are a subset of HMXBs showing sporadic outbursts that've fascinated X-ray community and many theories attempt to explain their erratic behaviour.The most direct way to disentangle these theories is to estimate magnetic field of SFXTs through cyclotron resonant scattering features (CRSFs).We propose first NuSTAR observation of SFXT IGR J17391-3021 for 40ks during its outburst at periastron to confirm hints of CRSF at 30keV seen by INTEGRAL and Suzaku. We will also study hard X-ray properties of IGR J17391-3021 in the context of other SFXTs and superslow pulsars.In an unlikely event of quiescence at periastron, we will characterize the quiescence spectrum of IGR J17391-3021 with greatest accuracy yet made possible by large area of FPMs.               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pradhan                       Pragati                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHARACTERIZING THE HARD X-RAY SPECTRUM OF SFXT IGR J17391-3021 WITH NUSTAR                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20200811ABELL_2029                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÔ€?r‘£@ÏÔ€Õ´∏170660001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÔ‰]|∆ª␀␀Ë˜Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀›Galaxy clusters are the most massive virialized objects in the universe, and their growth over cosmic time is sensitive to the underlying cosmology. Cluster cosmology requires accurate mass measurements, which generally depend on temperature measurements. Unfortunately, the main sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. The hard band sensitivity of NuSTAR, however, has the potential to resolve this discrepancy and reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose deep observations of 4 low redshift, relaxed clusters, with extensive XMM and Chandra (and future XRISM) data, where both calibration and physical differences can be assessed in the simplest possible systems.                                      Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ACCURATE GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: A LEGACY DATASET WITH CHANDRA, XMM, AND XRISM                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀     