4                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÒÕ?r‘£@ÏÒÕÍM60665001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÒ„Ögâ¨␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀3The total accretion luminosity in the Universe is a key parameter for cosmology, but despite decades of study, the fraction hidden behind obscuring material is unknown. A Chandra survey of luminous, high-redshift, radio-loud 3CRR AGN found that narrow-line radio galaxies (NLRGs) have 10-1000 times lower X-ray luminosities than quasars of the same radio luminosity. If obscuration causes the X-ray weakness, 50% of luminous AGN are obscured, 25% are Compton-Thick (CT), and typical AGN surveys miss much of the accretion luminosity. The alternative hypothesis is a new population of X-ray-weak AGN. Either scenario is important for cosmology, and we propose NuSTAR observations of three CT candidates to distinguish between them.                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kuraszkiewicz                 Joanna                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       WHAT FRACTION OF POWERFUL RADIO GALAXIES IS COMPTON-THICK IN X-RAYS ?                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C324                                             @mØ⁄QŒ@5w_o“Û@AÄÀ)^û@H≠øHÀí@c$‡uˆ˝"@ÏÒÕÍM@ÏÒÎ±9Ò60665001002@‰u3˛\ë—@‰AÜ˘	k∫@ÂËM©*0U@ÂËç©*0USCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÚ:O§˙␀␀ÈHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                         