                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÚÎèFj@ÏÚÎ±9Ò60160681001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÛ&~¨/␀␀ÁüHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ18074992p1120494                            @q␀üæv…@&¿uˆ˝!ˇ@CÊö‘,@-„,•zxl@c»!ñRΩ<@ÏÚÎ±9Ò@ÏÚ˙[‰õø60160681002@“¨qué"@“Äè‚Ç@∏@‘DÃÃÃÕ@‘ÙÃÃÃÕSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÛØ˘Ô␀␀ÁüHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210210CIZA_0107d7p5408                                  @1Ω•ú‡@Kâ†'RT@_Mõ=»K¿!≤ïÈ·∞ä@pÁâ7K∆®@ÏÚ˙[‰õø@ÏÚ˚#Ü70601004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÛ£◊
␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀SGalaxy cluster mergers provide key insights into the way dark matter behaves. In rare cases, there can be a dissociative merger in which the baryonic matter and dark matter are separated noticeably, as is the case with the Bullet Cluster and CIZA0107.7+5408. Our objective is to constrain the self interacting dark matter (sigma_SIDM) cross section in CIZA0107 by using the methods first discussed in Markevitch et al. 2004 and applied in Randall et al. 2016. We will determine the temperature jump in a region where archival Chandra data suggests the presence of a shock front and use this to calculate the Mach number and gas merger velocity, which will be used to find sigma_SIDM in numerical simulations. We will also rule out this temperature spike being due to non-thermal emission.            Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Rojas                         Randall                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CONSTRAINING THE NATURE OF DARK MATTER WITH CIZA 0107.7+5408                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CIZA_0107d7p5408                                  @0˚'ª/Ï@K
