¶L@⁄∂ñÇ@∑Ä@⁄π÷Ç@∑ÄSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏıÎùdÅ␀␀Á¶Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315Cen_X4                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÛx#Ü@ÏÛy#Ü30502024015␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÛÅ±3ı␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cen_X4                                            @lj~˘€#¿?•õ=»K@t¡QÇ©ì@8$tSéÛ@fø/ÏV’–@ÏÛy#Ü@ÏÛùÍM30502024016@ËÔ¶êñº@Ë¥I•ú‡@Í£å›/†@Í§Ã›/†SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÛ≤ªB^—␀␀ÈHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀dWe propose a campaign of joint NuSTAR and NICER observations of the quiescent neutron star Cen X-4, to study the relationship between its soft X-ray flux level and its hard X-ray spectrum. The hard X-ray spectrum has been hypothesized to be due to thermal bremsstrahlung from active accretion onto the neutron star, and may transit to synchrotron emission at low flux. If the bremsstrahlung/active accretion hypothesis is correct, we should be able to measure spectral changes with our proposed campaign.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    NOWAK                         MICHAEL                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N05       REVEALING THE RELATION BETWEEN HARD AND SOFT QUIESCENT X-RAY SPECTRA IN CEN X-4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(8/10)                                                                                                                  archived  20210210MCG_p04_22_042                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏÛùÍM@ÏÛü?r‘£60602018003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÛ≤ä=p§␀␀ÈSHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀MCG +04-22-042 is a Seyfert 1.2 AGN which shows evidence of significant hard X-ray variability. Curiously, previous analyses of the Swift BAT and a short NuSTAR observation suggest that the source shows increased variability at high energies (above 20keV) than at lower energies. Archival data also suggest that the spectrum appears harder when the source is brighter. These behaviours have been observed in radio loud AGN, but are extremely atypical for radio quiet, type-1 AGN. We propose to monitor the hard X-ray flux of this source using 3x40ks observations of the source throughout AO6. NuSTAR can uniquely provide the opportunity to study the source on short and long timescales across a wide energy range,and permit the search for spectral changes associated with the variability.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHARACTERISING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF THE SEYFERT 1.2 GALAXY MCG +04-22-042                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_p04_22_042                                    @aú<ûÏø±@6·—N;Õ6@iª)^û	@Ee„º”[?’XbM“Ú@ÏÛü?r‘£@ÏÛΩÕ´∏160602018004@‰ ˜/üæ@‰óèÅ◊€ı@ÊOVÁ’fœ@ÊOñÁ’fœSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÏÛ¬_€óS␀␀ÈSHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀MCG +04-22-042 is a Seyfert 1.2 AGN which shows evidence of significant hard X-ray variability. Curiously, previous analyses of the Swift BAT and a short NuSTAR observation suggest that the source shows increased variability at high energies (above 20keV) than at lower energies. Archival data also suggest that the spectrum appears harder when the source is brighter. These behaviours have been observed in radio loud AGN, but are extremely atypical for radio quiet, type-1 AGN. We propose to monitor the hard X-ray flux of this source using 3x40ks observations of the source throughout AO6. NuSTAR can uniquely provide the opportunity to study the source on short and long timescales across a wide energy range,and permit the search for spectral changes associated with the variability.            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       CHARACTERISING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF THE SEYFERT 1.2 GALAXY MCG +04-22-042                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3) / Kp=5-6                                                                                                          archived  20210210PNV_J23244760p6111140                             @uÙÍ≥g†˘@Nì∂E° ¡@\$ı¬è\)ø±ñRΩ<6@m@y=Ÿc@ÏÛΩÕ´∏1@ÏÛæî»)¯90701312001@^"GE8Ô5@]–Œp:˚@`␀␀␀␀␀␀@`␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÏÛ¬3333␀␀Á¶Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                           