warded a Large 198-orbit Hubble Space Telescope program and a Las Cumbres Observatory Key Project to measure reverberation lags in Mrk 817. Previous UV/optical reverberation mapping campaigns have successfully mapped the outer accretion disc and broad line region, but reveal that X-rays (thought to be the primary driver of multi-wavelength variability) do not correlate with longer wavelengths as well as expected. X-ray spectral and timing measurements are necessary to track changes in the geometry and dynamics of the inner accretion flow that likely affect our inference of the outer disc and outflow strActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X20       MAPPING GAS FLOWS IN THE AGN MRK 817 WITH XMM-NEWTON AND HST                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_817                                           @kao␀h€å@MiŒ_o“@Y$xF‹@Jπ€"–Â`@S*	ﬁ␀“@Ï˜_[‰õø@Ï˜é#Ü60702008002@Ô∑–ÿD–@Ôqµ3333@Ùq∏QÎÖ@ı·∏QÎÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï˜›ç^oÅ␀␀È˙Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀X™Knowledge of the structure and kinematics of gas around supermassive black holes is vital to understanding accretion and thus AGN feedback. To this end, we, the AGN STORM 2 team, have been awarded a Large 198-orbit Hubble Space Telescope program and a Las Cumbres Observatory Key Project to measure reverberation lags in Mrk 817. Previous UV/optical reverberation mapping campaigns have successfully mapped the outer accretion disc and broad line region, but reveal 