@Ï˜©ÍN@Ï˜ŸÍN30602027004@ÒÇì›óˆ@ÏãL/É{@Ú8ä<64@Ú9Z<64SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï˜Ò0[∞␀␀È6Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀EThe galaxy M82 harbors two notable ultraluminous X-ray sources (ULX): M82 X-2, the archetipal pulsating ultraluminous X-ray source (PULX), and M82 X-1, a IMBH candidate. The extreme luminosity of ULXs, and the observation of strong winds from these sources, suggests that these sources are undergoing strongly super-Eddington accretion. The supply of matter that powers these accreting systems is likely to come from Roche Lobe overflow from a more massive companion star, and should produce changes of orbital period observable in time scales of ~years. This program aims at detecting this orbital period derivative through pulsar timing in M82 X-2, and study in detail the quasi-periodic oscillations from both ULXs.                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       BROADBAND TIMING OF THE ULTRALUMINOUS X-RAY SOURCES IN M82: ORBITAL DECAY, QPOS, AND MORE.                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315NGC1365                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï˜ŸÍN@Ï˜€èFj60702058003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï˜ıI2q␀␀ÈDHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀_NGC 1365 is a Seyfert 1.8 galaxy, known for transitioning quickly between AGN types, likely due to variable broad line region obscuration. In its Compton-thin state (which occurs ~72% of the time), NGC 1365 routinely shows the most robust evidence for an ionised outflow, through the detection of 4 separate iron absorption lines, associated with Fe XXV and Fe XXVI Kalpha and Kbeta. These lines are not resolved, and have never been detected with a high-resolution spectrograph, but near the beginning of NuSTAR Cycle 7, Chandra will observe NGC 1365 with high-energy transmission gratings for 300 ks, providing an unprecedented view of a low-velocity ionised wind. We request accompanying NuSTAR observations to constrain the continuum with hard X-rays, and to simultaneously probe the             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       ACCRETION AND EJECTION IN NGC 1365 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA/HETG                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC1365                                           @J´ã¨q≥¿B‚ÎC-@m¡»K]Ãd¿KgAÚ◊s@B'_o“Û@Ï˜€èFj@Ï˜˘xVb‹60702058004@‚ìû?@‚l≤˛≈m@„ÎÁzxl"@„Ógzxl"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï¯0º”␀␀ÈDHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀_NGC 1365 is a Seyfert 1.8 galaxy, known for transitioning quickly between AGN types, likely due to variable broad line region obscuration. In its Compton-thin state (which occurs ~72% of the time), NGC 1365 routinely shows the most robust evidence for an ionised outflow, through the detection of 4 separate iron absorption lines, associated with 