y during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J1803m298                                    @pÏ“àŒp;¿=À”√a@? ¿Énò¿Â…„Ω@dîFsÅÿ@Ï˘v[‰õø@Ï˘çÍN90702316002@ŸÓy˙C˛]@⁄2ô[W>´@‡»Ø8Ô4◊@‡ 8Ô4◊SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï˘¶)∑Ò␀␀Á◊Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20210315NGC1365                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï˘çÍN@Ï˘éÕ´∏160702058009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï˘•€NÅµ␀␀ÈDHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀_NGC 1365 is a Seyfert 1.8 galaxy, known for transitioning quickly between AGN types, likely due to variable broad line region obscuration. In its Compton-thin state (which occurs ~72% of the time), NGC 1365 routinely shows the most robust evidence for an ionised outflow, through the detection of 4 separate iron absorption lines, associated with Fe XXV and Fe XXVI Kalpha and Kbeta. These lines are not resolved, and have never been detected with a high-resolution spectrograph, but near the beginning of NuSTAR Cycle 7, Chandra will observe NGC 1365 with high-energy transmission gratings for 300 ks, providing an unprecedented view of a low-velocity ionised wind. We request accompanying NuSTAR observations to constrain the continuum with hard X-rays, and to simultaneously probe the             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       ACCRETION AND EJECTION IN NGC 1365 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA/HETG                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC1365                                           @J¨j~˘€#¿BjOv@m¡ [¿7¿Kf§®¡T @Jqâ7K∆®@Ï˘éÕ´∏1@Ï˘±î»)¯60702058010@‰@√/üæ@‰†#9¿Ï@Âª#8Ô4◊@ÂΩMûÉ‰&SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï˘∫ãx˚‘␀␀ÈDHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀_NGC 1365 is a Seyfert 1.8 galaxy, known for transitioning quickly between AGN types, likely due to variable broad line region obscuration. In its Compton-thin state (which occurs ~72% of the time), NGC 1365 routinely shows the most robust evidence for an ionised outflow, through the detection of 4 separate iron absorption lines, associated with Fe XXV and Fe XXVI Kalpha and Kbeta. These lines are not resolved, and have never been detected with a high-resolution spectrograph, but near the beginning of NuSTAR Cycle 7, Chandra will observe NGC 1365 with high-energy transmission gratings for 300 ks, providing an unprecedented view of a low-velocity ionised wind. We request accompanying NuSTAR observations to constrain the continuum with hard X-rays, and to simultaneously probe the             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       ACCRETION AND EJECTION IN NGC 1365 WITH NUSTAR AND CHANDRA/HETG                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(5/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315Sol_21127_DL                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï˘±î»)¯@Ï˘≤xVb‹20601015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï˘∫>Fã␀␀Á◊Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sThe goal of this proposal is to take advantage of opportunities for co-observation between NuSTAR and the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) during six of the PSP perihelia ocurring during NuSTAR GO Cycles 6 & 7. These Earth-facing perihelia provide compelling opportunities to combine NuSTAR s capability for highly sensitive remote X-ray observation with PSP s powerful suite of in-situ instruments. By arranging these co-observations, the capacity of NuSTAR and PSP will be used in tandem to examine connectivity between the corona and the inner heliosphere. Additionally, solar observations performed at these times will allow the NuSTAR observatory to continue to contribute to the rich, multi-instrument solar datasets achieved when the heliophysics community unites in support of each PSP perihelia.    Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Duncan                        Jessie                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       COORDINATED NUSTAR OBSERVATION IN SUPPORT OF SIX EARTH-SIDE PARKER SOLAR PROBE PERIHELIA                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀      