n), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ03561995m6251391                            @MÉÍ≥g†˘¿OhŒp:˚@q@”ZÖáî¿E¥,<ûÏ¿@S>á+J@Ï˝.?r‘£@Ï˝<î»)¯60160172002@ÿzqﬁi≠@◊÷˛ÎC-@ŸÕ␀␀␀␀␀@Ÿ—␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï˝C;ÏGM␀␀ÁÌHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315SMC_X1                                            @3P∑Ä4m∆¿RY©˚Álã@r«p:˚~ë¿EøiDg8@\~JåLò@Ï˝<î»)¯@Ï˝<ÍN30702018005@l(â†'RT@lT…Öo@p␀␀␀␀␀@p␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï˝CF␀␀Í∑Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀BThe accreting pulsar SMC X-1 exhibits a superorbital cycle likely caused by a warped precessing accretion disk, which drives behaviors like pulsation transience and varying spectral obscuration levels. The superorbital cycle is typically ~55 days but has excursions, driven by instability in the disk geometry, where the period increases to 44 days. To date, there exists no broad-band spectral and timing coverage of superorbital excursion. This proposal asks for simultaneous NuSTAR and XMM observations to sample the upcoming 2022 excursion. The next excursion is not expected until 2030, meaning that we have a unique opportunity to use tomography to probe the fluctuating disk geometry and investigate the effect of excursion on obscuration levels, pulse shape, and pulsation transience.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       INVESTIGATING PULSATION TRANSIENCE IN SMC X-1 DURING SUPERORBITAL PERIOD EXCURSION                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20210315SMC_X1                                            @3Höu%F¿RY8Ô4÷°@r«ù≤-V¿E¿oiDg8@\ë0æÌ)@Ï˝<ÍN@Ï˝LÕ´∏130702018006@·␀( Wßá@‡ÙÛZÖáî@„ÜT‘,<ü@„Üt‘,<üSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ï˝v8!_Ù␀␀Í∑Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀BThe accreting pulsar SMC X-1 exhibits a superorbital cycle likely caused by a warped precessing accretion disk, which drives behaviors like pulsation transience and varying spectral obscuration levels. The superorbital cycle is typically ~55 days but has excursions, driven by instability in the disk geometry, where the period increases to 44 days. To date, there exists no broad-band spectral and timing coverage of superorbital excursion. This proposal asks for simultaneous NuSTAR and XMM observations to sample the upcoming 2022 excursion. The next excursion is not expected until 2030, meaning that we have a unique opportunity to use tomography to probe the fluctuating disk geometry and investigate the effect of excursion on obscuration levels, pulse shape, and pulsation transience.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       INVESTIGATING PULSATION TRANSIENCE IN SMC X-1 DURING SUPERORBITAL PERIOD EXCURSION                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(3/3) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20210315SGR_J1555d2m5402                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ï˝LÕ´∏1@Ï˝M[‰õø90701319001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ï˝vz·H␀␀ÁÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                     