STAR exposures to cover 8 nights of VLTI-Gravity+(XMM-or-Chandra) observations of Sgr A*. The required exposure time of Gravity, XMM and Chandra have been already secured. The Gravity team has recently measured the astrometric motion of the near infrared (NIR) source during three bright NIR flux excursions of Sgr A*. Simultaneous NuSTAR+Gravity observations will allow us to understand the evolution of the radiative mechanism and to test whether, during bright X-ray flares, the NIR source will display similar circular orbital motions or whether it will be characterised by a dramatically different pattern, such as by linear motions, reminiscent of jet like ejections. This will provide us with a groundbreaking test of accretion physics.               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       THE MOTION OF SGR A*'S NIR SOURCE DURING X-RAY FLARES                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA rally / INERTIAL only                                                                                              archived  20150316ESO495mG005                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÏˇÔÕ´∏1@Ïˇ±9Ò60668002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì␀næc^␀␀ÈuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀mTo tackle some of the biggest ongoing questions regarding AGN activity and feedback, we are constructing a complete census of local Seyfert-luminosity AGN. The Local AGN Survey (LASr) is a multi-tiered selection of AGN designed to reveal all AGN in the local Universe, even in the presence of heavy obscuration. As a proof-of-concept, we propose 3x100ks NuSTAR observations of 3 previously-unknown Compton-thick AGN candidates to quantify the efficiency of our selection. Extensive simulations show that the requested exposure is sufficient to confirm the presence or lack of a heavily obscured AGN to high significance in each. The observations will test the robustness of the LASr selection and pave the way towards achieving it's primary goal: mitigating obscuration biases of AGN selection.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boorman                       Peter                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       VALIDATING A NEW AGN SELECTION AS A MEANS TO CONSTRUCT THE LOCAL CENSUS OF AGN                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO495mG005                                       @_0©*0U¿9+~êˇó$@n§Z¨1@ÁlãCïÅ@@)ué!ñS@Ïˇ±9Ò@Ì␀9xVb‹60668002002@¯’äÄùIR@¯ú¨SéÛM@˙ú≠∂Æ}V@˙ùù∂Æ}VSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì␀HﬁoÄí␀␀ÈuHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀mTo tackle some of the biggest ongoing questions regarding AGN activity and feedback, we are constructing a complete census of local Seyfert-luminosity AGN. The Local AGN Survey (LASr) is a multi-tiered selection of AGN designed to reveal all AGN in the local Universe, even in the presence of heavy obscuration. As a proof-of-concept, we propose 3x100ks NuSTAR observations of 3 previously-unknown Compton-thick AGN candidates to quantify the efficiency of our selection. Extensive simulations show that the requested exposure is sufficient to confirm the presence or lack of a heavily obscured AGN to high significance in each. The observations will test the robustness of the LASr selection and pave the way towards achieving it's primary goal: mitigating obscuration biases of AGN selection.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boorman                       Peter                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       VALIDATING A NEW AGN SELECTION AS A MEANS TO CONSTRUCT THE LOCAL CENSUS OF AGN                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315CDWFS_863                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì␀9xVb‹@Ì␀:[‰õø60701010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì␀Hêó¥&␀␀ÈyHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀YRecent studies of AGN data from deep X-ray surveys suggest that signatures of reprocessing of the intrinsic X-ray continuum in the circumnuclear environment grow weaker with increasing luminosity, redshift, and line-of-sight column density. Selecting two bright and hard sources from a wide and deep X-ray survey, we propose to directly measure the strength of reprocessing signatures in individual high-luminosity AGN. Leveraging detailed studies of their lower-luminosity local counterparts, we will directly and independently test the reported trends. The proposed joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations offer a unique opportunity to advance studies of the obscuring circumnuclear material in AGN outside of the local Universe.                                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Balokovic                     Mislav                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X20       Obscuration and X-ray Reprocessing in High-luminosity Quasars                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CDWFS_863                                         @k$›/†@@ûßÔù≤-@KÒ≤ïÈ‚@QLÀí:*@"ƒtSéÛM@Ì␀:[‰õø@Ì␀^xVb‹60701010002@Ë¶FsÅÿ@Á›Z€ã¨q@ÈÕ)$tSè@ÈœÈ$tSèSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì␀hËî´m␀␀ÈyHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀YRecent studies of AGN data from deep X-ray surveys suggest that signatures of reprocessing of the intrinsic X-ray continuum in the circumnuclear environment grow weaker with increasing luminosity, redshift, and line-of-sight column density. Selecting two bright and hard sources from a wide and deep X-ray survey, we propose to directly measure the strength of reprocessing signatures in individual high-luminosity AGN. Leveraging detailed studies of their lower-luminosity local counterparts, we will directly and independently test the reported trends. The proposed joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations offer a unique opportunity to advance studies of the obscuring circumnuclear material in AGN outside of the local Universe.                                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Balokovic                     Mislav                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X20       Obscuration and X-ray Reprocessing in High-luminosity Quasars                                                        