un2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @r#9¿ÎÓ@%|Ñµ‹∆@FúÑµ‹∆??ò¸PH@L.öu@Ì(Õ´∏1@Ì9î»)¯90701323002@”ˆôıY≥–@‘M—N;Õ@ÿÙ‘Ú{≤ˇ@ÿÙ‘Ú{≤ˇSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌJ4∑ê␀␀ËHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER Chandra                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20210315W50_WESTERN_LOBE                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì9î»)¯@Ì9Õ´∏140701002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌI„◊
=␀␀ÈÉHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀®The long-awaited HAWC discovery of TeV gamma-ray emission from the eastern and western lobes of W50 nebula, powered by the microquasar SS433, was the first evidence that accreting X-ray binaries can efficiently accelerate particles to TeV-PeV energies, indicating a new class of Galactic PeVatrons. The proposed NuSTAR and XMM observations will complete a broad-band X-ray survey of the particle acceleration and jet termination sites of the W50/SS433 system. Spatially-resolved X-ray spectroscopy will resolve the non-thermal X-ray emission, characterize thermal X-ray knots in the western lobe and constrain the jet acceleration efficiency. Our multi-wavelength approach with the proposed observations will provide deep insights to this unique mini-AGN jet interacting with the W50 nebula.         Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Mori                          Kaya                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       BROAD-BAND X-RAY SURVEY OF A GALACTIC PEVATRON ACCELERATOR ASSOCIATED WITH THE MICROQUASAR SS433                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316W50_WESTERN_LOBE                                  @q˙†ê-‡@ôôôôö@Cπ0æÌ)ø¸Vìu@H¥éäqﬁj@Ì9Õ´∏1@ÌÜÕ´∏140701002002@˜”ƒÑµ‹∆@˜òFú‡u˜@˘ïº@˘ñ§ºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì¶…ìà'␀␀ÈÉHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀®The long-awaited HAWC discovery of TeV gamma-ray emission from the eastern and western lobes of W50 nebula, powered by the microquasar SS433, was the first evidence that accreting X-ray binaries can efficiently accelerate particles to TeV-PeV energies, indicating a new class of Galactic PeVatrons. The proposed NuSTAR and XMM observations will complete a broad-band X-ray survey of the particle acceleration and jet termination sites of the W50/SS433 system. Spatially-resolved X-ray spectroscopy will resolve the non-thermal X-ray emission, characterize thermal X-ray knots in the western lobe and constrain the jet acceleration efficiency. Our multi-wavelength approach with the proposed observations will provide deep insights to this unique mini-AGN jet interacting with the W50 nebula.         Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Mori                          Kaya                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       BROAD-BAND X-RAY SURVEY OF A GALACTIC PEVATRON ACCELERATOR ASSOCIATED WITH THE MICROQUASAR SS433                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315NGC1052                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌÜÕ´∏1@ÌáÍN90701624001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì¶y ␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC1052                                           @D%µsÍ≥h¿ mB√…ÓÃ@fπÍ≥g†˘¿Má+@`d†ê-‡@ÌáÍN@ÌñÍN90701624002@”ˇ6sÅ◊‹@” ZÒ©˚Á@’vÄùIR@’xZÄùIRSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì¶bR≠≥␀␀ËHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20210315Mrk_817                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌñÍN@ÌòèFj60702008003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì¶Al¡␀␀È˙Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀X™Knowledge of the structure and kinematics of gas around supermassive black holes is vital to understanding accretion and thus AGN feedback. To this end, we, the AGN STORM 2 team, have been awarded a Large 198-orbit Hubble Space Telescope program and a Las Cumbres Observatory Key Project to measure reverberation lags in Mrk 817. Previous UV/optical reverberation mapping campaigns have successfully mapped the outer accretion disc and broad line region, but reveal that X-rays (thought to be the primary driver of multi-wavelength variability) do not correlate with longer wavelengths as well as expected. X-ray spectral and timing measurements are necessary to track changes in the geometry and dynamics of the inner accretion flow that likely affect our inference of the outer disc and outflow strActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X20       MAPPING GAS FLOWS IN THE AGN MRK 817 WITH XMM-NEWTON AND HST                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_817                                           @k_≥–|Ñ∂@Mc≤˛≈m]@Y"¯7¥¢4@J¿—∑X‚@v+É‰%ÆÊ@ÌòèFj@Ì»±9Ò60702008004@ÒÄ…<64@ÒTx7K∆®@Úºmºj~˙@ÚΩÓ"h	’SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì‚d…ƒ␀␀È˙Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀X™Knowledge of the structure and kinematics of gas around supermassive black holes is vital to understanding accretion and thus AGN feedback. To this end, we, the AGN STORM 2 team, have been awarded a Large 198-orbit Hubble Space Telescope program and a Las Cumbres Observatory Key Project to measure reverberation lags in Mrk 817. Previous UV/optical reverberation mapping campaigns have successfully mapped the outer accretion disc and broad line region, but reveal that X-rays (thought to be the primary driver of multi-wavelength variability) do not correlate with longer wavelengths as well as expected. X-ray spectral and timing measurements are necessary to track changes in the geometry and dynamics of the inner accretion flow that likely affect our inference of the outer disc and outflow strActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X20       MAPPING GAS FLOWS IN THE AGN MRK 817 WITH XMM-NEWTON AND HST                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM Chandra HST                                                                                     ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315SDSSJ1253p1012                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì»±9Ò@Ì…?r‘£60763002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì‚&«}â␀␀ÈÉHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀áThe intrinsic column density (NH) distribution of quasars is poorly known. Many optically selected obscured quasars show indirect evidence of being heavily obscured, at or above the Compton-thick threshold. Such buried QSO2s are extremely challenging to study at X-ray energies of E < 10 keV. NuSTAR is unique in its ability to constrain NH for buried QSO2s, and thus reveal the hidden accretion power of these most extreme sources. Despite this, only five buried QSO2s have been detected with NuSTAR, with even fewer having sufficient counts for spectral modelling. The primary aim of this proposal is to double the number of NuSTAR-observed buried QSO2s with direct constraints on NH (at a precision of D log NH < 0.2), by observing five promising new targets, with a total exposure of 220 ks.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lansbury                      George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       HIDDEN POWER: DOUBLING THE NUSTAR CENSUS OF BURIED TYPE 2 QUASARS                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ1253p1012                                    @h&‚e+‘@$`—∑X‚@rÛ‡qv@RK)^û	@vÌëhr∞@Ì…?r‘£@Ìﬁ±9Ò60763002002@Ÿ~d¬¯8@Ÿ>ª3333@€Jêæﬂ§@€L»æﬂ§SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì‚tnæ␀␀ÈÉHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀áThe intrinsic column density (NH) distribution of quasars is poorly known. Many optically selected obscured quasars show indirect evidence of being heavily obscured, at or above the Compton-thick threshold. Such buried QSO2s are extremely challenging to study at X-ray energies of E < 10 keV. NuSTAR is unique in its ability to constrain NH for buried QSO2s, and thus reveal the hidden accretion power of these most extreme sources. Despite this, only five buried QSO2s have been detected with NuSTAR, with even fewer having sufficient counts for spectral modelling. The primary aim of this proposal is to double the number of NuSTAR-observed buried QSO2s with direct constraints on NH (at a precision of D log NH < 0.2), by observing five promising new targets, with a total exposure of 220 ks.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Lansbury                      George                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       HIDDEN POWER: DOUBLING THE NUSTAR CENSUS OF BURIED TYPE 2 QUASARS                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315Sol_21201_DL                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìﬁ±9Ò@Ìﬂ[‰õø80701001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì·ÈË|f␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sThe aim of this proposal is to search for X-ray bremsstrahlung signatures, produced by non-thermal electrons within solar flares, in the solar corona. We propose to use the solar disk as a natural occulter ( coronagraph ) to shield NuSTAR from the intense flare emission lower down and thus reveal the much fainter non-thermal signatures from the corona. To also capture the main flare emissions that are not visible to NuSTAR, we will use Solar Orbiter STIX hard X-ray observations taken from a different vantage point than Earth. The time frame for observing occulted flares is ideal in Cycle 7, as solar activity is rising with moderately bright flares that are most suitable for NuSTAR solar observations.                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Krucker                       Sam                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       USING DISK-OCCULTATION TO STUDY NON-THERMAL PROCESSES IN SOLAR FLARES WITH NUSTAR AND SOLAR ORBITER/STIX                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / dogleg position                                                                                               archived  20150316Sol_21201_SADA                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìﬂ[‰õø@ÌﬂÕ´∏180701002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì·◊Éö\␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sThe aim of this proposal is to search for X-ray bremsstrahlung signatures, produced by non-thermal electrons within solar flares, in the solar corona. We propose to use the solar disk as a natural occulter ( coronagraph ) to shield NuSTAR from the intense flare emission lower down and thus reveal the much fainter non-thermal signatures from the corona. To also capture the main flare emissions that are not visible to NuSTAR, we will use Solar Orbiter STIX hard X-ray observations taken from a different vantage point than Earth. The time frame for observing occulted flares is ideal in Cycle 7, as solar activity is rising with moderately bright flares that are most suitable for NuSTAR solar observations.                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Krucker                       Sam                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       USING DISK-OCCULTATION TO STUDY NON-THERMAL PROCESSES IN SOLAR FLARES WITH NUSTAR AND SOLAR ORBITER/STIX                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    archived  20150316Sol_21201_POS01                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌﬂÕ´∏1@Ì‚?r‘£80710201001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì´ùdÅ␀␀ËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀sThe aim of this proposal is to search for X-ray bremsstrahlung signatures, produced by non-thermal electrons within solar flares, in the solar corona. We propose to use the solar disk as a natural occulter ( coronagraph ) to shield NuSTAR from the intense flare emission lower down and thus reveal the much fainter non-thermal signatures from the corona. To also capture the main flare emissions that are not visible to NuSTAR, we will use Solar Orbiter STIX hard X-ray observations taken from a different vantage point than Earth. The time frame for observing occulted flares is ideal in Cycle 7, as solar activity is rising with moderately bright flares that are most s