␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì¨±9Ò@Ì≠[‰õø30702014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì¬“ˆÑæ␀␀Í©Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˚NGC 7793 P13 is an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion in ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), as it is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known and the only one with a fully determined ephemeris. In 2020 P13 entered an off-state, from which it is currently re-emerging. This gives us the unique opportunity to study the accretion flow as the accretion geometry is changing, and will allow us to infer more physical parameter of the system, like the magnetic field. We therefore propose to observe P13 twice with NuSTAR during cycle-7, continuing the successful monitoring observations in the past.                                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       CONTINUED SPIN-TRACKING OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7793_P13                                      @vxÅo␀i¿@Ktºj~˙@hXy=Ÿ¿S<„º”ZÜ@dÖhr∞ ≈@Ì≠[‰õø@Ìﬂ?r‘£30702014002@(0Ìëhs@ócä	@ÒoL.HËß@ÒpÃ.HËßSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì„∆ZCÇ␀␀Í©Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀˚NGC 7793 P13 is an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion in ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), as it is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known and the only one with a fully determined ephemeris. In 2020 P13 entered an off-state, from which it is currently re-emerging. This gives us the unique opportunity to study the accretion flow as the accretion geometry is changing, and will allow us to infer more physical parameter of the system, like the magnetic field. We therefore propose to observe P13 twice with NuSTAR during cycle-7, continuing the successful monitoring observations in the past.                                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       CONTINUED SPIN-TRACKING OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20210315G1d9p0d3                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìﬂ?r‘£@Ì‡?r‘£40702003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì
tá,␀␀ÈÓHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÕWith an age of roughly 110 years, G1.9+0.3 is the youngest known supernova remnant in the Milky Way. It is also one of the few remnants in our Galaxy whose evolution we can monitor and which show evidence for emission from the 44-Ti decay chain. We propose to observe G1.9+0.3 for 600 ks. This will allow us to identify changes of spectrum, flux, and morphology between the original NuSTAR observation from 2013 and now in the hard X-ray band. In addition, this observation will significantly improve the upper limits of the 44-Ti emission, and deeply probe the uncertainty range of a potential detection of 44-Ti by Chandra, and, in the case emission is confirmed, enable its characterization. With its unique hard X-ray capabilities, only NuSTAR can carry out these observations.                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Zoglauer                      Andreas                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MONITORING THE HARD X-RAY EVOLUTION OF THE YOUNG SNR G1.9+0.3 AND IMPROVING THE NUSTAR UPPER LIMITS OF ITS 44TI EMISSION␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316G1d9p0d3                                          @p≤_ÿ≠´ü¿;:JåLò?˙zêñªô?‚ÉnóçP@t·0U2a|@Ì‡?r‘£@Ì$±9Ò40702003002@˜#¡HÀí@ˆˇ*†˘	l@¯Ï:9¿ÎÓ@¯Ì⁄9¿ÎÓSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌD,®d ␀␀ÈÓHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀ÕWith an age of roughly 110 years, G1.9+0.3 is the youngest known supernova remnant in the Milky Way. It is also one of the few remnants in our Galaxy whose evolution we can monitor and which show evidence for emission from the 44-Ti decay chain. We propose to observe G1.9+0.3 for 600 ks. This will allow us to identify changes of spectrum, flux, and morphology between the original NuSTAR observation from 2013 and now in the hard X-ray band. In addition, this observation will significantly improve the upper limits of the 44-Ti emission, and deeply probe the uncertainty range of a potential detection of 44-Ti by Chandra, and, in the case emission is confirmed, enable its characterization. With its unique hard X-ray capabilities, only NuSTAR can carry out these observations.                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Zoglauer                      Andreas                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MONITORING THE HARD X-RAY EVOLUTION OF THE YOUNG SNR G1.9+0.3 AND IMPROVING THE NUSTAR UPPER LIMITS OF ITS 44TI EMISSION␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315FRB201124                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì$±9Ò@Ì&èFj80601509001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌC„]≠R␀␀ËÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀¥®Despite rapid observational progress, the nature of fast radio bursts and their progenitors remains unknown. We propose a multi-wavelength follow-up campaign in order to detect their counterparts and narrow down the range of possible models, closing in on their elusive progenitors. Rapid observations at X-ray and radio wavelengths are requested to search for a transient, short-lived afterglow counterpart. Late-time X-ray observations could instead unveil the smoking gun signature of a magnetar-powered nebula.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Piro                          Luigi                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀S16       CLOSING IN ON THE PROGENITORS OF FAST RADIO BURSTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316FRB201124                                         @SD'RT`™@:¿ÎÌ˙D@f3Ü¬&Äù¿!m‡qv@b‰ò_ˆî@Ì&èFj@Ì7î»)¯80601509002@”‹¸£◊
