and its connection to the radio emission in radio-quiet AGN, we propose here the joint study of the mm/X-ray variability of a radio-quiet AGN, using NuSTAR, XMM-Newton and ALMA to monitor the brightest unobscured AGN in the southern sky, IC4329A, observing it five times, once every two days over ten consecutive days. This is part of a large campaign that includes ALMA (10 obs., highest resolution), XMM-Newton (10), Swift (10) and NICER (20) observations, all of which have already been approved. The NuSTAR observations proposed here will be fundamental to infer the physical properties of the Comptonizing plasma (temperature and optical depth), and to study how they change with the submm emission produced by the electrons in the corona. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNDERSTANDING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN THE X-RAY CORONA AND THE NUCLEAR RADIO EMISSION IN AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC_4329A                                          @iË¬&ÄùI¿>Qué!ñS@s“’œ™Õü@?K∆ßÔû@v}␀—∑Y@Ìñ[‰õø@Ì•#Ü60702050002@‘@ë—N<@”ÙÁJ#9¡@’œ≥4÷°b@’—≥4÷°bSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì¬p4V␀␀È≠Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀<In order to understand the origin of the X-ray corona, and its connection to the radio emission in radio-quiet AGN, we propose here the joint study of the mm/X-ray variability of a radio-quiet AGN, using NuSTAR, XMM-Newton and ALMA to monitor the brightest unobscured AGN in the southern sky, IC4329A, observing it five times, once