connection to the radio emission in radio-quiet AGN, we propose here the joint study of the mm/X-ray variability of a radio-quiet AGN, using NuSTAR, XMM-Newton and ALMA to monitor the brightest unobscured AGN in the southern sky, IC4329A, observing it five times, once every two days over ten consecutive days. This is part of a large campaign that includes ALMA (10 obs., highest resolution), XMM-Newton (10), Swift (10) and NICER (20) observations, all of which have already been approved. The NuSTAR observations proposed here will be fundamental to infer the physical properties of the Comptonizing plasma (temperature and optical depth), and to study how they change with the submm emission produced by the electrons in the corona. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNDERSTANDING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN THE X-RAY CORONA AND THE NUCLEAR RADIO EMISSION IN AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC_4329A                                          @iËÕûÉ‰&¿>TÍJå@s“œAÚ◊@?ºj~˘€?ÍËßÊö‘@ÌèFj@Ì#Õ´∏160702050006@”W§9Xb@”-∑ÁlãD@‘ÚL–©*@‘ı–©*SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌJñÉö␀␀È≠Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀<In order to understand the origin of the X-ray corona, and its connection to the radio emission in radio-quiet AGN, we propose here the joint study of the mm/X-ray variability of a radio-quiet AGN, using NuSTAR, XMM-Newton and ALMA to monitor the brightest unobscured AGN in the southern sky, IC4329A, observing it five times, once every two days over ten consecutive days. This is part of a large campaign that includes ALMA (10 obs., highest resolution), XMM-Newton (10), Swift (10) and NICER (20) observations, all of which have already been approved. The NuSTAR observations proposed here will be fundamental to infer the physical properties of the Comptonizing plasma (temperature and optical depth), and to study how they change with the submm emission produced by the electrons in the corona. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNDERSTANDING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN THE X-RAY CORONA AND THE NUCLEAR RADIO EMISSION IN AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM ALMA                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀(3/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315MRK_1018                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì#Õ´∏1@Ì%èFj60761011001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌJu0Ï®␀␀Ë9Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀&Nearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. The Swift-BAT 105-month survey, with its all-sky coverage, provides the largest, most complete sample of local (z<0.1) powerful AGN. We propose 13 joint XMM/NuSTAR observations to complement an approved HST program that is observing 137 type 1 BAT AGN in the near ultraviolet (<3000 A) to accurately calibrate AGN bolometric luminosity. These results for the first time with 100 pc resolution of HST to measure the AGN emission are critical to determining the intrinsic scatter of the UV/X-r