14_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀<In order to understand the origin of the X-ray corona, and its connection to the radio emission in radio-quiet AGN, we propose here the joint study of the mm/X-ray variability of a radio-quiet AGN, using NuSTAR, XMM-Newton and ALMA to monitor the brightest unobscured AGN in the southern sky, IC4329A, observing it five times, once every two days over ten consecutive days. This is part of a large campaign that includes ALMA (10 obs., highest resolution), XMM-Newton (10), Swift (10) and NICER (20) observations, all of which have already been approved. The NuSTAR observations proposed here will be fundamental to infer the physical properties of the Comptonizing plasma (temperature and optical depth), and to study how they change with the submm emission produced by the electrons in the corona. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNDERSTANDING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN THE X-RAY CORONA AND THE NUCLEAR RADIO EMISSION IN AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC_4329A                                          @iËø±[W?¿>SF‹]cà@s“À˚µt@?é!ñRΩ@€ã¨q≥@ÌïÍN@Ì§î»)¯60702050010@—úHÀ)^û@—v√ã¨q@”C+J@”√+JSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì¬+ùe␀␀È≠Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀<In order to understand the origin of the X-ray corona, and its connection to the radio emission in radio-quiet AGN, we propose here the joint study of the mm/X-ray variability of a radio-quiet AGN, using NuSTAR, XMM-Newton and ALMA to monitor the brightest unobscured AGN in the southern sky, IC4329A, observing it five times, once every two days over ten consecutive days. This is part of a large campaign that includes ALMA (10 obs., highest resolution), XMM-Newton (10), Swift (10) and NICER (20) observations, all of which have already been approved. The NuSTAR observations proposed here will be fundamental to infer the physical properties of the Comptonizing plasma (temperature and optical depth), and to study how they change with the submm emission produced by the electrons in the corona. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ricci                         Claudio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNDERSTANDING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN THE X-RAY CORONA AND THE NUCLEAR RADIO EMISSION IN AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM ALMA                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀(5/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315RBS1473                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì§î»)¯@Ì§ÍN60061262001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì¡Ó]L;␀␀Ë@Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RBS1473                                           @l~(ı¬è\¿5VOv_Ÿ@uTßÜ¬&Å@?4÷°aÂ@u„[W>´6@Ì§ÍN@Ì≥±9Ò60061262002@‘°˜{J#:@‘mX«‚ÇA@÷8}ø@÷ì8}øSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì¡ÿÔ±␀␀Ë@Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315G1d9p0d3                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì≥±9Ò@Ì¥#Ü40702003003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì¡π,_ì␀␀ÈÓHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÕWith an age of roughly 110 years, G1.9+0.3 is the youngest known supernova remnant in the Milky Way. It is also one of the few remnants in our Galaxy whose evolution we can monitor and which show evidence for emission from the 44-Ti decay chain. We propose to observe G1.9+0.3 for 600 ks. This will allow us to identify changes of spectrum, flux, and morphology between the original NuSTAR observation from 2013 and now in the hard X-ray band. In addition, this observation will significantly improve the upper limits of the 44-Ti emission, and deeply probe the uncertainty range of a potential detection of 44-Ti by Chandra, and, in the case emission is confirmed, enable its characterization. With its unique hard X-ray capabilities, only NuSTAR can carry out these observations.                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Zoglauer                      Andreas                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MO