⁄πıAûYEm\˚A†Em\˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌÈÆuí␀␀ÈÓHea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀ÕWith an age of roughly 110 years, G1.9+0.3 is the youngest known supernova remnant in the Milky Way. It is also one of the few remnants in our Galaxy whose evolution we can monitor and which show evidence for emission from the 44-Ti decay chain. We propose to observe G1.9+0.3 for 600 ks. This will allow us to identify changes of spectrum, flux, and morphology between the original NuSTAR observation from 2013 and now in the hard X-ray band. In addition, this observation will significantly improve the upper limits of the 44-Ti emission, and deeply probe the uncertainty range of a potential detection of 44-Ti by Chandra, and, in the case emission is confirmed, enable its characterization. With its unique hard X-ray capabilities, only NuSTAR can carry out these observations.                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Zoglauer                      Andreas                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MONITORING THE HARD X-RAY EVOLUTION OF THE YOUNG SNR G1.9+0.3 AND IMPROVING THE NUSTAR UPPER LIMITS OF ITS 44TI EMISSION␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/4) / Kp=5 / M-class flare                                                                                            archived  20210315Crab                                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì”±9Ò@Ì’èFj10702303005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌÈCQän␀␀ËCHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab                                              @TÎ8}øH@6À˚µt@g§®¡T ¿Ì˙C˛\í@bÈ‹∆?@Ì’èFj@Ì€èFj10702303006@≠WC◊
