 represent the growing phase of supermassive black holes (SMBHs), allow us to study the accretion history of the SMBH. Population synthesis models predict that, at the peak of the global SMBH growth (z~1.5--3), nearly 50% of all AGN should be Compton thick (CT). Discovery of CT-AGN at high-redshift is still a challenging task. Here, we propose to observe jointly with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton a bright CT-AGN candidate at redshift z=2.99, selected from the XMM serendipitous source catalog. The unabsorbed rest-frame 2-10 keV luminosity of the source is found to be Lx~5e46 erg/s, which potentially makes it the largest intrinsic luminosity quasar ever observed in X-rays, and currently the only CT-AGN candidate that is observable by NuSTAR at z>~3.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNVEILING WITH NUSTAR THE MOST POWERFUL, HEAVILY OBSCURED QUASAR EVER DISCOVERED IN X-RAYS                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164XMM_J171419d3p60272                              @p(N§®¡U@N5Oﬂ;dZ@Va∑X‚@A®D–©*@sËÊ1¯†ê@Ìû#Ü@ÌÊÍN60701056002@˙nŸGÆ{@˙-®`™d√@¸=?”√a@¸?ˇ”√aSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì~Å#Eh␀␀È◊Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀RActive galactic nuclei (AGN), which represent the growing phase of supermassive black holes (SMBHs), allow us to study the accretion history of the SMBH. Population synthesis models predict that, at the peak of the global SMBH growth (z~1.5--3), nearly 50% of all AGN should be Compton thick (CT). Discovery of CT-AGN at high-redshift is still a challenging task. Here, we propose to observe jointly with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton a bright CT-AGN candidate at redshift z=2.99, selected from the XMM serendipitous source catalog. The unabsorbed rest-frame 2-10 keV luminosity of the source is found to be Lx~5e46 erg/s, which potentially makes it the largest intrinsic luminosity quasar ever observed in X-rays, and currently the only CT-AGN candidate that is observable by NuSTAR at z>~3.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNVEILING WITH NUSTAR THE MOST POWERFUL, HEAVILY OBSCURED QUASAR EVER DISCOVERED IN X-RAYS                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌÊÍN@ÌÁxVb‹90701332001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌDΩ°/␀␀ËjHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                      