 CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing previously successful selection criteria, we propose to target 2 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the Swift-BAT 150-month catalog with joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       HUNTING FOR COMPTON-THICK AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_362m8                                         @Sv␀—∑Y¿A.ç∏ª@m≤K]ÃcÒ¿AcI∫^@eë5®Xy>@ÌÇÍN@Ì¶ÍN60701048002@Á≤`ªò«„@ÁpÖÆz·@È_ä^5?}@È^™^5?}SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌÇ¬øÁ∫␀␀È◊Hea_11Aug2020_V6.28_nustardas_19Jun20_v2.0.0                                    ␀␀3The X-ray spectral analysis of new Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting super-massive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing previously successful selection criteria, we propose to target 2 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the Swift-BAT 150-month catalog with joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations each. This analysis will help de