ometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER (5x10 ks) to exploit their groundbreaking capabilities.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/5) ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  20210315GX_339m4                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìb?r‘£@Ìb±9Ò80702316003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌÅÒ~K␀␀ÈBHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀8We request 40 ks of NICER and 60 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger ToO observations to obtain high signal-to-noise spectra from the relatively under-explored fading phases of a non-failed outburst of transient black hole. Our principal goal is to track the evolving properties of the accretion-corona system viz., inner disk truncation, disk density, coronal geometry, and disk inclination as the outburst enters the returning hard-intermediate to quiescent-hard state. This will be achieved with a suite of relativistic reflection and reverberation models to investigate the implications of accretion physics on the origin of hysteresis in the hardness-intensity diagram.                                                                                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sridhar                       Navin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R03       ILLUMINATING THE ACCRETION DISK-CORONAE OF BLACK HOLES DURING THEIR FADING PHASE WITH NICER                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_339m4                                          @oÛ≤˛≈m]¿Hf°a‰˜f@u,*ô0æ¿>BZÓc @vmlÙ!-@Ìb±9Ò@Ìq[‰õø80702316004@‘◊PI∫^5@‘´≤($x@÷LNp:˚@÷LNp:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌÅ€Øò◊␀␀ÈBHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀8We request 40 ks of NICER and 60 ks of NuSTAR time to trigger ToO observations to obtain high signal-to-noise spectra from the relatively under-explored fading phases of a non-failed outburst of transient black hole. Our principal goal is to track the evolving properties of the accretion-corona system viz., inner disk truncation, disk density, coronal geometry, and disk inclination as the outburst enters the returning hard-intermediate to quiescent-hard state. This will be achieved with a suite of relativistic reflection and reverberation models to investigate the implications of accretion physics on the origin of hysteresis in the hardness-intensity diagram.                                                                                                                                     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sridhar                       Navin                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R03       ILLUMINATING THE ACCRETION DISK-CORONAE OF BLACK HOLES DURING THEIR FADING PHASE WITH NICER                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20210315G1d9p0d3                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìq[‰õø@ÌqÕ´∏140702003009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌÅ∫·GÆ␀␀ÈÓHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÕWith an age of roughly 110 years, G1.9+0.3 is the youngest known supernova remnant in the Milky Way. It is also one of the few remnants in our Galaxy whose evolution we can monitor and which show evidence for emission from the 44-Ti decay chain. We propose to observe G1.9+0.3 for 600 ks. This will allow us to identify changes of spectrum, flux, and morphology between the original NuSTAR observation from 2013 and now in the hard X-ray band. In addition, this observation will significantly improve the upper limits of the 44-Ti emission, and deeply probe the uncertainty range of a potential detection of 44-Ti by Chandra, and, in the case emission is confirmed, enable its characterization. With its unique hard X-ray capabilities, only NuSTAR can carry out these observations.                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Zoglauer                      Andreas                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MONITORING THE HARD X-RAY EVOLUTION OF THE YOUNG SNR G1.9+0.3 AND IMPROVING THE NUSTAR UPPER LIMITS OF ITS 44TI EMISSION␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archi