s been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316EXO_2030p375                                      @s@å~($@BÀÕ5®Xy@S<ÿD–©øÚ•„S˜ŒŸ@s‡€Ùá¸π@Ìƒ±9Ò@Ì◊ÍN90701336002@÷Ô±0æÌ@◊˙-V@›1FWßÜ¬@›2FWßÜ¬SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì‰@©Ë|␀␀ËçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                      archived  20210315MCGp06m16m028                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì◊ÍN@ÌÿÕ´∏160702053003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì‰ˆ$␀␀ÍjHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀HFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of 4 CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCGp06m16m028                                     @["˛≈m\˚@A® ƒõ•„@f åLò_@3Z)«y¶µ@cKoiDg@ÌÿÕ´∏1@ÌÔÍN60702053004@›ò‘˝Û∂@‹œB√…Ô@ﬂM»Lò_@ﬂQHLò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌÃÃÕ␀␀ÍjHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀HFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of 4 CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20210315V4641_Sgr_SADA_21313                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌÔÍN@ÌÒ#Ü80702024001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì‰˙O•␀␀ÈBHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀…With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, energy resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of BH accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in June 2017, we took a great step forward. NICER is to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they can produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER (5x10 ks) to exploit their groundbreaking capabilities.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V4641_Sgr                                         @q,°a‰˜f¿9qñRΩ<6@K∆ßÔù≤¿)^û	@tﬁãCïÅ@ÌÒ#Ü@Ì␀èFj80702324009@”•π‘ï+@”Ü JåM@’¬4ìtºj@’√tìtºjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"âJ∂Œ␀␀ÈBHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀…With its excellent hard X-ray sensitivity, energy resolution, and timing, NuSTAR has revolutionized our view of BH accretion, particularly through studies of Compton scattering and relativistic reflection. But we still have yet to attain a fully self-consistent model of the accretion flow, in part because of the difficulty of constraining seed photons: thermal emission from cool, faint disks. With the launch of NICER in June 2017, we took a great step forward. NICER is to soft X-rays what NuSTAR is to hard X-rays, and together they can produce incredibly tight constraints on the geometry of the inner disk, corona, and their interactions. We propose 5x20 ks coordinated observations of a black hole transient with NuSTAR and NICER (5x10 ks) to exploit their groundbreaking capabilities.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Neilsen                       Joseph                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       NUSTAR-NICER OBSERVATIONS OF BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀(5/5) ToO / idiagnostics / M-class solar flare                                                                          archived  20210315Fairall51                                         @qëÄ4m≈÷¿O1·∞â†'@t—öu¿6”F‹]cà@vSa|⁄Q@Ì␀èFj@Ì␀î»)¯60160697001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì"f«}â␀␀ËçHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Fairall51                                         @qëÈ·∞â†¿O0Hé@t—^5?|Ó¿6’fœAÚ@vUüUõ=@Ì␀î»)¯@Ì±9Ò60160697002@”ﬂa≤-V@”≤§åÁ∞@’B~u%F@’E~u%FSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"QY‚k␀␀ËçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nu