␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌEX¶ﬂ√␀␀ËùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS0101m649                                       @/ƒZ¨1¿P,åÁØ∏@rœÅ$›/¿J#a@N•@rs¸PH@Ì7Õ´∏1@ÌF±9Ò60160034002@‘}›õ=»@‘P=áì›ò@÷ıÅ$›@÷ıÅ$›SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìc›L;*␀␀ËùHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315PICTOR_A_WESTERN_HS                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌF±9Ò@ÌG#Ü60701064001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìcºç␀␀ÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀High-resolution imaging with Chandra and HST has revealed that many powerful jets emit strongly in the optical/UV and X-rays on kpc to Mpc scales. In the most powerful jets, the optical and X-ray have spectral properties which show they are not a continuation of the radio synchrotron emission. These extra high-energy emission components, despite dominating the energetic output, are currently unidentified. Understanding the emission mechanism in jets is the only way to get a proper accounting of the energy they carry and the impact on their environments. We propose to observe Pictor A, the only large-scale resolved jet target feasible for NuSTAR, to measure the hard X-ray spectrum to high accuracy in order to break degeneracies and constrain competing models for the emission mechanism.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Meyer                         Eileen                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       THE FIRST HARD X-RAY MEASUREMENT OF A KPC-SCALE JET                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PICTOR_A_WESTERN_HS                               @S˘sÍ≥g°¿F‚–Â`Aâ@os(åÁ∞¿Ad1&Èy@lÍtSéÛM@ÌG#Ü@Ì£#Ü60701064002@˛ÕÂv»¥9@˛Ç§¡Ω•A␀{Hix‘˛A␀{ãﬁ␀“SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìπ'Í¬␀␀ÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀High-resolution imaging with Chandra and HST has revealed that many powerful jets emit strongly in the optical/UV and X-rays on kpc to Mpc scales. In the most powerful jets, the optical and X-ray have spectral properties which show they are not a continuation of the radio synchrotron emission. These extra high-energy emission components, despite dominating the energetic output, are currently unidentified. Understanding the emission mechanism in jets is the only way to get a proper accounting of the energy they carry and the impact on their environments. We propose to observe Pictor A, the only large-scale resolved jet target feasible for NuSTAR, to measure the hard X-