=q␀␀ÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Supernova remnant (SNR) G106.3+2.7 is a PeVatron candidate with detected gammy-ray up to 100 TeV. It's in a unique environment beneficial to particle acceleration. This peculiar SNR is the first one detected with extended synchrotron-originated X-ray emission. The multiwavelength data indicates efficient particle acceleration in the SNR. We propose NuSTAR observations to probe the acceleration limit of this peculiar SNR, which will further help us to constrain the maximum proton energy and the velocity of blast wave in SNR. The knowledge of aforementioned information is crucially important for us to understand the acceleration mechanism and to solve the century puzzle of the origin of Galactic cosmic-rays.                                                                                     Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Ge                            Chong                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       EXPLORING THE ACCELERATION LIMIT OF THE PECULIAR SUPERNOVA REMNANT G106.3+2.7 WITH NUSTAR                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SNR_G106d3p2d7_TAIL2                              @u˘	kπå@Nxr∞ ƒú@Zå~($x@ì›óˆ+@süè≈Åo@ÌèFj@Ì/Õ´∏140701006004@∆N'ª0@ò·'ª@ÒÂÄﬂ§?Ê@ÒÊ ﬂ§?ÊSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌEÚXø␀␀ÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀Supernova remnant (SNR) G106.3+2.7 is a PeVatron candidate with detected gammy-ray up to 100 TeV. It's in a unique environment beneficial to particle acceleration. This peculiar SNR is t