                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316eRASSUJ050810d4m660653                            @SF–Â`Aâ¿PâjOv@qI*ô0æ¿A~_ÿ≠´ü@p␀8}øH@ÌÍN@ÌM[‰õø90701342002@Î<Ì+J@Î°e˝ä€@Ì{≠g†˘	@Ì≠g†˘	SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì4†m:␀␀Ë∑Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀ToO / idiagnostics / SL-A23 (LMC X-4) / M-class solar flares                                                            archived  20210315MRK_1044                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌM[‰õø@ÌNèFj60761010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì4d˙O•␀␀Ë∑Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀&Nearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. The Swift-BAT 105-month survey, with its all-sky coverage, provides the largest, most complete sample of local (z<0.1) powerful AGN. We propose 13 joint XMM/NuSTAR observations to complement an approved HST program that is observing 137 type 1 BAT AGN in the near ultraviolet (<3000 A) to accurately calibrate AGN bolometric luminosity. These results for the first time with 100 pc resolution of HST to measure the AGN emission are critical to determining the intrinsic scatter of the UV/X-ray relation for cosmological parameters and X-ray bolometric corrections, precisely measuring the Eddington ratio distribution of AGN and linking it to feedback and SFR.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       RESOLVING THE AGN BOLOMETRIC EMISSION                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRK_1044                                          @B¿ı¬è\)¿"Öáì›ò@fq⁄QŒ¿Nctºj~˙@rÿÀ)^û@ÌNèFj@Ì^±9Ò60761010002@◊fÌ˚Álã@◊)Äµ‹∆?@ÿ˝!!ˇ.I@ÿ˛!!ˇ.ISCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì4NÅ¥Ë␀␀Ë∑Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀&Nearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. The Swift-BAT 105-month survey, with its all-sky coverage, provides the largest, most complete sample of local (z<0.1) powerful AGN. We propose 13 joint XMM/NuSTAR observations to complement an approved HST program that is observing 137 type 1 BAT AGN in the near ultraviolet (<3000 A) to accurately calibrate AGN bolometric luminosity. These results for the first time with 100 pc resolution of HST to measure the AGN emission are critical to determining the intrinsic scatter of the UV/X-ray relation for cosmological parameters and X-ray bolometric corrections, precisely measuring the Eddington ratio distribution of AGN and linking it to feedback and SFR.                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       RESOLVING THE AGN BOLOMETRIC EMISSION                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              HST                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315MRK_590                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì^±9Ò@Ì_èFj80602604001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì4,wÿè␀␀ÎwHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀^After a 10-year break, Mrk 590 is rekindling its nuclear activity with a rise in X-ray flux! We wish to seize this rare opportunity to record, for the first time, the onset of AGN activity as it occurs since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We ask to trigger up to 3 single-visit joint NuSTAR/Swift/XMM observations to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on or if alternate physics are at play. Relatively inexpensive XMM exposures are needed to constrain any intrinsic absorption. The data are also ideal for testing the recent prediction that quasi-periodic eruptions can explain the flux changes seen for Mrk 590.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Vestergaard                   Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ToO observations of a re-awakening AGN: Exploring the physics of changing-look AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRK_590                                           @@œy¶µøÍÚ‰éär@dg/ÏV’–¿Lù+”√a@s3g†˘	l@Ì_èFj@ÌâÕ´∏180602604002@ÍÿU2a|@ÈæOﬂ;d@Î¸ì\(ı√@Î˛4(ı¬èSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì4˚”␀␀ÎwHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀^After a 10-year break, Mrk 590 is rekindling its nuclear activity with a rise in X-ray flux! We wish to seize this rare opportunity to record, for the first time, the onset of AGN activity as it occurs since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We ask to trigger up to 3 single-visit joint NuSTAR/Swift/XMM observations to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on or if alternate physics are at play. Relatively inexpensive XMM exposures are needed to constrain any intrinsic absorption. The data are also ideal for testing the recent prediction that quasi-periodic eruptions can explain the flux changes seen for Mrk 590.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Vestergaard                   Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ToO observations of a re-awakening AGN: Exploring the physics of changing-look AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20210315NGC_4631                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌâÕ´∏1@ÌãèFj50701002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì§—Y‚k␀␀Í,Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀We propose a joint NuSTAR and XMM observation of NGC4631 (d=7Mpc), a nearby metal-poor galaxy (Z/Z_sun ~ 0.2) with multiple luminous HMXBs. We will (1) examine the nature and accretion states of the HMXBs via color diagnostics, (2) measure the galaxy-integrated 0.3-30 keV spectrum to calibrate the bolometric correction for low metallicity galaxies, and (3) investigate the contribution of each bright source to this spectrum. As an ancillary science goal, we will search for the signature of inverse Compton emission from electrons accelerated in the magnetic field of the galaxy.                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Yukita                        Mihoko                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       X-RAY BINARIES IN THE LOW-METALLICITY GALAXY NGC 4631                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4631                                          @g–:)«yß@@FL/É{J@bGE8Ô5@UVlÙ!@e≈≤ïÈ‚@ÌãèFj@ÌXxVb‹50701002002AÛú∆?AÊƒÍJåA<◊∫¨ŸËA=o∫¨ŸËSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌÇÖ6˛␀␀Í,Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀We propose a joint NuSTAR and XMM observation of NGC4631 (d=7Mpc), a nearby metal-poor galaxy (Z/Z_sun ~ 0.2) with multiple luminous HMXBs. We will (1) examine the nature and accretion states of the HMXBs via color diagnostics, (2) measure the galaxy-integrated 0.3-30 keV spectrum to calibrate the bolometric correction for low metallicity galaxies, and (3) investigate the contribution of each bright source to this spectrum. As an ancillary science goal, we will search for the signature of inverse Compton emission from electrons accelerated in the magnetic field of the galaxy.                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Yukita                        Mihoko                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       X-RAY BINARIES IN THE LOW-METALLICITY GALAXY NGC 4631                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀M-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  20210315POX_52                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXxVb‹@ÌYèFj60701047001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌÅÃÂï␀␀Í,Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀2While future soft X-ray (0.5--2 keV) surveys are being designed to discover high-redshift (z > 9) heavy-seed mass black holes (hsBHs) with M_BH ~ 10^5 M_SUN, a rest-frame hard X-ray spectral model is crucial to understand how many hsBHs can be detected. Motivated by this, we propose a simultaneous 70-ksec NuSTAR and 20-ksec XMM-Newton observation to a local AGN in POX 52 with M_BH ~ 3x10^5 M_SUN and L_2-10 ~ 10^42 erg s^-1, resembling the future targets. While there are two expected X-ray models: (1) a power-law with low-energy cutoff like ultra-luminous X-ray sources and (2) one with high-energy cutoff and reflected emission like Seyfert galaxies, the superior sensitivity of NuSTAR above 10 keV will distinguish them and provide a reliable hard X-ray spectral template.                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kawamuro                      Taiki                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A HARD X-RAY SPECTRUM OF AN ACTIVE HEAVY-SEED MASS BH OF POX 52                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316POX_52                                            @fô„º”¿4ÎxF‹]@q˙º”ZÖà@DLÌëhr∞@fo›óˆ+k@ÌYèFj@Ìç±9Ò60701047002@ÒÅ‡ı¬è\@ÒRP„º”@ÚªnP∞Ú|@ÚªP∞Ú|SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì§zÄ0å␀␀Í,Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀2While future soft X-ray (0.5--2 keV) surveys are being designed to discover high-redshift (z > 9) heavy-seed mass black holes (hsBHs) with M_BH ~ 10^5 M_SUN, a rest-frame hard X-ray spectral model is crucial to understand how many hsBHs can be detected. Motivated by this, we propose a simultaneous 70-ksec NuSTAR and 20-ksec XMM-Newton observation to a local AGN in POX 52 with M_BH ~ 3x10^5 M_SUN and L_2-10 ~ 10^42 erg s^-1, resembling the future targets. While there are two expected X-ray models: (1) a power-law with low-energy cutoff like ultra-luminous X-ray sources and (2) one with high-energy cutoff and reflected emission like Seyfert galaxies, the superior sensitivity of NuSTAR above 10 keV will distinguish them and provide a reliable hard X-ray spectral template.                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kawamuro                      Taiki                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A HARD X-