)«y¶µ@@≈Êö‘,=@iŒ_p@ÌYÍN@ÌÇxVb‹60666012002@ı¡ˆîF@Ù“Íí£S@ˆq∆b∂Æ}@ˆrñb∂Æ}SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì§áeC!␀␀ÍGHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀JThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the North Ecliptic Pole (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have continuous visibility and 8-band deep imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST. In the past few years, the NEP DTDF has grown into a comprehensive survey with deep radio to X-ray data, becoming the most promising new field for extragalactic studies and time-domain investigations. Given interesting results from Cycle 5 NuSTAR program, we request 780 ks for long term monitoring of the NEP DTDF, split into 4 epochs (spaced before and after JWST launch over 2 NuSTAR cycles), enabling studies of hard X-ray variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN luminosity function at >8 keV, the obscured AGN fraction, and a bright NLSy1 at z> 1.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       DEEP NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF THE JWST-NEP TIME DOMAIN FIELD: LONG TERM MONITORING                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315XY_Ari                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌÇxVb‹@ÌÉ?r‘£30701007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì§M^oÅ␀␀ÍGHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀£This proposal argues for use of NuSTAR to observe four deeply eclipsing intermediate polar (IP) cataclysmic variables (CV) to define their hard (20-60 keV) spectra. The available data on these systems only provide lower limits to the fitted temperatures of their hard spectral component. This hard component is expected to arise from the accretion shock in these systems. A recent study demonstrated that Compton reflection exists in these IPs implying that the post-shock accretion region is tall and narrow. A previous study argued that the accretion region could be short and wide. The NuSTAR data will be used to investigate the post-shock accretion region: is it short and wide or tall and narrow?                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Schlegel                      Eric                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       ECLIPSING INTERMEDIATE POLARS WITH NUSTAR: SHORT & WIDE OR TALL & NARROW ACCRETION FOOTPRINTS?                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316XY_Ari                                            @F␀eîØO@3g€Ùá¸π@c‡/É{J#¿Ac¸PH@sÁ÷°a‰˜@ÌÉ?r‘£@Ì£xVb‹30701007002@Êe@N§©@Âÿ9xF‹@Á†Ò¥¢3ú@Á°ë¥¢3úSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì√ó¸˜G␀␀ÍGHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀£This proposal argues for use of NuSTAR to observe four deeply eclipsing intermediate polar (IP) cataclysmic variables (CV) to define their hard (20-60 keV) spectra. The available data on these systems only provide lower limits to the fitted temperatures of their hard spectral component. This hard component is expected to arise from the accretion shock in these systems. A recent study demonstrated that Compton reflection exists in these IPs implying that the post-shock accretion region is tall and narrow. A previous study argued that the accretion region could be short and wide. The NuSTAR data will be used to investigate the post-shock accretion region: is it short and wide or tall and narrow?                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Schlegel                      Eric                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       ECLIPSING INTERMEDIATE POLARS WITH NUSTAR: SHORT & WIDE OR TALL & NARROW ACCRETION FOOTPRINTS?                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315RX_J1713_SADA_22026                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì£xVb‹@Ì§Õ´∏140701104001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì√hd‹␀␀ÍNHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¯Previous observations with NuSTAR revealed near the outer edge of SNR RX J1713.7-3946 the presence of a region with an extremely hard spectrum. The measured photon index of 1 is incompatible with synchrotron radiation from accelerated electrons, implying the bremsstrahlung emission of sub-MeV electrons. We propose a follow-up observation of this hard component to precisely determine its extension and spectrum above 20 keV. This information would lead us to determine the nature (nonthermal or thermal) of the parent particles, presumably, shedding light on the injection process, one of the critical problems in diffusive shock acceleration theory.                                                                                                                                                    Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Tsuji                         Naomi                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNVEILING THE INJECTION PROBLEM OF DSA BY REVISITING HARD X-RAY COMPONENT IN SNR RXJ 1713.7-3946                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RX_J1713d7m3946_P3                                @päqﬁi≠¿C«1è≈Å@u±úwökQ?Œi≠B√…Ô@bãCïÅ@Ì§Õ´∏1@Ì”ÍN40701004001@ÎÍeöu@Î±@,<ûÌ@Ó`áˆ+jË@Ó`ßˆ+jËSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌÒd‚	‹␀␀ÍNHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¯Previous observations with NuSTAR revealed near the outer edge of SNR RX J1713.7-3946 the presence of a region with an extremely hard spectrum. The measured photon index of 1 is incompatible with synchrotron radiation from accelerated electrons, implying the bremsstrahlung emission of sub-MeV electrons. We propose a follow-up observation of this hard component to precisely determine its extension and spectrum above 20 keV. This information would lead us to determine the nature (nonthermal or thermal) of the parent particles, presumably, shedding light on the injection process, one of the critical problems in diffusive shock acceleration theory.                                                                                                                                                    Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Tsuji                         Naomi                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNVEILING THE INJECTION PROBLEM OF DSA BY REVISITING HARD X-RAY COMPONENT IN SNR RXJ 1713.7-3946                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀INERTIAL only / idiagnostics / SL-A23B03 (4U 1700-377, 2MASS J17004888-4139214)                                         archived  202103154U_1700m37                                        @p␀·GÆ{¿B·∫^5?}@uª!-w2@uY≥–|Ö@bö‘,<ü@Ì”ÍN@ÌˇxVb‹30701023001@Ë†Oì‡ﬂ@Ë≤3úø@Ì¡Â·∞â†@Ì¬•·∞â†SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìå¥á␀␀ÍNHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀oWe propose an 80 ks observation of the supergiant HMXB 4U 1700-37 to confirm the presence of a cyclotron line, search for pulsations, and study the accreting matter via flares. We aim to conclusively establish whether the compact object is a neutron star. We expect to confirm the putative 16 keV cyclotron line at high significance. We will rigorously test any distortions of the line profile and create a physical model of the accretion column. Spectro-temporal analysis of the source will yield valuable information about the accretion environment like density and clumpiness of the wind, accretion disk properties, or plasma instabilities in the accretion column. These data will boost our understanding of how characteristics of supergiant HMXBs depend on the properties of the compact object.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bala                          Suman                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       CONFIRMATION OF THE CYCLOTRON FEATURE IN 4U 1700-37                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀INERTIAL only / idiagnostics / SL-A0123 (GX 349+2)                                                                      archived  20210315MAXI_J0709m159                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌˇxVb‹@Ì␀xVb‹90801304001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì#≠À©á␀␀Ë·Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M