 components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20210315AT2022cmc                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìó#Ü@Ìò[‰õø80701510001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì©ˇÁ∫7␀␀È´Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀»A growing number of tidal disruption events (TDEs) are expected to be discovered by the ongoing Spektrum-Roentgen-Gamma (SRG) all-sky X-ray survey. We propose 2x40ks NuSTAR observations on two subgroups of SRG-discovered TDEs with hard X-ray emission. For non-relativistic TDEs with non-thermal X-ray emission, the observation will allow us to search for spectral features characteristic of relativistic reflection. For relativistic TDEs, this will provide the highest resolution hard X-ray spectrum ever obtained, shedding light on the power mechanism of TDE jets.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Yao                           Yuhan                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF TIDAL DISRUPTION EVENTS                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2022cmc                                         @ivoiDg8@@°˘rGE@QtØOÑM@Sª»K]Ãd@a¿4m≈÷9@Ìò[‰õø@Ì∏Õ´∏180701510002@Â‡ÀŒ_p@Â®√*ô1@Á^®¡T @Á^^®¡T SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì√Ü£␀␀È´Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀»A growing number of tidal disruption events (TDEs) are expected to be discovered by the ongoing Spektrum-Roentgen-Gamma (SRG) all-sky X-ray survey. We propose 2x40ks NuSTAR observations on two subgroups of SRG-discovered TDEs with hard X-ray emission. For non-relativistic TDEs with non-thermal X-ray emission, the observation will allow us to search for spectral features characteristic of relativistic reflection. For relativistic TDEs, this will provide the highest resolution hard X-ray spectrum ever obtained, shedding light on the power mechanism of TDE jets.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Yao                           Yuhan                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       NUSTAR OBSERVATIONS OF TIDAL DISRUPTION EVENTS                                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20210315IRAS_04416p1216                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì∏Õ´∏1@ÌπÍN60701026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì√P‡ÖÄ␀␀ÍjHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ﬁWe propose here a broadband X-ray study of a peculiar hyper-Eddington Active Galactic Nucleus, namely IRAS 04416+1215, by observing it simultaneously with NuSTAR (170ks) and XMM-Newton (40ks). IRAS 04416+1215 has one of the highest Eddington-ratio in the local Universe (lambda_Edd=427), it represents a prototype for the elusive class of hyper-Eddington AGN. The aim of these simultaneous observations is to understand the behaviour of the primary power-law and the reprocessed radiation in the regime of extreme accretion, as well as the properties of soft-excess and the outflows. To this end, the combined large effective area of XMM-Newton and high sensitivity of NuSTAR at high energy are fundamental to constrain the spectral parameter of the proposed target.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tortosa                       Alessia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       BROAD-BAND X-RAY STUDY OF A PECULIAR HYPER-EDDINGTON AGN: IRAS04416+1215                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_04416p1216                                   @Q≈≤-V@(§µ‹∆?@g6´6zë¿5e° ¿É@tYàŒp:˚@ÌπÍN@ÌCèFj60701026002A1[⁄Óc A¸„0â†'Aæoá»K^A¿óá»K^SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌajIìà␀␀ÍjHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ﬁWe propose here a broadband X-ray study of a peculiar hyper-Eddington Active Galactic Nucleus, namely IRAS 04416+1215, by observing it simultaneously with NuSTAR (170ks) and XMM-Newton (40ks). IRAS 04416+1215 has one of the highest Eddington-ratio in the local Universe (lambda_Edd=427), it represents a prototype for the elusive class of hyper-Eddington AGN. The aim of these simultaneous observations is to understand the behaviour of the primary power-law and the reprocessed radiation in the regime of extreme accretion, as well as the properties of soft-excess and the outflows. To this end, the combined large effective area of XMM-Newton and high sensitivity of NuSTAR at high energy are fundamental to constrain the spectral parameter of the proposed target.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Tortosa                       Alessia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       BROAD-BAND X-RAY STUDY OF A PECULIAR HYPER-EDDINGTON AGN: IRAS04416+1215                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀         