ed shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2022cmc                                         @iv•zxl"@@¢¶L/É{@Qw=»K^@SªÀ)^û@`e?|Ìëh@Ì—±9Ò@ÌÓ[‰õø90802306002@‰-∏ê-‡@„¸l™d¬¯@Âºò4m≈÷@Âºÿ4m≈÷SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì$P¯ÀH␀␀Ë˝Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2) ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  20220215MCGp06m16m028                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌÓ[‰õø@ÌÔ?r‘£60702053005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì$$±~K␀␀ÍjHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀HFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of 4 CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCGp06m16m028                                     @[3úæ‡@A°0æÌ)@fÀZÖáìﬁ@3Cúæﬂ§@uÍ„º”ZÜ@ÌÔ?r‘£@Ì?r‘£60702053006@⁄1û‚ÎC@Ÿ‰À⁄QŒ@€ˇ;=»K@€ˇ√=»KSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì*ïˇ=“␀␀ÍjHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀HFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of 4 CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215AT2018cow                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì?r‘£@Ì?r‘£80502407001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì*r(3î␀␀ÍyHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀IÉWe propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       XMM-NuSTAR Observations of Rapid, Luminous and Blue Stellar Explosions                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2018cow                                         @nÅA [¿@6L/É{J#@C[dZ¨@EÂsÍ≥g°@c§HËßÁ@Ì?r‘£@ÌaxVb‹80502407002@˛¯ÄP∞Ú|@˛ßüj~˘€A␀ë∑Ú‰éäA␀í«Ú‰éäSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì˚L;*␀␀ÍyHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀IÉWe propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on our recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X18       XMM-NuSTAR Observations of Rapid, Luminous and Blue Stellar Explosions                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀ToO / M-class solar flare                                                                                               archived  20220215IceCube220303A                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌaxVb‹@ÌaÍN80701610001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌÅ»—Y‚␀␀ÈºHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¸We propose to perform two 40 ksec target of opportunity follow-up observations of X-ray luminous sources that we identify in association with two separate ``gold''-quality IceCube high-energy neutrino alerts, which have a likelihood of at least 70% to be astrophysical in origin. The first identification of an astrophysical source of high-energy neutrinos occurred in 2018, following IceCube-170922A and the BL~Lac-type blazar TXS 0506+056. X-ray follow-up of likely-cosmic neutrino alerts has thus proven its utility for identifying possible neutrino counterparts, and most importantly to measure the hadronic contribution to the high-energy emission. We aim to use this approach to identify more such sources and calculate their neutrino flux.                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    McBride                       Felicia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       BLAZAR X-RAYS AND NEUTRINOS: INVESTIGATING THE MOST PROMISING ICECUBE NEUTRINO ALERTS                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IceCube220303A                                    @pº?º@%ÛéÛMj@BOv`@2çcàeîØ@dSA [¿@ÌaÍN@ÌÅÕ´∏180701610002@‰}])^û@‰G¶÷°aÂ@ÂÙe° ¿É@ÂˆE° ¿ÉSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì¢≥Kx¸␀␀ÈºHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¸We propose to perform two 40 ksec target of opportunity follow-up observations of X-ray luminous sources that we identify in association with two separate ``gold''-quality IceCube high-energy neutrino alerts, which have a likelihood of at least 70% to be astrophysical in origin. The first identification of an astrophysical source of high-energy neutrinos occurred in 2018, following IceCube-170922A and the BL~Lac-type blazar TXS 0506+056. X-ray follow-up of likely-cosmic neutrino alerts has thus proven its utility for identifying possible neutrino counterparts, and most importantly to measure the hadronic contribution to the high-energy emission. We aim to use this approach to identify more such sources and calculate their neutrino flux.                                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    McBride                       Felicia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       BLAZAR X-RAYS AND NEUTRINOS: INVESTIGATING THE MOST PROMISING ICECUBE NEUTRINO ALERTS                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO / Kp=5                                                                                                              archived  20220215NGC_591                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌÅÕ´∏1@ÌÇÕ´∏160701057001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì¢Ö∏R␀␀ÍqHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀]A 150 ks NuSTAR observation of the extreme candidate Compton-thick AGN NGC 591 (z=0.0152) is proposed. A previous XMM snapshot of 12 ks revealed the presence of a very strong Fe k-alpha emission line EW=3.6 +/- 1.1/1.4 keV. This suggests that this object is characterized by heavy obscuration and hence could be identified as an extreme Compton-thick AGN. With a NuSTAR observation we will obtain crucial information on its hard X-ray spectral properties and derive a robust constraint on the intrinsic column density (N_H). These measurements, will allow us to characterize in great details the properties of the strong Fe K-alpha line and the physical properties of the torus with the ultimate goal of deriving a more physical picture of the innermost regions of highly obscured AGN.               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Matzeu                        Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A NEW EXTREME COMPTON-THICK AGN CANDIDATE NGC 591                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_591                                           @7^Vìu@AÕ÷8ÜYK@`Ü§®¡T ¿:£˛\ë—N@r+a@N•@ÌÇÕ´∏1@ÌÏî»)¯60701057002Aø®åÁAÍÂaGÆAP‚«Æ{AP˙«Æ{SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì h≈6˛␀␀ÍqHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀]A 150 ks NuSTAR observation of the extreme candidate Compton-thick AGN NGC 591 (z=0.0152) is proposed. A previous XMM snapshot of 12 ks revealed the presence of a very strong Fe k-alpha emission line EW=3.6 +/- 1.1/1.4 keV. This suggests that this object is characterized by heavy obscuration and hence could be identified as an extreme Compton-thick AGN. With a NuSTAR observation we will obtain crucial information on its hard X-ray spectral properties and derive a robust constraint on the intrinsic column density (N_H). These measurements, will allow us to characterize in great details the properties of the strong Fe K-alpha line and the physical properties of the torus with the ultimate goal of deriving a more physical picture of the innermost regions of highly obscured AGN.               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Matzeu                        Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A NEW EXTREME COMPTON-THICK AGN CANDIDATE NGC 591                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215MKN501                                            @o∞⁄πıY¥@CÁﬂ;dZ@OŸb∂Æ}@C{6zêó@cD7¥¢3ú@ÌÏî»)¯@ÌÌ[‰õø60701032001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì ˆ;ÏG␀␀ÍqHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙The investigators propose to observe up to 6 X-ray bright blazars plus the radio galaxy Cen A with NuSTAR during X-ray linear polarization measurements by the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). The 0.3-70 keV continuum spectrum derived from synchronous NuSTAR and Swift observations will allow modeling needed to interpret the IXPE polarization data. Comparison of millimeter-wave, optical, and X-ray polarization, and measurement of the mm/optical/UV/X-ray continuum spectra of the blazars and Cen A with NuSTAR, Swift, and ground-based telescopes, will provide data needed to determine the mechanism and location of the X-ray emission, which have yet to be determined conclusively. It will also test models for particle acceleration that produces high-energy emission in relativistic jets. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MULTI-ENERGY X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF IXPE BLAZAR TARGETS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MKN501                                            @o∞ö‘,<ü@CÂ„S˜ŒŸ@O÷ﬂ§?Â…@C{Õ5®Xy@c@ûö@ÌÌ[‰õø@Ì¸èFj60701032002@”BˇW>´6@”ËßÁ@‘«ƒ2 X@‘ ƒ2 XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì ﬂ1Æ◊␀␀ÍqHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˙The investigators propose to observe up to 6 X-ray bright blazars plus the radio galaxy Cen A with NuSTAR during X-ray linear polarization measurements by the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). The 0.3-70 keV continuum spectrum derived from synchronous NuSTAR and Swift observations will allow modeling needed to interpret the IXPE polarization data. Comparison of millimeter-wave, optical, and X-ray polarization, and measurement of the mm/optical/UV/X-ray continuum spectra of the blazars and Cen A with NuSTAR, Swift, and ground-based telescopes, will provide data needed to determine the mechanism and location of the X-ray emission, which have yet to be determined conclusively. It will also test models for particle acceleration that produces high-energy emission in relativistic jets. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MULTI-ENERGY X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF IXPE BLAZAR TARGETS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                  