,FsÅÿ@”⁄%Ì(åÁ@’z≠‘ï+@’|m‘ï+SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì #ÌÀ™␀␀ÈHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215SGR_1830m0645                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì èFj@Ì î»)¯30701006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì "Óuí␀␀ÍxHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀âMagnetars form a small group of isolated X-ray pulsars powered by the dissipation of their magnetic energy. The physical mechanism powering their activity is currently poorly understood. It is in particular debated whether the long-term evolution of their outbursts is due to the cooling of the stellar crust, and/or the untwisting of complex magnetic field structures in the magnetosphere. We propose joint observations with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton of the latest discovered magnetar in outburst, SGR 1830-0645, to map the thermal structures on its surface, test the model predictions for magnetar outbursts, and gain insights into the physics of magnetars' emission.                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Coti Zelati                   Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNRAVELLING THE EMISSION MECHANISMS OF MAGNETARS: DEEP X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF SGR 1830-0645 TOWARDS ITS QUIESCENCE       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SGR_1830m0645                                     @q[ê-‡¿›≤-V@8vYJÙÿ?˚∑X‚e@cÖ2a|⁄@Ì î»)¯@Ì T#Ü30701006002@ı«‡L/É{@ıí2ffff@˜ßWÿD–@˜®ÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì q}'“}␀␀ÍxHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀âMagnetars form a small group of isolated X-ray pulsars powered by the dissipation of their magnetic energy. The physical mechanism powering their activity is currently poorly understood. It is in particular debated whether the long-term evolution of their outbursts is due to the cooling of the stellar crust, and/or the untwisting of complex magnetic field structures in the magnetosphere. We propose joint observations with NuSTAR and XMM-Newton of the latest discovered magnetar in outburst, SGR 1830-0645, to map the thermal structures on its surface, test the model predictions for magnetar outbursts, and gain insights into the physics of magnetars' emission.                                                                                                                                       Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Coti Zelati                   Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNRAVELLING THE EMISSION MECHANISMS OF MAGNETARS: DEEP X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF SGR 1830-0645 TOWARDS ITS QUIESCENCE       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀idiagnostics / M-class solar flare                                                                                      archived  20220215IGR_J18434m0508                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì T#Ü@Ì T[‰õø30760002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì q*aŸQ␀␀ÍxHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀The hard X-rays from accreting white dwarfs (WDs) in magnetic cataclysmic variables (CVs) are produced by shock-heating in the accretion column, and the measured temperature of the accreting material depends on the mass of the WD. Thus, with high-quality hard X-ray spectra, it is possible to determine the WD mass. An important question is whether the WD mass distribution extends up to the Chandrasekhar limit. The best place to look for high-mass WDs is among CVs with the hardest spectra, making CVs found in the hard X-ray INTEGRAL survey excellent candidates for having the most massive WDs. We propose NuSTAR and XMM observations of two IGR CVs. In addition to measuring the WD masses, the observations will be used to search for periodic signals expected to be seen at the WD rotation period.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       WHITE DWARF MASSES AND SPIN PERIODS FOR HARD X-RAY-SELECTED GALACTIC SOURCES                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J18434m0508                                   @qç|⁄Q¿6_ÿ≠´ü@;\ÁØ∑Èø–ÿD–©@czÊ1¯†ê@Ì T[‰õø@Ì t[‰õø30760002002@„ûU&Èx’@„p˘^û	@ÂIL‚ÎC@ÂK¨‚ÎCSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì É∞‘bõ␀␀ÍxHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀The hard X-rays from accreting white dwarfs (WDs) in magnetic cataclysmic variables (CVs) are produced by shock-heating in the accretion column, and the measured temperature of the accreting material depends on the mass of the WD. Thus, with high-quality hard X-ray spectra, it is possible to determine the WD mass. An important question is whether the WD mass distribution extends up to the Chandrasekhar limit. The best place to look for high-mass WDs is among CVs with the hardest spectra, making CVs found in the hard X-ray INTEGRAL survey excellent candidates for having the most massive WDs. We propose NuSTAR and XMM observations of two IGR CVs. In addition to measuring the WD masses, the observations will be used to search for periodic signals expected to be seen at the WD rotation period.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       WHITE DWARF MASSES AND SPIN PERIODS FOR HARD X-RAY-SELECTED GALACTIC SOURCES                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                           XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀idiagnostics / CME Kp=6                                                                                                 archived  20220215IGRJ19405m3016                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì t[‰õø@Ì tÕ´∏160160717001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì ÉÅl¡␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                