ctic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting super-massive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing previously successful selection criteria, we propose to target 2 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the Swift-BAT 150-month catalog with joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       HUNTING FOR COMPTON-THICK AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IC_2227                                           @^nÊ1¯†ê@BéÛMj@ga‰˜e˛@=ÿ*ô0æ@vÅo␀h‹@Ì"G±9Ò@Ì"mÍN60701049002@ÈyqÔù≤-@È4ã,•zx@ÎQƒõ•„@ÎR∫ƒõ•„SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"âœå¥á␀␀ÍÜHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀3The X-ray spectral analysis of new Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting super-massive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing previously successful selection criteria, we propose to target 2 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the Swift-BAT 150-month catalog with joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       HUNTING FOR COMPTON-THICK AGN IN THE LOCAL UNIVERSE                                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀M-class solar flares                                                                                                    archived  202202154U_1820m30                                        @q?d¬¯7µ¿>Qƒ2 W®@â†'RTa¿•îØOÑ@cJ#9¿Ï@Ì"mÍN@Ì"o?r‘£80702318004␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì"âöºﬂ␀␀È–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀CThe modeling of neutron-star LMXB X-ray spectra often suffers from a lack of coverage at either the low or high energy end. NICER spectra offer great low-energy coverage, with the potential to obtain excellent constraints on the thermal components, including parameters such as the inner disk radius and size of the boundary layer. However, this still requires additional coverage above 10 keV. Here we request three 20 ks NICER/NuSTAR ToO observations of the ultra-compact neutron-star LMXB 4U 1820-30, a bright source with large spectral variations. The observations will be triggered to observe the source at select locations in its spectral range, with the goal of improving our understanding of the evolution of the various spectral components and the underlying changes in the accretion flow.  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Homan                         Jeroen                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R03       BROADBAND SPECTRA OF THE ULTRA-COMPACT X-RAY BINARY 4U 1820-30                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1820m30                                        @q?9XbN¿>R˛≈m\˚@x7¥¢3ú¿üo“ÚÂ@c¬¯7¥¢@Ì"o?r‘£@Ì"î»)¯80702318005@“z¸£◊
=@“≈~␀—∑@⁄:bM”@⁄:bM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"âf«}â␀␀È–Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀CThe modeling of neutron-star LMXB X-ray spectra often suffers from a lack of coverage at either the low or high energy end. NICER spectra offer great low-energy coverage, with the potential to obtain excellent constraints on the thermal components, including parameters such as the inner disk radius and size of the boundary layer. However, this still requires additional coverage above 10 keV. Here we request three 20 ks NICER/NuSTAR ToO observations of the ultra-compact neutron-star LMXB 4U 1820-30, a bright source with large spectral variations. The observations will be triggered to observe the source at select locations in its spectral range, with the goal of improving our understanding of the evolution of the various spectral components and the underlying changes in the accretion flow.  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Homan                         Jeroen                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R03       BROADBAND SPECTRA OF THE ULTRA-COMPACT X-RAY BINARY 4U 1820-30                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀(3/3) ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  20220215IGR_J17091m3624                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì"î»)¯@Ì"ÍN80702315005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì"âCQän␀␀È–Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Black hole astrophysics can be regarded as a fundamental tool in studying the accretion and ejection physics in the strongest gravity regime in the Universe. Reflection spectroscopy studies the time-averaged energy spectrum, providing constraints on properties in the accretion disk and the corona, but degeneracies take place in problems such as the truncation level of the disk. Reverberation mapping is a timing technique revealing the disk-corona geometry in the innermost regions, which could help break degeneracies. With recent cutting-edge developments in physical spectral-timing models, the main goal in this proposal is to find clues on the state transition mechanism, and the coupling between the disk, corona and jet in black hole binaries.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Wang                          Jingyi                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R03       RELATIVISTIC REFLECTION AND REVERBERATION MAPPING IN A BLACK HOLE BINARY                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J17091m3624                                   @pF‹]cà¿B/
=p£◊@u÷«y¶µ@ÆÊ1¯†ê@dê˜e˝ä€@Ì"ÍN@Ì"éÍN80702315006@Œ(2Öáìﬁ@Œlj~˘€@‘÷êxG@‘◊xGSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"∞»—Y‚␀␀È–Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀Black hole astrophysics can be regarded as a fundamental tool in studying the accretion and ejection physics in the strongest gravity regime in the Universe. Reflection spectroscopy studies the time-averaged energy spectrum, providing constraints on properties in the accretion disk and the corona, but degeneracies take place in problems such as the truncation level of the disk. Reverberation mapping is a timing technique revealing the disk-corona geometry in the innermost regions, which could help break degeneracies. With recent cutting-edge developments in physical spectral-timing models, the main goal in this proposal is to find clues on the state transition mechanism, and the coupling between the disk, corona and jet in black hole binaries.                                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Wang                          Jingyi                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R03       RELATIVISTIC REFLECTION AND REVERBERATION MAPPING IN A BLACK HOLE BINARY                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/3) ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  202202152MASXJ20343135m3037289                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì"éÍN@Ì"èxVb‹60061301001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì"∞¶fff␀␀ÈHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASXJ20343135m3037289                            @sJí£S&¿>ìtºj~˙@)çjOv¿A° ¿É@`—X‚e,@Ì"èxVb‹@Ì"û?r‘£60061301002@’`6!ñRΩ@’'ÍHËß@÷˙™àŒp;@÷˚jàŒp;SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"∞é ÜB␀␀ÈHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀X-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  202202151H1934m063                                        @rfÙá¸π$¿¶¡Ω•@@=÷8ÜYK¿)≥Í≥g†˘@bëC,•z@Ì"û?r‘£@Ì"û±9Ò60702018007@b∑úæﬂ§@bç÷8ÜYK@dÀ˚µt@dÀ˚µtSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì"∞m:‘␀␀ÍçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¨NuSTAR can make a transformational advance in our understanding of how luminous accreting black holes work through long observations of rapidly-variable, X-ray-bright AGN. This enables X-ray reverberation studies, already a commonplace feature of AGN, to proceed to the next level and follow the behavior of the powerful dynamic corona and map the accretion flow immediately next to the black hole, measuring both spin and mass of the black hole. We propose a 500ks NuSTAR observation of the X-ray brightest high variability NLS1 AGN 1H1934-063, with 260ks of XMM, to measure relativistic reflection and reverberation in 3 flux states.                                                                                                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A DEEP SPECTRAL-TIMING STUDY OF THE X-RAY BRIGHT AGN 1H 1934-063                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202202151H1934m063                                        @rfÁlãCñ¿ØÉ{J#:@@<ë—N;Õ¿)¥ï*ô1@b}F
¶L0@Ì"û±9Ò@Ì"ﬂèFj60702018008@ˆâ(åÁ@ı ÃcÒA@˜õq•zxl@˜ú•zxlSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì"„HpB¿␀␀ÍçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¨NuSTAR can make a transformational advance in our understanding of how luminous accreting black holes work through long observations of rapidly-variable, X-ray-bright AGN. This enables X-ray reverberation studies, already a commonplace feature of AGN, to proceed to the next level and follow the behavior of the powerful dynamic corona and map the accretion flow immediately next to the black hole, measuring both spin and mass of the black hole. We propose a 500ks NuSTAR observation of the X-ray brightest high variability NLS1 AGN 1H1934-063, with 260ks of XMM, to measure relativistic reflection and reverberation in 3 flux states.                                                                                                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A DEEP SPECTRAL-TIMING STUDY OF THE X-RAY BRIGHT AGN 1H 1934-063                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare CME                                                                                                                   ␀␀␀(4a/4) M-class solar flare / CME Kp=5                                                                                   archived  20220215SN2022ffg                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì"ﬂèFj@Ì"‡?r‘£80702508001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì##]≠R␀␀ÈfHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¬Contrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated XMM-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program has the immediate goal to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. This programs has the potential enlarge the very small sample of two strongly interacting SNe with broad-band X-ray detections.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A BROAD-BAND X-RAY VIEW OF THE MOST EXTREME MASS-LOSS WITH NUSTAR+XMM                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2022ffg                                         @c,è\(ı√@2πıY≥–@kBöu%F@J3–|Ñµ›@2a|⁄Q@Ì"‡?r‘£@Ì#î»)¯80702508002@È≤ÑbM”@ÈoŒp:˚@Îíå˚~ë␀@Îî¨˚~ë␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì#$„-"␀␀ÈfHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¬Contrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated XMM-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program has the immediate goal to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. This programs has the potential enlarge the very small sample of two strongly interacting SNe with broad-band X-ray detections.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A BROAD-BAND X-RAY VIEW OF THE MOST EXTREME MASS-LOSS WITH NUSTAR+XMM                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare CME                                                                                                                   ␀␀␀(1/2) M-class solar flares / CME Kp=5                                                                                   archived  202202151H1934m063                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì#î»)¯@Ì#	Õ´∏160702018009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì##œ\(ˆ␀␀ÍçHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¨NuSTAR can make a transformational advance in our understanding of how luminous accreting black holes work through long observations of rapidly-variable, X-ray-bright AGN. This enables X-ray reverberation studies, already a commonplace feature of AGN, to proceed to the next level and follow the behavior of the powerful dynamic corona and map the accretion flow immediately next to the black hole, measuring both spin and mass of the black hole. We propose a 500ks NuSTAR observation of the X-ray brightest high variability NLS1 AGN 1H1934-063, with 260ks of XMM, to measure relativistic reflection and reverberation in 3 flux states.                                                                                                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A DEEP SPECTRAL-TIMING STUDY OF THE X-RAY BRIGHT AGN 1H 1934-063                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161H1934m063                                        @rf—∑X‚¿µY≥–|Ö@@;ò«‚ÇA¿)≥tºj~˙@bq¯†ê-‡@Ì#	Õ´∏1@Ì#+±9Ò60702018010@Áù˜È·∞ä@Á_ŸsÍ≥h@ÈEiGÆ{@ÈFIGÆ{SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì#J‰…ƒ␀␀ÍçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¨NuSTAR can make a transformational advance in our understanding of how luminous accreting black holes work through long observations of rapidly-variable, X-ray-bright AGN. This enables X-ray reverberation studies, already a commonplace feature of AGN, to proceed to the next level and follow the behavior of the powerful dynamic corona and map the accretion flow immediately next to the black hole, measuring both spin and mass of the black hole. We propose a 500ks NuSTAR observation of the X-ray brightest high variability NLS1 AGN 1H1934-063, with 260ks of XMM, to measure relativistic reflection and reverberation in 3 flux states.                                                                                                                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Fabian                        Andrew                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A DEEP SPECTRAL-TIMING STUDY OF THE X-RAY BRIGHT AGN 1H 1934-063                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(4b/4)                                                                                                                  archived  20220215HB89_0716p714                                     @[ì§?Â…@Q‘K]ÃcÒ@a˛l"h	’@;ƒ9XbN@uoZ¨1@Ì#+±9Ò@Ì#,±9Ò60701037001@;®¡T…Ü@~ÈπıY≥–@Ä†␀␀␀␀␀@Ä†␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì#J¥á,␀␀ÍçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˙The investigators propose to observe up to 6 X-ray bright blazars plus the radio galaxy Cen A with NuSTAR during X-ray linear polarization measurements by the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). The 0.3-70 keV continuum spectrum derived from synchronous NuSTAR and Swift observations will allow modeling needed to interpret the IXPE polarization data. Comparison of millimeter-wave, optical, and X-ray polarization, and measurement of the mm/optical/UV/X-ray continuum spectra of the blazars and Cen A with NuSTAR, Swift, and ground-based telescopes, will provide data needed to determine the mechanism and location of the X-ray emission, which have yet to be determined conclusively. It will also test models for particle acceleration that produces high-energy emission in relativistic jets. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MULTI-ENERGY X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF IXPE BLAZAR TARGETS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215HB89_0716p714                                     @[ñe+”√@Q‘7¥¢3ú@a˛{≤˛≈m@;«_o“Û@uo7¥¢3ú@Ì#,±9Ò@Ì#9±9Ò60701037002@’ƒ€3333@’á]…‰@◊(Óäqﬁj@◊'ÓäqﬁjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì#Jû∏QÏ␀␀ÍçHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˙The investigators propose to observe up to 6 X-ray bright blazars plus the radio galaxy Cen A with NuSTAR during X-ray linear polarization measurements by the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). The 0.3-70 keV continuum spectrum derived from synchronous NuSTAR and Swift observations will allow modeling needed to interpret the IXPE polarization data. Comparison of millimeter-wave, optical, and X-ray polarization, and measurement of the mm/optical/UV/X-ray continuum spectra of the blazars and Cen A with NuSTAR, Swift, and ground-based telescopes, will provide data needed to determine the mechanism and location of the X-ray emission, which have yet to be determined conclusively. It will also test models for particle acceleration that produces high-energy emission in relativistic jets. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       MULTI-ENERGY X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF IXPE BLAZAR TARGETS                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀CVZ                                                                                                                     archived  20220215VIIZw118                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì#9±9Ò@Ì#:èFj60160283001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì#JÁ∫7␀␀È Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316VIIZw118                                          @ZÆnóçOﬂ@P$ÃÃÃÃÕ@bÈÆÊ1¯°@9ª=»K^@u:‹∆?@Ì#:èFj@Ì#IèFj60160283002@◊QNHËß@◊sÜ¬&Å@ÿˇE8Ô5@Ÿ√E8Ô5SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì#k^–ò␀␀È Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215M82_X2                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì#IèFj@Ì#I[‰õø30702012003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì#j‡ù≈ò␀␀ÍîHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÏUltraluminous x-ray sources (ULXs) are X-ray binaries radiating above the Eddington luminosity. An increasing sample of ULXs are known to be powered by accreting pulsars (PULXs), and it is currently unclear if their observed emission is amplified by strong radiation beaming or it denotes a genuine super-Eddington mass accretion rate. In the second case, the large mass transfer required is expected to produce detectable effects on the binary orbit, which were tentatively detected in past observations. In this program, we use pulsar timing to track the orbital evolution of the first discovered PULX, M82 X-2, producing a strong constraint on the mass transfer and finally discriminate between the beaming and the extreme super-Eddington models.                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       TRACKING THE ORBITAL ADVANCE OF THE FIRST ULTRALUMINOUS PULSAR M82 X-2                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M82_X2                                            @böõ•„S¯@Qke˝ä⁄∫@aÆêˇó$t@D+í:)«z@2ª/ÏV÷@Ì#I[‰õø@Ì#öÕ´∏130702012004@¸∞#óçOﬂ@¸j˝Ä4m∆@˛◊¥”ZÖà@˛Ÿ”ZÖàSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì#©*€6<␀␀ÍîHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÏUltraluminous x-ray sources (ULXs) are X-ray binaries radiating above the Eddington luminosity. An increasing sample of ULXs are known to be powered by accreting pulsars (PULXs), and it is currently unclear if their observed emission is amplified by strong radiation beaming or it denotes a genuine super-Eddington mass accretion rate. In the second case, the large mass transfer required is expected to produce detectable effects on the binary orbit, which were tentatively detected in past observations. In this program, we use pulsar timing to track the orbital evolution of the first discovered PULX, M82 X-2, producing a strong constraint on the mass transfer and finally discriminate between the beaming and the extreme super-Eddington models.                                                   Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Bachetti                      Matteo                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       TRACKING THE ORBITAL ADVANCE OF THE FIRST ULTRALUMINOUS PULSAR M82 X-2                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20220215Mrk_817                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì#öÕ´∏1@Ì#õ?r‘£90801608001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì#®”o·©␀␀È'Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs           