                           @nóS&¡æ¿MπGÆz·@thxl"h
¿5¬è\(ˆ@hÎ=Ÿb∑@Ì%7±9Ò@Ì%FèFj60061269002@÷§å†ê-‡@÷i¿®¡T @ÿ ∏64@ÿ ∏64SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì%bÒÓ-␀␀È1Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀ M-class solar flare                                                                                                    archived  20220215IGR_J17091m3624                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì%FèFj@Ì%FxVb‹80802321001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì%bŒ8„é␀␀ÍOHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛Black hole astrophysics can be regarded as a fundamental tool in studying the accretion and ejection physics in the strongest gravity regime in the Universe. Reflection spectroscopy studies the time-averaged flux-energy spectrum, providing constraints on properties in the accretion disk and the corona, but degeneracies take place in problems such as the truncation level of the disk. Reverberation mapping is a timing technique revealing the disk-corona geometry in the innermost regions, which could help break degeneracies. With recent cutting-edge developments in physical spectral-timing models, the main goal in this proposal is to find clues on the state transition mechanism, and the coupling between the disk, corona and jet in black hole binaries.                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Wang                          Jingyi                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R04       RELATIVISTIC REFLECTION AND REVERBERATION MAPPING IN A BLACK HOLE BINARY                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J17091m3624                                   @pPH¿B0eîØO@u÷©*0U2@ûÏø±[W@el⁄QŒ@Ì%FxVb‹@Ì%W?r‘£80802321002@—/ë≈÷8Ü@—Tç+”√a@÷∞s;dZ@÷¥3;dZSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì%b¶Ø7¿␀␀ÍOHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˛Black hole astrophysics can be regarded as a fundamental tool in studying the accretion and ejection physics in the strongest gravity regime in the Universe. Reflection spectroscopy studies the time-averaged flux-energy spectrum, providing constraints on properties in the accretion disk and the corona, but degeneracies take place in problems such as the truncation level of the disk. Reverberation mapping is a timing technique revealing the disk-corona geometry in the innermost regions, which could help break degeneracies. With recent cutting-edge developments in physical spectral-timing models, the main goal in this proposal is to find clues on the state transition mechanism, and the coupling between the disk, corona and jet in black hole binaries.                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Wang                          Jingyi                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R04       RELATIVISTIC REFLECTION AND REVERBERATION MAPPING IN A BLACK HOLE BINARY                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/3) idiagnostics / SL-B (4U 1700-377)                                                                                 archived  202202151E_1547d0m5408                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì%W?r‘£@Ì%W±9Ò90801309001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì%bÉi–7␀␀È1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161E_1547d0m5408                                    @m∫:˚~ë␀¿K$Ñµ‹∆?@tr?º?≠VlÙ!-@iπ•ú‡v@Ì%W±9Ò@Ì%gxVb‹90801309002@◊x¿y=Ÿ@◊B·„S˜œ@ŸMVåLò@ŸNVåLòSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì%àO\(ˆ␀␀È1Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀ToO / idiagnostics / SL-AB03 (H 1538-522) / M-class solar flare                                                         archived  20220215EFEDS_SRCID_7                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì%gxVb‹@Ì%hxVb‹60762001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì%à,GL˝␀␀ÍûHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀qThe eROSITA instrument on-board Spectrum-Roentgen-Gamma (SRG) has performed the first imaging all-sky survey in the hard X-ray (2-10keV) band. The final survey will be over 30 times deeper than previous hard X-ray surveys, yielding a large and very pure sample of AGN. Combining eROSITA data with observations from other multi-wavelength missions, a detailed picture of the AGN population can be constructed, including black hole mass, luminosity, and spectral shape. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR+XMM-Newton follow up observations of a group of hard X-ray selected AGN observed with eROSITA. This pilot study will demonstrate the power of joint NuSTAR and XMM observations to break degeneracies present in different spectral models, motivating future joint NuSTAR, XMM and eROSITA investigations.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       PHYSICAL CONSTRAINTS FROM AGN X-RAY SPECTRA: A PILOT NUSTAR-XMM STUDY OF EROSITA-SELECTED SOURCES                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316EFEDS_SRCID_7                                     @ag<64øËcä	ﬁ@lˇCïÅ%@?A≤ïÈ‚@u¥∆ßÔù≤@Ì%hxVb‹@Ì%ô[‰õø60762001002@ÌÀ‚Ç@∏@¡—Xy=Ÿ@ÚÚ‰è@ÚlÚ‰èSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì%™?=—ª␀␀ÍûHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀qThe eROSITA instrument on-board Spectrum-Roentgen-Gamma (SRG) has performed the first imaging all-sky survey in the hard X-ray (2-10keV) band. The final survey will be over 30 times deeper than previous hard X-ray surveys, yielding a large and very pure sample of AGN. Combining eROSITA data with observations from other multi-wavelength missions, a detailed picture of the AGN population can be constructed, including black hole mass, luminosity, and spectral shape. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR+XMM-Newton follow up observations of a group of hard X-ray selected AGN observed with eROSITA. This pilot study will demonstrate the power of joint NuSTAR and XMM observations to break degeneracies present in different spectral models, motivating future joint NuSTAR, XMM and eROSITA investigations.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       PHYSICAL CONSTRAINTS FROM AGN X-RAY SPECTRA: A PILOT NUSTAR-XMM STUDY OF EROSITA-SELECTED SOURCES                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀M-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  20220215SgrAst