lti-wavelength missions, a detailed picture of the AGN population can be constructed, including black hole mass, luminosity, and spectral shape. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR+XMM-Newton follow up observations of a group of hard X-ray selected AGN observed with eROSITA. This pilot study will demonstrate the power of joint NuSTAR and XMM observations to break degeneracies present in different spectral models, motivating future joint NuSTAR, XMM and eROSITA investigations.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       PHYSICAL CONSTRAINTS FROM AGN X-RAY SPECTRA: A PILOT NUSTAR-XMM STUDY OF EROSITA-SELECTED SOURCES                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215EFEDS_SRCID_9                                     @aOVì@n.±ƒ2 @l1ú‡uˆ˝@@–çπ@u€IQÇ©ì@Ì%±xVb‹@Ì%€xVb‹60762003002@ÌnØ»K^@ÌøxF‹@Ôrpç∏ª@Ôspç∏ªSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì%Íç^oÅ␀␀ÍûHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀qThe eROSITA instrument on-board Spectrum-Roentgen-Gamma (SRG) has performed the first imaging all-sky survey in the hard X-ray (2-10keV) band. The final survey will be over 30 times deeper than previous hard X-ray surveys, yielding a large and very pure sample of AGN. Combining eROSITA data with observations from other multi-wavelength missions, a detailed picture of the AGN population can be constructed, including black hole mass, luminosity, and spectral shape. We propose simultaneous NuSTAR+XMM-Newton follow up observations of a group of hard X-ray selected AGN observed with eROSITA. This pilot study will demonstrate the power of joint NuSTAR and XMM observations to break degeneracies present in different spectral models, motivating future joint NuSTAR, XMM and eROSITA investigations.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Waddell                       Sophia                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       PHYSICAL CONSTRAINTS FROM AGN X-RAY SPECTRA: A PILOT NUSTAR-XMM STUDY OF EROSITA-SELECTED SOURCES                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀M-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  202202152MASXJ07011026m3234534                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì%€xVb‹@Ì%‹èFj60161282001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì%ÍW¥%Ì␀␀È1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison