¶L0@vóçOﬂ;@vóçOﬂ;SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì&Pg(ï␀␀Í©Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˚NGC 7793 P13 is an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion in ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), as it is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known and the only one with a fully determined ephemeris. In 2020 P13 entered an off-state, from which it is currently re-emerging. This gives us the unique opportunity to study the accretion flow as the accretion geometry is changing, and will allow us to infer more physical parameter of the system, like the magnetic field. We therefore propose to observe P13 twice with NuSTAR during cycle-7, continuing the successful monitoring observations in the past.                                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       CONTINUED SPIN-TRACKING OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215NGC_7793_P13                                      @vwxl"h
¿@I0æÌ)@›≤-V¿S:“àŒp;@Z`˘	kπå@Ì%Ï±9Ò@Ì&[‰õø30702014004@Ôûtj~˘€@ÔMAâ7L@Ò® ¿É@Òx ¿ÉSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì&+P6ù␀␀Í©Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˚NGC 7793 P13 is an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion in ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs), as it is an almost persistent source, with a relatively high flux and strong pulsations. Additionally, it is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known and the only one with a fully determined ephemeris. In 2020 P13 entered an off-state, from which it is currently re-emerging. This gives us the unique opportunity to study the accretion flow as the accretion geometry is changing, and will allow us to infer more physical parameter of the system, like the magnetic field. We therefore propose to observe P13 twice with NuSTAR during cycle-7, continuing the successful monitoring observations in the past.                                                                          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       CONTINUED SPIN-TRACKING OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀(2/2) / idiagnostics / CME Kp=5                                                                                         archived  20220215SMC_X1                                            @33a@N•¿RXÅo␀h‹@r»(åÁ∞¿E¡†'RTa@SÍ°a‰˜f@Ì&[‰õø@Ì&ÍN30702018009@~RÖoiD@~=rGE8Ô@Ä¸ˇÀí:*@Ä¸ˇÀí:*SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì&+‡ÖÄ␀␀Í∑Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀BThe accreting pulsar SMC X-1 exhibits a superorbital cycle likely caused by a warped precessing accretion disk, which drives behaviors like pulsation transience and varying spectral obscuration levels. The superorbital cycle is typically ~55 days but has excursions, driven by instability in the disk geometry, where the period increases to 44 days. To date, there exists no broad-band spectral and timing coverage of superorbital excursion. This proposal asks for simultaneous NuSTAR and XMM observations to sample the upcoming 2022 excursion. The next excursion is not expected until 2030, meaning that we have a unique opportunity to use tomography to probe the fluctuating disk geometry and investigate the effect of excursion on obscuration levels, pulse shape, and pulsation transience.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       INVESTIGATING PULSATION TRANSIENCE IN SMC X-1 DURING SUPERORBITAL PERIOD EXCURSION                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215SMC_X1                                            @31o␀h€å¿RXÎÌ˙C˛@r»9¿ÎÌ˙¿E¿‘˝Û∂F@S˚üUõ=@Ì&ÍN@Ì&/Õ´∏130702018010@‡\D$›/@‡GC‡ﬁ“â@‚}iŒŸá@‚©ŒŸáSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì&B|∆ª␀␀Í∑Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀BThe accreting pulsar SMC X-1 exhibits a superorbital cycle likely caused by a warped precessing accretion disk, which drives behaviors like pulsation transience and varying spectral obscuration levels. The superorbital cycle is typically ~55 days but has excursions, driven by instability in the disk geometry, where the period increases to 44 days. To date, there exists no broad-band spectral and timing coverage of superorbital excursion. This proposal asks for simultaneous NuSTAR and XMM observations to sample the upcoming 2022 excursion. The next excursion is not expected until 2030, meaning that we have a unique opportunity to use tomography to probe the fluctuating disk geometry and investigate the effect of excursion on obscuration levels, pulse shape, and pulsation transience.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Brumback                      Mckinley                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       INVESTIGATING PULSATION TRANSIENCE IN SMC X-1 DURING SUPERORBITAL PERIOD EXCURSION                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                           XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(5/6) / idiagnostics / CME Kp=5                                                                                         archived  20220215ZTF_J185139p171430                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì&/Õ´∏1@Ì&0±9Ò30801008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì&AˆÕé˚␀␀Í©Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÊWe propose to observe a white dwarf (WD) pulsar candidate ZTF J185139.81+171430.3 with NICER and NuSTAR. The first WD pulsar system, AR Sco, is one of the rare astrophysical manifestations on how a magnetically interacting binary system can accelerate particles in the magnetosphere and generate broad-band non-thermal synchrotron radiation from radio to X-ray band. ZTF observations revealed a 12.37-minute highly-modulated, coherent periodicity from our target. This is a unique feature previously detected from AR Sco. Alternatively, the source could be an AM CVn star or an accreting intermediate polar with unusually high optical modulation. The proposed NICER + NuSTAR observations will explore the nature of this peculiar CV by investigating its X-ray spectral and timing properties.          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mori                          Kaya                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R04       CHARACTERIZING X-RAY EMISSION OF A WHITE DWARF PULSAR CANDIDATE DISCOVERED IN THE ZTF OPTICAL SURVEY                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ZTF_J185139p171430                                @qØ*0U2a@1L(ı¬è\@H*Ç@∑Ä4@®r∞ ƒú@`¨Ñµ‹∆?@Ì&0±9Ò@Ì&@î»)¯30801008002@÷∫P<ûÏ¿@÷}É◊
=q@ÿL|£n/@ÿL¸£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì&bYˆÊ␀␀Í©Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÊWe propose to observe a white dwarf (WD) pulsar candidate ZTF J185139.81+171430.3 with NICER and NuSTAR. The first WD pulsar system, AR Sco, is one of the rare astrophysical manifestations on how a magnetically interacting binary system can accelerate particles in the magnetosphere and generate broad-band non-thermal synchrotron radiation from radio to X-ray band. ZTF observations revealed a 12.37-minute highly-modulated, coherent periodicity from our target. This is a unique feature previously detected from AR Sco. Alternatively, the source could be an AM CVn star or an accreting intermediate polar with unusually high optical modulation. The proposed NICER + NuSTAR observations will explore the nature of this peculiar CV by investigating its X-ray spectral and timing properties.          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Mori                          Kaya                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R04       CHARACTERIZING X-RAY EMISSION OF A WHITE DWARF PULSAR CANDIDATE DISCOVERED IN THE ZTF OPTICAL SURVEY                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215Mrk0198                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì&@î»)¯@Ì&A[‰õø60061223001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì&b6µI2␀␀È<Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks p