0220215TC0052                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì(ÄxVb‹@Ì(Å#Ü80702614002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì(≤bjÛ|␀␀Í3Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀5Tidal disruption events in AGN can drive bright, slowly evolving optical flares. The few known TDE candidates in AGN lack extensive multi-wavelength follow up. In particular, both hard and soft X-ray observations are key to understanding how a TDE impacts the AGN disk and its X-ray corona. For example, a TDE is predicted to drive super-Eddington inflows which can destroy the AGN corona and result in a dramatic reduction in soft emission. Also, the collision of diffuse stellar debris with the AGN disk can produce hard X-ray flares. We propose a pilot program of joint NuSTAR+Swift observations of the most promising optically selected TDE-AGN candidate found in our vigorous program to discover such events in optical time-domain surveys, to probe physical conditions in their innermost regions.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Somalwar                      Jean                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       TIDAL DISRUPTION EVENTS IN ACTIVE GALACTIC NUCLEI                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TC0052                                            @s;∞â†'R@`™d¬¯@HMú‡u˜¿3£g†˘	l@`±‰%ÆÊ2@Ì(Å#Ü@Ì(ëÕ´∏180702614003@÷Õ•Ë>B[@÷ç¯ìtº@ÿ}Ï”ZÖà@ÿ,”ZÖàSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì(≤K⁄˜␀␀Í3Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀5Tidal disruption events in AGN can drive bright, slowly evolving optical flares. The few known TDE candidates in AGN lack extensive multi-wavelength follow up. In particular, both hard and soft X-ray observations are key to understanding how a TDE impacts the AGN disk and its X-ray corona. For example, a TDE is predicted to drive super-Eddington inflows which can destroy the AGN corona and result in a dramatic reduction in soft emission. Also, the collision of diffuse stellar debris with the AGN disk can produce hard X-ray flares. We propose a pilot program of joint NuSTAR+Swift observations of the most promising optically selected TDE-AGN candidate found in our vigorous program to discover such events in optical time-domain surveys, to probe physical conditions in their innermost regions.Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Somalwar                      Jean                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       TIDAL DISRUPTION EVENTS IN ACTIVE GALACTIC NUCLEI                                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20220215BZQ_J1951p0134                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì(ëÕ´∏1@Ì(í#Ü60863003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì(≤*˜␀␀ÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ We request to observe 11 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 400 ks. All found beyond redshift 4, these sources are prototypical MeV blazars, and some of the most powerful of their class. However, they crucially lack hard-Xray measurements. NuSTAR will allow us to accurately detect the rising part of the inverse Compton spectrum up to 50 keV, expected to be very hard. This will be fundamental to determine the position of the high-energy peak, hence confirming their MeV blazar nature. Further, NuSTAR will enable us to determine the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNCOVERING THE MOST POWERFUL JETS AT THE DAWN OF COSMIC TIME                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZQ_J1951p0134                                    @rûè\(ı√?˘Ò©˚Álã@Döu%F¿(¬&ÄùIR@`åƒõ•„T@Ì(í#Ü@Ì(†Õ´∏160863003002@‘¶ˇ.HÈ@‘lè›óˆ+@’ˆ˘†'RT@’ˆ˘†'RTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì(≈˝°/h␀␀ÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ We request to observe 11 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 400 ks. All found beyond redshift 4, these sources are prototypical MeV blazars, and some of the most powerful of their class. However, they crucially lack hard-Xray measurements. NuSTAR will allow us to accurately detect the rising part of the inverse Compton spectrum up to 50 keV, expected to be very hard. This will be fundamental to determine the position of the high-energy peak, hence confirming their MeV blazar nature. Further, NuSTAR will enable us to determine the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNCOVERING THE MOST POWERFUL JETS AT THE DAWN OF COSMIC TIME                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215Cyg_X1_sl_PA124                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì(†Õ´∏1@Ì(°?r‘£10802310001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì(≈€F␀␀ÈJHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Ca