 provide time-resolved hard and soft X-ray spectroscopy on the sub-hour timescales over which the proposed targets vary.The NuSTAR data would play an uniquely important role in the investigation of several important scientific questions, like the processes generating the flux variability, and the evaluation of whether the blazar emission is dominated by one zone or by multiple zones.                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paneque                       David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       UNRAVELLING THE COMPLEX AND EXTREME BEHAVIOURS IN THE CLASSICAL TEV BLAZARS MRK421 AND MRK501                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215HD_93129A                                         @d£n.≤¿M…XbM”@qÛá+Jø‚SéÛMj@uÍÀ)^û@Ì+A#Ü@Ì+BèFj30802012001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì+f-≥cø␀␀Í»Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ‘
ˆColliding-wind binaries (CWBs) are expected to be non-thermal (NT) X-ray sources; however, until now only one has been confirmed as such. The extreme CWB HD 93129A went through periastron passage in 2018. This system is in a highly eccentric and long-period orbit, making this a unique opportunity to monitor its X-ray emission during the most determining orbital phases. The intensity of its wind-collision region was expected to peak during periastron and decrease as the stars separate. A potential detection of NT hard X-rays from HD 93129A was recently reported close to its periastron. An observation in the post-periastron epoch will probe the variability of the source, helping identify potential orbital-related effects, and provide insight of the properties of the shocks of CWBs.          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    del Placio                    Santiago                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C23       Evolution of the X-ray emission from the colliding-wind binary HD 93129A after its periastron passage                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HD_93129A                                         @dÓcä	¿M…«y¶µ@qÛ¬è\(ˆø‚2 WßÜ¬@uÒÍJå@Ì+BèFj@Ì+Y?r‘£30802012002@„LD–©@‚“‰YJÙÒ@‰NÕú‡u˜@‰P-ú‡u˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì+fÉ˚␀␀Í»Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ‘
ˆColliding-wind binaries (CWBs) are expected to be non-thermal (NT) X-ray sources; however, until now only one has been confirmed as such. The extreme CWB HD 93129A went through periastron passage in 2018. This system is in a highly eccentric and long-period orbit, making this a unique opportunity to monitor its X-ray emission during the most determining orbital phases. The intensity of its wind-collision region was expected to peak during periastron and decrease as the stars separate. A potential detection of NT hard X-rays from HD 93129A was recently reported close to its periastron. An observation in the post-periastron epoch will probe the variability of the source, helping identify potential orbital-related effects, and provide insight of the properties of the shocks of CWBs.          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    del Placio                    Santiago                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C23       Evolution of the X-ray emission from the colliding-wind binary HD 93129A after its periastron passage                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202202151A_1744m361_SADA_22159                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì+Y?r‘£@Ì+Z#Ü90801012001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì+eÏ®d ␀␀ÈfHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA setup STELLAR only                                                                                                 archived  201503161A_1744m361                                       @p±©*0U2¿B>BZÓc@vÿD–¿≥33333@j05?|Ìë@Ì+Z#Ü@Ì+x?r‘£90801312001@‡∫Å“Ò©¸@‡’+J@‰‰3333@‰ÊW3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì+ä≥¨ê␀␀ÈfHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀ToO / INERTIAL only / idiagnostics                                                                                      archived  202202151A_1744m361_SADA_22159                            @qQú‡u˜¿B|M:í£@vP¿ÎÌ˙D¿&÷ìtº@gÇ_o“ @Ì+x?r‘£@Ì+x±9Ò90801012002@D	∫^5?}@C„6zêó@E`␀␀␀␀␀@E`␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì+äÜ)∑Ò␀␀ÈfHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA rally INERTIAL only                                                                                                archived  20220215SN2022ffg                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì+x±9Ò@Ì+yÍN80702508003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì+är“␀␀ÈfHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀¬Contrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated XMM-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program has the immediate goal to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. This programs has the potential enlarge the very small sample of two strongly interacting SNe with broad-band X-ray detections.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A BROAD-BAND X-RAY VIEW OF THE MOST EXTREME MASS-LOSS WITH NUSTAR+XMM                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2022ffg                                         @c,‡uˆ˝"@2˙¨ŸË>@kBYJÙÿ@J5VlÙ!@vMjO@Ì+yÍN@Ì+úèFj80702508004@ÁI‡ﬁ”@Á@ßX‚e@È!B•zxl@È"¢•zxlSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì+£÷H,␀␀ÈfHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¬Contrary to expectations from current stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material by massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated XMM-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program has the immediate goal to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. This programs has the potential enlarge the very small sample of two strongly interacting SNe with broad-band X-ray detections.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       A BROAD-BAND X-RAY VIEW OF THE MOST EXTREME MASS-LOSS WITH NUSTAR+XMM                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(2/2) ToO / M-class solar flare                                                                                         archived  20220215NGC_4490                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì+úèFj@Ì+úxVb‹50760003005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì+£•ãÚY␀␀ÍËHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀øUnderstanding the emission from X-ray binaries in high-redshift galaxies is of utmost importance to understanding the heating of the primordial IGM. This program, to observe five starbursting, High Mass X-ray Binary (HMXB)-dominated galaxies, for a total of 1.49 Ms with NuSTAR and 207 ks with XMM-Newton, will provide a representative sample of starburst galaxies for establishing the connection between the 12-25~keV emission arising from neutron star and black hole populations and their host galaxy properties that is highly relevant to high-z galaxies. We will double the number of HMXB-dominated galaxies with measured 0.5-30~keV Spectral Energy Distributions, so we may correctly interpret high-z galaxies' X-ray emission where we only sample the rest-frame hard X-ray emission.               Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Hornschemeier                 Ann                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       THE BIRTH OF BLACK HOLES AND NEUTRON STARS IN STARBURSTS: A LEGACY FOR NUSTAR BUILDING ON A HST TREASURY PROGRAM        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4490                                          @grwökP±@D‘"h	‘ï@aX}øHÄ@R™GE8Ô5@càeîØO@Ì+úxVb‹@Ì+œxVb‹50760003006@Ò$x-w1ê@˜Rπå~(@ÚT0è≈Å@ÚU‡è≈ÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì+‚†#␀␀ÍËHea_04Aug2021_V