␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì.èFj@Ì.Õ´∏160801009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì.#D’Ê¯␀␀Í3Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀WWe propose NuSTAR observations (with 200 ks total exposure) of two high-redshift radio-loud quasars that launch among the most powerful relativistic jets with FR II type morphologies and have radio-loudness parameters of R=3000-7000. These NuSTAR observations, combined with archival X-ray data below 10 keV, can detect the expected high-energy cutoff of the hard X-ray power law up to 200-300 keV. Measuring the cutoff of the X-ray spectra of RLQs will not only shed light on the nature of their hard X-ray emission but also reveal how the corona is linked to the relativistic jets.                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhu                           Shifu                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE CORONA-JET CONNECTION OF RLQS IN LIGHT OF NUSTAR                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C_270d1                                          @g"≈m\˙≠@@ŸŒ_p@dﬂb∂Æ}@T¥¢3ú@v␀¬¯7¥¢@Ì.Õ´∏1@Ì.Y±9Ò60801009002@¯úN-w1ê@¯Zá6‚Î@˙XF‹]d@˙YF‹]dSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì.jŸP»@␀␀Í3Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀WWe propose NuSTAR observations (with 200 ks total exposure) of two high-redshift radio-loud quasars that launch among the most powerful relativistic jets with FR II type morphologies and have radio-loudness parameters of R=3000-7000. These NuSTAR observations, combined with archival X-ray data below 10 keV, can detect the expected high-energy cutoff of the hard X-ray power law up to 200-300 keV. Measuring the cutoff of the X-ray spectra of RLQs will not only shed light on the nature of their hard X-ray emission but also reveal how the corona is linked to the relativistic jets.                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhu                           Shifu                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE CORONA-JET CONNECTION OF RLQS IN LIGHT OF NUSTAR                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=5                                                                                                                archived  20220215IGR_J20596p4303                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì.Y±9Ò@Ì.ZxVb‹30801004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì.jÜ£‹␀␀ÍÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀`}Accreting white dwarfs (WDs) can be strong emitters of X-rays. In intermediate polar (IP) type cataclysmic variables, the accreting material is funneled onto the WD's magnetic poles. The X-rays are produced by shock-heating, and the temperature depends on the WD mass. Thus, X-ray spectra provide a WD mass measurement. An important question is whether the WD mass distribution extends up to the Chandrasekhar limit. The best place to look for high-mass WDs is among IPs with the highest temperatures, making IPs found by INTEGRAL strong candidates for hosting the most massive WDs. Here, we propose XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of two IGR sources that are likely IPs. The goals include detecting the WD spin period to confirm the IP natures of the sources and to measure the WD masses.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X21       White Dwarf Masses and Spin Periods for Hard X-ray-Selected Galactic Sources                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J20596p4303                                   @s¨Œp:˚@EáÊö‘,@U@N§®¡ø˝#¢úwök@XiDg8@Ì.ZxVb‹@Ì.u[‰õø30801004002@‚ÂênóçP@‚¥±Í≥g°@‰-ì3333@‰.3333SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì.êa"É␀␀ÍÔHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀`}Accreting white dwarfs (WDs) can be strong emitters of X-rays. In intermediate polar (IP) type cataclysmic variables, the accreting material is funneled onto the WD's magnetic poles. The X-rays are produced by shock-heating, and the temperature depends on the WD mass. Thus, X-ray spectra provide a WD mass measurement. An important question is whether the WD mass distribution extends up to the Chandrasekhar limit. The best place to look for high-mass WDs is among IPs with the highest temperatures, making IPs found by INTEGRAL strong candidates for hosting the most massive WDs. Here, we propose XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of two IGR sources that are likely IPs. The goals include detecting the WD spin period to confirm the IP natures of the sources and to measure the WD masses.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Tomsick                       John                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X21       White Dwarf Masses and Spin Periods for Hard X-ray-Selected Galactic Sources                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215NGC5273                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì.u[‰õø@Ì.v#Ü90801618001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì.ê6ùj␀␀È{Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison   