␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì0!⁄§ô9␀␀ÍÔHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    b]Strong magnetism and fast rotation among early type stars produce copious emission in radio and X-rays bands coming from different regions of the spinning magnetosphere. We propose a joint and simultaneous observation of HR 5907 with Chandra and NuSTAR in order to acquire its spectrum from soft (~0.5 keV) to hard (~80 keV) X-rays. HR 5907 is a B2.5V star, it is an extreme rotator with a strong magnetic field. This unique target allows us to test the Oblique Rotator Model in such a regime of fast rotation and magnetism.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pillitteri                    Ignazio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C23       THE MAGNETOSPHERE OF THE FAST SPINNING B STAR HR5907                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HR_5907                                           @mŒ1¯†ê.¿8h	‘ï@uªLò_@6Ã∆?@u}◊€Ùá˝@Ì0ÍN@Ì07ÍN30802001004@‚«{˝ä⁄∫@‚ó5T…Ö@‰¿»¥9X@‰@»¥9XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì0B7èΩ@␀␀ÍÔHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   b]Strong magnetism and fast rotation among early type stars produce copious emission in radio and X-rays bands coming from different regions of the spinning magnetosphere. We propose a joint and simultaneous observation of HR 5907 with Chandra and NuSTAR in order to acquire its spectrum from soft (~0.5 keV) to hard (~80 keV) X-rays. HR 5907 is a B2.5V star, it is an extreme rotator with a strong magnetic field. This unique target allows us to test the Oblique Rotator Model in such a regime of fast rotation and magnetism.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pillitteri                    Ignazio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C23       THE MAGNETOSPHERE OF THE FAST SPINNING B STAR HR5907                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215IGR_J12319m0749                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì07ÍN@Ì08xVb‹60862001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì0B4␀␀ÍAHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÌWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of 12 high-redshift (z > 3) blazars which exhibit brightest and flattest X-ray spectra and host massive (>1e9 Msun) black holes. Joint Xmm and NuSTAR observations will lead to the most accurate estimation of the beaming factor which will allow us to put stringent constraint on the space density of blazar parent population, and in turn, the evolution of massive black holes in jetted AGNs within the first two billion years. Using XMM+NuSTAR data, we will also probe the role played by the intergalactic medium in the X-ray absorption towards high-redshift blazars.                                                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE QUEST FOR ELUSIVE COSMIC MONSTERS                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGR_J12319m0749                                   @g~HËßÁ¿@4m≈÷9@r_~˘€"—@Kh¥9Xb@vfsÅ◊€ı@Ì08xVb‹@Ì0G#Ü60862001002@‘€}ûÉ‰&@‘•M„º”@÷@yÛ∂E¢@÷@yÛ∂E¢SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì0bÖgâ¨␀␀ÍAHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÌWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of 12 high-redshift (z > 3) blazars which exhibit brightest and flattest X-ray spectra and host massive (>1e9 Msun) black holes. Joint Xmm and NuSTAR observations will lead to the most accurate estimation of the beaming factor which will allow us to put stringent constraint on the space density of blazar parent population, and in turn, the evolution of massive black holes in jetted AGNs within the first two billion years. Using XMM+NuSTAR data, we will also probe the role played by the intergalactic medium in the X-ray absorption towards high-redshift blazars.                                                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE QUEST FOR ELUSIVE COSMIC MONSTERS                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215NGC_4552                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì0G#Ü@Ì0GxVb‹60801045001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì0bdPg)␀␀ÍAHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ %The Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4552                                          @gõàeîØO@)ÿD–©@qÈ˚ÁlãD@RæÕûÉ‰&@uˇIQÇ©ì@Ì0GxVb‹@Ì0kî»)¯60801045002@Ëª•J#9¡@ËyÏ~($@Íhèqué@ÍhèquéSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì0â˜.¶␀␀ÍAHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ %The Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar CME                                                                                                                         ␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20220215NGC_4070                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì0kî»)¯@Ì0kÍN60864002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì0âƒ+˛|␀␀ÍAHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4070                                          @fü“Ú‰è@4c‡ﬁ“â@n${≤˛≈m@SD˜e˝ä€@u®ƒ2 W®@Ì0kÍN@Ì0z±9Ò60864002002@‘ô•ú‡@”‘¿ÎÌ˙D@’x∏QÎÖ@’x“∏QÎÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì0âÆEs␀␀ÍAHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215SgrAstar                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì0z±9Ò@Ì0{xVb‹30702013004␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì0âåÉ˚s␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÚWe propose a long (100 ks) continuous NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2021 to cover 3 con nights of VLTI-Gravity+Chandra observations of Sgr A*. The required exposure time of Gravity and Chandra has been already secured. The Gravity team has recently measured the astrometric motion of the near infrared (NIR) source during three bright NIR flux excursions of Sgr A*. Simultaneous NuSTAR+Gravity observations will allow us to understand the evolution of the radiative mechanism and to test whether, during bright X-ray flares, the NIR source will display similar circular orbital motions or whether it will be characterised by a dramatically different pattern, such as by linear motions, reminiscent of jet like ejections. This will provide us with a groundbreaking test of accretion physics.              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       THE MOTION OF SGR A*'S NIR SOURCE DURING X-RAY FLARES                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SgrAstar                                          @p¶üæv…¿=„º”[@v{œ™ÕûÑ?Ã~($x@tm\˙¨ŸË@Ì0{xVb‹@Ì0ä±9Ò30702013005@”cÔª/ÏW@”.˛∞ ƒú@’&˚8}ø@’)ª8}øSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì0¢Ùnæc␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÚWe propose a long (100 ks) continuous NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2021 to cover 3 con nights of VLTI-Gravity+Chandra observations of Sgr A*. The required exposure time of Gravity and Chandra has been already secured. The Gravity team has recently measured the astrometric motion of the near infrared (NIR) source during three bright NIR flux excursions of Sgr A*. Simultaneous NuSTAR+Gravity observations will allow us to understand the evolution of the radiative mechanism and to test whether, during bright X-ray flares, the NIR source will display similar circular orbital motions or whether it will be characterised by a dramatically different pattern, such as by linear motions, reminiscent of jet like ejections. This will provide us with a groundbreaking test of accretion physics.              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       THE MOTION OF SGR A*'S NIR SOURCE DURING X-RAY FLARES                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/5) idiagnostics                                                                                                      archived  20220215EXO_0748m676                                      @]F@∑Ä4n¿PÌûÉ‰%Ø@q~u%F¿3ÀC,•z@P£ó$tSè@Ì0ä±9Ò@Ì0ãxVb‹30801026001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì0¢“ˆÑæ␀␀ÍHHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ We request 40 ks observations for two Accretion Disk Corona (ADC) candidates EXO 0748 676 and 4U 1624-490 with NuSTAR in order to map the X-ray emission region of these two sources and model eclipse depth with energy to understand the ADC structure in them. We will also search for cyclotron line features in both these sources similar to what is claimed in the prototypical ADC source 4U 1822-371. As a part of timing analysis, we will also search for pulsations and quasi-periodic oscillations in the hard X-ray band. Such studies will increase our understanding of physics in the formation/stability 