uces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_424                                           @1Íêñªô¿C÷8ÜYK@q¡çπ¿Sê4m≈÷9@b*tSéÛM@Ì0©#Ü@Ì0∑±9Ò60802013004@—òåÅo␀i@—hWn.±ƒ@“Ì~qﬁi≠@“>qﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì0– U∂l␀␀ÍƒHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀éFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN 