to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC_9799                                          @l§9XbN@‹]càf@"n_o“ @I5˙C˛\í@v5Èx‘˝Ù@Ì1g?r‘£@Ì1Ö?r‘£60801046002@„È€Õ5®X@„∏¥9X@ÂPY>´6z@ÂQ˘>´6zSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì1¢R≠≥d␀␀ÍOHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ %The Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀PID 08229                                                                                                               archived  20220215DQ_Tau                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì1Ö?r‘£@Ì1ÜxVb‹30801011001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì1¢&5⁄’␀␀ÍOHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀cX-ray flares are expected to induce time-variable ion-molecular chemistry in young protoplanetary disks. DQ Tau is a young, high eccentricity stellar binary that exhibits X-ray/optical/mm-band flux increases near periastron and offers a unique laboratory for gas-phase ion disk chemistry. We won ALMA program to observe the reaction of molecular line emission to an increase in ionizing radiation of DQ Tau. We propose joint NuSTAR/Swift observations near periastron of DQ Tau to measure non-thermal hard- and thermal soft-band X-ray fluxes as inputs to a state-of-the-art time-resolved physical-chemical disk model. The model and ALMA data will provide unique estimates of disk density distribution that have far-reaching implications for understanding evolution of disks and formation of planets.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Getman                        Konstantin                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       X-RAY DRIVEN CHEMISTRY IN THE PROTOPLANETARY DISK OF DQ TAU                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316DQ_Tau                                            @Q≠´üUõ@1ã¨q≥@f¬bM“Ò™¿2¸PH@a„sÍ≥g°@Ì1ÜxVb‹@Ì1˙#Ü30801011002AË/OÑA’∫S√aAX≠1&ÈyAYù1&ÈySCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì28„é␀␀ÍOHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀cX-ray flares are expected to induce time-variable ion-molecular chemistry in young protoplanetary disks. DQ Tau is a young, high eccentricity stellar binary that exhibits X-ray/optical/mm-band flux increases near periastron and offers a unique laboratory for gas-phase ion disk chemistry. We won ALMA program to observe the reaction of molecular line emission to an increase in ionizing radiation of DQ Tau. We propose joint NuSTAR/Swift observations near periastron of DQ Tau to measure non-thermal hard- and thermal soft-band X-ray fluxes as inputs to a state-of-the-art time-resolved physical-chemical disk model. The model and ALMA data will provide unique estimates of disk density distribution that have far-reaching implications for understanding evolution of disks and formation of planets.  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Getman                        Konstantin                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       X-RAY DRIVEN CHEMISTRY IN THE PROTOPLANETARY DISK OF DQ TAU                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀uplink card reset 2022:210:10:46:33                                                                                     archived  20220215IGR_J17091m3624                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì1˙#Ü@Ì1˚[‰õø80802321004␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì2ôôôö␀␀ÍOHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛Black hole astrophysics can be regarded as a fundamental tool in studying the accretion and ejection physics in the strongest gravity regime in the Universe. Reflection spectroscopy studies the time-averaged flux-energy spectrum, providing constraints on properties in the accretion disk and the corona, but degeneracies take place in problems such as the truncation level of the disk. Reverberation mapping is a timing technique revealing the disk-corona geometry in the innermost regions, which could help break degeneracies. With recent cutting-edge developments in physical spectral-timing models, the main goal in this proposal is to find clues on the state transition mechanism, and the coupling between the disk, corona and jet in black hole binaries.                                          Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Wang                          Jingyi                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀R04       RELATIVISTIC REFLECTION AND REVERBERATION MAPPING IN A BLACK HOLE BINARY                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                         