n. We propose one more simultaneous XMM-Newton/NuSTAR campaign of 1ES 1927+654, totaling 70 ks over two 35 ks observations, to probe the late-stage evolution of this paradigm-defying AGN. The hard X-ray spectrum will allow us to track the heating of a newly-formed corona for the first time and determine whether the iron line has appeared. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Masterson                     Megan                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X21       1ES 1927+654: Constraining the Late Stages of an Extreme Nuclear Transient                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161ES_1927p654                                      @r:«‚Ç@∏@Pf$›/†@X<Aâ7K«@5ökP∞Ú@FÅA [¿@Ì2î»)¯@Ì22Õ´∏160802003004@›È•VlÙ@›ùà™d¬¯@‡ˆõ•„T@‡õ•„TSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì2W˙˘çw␀␀ÎHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀`ì1ES 1927+654, the first changing-look AGN caught in the act of changing phases, has undergone extreme spectral changes over the last 3 years, including the destruction and recreation of the X-ray corona, possibly due to a tidal disruption event in a pre-existing AGN accretion disk. The latest XMM-Newton observation is similar to the pre-outburst observation from May 2011, but reveals a hint of an iron line, which was notably missing in the 2011 observation. We propose one more simultaneous XMM-Newton/NuSTAR campaign of 1ES 1927+654, totaling 70 ks over two 35 ks observations, to probe the late-stage evolution of this paradigm-defying AGN. The hard X-ray spectrum will allow us to track the heating of a newly-formed corona for the first time and determine whether the iron line has appeared. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Masterson                     Megan                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀X21       1ES 1927+654: Constraining the Late Stages of an Extreme Nuclear Transient                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215NGC_7793_P13                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì22Õ´∏1@Ì23Õ´∏130802020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì2W“}'“␀␀Î`Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ØNGC 7793 P13 is an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion in ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs) as it is relatively nearby and is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known and the only one with a fully determined orbital ephemeris. In 2020 P13 entered an off-state, from which it is currently re-emerging. This gives us the unique opportunity to study the accretion flow as the accretion geometry is changing, and will allow us to infer more physical parameter of the system, like the magnetic field. We therefore propose to observe P13 twice with NuSTAR during cycle-8, continuing the successful monitoring observations in the past.                                                                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONTINUED SPIN-TRACKING OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_7793_P13                                      @vwˆ˝!ˇ.¿@K'ª/Ï@tºj~˘€¿S<ç∏∫«@da∂E° ¡@Ì23Õ´∏1@Ì2bxVb‹30802020002@ÓÈÌÚ◊s@Óí#˚Álã@ÇÚ`™d√@Ç¬`™d√SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì2Éﬁio␀␀Î`Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ØNGC 7793 P13 is an ideal target to study super-Eddington accretion in ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs) as it is relatively nearby and is one of only two ULXPs for which the mass donor is known and the only one with a fully determined orbital ephemeris. In 2020 P13 entered an off-state, from which it is currently re-emerging. This gives us the unique opportunity to study the accretion flow as the accretion geometry is changing, and will allow us to infer more physical parameter of the system, like the magnetic field. We therefore propose to observe P13 twice with NuSTAR during cycle-8, continuing the successful monitoring observations in the past.                                                                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONTINUED SPIN-TRACKING OF THE NEUTRON STAR ULX NGC7793 P13 WITH NUSTAR                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215Mrk352                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì2bxVb‹@Ì2c#Ü60160031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì2É£€¯␀␀ÈûHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk352                                            @-ˇ!-w1ê@?‡:˚~ë␀@_0g8}ø¿?á+J@^Úz·GÆ@Ì2c#Ü@Ì2qÕ´∏160160031002@“∆5‰˜e˛@“ò§/É{J@