nd PG 1244+026, the two remaining members of the sample of Seyfert galaxies in which iron K reverberation time delays have been significantly detected that have not yet been observed by NuSTAR. The primary objective is to measure the spectrum of the X-rays reflected from the accretion disk that are expected to give rise to these time delays. These measurements will be important to validate the X-ray reverberation model, to determine the consistency between the X-ray spectrum and the observed time lags, and to enable the tightest constrains on the structure of the corona and the spin of the black holes.                                                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Wilkins                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       COMPLETING HARD X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF THE IRON K REVERBERATION SAMPLE OF SEYFERT GALAXIES                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1244p026                                       @gÚºj~˘€@…QÇ©ì@r≥h	‘ï@PR‚ÎC-@u¸ó$tSè@Ì3 Õ´∏1@Ì3pèFj60801033006@¯}é“Ò©¸@¯Bô¿Éo@˙?ÇMj@˙A¬MjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì3É9æG␀␀Í]Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ We request 200ks NuSTAR observations of the AGN MS 22549-3712 and PG 1244+026, the two remaining members of the sample of Seyfert galaxies in which iron K reverberation time delays have been significantly detected that have not yet been observed by NuSTAR. The primary objective is to measure the spectrum of the X-rays reflected from the accretion disk that are expected to give rise to these time delays. These measurements will be important to validate the X-ray reverberation model, to determine the consistency between the X-ray spectrum and the observed time lags, and to enable the tightest constrains on the structure of the corona and the spin of the black holes.                                                                                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Wilkins                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       COMPLETING HARD X-RAY OBSERVATIONS OF THE IRON K REVERBERATION SAMPLE OF SEYFERT GALAXIES                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215PMN_J1458p0855                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì3pèFj@Ì3pxVb‹60862002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì3ÇÂ[f«␀␀Í]Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÌWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of 12 high-redshift (z > 3) blazars which exhibit brightest and flattest X-ray spectra and host massive (>1e9 Msun) black holes. Joint Xmm and NuSTAR observations will lead to the most accurate estimation of the beaming factor which will allow us to put stringent constraint on the space density of blazar parent population, and in turn, the evolution of massive black holes in jetted AGNs within the first two billion years. Using XMM+NuSTAR data, we will also probe the role played by the intergalactic medium in the X-ray absorption towards high-redshift blazars.                                                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE QUEST FOR ELUSIVE COSMIC MONSTERS                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PMN_J1458p0855                                    @l¸PH@!Õ´üUõ=@Ö∏QÎÖ@K•qué"@u¡($x@Ì3pxVb‹@Ì3Ä±9Ò60862002002@“ï®*ô0æ@“d˜öu@‘/öu@‘ØöuSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì3£ù≠RM␀␀Í]Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÌWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of 12 high-redshift (z > 3) blazars which exhibit brightest and flattest X-ray spectra and host massive (>1e9 Msun) black holes. Joint Xmm and NuSTAR observations will lead to the most accurate estimation of the beaming factor which will allow us to put stringent constraint on the space density of blazar parent population, and in turn, the evolution of massive black holes in jetted AGNs within the first two billion years. Using XMM+NuSTAR data, we will also probe the role played by the intergalactic medium in the X-ray absorption towards high-redshift blazars.                                                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE QUEST FOR ELUSIVE COSMIC MONSTERS                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215BZQ_J1155m3107                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì3Ä±9Ò@Ì3Å[‰õø60863009001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì3£{ã0*␀␀Í]Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ We request to observe 11 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 400 ks. All found beyond redshift 4, these sources are prototypical MeV blazars, and some of the most powerful of their class. However, they crucially lack hard-Xray measurements. NuSTAR will allow us to accurately detect the rising part of the inverse Compton spectrum up to 50 keV, expected to be very hard. This will be fundamental to determine the position of the high-energy peak, hence confirming their MeV blazar nature. Further, NuSTAR will enable us to determine the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNCOVERING THE MOST POWERFUL JETS AT THE DAWN OF COSMIC TIME                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZQ_J1155m3107                                    @fVè\(ı√¿?≈Åo␀@r	¬¯7¥¢@>BZÓcä@5‚‰éäqﬁ@Ì3Å[‰õø@Ì3ßxVb‹60863009002@Ëº”ZÜ@ÁŸ±πå~(@È…r<ûÏ¿@ÈÃ2<ûÏ¿SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì3√uax:␀␀Í]Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ We request to observe 11 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 400 ks. All found beyond redshift 4, these sources are prototypical MeV blazars, and some of the most powerful of their class. However, they crucially lack hard-Xray measurements. NuSTAR will allow us to accurately detect the rising part of the inverse Compton spectrum up to 50 keV, expected to be very hard. This will be fundamental to determine the position of the high-energy peak, hence confirming their MeV blazar nature. Further, NuSTAR will enable us to determine the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNCOVERING THE MOST POWERFUL JETS AT THE DAWN OF COSMIC TIME                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215ESO_317m41                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì3ßxVb‹@Ì3ßÕ´∏160802014003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì3√AÂs≠␀␀ÍµHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀éFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_317m41                                        @cπ	kπå~¿E~($x@qJïÅ$›@+CàeîØO@Hº@Ì3ßÕ´∏1@Ì3∫èFj60802014004@◊’ÍM“Ò™@◊ûéh	‘ï@ŸöM“Ò™@ŸÅ⁄M“Ò™SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì3√+µ™J␀␀ÍµHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀éFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215MAXI_J0655_SADA_22226                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì3∫èFj@Ì3∫Õ´∏190801021001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì3√ïŒ≤␀␀È•Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA setup STELLAR only                                                                                                 archived  20150316MAXI_J0655m013                                    @YıS&¡æøˆÅo␀h€å@jœ2a|⁄øÑFsÅ◊€ı@`iN;Õ5®@Ì3∫Õ´∏1@Ì3 Õ´∏190801321001@∆#RÎC-@≈Ô7»K]Ã@«ª/È˘r@«ª/È˘rSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì3„éW˝␀␀È•Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀DDT / idiagnostics                                                                                                      archived  20220215MRK18_SADA_22227                                  @`‚Ê1¯†ê@P≈Åo␀@bî?º@B≠!ˇ.HÈ@mí:)«z@Ì3 Õ´∏1@Ì3ÀÕ´∏160760014001@L»ı¬è\)@Lx«‚Ç@∏@NÉdZ¨@NÉdZ¨SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì3„d8!`␀␀ÎHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ZaçWe propose a survey of 7 highly obscured Active Galactic Nuclei (logNh>23) to find or set limits at the 10% level on their extended hard (2-7 keV) continuum and Fe-K emission. The Chandra discovery that 10-30% of the 2-7 keV continuum from high Nh AGN can be extended on scales from ~100 pc up to ~2 kpc affects the structure of the obscuring torus determined from NuSTAR spectra, and demonstrates the AGN impact on the host galaxy, i.e. AGN feedback. By completing a sample of 20 BASS high Nh AGN we can look for predictors of hard extent; their feedback duty cycle may also be estimated on ~1000 year timescales.                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Qua