@Ì9FÕ´∏1@Ì9q[‰õø60801015008@Ì#«y¶µ@Ï◊TÑµ‹∆@Ô"[˙C˛]@Ô$˙C˛]SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì9ÉÉi–7␀␀ÍåHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ëWe propose a joint XMM-NuSTAR observation of GraL J0659+1629, a quad-lensed z=3.083 quasar with an extreme observed rest-frame luminosity (L[2-10] = 2.8E46 erg/s) owing to its significant magnification (mu=37.6). Through these observations we will measure E_c, the cut-off energy of the X-ray power law continuum, and, in turn, constrain the temperature of the corona. The combination of large redshift, which band shifts high energies into NuSTAR's observing window, and elevated flux, which enables NuSTAR to nevertheless observe the distant object, make GraL J0659+1629 an ideal target for this investigation. Furthermore, as a quad lens, the sizes and characteristics of GraL J0659+1629's accreting region can be directly measured through microlensing studies already underway.                   Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Connor                        Thomas                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       BREAKING THE LENS: AGN CUTOFF ENERGY ABOVE REDSHIFT 3                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(4/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215Crab                                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì9q[‰õø@Ì9q±9Ò10802303005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì9ÉLSo‚␀␀È÷Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab                                              @TÍπıY≥–@6ÑM:ì@gnóçOﬂ¿F‹]cà@c2a|⁄@Ì9q±9Ò@Ì9w±9Ò10802303006@≠pµ‹∆?@Ø%„S˜œ@¬™i≠Bƒ@¬´i≠BƒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì9É%Ì	{␀␀È÷Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀Calibration / idiagnostics                                                                                              archived  20220215BZQ_J0810p3847                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì9w±9Ò@Ì9x#Ü60862007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì9Ékµ™␀␀ÍåHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÌWe propose joint XMM and NuSTAR observations of 12 high-redshift (z > 3) blazars which exhibit brightest and flattest X-ray spectra and host massive (>1e9 Msun) black holes. Joint Xmm and NuSTAR observations will lead to the most accurate estimation of the beaming factor which will allow us to put stringent constraint on the space density of blazar parent population, and in turn, the evolution of massive black holes in jetted AGNs within the first two billion years. Using XMM+NuSTAR data, we will also probe the role played by the intergalactic medium in the X-ray absorption towards high-redshift blazars.            