                                       @pnOv_Ÿ¿0ˇÉ{J#:@ õqué!ñ@"ßRT`™e@tË–|Ñµ›@Ì:≠?r‘£@Ì:≠Õ´∏130801021001@qvA [¿@qπ:í£@xÇfffff@xÇfffffSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì:¬ËK⁄␀␀ÍìHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¿The nature and geometry of the X-ray corona near neutron stars have been open questions for decades. The Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE) will observe three bright neutron star low-mass X-ray binaries in 2022: GS 1826-238, Cygnus X-2 and GX 9+9. IXPE will, for the first time, provide polarimetric information, vital to assess the geometry of the X-ray corona, left unconstrained by spectroscopy alone. The limited energy resolution and bandwidth of IXPE will, however, make it difficult to disentangle the various spectral components, leading to ambiguities in the results. Coordinated NuSTAR observations are needed to provide this key spectral information. We thus propose NuSTAR observations of these sources, coordinated with the IXPE ones, for a total observing time of 170 ks.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ursini                        Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       JOINT X-RAY POLARIMETRY AND SPECTROSCOPY OF WEAKLY MAGNETISED NEUTRON-STAR X-RAY BINARIES                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215GX_9p9                                            @pn;dZ¨¿1␀∑Ä4m∆@ õ=»K^@"£ä	ﬁ@tÂ=»K^@Ì:≠Õ´∏1@Ì:”Õ´∏130801021002@„åÜYJı@„`JÁ’fœ@Í·‰˜e˛@Í A‰˜e˛SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì:„.÷©&␀␀ÍìHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀¿The nature and geometry of the X-ray corona near neutron stars have been open questions for decades. The Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE) will observe three bright neutron star low-mass X-ray binaries in 2022: GS 1826-238, Cygnus X-2 and GX 9+9. IXPE will, for the first time, provide polarimetric information, vital to assess the geometry of the X-ray corona, left unconstrained by spectroscopy alone. The limited energy resolution and bandwidth of IXPE will, however, make it difficult to disentangle the various spectral components, leading to ambiguities in the results. Coordinated NuSTAR observations are needed to provide this key spectral information. We thus propose NuSTAR observations of these sources, coordinated with the IXPE ones, for a total observing time of 170 ks.        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ursini                        Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       JOINT X-RAY POLARIMETRY AND SPECTROSCOPY OF WEAKLY MAGNETISED NEUTRON-STAR X-RAY BINARIES                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀idiagnostics                                                                                                            archived  20220215IGR_J12341m6143                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì:”Õ´∏1@Ì:‘î»)¯90801328001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì:‚˘÷H␀␀È›Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1