 ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCGm5m23m16                                       @b_Y≥–|Ö¿>ÍOv`@phtSéÛM@1;dZ¨@dìì›óˆ+@Ì>¿èFj@Ì>˝[‰õø90801630002@ÙÓÍ4Í@ÙΩw1è≈@ˆáUb∂Æ@ˆàÖb∂ÆSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì?#æìÈ?␀␀Í␀Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20220215ESO_317m41                                        @cº?º¿Eg8}ø@qKûÉ‰%Ø@+XF‹]d@cW∏∫«À@Ì>˝[‰õø@Ì>˝±9Ò60802014005@yò«‚ÇA@xΩπå~($@zê␀␀␀␀␀@zê␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì?#xQÎÖ␀␀ÍµHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀éFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215ESO_317m41                                        @cº«À)_¿EÁlãCñ@qK¡Ω•ù@+]»K]Ã@c]?|Ìëh@Ì>˝±9Ò@Ì?èFj60802014006@÷ªvu%F@÷Ä§%ÆÊ2@ÿ(¯ŸË>B@ÿ(¯ŸË>BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì?0õf«~␀␀ÍµHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀éFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215AKARI_J0941                                       @b)«y¶µ@-BZÓcä@kOJ#9¿Ï@EÙZ¨1@e∞ﬁ“àŒp@Ì?èFj@Ì?±9Ò60801017001@ÄÂ+”√a@ΩCïÅ%@Åà␀␀␀␀␀@Åà␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì?0x	+␀␀ÍµHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀íPowerful outflows are considered as indicators of AGNs quenching their host galaxies. However, recent studies report a puzzling co-existence of extreme outflows and starbursts in eight ULIRGs. More interestingly, NuSTAR follow-ups for two ULIRGs of them show declined AGN central engines, implying a scenario that the AGNs quench themselves by strong winds before severely affecting star formation. In order to understand these fading cases, NuSTAR observations for other ULIRGs in the sample are necessary. Utilizing the sample with the strongest ionized outflows and the highest SFR at z<1, the project provides a unique opportunity to study nuclear fading with extreme galaxy-scale outflows, and to further understand the cumulative feedback effect of AGNs on stellar build-up in their hosts.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Xiaoyang                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       NUSTAR VIEW OF ULIRGS WITH EXTREME OUTFLOWS: DO AGNS QUENCH THEMSELVES BEFORE SUPPRESSING STELLAR BUILD-UP IN HOSTS?    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215AKARI_J0941                                       @b)üUõ=@->Ov_ÿÆ@kOÜ¬&Äù@EÛdZ¨@e±4÷°aÂ@Ì?±9Ò@Ì?J[‰õø60801017002@Ûó∞^û	@Ûfs¸P@ÙË¶ßÊõ@ÙÈvßÊõSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì?jRﬁ>ı␀␀ÍµHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀íPowerful outflows are considered as indicators of AGNs quenching their host galaxies. However, recent studies report a puzzling co-existence of extreme outflows and starbursts in eight ULIRGs. More interestingly, NuSTAR follow-ups for two ULIRGs of them show declined AGN central engines, implying a scenario that the AGNs quench themselves by strong winds before severely affecting star formation. In order to understand these fading cases, NuSTAR observations for other ULIRGs in the sample are necessary. Utilizing the sample with the strongest ionized outflows and the highest SFR at z<1, the project provides a unique opportunity to study nuclear fading with extreme galaxy-scale outflows, and to further understand the cumulative feedback effect of AGNs on stellar build-up in their hosts.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Xiaoyang                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       NUSTAR VIEW OF ULIRGS WITH EXTREME OUTFLOWS: DO AGNS QUENCH THEMSELVES BEFORE SUPPRESSING STELLAR BUILD-UP IN HOSTS?    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215AKARI_J0903                                       @`˙ç∏∫«@ƒ–©*0@l^êˇó$t@=‰Åo␀h‹@eõRT`™e@Ì?J[‰õø@Ì?J±9Ò60801016003@zæâ†'RT@zp¥¢3ú@|`␀␀␀␀␀@|`␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì?j-„Ô␀␀ÍµHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀íPowerful outflows are considered as indicators of AGNs quenching their host galaxies. However, recent studies report a puzzling co-existence of extreme outflows and starbursts in eight ULIRGs. More interestingly, NuSTAR follow-ups for two ULIRGs of them show declined AGN central engines, implying a scenario that the AGNs quench themselves by strong winds before severely affecting star formation. In order to understand these fading cases, NuSTAR observations for other ULIRGs in the sample are necessary. Utilizing the sample with the strongest ionized outflows and the highest SFR at z<1, the project provides a unique opportunity to study nuclear fading with extreme 