t limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316LXT221107A                                        @V∆’œ™Õü@)ïµsÍ≥h@hÄàŒp:˚¿qué!ñS@e"¶L/É{@Ì?qÍN@Ì?ÍN90801332002@‘0Ô∂E°À@”¸5A [¿@’à˚8}ø@’à˚8}øSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì?äG+˛␀␀Í␀Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20220215ABELL_3266_POS1                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì?ÍN@Ì?Ä±9Ò70801001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì?£=—ª␀␀ÍƒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˙The most energetic events in the Universe since the Big Bang have been galaxy clusters merging. In rare cases, multiple subclusters have been observed merging together. Abell 3266 is one such cluster. As the subclusters merge, shock fronts form and can be distinguished with telltale signs like a surface brightness edge. Such an edge was observed in this cluster with eROSITA, however it is located in a complex temperature environment with multiple hot regions that is difficult to constrain with that telescope. NuSTAR's ability to probe hard X-ray energies can provide key insights into this region and allow us to constrain the Mach number of the shock as well as potential inverse Compton scattering that may occur from the reacceleration of electrons by the shock.                             Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Rojas                         Randall                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FASCINATING MERGER OF ABELL 3266                                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ABELL_3266_POS1                                   @PÛ∂E° ¡¿N∫C˛\ë—@qâ†'RT¿D∏∫«À@l®‘˝Û∂F@Ì?Ä±9Ò@Ì?¬î»)¯70801001002@˜Dòq≤ñ@˜ßZ¨@¯·È·∞ä@¯·°È·∞äSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì?‚io␀␀ÍƒHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˙The most energetic events in the Universe since the Big Bang have been galaxy clusters merging. In rare cases, multiple subclusters have been observed merging together. Abell 3266 is one such cluster. As the subclusters merge, shock fronts form and can be distinguished with telltale signs like a surface brightness edge. Such an edge was observed in this cluster with eROSITA, however it is located in a complex temperature environment with multiple hot regions that is difficult to constrain with that telescope. NuSTAR's ability to probe hard X-ray energies can provide key insights into this region and allow us to constrain the Mach number of the shock as well as potential inverse Compton scattering that may occur from the reacceleration of electrons by the shock.                             Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Rojas                         Randall                       NULL                        