=p§@q°∆?@Ì?√èFj@Ì?—±9Ò60802013006@÷¡	kπå@’÷™ÕûÑ@◊åÎZÖáî@◊ç+ZÖáîSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì?·Æ]L;␀␀ÍƒHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀éFor more than 30 years, it has been assumed that a pc-scale torus produces a constant Compton reflection component in heavily obscured AGN. The 157-month Swift BAT processing has recently been finished and nearly all (87%, 14/16) of the brightest Compton-thick AGN are variable in the 20-50 keV band. We propose a variability study of two CT AGN with large NH variability detected in multi-epoch spectra showing changes from reflection to transmission dominated or Compton-thick to thin or vice versa, to place constraints on the size of the variable absorber. These observations will test the size and patchiness in the absorbing material that is the chief component of the AGN unified model.                                                                                                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       USING VARIABILITY TO STUDY THE SIZE OF THE ABSORBER IN COMPTON-THICK AGN                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215ESO_501m023_ULX                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì?—±9Ò@Ì?“î»)¯30801030001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì?·é8„é␀␀ÍƒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ The ultraluminous X-ray source population is now broadly expected to be dominated by super-Eddington accretors, thanks to the broadband spectroscopy provided by NuSTAR and XMM in combination, and the detection of X-ray pulsations from a growing subset of ULXs (unambiguously confirming them as super-Eddington neutron stars). IC5052 ULX and ESO501-023 ULX are extreme ULXs (Lx,peak > 1e40 erg/s) that have received very little observational attention to date. Here, we propose coordinated 100+50 ks NuSTAR+XMM observations of these sources in order to constrain their broadband spectra and search for pulsations. These observations will provide the first robust constraints on the nature of these two extreme binary systems.                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       BROADBAND OBSERVATIONS OF NEW ULXS: IC5052 ULX AND ESO501-023 ULX                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO_501m023_ULX                                   @c‹¨ŸË>B¿8∫Ov`@p¥”ZÖáî@<†|Ñµ‹∆@dÚìtºj@Ì?“î»)¯@Ì@ èFj30801030002@˘g8~@¯⁄„QÇ©ì@˙ﬁz<ûÏ¿@˙‡j<ûÏ¿SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì@ByæG␀␀ÍƒHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ The ultraluminous X-ray source population is now broadly expected to be dominated by super-Eddington accretors, thanks to the broadband spectroscopy provided by NuSTAR and XMM in combination, and the detection of X-ray pulsations from a growing subset of ULXs (unambiguously confirming them as super-Eddington neutron stars). IC5052 ULX and ESO501-023 ULX are extreme ULXs (Lx,peak > 1e40 erg/s) that have received very little observational attention to date. Here, we propose coordinated 100+50 ks NuSTAR+XMM observations of these sources in order to constrain their broadband spectra and search for pulsations. These observations will provide the first robust constraints on the nature of these two extreme binary systems.                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       BROADBAND OBSERVATIONS OF NEW ULXS: IC5052 ULX AND ESO501-023 ULX                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215S2_0109p22                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì@ èFj@Ì@![‰õø60801029001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì@B&5⁄’␀␀ÍƒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˛The low- and intermediate-synchrotron-peaked blazars (LSPs & ISPs) observed at very high energies usually require external inverse-Compton (EIC) processes to describe their spectra, but the origin of the external photon fields interacting with relativistic particles is still a matter of debate. NuSTAR is currently the only instrument with the capability to define the spectral change between synchrotron and IC emission, which takes place in the 1-100 keV range for these blazars. We request 3 observations from a target list designed to complete the sample of Northern TeV LSP/ISP blazars seen by NuSTAR. These observations will be simultaneously augmented by Swift-UVOT, Swift-XRT, Fermi-LAT, and VERITAS. The relevance of one-zone models will be tested with an MCMC SED fitting algorithm.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Hervet                        Olivier                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       DEFINING THE SPECTRAL TRANSITION BETWEEN SYNCHROTRON ANDINVERSE-COMPTON IN NORTHERN TEV INTERMEDIATE BLAZARS            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316S2_0109p22                                        @1˛À˚µt@6∂’œ™Õü@`©ì‡ﬂ¿DˆîFsÇ@t®C,•z@Ì@![‰õø@Ì@I[‰õø60801029002@Î¸~ÜYJı@Î∞fœAÚ@ÌıÃ‚e,@ÌˆL‚e,SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì@b¥ßA␀␀ÍƒHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˛The low- and intermediate-synchrotron-peaked blazars (LSPs & ISPs) observed at very high energies usually require external inverse-Compton (EIC) processes to describe their spectra, but the origin of the external photon fields interacting with relativistic particles is still a matter of debate. NuSTAR is currently the only instrument with the capability to define the spectral change between synchrotron and IC emission, which takes place in the 1-100 keV range for these blazars. We request 3 observations from a target list designed to complete the sample of Northern TeV LSP/ISP blazars seen by NuSTAR. These observations will be simultaneously augmented by Swift-UVOT, Swift-XRT, Fermi-LAT, and VERITAS. The relevance of one-zone models will be tested with an MCMC SED fitting algorithm.       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Hervet                        Olivier                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       DEFINING THE SPECTRAL TRANSITION BETWEEN SYNCHROTRON ANDINVERSE-COMPTON IN NORTHERN TEV INTERMEDIATE BLAZARS            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              VERITAS                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215AT2022tsd_SADA_22327                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì@I[‰õø@Ì@JèFj80901405001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì@bÄ0ãë␀␀ÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    uÿ8We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on the recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Mining the Transient Sky: Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of the Fastest Explosions                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀       