 of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164Cp34d47                                          @pMŸb∂Æ@A!ì‡ﬁ”@MÀ)^û@@(¥9Xb@o'4ÍJå@ÌBLèFj@ÌB]xVb‹60160664002@’Ô∞â†'@’∑Çæﬂ§@@◊á≈Oﬂ;d@◊àÖOﬂ;dSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌBkì∏≥␀␀ÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215Sol_22343_DL                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌB]xVb‹@ÌB^xVb‹20801001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌBj ÁY ␀␀ÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀—A multi-messenger approach to the study of solar flare accelerated particles provides important opportunities for connecting the activity on the Sun and in the heliosphere. In recent years, the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) perihelia have defined intervals in which many observatories, including NuSTAR, simultaneously observe the Sun. NuSTAR's sensitivity to hard X-rays (HXR) is unique among solar observers and has made substantial contributions towards understanding the nature of microflare-scale phenomena. To extend this effort, this proposal seeks to utilize NuSTAR s significant capabilities in the HXR range to study solar flare particle acceleration, heating, and propagation via organized co-observations with a cohort of solar instruments.                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Peterson                      Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       EXPANDING THE SCIENCE OF SOLAR FLARE-ACCELERATED PARTICLES WITH MULTI-MESSENGER CO-OBSERVATIONS                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR dogleg setup / idiagnostics                                                                                     archived  20150316Sol_22343_SADA                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌB^xVb‹@ÌB^ÍN20801002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌBj˚*␀␀ÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀—A multi-messenger approach to the study of solar flare accelerated particles provides important opportunities for connecting the activity on the Sun and in the heliosphere. In recent years, the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) perihelia have defined intervals in which many observatories, including NuSTAR, simultaneously observe the Sun. NuSTAR's sensitivity to hard X-rays (HXR) is unique among solar observers and has made substantial contributions towards understanding the nature of microflare-scale phenomena. To extend this effort, this proposal seeks to utilize NuSTAR s significant capabilities in the HXR range to study solar flare particle acceleration, heating, and propagation via organized co-observations with a cohort of solar instruments.                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Peterson      