will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC7811                                           ?‚ªò«‚ÇA@
v˝!ˇ.I@XÕ!ˇ.HÈ¿L≠Oﬂ;dZ@syOv_Ÿ@ÌBÕ[‰õø@ÌBﬁÕ´∏160160003002@÷íá”√a@÷RK‚Ç@∏@ÿ7è≈Å@ÿ8›è≈ÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌBÍÁ@⁄t␀␀ÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215Mrk_477                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌBﬁÕ´∏1@ÌBﬂÕ´∏160802020001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌBÍƒΩ°/␀␀ÎòHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_477                                           @káœ™ÕûÑ@JøØ∑È@WMñRΩ<6@LfÈx‘˝Ù@iÊ WßÜ¬@ÌBﬂÕ´∏1@ÌBÓ?r‘£60802020002@’úNœAÚ@’jçOﬂ;@◊≥¢úwö@◊≥¢úwöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌC«eC!␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215NGC_4395                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌBÓ?r‘£@ÌBÓ±9Ò60802027003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌC¶ï␀␀ÍÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˝Low mass (<10^6Msun) AGN are important not just for understanding the evolution of supermassive black holes, but they vary rapidly through accretion states, allowing the study of physical processes which are unaccessible with larger mass AGN. NGC 4395 (~10^5Msun) is the brightest and most variable low mass AGN. Previous observations suggest that the X-ray spectrum shows both harder when brighter and softer when brighter behaviour. This transition is not seen in any other single AGN and may represent a change from coronal synchrotron to disc black body seed photons. This scenario cannot be confirmed without Nustar's high energy band. Thus, we propose 6x50 ks NuSTAR+6x25 XMM simultaneous pointings. We will also coordinate with HiPERCAM@GTC to obtain precise lags to probe the outer disc.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST SIMULTANEOUS NUSTAR/XMM-NEWTON OBSERVATIONS OF NGC 4395                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4395                                          @gPh	‘ï@@«Ü¬&Äù@dK¡Ω•ù@TT6‚Î@f≥µsÍ≥h@ÌBÓ±9Ò@ÌCxVb‹60802027004@Ê}–Åo␀i@ÊC°#¢úx@Ë$_o“@Ë§_o“SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌC0"É9E␀␀ÍÊHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˝Low mass (<10^6Msun) AGN are important not just for understanding the evolution of supermassive black holes, but they vary rapidly through accretion states, allowing the study of physical processes which are unaccessible with larger mass AGN. NGC 4395 (~10^5Msun) is the brightest and most variable low mass AGN. Previous observations suggest that the X-ray spectrum shows both harder when brighter and softer when brighter behaviour. This transition is not seen in any other single AGN and may represent a change from coronal synchrotron to disc black body seed photons. This scenario cannot be confirmed without Nustar's high energy band. Thus, we propose 6x50 ks NuSTAR+6x25 XMM simultaneous pointings. We will also coordinate with HiPERCAM@GTC to obtain precise lags to probe the outer disc.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST SIMULTANEOUS NUSTAR/XMM-NEWTON OBSERVATIONS OF NGC 4395                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215AT2019wey                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌCxVb‹@ÌC?r‘£80801328001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌC/Ú(3î␀␀ÍÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀èWe propose a joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 5 ks NICER TOO observation of a transient black hole (BH) X-ray binary source during outburst. Using NICER monitoring to determine the source state, we will trigger the primary joint NuSTAR/NICER observation when the source transitions from the hard state to its intermediate state. Modeling reflection features in the source spectrum will be used to measure the BH spin, as well as constrain the innermost environment of the accretion disk. We will only target sources that have not yet been observed by NuSTAR during outburst, whether that means an entirely new transient BH candidate or a previously known BH X-ray binary that has been in quiescence since the launch of NuSTAR. Examples of well-known sources include GRO J1655-40 and XTE J1550-564.             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Coughenour                    Benjamin                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       SPIN AND REFLECTION IN A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                       