126-041, with NuSTAR (120ks) and simultaneous with XMM (90ks), to study its accretion disk wind. This will be the first ever hard X-ray observation of this AGN, whose line of sight intercepts multiple phases (UV, soft X-ray and Fe K) of a variable wind originating on accretion disk scales. Detailed studies of AGN disk winds are rare, here PG1126 resembles PDS456, the archetypical luminous AGN with a powerful accretion disk wind. The observations will: (i) measure the Fe K and hard X-ray wind profile, allowing the fast wind energetics to be accurately derived, (ii) probe rapid wind variability on timescales of tens of ks in the innermost disk and (iii) link all the phases of the wind from the UV to hard X-rays.                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       HEADING INTO THE WIND: A HARD X-RAY NUSTAR VIEW OF PG 1126-041.                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1126m041                                       @eãù≤-V¿è\(ı¬è@p≤ÍJåM@J^E° ¿É@fMÆÊ1¯°@ÌC†î»)¯@ÌCÿÍN60801008002@ÛZﬂßÜ¬'@Û'GE8Ô@Ù≥m≈÷9@Ù≥0m≈÷9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌCÍEgâ¨␀␀ÍÊHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀DWe propose a hard X-ray look at the mini-BAL QSO, PG 1126-041, with NuSTAR (120ks) and simultaneous with XMM (90ks), to study its accretion disk wind. This will be the first ever hard X-ray observation of this AGN, whose line of sight intercepts multiple phases (UV, soft X-ray and Fe K) of a variable wind originating on accretion disk scales. Detailed studies of AGN disk winds are rare, here PG1126 resembles PDS456, the archetypical luminous AGN with a powerful accretion disk wind. The observations will: (i) measure the Fe K and hard X-ray wind profile, allowing the fast wind energetics to be accurately derived, (ii) probe rapid wind variability on timescales of tens of ks in the innermost disk and (iii) link all the phases of the wind from the UV to hard X-rays.                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       HEADING INTO THE WIND: A HARD X-RAY NUSTAR VIEW OF PG 1126-041.                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215AT2019wey                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌCÿÍN@ÌCŸÍN80801328003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌCÍ∏R␀␀ÍÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀èWe propose a joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 5 ks NICER TOO observation of a transient black hole (BH) X-ray binary source during outburst. Using NICER monitoring to determine the source state, we will trigger the primary joint NuSTAR/NICER observation when the source transitions from the hard state to its intermediate state. Modeling reflection features in the source spectrum will be used to measure the BH spin, as well as constrain the innermost environment of the accretion disk. We will only target sources that have not yet been observed by NuSTAR during outburst, whether that means an entirely new transient BH candidate or a previously known BH X-ray binary that has been in quiescence since the launch of NuSTAR. Examples of well-known sources include GRO J1655-40 and XTE J1550-564.             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Coughenour                    Benjamin                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       SPIN AND REFLECTION IN A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2019wey                                         @Q1'ª0@K± [¿7@bﬂä⁄πıZ@6îFsÅÿ@>MÛ∂E°À@ÌCŸÍN@ÌCÌ#Ü80801328004@◊w≈«y¶µ@◊d^êˇó$@⁄¬¿”ZÖà@⁄ƒ¿”ZÖàSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌD
1Æ÷©␀␀ÍÊHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀èWe propose a joint 50 ks NuSTAR and 5 ks NICER TOO observation of a transient black hole (BH) X-ray binary source during outburst. Using NICER monitoring to determine the source state, we will trigger the primary joint NuSTAR/NICER observation when the source transitions from the hard state to its intermediate state. Modeling reflection features in the source spectrum will be used to measure the BH spin, as well as constrain the innermost environment of the accretion disk. We will only target sources that have not yet been observed by NuSTAR during outburst, whether that means an entirely new transient BH candidate or a previously known BH X-ray binary that has been in quiescence since the launch of NuSTAR. Examples of well-known sources include GRO J1655-40 and XTE J1550-564.             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Coughenour                    Benjamin                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       SPIN AND REFLECTION IN A BLACK HOLE TRANSIENT                                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(2/2) ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  20220215NGC_4395                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌCÌ#Ü@ÌCÌÍN60802027007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌD

ûà␀␀ÍÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀˝Low mass (<10^6Msun) AGN are important not just for understanding the evolution of supermassive black holes, but they vary rapidly through accretion states, allowing the study of physical processes which are unaccessible with larger mass AGN. NGC 4395 (~10^5Msun) is the brightest and most variable low mass AGN. Previous observations suggest that the X-ray spectrum shows both harder when brighter and softer when brighter behaviour. This transition is not seen in any other single AGN and may represent a change from coronal synchrotron to disc black body seed photons. This scenario cannot be confirmed without Nustar's high energy band. Thus, we propose 6x50 ks NuSTAR+6x25 XMM simultaneous pointings. We will also coordinate with HiPERCAM@GTC to obtain precise lags to probe the outer disc.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST SIMULTANEOUS NUSTAR/XMM-NEWTON OBSERVATIONS OF NGC 4395                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4395                                          @gOÏV’œ´@@«ÏV’œ´@dMCïÅ%@TSiDg8@f&}øHÄ@ÌCÌÍN@ÌD?r‘£60802027008@Èd´ôôôö@È!’y¶µ@Î(ù∂E°À@Î)=∂E°ÀSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌD#Ö∞[␀␀ÍÊHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀˝Low mass (<10^6Msun) AGN are important not just for understanding the evolution of supermassive black holes, but they vary rapidly through accretion states, allowing the study of physical processes which are unaccessible with larger mass AGN. NGC 4395 (~10^5Msun) is the brightest and most variable low mass AGN. Previous observations suggest that the X-ray spectrum shows both harder when brighter and softer when brighter behaviour. This transition is not seen in any other single AGN and may represent a change from coronal synchrotron to disc black body seed photons. This scenario cannot be confirmed without Nustar's high energy band. Thus, we propose 6x50 ks NuSTAR+6x25 XMM simultaneous pointings. We will also coordinate with HiPERCAM@GTC to obtain precise lags to probe the outer disc.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vincentelli                   Federico                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST SIMULTANEOUS NUSTAR/XMM-NEWTON OBSERVATIONS OF NGC 4395                                                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(4/6)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215SN2022jli                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌD?r‘£@ÌDxVb‹90801535003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌD#S†m:␀␀Í1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                         