=p£◊¿ Ÿ=Ÿb∑@[RÚ‰éä¿QºI∫^5?@se ¿Éo@ÌDxVb‹@ÌD$[‰õø90801535004@”¡ﬁÛMj@”â¯%ÆÊ2@’bH1&È@’dà1&ÈSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌDCØ±4␀␀Í1Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3) DDT                                                                                                               archived  20220215PG_1126m041                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌD$[‰õø@ÌD%î»)¯60801008003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌDCåÃÃÕ␀␀ÍÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀DWe propose a hard X-ray look at the mini-BAL QSO, PG 1126-041, with NuSTAR (120ks) and simultaneous with XMM (90ks), to study its accretion disk wind. This will be the first ever hard X-ray observation of this AGN, whose line of sight intercepts multiple phases (UV, soft X-ray and Fe K) of a variable wind originating on accretion disk scales. Detailed studies of AGN disk winds are rare, here PG1126 resembles PDS456, the archetypical luminous AGN with a powerful accretion disk wind. The observations will: (i) measure the Fe K and hard X-ray wind profile, allowing the fast wind energetics to be accurately derived, (ii) probe rapid wind variability on timescales of tens of ks in the innermost disk and (iii) link all the phases of the wind from the UV to hard X-rays.                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       HEADING INTO THE WIND: A HARD X-RAY NUSTAR VIEW OF PG 1126-041.                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PG_1126m041                                       @eã•ú‡v¿åLò_@p≤‰%ÆÊ2@J^Æ}VlÙ@f]RT`™e@ÌD%î»)¯@ÌDI±9Ò60801008004@È%†»¥9X@Ë‰¢0U2a@ÍŸÏêñº@Í⁄lêñºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌDfî %␀␀ÍÊHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀DWe propose a hard X-ray look at the mini-BAL QSO, PG 1126-041, with NuSTAR (120ks) and simultaneous with XMM (90ks), to study its accretion disk wind. This will be the first ever hard X-ray observation of this AGN, whose line of sight intercepts multiple phases (UV, soft X-ray and Fe K) of a variable wind originating on accretion disk scales. Detailed studies of AGN disk winds are rare, here PG1126 resembles PDS456, the archetypical luminous AGN with a powerful accretion disk wind. The observations will: (i) measure the Fe K and hard X-ray wind profile, allowing the fast wind energetics to be accurately derived, (ii) probe rapid wind variability on timescales of tens of ks in the innermost disk and (iii) link all the phases of the wind from the UV to hard X-rays.                           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       HEADING INTO THE WIND: A HARD X-RAY NUSTAR VIEW OF PG 1126-041.                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215Cyg_X3                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌDI±9Ò@ÌDJ±9Ò90801336001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌDfa/hL␀␀Í1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                      