                             ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀^␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(3/3) DDT / 601s data gaps                                                                                              archived  20220215TXS_2320p343                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌD§î»)¯@ÌD•#Ü80801639001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌD‘Èo{␀␀ÍÊHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÎWe propose to perform two 40 ksec target of opportunity follow-up observations of X-ray luminous sources that we identify in association with two separate 'gold-quality' IceCube high-energy neutrino alerts. These alerts are likely to be from astrophysical neutrinos. The first identification of an astrophysical source of high-energy neutrinos occurred in 2018, following IceCube-170922A and the BL Lac-type blazar TXS 0506+056. X-ray follow-up of likely-cosmic neutrino alerts has thus proven its utility for identifying possible neutrino counterparts, and most importantly to measure the hadronic contribution to the high-energy emission. We aim to use this approach to identify more such sources and calculate their neutrino flux.                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Mcbride                       Felicia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       BLAZAR X-RAYS AND NEUTRINOS: INVESTIGATING THE MOST PROMISING ICECUBE NEUTRINO ALERTS                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316TXS_2320p343                                      @uÍÑµ‹∆?@AH$xG@Y¶∂Æ}Vm¿8€/ÏV’–@s#˙¨ŸË>@ÌD•#Ü@ÌD≈#Ü80801639002@Âxt9Xb@ÂD.␀␀␀␀@Á≤	‘ï@Á≤	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌD˚s¨ê␀␀ÍÊHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÎWe propose to perform two 40 ksec target of opportunity follow-up observations of X-ray luminous sources that we identify in association with two separate 'gold-quality' IceCube high-energy neutrino alerts. These alerts are likely to be from astrophysical neutrinos. The first identification of an astrophysical source of high-energy neutrinos occurred in 2018, following IceCube-170922A and the BL Lac-type blazar TXS 0506+056. X-ray follow-up of likely-cosmic neutrino alerts has thus proven its utility for identifying possible neutrino counterparts, and most importantly to measure the hadronic contribution to the high-energy emission. We aim to use this approach to identify more such sources and calculate their neutrino flux.                                                                   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Mcbride                       Felicia                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       BLAZAR X-RAYS AND NEUTRINOS: INVESTIGATING THE MOST PROMISING ICECUBE NEUTRINO ALERTS                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                      archived  20220215NGC1275                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌD≈#Ü@ÌD≈±9Ò90801637001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌD˚CÇˇ␀␀Í1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      