#9¿ÎÓ@q4÷°aÂ@ÌF—èFj@ÌFﬂ±9Ò30802016006@‘Ì˝‹∆?@‘ª«nóç@÷NlbM”@÷NlbM”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌGº5ß␀␀ÎJHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀£We propose a monitoring campaign, using NuSTAR plus NICER, to supplement existing coverage of Cyg X-1, LMC X-3, and GRS 1915+105: three persistently bright, variable, and impactful black hole systems. Regular monitoring of these dynamic systems at the ~100 day timescale commensurate to their variability enables a multitude of science return and establishes a lasting data archive of some of the richest sources in the X-ray sky.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       NUSTAR+NICER MONITORING OF THREE PERSISTENT AND VARIABLE BLACK HOLES                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215ESO553mG022                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌFﬂ±9Ò@ÌF‡?r‘£60160223001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌGõ«ﬁ†␀␀Í?Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ESO553mG022                                       @Sª/ÏV÷¿2áe˝ä⁄∫@kqÁlãCñ¿>6¬&ÄùI@rMjO@ÌF‡?r‘£@ÌFÓ±9Ò60160223002@“c°Û∂E¢@“8©≠B√ @”Ê(£n/@”ËË£n/SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌGÖ∞[␀␀Í?Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=5                                                                                                                archived  20220215SN2012ap                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌFÓ±9Ò@ÌFÔèFj80802504001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌGf¯	␀␀ÎßHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    fñ˝Contrary to expectations from stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program is designed to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. The overarching goal is to constrain the nature of the physical process responsible for mass loss in evolved massive stars.                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Chornock                      Joey                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C23       A broad-band view of Extreme Mass Loss events from Evolved Massive Stars with Chandra+NuSTAR                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2012ap                                          @R√@N§®¡¿>´6zë@iRQŒ_¿:xl"h	’@r„º£@ÌFÔèFj@ÌGÍN80802504002@ÈÅqÓÀ˚@È<5a@O@ÎD%≥–|Ö@ÎF≥–|ÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌG#:˘çw␀␀ÎßHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   fñ˝Contrary to expectations from stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program is designed to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. The overarching goal is to constrain the nature of the physical process responsible for mass loss in evolved massive stars.                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Chornock                      Joey                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀C23       A broad-band view of Extreme Mass Loss events from Evolved Massive Stars with Chandra+NuSTAR                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                           Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(1/2) ToO / CME Kp=5                                                                                                    archived  20220215SDSSJ113915d13                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌGÍN@ÌG?r‘£60860008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌG#
%*€␀␀ÍÙHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀óNearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. Most recently, the BASS survey has identified a sample of 12 highly luminous obscured AGN (5e44-1e46 ergs/s) at 0.2<z<0.4, that have never before been observed in the X-rays outside of short Swift XRT exposures. NuSTAR observations enable accurate X-ray spectral modeling of of NH, Fe Ka, and intrinsic X-ray luminosity to test models of Eddington limited feedback. This survey would form a complete census of the 17 most luminous obscured AGN at z<0.6 detected in the ultra-hard X-rays, providing a template of high luminosity luminosity obscured AGN emission above 10 keV.                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A SURVEY OF THE MOST LUMINOUS HARD X-RAY SELECTED OBSCURED QUASARS AT Z=0.2-0.4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SDSSJ113915d13                                    @e€'ª0@9¢I∫^5@j±≥–|Ñ∂@Rc…ÓÀ˚@d;ñRΩ<6@ÌG?r‘£@ÌG%#Ü60860008002@‘è\'RTa@‘Xïm\˙≠@’˜)õ=»@’¯©õ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌG[∏QÎÖ␀␀ÍÙHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀óNearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. Most recently, the BASS survey has identified a sample of 12 highly luminous obscured AGN (5e44-1e46 ergs/s) at 0.2<z<0.4, that have never before been observed in the X-rays outside of short Swift XRT exposures. NuSTAR observations enable accurate X-ray spectral modeling of of NH, Fe Ka, and intrinsic X-ray luminosity to test models of Eddington limited feedback. This survey would form a complete census of the 17 most luminous obscured AGN at z<0.6 detected in the ultra-hard X-rays, providing a template of high luminosity luminosity obscured AGN emission above 10 keV.                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A SURVEY OF THE MOST LUMINOUS HARD X-RAY SELECTED OBSCURED QUASARS AT Z=0.2-0.4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215CGCG381m051                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌG%#Ü@ÌG&[‰õø60801034001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌG[ó‰±~␀␀Í˚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ The proposed observation of two obscured AGN are necessary to obtain a complete and uniform coverage of the X-ray emission for the sample of Seyfert-2 galaxies from the 12micron galaxy sample (12MGS). Coupled with the broad-band X-ray observations available for the remaining 12MGS AGN, the proposed observations will allow us to investigate the column density distribution at low redshift for a complete and unbiased AGN sample (because of its mid-IR selection) and to connect the accretion properties to those of the hosts by exploiting the unique multi-band characterization and coverage of the proposed sample. This will also allow us to complete the X-ray spectral coverage of the faint-end distribution of local obscured AGN.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Salvestrini                   Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNVEILING THE INTRINSIC X-RAY PROPERTIES OF THE 12 MICRON SY2 SAMPLE: TOWARDS A COMPLETE X-RAY COVERAGE                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CGCG381m051                                       @vRy=Ÿc@¶Èx‘˝Ù@W$›/üæ¿L|Åo␀h‹@sMlãCïÅ@ÌG&[‰õø@ÌGA?r‘£60801034002@‚˚( ƒõ¶@‚…Æ}øH@‰G'æv»¥@‰G'æv»¥SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌGc„¶~¨␀␀Í˚Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ The proposed observation of two obscured AGN are necessary to obtain a complete and uniform coverage of the X-ray emission for the sample of Seyfert-2 galaxies from the 12micron galaxy sample (12MGS). Coupled with the broad-band X-ray observations available for the remaining 12MGS AGN, the proposed observations will allow us to investigate the column density distribution at low redshift for a complete and unbiased AGN sample (because of its mid-IR selection) and to connect the accretion properties to those of the hosts by exploiting the unique multi-band characterization and coverage of the proposed sample. This will also allow us to complete the X-ray spectral coverage of the faint-end distribution of local obscured AGN.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Salvestrini                   Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNVEILING THE INTRINSIC X-RAY PROPERTIES OF THE 12 MICRON SY2 SAMPLE: TOWARDS A COMPLETE X-RAY COVERAGE                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215XTE_J2012p381                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌGA?r‘£@ÌGAÍN80802344003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌGcµÛÌ␀␀ÍÙHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ The distribution of spins across stellar mass black holes (BH) is currently not well understood. NuSTAR is the instrument best suited for spin measurements of BH in X-ray binaries using relativistic reflection. We propose dedicating 60 ks spread in two 30 ks observations to four upcoming, previously unobserved by NuSTAR, BH transients with fluxes above 50 mCrab. For this flux threshold, a 30ks observation would suffice to measure the BH spin, while two observations taken ~15 days apart in different spectral states allow probing the evolution of the system. This treatment followed over the next observing cycles will maximize the number of new BHs observed and, together with gravitational wave observations, will allow developing a unifying view of the distribution of stellar mass BHs.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Draghis                       Paul                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       MEASURING THE SPIN OF FUTURE BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316XTE_J2012p381                                     @rÚ˛≈m\˚@CI∫^@RŒ=p£◊
@v˝!ˇ.I@n¸º£n@ÌGAÍN@ÌGWèFj80802344004@÷ú/ïÅ%@÷Ø‰ßÊõ@€»ø±[W@€»\ø±[WSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌGcèΩ@␀␀ÍÙHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ The distribution of spins across stellar mass black holes (BH) is currently not well understood. NuSTAR is the instrument best suited for spin measurements of BH in X-ray binaries using relativistic reflection. We propose dedicating 60 ks spread in two 30 ks observations to four upcoming, previously unobserved by NuSTAR, BH transients with fluxes above 50 mCrab. For this flux threshold, a 30ks observation would suffice to measure the BH spin, while two observations taken ~15 days apart in different spectral states allow probing the evolution of the system. This treatment followed over the next observing cycles will maximize the number of new BHs observed and, together with gravitational wave observations, will allow developing a unifying view of the distribution of stellar mass BHs.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Draghis                       Paul                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       MEASURING THE SPIN OF FUTURE BLACK HOLE TRANSIENTS                                                                      ␀␀
≤␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(2/2) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20220215CGCG381m051                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌGWèFj@ÌGW±9Ò60801034003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌGch3îJ␀␀Í˚Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ The proposed observation of two obscured AGN are necessary to obtain a complete and uniform coverage of the X-ray emission for the sample of Seyfert-2 galaxies from the 12micron galaxy sample (12MGS). Coupled with the broad-band X-ray observations available for the remaining 12MGS AGN, the proposed observations will allow us to investigate the column density distribution at low redshift for a complete and unbiased AGN sample (because of its mid-IR selection) and to connect the accretion properties to those of the hosts by exploiting the unique multi-band characterization and coverage of the proposed sample. This will also allow us to complete the X-ray spectral coverage of the faint-end distribution of local obscured AGN.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Salvestrini                   Francesco                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNVEILING THE INTRINSIC X-RAY PROPERTIES OF THE 12 MICRON SY2 SAMPLE: TOWARDS A COMPLETE X-RAY COVERAGE                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  2015031