STAR observations will lead to the most accurate estimation of the beaming factor which will allow us to put stringent constraint on the space density of blazar parent population, and in turn, the evolution of massive black holes in jetted AGNs within the first two billion years. Using XMM+NuSTAR data, we will also probe the role played by the intergalactic medium in the X-ray absorption towards high-redshift blazars.                                                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Paliya                        Vaidehi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE QUEST FOR ELUSIVE COSMIC MONSTERS                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215Mrk_477                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌI␀ÍN@ÌIxVb‹60802020005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌI#‰±~K␀␀ÎòHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_477                                           @káo“ÚÂ@Jƒò_ˆî@WR¶L/É{@LcÁlãCñ@d=T…Öo@ÌIxVb‹@ÌI±9Ò60802020006@’6^5?}@‘Ê!≤-V@÷Öy◊€Ùà@÷Üé§®¡USCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌI#œ’Ö·␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215AT2022tsd                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌI±9Ò@ÌI±9Ò80902404007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌI#ØÄë£␀␀ÍJHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    uÿ8We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on the recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Mining the Transient Sky: Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of the Fastest Explosions                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316AT2022tsd                                         @IF‹]cà@!oêñªò»@e£Ω•ú‡¿Cª=»K^@sÊˆîFsÇ@ÌI±9Ò@ÌI1?r‘£80902404008@‰lÁØ∏@„”g∏∫«@ÂÂ¢úwö@ÂÇ≈¢úwöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌIA∂Â‘ƒ␀␀ÍJHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   uÿ8We propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on the recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and start the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Matthews                      PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Mining the Transient Sky: Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of the Fastest Explosions                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀(5/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  202202153C59                                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌI1?r‘£@ÌI1±9Ò60160088001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌIAä%*€␀␀ÍTHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503163C59                                              @?≈zxl"h@=~æﬂ§?Ê@a∏Ìëhr∞¿>¿N§®¡U@sè1&Èy@ÌI1±9Ò@ÌIAxVb‹60160088002@“w∑º”ZÜ@“Eıo␀h‹@”Á”3333