       NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF AGN WITH NUSTAR AND SWIFT-BAT                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215RX_J0440d9p4431                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌIqÍN@ÌIr?r‘£90901304001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌIy ÁY ␀␀ÍTHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316RX_J0440d9p4431                                   @QåÿD–@F?s¸PH@cˆI∫^5ø˘'ª/ÏW@u$ä	ﬁ@ÌIr?r‘£@ÌIÄÍN90901304002@¡y=Ÿ@¡”≈VlÙ@”Ä€jÁ’g@”Ç[jÁ’gSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌI¢D•[g␀␀ÍTHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀DDT / idiagnostics                                                                                                      archived  20220215BZQ_J1325p1123                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌIÄÍN@ÌIÇèFj60863011001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌI¢$Pg)␀␀Î	Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ We request to observe 11 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 400 ks. All found beyond redshift 4, these sources are prototypical MeV blazars, and some of the most powerful of their class. However, they crucially lack hard-Xray measurements. NuSTAR will allow us to accurately detect the rising part of the inverse Compton spectrum up to 50 keV, expected to be very hard. This will be fundamental to determine the position of the high-energy peak, hence confirming their MeV blazar nature. Further, NuSTAR will enable us to determine the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNCOVERING THE MOST POWERFUL JETS AT THE DAWN OF COSMIC TIME                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316BZQ_J1325p1123                                    @i*Â`Aâ7@&’‹∆?@t¨∏QÎÖ@R'üæv…@d”Ú◊s@ÌIÇèFj@ÌI¢èFj60863011002@Â‘B␀␀␀␀@Âò}
=p§@Á\áó$tT@Á\ßó$tTSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌI¬îì'␀␀Î	Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ We request to observe 11 high redshift blazars with NuSTAR for a total of 400 ks. All found beyond redshift 4, these sources are prototypical MeV blazars, and some of the most powerful of their class. However, they crucially lack hard-Xray measurements. NuSTAR will allow us to accurately detect the rising part of the inverse Compton spectrum up to 50 keV, expected to be very hard. This will be fundamental to determine the position of the high-energy peak, hence confirming their MeV blazar nature. Further, NuSTAR will enable us to determine the power of the jet, deriving the underlying electron population responsible for the emission and measuring the location of the emission region.                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       UNCOVERING THE MOST POWERFUL JETS AT THE DAWN OF COSMIC TIME                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215MRK_590                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌI¢èFj@ÌI£[‰õø80602604003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌI¬ffff␀␀ÎwHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀^After a 10-year break, Mrk 590 is rekindling its nuclear activity with a rise in X-ray flux! We wish to seize this rare opportunity to record, for the first time, the onset of AGN activity as it occurs since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We ask to trigger up to 3 single-visit joint NuSTAR/Swift/XMM observations to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on or if alternate physics are at play. Relatively inexpensive XMM exposures are needed to constrain any intrinsic absorption. The data are also ideal for testing the recent prediction that quasi-periodic eruptions can explain the flux changes seen for Mrk 590.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Vestergaard                   Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ToO observations of a re-awakening AGN: Exploring the physics of changing-look AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRK_590                                           @@Œv»¥9XøÈ€"–Â`B@deökP∞Ú¿Lü∑È˘r@s„Å$›/@ÌI£[‰õø@ÌI√èFj80602604004@‰M:ì@„ÕÄ~˘€#@Ât>ìu@ÂuﬁìuSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌIÈâb¸ñ␀␀ÎwHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀^After a 10-year break, Mrk 590 is rekindling its nuclear activity with a rise in X-ray flux! We wish to seize this rare opportunity to record, for the first time, the onset of AGN activity as it occurs since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We ask to trigger up to 3 single-visit joint NuSTAR/Swift/XMM observations to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on or if alternate physics are at play. Relatively inexpensive XMM exposures are needed to constrain any intrinsic absorption. The data are also ideal for testing the recent prediction that quasi-periodic eruptions can explain the flux changes seen for Mrk 590.                                                                        Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Vestergaard                   Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N06       ToO observations of a re-awakening AGN: Exploring the physics of changing-look AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(2/3) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20220215N63A                                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌI√èFj@ÌI√±9Ò40801006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌIÈ\¢RÆ␀␀ÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀•Supernova remnant N63A provides us with a unique environment for studying the effect of shock-cloud interaction to particle acceleration process with clearly separated interaction and non-interaction regions. Hard X-ray information is an effective probe for understanding how the interaction enhances the acceleration process. Here we propose a 100-ksec observation of N63A with NuSTAR. With the spectral information at the energies ~8-20 keV, we can distinguish the origin of the hard X-ray component: bremsstrahlung, synchrotron, or thermal emission. The broadband spectrum from radio to gamma-rays and spatially-resolved X-ray properties will enable us to quantify how the interaction modifies the thermal and non-thermal properties if combined with the NuSTAR spectrum.                           Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Suzuki                        Hiromasa                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       ENHANCED PARTICLE ACCELERATION BY SHOCK-CLOUD INTERACTION IN THE SUPERNOVA REMNANT N63A                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316N63A                                              @T˚Å◊€Ùà¿Pá'ª/ÏW@q@0U2a|¿@"&ÄùIR@r¨˙¨ŸË>@ÌI√±9Ò@ÌJ
