ability and in particular its connection to the variability at shorter timescales have not been well-studied due to a lack of well-sampled data over a sufficiently wide range in timescale. We propose to combine short-timescale NuSTAR observations with the latest Swift Burst Alert Telescope (BAT) archival data on longer timescales to obtain the first well-sampled time variability characteristics in the hard X-rays for a sample of 4 AGN (2 Seyferts and 2 blazars) over timescales ranging from minutes to 15 years.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mundo                         Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF AGN WITH NUSTAR AND SWIFT-BAT                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀(3/4) / M-class solar flare                                                                                             archived  20220215Mrk374                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌJWxVb‹@ÌJWÕ´∏160464054001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌJcK¡Õ.␀␀Í[Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk374                                            @Z5càeîØ@KÄ4m≈÷@dFh€ã¨q@6é§®¡T @%Ì5®Xy>@ÌJWÕ´∏1@ÌJfxVb‹60464054002@‘Æí§®¡U@‘q∑ÁlãD@÷iôôôö@÷ÈôôôöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌJÉπ,_ì␀␀Í[Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215GALEXASC_J063634                                  ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌJfxVb‹@ÌJf±9Ò60860003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌJÉòø%å␀␀ÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀óNearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. Most recently, the BASS survey has identified a sample of 12 highly luminous obscured AGN (5e44-1e46 ergs/s) at 0.2<z<0.4, that have never before been observed in the X-rays outside of short Swift XRT exposures. NuSTAR observations enable accurate X-ray spectral modeling of of NH, Fe Ka, and intrinsic X-ray luminosity to test models of Eddington limited feedback. This survey would form a complete census of the 17 most luminous obscured AGN at z<0.6 detected in the ultra-hard X-rays, providing a template of high luminosity luminosity obscured AGN emission above 10 keV.                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A SURVEY OF THE MOST LUMINOUS HARD X-RAY SELECTED OBSCURED QUASARS AT Z=0.2-0.4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GALEXASC_J063634                                  @X√êñªò»@MöàŒp:˚@cÅx‘˝Û∂@4Û9¿ÎÌ˙@êHé@ÌJf±9Ò@ÌJuxVb‹60860003002@÷%\˙¨⁄@’‚D®¡T @◊Çπ0æÌ@◊Çπ0æÌSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌJÉÑ+˛|␀␀ÎHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀óNearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. Most recently, the BASS survey has identified a sample of 12 highly luminous obscured AGN (5e44-1e46 ergs/s) at 0.2<z<0.4, that have never before been observed in the X-rays outside of short Swift XRT exposures. NuSTAR observations enable accurate X-ray spectral modeling of of NH, Fe Ka, and intrinsic X-ray luminosity to test models of Eddington limited feedback. This survey would form a complete census of the 17 most luminous obscured AGN at z<0.6 detected in the ultra-hard X-rays, providing a template of high luminosity luminosity obscured AGN emission above 10 keV.                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A SURVEY OF THE MOST LUMINOUS HARD X-RAY SELECTED OBSCURED QUASARS AT Z=0.2-0.4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215MCGp08m11m011                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌJuxVb‹@ÌJuÕ´∏160802037007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌJÉdÄÚ∫␀␀ÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ Timing analyses provide valuable insight into the relationship between AGN variability and the mechanisms contributing to X-ray emission from AGN. While recent studies have shed light on the nature of variations on short timescales, in general, the nature of the long-term X-ray variability and in particular its connection to the variability at shorter timescales have not been well-studied due to a lack of well-sampled data over a sufficiently wide range in timescale. We propose to combine short-timescale NuSTAR observations with the latest Swift Burst Alert Telescope (BAT) archival data on longer timescales to obtain the first well-sampled time variability characteristics in the hard X-rays for a sample of 4 AGN (2 Seyferts and 2 blazars) over timescales ranging from minutes to 15 years.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mundo                         Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF AGN WITH NUSTAR AND SWIFT-BAT                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCGp08m11m011                                     @V*=p£◊
@G4ºj~˘€@d≤Ù!-w@$0â†'RT@uÿ!-w2@ÌJuÕ´∏1@ÌJÑ[‰õø60802037008@‘}ƒ'RTa@‘?n£S&@÷	p©*0@÷	0©*0SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌJ£ÆÓÓÔ␀␀ÎHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ Timing analyses provide valuable insight into the relationship between AGN variability and the mechanisms contributing to X-ray emission from AGN. While recent studies have shed light on the nature of variations on short timescales, in general, the nature of the long-term X-ray variability and in particular its connection to the variability at shorter timescales have not been well-studied due to a lack of well-sampled data over a sufficiently wide range in timescale. We propose to combine short-timescale NuSTAR observations with the latest Swift Burst Alert Telescope (BAT) archival data on longer timescales to obtain the first well-sampled time variability characteristics in the hard X-rays for a sample of 4 AGN (2 Seyferts and 2 blazars) over timescales ranging from minutes to 15 years.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mundo                         Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF AGN WITH NUSTAR AND SWIFT-BAT                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215V902_Mon                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌJÑ[‰õø@ÌJÑÍN30801031001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌJ£è+ùe␀␀ÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ 0The eclipsing Intermediate Polar V902 Mon was found to be X-ray faint but with a very strong fluorescent Fe line. We hypothesize that the source has a high intrinsic luminosity, but the white dwarf is seen through the accretion disk, which has a high enough column density to absorb most of the X-rays below 10 keV. In this scenario, the X-rays we do observe are the result of scattering by a medium, such as the pre-shock accretion column above the orbital plane, and are not subject to absorption by the disk. Hard X-rays above 10 keV may well be able to reach us, depending on the absorbing column through the disk. Here we propose joint NuSTAR and XMM observations of V902 Mon in search of a hard X-ray excess, and if found, to use it to infer the size of the X-ray scattering region.            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Islam                         Nazma                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE ACCRETION GEOMETRY OF THE ECLIPSING IP V902 MON WITH A JOINT NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON OBSERVATION        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V902_Mon                                          @X:»¥9X?¸%F
