@Ú‘QÎÖ@ÚƒQÎÖSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌKbên]␀␀ÎHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÖThe black hole X-ray binary Swift J1728.9-3613 is coincident with the supernova remnant G351.9-0.9. An extensive analysis concludes that the two are likely associated, marking this remnant as window on a black hole progenitor star, a black hole formation event, and subsequent interactions between the compact object and ejecta. G351.9-0.9 is detected in radio bands, in soft X-rays, and with Fermi. We request an 80 ks observation with NuSTAR in order to explore the spatial variation in non-thermal X-ray emission, and to determine spectral slopes with the accuracy required to constrain emission mechanisms via comparisons to radio and gamma-ray slopes.                                                                                                                                                Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR LOOK AT G351.9-0.9                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀SL-AB12 (4U 1728-34)                                                                                                    archived  20220215IGRJ14471m6319                                    @kΩb∂Æ}¿O£)^û	@s∂«À)_¿Ìëhr∞!@f75®Xy>@ÌKU#Ü@ÌKUî»)¯60464115001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌKbR}'“␀␀ÍbHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IGRJ14471m6319                                    @kΩF
¶L0¿O¢C˛\ë—@s∂«‚Ç@∏¿›ÃcÒA @fA∏∫«À@ÌKUî»)¯@ÌKd?r‘£60464115002@◊√&˛≈m]@◊É?1è≈@Ÿ60Lò_@Ÿ60Lò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌKÇ≤ÌÃ␀␀ÍbHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215RT_Cru                                            @gôMjO¿P$j~˘€#@r–ÜYJÙÒø˙+jÁ’fœ@j˘	kπå@ÌKd?r‘£@ÌKdî»)¯30901002001@pÚ=Ÿb∑@pæ[W>´6@rŒ_o“@rŒ_o“SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌKÇí4Vy␀␀ÎﬁHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀g´The extraordinary symbiotic binary RT Cru, which hosts a massive (>1.25 solar masses) white dwarf (WD) and thus is a serious candidate to be a SNIa progenitor, is currently in a new optical/hard X-ray bright state; the previous one was detected in 2012 in optical photometry and with Swift/BAT. At that time the accretion rate onto the WD increased, but did not reach the value where the accretion disk boundary layer transitions to the optically thick regime. We propose to observe RT~Cru with XMM and NuSTAR during the current maximum --an event that is unlikely to repeat within the next ten years -- in order to fully characterize the 0.3-80 keV spectrum during the maximum, allowing us to measure the shock temperature and thus the WD mass and the accretion rate through the boundary layer.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walter                        Frederick                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Measuring the mass of the white dwarf in the symbiotic binary RT Cru                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215RT_Cru                                            @göv»¥9X¿P$PH@r–≈÷8ÜYø˙ ƒõ•„T@j04m≈÷9@ÌKdî»)¯@ÌKíÕ´∏130901002002@[ú‡u˜@1x≈m\˚@Òç“ﬁ“àŒ@ÒêHºSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌK©ÏÒ5z␀␀ÎﬁHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀g´The extraordinary symbiotic binary RT Cru, which hosts a massive (>1.25 solar masses) white dwarf (WD) and thus is a serious candidate to be a SNIa progenitor, is currently in a new optical/hard X-ray bright state; the previous one was detected in 2012 in optical photometry and with Swift/BAT. At that time the accretion rate onto the WD increased, but did not reach the value where the accretion disk boundary layer transitions to the optically thick regime. We propose to observe RT~Cru with XMM and NuSTAR during the current maximum --an event that is unlikely to repeat within the next ten years -- in order to fully characterize the 0.3-80 keV spectrum during the maximum, allowing us to measure the shock temperature and thus the WD mass and the accretion rate through the boundary layer.     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walter                        Frederick                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Measuring the mass of the white dwarf in the symbiotic binary RT Cru                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀SL-AB (GX 301-2) / M-class solar flare                                                                                  archived  20220215UGC_9367                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌKíÕ´∏1@ÌKì±9Ò60864008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌK©¥üIı␀␀ÎHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316UGC_9367                                          @kK√a@O@AcS˜ŒŸ@Mt"h	‘ï@PƒæÌ(ç@bàØOÑM@ÌKì±9Ò@ÌK•èFj60864008002@ÿ˜+AÚ◊@ÿ¥y(åÁ@⁄åªÖ∏R@⁄åªÖ∏RSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌKÀ␀ë¢¥␀␀ÎHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀M-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  20220215GK_Per                                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌK•èFj@ÌK•ÍN90901305001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌK ›≠RM␀␀ÍiHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                         