 XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=5--7                                                                                                             archived  20220215NEP_DTDF_MOS08006                                 @pD˚~êˇó@PróçOﬂ;@Wˆ«À)_@@‚ô0æÌ@dŒ␀—∑Y@ÌLn?r‘£@ÌLnxVb‹60810006001@D§¬¯7¥¢@DÑπ#¢úx@EÔçOﬂ;d@EÔçOﬂ;dSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌLÅêØ˘Ô␀␀Î(Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215NEP_DTDF_MOS08006                                 @pEbM“Ò™@PrÓcä	@W˜'ª/Ï@@·4ÍJå@d‚ı¬è\)@ÌLnxVb‹@ÌL∞[‰õø60810006002@˘ˆ∏çOﬂ;@˘∂ÿ¿6„@˚πçJ#9¡@˚ªﬁéSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌL…}πu1␀␀Î(Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀M-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  20220215PKS_1502p106                                      @lDüæv»¥@%xF‹]@&´]ÃcÒA@KkàeîØO@c≠a@N•@ÌL∞[‰õø@ÌL±èFj60902009001@<@Â`Aâ7@;Ó($x@=ˆ¬&ÄùI@=ˆ¬&ÄùISCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌL….ç◊Ã␀␀ÏvHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀4Multimessenger astronomy is a powerful new method for understanding astrophysical processes. This was illustrated in 2017 when IceCube reported a high-energy neutrino found to be coincident with a very-high-energy flaring blazar (detected by Fermi and MAGIC). Since then, various multimessenger studies involving neutrinos and photon data have occurred, but the associations between neutrinos and active galactic nuclei (AGN) remain uncertain. We propose a monitoring program for two AGN, NGC1068 and PKS1502+106, using NICER and NuSTAR to study the X-ray-neutrino connection. This analysis will make use of both model-dependent and model-independent approaches using observed X-ray and IceCube non-public data and will also serve as a trigger for real-time follow-up using IceCube.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vandenbroucke                 Justin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       PROBING THE AGN NEUTRINO CONNECTION USING MULTIMESSENGER OBSERVATIONS WITH NICER AND ICECUBE                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215PKS_1502p106                                      @lDÖáì›ò@%5®Xy>@&¨cÒA \@Kl⁄QŒ@c©ÑM:ì@ÌL±èFj@ÌLøî»)¯60902009002@‘æôÌ(åÁ@‘Ü&úwök@÷lßÊõ@÷lßÊõSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌL…ôôö␀␀ÏvHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀4Multimessenger astronomy is a powerful new method for understanding astrophysical processes. This was illustrated in 2017 when IceCube reported a high-energy neutrino found to be coincident with a very-high-energy flaring blazar (detected by Fermi and MAGIC). Since then, various multimessenger studies involving neutrinos and photon data have occurred, but the associations between neutrinos and active galactic nuclei (AGN) remain uncertain. We propose a monitoring program for two AGN, NGC1068 and PKS1502+106, using NICER and NuSTAR to study the X-ray-neutrino connection. This analysis will make use of both model-dependent and model-independent approaches using observed X-ray and IceCube non-public data and will also serve as a trigger for real-time follow-up using IceCube.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vandenbroucke                 Justin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       PROBING THE AGN NEUTRINO CONNECTION USING MULTIMESSENGER OBSERVATIONS WITH NICER AND ICECUBE                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215NGC5940                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌLøî»)¯@ÌLøÕ´∏160463051001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌL»˘çvµ␀␀ÍpHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC5940                                           @mπå~($@Û–|Ñµ›@)·ˇ.HËß@GŒ$›/†@cÈ]ÃcÒA@ÌLøÕ´∏1@ÌLŒî»)¯60463051002@–≥x…Öo@–ÖÄ¬¯7µ@“ ?Â…@“ ?Â…SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌL‚]3ıa␀␀ÍpHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌLŒî»)¯@ÌLœ#Ü30802017003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌL‚=åµ␀␀ÎnHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀£We propose a monitoring campaign, using NuSTAR plus NICER, to supplement existing coverage of Cyg X-1, LMC X-3, and GRS 1915+105: three persistently bright, variable, and impactful black hole systems. Regular monitoring of these dynamic systems at the ~100 day timescale commensurate to their variability enables a multitude of science return and establishes a lasting data archive of some of the richest sources in the X-ray sky.                                                     