ars, the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) perihelia have defined intervals in which many observatories, including NuSTAR, simultaneously observe the Sun. NuSTAR's sensitivity to hard X-rays (HXR) is unique among solar observers and has made substantial contributions towards understanding the nature of microflare-scale phenomena. To extend this effort, this proposal seeks to utilize NuSTAR s significant capabilities in the HXR range to study solar flare particle acceleration, heating, and propagation via organized co-observations with a cohort of solar instruments.                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Peterson                      Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       EXPANDING THE SCIENCE OF SOLAR FLARE-ACCELERATED PARTICLES WITH MULTI-MESSENGER CO-OBSERVATIONS                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀STELLAR dogleg rally                                                                                                    archived  20150316ESO187mG002                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌN¡[‰õø@ÌN¬[‰õø60464180001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌN‚ÊñÒ¯␀␀Í~Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in 