ised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=7                                                                                                                archived  20220215Cas_A                                             @uÔöu%@Md,<ûÏ¿@[Ê?º¿˛($x@lÕ33333@ÌOaèFj@ÌOaxVb‹40801003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌOë[∞[␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀xA decade ago, NuSTAR legacy observations of Cas A unexpectedly revealed that hard X-ray emission above 15 keV was concentrated in the western knots and apart from the forward/reverse shock regions. Base on the Chandra observations and comprehensive modeling of the inward shocks (which coincide with the hard X-ray knots), a new NuSTAR observation will likely detect their year-scale variability due to fast synchrotron cooling. Our proposal aims to (1) establish the synchrotron origin of non-thermal X-ray emission, (2) determine the knot B-fields, and (3) constrain the maximum electron energy. A decadal NuSTAR survey of Cas A will explore the dynamics and energetics in the most active particle acceleration site then ultimately tell us why young SNRs are unlikely PeVatron accelerators.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Woo                           Jooyun                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       REVISITING CASSIOPEIA A AFTER 10 YEARS: SEARCHING FOR HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cas_A                                             @uÌÊö‘,=@Me´üUõ=@[‰I∫^5?¿Œp:˚~ë@lë•„S˜œ@ÌOaxVb‹@ÌO§ÍN40801003002@˘?Åo␀i@˘7S&¬@˚Èç∏∫«@˚Í¡é!ñSSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌOƒ4>2“␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀xA decade ago, NuSTAR legacy observations of Cas A unexpectedly revealed that hard X-ray emission above 15 keV was concentrated in the western knots and apart from the forward/reverse shock regions. Base on the Chandra observations and comprehensive modeling of the inward shocks (which coincide with the hard X-ray knots), a new NuSTAR observation will likely detect their year-scale variability due to fast synchrotron cooling. Our proposal aims to (1) establish the synchrotron origin of non-thermal X-ray emission, (2) determine the knot B-fields, and (3) constrain the maximum electron energy. A decadal NuSTAR survey of Cas A will explore the dynamics and energetics in the most active particle acceleration site then ultimately tell us why young SNRs are unlikely PeVatron accelerators.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Woo                           Jooyun                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       REVISITING CASSIOPEIA A AFTER 10 YEARS: SEARCHING FOR HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215Mrk_477                                           @kÑ_ÿ≠´ü@J»*ô0æ@WYáì›óˆ@Lf‚ÎC-@ZUé!ñRΩ@ÌO§ÍN@ÌO•î»)¯60802020007@lˆâ†'RT@lö∫«À)@n¿”ZÖáî@n¿”ZÖáîSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌO√‰ÄÚ∫␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215Mrk_477                                           @kÑ’œ™Õü@Jƒò_ˆî@WVsÅ◊€ı@Lhy=Ÿc@ZHÚ{≤˛≈@ÌO•î»)¯@ÌO¥?r‘£60802020008@’ö‹V’œ´@’^Â„º”@◊	Lò_@◊
Lò_SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌO√œ\(ˆ␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(4/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215GALEXMSC_SADA_23085                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌO¥?r‘£@ÌOµî»)¯60860014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌO√ØÄë£␀␀ÎCHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀óNearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. Most recently, the BASS survey has identified a sample of 12 highly luminous obscured AGN (5e44-1e46 ergs/s) at 0.2<z<0.4, that have never before been observed in the X-rays outside of short Swift XRT exposures. NuSTAR observations enable accurate X-ray spectral modeling of of NH, Fe Ka, and intrinsic X-ray luminosity to test models of Eddington limited feedback. This survey would form a complete census of the 17 most luminous obscured AGN at z<0.6 detected in the ultra-hard X-rays, providing a template of high luminosity luminosity obscured AGN emission above 10 keV.                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A SURVEY OF THE MOST LUMINOUS HARD X-RAY SELECTED OBSCURED QUASARS AT Z=0.2-0.4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA setup STELLAR only                                                                                                 archived  20150316GALEXMSC_J025952                                  @Fz)«y¶µ@8ÿ¡T…Ö@cä¡T…Ö¿=¿uˆ˝!ˇ@s[˙¨ŸË>@ÌOµî»)¯@ÌO«±9Ò60860004002@’Œ-Ω•ù@’óm¬è\)@◊heîØO@◊kpeîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌO‰""""␀␀ÎCHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀óNearby powerful AGN provide the best way to understand the growth of supermassive black holes and their effect on galaxies. Most recently, the BASS survey has identified a sample of 12 highly luminous obscured AGN (5e44-1e46 ergs/s) at 0.2<z<0.4, that have never before been observed in the X-rays outside of short Swift XRT exposures. NuSTAR observations enable accurate X-ray spectral modeling of of NH, Fe Ka, and intrinsic X-ray luminosity to test models of Eddington limited feedback. This survey would form a complete census of the 17 most luminous obscured AGN at z<0.6 detected in the ultra-hard X-rays, providing a template of high luminosity luminosity obscured AGN emission above 10 keV.                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Koss                          Michael                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A SURVEY OF THE MOST LUMINOUS HARD X-RAY SELECTED OBSCURED QUASARS AT Z=0.2-0.4                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215IGRJ22292p6647                                    @u:)«yß@P∞∫«À)@[_§?Â…@71è≈Å@jµ–|Ñµ›@ÌO«±9Ò@ÌO»[‰õø60160790001@zcêñªô@z{≤˛≈m@|Õ5®Xy@|Õ5®XySCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌO„¯!_Ù␀␀ÍåHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                               