    NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202202152FHLJ1745d1m3035                                  @p•∏QÎÖ¿>ì˜ŒŸá@vfØOÑMø·iDg8~@c£◊
=p§@ÌOÿxVb‹@ÌOŸ[‰õø30801014001@qHoiDg8@qˆîFsÇ@ru3úæ‡@ru3úæ‡SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌO„¿0ãë␀␀ÎCHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀nVery High Energy (VHE, >50 GeV) gamma rays provide a direct view of some of the most extreme environments in our Galaxy and are an excellent probe of non-thermal astrophysical processes. Studies of the non-thermal Galactic source population are essential to understand where and how the bulk of the cosmic rays are accelerated in our Galaxy. We propose for a NuSTAR observation of an intriguing VHE source, 2FHLJ1745.1-3035, a candidate highly energetic pulsar wind nebula with extremely hard spectrum both in the 0.5-10 keV and in the >50 GeV energy range. Thanks to NuSTAR unique capabilities, we will be able to characterize the age, energetics and emission mechanisms of this extreme VHE object.                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       2FHLJ1745.1--3035: A NEW EFFICIENT GALACTIC ACCELERATOR                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202202152FHLJ1745d1m3035                                  @p•°a‰˜f¿>ï'ª0@vfñRΩ<6ø·Y≥–|Ñ∂@c≠8}øH@ÌOŸ[‰õø@ÌP*[‰õø30801014002@˚6{8}ø@˙ÁLC˛\í@˝''ŸË>B@˝(«ŸË>BSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌPBkÊ5€␀␀ÎCHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀nVery High Energy (VHE, >50 GeV) gamma rays provide a direct view of some of the most extreme environments in our Galaxy and are an excellent probe of non-thermal astrophysical processes. Studies of the non-thermal Galactic source population are essential to understand where and how the bulk of the cosmic rays are accelerated in our Galaxy. We propose for a NuSTAR observation of an intriguing VHE source, 2FHLJ1745.1-3035, a candidate highly energetic pulsar wind nebula with extremely hard spectrum both in the 0.5-10 keV and in the >50 GeV energy range. Thanks to NuSTAR unique capabilities, we will be able to characterize the age, energetics and emission mechanisms of this extreme VHE object.                                                                                                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Marchesi                      Stefano                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       2FHLJ1745.1--3035: A NEW EFFICIENT GALACTIC ACCELERATOR                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀SL-A123 (2E 1743, IGR J17473) / M-class solar flare                                                                     archived  202202152MASXJ18564935m5442298                            @qƒU2a|¿KVYJÙÿ@uY~˘€"—¿6_4÷°aÂ@boÆz·H@ÌP*[‰õø@ÌP*Õ´∏160160702001@`…¨q≤ñ@`ùÉ{J#:@a⁄h	‘ï@a⁄h	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌPB/…c␀␀ÍåHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202202152MASXJ18564935m5442298                            @qƒZÓcä¿KV4ÍK@uYâ7K∆®¿6_;dZ¨@bs¸π#¢ú@ÌP*Õ´∏1@ÌP9[‰õø60160702002@”CÆHËß@”4r∞ ≈@‘ø˜K∆ß@‘ƒ∑K∆ßSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌPB␀¬.E␀␀ÍåHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215AX_J1742d6m3022                                   @põ¯†ê-‡¿>QÚ◊s@veÀí:)«?£úæﬂ§@@c∫ WßÜ¬@ÌP9[‰õø@ÌP9Õ´∏160801024001@r%7K∆ß@qÚ‡ﬁ“àŒ@s\œ™ÕûÑ@s\œ™ÕûÑSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌPA·"É␀␀ÎCHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀‡We request a total of 120 ks NuSTAR observation in order to identify the nature and measure the properties of a possibly extremely powerful disk wind. The source was identified as an AGN hidden behind the Galactic plane with a recent 20ks XMM exposure. Its X-ray spectrum exhibits an interesting iron complex at 6.4-7.0 keV reminiscent of P Cygni profile which is indicative of an outflow, and an absorption line equivalent width of 150 eV which lies at the higher end of winds observed in AGN. The characterization of the X-ray spectrum above 7 keV, will only be possible with the proposed NuSTAR observations, which together with the existing XMM 20ks exposures, will allow for detailed modelling of the properties and energetics of the wind.                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Anastasopoulou                Konstantina                   NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A GEM BEHIND THE CURTAIN: NUSTAR STUDY OF THE IRON COMPLEX OF ONE OF THE MOST BRILLIANT AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215AX_J1742d6m3022                                   @púu%F¿>NÛMj@ve¯†ê-‡?•‹∆?@cƒ_ˆîF@ÌP9Õ´∏1@ÌPØÕ´∏160801024002A+,nÀ˚Aı8“ÚÂAí^¢úwöAì÷¢úwöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌP√ˆÊ␀␀ÎCHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀‡We request a total of 120 ks NuSTAR observation in order to identify the nature and measure the properties of a possibly extremely powerful disk wind. The source was identified as an AGN hidden behind the Galactic plane with a recent 20ks XMM exposure. Its X-ray spectrum exhibits an interesting iron complex at 6.4-7.0 keV reminiscent of P Cygni profile which is indicative of an outflow, and an absorption line equivalent width of 150 eV which lies at the higher end of winds observed in AGN. The characterization of the X-ray spectrum above 7 keV, will only be possible with the proposed NuSTAR observations, which together with the existing XMM 20ks exposures, will allow for detailed modelling of the properties and energetics of the wind.                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Anastasopoulou                Konstantina                   NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A GEM BEHIND THE CURTAIN: NUSTAR STUDY OF THE IRON COMPLEX OF ONE OF THE MOST BRILLIANT AGN                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀               