sponse time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20220215Cas_A_POS2                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌP—xVb‹@ÌP“#Ü40801013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌP‚–N‚Ã␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀xA decade ago, NuSTAR legacy observations of Cas A unexpectedly revealed that hard X-ray emission above 15 keV was concentrated in the western knots and apart from the forward/reverse shock regions. Base on the Chandra observations and comprehensive modeling of the inward shocks (which coincide with the hard X-ray knots), a new NuSTAR observation will likely detect their year-scale variability due to fast synchrotron cooling. Our proposal aims to (1) establish the synchrotron origin of non-thermal X-ray emission, (2) determine the knot B-fields, and (3) constrain the maximum electron energy. A decadal NuSTAR survey of Cas A will explore the dynamics and energetics in the most active particle acceleration site then ultimately tell us why young SNRs are unlikely PeVatron accelerators.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Woo                           Jooyun                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       REVISITING CASSIOPEIA A AFTER 10 YEARS: SEARCHING FOR HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cas_A_POS2                                        @uÌ–|Ñµ›@Md]càeï@[„Â…‰¿‡qué@k+C,•z@ÌP“#Ü@ÌQ [‰õø40801013002@˚©pﬁ␀—∑@˚hèÜ¬&Å@˛ïÍÖoi@˛óÍÖoiSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌQJœå¥á␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀xA decade ago, NuSTAR legacy observations of Cas A unexpectedly revealed that hard X-ray emission above 15 keV was concentrated in the western knots and apart from the forward/reverse shock regions. Base on the Chandra observations and comprehensive modeling of the inward shocks (which coincide with the hard X-ray knots), a new NuSTAR observation will likely detect their year-scale variability due to fast synchrotron cooling. Our proposal aims to (1) establish the synchrotron origin of non-thermal X-ray emission, (2) determine the knot B-fields, and (3) constrain the maximum electron energy. A decadal NuSTAR survey of Cas A will explore the dynamics and energetics in the most active particle acceleration site then ultimately tell us why young SNRs are unlikely PeVatron accelerators.        Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernov