¶L@råfffff@råfffffSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌQJy÷H␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÚWe propose a long (100 ks) continuous NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2021 to cover 3 con nights of VLTI-Gravity+Chandra observations of Sgr A*. The required exposure time of Gravity and Chandra has been already secured. The Gravity team has recently measured the astrometric motion of the near infrared (NIR) source during three bright NIR flux excursions of Sgr A*. Simultaneous NuSTAR+Gravity observations will allow us to understand the evolution of the radiative mechanism and to test whether, during bright X-ray flares, the NIR source will display similar circular orbital motions or whether it will be characterised by a dramatically different pattern, such as by linear motions, reminiscent of jet like ejections. This will provide us with a groundbreaking test of accretion physics.              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       THE MOTION OF SGR A*'S NIR SOURCE DURING X-RAY FLARES                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215SgrAstar                                          @pßá¸π#£¿<¯¥9Xb@v}p:˚~ë? ‘,<ûÏ¿@c¡IQÇ©ì@ÌQ!?r‘£@ÌQ1Õ´∏130702013009@”_£9¿ÎÓ@”>¶L/É@’Ylﬁ“àŒ@’\Ïﬁ“àŒSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌQJ`Öœ␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÚWe propose a long (100 ks) continuous NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2021 to cover 3 con nights of VLTI-Gravity+Chandra observations of Sgr A*. The required exposure time of Gravity and Chandra has been already secured. The Gravity team has recently measured the astrometric motion of the near infrared (NIR) source during three bright NIR flux excursions of Sgr A*. Simultaneous NuSTAR+Gravity observations will allow us to understand the evolution of the radiative mechanism and to test whether, during bright X-ray flares, the NIR source will display similar circular orbital motions or whether it will be characterised by a dramatically different pattern, such as by linear motions, reminiscent of jet like ejections. This will provide us with a groundbreaking test of accretion physics.              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ponti                         Gabriele                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N07       THE MOTION OF SGR A*'S NIR SOURCE DURING X-RAY FLARES                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀VLT-Gravity                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀␀(5/5) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20220215RX_J0440d9p4431                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌQ1Õ´∏1@ÌQ3èFj90901313001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌQJ>2“␀␀ÍìHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated