                      ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215SgrAstar                                          @pßè≈Åo¿<˘Ë>BZÓ@v}cä	? T`™d¬¯@c·OÑM@ÌRAî»)¯@ÌRB±9Ò30801024003@1&ÄùIR@0ã6zêó@7␀␀␀␀␀␀@7␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌRm\YÅT␀␀ÎYHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÚFollowing successful joint NuSTAR and EHT observation in previous years, we proposal a 100 ks NuSTAR observation to overlap with the EHT observation window in spring 2023 during NuSTAR cycle 8. Our primary science goals include: 1) Detect at least one bright X-ray flare, determine start-stop time, X-ray variability and spectrum during a flare, and feed these information to the EHT Sgr A* analysis; 2) Enlarge the NuSTAR Sgr A* flare sample by detecting characterizing new flares, and test the trend that brighter X-ray flares have harder spectra. A bonus science goal of this observation is to further investigate a newly discovered hard X-ray source located merely 1 pc from Sgr A*.                                                                                                                  Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Zhang                         Shuo                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       JOINT NUSTAR AND EHT PROBE OF SGR A*: FLARES, BLACK HOLE SHADOW AND A NEW HARD X-RAY SOURCE                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215SgrAstar                                          @pßkP∞Ú|¿<¯Œp:˚@v}a|⁄Q?ÀxF‹]d@c„$›/@ÌRB±9Ò@ÌRS#Ü30801024004@”Ã:)«yß@”¶∞|Ñµ›@’”d(ı¬è@’’§(ı¬èSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌRmB.E␀␀ÎYHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÚFollowing successful joint NuSTAR and EHT observation in previous years, we proposal a 100 ks NuSTAR observation to overlap with the EHT observation window in spring 2023 during NuSTAR cycle 8. Our primary science goals include: 1) Detect at least one bright X-ray flare, determine start-stop time, X-ray variability and spectrum during a flare, and feed these information to the EHT Sgr A* analysis; 2) Enlarge the NuSTAR Sgr A* flare sample by detecting characterizing new flares, and test the trend that brighter X-ray flar