=p§␀␀Î{Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              JWST                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202202154U_1957p11                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌWÍN@ÌWî»)¯30902042005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌW$Êfff␀␀ÎuHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$A groundbreaking recent study of the hard state of Cygnus X-1 with IXPE, NICER and NuSTAR provided the first robust soft X-ray polarimetric detection of an accreting black hole. The spectro-polarimetric results yielded unprecedented constraints on the jet, corona, and accretion disk geometry. This was anchored in the broadband X-ray spectral data from NICER and NuSTAR being modeled in conjunction with IXPE's polarimetric data. Based on that success, we request 3x20ks NuSTAR + 24ks NICER time simultaneous with an approved IXPE campaign for each of the two persistent black holes in LMC X-3 and 4U 1957+11, neither of which has yet been observed with IXPE.                                                                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       NUSTAR, NICER, AND IXPE SPECTRO-POLARIMETRIC CONSTRAINTS ON LMC X-3 AND 4U 1957+11                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1957p11                                        @rΩºj~˘€@'Å∞â†'R@Iîj~˘€#¿";⁄QŒ@_i¯†ê-‡@ÌWî»)¯@ÌW?r‘£30902042006@‘ˆπıY¥@”˘§m≈÷9@÷w{X‚e@÷|;X‚eSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌW$À0*z␀␀ÎuHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$A groundbreaking recent study of the hard state of Cygnus X-1 with IXPE, NICER and NuSTAR provided the first robust soft X-ray polarimetric detection of an accreting black hole. The spectro-polarimetric results yielded unprecedented constraints on the jet, corona, and accretion disk geometry. This was anchored in the broadband X-ray spectral data from NICER and NuSTAR being modeled in conjunction with IXPE's polarimetric data. Based on that success, we request 3x20ks NuSTAR + 24ks NICER time simultaneous with an approved IXPE campaign for each of the two persistent black holes in LMC X-3 and 4U 1957+11, neither of which has yet been observed with IXPE.                                                                                                                                            Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       NUSTAR, NICER, AND IXPE SPECTRO-POLARIMETRIC CONSTRAINTS ON LMC X-3 AND 4U 1957+11                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀(3/3) / idiagnostics                                                                                                    archived  20220215Cygnus_X1                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌW?r‘£@ÌW±9Ò80902318001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌW$®|e§␀␀ÎêHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ˇA groundbreaking recent study of the hard state of Cygnus X-1 with IXPE, NICER and NuSTAR provided the first robust soft X-ray polarimetric detection of an accreting black hole. The spectro-polarimetric results yielded unprecedented constraints on the jet, corona, and accretion disk geometry. This was anchored in the broadband X-ray spectral data from NICER and NuSTAR being modeled in conjunction with IXPE's polarimetric data. If Cyg X-1 transitions to its soft state, an approved 600ks IXPE campaign will be triggered. To achieve equivalent constraints on the soft state's accretion geometry as with the hard state campaign, we request an accompanying 5ks per day for 12 days with NICER, and 3 x 20ks observations with NuSTAR during the IXPE run, 60ks apiece for NICER and NuSTAR.               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       NICER, NUSTAR, AND IXPE SPECTRO-POLARIMETRIC CONSTRAINTS ON THE SOFT STATE OF CYGNUS X-1                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Cygnus_X1                                         @rπzxl"h@Aüs¸PH@QŒí£S&@	¶Èx‘˝Ù@]≥πå~($@ÌW±9Ò@ÌW)#Ü80902318002@ a≠IQÇ™@ÀÆàŒp;@ÿÙUõ=@ÿ˜Uõ=SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌWDààâ␀␀ÎêHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ˇA groundbreaking recent study of the hard state of Cygnus X-1 with IXPE, NICER and NuSTAR provided the first robust soft X-ray polarimetric detection of an accreting black hole. The spectro-polarimetric results yielded unprecedented constraints on the jet, corona, and accretion disk geometry. This was anchored in the broadband X-ray spectral data from NICER and NuSTAR being modeled in conjunction with IXPE's polarimetric data. If Cyg X-1 transitions to its soft state, an approved 600ks IXPE campaign will be triggered. To achieve equivalent constraints on the soft state's accretion geometry as with the hard state campaign, we request an accompanying 5ks per day for 12 days with NICER, and 3 x 20ks observations with NuSTAR during the IXPE run, 60ks apiece for NICER and NuSTAR.               Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       NICER, NUSTAR, AND IXPE SPECTRO-POLARIMETRIC CONSTRAINTS ON THE SOFT STATE OF CYGNUS X-1                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/3) ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  202202151ES_1927p654                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌW)#Ü@ÌW)î»)¯80902632001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌWCÂ*€6␀␀Î‹Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#}Large-scale time-domain surveys have lead to the identification of new types of extreme variability in active galaxies, called changing look active galactic nuclei (CL-AGNs). Drastic changes in coronal emission have been seen in CL-AGNs at different timescales (factor of 10-100 in flux and spectral slope ($\Gamma=1-5$) in a matter of months/years), thus providing an ideal laboratory to understand the fundamental physics behind coronal emission. Here we propose to observe a CL-AGN with Nustar for 40ks, once every month for 4 months, to measure the X-ray cut-off energy, which may be evolving during the highly variable state. We will therefore be able to constrain any changes in the thermal/non-thermal fraction in the coronal plasma in real time, for the first time.                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Laha                          Sibasish                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TRACKING THE EVOLUTION OF CORONAL PROPERTIES IN A CHANGING LOOK AGN                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161ES_1927p654                                      @r=ßÊö‘@Pg•„S˜œ@X?P∞Ú{≥@4˜ßÜ¬&Å@[è␀h€ã¨@ÌW)î»)¯@ÌWB#Ü80902632002@‚özêó@·—!ˇ.I@„_êÔ4÷°@„`–Ô4÷°SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌWk^–ò␀␀Î‹Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#}Large-scale time-domain surveys have lead to the identification of new types of extreme variability in active galaxies, called changing look active galactic nuclei (CL-AGNs). Drastic changes in coronal emission have been seen in CL-AGNs at different timescales (factor of 10-100 in flux and spectral slope ($\Gamma=1-5$) in a matter of months/years), thus providing an ideal laboratory to understand the fundamental physics behind coronal emission. Here we propose to observe a CL-AGN with Nustar for 40ks, once every month for 4 months, to measure the X-ray cut-off energy, which may be evolving during the highly variable state. We will therefore be able to constrain any changes in the thermal/non-thermal fraction in the coronal plasma in real time, for the first time.                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Laha                          Sibasish                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TRACKING THE EVOLUTION OF CORONAL PROPERTIES IN A CHANGING LOOK AGN                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/4) ToO                                                                                                               archived  20220215NGC_4388                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌWB#Ü@ÌWBÍN60802036007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌWj⁄[«ﬂ␀␀Î{Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ Timing analyses provide valuable insight into the relationship between AGN variability and the mechanisms contributing to X-ray emission from AGN. While recent studies have shed light on the nature of variations on short timescales, in general, the nature of the long-term X-ray variability and in particular its connection to the variability at shorter timescales have not been well-studied due to a lack of well-sampled data over a sufficiently wide range in timescale. We propose to combine short-timescale NuSTAR observations with the latest Swift Burst Alert Telescope (BAT) archival data on longer timescales to obtain the first well-sampled time variability characteristics in the hard X-rays for a sample of 4 AGN (2 Seyferts and 2 blazars) over timescales ranging from minutes to 15 years.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mundo                         Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF AGN WITH NUSTAR AND SWIFT-BAT                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_4388                                          @gLﬂ§?Â…@)KC,•z@q]êñªò»@Rî7¥¢3ú@∑Ä4m≈÷@ÌWBÍN@ÌWQxVb‹60802036008@“‰Ekπå~@“≤y-w1ê@‘MÆ~˘€#@‘Q.~˘€#SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌWjƒDDD␀␀Î{Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ Timing analyses provide valuable insight into the relationship between AGN variability and the mechanisms contributing to X-ray emission from AGN. While recent studies have shed light on the nature of variations on short timescales, in general, the nature of the long-term X-ray variability and in particular its connection to the variability at shorter timescales have not been well-studied due to a lack of well-sampled data over a sufficiently wide range in timescale. We propose to combine short-timescale NuSTAR observations with the latest Swift Burst Alert Telescope (BAT) archival data on longer timescales to obtain the first well-sampled time variability characteristics in the hard X-rays for a sample of 4 AGN (2 Seyferts and 2 blazars) over timescales ranging from minutes to 15 years.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Mundo                         Sergio                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       INVESTIGATING THE HARD X-RAY VARIABILITY OF AGN WITH NUSTAR AND SWIFT-BAT                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215NEP_DTDF_MOS08009                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌWQxVb‹@ÌWR#Ü60810009003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌWj§˙O•␀␀Î{Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NEP_DTDF_MOS08009                                 @pLOv_ÿÆ@Ptâ†'RT@WˆÊ1¯†ê@@ C˛\ë—@SóAÚ◊s@ÌWR#Ü@ÌWuî»)¯60810009004@ÁBëRT`™@ÁÌ‡q@Ë“â(åÁ@Ë‘)(åÁSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌWè«L˝X␀␀Î{Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              JWST                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  202202152MASX_J11445486p1946349                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌWuî»)¯@ÌWv?r‘£60864003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌWèñH,␀␀Î{Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASX_J11445486p1946349                           @fÌ˙C˛]@3ƒìtºj@mH–Â`Aâ@R7nóçP@gØ∑È@ÌWv?r‘£@ÌWÑ[‰õø60864003002@”g˝∏QÏ@”2cÑM@‘€ÓåLò@‘›nåLòSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌW°‰±~K␀␀Î{Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215NGC_3516                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌWÑ[‰õø@ÌWÑÍN80901608001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌW°≈OC„␀␀ÏCHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀h´NGC 3516 is a remarkable Seyfert-1 galaxy, which over the last decade has undergone major spectral transformations, classifying it as a changing-look AGN. Understanding the nature and origin of spectral variability in such changing-look AGN would provide key insights into the accretion-outflow mechanism. Swift monitoring of NGC 3516 shows dramatic flux rises and declines during its changing-look state. We propose ToO observations with XMM, NuSTAR, and HST, triggered using Swift monitoring, to catch NGC 3516 at a high flux level to be able to carry out the first high resolution X-ray and UV spectroscopy of its winds during the changing-look state. The proposed observations would enable us to ascertain the role of winds in the transformation of NGC 3516.                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Mehdipour                     Missagh                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Catching changing-look NGC 3516 at a bright flux to establish the role of winds                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3516                                          @d–cä	@R#=»K^@`´_o“Û@E?Â…@u˝	‘ï+@ÌWÑÍN@ÌW´xVb‹80901608002@&z;dZ@Ô¯”kπå~@ÒEL*0U2@ÒF‹*0U2SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌW…√≤¢␀␀ÏCHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀h´NGC 3516 is a remarkable Seyfert-1 galaxy, which over the last decade has undergone major spectral transformations, classifying it as a changing-look AGN. Understanding the nature and origin of spectral variability in such changing-look AGN would provide key insights into the accretion-outflow mechanism. Swift monitoring of NGC 3516 shows dramatic flux rises and declines during its changing-look state. We propose ToO observations with XMM, NuSTAR, and HST, triggered using Swift monitoring, to catch NGC 3516 at a high flux level to be able to carry out the first high resolution X-ray and UV spectroscopy of its winds during the changing-look state. The proposed observations would enable us to ascertain the role of winds in the transformation of NGC 3516.                                      Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Mehdipour                     Missagh                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Catching changing-look NGC 3516 at a bright flux to establish the role of winds                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM HST                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20220215SN2023ixf                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌW´xVb‹@ÌW´ÍN90902520003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌW»·_Û›␀␀ÍƒHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2023ixf                                         @jZï*ô1@K)*0U2a@Yí¯7¥¢4@M‡ËßÊõ@?≠∏∫«À@ÌW´ÍN@ÌW…xVb‹90902520004@‰âz?@‰QüUõ=@ÂÒÈ≈÷8Ü@ÂÛ©≈÷8ÜSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌW‚GïŒ≤␀␀ÍƒHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/3) DDT                                                                                                               archived  20220215HB89_1257p346                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌW…xVb‹@ÌW…ÍN60861004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌW‚«ﬁ†␀␀ÎâHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÍThe primary X-ray emission observed in AGN is believed to be produced from a tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. A critical coronal compactness versus temperature threshold is predicted above which any increase in the source luminosity would then generate positron-electron pairs rather than continue heating the coronal plasma. Current observations show that all local AGNs populate the region below this critical line. However, these models have rarely been probed in the high-luminosity regime where the tightest constraints can be made on the coronal models. Here, we propose four high-luminosity quasars (z~1-2) to more than double the current sample size of high-luminosity quasars to further constrain the coronal models and thus better understand the physics of coronae.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE PROPERTIES OF AGN CORONAE USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS WITH NUSTAR                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HB89_1257p346                                     @h\F‹]d@A2¿Énò@[˙Ïø±[W@TùVlÙ!@-—4ÍJå@ÌW…ÍN@ÌW˝èFj60861004002@ÒbßÔù≤@Ò1LU2a|@Úê¶r∞ ≈@ÚëÊr∞ ≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌXZ’$ ␀␀ÎâHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÍThe primary X-ray emission observed in AGN is believed to be produced from a tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. A critical coronal compactness versus temperature threshold is predicted above which any increase in the source luminosity would then generate positron-electron pairs rather than continue heating the coronal plasma. Current observations show that all local AGNs populate the region below this critical line. However, these models have rarely been probed in the high-luminosity regime where the tightest constraints can be made on the coronal models. Here, we propose four high-luminosity quasars (z~1-2) to more than double the current sample size of high-luminosity quasars to further constrain the coronal models and thus better understand the physics of coronae.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE PROPERTIES OF AGN CORONAE USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS WITH NUSTAR                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215NEP_DTDF_MOS08008                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌW˝èFj@ÌW˝î»)¯60810008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌXË›}␀␀ÎÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NEP_DTDF_MOS08008                                 @pP€Ùá¸π@Po‡ﬁ“àŒ@Wº£@@ºŸË>B[@Sˆ˝!ˇ.@ÌW˝î»)¯@ÌX4?r‘£60810008002@Òÿπéäqﬁ@Ò´z¢3ú@ÛÓç∏∫«@Û>ç∏∫«SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌXB∆r(␀␀ÎÇHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÙThe JWST Deep Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP DTDF) is a GTO target that will have excellent 8-band deep (m~28) imaging and grism spectra in the NIR by JWST and already has exquisite multiwavelength data. As JWST is now at its L2 location, the NEP observations are planned to start in June/July and will repeat every 90 days. Given the interesting results from Cycle 5+6 NuSTAR programs, we request 855 ks (3 epochs simultaneous with JWST) for monitoring the NEP in the hard band to focus on variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, the obscured fraction, and a variable z>1 FSRQ. Combining Cycle 5+6+8 data, we will get the deepest NuSTAR survey, with 65-70 sources in 3-24 keV, and ~20 single epoch detections.    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       THE FIRST TIME DOMAIN NUSTAR AND JWST SURVEY                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215ABELL_1650                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌX4?r‘£@ÌX5èFj70860006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌXBÑ•[g␀␀ÎÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ Cosmological constraints using the abundance of galaxy clusters requires accurate mass estimates, which are proportional to temperature measurements of the gas. Unfortunately, the best sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. Deep NuSTAR observations of 4 relaxed clusters suggest lower temperatures than seen by Chandra in 3 cases, in better agreement with XMM, with the fourth cluster better matched to Chandra's temperature. Measurements of more clusters are needed to characterize the relations, identify and investigate outliers, and thereby reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose to quadruple the number of NuSTAR relaxed clusters to potentially solve this longstanding issue.                    Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: SOLVING THE CHANDRA--XMM-NEWTON TEMPERATURE DISCREPANCY                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ABELL_1650                                        @hS˚ÁlãDø¸{J#9¿Ï@s≠B√…Ô@Nó!-w1ê@v~M“Ò©¸@ÌX5èFj@ÌXCî»)¯70860006002@“
ıﬁi≠C@—‡XÅo␀i@”m‚É‰%Ø@”qbÉ‰%ØSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌXj››ﬁ␀␀ÎÇHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ Cosmological constraints using the abundance of galaxy clusters requires accurate mass estimates, which are proportional to temperature measurements of the gas. Unfortunately, the best sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. Deep NuSTAR observations of 4 relaxed clusters suggest lower temperatures than seen by Chandra in 3 cases, in better agreement with XMM, with the fourth cluster better matched to Chandra's temperature. Measurements of more clusters are needed to characterize the relations, identify and investigate outliers, and thereby reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose to quadruple the number of NuSTAR relaxed clusters to potentially solve this longstanding issue.                    Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: SOLVING THE CHANDRA--XMM-NEWTON TEMPERATURE DISCREPANCY                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215Serpens_X1                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXCî»)¯@ÌXDî»)¯30901004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌXi¸e§8␀␀ÎÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀Serpens X-1 is a persistently accreting neutron star low-mass X-ray binary that exhibits multiple emission line components in its reflection spectrum. The detection of multiple emission line components (e.g., Fe K and Fe L) in the reflection spectrum provides the opportunity to test different theories about where the emission arises in the disk and challenge our current understanding of accretion. Therefore, we request a 30 ks NICER observation with a contemporaneous 30 ks NuSTAR observation in order to test the emission radius of the Fe K line and lower-energy Fe L blend. We demonstrate that the requested observations are necessary to test the different ideas with respect to emission radii and enhance our understanding of accretion.                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ludlam                        Renee                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       UNVEILING THE ORIGIN OF FE L EMISSION IN SERPENS X-1                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Serpens_X1                                        @qÆÊ1¯°@Vá+J@B:í£S@ué!ñRΩ@[w¢úwök@ÌXDî»)¯@ÌX[±9Ò30901004002@ÿ*.kP∞Ú@ÿâÂ&Èx’@‡eM¢úwö@‡f-¢úwöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌXi…+<␀␀ÎÇHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀Serpens X-1 is a persistently accreting neutron star low-mass X-ray binary that exhibits multiple emission line components in its reflection spectrum. The detection of multiple emission line components (e.g., Fe K and Fe L) in the reflection spectrum provides the opportunity to test different theories about where the emission arises in the disk and challenge our current understanding of accretion. Therefore, we request a 30 ks NICER observation with a contemporaneous 30 ks NuSTAR observation in order to test the emission radius of the Fe K line and lower-energy Fe L blend. We demonstrate that the requested observations are necessary to test the different ideas with respect to emission radii and enhance our understanding of accretion.                                                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Ludlam                        Renee                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       UNVEILING THE ORIGIN OF FE L EMISSION IN SERPENS X-1                                                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215ABELL_3112                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌX[±9Ò@ÌX\±9Ò70860008001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌXi†TÙ>␀␀ÎÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ Cosmological constraints using the abundance of galaxy clusters requires accurate mass estimates, which are proportional to temperature measurements of the gas. Unfortunately, the best sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. Deep NuSTAR observations of 4 relaxed clusters suggest lower temperatures than seen by Chandra in 3 cases, in better agreement with XMM, with the fourth cluster better matched to Chandra's temperature. Measurements of more clusters are needed to characterize the relations, identify and investigate outliers, and thereby reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose to quadruple the number of NuSTAR relaxed clusters to potentially solve this longstanding issue.                    Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: SOLVING THE CHANDRA--XMM-NEWTON TEMPERATURE DISCREPANCY                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ABELL_3112                                        @HºcÒA \¿Fl"h	’@o¢¨1&È¿Lπå~($@U}càeîØ@ÌX\±9Ò@ÌXkÕ´∏170860008002@’©√v_ÿÆ@’j'>´6z@◊Ω„º”@◊}„º”SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌXâ˚rÍb␀␀ÎÇHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ Cosmological constraints using the abundance of galaxy clusters requires accurate mass estimates, which are proportional to temperature measurements of the gas. Unfortunately, the best sources for X-ray temperatures are Chandra and XMM, which systematically disagree with each other. Deep NuSTAR observations of 4 relaxed clusters suggest lower temperatures than seen by Chandra in 3 cases, in better agreement with XMM, with the fourth cluster better matched to Chandra's temperature. Measurements of more clusters are needed to characterize the relations, identify and investigate outliers, and thereby reduce the error budget on cluster-derived cosmological parameters. We propose to quadruple the number of NuSTAR relaxed clusters to potentially solve this longstanding issue.                    Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Wik                           Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       GALAXY CLUSTER TEMPERATURES: SOLVING THE CHANDRA--XMM-NEWTON TEMPERATURE DISCREPANCY                                    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215NGC4785                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXkÕ´∏1@ÌXlî»)¯60161523003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌXâŸ‚jÛ␀␀ÎÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                