¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌX{[‰õø@ÌX{ÍN90901318005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌXâ£æƒu␀␀ÍÀHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1626m67                                        @oS&¬¿Pﬂy¶µ@t¬¯7¥¢¿)‡Énóç@oN@∑Ä4n@ÌX{ÍN@ÌXã±9Ò90901318006@“	x‘˝Ù@—Áv¨1@”◊T ƒõ¶@”⁄‘ ƒõ¶SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌX™>‹∫ò␀␀ÍÀHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/4) DDT / idiagnostics                                                                                                archived  202202152MASX_J12382172p4212196                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXã±9Ò@ÌXåÕ´∏160864004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌX™Î$␀␀ÍÀHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503162MASX_J12382172p4212196                           @g±	kπå~@E ¿Éo@`ûí£S&@R°5®Xy>@!B©ì‡ﬂ@ÌXåÕ´∏1@ÌXõî»)¯60864004002@’(Ø@N§©@‘ÎY»K^@÷çBzêó@÷ç¬zêóSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌX™ªZ•␀␀ÍÀHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ +Post-merger galaxies are highly disturbed, coalesced mergers featuring shells and tidal tails, expected to host LLAGN. With deep Chandra/XMM data, we found a high X-ray AGN fraction of ~62% in a sample of 79 post mergers (0.02<z<0.06, logM>10.5) with L~10^41.5 erg/s in 2-10keV. As strong obscuration is expected to be present in late-stage mergers, these LLAGN could be highly obscured which are intrinsically luminous. While the true nature of these LLAGN remains ambiguous with Chandra/XMM data, NuSTAR hard X-ray data can distinguish the obscured luminous AGN from truly low-luminosity AGN. We request 205 ks of NuSTAR time for a well selected sample of 10 Chandra/XMM detected post mergers. Together with the archive data, this work will improve our knowledge of the history of AGN in mergers.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Li                            Wenhao                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       A NUSTAR VIEW OF AGN IN POST-MERGER GALAXIES                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20220215GRS_1915p105                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXõî»)¯@ÌXú[‰õø80801342001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌX©Ê5⁄’␀␀ÎÇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ 	Despite ~50 years of compact object studies in the Galaxy, the physics of relativistic jet launching remains an active and open field of research. Luckily, with the recent successful launch of JWST, a new parameter space is opening up in the field of jet-physics, allowing for the study of the spectral break above which the jet becomes optically thin on sub-second time scales, providing a crucial link between light and plasma properties. We are proposing to use the unique combination of high time resolution and hard X-ray coverage of NuSTAR to study the outburst of two BH-LMXBs in a jet-dominated hard state. We will probe evolving, rapid, sub-second variations, and, for the first time ever, we will search for correlated variability with mid-infrared spectral observations with JWST.         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Shaw                          Aarran                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING RAPID VARIABILITY IN BLACK HOLE X-RAY BINARY JETS                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRS_1915p105                                      @r∑Ä4m∆@%˛“àŒp;@F†F‹]d?ôe+”√a@[ë®Xy=Ÿ@ÌXú[‰õø@ÌXÆ?r‘£80801342002@’wtò_˜@’P¨~($@◊∏Óqﬁi≠@◊ºÓqﬁi≠SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌX -À©á␀␀ÎÇHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ 	Despite ~50 years of compact object studies in the Galaxy, the physics of relativistic jet launching remains an active and open field of research. Luckily, with the recent successful launch of JWST, a new parameter space is opening up in the field of jet-physics, allowing for the study of the spectral break above which the jet becomes optically thin on sub-second time scales, providing a crucial link between light and plasma properties. We are proposing to use the unique combination of high time resolution and hard X-ray coverage of NuSTAR to study the outburst of two BH-LMXBs in a jet-dominated hard state. We will probe evolving, rapid, sub-second variations, and, for the first time ever, we will search for correlated variability with mid-infrared spectral observations with JWST.         Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Shaw                          Aarran                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING RAPID VARIABILITY IN BLACK HOLE X-RAY BINARY JETS                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              JWST                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀ToO / idiagnostics                                                                                                      archived  20220215HB89_1257p346                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXÆ?r‘£@ÌXØ#Ü60861004003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌX 
U∂l␀␀ÎâHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ÍThe primary X-ray emission observed in AGN is believed to be produced from a tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. A critical coronal compactness versus temperature threshold is predicted above which any increase in the source luminosity would then generate positron-electron pairs rather than continue heating the coronal plasma. Current observations show that all local AGNs populate the region below this critical line. However, these models have rarely been probed in the high-luminosity regime where the tightest constraints can be made on the coronal models. Here, we propose four high-luminosity quasars (z~1-2) to more than double the current sample size of high-luminosity quasars to further constrain the coronal models and thus better understand the physics of coronae.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE PROPERTIES OF AGN CORONAE USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS WITH NUSTAR                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316HB89_1257p346                                     @h\ò_ˆî@A1†'RTa@[Úﬁ␀—∑@Tù∞â†'R@$…ì‡ﬁ”@ÌXØ#Ü@ÌXÒ#Ü60861004004@Ù‘ô≤-V@ÙömÈ·∞ä@ˆW&ﬂ;dZ@ˆY6ﬂ;dZSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌYúäÂ␀␀ÎâHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ÍThe primary X-ray emission observed in AGN is believed to be produced from a tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. A critical coronal compactness versus temperature threshold is predicted above which any increase in the source luminosity would then generate positron-electron pairs rather than continue heating the coronal plasma. Current observations show that all local AGNs populate the region below this critical line. However, these models have rarely been probed in the high-luminosity regime where the tightest constraints can be made on the coronal models. Here, we propose four high-luminosity quasars (z~1-2) to more than double the current sample size of high-luminosity quasars to further constrain the coronal models and thus better understand the physics of coronae.     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       CONSTRAINING THE PROPERTIES OF AGN CORONAE USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS WITH NUSTAR                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(2/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20220215SN2023ixf                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌXÒ#Ü@ÌXÒî»)¯90902520005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌYT2Ó␀␀Í“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                        