e (PWNe) combined with available multi-wavelength data have revealed the energetic nature of these sources which are believed to produce a significant fraction of cosmic rays (CRs). Through multiwavelength investigations, we are able to constrain several physical properties for the PWN in order to determine the underlying particle characteristics. We request to observe an evolved PWN, located in the Large Magellanic Cloud and belonging to the composite SNR B0453-685, where Fermi-LAT gamma-ray emission has recently been discovered. The proposed observation will enable an accurate characterization of the synchrotron cut-off regime for the PWN through broadband modeling.                                                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Eagle                         Jordan                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       BROADBAND INVESTIGATION OF AN EVOLVED PULSAR WIND NEBULA IN THE LARGE MAGELLANIC CLOUD                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20220215B0453m685                                         @RU4ÍJå¿Qê-‡@q|*ô0æ¿AÊœAÚ◊@Qí´6zë@ÌY?ÍN@ÌYÇ?r‘£40901001002A–ÔÒué"A¶äÉnòA*è'ª/ÏA+◊'ª/ÏSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌY¢Å;ã0␀␀ÎâHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#KX-ray studies for the majority of known pulsar wind nebulae (PWNe) combined with available multi-wavelength data have revealed the energetic nature of these sources which are believed to produce a significant fraction of cosmic rays (CRs). Through multiwavelength investigations, we are able to constrain several physical properties for the PWN in order to determine the underlying particle characteristics. We request to observe an evolved PWN, located in the Large Magellanic Cloud and belonging to the composite SNR B0453-685, where Fermi-LAT gamma-ray emission has recently been discovered. The proposed observation will enable an accurate characterization of the synchrotron cut-off regime for the PWN through broadband modeling.                                                                  Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Eagle                         Jordan                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       BROADBAND INVESTIGATION OF AN EVOLVED PULSAR WIND NEBULA IN THE LARGE MAGELLANIC CLOUD                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀idiagnostics                                                                                                            archived  202305302MASXJ09023729m4813339                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌYÇ?r‘£@ÌYÇ±9Ò60160345001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌY¢1ñê·␀␀Í“Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                            