imited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1626m67                                        @o|Ìëhs¿P‡eîØO@tb∂Æ}V¿)ﬁ\ë—N<@qe+”√@ÌZ†#Ü@ÌZÆ#Ü90901318008@’ŒÈ+”√a@’¢‚lÙ!@◊¢Wæv»¥@◊¢Wæv»¥SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌZ√ûoÄí␀␀Í‡Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/4) DDT                                                                                                               archived  20230530NGC4074                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌZÆ#Ü@ÌZØèFj60061220001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌZ√~KÂ␀␀Í‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC4074                                           @f¢-V@4MøHÀí@n2@∑Ä4n@SEIQÇ©ì@vIX‚e,@ÌZØèFj@ÌZøÕ´∏160061220002@’©˜ã¨q@’tÃÚ{≤ˇ@◊{;dZ@◊|õ;dZSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌZ√f5⁄’␀␀Í‡Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530MS_1221d8p2452                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌZøÕ´∏1@ÌZ¿èFj60962005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌZ„ÄH—Z␀␀ÎòHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six objects that had only previously been detected in flaring states. We request NuSTAR observations of six of these extreme BL Lacs to characterize their synchrotron emission.                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Errando                       Manel                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A SURVEY OF HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM NEW TEV-PEAKED BL LACS                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MS_1221d8p2452                                    @gAp£◊
=@8ó€Ùá¸π@m␀e+”√@TÀ)^û	@vV≈m\˙≠@ÌZ¿èFj@ÌZ˚ÍN60962005002@Û˚êº£@Û…<Lò_@ıyíŸb∑@ız¢Ÿb∑SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì[Ëı¬è␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six objects that had only previously been detected in flaring states. We request NuSTAR observations of six of these extreme BL Lacs to characterize their synchrotron emission.                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Errando                       Manel                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A SURVEY OF HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM NEW TEV-PEAKED BL LACS                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202305301ES_1927p654                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌZ˚ÍN@ÌZ¸î»)¯80902632003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì[§ô8Ç␀␀Î‹Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#}Large-scale time-domain surveys have lead to the identification of new types of extreme variability in active galaxies, called changing look active galactic nuclei (CL-AGNs). Drastic changes in coronal emission have been seen in CL-AGNs at different timescales (factor of 10-100 in flux and spectral slope ($\Gamma=1-5$) in a matter of months/years), thus providing an ideal laboratory to understand the fundamental physics behind coronal emission. Here we propose to observe a CL-AGN with Nustar for 40ks, once every month for 4 months, to measure the X-ray cut-off energy, which may be evolving during the highly variable state. We will therefore be able to constrain any changes in the thermal/non-thermal fraction in the coronal plasma in real time, for the first time.                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Laha                          Sibasish                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TRACKING THE EVOLUTION OF CORONAL PROPERTIES IN A CHANGING LOOK AGN                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161ES_1927p654                                      @r<ï*ô1@PfkP∞Ú|@X=p£◊
