energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ARTXCJ1801d8                                      @pÂ«À)_@P´HÀí:@X!Vì@=™Á’fœB@NDá¸π#£@Ì[xVb‹@Ì[A#Ü60963006002@Ë	¯µ‹∆?@ÁÀ‡ﬁ␀—∑@Èæ€qué"@Èøªqué"SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì[}\∫òv␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀?␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀data gap 6207s                                                                                                          archived  202305302MASXJ02013241p6824219                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì[A#Ü@Ì[Aî»)¯60161085001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì[}$h¨Ò␀␀Í‡Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                        