               Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀Ã␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀data gaps 1228s                                                                                                         archived  20230530Swift_J0243d6p6124                                @DÜÀ˚µt@N∫˛≈m\˚@`˙uˆ˝!ˇ?Ù÷°a‰˜f@e√a‰˜e˛@Ì[PÕ´∏1@Ì[bxVb‹90901321002@«R¿¡T…Ü@»<≠B√ @◊oI“Ò©¸@◊pI“Ò©¸SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì[Çq˜®␀␀Í‡Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀+*␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀DDT / data gaps 11050s                                                                                                  archived  20230530Mrk_477                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì[bxVb‹@Ì[c#Ü60802020009␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì[ÇM-„Ô␀␀ÎòHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_477                                           @kÇ}øHÄ@J¿h€ã¨q@WWökP∞Ú@Lp©*0U@,⁄QŒ_@Ì[c#Ü@Ì[qÕ´∏160802020010@’uôÅ$›@’>ØÀí:*@◊:≈m\˚@◊˙≈m\˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì[Ç6`∂␀␀ÎòHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀æActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are powered by accreting supermassive black holes, surrounded by a torus of obscuring material. Recent observations have detected variability in the line of sight column density, giving rise to the 'patchy torus' hypothesis. Other observations, however, fail to find the variability expected from a patchy torus in Sy2 galaxies. Here, we propose to monitor a Sy2 galaxy, with confirmed yearly column density variability, but that shows no variability in timescales of days. We need additional observations on timescales of months to properly constrain the obscurer properties. The number of sources for which a monitoring campaign (i.e. > two observations) has shown variability is only a handful, making the addition of every possible source vital.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Torres-Alba                   Nuria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       PROBING AGN OBSCURATION VARIABILITY WITH NUSTAR AND NICER                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(5/5)                                                                                                                   archived  20230530WISEJ1634p7031                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì[qÕ´∏1@Ì[r#Ü60961001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì[ÇUUU␀␀ÎüHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ﬁThe X-ray emission from AGN is believed to be generated from the tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. The cutoff energy in the AGN X-ray spectrum (at a few hundred keV) can be used to probe the coronal properties. However, only lower limits to the cutoff energy of low-redshift AGN were measured due to the limited bandpass (up to 80 keV). High-z quasars, instead, are the best targets to constrain the cutoff energy thanks to the cosmological redshifting. So far, only four z>1 quasars have their cutoff energy constrained. Here, we propose other four z>1 quasars to increase the current sample size to statistically constrain the AGN coronal properties and thus better understand the physics of coronal Comptonization.                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SYSTEMATICALLY CONSTRAINING THE AGN CORONAL PROPERTIES WITH NUSTAR USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WISEJ1634p7031                                    @o&Èx‘˛@Q£MjO@YºN§®¡U@BR¿Énò@BŒîFsÅÿ@Ì[r#Ü@Ì[®ÍN60961001002@Û¨¬Ç@∑Ä@ÛyﬂÈ˘r@ıgAâ7L@ı◊Aâ7LSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì[¬5¶‡␀␀ÎüHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ﬁThe X-ray emission from AGN is believed to be generated from the tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. The cutoff energy in the AGN X-ray spectrum (at a few hundred keV) can be used to probe the coronal properties. However, only lower limits to the cutoff energy of low-redshift AGN were measured due to the limited bandpass (up to 80 keV). High-z quasars, instead, are the best targets to constrain the cutoff energy thanks to the cosmological redshifting. So far, only four z>1 quasars have their cutoff energy constrained. Here, we propose other four z>1 quasars to increase the current sample size to statistically constrain the AGN coronal properties and thus better understand the physics of coronal Comptonization.                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SYSTEMATICALLY CONSTRAINING THE AGN CORONAL PROPERTIES WITH NUSTAR USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀M-class solar flare                                                                                                     archived  202305301ES_1028p511                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì[®ÍN@Ì[©xVb‹60962001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì[¡Ò«r␀␀ÎüHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six ob