ot limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(4/4)                                                                                                                   archived  20230530PG1448p273                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì\\Õ´∏1@Ì\]±9Ò60902001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì\i∂ùj␀␀ÏâHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀gyPG1448+273 is a luminous NLS1, thought to be accreting at the Eddington limit. An XMM observation in 2017 discovered an ultra fast outflow (v=0.1c) in PG1448, via a deep Fe absorption trough at 7.5 keV. A 2022 NuSTAR observation then revealed a drastic increase in wind velocity, to 0.3c, coincident with a pronounced drop in flux. Here we wish to further understand the extreme variability of the disk wind, through 3x80ks and 3x50ks simultaneous XMM and NuSTAR observations, to determine the wind changes in velocity and outflow rate vs flux. A 40x1ks Swift daily monitoring campaign is proposed to monitor the UV to X-ray SED over the XMM window, to help determine how the wind reacts to the continuum. The observations will test the physics of accretion disk winds near the Eddington limit.      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Reeves                        James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Reaching the limit: the variable disk wind of PG 1448+273                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316PG1448p273                                        @k◊ù≤-V@;'K∆ßÔû@D	∫^5?}@O⁄í£S&@Ï"h	‘ï@Ì\]±9Ò@Ì\Ñ#Ü60902001002@Èo¨ŸË>@È-[º@Îd–ÎÌ˙D@ÎfêÎÌ˙DSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì\±O§˙P␀␀ÏâHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀gyPG1448+273 is a luminous NLS1, thought to be accreting at the Eddington limit. An XMM observation in 2017 discovered an ultra fast outflow (v=0.1c) in PG1448, via a deep Fe absorption trough at 7.5 keV. A 2022 NuSTAR observation then revealed a drastic increase in wind velocity, to 0.3c, coincident with a pronounced drop in flux. Here we wish to further understand the extreme variability of the disk wind, through 3x80ks and 3x50ks simultaneous XMM and NuSTAR observations, to determine the wind changes in velocity and outflow rate vs flux. A 40x1ks Swift daily monitoring campaign is proposed to monitor the UV to X-ray SED over the XMM window, to help determine how the wi