¶L/É@q¬j~˘€#ø˙|Ìëhr∞@8‡uˆ˝"@Ì]Õ´∏1@Ì]î»)¯30901016001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì]*v#¶␀␀Î¶Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#àWe propose contemporaneous 60 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations of the HMGB 1FGL J1018.6-5856 during the sharp decline of the pre-periastron keV--TeV ``spike'' of its 16.5 day orbit. With the first combined NuSTAR+XMM-Newton observations, we will monitor the evolution of the 0.7-30 keV spectral shape from the peak of the spike to after its decay to discriminate between the synchrotron or IC losses driving its extreme physical conditions and search for signatures of a NS or BH using the excellent timing resolution thanks to both NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. Thanks to their broadband capabilities, large effective areas and superior timing resolution, our observations of J1018 will reveal crucial insights on physical mechanisms driving emission in the HMGB population.                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Coley                         Joel                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       EXTREME PARTICLE ACCELERATION IN THE GAMMA-RAY BINARY 1FGL J1018.6-5856                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161FGL_J1018d6m5856                                 @cU˘rGE¿Mx7¥¢3ú@q¬£S&ø˙´6zêó@8≠qué"@Ì]î»)¯@Ì];±9Ò30901016002@Ì8º4m≈÷@ÏÓØˆ+jË@Ôgæ`™d√@Ôi`™d√SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì]Q” Ü␀␀Î¶Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#àWe propose contemporaneous 60 ks NuSTAR and 30 ks XMM-Newton observations of the HMGB 1FGL J1018.6-5856 during the sharp decline of the pre-periastron keV--TeV ``spike'' of its 16.5 day orbit. With the first combined NuSTAR+XMM-Newton observations, we will monitor the evolution of the 0.7-30 keV spectral shape from the peak of the spike to after its decay to discriminate between the synchrotron or IC losses driving its extreme physical conditions and search for signatures of a NS or BH using the excellent timing resolution thanks to both NuSTAR and XMM-Newton. Thanks to their broadband capabilities, large effective areas and superior timing resolution, our observations of J1018 will reveal crucial insights on physical mechanisms driving emission in the HMGB population.                     Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Coley                         Joel                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       EXTREME PARTICLE ACCELERATION IN THE GAMMA-RAY BINARY 1FGL J1018.6-5856                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀M-class solar flares                                                                                                    archived  20230530IGRJ09523m6231                                    @böu%¿OB3úæ‡@q∫Ø∑È˘¿Ôù≤-V@>˝óˆ+jË@Ì];±9Ò@Ì];ÍN60160376001@U’N;Õ5®@Uóù≤-V@W@␀␀␀␀␀@W@␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì]Q†$h≠␀␀ÍıHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530IGRJ09523m6231                                    @b_o“Û¿OBﬁ␀—∑@q∫Õ5®Xy¿â†'RT@?>äqﬁi≠@Ì];ÍN@Ì]K±9Ò60160376002@€kπå~@⁄ŸRûö@›L‘â†'R@›O‘â†'RSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì]hî %␀␀ÍıHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530J031038d2m73493                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì]K±9Ò@Ì]L#Ü50901002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì]hq«r␀␀Î™Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$"We request a 50 ks NuSTAR, and 25 ks XMM-Newton, joint observation to perform a broad-band X-ray timing and spectral study of eRASSU J031038.2-73493, an enigmatic object located in the MCB. The high throughput NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations will allow us to perform detailed spectral and temporal analysis of the source and reveal for the first time its nature by combining information from optical spectroscopic observation. This is also pivotal in order to understand the tidal interactions between the Large Magellanic Cloud and the Small Magellanic Cloud and trace the history of the Local Galaxy group.                                                                                                                                                                                             Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Maitra                        Chandreyee                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       INVESTIGATING THE NATURE OF THE ENIGMATIC OBJECT ERASSU J031038.2-73493 IN THE MAGELLANIC BRIDGE                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316J031038d2m73493                                   @G÷Ê1¯†ê¿Rre+”√@r/ÍJåM¿CÓfffff@_;v_ÿ≠¨@Ì]L#Ü@Ì]j?r‘£50901002002@Í®]Ì˙C˛@Íd3∏QÏ@Ï∞Aâ7L@Ï±ﬁAâ7LSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì]à¯˚‘␀␀Î™Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$"We request a 50 ks NuSTAR, and 25 ks XMM-Newton, joint observation to perform a broad-band X-ray timing and spectral study of eRASSU J031038.2-73493, an enigmatic object located in the MCB. The high throughput NuSTAR and XMM-Newton observations will allow us to perform detailed spectral and temporal analysis of the source and reveal for the first time its nature by combining information from optical spectroscopic observation. This is also pivotal in order to understand the tidal interactions between the Large Magellanic Cloud and the Small Magellanic Cloud and trace the history of the Local Galaxy group.                                                                                                                                                                                             Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Maitra                        Chandreyee                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       INVESTIGATING THE NATURE OF THE ENIGMATIC OBJECT ERASSU J031038.2-73493 IN THE MAGELLANIC BRIDGE                      