ets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              JWST-NIRCAM                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20230530PSR_J0058m7218                                    @-p ƒõ•„¿R"–Â`B@r„ïÈ·∞ä¿FYá+@hÚ‘,<ûÌ@Ìb´î»)¯@Ìb¨?r‘£40901003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìb…≥ß¢␀␀Î’Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ãWe request a 100 ks observation to perform a broad-band X-ray timing and spectral study of PSR J0058-7218. Studying a young, energetic and ultra-fast pulsar like PSR J0058-7218 provides a unique opportunity to probe the braking mechanisms and birth-spin models of rotation-powered pulsars. X-ray is the only wavelength that can shed light into the properties of the unique object, and the observation with NuSTAR will enable us to study the magnetospheric emission properties of the source in the hard X-ray band for the first time. Our NICER monitoring of the target will allow to determine the hard X-ray pulse profile with high precision, and determine the pulse profile alignment between the soft and hard X-rays.                                                                                   Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Maitra                        Chandreyee                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       INVESTIGATING THE YOUNG AND ENERGETIC PULSAR PSR J0058-7218 THROUGH THE HARD X-RAY EYES OF NUSTAR                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PSR_J0058m7218                                    @-i·∞â†'¿R◊s¸P@r„™d¬¯8¿FY∞â†'R@i	GÆz·@Ìb¨?r‘£@Ìb—xVb‹40901003002@ÈÄîFsÇ@È@r‰éär@ÎÑKœ™Õü@ÎÖÀœ™ÕüSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌbÍÔhK⁄␀␀Î’Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ãWe request a 100 ks observation to perform a broad-band X-ray timing and spectral study of PSR J0058-7218. Studying a young, energetic and ultra-fast pulsar like PSR J0058-7218 provides a unique opportunity to probe the braking mechanisms and birth-spin models of rotation-powered pulsars. X-ray is the only wavelength that can shed light into the properties of the unique object, and the observation with NuSTAR will enable us to study the magnetospheric emission properties of the source in the hard X-ray band for the first time. Our NICER monitoring of the target will allow to determine th