itor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀	CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Crab_sl_PA150                                     @U\«‚Ç@∏@6ËËßÊõ@gÈx‘˝Ù¿®¡T…Ö@b§}VlÙ@Ìdﬁî»)¯@ÌdÈèFj10902306002@Ã¿ZÖáìﬁ@Ãy‚ÎC@œÍ&sÅ◊‹@œÍ&sÅ◊‹SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìeÿéô˚␀␀Î-Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀	CAL       NuSTAR calibration campaign                                                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀straylight calibration                                                                                                  archived  20230530IRAS_06269m0543                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌdÈèFj@ÌdÈxVb‹60901030001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìe∫·GÆ␀␀ÎÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$%IRAS 06269-0543 is a flat-spectrum radio-loud (RL) narrow-line Seyfert 1 galaxy (NLS1) that is classified as an ultra-luminous infrared galaxy in the final stages of a merging event. As such, it is a prime candidate in which to explore the existence of a low- or intermediate-spin black hole (a<0.9), yet it has never benefited from a deep exposure. We request a simultaneous NuSTAR (150 ks) + XMM (120 ks) observation to distinguish (>99.9% confidence) two competing physical scenarios, facilitate the precise measurement (within 10\%) of spin, and enable tests that explore black hole spin evolution. This observation will make a significant contribution to the under-sampled class of RL-NLS1s, providing important broadband information on the properties of this interesting type of active galaxy. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Gonzalez                      Adam                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MEASURING BLACK HOLE SPIN IN THE RL-NLS1 ULIRG IRAS 06269-0543                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316IRAS_06269m0543                                   @XYûÉ‰%Ø¿Ÿá+@jÌJÙÿE¿ƒg8}ø@bì€"–Â`@ÌdÈxVb‹@ÌeG[‰õø60901030002@ˇ\‡)«yß@ˇlSéÛMA␀‚ôˆîFtA␀„ôˆîFtSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌefUŒ≤@␀␀ÎÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$%IRAS 06269-0543 is a flat-spectrum radio-loud (RL) narrow-line Seyfert 1 galaxy (NLS1) that is classified as an ultra-luminous infrared galaxy in the final stages of a merging event. As such, it is a prime candidate in which to explore the existence of a low- or intermediate-spin black hole (a<0.9), yet it has never benefited from a deep exposure. We request a simultaneous NuSTAR (150 ks) + XMM (120 ks) observation to distinguish (>99.9% confidence) two competing physical scenarios, facilitate the precise measurement (within 10\%) of spin, and enable tests that explore black hole spin evolution. This observation will make a significant contribution to the under-sampled class of RL-NLS1s, providing important broadband information on the properties of this interesting type of active galaxy. Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Gonzalez                      Adam                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MEASURING BLACK HOLE SPIN IN THE RL-NLS1 ULIRG IRAS 06269-0543                                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                           XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀G2 CME                                                                                                                  archived  20230530SwiftJ1727d8m1613                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌeG[‰õø@ÌeHî»)¯80901321001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌeeÓ,¿™␀␀ÎÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀;We propose monitoring two future, previously unobserved BH transients with fluxes above 50 mCrab by dedicating 45 ks of NICER time over 15 observations spread throughout each of the two outbursts. By pairing a 30 ks NuSTAR observation with a NICER observation, we can obtain a precise spin measurement for each BH. The NICER monitoring campaign will probe the effects of the evolution of the outburst on the spin measurement. This project will test the assumptions and hypotheses of BH spin measurements in X-ray binaries, and will serve as a legacy for NICER and NuSTAR. In the future, this treatment will maximize the number of new BHs observed and, together with gravitational wave observations, will allow developing a unifying understanding of stellar-mass black hole formation and evolution.   Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Draghis                       Paul                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       TRACKING TWO FUTURE BLACK HOLE OUTBURST WITH NICER AND NUSTAR                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                 