   ␀	R05       THE FIRST X-RAY POLARIMETRY AND SPECTROSCOPY OBSERVATIONS OF TRANSIENT BLACK HOLE X-RAY BINARIES                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SwiftJ1727d8m1613                                 @p^z·GÆ¿0=≈÷8ÜY@ ·$›/@%—N;Õ6@ujOv@ÌeÜî»)¯@Ìeà#Ü80902313006@v¡S&¡æ@xè¥¢3ú@©EÄ␀␀␀␀@©EÄ␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìe£—Aú¢␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀©We propose 3 joint NICER+NuSTAR ToOs observations of transient black hole X-ray binaries (TBHXBs), to be taken in coordination with the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer(IXPE), a NASA-ASI mission launched on December 9, 2021. As the first year, the second year IXPE observing plan includes 3 ToOs of TBHXB in outburst. Each ToO includes one long (# 300 ks) exposure in the hard state and another in the soft state. In fact, until now IXPE has not had an opportunity to observe a complete outburst of a TBHXB because of the lack of bright standard outbursts occurred within the visibility windows. We propose (for each ToO) two 30 ks NICER+NuSTAR exposures during the hard state IXPE observation and one 20 ks NICER+NuSTAR exposure during the soft state IXPE observation. This will provide, as demonsProposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Capitanio                     Fiamma                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       THE FIRST X-RAY POLARIMETRY AND SPECTROSCOPY OBSERVATIONS OF TRANSIENT BLACK HOLE X-RAY BINARIES                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1c/2) ToO                                                                                                              archived  20230530ARTXCJ1742d3                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìeà#Ü@ÌeàÍN60963002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìe£ºM^p␀␀ÎÍHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ARTXCJ1742d3                                      @pó€Ùá¸π@OÈƒ2 W®@WP˝Û∂E¢@?«RT`™e@uë—N;Õ@ÌeàÍN@Ìeõ±9Ò60963002002@‹eŒΩ<6@‹KK∆ß@ﬁk‰…Öo@ﬁlt…ÖoSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìe£¢õ␀␀ÎÍHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530Mrk_876                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìeõ±9Ò@ÌeúèFj60901028001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìe£}åµ␀␀ÎÚHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$AGNs are powered by their supermassive black hole. Its spin is an important property for the understanding of the accretion in AGNs. However, the spin measurement is not straightforward requiring long observations and the knowledge in advance that the SMBH is actually spinning. Only approximately a dozens of measurements performed at high energies have fully constrained spin values. Recent observations of Mrk 876 report evidence that its SMBH is spinning. The fortunate geometry of the sources and its properties allow for an exceptionally clean view on the active nucleus, which facilitates the spin measurement due to the Compton hump above 10 keV and the skewed Fe-line system. We propose to perform this important measurement by observing Mrk 876 for 200 ks with NuSTAR.                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bottacini                     Eugenio                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MEASURING THE SPIN OF A SUPERMASSIVE BLACK HOLE WITH NUSTAR                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Mrk_876                                           @nmÑM;@Pk
