                     NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNVEILING A POTENTIAL HARD X-RAY COUNTERPART TO THE UNIDENTIFIED TEV SOURCE H.E.S.S. J1626-490                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀SL / CME Kp=5 / C-class solar flares                                                                                    archived  202305304Cp23d42                                          @mÕŒ_p@7∆Èx‘˝Ù@Cb◊s¸@HòU2a|@sÒ`™d¬¯@Ìf|?r‘£@Ìf}èFj60160625001@4I†'RTa@48y=Ÿc@5¿␀␀␀␀␀@5¿␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌfÇ+ù␀␀Î;Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202305304Cp23d42                                          @mÃÍJåM@7ƒÍJåM@C`U2a|@HòæÌ(ç@sÓ6‚Î@Ìf}èFj@Ìfãî»)¯60160625002@‘mÂÖoi@‘=ƒãCïÅ@’·Fäqﬁj@’·FäqﬁjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìf©PW;␀␀Î;Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=5                                                                                                                archived  20230530Arp_220                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìfãî»)¯@ÌfãÕ´∏160901022001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìf©.]L;␀␀Î˘Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÌWe propose a new (180 ks) NuSTAR observation of Arp 220W, a candidate of the most heavily obscured AGN among local ultra-/luminous infrared galaxies (U/LIRGs). Its central engine (AGN or starburst) has been long debated to understand the nuclear environment in the final phase of mergers. Although multiwavelength studies imply the presence of an AGN, it remains unclear because of the extreme obscuration (NH~10^25-10^26 cm^-2). The previous 60 ks NuSTAR data only marginally detected the source above 8 keV. The proposed observation will detect the 10-40 keV AGN emission at >5 sigma significance, which is crucial to distinguish from a starburst origin. This observation will first resolve the true energy source of Arp 220W, which cannot be done in any other wavelengths.                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Yamada                        Satoshi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       RESOLVING THE LONGSTANDING MYSTERY OF THE EXTREMELY OBSCURED NUCLEUS IN ARP 220W                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Arp_220                                           @m6Ñµ‹∆?@7rÎC,•@BFÏø±[W@J§µ‹∆?@s• ƒõ•„@ÌfãÕ´∏1@Ìg#Ü60901022002AFˆ‡Aâ7AÌ≈÷9AÏ§pçπAÌpçπSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìg*=@F␀␀Î˘Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ÌWe propose a new (180 ks) NuSTAR observation of Arp 220W, a candidate of the most heavily obscured AGN among local ultra-/luminous infrared galaxies (U/LIRGs). Its central engine (AGN or starburst) has been long debated to understand the nuclear environment in the final phase of mergers. Although multiwavelength studies imply the presence of an AGN, it remains unclear because of the extreme obscuration (NH~10^25-10^26 cm^-2). The previous 60 ks NuSTAR data only marginally detected the source above 8 keV. The proposed observation will detect the 10-40 keV AGN emission at >5 sigma significance, which is crucial to distinguish from a starburst origin. This observation will first resolve the true energy source of Arp 220W, which cannot be done in any other wavelengths.                         Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Yamada                        Satoshi                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       RESOLVING THE LONGSTANDING MYSTERY OF THE EXTREMELY OBSCURED NUCLEUS IN ARP 220W                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=5                                                                                                                archived  20230530Mrk_590                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìg#Ü@Ìg[‰õø80802652001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìg)ªªªº␀␀ÏõHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ )After a 10-year hiatus, Mrk 590 has partially re-ignited, with repeated major X-ray and UV flare-ups captured by Swift monitoring. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document the onset of AGN activity as it occurs, since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We will use approved Swift XRT monitoring to trigger up to 4 single-visit NuSTAR observations, of which up to 3 are joint with XMM (or NICER). Together, these data are needed to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on (and then characterize this event) or if alternate physics are at play.                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Lawther                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       TOO OBSERVATIONS OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: EXPLORING THE PHYSICS OF CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316Mrk_590                                           @@’‹∆?øÁsÍ≥g†˘@de÷8ÜYK¿Lìúæﬂ§@d.◊s¸P@Ìg[‰õø@Ìg ?r‘£80802652002@‘V<õ•„T@‘$ì”√a@’ª>p:˚@’ª>p:˚SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌgJeC!␀␀ÏõHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀ )After a 10-year hiatus, Mrk 590 has partially re-ignited, with repeated major X-ray and UV flare-ups captured by Swift monitoring. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document the onset of AGN activity as it occurs, since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We will use approved Swift XRT monitoring to trigger up to 4 single-visit NuSTAR observations, of which up to 3 are joint with XMM (or NICER). Together, these data are needed to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on (and then characterize this event) or if alternate physics are at play.                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Lawther                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       TOO OBSERVATIONS OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: EXPLORING THE PHYSICS OF CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/4)                                                                                                                   processed 20230530PKS_0335m122                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìg ?r‘£@Ìg î»)¯60901027001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌgIÊfff␀␀Î˘Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ˇWe have selected 3 of the remaining high-latitude, 3<z<4 redshift blazars present in the 4LAC catalog that lack NuSTAR observations. These blazars are very likely to be powerful MeV blazars. MeV blazars can be used to constrain the space density of black holes in the early Universe, as well as test how early galaxy interactions resulted in the formation of relativistic jets. NuSTAR observations of these sources will enable us to detect the proposed sources in the hard X-ray band, accurately measure the spectral shape and flux in this band, and sample the onset of the inverse Compton emission. These properties are fundamental to determine the power of the jet and to derive the shape of the underlying electron population responsible for the emission.                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    J