3d77p4414                              @\[]ÃcÒA@F$õ•„S¯@e∆ø±[W?@9áX‚e@dê¡T…Ö@Ìh~Õ´∏1@ÌhÄèFj60960006001@/X«‚Ç@∏@.–Hé@1␀␀␀␀␀␀@1␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìh©'qf␀␀Ï␀Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ÖThe X-ray spectral analysis of new Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing a previously successful machine learning algorithm, we propose to target 6 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the BAT 150-Month catalog for 30 ks of NuSTAR and 25 ks of XMM-Newton time each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DISCOVERING COMPTON-THICK AGN WITH A.I.                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530SDSS_J073323d77p4414                              @\YÈ·∞â†@F"˚~êˇó@e«≤ïÈ‚@9ÇGE8Ô5@dèıY≥–}@ÌhÄèFj@ÌhïxVb‹60960006002@‹*?Êö‘@€Ó€üæw@ﬁh6«À)@ﬁiˆ«À)SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìh®Ì¸5␀␀Ï␀Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ÖThe X-ray spectral analysis of new Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing a previously successful machine learning algorithm, we propose to target 6 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the BAT 150-Month catalog for 30 ks of NuSTAR and 25 ks of XMM-Newton time each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DISCOVERING COMPTON-THICK AGN WITH A.I.                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530ABELL_2255                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌhïxVb‹@Ìhñ#Ü70901002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìh®«∆ZD␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$	The most energetic events in the Universe since the Big Bang have been the merging of two or more galaxy clusters. As the subclusters merge, shock fronts and turbulence occur and can be distinguished with telltale signs like surface brightness edges. Normally, these edges are associated with radio relics, however in rare instances we have observed such edges on the outskirts of radio halos, like in the Coma Cluster. NuSTAR's ability to probe hard X-ray energies can provide key insights into the physical properties of such regions. NuSTAR's precision when measuring temperatures will allow for a robust measurement of the shock and it's ability to probe the higher energy non-thermal regime will provide constraints for any potential inverse Compton scattering that may be associated with it.   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Rojas                         Randall                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING THE SHARP RADIO EDGE OF ABELL 2255                                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ABELL_2255                                        @p"üæv»¥@PxF‹]@W}x‘˝Û∂@AyΩ•ú‡@s
§?Â…@Ìhñ#Ü@ÌhÂ#Ü70901002002@˛„üæw@˝…f≈ÅoA␀/ß˛≈m]A␀1˛≈m]SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìi√ ˛›␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$	The most energetic events in the Universe since the Big Bang have been the merging of two or more galaxy clusters. As the subclusters merge, shock fronts and turbulence occur and can be distinguished with telltale signs like surface brightness edges. Normally, these edges are associated with radio relics, however in rare instances we have observed such edges on the outskirts of radio halos, like in the Coma Cluster. NuSTAR's ability to probe hard X-ray energies can provide key insights into the physical properties of such regions. NuSTAR's precision when measuring temperatures will allow for a robust measurement of the shock and it's ability to probe the higher energy non-thermal regime will provide constraints for any potential inverse Compton scattering that may be associated with it.   Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Rojas                         Randall                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING THE SHARP RADIO EDGE OF ABELL 2255                                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530V1674_HER                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌhÂ#Ü@ÌhÂ±9Ò30901021001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìig∫7_␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#´NuSTAR observations of the recent classical nova V1674 Her will test and connect theories of intermediate polars (white dwarfs accreting through a disrupted disk and a magnetic stream) and of nova outbursts. The high energy spectrum and the X-ray luminosity at quiescence allow to estimate the white dwarf mass, the accretion rate, and the accretion disk and column parameters. Measuring the dependence of the spin period amplitude on energy range will constrain the absorption effects, and the geometry of the accretion columns. A coordinated XMM-Newton exposure will measure absorption and complete the physical picture with the spectrum and the timing of the residual soft emission from the polar caps, possibly still due to magnetically confined burning near the surface.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Orio                          Marina                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       V1674 HERCULIS: WHERE MAGNETISM MEETS THE CLASSICAL NOVA                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316V1674_HER                                         @q≈ãCïÅ@0€xF‹]@HB£S&@ÃcÒA @tUçπ@ÌhÂ±9Ò@ÌixVb‹30901021002@‰lwµsÍ≥@‰:Ï⁄πıZ@ÊΩú‡u˜@ÊΩú‡u˜SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìi)¡l¡␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#´NuSTAR observations of the recent classical nova V1674 Her will test and connect theories of intermediate polars (white dwarfs accreting through a disrupted disk and a magnetic stream) and of nova outbursts. The high energy spectrum and the X-ray luminosity at quiescence allow to estimate the white dwarf mass, the accretion rate, and the accretion disk and column parameters. Measuring the dependence of the spin period amplitude on energy range will constrain the absorption effects, and the geometry of the accretion columns. A coordinated XMM-Newton exposure will measure absorption and complete the physical picture with the spectrum and the timing of the residual soft emission from the polar caps, possibly still due to magnetically confined burning near the surface.                         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Orio                          Marina                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       V1674 HERCULIS: WHERE MAGNETISM MEETS THE CLASSICAL NOVA                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530NGC_1275                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌixVb‹@Ìi[‰õø60902033001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìi)íLúA␀␀ÏˆHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$9NGC 1275 is one of the few radio galaxies known to emit very high energy gamma rays. The physical mechanisms are not entirely understood in either the flare or quiescent state. Studies without NuSTAR data show a multi-component model is needed to fit the multi-wavelength spectral energy distribution (SED) of the 2017 flare while a single-zone model fits the quiescent SED. NuSTAR is the only instrument capable of probing the hard X-ray range of the SED and will clearly determine whether a single zone SSC is sufficient for the quiescent state or an additional component is required. We request a total of 90ks of NGC 1275 observations to occur in three separate visits. We plan to carry out an SED modeling study with simultaneous observations from Swift-UVOT, Swift-XRT, Fermi-LAT and VERITAS.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Talluri                       Anjana                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING A MULTI-COMPONENT SED OF NGC 1275 WITH NUSTAR IN FLARING AND NON-FLARING TEV GAMMA-RAY STATES                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316NGC_1275                                          @H¯∞Ú{≤ˇ@D∆Æ}VlÙ@bÀ.HËß¿*È†'RTa@[˚b∂Æ}V@Ìi[‰õø@Ìiî»)¯60902033002@‡c#oiD@‡Bç◊€Ùà@‚Ä&ÄùI@‚†&ÄùISCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìi)u0Ï®␀␀ÏˆHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀$9NGC 1275 is one of the few radio galaxies known to emit very high energy gamma rays. The physical mechanisms are not entirely understood in either the flare or quiescent state. Studies without NuSTAR data show a multi-component model is needed to fit the multi-wavelength spectral energy distribution (SED) of the 2017 flare while a single-zone model fits the quiescent SED. NuSTAR is the only instrument capable of probing the hard X-ray range of the SED and will clearly determine whether a single zone SSC is sufficient for the quiescent state or an additional component is required. We request a total of 90ks of NGC 1275 observations to occur in three separate visits. We plan to carry out an SED modeling study with simultaneous observations from Swift-UVOT, Swift-XRT, Fermi-LAT and VERITAS.  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Talluri                    