530RGB_J1725p118                                     @pSÿ≠´üV@'¢∂Æ}Vm@@˙¨1'@8πb∂Æ}@sJa@N•@ÌjØ?r‘£@ÌjØ±9Ò60962006001@T«&¡Ω•@TAÚ◊s@_¿␀␀␀␀␀@_¿␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìj¡®›|«␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six objects that had only previously been detected in flaring states. We request NuSTAR observations of six of these extreme BL Lacs to characterize their synchrotron emission.                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Errando                       Manel                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A SURVEY OF HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM NEW TEV-PEAKED BL LACS                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530RGB_J1725p118                                     @pSË>BZÓ@'•…„Ω@@˚¸PH@8πDg8~@s<Ç©ì·@ÌjØ±9Ò@ÌjÌ[‰õø60962006002@Ûc¢u%F@Û6$Oﬂ;d@ÙÙß@N§©@Ùˆ∑@N§©SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìk3ß¢␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six objects that had only previously been detected in flaring states. We request NuSTAR observations of six of these extreme BL Lacs to characterize their synchrotron emission.                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Errando                       Manel                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A SURVEY OF HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM NEW TEV-PEAKED BL LACS                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202305302MASXJ17232321p3630097                            @pM=»K^@B:£S&@NM˙C˛\í@@g“Ú‰è@rjˆ+jÁ’@ÌjÌ[‰õø@ÌjÌÕ´∏160160665001@r„6zêó@r≥S&¡æ@t[ˇó$tT@t[ˇó$tTSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌkÎ<M^␀␀Î^Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  202305302MASXJ17232321p3630097                            @pM`™d¬¯@B:JåLò@NM…„Ω@@f‚ÎC-@re∆ßÔù≤@ÌjÌÕ´∏1@Ìj¸?r‘£60160665002@—Juﬁ␀“@—F‹]@“ƒÓffff@“≈nffffSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìk‘z·H␀␀Î^Hea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530V603_Aql                                          @q£?º?·jÁ’fœB@@z WßÜ¬?ÒN§®¡T @sÒz·GÆ@Ìj¸?r‘£@Ìj¸Õ´∏130901023001@G‰á¸π#£@G≤Öáì›ò@I†␀␀␀␀␀@I†␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìk≥ƒ’Á␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀# NuSTAR observations of the two quiescent X-ray hardest novae will reveal the physics of accretion in these systems, by: 1) Estimating the white dwarf mass and mass accretion rate, 2) Probing the accretion mode, 3) Assessing whether the magnetic field of one target constrains nuclear burning and evolution, 4) Verifying whether there is non-thermal emission from a suspected jet in one of them. These goals cannot be achieved with non-imaging telescopes, neither with detectors at lower energy. The NuSTAR observations will significantly constrain the models of secular evolution leading to nuclear burning and nova outbursts.                                                                                                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Orio                          Marina                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TWO LUMINOUS QUIESCENT NOVAE                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530V603_Aql                                          @q£1¯†ê.?·7K∆ßÔû@@y·∞â†'?ÒN§®¡T @sÏDg8~@Ìj¸Õ´∏1@Ìk(xVb‹30901023002@Ï‡Îﬂ;dZ@Ïû¢ı¬è\@Ô+hÀ˚µ@Ô,hÀ˚µSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌkB∏Çw␀␀ÏHea_04Aug2021_V6.29b_nustardas_04May21_v2.1.1                                   ␀␀# NuSTAR observations of the two quiescent X-ray hardest novae will reveal the physics of accretion in these systems, by: 1) Estimating the white dwarf mass and mass accretion rate, 2) Probing the accretion mode, 3) Assessing whether the magnetic field of one target constrains nuclear burning and evolution, 4) Verifying whether there is non-thermal emission from a suspected jet in one of them. These goals cannot be achieved with non-imaging telescopes, neither with detectors at lower energy. The NuSTAR observations will significantly constrain the models of secular evolution leading to nuclear burning and nova outbursts.                                                                                                                                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Orio                          Marina                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TWO LUMINOUS QUIESCENT NOVAE                                                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀PID 09162                                                                                                               archived  20230530SwiftJ1727_SADA_23305                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìk(xVb‹@Ìk)[‰õø80902047001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌkB~¨/␀␀ÏÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$IStellar-mass black holes in X-ray transients undergo months-long outbursts during which they explore wide-ranging accretion rates and spectral-timing states. Following transitioning to the thermal/soft state, the black hole undergoes a protracted thermal decline. The thermal/soft state includes only modest contribution from nonthermal components; instead, most of the emission comes from the thermal accretion disk continuum. Accordingly, this state is the gold-standard for spin measurements via X-ray continuum fitting. We request 4x20 ks NuSTAR observations during the thermal phase of the outburst, to monitor a bright black-hole transient in decline, in order to determine its spin. We request a corresponding set of 4x5 ks NICER observations, which significantly improves the constraint.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONTINUUM-FITTING SPIN MEASUREMENT OF A BLACK-HOLE TRANSIENT                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup slew                                          