               ␀␀#ÁThe investigators propose to observe two X-ray flaring blazars with NuSTAR and Swift during X-ray linear polarization measurements by the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). The combined simultaneous 0.3-70 keV continuum spectrum and polarization derived from synchronous NuSTAR, Swift, and IXPE observations, along with mm-wave and optical polarization measurements, will provide a definitive test of the synchrotron self-Compton and external Compton models for X-ray emission from low-synchrotron-peak blazars, and for the hard X-ray emission from intermediate-synchrotron-peak blazars.                                                                                                                                                                                                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Jorstad                       Svetlana                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       COMBINING X-RAY POLARIZATION AND CONTINUUM SPECTRA OF BLAZARS                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20230530NEPTDF2_POS2                                      @pJ™d¬¯8@Ptæﬂ§@@WˆœAÚ◊@@œæv»¥9@q+‰%ÆÊ2@Ìl√Õ´∏1@Ìlƒ[‰õø60910005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìmâ2q␀␀Ï"Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$3The JWST Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP) is a GTO target currently being observed every 90 days (4 epochs) with JWST NIRCAM and NIRISS. The NEP has exquisite multiwavelength data. NuSTAR+XMM (Cycle 8) are currently observing it simultaneously with JWST. In JWST Cycle 2, a new proposal to observe the NEP for 2 more years has been submitted. Given the interesting results from NuSTAR Cycle 5+6+8, we request 900 ks (4 epochs) for continuing to monitor the NEP in the hard X-ray band simultaneously with JWST: this dataset will be unique for X-ray-NIR time-domain studies. This additional monitoring will allow studies of AGN variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, and the obscured fraction.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TIME-DOMAIN STUDIES IN THE NEP FIELD WITH NUSTAR AND JWST                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NEPTDF2_POS2                                      @pJìtºj@Ps~êˇó$@Wˆ+jÁ’g@@–1&Èx’@q=¯†ê-‡@Ìlƒ[‰õø@Ìl¯èFj60910005002@Ùa÷_ÿ≠¨@Ù,ô®Xy>@ı‡eê-‡@ı‡Öê-‡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìm∂Õé˚␀␀Ï"Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$3The JWST Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP) is a GTO target currently being observed every 90 days (4 epochs) with JWST NIRCAM and NIRISS. The NEP has exquisite multiwavelength data. NuSTAR+XMM (Cycle 8) are currently observing it simultaneously with JWST. In JWST Cycle 2, a new proposal to observe the NEP for 2 more years has been submitted. Given the interesting results from NuSTAR Cycle 5+6+8, we request 900 ks (4 epochs) for continuing to monitor the NEP in the hard X-ray band simultaneously with JWST: this dataset will be unique for X-ray-NIR time-domain studies. This additional monitoring will allow studies of AGN variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, and the obscured fraction.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TIME-DOMAIN STUDIES IN THE NEP FIELD WITH NUSTAR AND JWST                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530XID_17473                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìl¯èFj@Ìl¯ÍN60901014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìm˛2“ˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#»We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of two high luminosity (log LX>45 erg/s), high redshift (z>3), highly obscured (log NH >23 cm-2) AGN in the eROSITA eFEDS field. Their optical spectroscopic classification as Broad Line QSO by the SDSS is at odds with their high X-ray obscuration which, unusually, is the case for nearly all the high-luminosity, high-redshift, high-NH eFEDS candidates. Understanding these AGN, which are rare in eROSITA samples because of its soft energy band, is essential to interpreting the upcoming eROSITA all-sky survey, but also important to test the standard unification model. We aim to (i) confirm the high obscuration with much better X-ray data and (ii) understand what these peculiar objects imply for the unexplored high-L, high-z, high-NH Universe.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Peca                          Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DECODING LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT, OBSCURED EROSITA AGN                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316XID_17473                                         @`Êˇó$tT?€1è≈Åo@lã‚Ç@∑Ä@<u¬è\(ˆ@e™‰éäqﬁ@Ìl¯ÍN@Ìl˝#Ü60901014002@∑]mp£◊
@∑%_OÑM@∏Y≠B√ @∏Y≠B√ SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìm %+ˇˇˇùHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#»We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of two high luminosity (log LX>45 erg/s), high redshift (z>3), highly obscured (log NH >23 cm-2) AGN in the eROSITA eFEDS field. Their optical spectroscopic classification as Broad Line QSO by the SDSS is at odds with their high X-ray obscuration which, unusually, is the case for nearly all the high-luminosity, high-redshift, high-NH eFEDS candidates. Understanding these AGN, which are rare in eROSITA samples because of its soft energy band, is essential to interpreting the upcoming eROSITA all-sky survey, but also important to test the standard unification model. We aim to (i) confirm the high obscuration with much better X-ray data and (ii) understand what these peculiar objects imply for the unexplored high-L, high-z, high-NH Universe.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Peca                          Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DECODING LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT, OBSCURED EROSITA AGN                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀interrupted for ToO                                                                                                     processed 20230530GRB231115A                                        @b•ÁlãCñ@Qm≈÷8ÜY@a™RΩ<6@D4z·GÆ@e"␀—∑Y@Ìl˝#Ü@Ìl˝Õ´∏190901332001@v	∑X‚@u‘>´6z@wê␀␀␀␀␀@wê␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìmﬁá∆Z␀␀ÎzHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530GRB231115A                                        @b¢eîØO@Qmå~($@a´_o“Û@D0À)^û@eë—N;Õ@Ìl˝Õ´∏1@Ìm$xVb‹90901332002@Ú  ƒõ¶@ÒÌ‰˜e˛@Û}^‡uˆ˝@Û.‡uˆ˝SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌmC∆râK␀␀ÎzHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀DDT                                                                                                                     archived  20230530ARTXCJ1753d7                                      @p«Dg8~@Plç∏∫«@Wÿ•zxl"@>r˛≈m\˚@qn˜e˝ä€@Ìm$xVb‹@Ìm$ÍN60963003001@u^*ô0æ@u5W>´6z@v–␀␀␀␀␀@v–␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌmCÆ]L;␀␀Ï1Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530ARTXCJ1753d7                                      @p«qué"@PkK∆ßÔû@W◊ŒŸá+@>tìtºj@qd¶L/É{@Ìm$ÍN@ÌmJxVb‹60963003002@Ì˜8‰éär@Ì´lŒp:˚@a@O@pa@OSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìmhì'O␀␀Ï1Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530NGC5907_ULX1                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌmJxVb‹@ÌmJÕ´∏130902035003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìmh|ç␀␀Ï1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#¯Following a series of remarkable recent discoveries, we now know that some of the most luminous members of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) population are actually powered by highly super-Eddington pulsars (i.e. neutron stars), making them the most extreme sustained accretors known. Six such systems are now known, but NGC5907 ULX1 stands out as the most luminous, peaking at Lx ~ 1e41 erg/s. Here we propose a pair of simultaneous NuSTAR+XMM observations of NGC5907 ULX1. These will allow us to continue to track the long-term evolution of the neutron star spin, help investigate why the pulsations are transient in some ULX pulsars, and explore the nature of the long-term 78d X-ray period present in this system.                                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND MONITORING OF NGC5907 ULX1                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC5907_ULX1                                      @l¢u%F
