@VËÎÌ˙C˛@Iå2 Wßá@m·‰%ÆÊ2@ÌmJÕ´∏1@Ìmî±9Ò30902035004@˝⁄´…ÓÀ˚@˝îKÉ{J#A␀ËÊ1¯°A␀0Ê1¯°SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìm–[«ﬂ␀␀Ï1Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#¯Following a series of remarkable recent discoveries, we now know that some of the most luminous members of the ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX) population are actually powered by highly super-Eddington pulsars (i.e. neutron stars), making them the most extreme sustained accretors known. Six such systems are now known, but NGC5907 ULX1 stands out as the most luminous, peaking at Lx ~ 1e41 erg/s. Here we propose a pair of simultaneous NuSTAR+XMM observations of NGC5907 ULX1. These will allow us to continue to track the long-term evolution of the neutron star spin, help investigate why the pulsations are transient in some ULX pulsars, and explore the nature of the long-term 78d X-ray period present in this system.                                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Walton                        Dominic                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MULTI-EPOCH BROADBAND MONITORING OF NGC5907 ULX1                                                                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(2/2) / C-class solar flares                                                                                            archived  20230530SN2022xxf                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìmî±9Ò@Ìmï[‰õø80902503003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìmœ˜www␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    u◊ÎContrary to expectations from stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a continuation of our coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program is designed to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. The overarching goal is to constrain the nature of the physical process responsible for mass loss in evolved massive stars.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Brethauer                     PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Mapping extreme mass loss from evolved massive stars with coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR observations                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SN2022xxf                                         @eím≈÷8Ü@"òÔ4÷°b@olE° ¿É@O€©*0U2@fvÊ1¯†ê@Ìmï[‰õø@Ìmπ[‰õø80902503004@Á“Ö∏QÏ@ÁòDL/É{@Èú8é@ÈúòéSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìm‰í Á␀␀ÏHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  u◊ÎContrary to expectations from stellar evolutionary models, recent observations uncovered the ejection of shells of material massive stars in the years before the supernova (SN) explosion. The physical mechanism behind the impulsive mass ejection synchronized with the stellar core-collapse is unclear. Here we propose a continuation of our coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR effort to map the evolution of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of 1 nearby (d<50 Mpc) strongly interacting SN and enable progress. Our program is designed to characterize the medium around strongly interacting SN, which originate from stellar progenitors with the most extreme mass loss before explosion. The overarching goal is to constrain the nature of the physical process responsible for mass loss in evolved massive stars.       Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Brethauer                     PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Mapping extreme mass loss from evolved massive stars with coordinated Chandra-NuSTAR observations                       ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(2/2) ToO / Data gaps 8s                                                                                                archived  20230530NEPTDF2_POS3                                      @pCµ'ª@PrΩ<64@W˜X‚e,@@Ê±ƒ2 X@p¶óçOﬂ;@Ìmπ[‰õø@Ìm∫#Ü60910006001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìm‰{ÏGM␀␀Ï1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$3The JWST Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP) is a GTO target currently being observed every 90 days (4 epochs) with JWST NIRCAM and NIRISS. The NEP has exquisite multiwavelength data. NuSTAR+XMM (Cycle 8) are currently observing it simultaneously with JWST. In JWST Cycle 2, a new proposal to observe the NEP for 2 more years has been submitted. Given the interesting results from NuSTAR Cycle 5+6+8, we request 900 ks (4 epochs) for continuing to monitor the NEP in the hard X-ray band simultaneously with JWST: this dataset will be unique for X-ray-NIR time-domain studies. This additional monitoring will allow studies of AGN variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, and the obscured fraction.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TIME-DOMAIN STUDIES IN THE NEP FIELD WITH NUSTAR AND JWST                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NEPTDF2_POS3                                      @pD}øHÄ@Pt	‘ï+@W¯¬¯7¥¢@@‰˜e˝ä€@pªƒõ•„T@Ìm∫#Ü@ÌmÓ#Ü60910006002@Ù⁄”√a@Ù®∆ßÔû@ˆi`™d√@ˆj`™d√SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìn	–ó¥␀␀Ï1Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$3The JWST Time-Domain Field in the NEP (NEP) is a GTO target currently being observed every 90 days (4 epochs) with JWST NIRCAM and NIRISS. The NEP has exquisite multiwavelength data. NuSTAR+XMM (Cycle 8) are currently observing it simultaneously with JWST. In JWST Cycle 2, a new proposal to observe the NEP for 2 more years has been submitted. Given the interesting results from NuSTAR Cycle 5+6+8, we request 900 ks (4 epochs) for continuing to monitor the NEP in the hard X-ray band simultaneously with JWST: this dataset will be unique for X-ray-NIR time-domain studies. This additional monitoring will allow studies of AGN variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, and the obscured fraction.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TIME-DOMAIN STUDIES IN THE NEP FIELD WITH NUSTAR AND JWST                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                                                                                                                               ␀␀␀CME Kp=5                                                                                                                archived  20230530XID_20296                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌmÓ#Ü@ÌmÔèFj60901015001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìn	w`␀␀Ï1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#»We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of two high luminosity (log LX>45 erg/s), high redshift (z>3), highly obscured (log NH >23 cm-2) AGN in the eROSITA eFEDS field. Their optical spectroscopic classification as Broad Line QSO by the SDSS is at odds with their high X-ray obscuration which, unusually, is the case for nearly all the high-luminosity, high-redshift, high-NH eFEDS candidates. Understanding these AGN, which are rare in eROSITA samples because of its soft energy band, is essential to interpreting the upcoming eROSITA all-sky survey, but also important to test the standard unification model. We aim to (i) confirm the high obscuration with much better X-ray data and (ii) understand what these peculiar objects imply for the unexplored high-L, high-z, high-NH Universe.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Peca                          Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DECODING LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT, OBSCURED EROSITA AGN                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316XID_20296                                         @böu@ˆîFt@l∂©ì‡ﬂ@Ca≤ïÈ‚@e“á+J@ÌmÔèFj@ÌnB±9Ò60901015002@˚%æ=»K@˙·u>´6z@˝-ŒeîØO@˝.ÆeîØOSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌnwÉπ␀␀Ï1Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#»We propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations of two high luminosity (log LX>45 erg/s), high redshift (z>3), highly obscured (log NH >23 cm-2) AGN in the eROSITA eFEDS field. Their optical spectroscopic classification as Broad Line QSO by the SDSS is at odds with their high X-ray obscuration which, unusually, is the case for nearly all the high-luminosity, high-redshift, high-NH eFEDS candidates. Understanding these AGN, which are rare in eROSITA samples because of its soft energy band, is essential to interpreting the upcoming eROSITA all-sky survey, but also important to test the standard unification model. We aim to (i) confirm the high obscuration with much better X-ray data and (ii) understand what these peculiar objects imply for the unexplored high-L, high-z, high-NH Universe.           Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Peca                          Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DECODING LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT, OBSCURED EROSITA AGN                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀CME                           XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀CME Kp=5                                                                                                                archived  20230530M31_FIELD_R                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌnB±9Ò@ÌnCÕ´∏150910007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìnw_IÙü␀␀Ï1Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$The X-ray Luminosity Function (XLF) of a galaxy can be linked to a variety of different galaxy characteristics. In addition, studies of accreting black hole and neutron star populations can provide insights into the history of star formation and evolution in a galaxy because they are directly connected to past, current, and future stellar populations through binary synthesis modeling. As our nearest Milky Way-type neighbor, M31 is an excellent laboratory for learning about our own galaxy. We propose an observation of nine fields in the disc of M31 totaling 825 ks to obtain complete coverage of all high luminosity sources in the hard band, allowing us to characterize the hard and full band XLFs and classify 20-60 sources for use in binary population demographics and binary synthesis models.Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Fritze                        Hannah                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       ALL THE LUMINOUS X-RAY BINARIES IN M31: HARD X-RAY DEMOGRAPHICS AND BINARY POPULATION SYNTHESIS APPLICATIONS            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316M31_FIELD_R                                       @%7ˆ+jÁ’@D∏-‡q@^7¸PH¿5Ñm≈÷8Ü@uÅ≤ïÈ‚@ÌnCÕ´∏1@Ình[‰õø50910007002@Á8¨åÁ∞@Ê˚Õ/ÏV÷@È'±üæw@È*ÒüæwSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìnê{rÍb␀␀Ï1Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$The X-ray Luminosity Function (XLF) of a galaxy can be linked to a variety of different galaxy characteristics. In addition, studies of accreting black hole and neutron star populations can provide insights into the history of star formation and evolution in a galaxy because they are directly connected to past, current, and future stellar populations through binary synthesis modeling. As our nearest Milky Way-type neighbor, M31 is an excellent laboratory for learning about our own galaxy. We propose an observation of nine fields in the disc of M31 totaling 825 ks to obtain complete coverage of all high luminosity sources in the hard band, allowing us to characterize the hard and full band XLFs and classify 20-60 sources for use in binary population demographics and binary synthesis models.Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Fritze                        Hannah                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       ALL THE LUMINOUS X-RAY BINARIES IN M31: HARD X-RAY DEMOGRAPHICS AND BINARY POPULATION SYNTHESIS APPLICATIONS            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530GRB231115A                                        ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ình[‰õø@ÌnièFj90901333001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌnêeÌ	{␀␀ÎÅHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GRB231115A                                        @b°®Xy=Ÿ@QmfœAÚ@a´•„S˜œ@D0 ƒõ•„@c‘I∫^5?@ÌnièFj@Ìnã?r‘£90901333002@È}øÖ∏R@ÈEkΩ•ù@ÎgΩ˜ŒŸ@Îg›˜ŒŸSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìo ŸˆÊ␀␀ÎÅHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Chandra HST                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20230530WISEAJ1458p5254                                   ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìnã?r‘£@Ìnã±9Ò60961003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìn€“}'“␀␀Ï8Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ﬁThe X-ray emission from AGN is believed to be generated from the tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. The cutoff energy in the AGN X-ray spectrum (at a few hundred keV) can be used to probe the coronal properties. However, only lower limits to the cutoff energy of low-redshift AGN were measured due to the limited bandpass (up to 80 keV). High-z quasars, instead, are the best targets to constrain the cutoff energy thanks to the cosmological redshifting. So far, only four z>1 quasars have their cutoff energy constrained. Here, we propose other four z>1 quasars to increase the current sample size to statistically constrain the AGN coronal properties and thus better understand the physics of coronal Comptonization.                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SYSTEMATICALLY CONSTRAINING THE AGN CORONAL PROPERTIES WITH NUSTAR USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS    ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316WISEAJ1458p5254                                   @lA [¿@Jq©˚Álã@VUÈ·∞â†@KáK∆ßÔû@lú‡uˆ˝@Ìnã±9Ò@Ìn¸î»)¯60961003002AdÄt!-A:÷î„º”A¡9Oﬂ;A¡◊@∑Ä4SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìo 2ÍaŸ␀␀Ï8Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#ﬁThe X-ray emission from AGN is believed to be generated from the tiny region surrounding the SMBH, namely the corona. The cutoff energy in the AGN X-ray spectrum (at a few hundred keV) can be used to probe the coronal properties. However, only lower limits to the cutoff energy of low-redshift AGN were measured due to the limited bandpass (up to 80 keV). High-z quasars, instead, are the best targets to constrain the cutoff energy thanks to the cosmological redshifting. So far, only four z>1 quasars have their cutoff energy constrained. Here, we propose other four z>1 quasars to increase the current sample size to statistically constrain the AGN coronal properties and thus better understand the physics of coronal Comptonization.                                                                Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Zhao                          Xiurui                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SYSTEMATICALLY CONSTRAINING THE AGN CORONAL PROPERTIES WITH NUSTAR USING A SAMPLE OF LUMINOUS, HIGH-REDSHIFT QUASARS    ␀␀S$␀␀␀␀Solar flare CME               XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀data gaps 21284s / Solar flare, CME, Kp=7                                                                               archived  202305302MASXJ13553383p3520573                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìn¸î»)¯@Ìn˝èFj60161566001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìo¿a#␀␀ÎÅHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                             