±fO␀␀ÎàHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503165BZQJ1430p4204                                    @k5∏QÎÖ@Eˇó$tT@SÍJåM@P=„º”@iÁ†˘	k∫@Ìoı?r‘£@ÌpÍN90901634002@Ïß;.HËß@Ïb«©*0U@Ó÷‡4m≈÷@Ó÷@4m≈÷SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìp1Òñê·␀␀ÎàHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀DDT / Solar flares                                                                                                      archived  20230530J011515d77p001248d7                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌpÍN@Ìp!èFj60965004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌpH’ˇ=“␀␀Ï?Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$MThe Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316J011515d77p001248d7                               @2Õqué"?≈˝ä⁄πıZ@`‰Æ}VlÙ¿O$cÒA \@sE˘€"–Â@Ìp!èFj@Ìp/î»)¯60965004002@‘’ÚOv@‘£z=p£◊@÷JÀ.HËß@÷JÀ.HËßSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌpH =p§␀␀Ï?Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$MThe Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.     