␀␀␀(2/8)                                                                                                                   processed 20230530CTA_1                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìp@?r‘£@Ìp@ÍN40901005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌpaéQ)W␀␀ÏFHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÊMany Galactic TeV sources are associated with middle-aged ~10^{4-5} yr pulsar wind nebulae (PWNe), with the gamma-ray emission extending beyond the X-rays. This suggests that high-energy e^{+-} first escape from the PWN, and then produce the gamma-rays through inverse compton (IC) scattering. However, how they escape from the PWN is poorly understood. Understanding this process requires spatially-resolved measurements of the diffuse, hard X-ray spectrum of the extended TeV source. CTA 1 is a rare extended X-ray and TeV source whose X-ray emission is both large and bright enough to measure the spatial variations in its hard X-ray spectrum. We will measure the particle spectrum and distribution in this source, important for understanding the injection of high energy e^{+-} into the ISM.     Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Alford                        Jason                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       COSMIC RAY LEPTONS ESCAPING FROM PWN PRISON: A NUSTAR INVESTIGATION OF CTA 1                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316CTA_1                                             ?˙Œ_o“ @RA|⁄Q@]·#¢úwö@$®r∞ ƒú@u'®¡T…Ü@Ìp@ÍN@Ìp°[‰õø40901005002Aeí_ÿ≠¨A2åéæﬂ§AÕ~HK]ÃAÕúHK]ÃSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìp‚
m¸5␀␀ÏFHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#ÊMany Galactic TeV sources are associated with middle-aged ~10^{4-5} yr pulsar wind nebulae (PWNe), with the gamma-ray emission extending beyond the X-rays. This suggests that high-energy e^{+-} first escape from the PWN, and then produce the gamma-rays through inverse compton (IC) scattering. However, how they escape from the PWN is poorly understood. Understanding this process requires spatially-resolved measurements of the diffuse, hard X-ray spectrum of the extended TeV source. CTA 1 is a rare extended X-ray and TeV source whose X-ray emission is both large and bright enough to measure the spatial variations in its hard X-ray spectrum. We will measure the particle spectrum and distribution in this source, important for understanding the injection of high energy e^{+-} into the ISM.     Non-ToO Supernovae, Supernova Remnants, and Galactic diffuse␀1    Alford                        Jason                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       COSMIC RAY LEPTONS ESCAPING FROM PWN PRISON: A NUSTAR INVESTIGATION OF CTA 1                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀Solar flares                                                                                                            archived  202305301ES_0806p524                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìp°[‰õø@Ìp°ÍN60962004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìp·„Egä␀␀ÏFHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six objects that had only previously been detected in flaring states. We request NuSTAR observations of six of these extreme BL Lacs to characterize their synchrotron emission.                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Errando                       Manel                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A SURVEY OF HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM NEW TEV-PEAKED BL LACS                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503161ES_0806p524                                      @^ùN;Õ5®@J-º£n/@d√&Èx‘˛@@Sã¨q≥@_éHËßÁ@Ìp°ÍN@Ìp›[‰õø60962004002@ıi≠ãCïÅ@ı7ŸD–©@˜␀·1&Èy@˜—1&ÈySCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌpÏ;*␀␀ÏFHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#ÛExtreme BL Lac objects are characterized by synchrotron emission peaking in the X-ray band and gamma-ray emission peaks located at energies above 1 TeV. Because of their efficient particle acceleration, extreme BL Lacs are proposed as sites where ultra-high energy cosmic rays and neutrinos may originate. In a search for TeV emission from a flux-limited sample of X-ray selected BL Lacs, the VERITAS observatory has discovered TeV emission from a new extreme BL Lac and characterized for the first time the TeV emission of another six objects that had only previously been detected in flaring states. We request NuSTAR observations of six of these extreme BL Lacs to characterize their synchrotron emission.                                                                                            Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Errando                       Manel                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A SURVEY OF HARD X-RAY EMISSION FROM NEW TEV-PEAKED BL LACS                                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀Solar flares                                                                                                            archived  202305304U_1630m472                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìp›[‰õø@Ìpﬁî»)¯90901335001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌpÎÔ±4␀␀ÎèHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            