#9¿ÏSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌqtßA␀␀ÎèHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀ToO / no CHU4                                                                                                           archived  20230530NGC_3729                                          @e±ÎÖ∏R@JîÌëhr∞@bT≈m\˙≠@N	ú‡uˆ˝@eﬁ␀—∑@Ìp?r‘£@ÌpÒ[‰õø60964001001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìqkµ™J␀␀ÏFHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$@Intrinsically Low-Luminosity AGN (LLAGN) considerably out-number known Seyferts & quasars, yet very little is currently known about their circumnuclear obscurers. We select four local galaxies from high-resolution radio observations of the publicly-available LeMMINGS survey, each with clear compact radio jets and a strong Chandra-detected core confirming their nature as LLAGN. However, without sensitive coverage above 10 keV we do not know their intrinsic underlying X-ray continuum. We propose a NuSTAR survey of these candidate LLAGN to determine their X-ray continua and hence differentiate their line-of-sight obscuration. A total NuSTAR exposure of 130 ks will provide a S/N>25 at 8-24 keV for all sources, conservatively assuming Compton-thick obscuration.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boorman                       Peter                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF RADIO-JETTED LOW-LUMINOSITY AGN                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC_3729                                          @e∞\ë—N<@JìJ#9¿Ï@bV√…ÓÀ˚@N∞Ú{≤ˇ@e˚ÁlãD@ÌpÒ[‰õø@Ìqî»)¯60964001002@·'¡qué"@·QCïÅ@‚b16zë@‚c16zëSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìq;º”␀␀ÏFHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$@Intrinsically Low-Luminosity AGN (LLAGN) considerably out-number known Seyferts & quasars, yet very little is currently known about their circumnuclear obscurers. We select four local galaxies from high-resolution radio observations of the publicly-available LeMMINGS survey, each with clear compact radio jets and a strong Chandra-detected core confirming their nature as LLAGN. However, without sensitive coverage above 10 keV we do not know their intrinsic underlying X-ray continuum. We propose a NuSTAR survey of these candidate LLAGN to determine their X-ray continua and hence differentiate their line-of-sight obscuration. A total NuSTAR exposure of 130 ks will provide a S/N>25 at 8-24 keV for all sources, conservatively assuming Compton-thick obscuration.                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boorman                       Peter                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR SURVEY OF RADIO-JETTED LOW-LUMINOSITY AGN                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Kp=5                                                                                                                    archived  20230530Mrk_421                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìqî»)¯@ÌqÍN60902024005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìq;ﬁá∆Z␀␀Ï⁄Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#˙The investigators propose to observe the BL Lac object Mrk421 with NuSTAR and Swift during X-ray linear polarization measurements by IXPE. Previous observations of Mrk421 have found rotation in the X-ray polarization angle while the degree of polarization remained essentially constant at a level several times the optical value. During this time, the X-ray spectrum became harder at higher flux levels. This behavior can be explained if the X-ray emission occurs upstream of the optical emission, with the former containing a uniform helical magnetic field and the latter a mostly turbulent field. Simultaneous NuSTAR, Swift, IXPE, and optical measurements during Cycle 9 will further probe the magnetic field and particle acceleration in the most upstream regions of the jet.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING THE INNERMOST REGIONS OF THE JET OF THE BLAZAR MRK421                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316Mrk_421                                           @dƒ˝!ˇ.I@Cëhr∞!@fuQÇ©ì@P1€"–Â`@eÙÿD–@ÌqÍN@Ìq[‰õø60902024006@”Öuƒ2 X@”c(y=Ÿ@’•Óv»¥9@’ßnv»¥9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìq;“ˆÑæ␀␀Ï⁄Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#˙The investigators propose to observe the BL Lac object Mrk421 with NuSTAR and Swift during X-ray linear polarization measurements by IXPE. Previous observations of Mrk421 have found rotation in the X-ray polarization angle while the degree of polarization remained essentially constant at a level several times the optical value. During this time, the X-ray spectrum became harder at higher flux levels. This behavior can be explained if the X-ray emission occurs upstream of the optical emission, with the former containing a uniform helical magnetic field and the latter a mostly turbulent field. Simultaneous NuSTAR, Swift, IXPE, and optical measurements during Cycle 9 will further probe the magnetic field and particle acceleration in the most upstream regions of the jet.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING THE INNERMOST REGIONS OF THE JET OF THE BLAZAR MRK421                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀␀(3/8) / Kp=6                                                                                                            processed 202305302MASXJ00331831p6127433                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìq[‰õø@Ìq#Ü60160014001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìq;√„-"␀␀ÎèHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                         