␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìq'î»)¯@Ìq(#Ü70901004003␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌqN†m:␀␀ÏFHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$&We propose a 375 ks of NuSTAR observation of the galaxy group MRC 0116+111. This unique galaxy group is known to host a remarkably bright extended diffuse radio emission along with a moderate X-ray luminosity and gas temperature. Recent XMM-Newton data detects extended inverse Compton (IC) emission from the location of the group with 4.6 sigma - potentially the very first of its kind. Thanks to its relatively cool intragroup temperature (kT ~ 0.6 keV), NuSTAR will be sensitive to the group's non-thermal emission only. By constraining the spectral slope of the latter, this observation will indubitably confirm or refute this detection claim, leading to either a breakthrough in our knowledge of magnetic fields at Mpc scales, or to the unique discovery of a system with extreme physics.        Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Tumer                         Aysegul                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONFIRMING THE FIRST X-RAY DETECTION OF INVERSE COMPTON IN A GALAXY GROUP WITH NUSTAR                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MRC_0116p111                                      @3ºV’œ™Œ@&¨<ûÏø±@`ß° ¿É¿IêÀí:*@sû'ª/Ï@Ìq(#Ü@ÌqJèFj70901004004@ÁÆ±[W>´@ÁtjRΩ<6@Èy‘h	‘ï@Èy‘h	‘ïSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌqnÇøÁ∫␀␀ÏFHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$&We propose a 375 ks of NuSTAR observation of the galaxy group MRC 0116+111. This unique galaxy group is known to host a remarkably bright extended diffuse radio emission along with a moderate X-ray luminosity and gas temperature. Recent XMM-Newton data detects extended inverse Compton (IC) emission from the location of the group with 4.6 sigma - potentially the very first of its kind. Thanks to its relatively cool intragroup temperature (kT ~ 0.6 keV), NuSTAR will be sensitive to the group's non-thermal emission only. By constraining the spectral slope of the latter, this observation will indubitably confirm or refute this detection claim, leading to either a breakthrough in our knowledge of magnetic fields at Mpc scales, or to the unique discovery of a system with extreme physics.        Galaxy clusters and extragalactic diffuse objects           ␀1    Tumer                         Aysegul                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONFIRMING THE FIRST X-RAY DETECTION OF INVERSE COMPTON IN A GALAXY GROUP WITH NUSTAR                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530SHBLJ012308d7p342049                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌqJèFj@ÌqJ[‰õø60160054001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìqnm„ÔP␀␀ÎèHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316SHBLJ012308d7p342049                              @4¬ WßÜ¬@A'È˘rG@`AiDg8¿<B√…ÓÀ˚@t`s¸PH@ÌqJ[‰õø@ÌqYèFj60160054002@‘Âê®¡T @‘ØÍT`™e@÷j!ƒ2 X@÷i°ƒ2 XSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìqnbõ␀␀ÎèHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530Mrk_421                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌqYèFj@ÌqZèFj60902024007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌqnS?V␀␀Ï⁄Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#˙The investigators propose to observe the BL Lac object Mrk421 with NuSTAR and Swift during X-ray linear polarization measurements by IXPE. Previous observations of Mrk421 have found rotation in the X-ray polarization angle while the degree of polarization remained essentially constant at a level several times the optical value. During this time, the X-ray spectrum became harder at higher flux levels. This behavior can be explained if the X-ray emission occurs upstream of the optical emission, with the former containing a uniform helical magnetic field and the latter a mostly turbulent field. Simultaneous NuSTAR, Swift, IXPE, and optical measurements during Cycle 9 will further probe the magnetic field and particle acceleration in the most upstream regions of the jet.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marscher                      Alan                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING THE INNERMOST REGIONS OF THE JET OF THE BLAZAR MRK421                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316Mrk_421                                           @dƒ…Öoi@CL/É{J@fu»K]Ã@P1p£◊
