TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              Swift                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530ARTXCJ1754d8                                      @pÕ‘˝Û∂F@PñÓcä	@X
¡Ω•@>Dá¸π#£@l≥Êö‘,=@Ìs=±9Ò@Ìs>?r‘£60963004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌsMY˙∞º␀␀ÏaHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316ARTXCJ1754d8                                      @pÃ+”√a@Pòâ†'RT@Xç∏∫«@>OˆîFt@lô‡ﬁ“â@Ìs>?r‘£@ÌstÕ´∏160963004002@ˆ˜¯qﬁi≠@ˆƒ⁄B√…Ô@¯®%E° ¡@¯®µE° ¡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌsÅ˝'“}␀␀ÏaHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$;NuSATR has provided an uninterrupted view towards the energy range covering the peak of the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB). To bridge the gap of current NuSTAR surveys between the wide-shallow and narrow-deep approaches, we propose joint NuSTAR-XMM observations for 23 sources based on the SRG ART-XC detections in the 200 deg2 North Ecliptic Pole field. The almost identical energy range between ART-XC and NuSTAR and the flux limit of the sample assures high quality spectra for the proposed observations. The NuSTAR-XMM broad-band X-ray observations will measure the physical properties of the sample accurately, providing insights into how a population of z<1.5, moderate luminosity sources contribute to the CXB and fit into the general picture of galaxy and AGN evolution.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Chen                          Chien-Ting                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A NUSTAR AND XMM-NEWTON SURVEY FOR THE SRG/ART-XC NEP CATALOG                                                           ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530MCG_02_34_058                                     @iP~($x¿%ªÁlãCñ@s⁄ﬁ␀—∑@Iáó$tSè@eı„S˜ŒŸ@ÌstÕ´∏1@Ìsuî»)¯60960003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌsÅ‡m:␀␀ÏaHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÖThe X-ray spectral analysis of new Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing a previously successful machine learning algorithm, we propose to target 6 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the BAT 150-Month catalog for 30 ks of NuSTAR and 25 ks of XMM-Newton time each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DISCOVERING COMPTON-THICK AGN WITH A.I.                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316MCG_02_34_058                                     @iPy=Ÿc¿%º]càeï@sŸ˝Û∂E¢@IáÄ4m≈÷@eˆÜYJÙÒ@Ìsuî»)¯@Ìsé±9Ò60960003002@‡÷,£◊
=@‡´=4ÍK@‚)Ã‘˝Û∂@‚*‘˝Û∂SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìs¢"‰P␀␀ÏaHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#ÖThe X-ray spectral analysis of new Compton thick (CT-) active galactic nuclei (AGN) represents a fundamental step to understand the physics of the obscuring material surrounding accreting supermassive black holes. However, at the present day, CT-AGN remain extremely hard to detect. Implementing a previously successful machine learning algorithm, we propose to target 6 low-redshift candidate CT-AGN from the BAT 150-Month catalog for 30 ks of NuSTAR and 25 ks of XMM-Newton time each. This analysis will help determine the true intrinsic fraction of CT-AGN in the local universe and their contribution to the Cosmic X-ray Background (CXB).                                                                                                                                                               Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Silver                        Ross                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       DISCOVERING COMPTON-THICK AGN WITH A.I.                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  202305303C273                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìsé±9Ò@ÌsèèFj11002608001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìs°˘•º~␀␀Î´Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Observations of celestial sources will be undertaken throughout the mission to determine, and then monitor changes to, the calibration of the X-ray observatory. Observations will enable the calibration of absolute flux, spectral sensitivity, point spread function, and the timing capabilities of the observatory as well as the observatory optical alignment. NuSTAR will also participate in cross-calibration campaigns with the Chandra, XMM-Newton, Swift, and Suzaku X-ray observatories.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Calibration Observations                                    ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀
