¶LSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìv‚=X^␀␀ÏvHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$MThe Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530Circinus_Galaxy                                   @j≠B√…ÓÃ¿PU ¿Éo@ssí£S&¿Dõ•„S¯@eÄ}VlÙ@Ìv≈î»)¯@Ìv∆î»)¯60901013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìv‚'“}(␀␀Î¿Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#≥We propose to observe the Circinus galaxy jointly with NuSTAR and XRISM, the most nearby (4.2 Mpc) Compton-thick AGN showing the brightest narrow Fe K line among all known AGNs. The bright fluorescence lines and Compton-thick nature make it the best target for studying the torus itself through high resolution spectroscopy. We use the Compton shoulder structure of Fe K and the properties of other fluorescence lines to unveil the torus structure. A simultaneous NuSTAR continuum spectrum in the hard band above 10 keV is critical to solve degeneracy among the torus parameters in combination with the high energy-resolution Resolve spectrum.                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ueda                          Yoshihiro                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNVEILING NATURE OF COMPTON-THICK TORUS IN CIRCINUS GALAXY                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Circinus_Galaxy                                   @j≠0æÌ)¿PT<ûÏø±@ss≠´üUõ¿ØOÑM@eÑêˇó$t@Ìv∆î»)¯@Ìv◊#Ü60901013002@◊d:lÙ!@◊/B∞ ƒú@ŸlõW>´6@ŸqW>´6SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìv‚Ø˙␀␀Î¿Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#≥We propose to observe the Circinus galaxy jointly with NuSTAR and XRISM, the most nearby (4.2 Mpc) Compton-thick AGN showing the brightest narrow Fe K line among all known AGNs. The bright fluorescence lines and Compton-thick nature make it the best target for studying the torus itself through high resolution spectroscopy. We use the Compton shoulder structure of Fe K and the properties of other fluorescence lines to unveil the torus structure. A simultaneous NuSTAR continuum spectrum in the hard band above 10 keV is critical to solve degeneracy among the torus parameters in combination with the high energy-resolution Resolve spectrum.                                                                                                                                                             Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ueda                          Yoshihiro                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNVEILING NATURE OF COMPTON-THICK TORUS IN CIRCINUS GALAXY                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XRISM XMM                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530NGC5231                                           @iÄ8ÜYJı@ ƒõ•„T@tÇæÌ(ç@OÓóçOﬂ;@ep—∑X‚@Ìv◊#Ü@Ìv◊Õ´∏160061333001@LœAÚ◊@K¡4ÍJå@N␀␀␀␀␀␀@N␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìv‚ÃÃÕ␀␀Î¿Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530NGC5231                                           @iˆ˝!ˇ.@+üUõ=@tÇåLò_@OÔökP∞Ú@eq∑È˘r@Ìv◊Õ´∏1@ÌvÊ[‰õø60061333002@‘mY≠B√ @‘;›$›/@’‰eÆÊ1˘@’‰eÆÊ1˘SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìw	Aú¢S␀␀Î¿Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530PKS_1502p106                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌvÊ[‰õø@ÌvÊÕ´∏160902009005␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìw	1ﬂb:␀␀ÏvHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀4Multimessenger astronomy is a powerful new method for understanding astrophysical processes. This was illustrated in 2017 when IceCube reported a high-energy neutrino found to be coincident with a very-high-energy flaring blazar (detected by Fermi and MAGIC). Since then, various multimessenger studies involving neutrinos and photon data have occurred, but the associations between neutrinos and active galactic nuclei (AGN) remain uncertain. We propose a monitoring program for two AGN, NGC1068 and PKS1502+106, using NICER and NuSTAR to study the X-ray-neutrino connection. This analysis will make use of both model-dependent and model-independent approaches using observed X-ray and IceCube non-public data and will also serve as a trigger for real-time follow-up using IceCube.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vandenbroucke                 Justin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       PROBING THE AGN NEUTRINO CONNECTION USING MULTIMESSENGER OBSERVATIONS WITH NICER AND ICECUBE                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PKS_1502p106                                      @lDô0æÌ@%ö‘,<ü@&°@N§©@KlÅo␀h‹@e]4÷°aÂ@ÌvÊÕ´∏1@Ìv˘±9Ò60902009006@◊Iö‘,=@◊†Z¨@Ÿpr{≤˛≈@Ÿtr{≤˛≈SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìw	%Ì	{␀␀ÏvHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀4Multimessenger astronomy is a powerful new method for understanding astrophysical processes. This was illustrated in 2017 when IceCube reported a high-energy neutrino found to be coincident with a very-high-energy flaring blazar (detected by Fermi and MAGIC). Since then, various multimessenger studies involving neutrinos and photon data have occurred, but the associations between neutrinos and active galactic nuclei (AGN) remain uncertain. We propose a monitoring program for two AGN, NGC1068 and PKS1502+106, using NICER and NuSTAR to study the X-ray-neutrino connection. This analysis will make use of both model-dependent and model-independent approaches using observed X-ray and IceCube non-public data and will also serve as a trigger for real-time follow-up using IceCube.                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Vandenbroucke                 Justin                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	R05       PROBING THE AGN NEUTRINO CONNECTION USING MULTIMESSENGER OBSERVATIONS WITH NICER AND ICECUBE                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(3/3)                                                                                                                   archived  20230530V750_Ara                                          @p`i≠B√ ¿G!-w1ê@ukq≤ïÍ¿ÔiDg8@a`ÕûÉ‰&@Ìv˘±9Ò@Ìv˙[‰õø30901001001@eà¬&ÄùI@e]‹∆?@g␀␀␀␀␀@g␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìw	Ö‡ÁˇˇˇùHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  qC¿We propose to obtain high resolution spectra with HETG of the gamma Cas-type star, V750 ara. These stars, of which about 25 are known, are Oe/Be stars which have very hard and strong X-ray emission. Their nature is enigmatic with several proposed explanations, one of which is that they have an He-star companion with a strong wind. With a 200 ks HETGS spectrum, we will characterize plasma temperatures from emission lines of Fe, Si, Mg, and S, and determine dynamical parameters from line widths and centroids. We also request a NuSTAR observation of 100 ks which is necessary to constrain the high temperature plasma, via the spectral energy distribution between 10 and 30 keV.                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Huenemoerder                  PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Characterizing the Energetics and Dynamics of  V750 Ara, a gamma Cas-type Star                                          ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20230530V750_Ara                                          @p`h€ã¨q¿G}µsÍ≥h@ukó$tSè¿È˘rGE@au≈Åo␀@Ìv˙[‰õø@Ìw@#Ü30901001002@˜>ê_o“ @˜:#¢úx@˘ôÒA \@˘	ÒA \SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìwb©∑‘ˇˇˇùHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  qC¿We propose to obtain high resolution spectra with HETG of the gamma Cas-type star, V750 ara. These stars, of which about 25 are known, are Oe/Be stars which have very hard and strong X-ray emission. Their nature is enigmatic with several proposed explanations, one of which is that they have an He-star companion with a strong wind. With a 200 ks HETGS spectrum, we will characterize plasma temperatures from emission lines of Fe, Si, Mg, and S, and determine dynamical parameters from line widths and centroids. We also request a NuSTAR observation of 100 ks which is necessary to constrain the high temperature plasma, via the spectral energy distribution between 10 and 30 keV.                                                                                                                        Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Huenemoerder                  PI                            NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	C24       Characterizing the Energetics and Dynamics of  V750 Ara, a gamma Cas-type Star                                          ␀␀␀/␀␀␀␀                              Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀SL-A2 (4U 1735-44) / data gaps                                                                                          processed 20230530MCGm6m30m15                                       @iÄ‰éäqﬁ¿A&äqﬁi≠@sê—N;Õ6@;◊1è≈Å@fºÿD–@Ìw@#Ü@Ìw@±9Ò60902004001@rß∞ ƒõ¶@ry‘˝Û∂F@t¨8Ô4÷°@t¨8Ô4÷°SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌwbàÈü´␀␀ÌRHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀gÓThe bright NLS1 galaxy MCG-6-30-15 harbors one of the most studied AGN in high-energy astrophysics, boasting one of the strongest broad Fe K lines ever seen as well as the first time-variable warm absorber. We will obtain a 100ks observation of MCG6 during XRISM's PV phase, which will characterize its spectral signatures with unprecedented resolution. Our primary goals are to establish the kinematics of the multi-phase outflow across all ionization states in the X-ray bandpass, to verify the spin of the supermassive black hole, and to identify any misalignment of the accretion flow between the inner and outer disk/torus.We request simultaneous observations with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR to realize the full science return of the XRISM data.                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       A Detailed Exploration of Reflection and Absorption in MCG--6-30-15                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20230530MCGm6m30m15                                       @iÄŒp:˚¿A#~êˇó$@sê€Ùá¸π@;›IQÇ©ì@f÷Ÿá+@Ìw@±9Ò@Ìw§ÍN60902004002@ˇ„iH@ˇï¶›/†ALvkπå~AM^kπå~SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìw…—Aú¢␀␀ÌRHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀gÓThe bright NLS1 galaxy MCG-6-30-15 harbors one of the most studied AGN in high-energy astrophysics, boasting one of the strongest broad Fe K lines ever seen as well as the first time-variable warm absorber. We will obtain a 100ks observation of MCG6 during XRISM's PV phase, which will characterize its spectral signatures with unprecedented resolution. Our primary goals are to establish the kinematics of the multi-phase outflow across all ionization states in the X-ray bandpass, to verify the spin of the supermassive black hole, and to identify any misalignment of the accretion flow between the inner and outer disk/torus.We request simultaneous observations with XMM-Newton and NuSTAR to realize the full science return of the XRISM data.                                                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Brenneman                     Laura                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       A Detailed Exploration of Reflection and Absorption in MCG--6-30-15                                                     ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM XRISM                                                                                           ␀␀␀                                                                                                                        processed 202305303FGL_J1544d6m1125                                 @máI∫^¿&„ïÅ$›@v@çOﬂ;d@@úºj~˘€@e	˚µsÎ@Ìw§ÍN@Ìw•[‰õø80901309001@b)ã¨q≥@b)◊
=p§@gÂökP∞Ú@hÖôôôôöSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìw…™aŸQ␀␀Ì<Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀h∫The XMM discovery of a ms pulsar swinging between an accretion-powered (X-ray) and a rotation-powered (radio) pulsar state demonstrated that transitions between the two states can be observed over timescales of a few weeks. We propose an XMM, NuSTAR and VLA ToO observation aimed at studying the accretion state of transitional ms pulsars, detecting X-ray accretion powered pulsations, and characterizing the X-ray variability over three decades in energy. Candidates are restricted to black widows and redbacks, systems that are in an evolutionary phase that makes them good candidates to observe a transition. Enlarging the number of transitional ms pulsars is crucial to test binary evolution theories, and to study the disk-field interaction over a large range of mass accretion rates.           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Zanon                         Arianna_Miraval               NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Hunting for transitional millisecond pulsars with XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and VLA                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 202305303FGL_J1544d6m1125                                 @mÜ‘˝Û∂F¿&‰éäqﬁj@v@tSéÛM@@ùú‡u˜@e`Aâ7L@Ìw•[‰õø@Ìw…#Ü80901309002@Ê5ìË>B[@Â˛k.HËß@Ëﬁ␀—∑@Ëﬁ␀—∑SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌwÈ‰‚	‹␀␀Ì<Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀h∫The XMM discovery of a ms pulsar swinging between an accretion-powered (X-ray) and a rotation-powered (radio) pulsar state demonstrated that transitions between the two states can be observed over timescales of a few weeks. We propose an XMM, NuSTAR and VLA ToO observation aimed at studying the accretion state of transitional ms pulsars, detecting X-ray accretion powered pulsations, and characterizing the X-ray variability over three decades in energy. Candidates are restricted to black widows and redbacks, systems that are in an evolutionary phase that makes them good candidates to observe a transition. Enlarging the number of transitional ms pulsars is crucial to test binary evolution theories, and to study the disk-field interaction over a large range of mass accretion rates.           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Zanon                         Arianna_Miraval               NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       Hunting for transitional millisecond pulsars with XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and VLA                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   GTC/HiPERCAM                                                                                        ␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     processed 20230530PNV_J17261813m3809354                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìw…#Ü@Ìw…î»)¯80901341001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌwÈœWú␀␀Î«Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ÓWe propose a 60ks observation of a new bright (V<7.5) nova likely to be detected in gamma-rays. The observation will probe shocks within the nova ejecta, constrain the non-thermal particle acceleration and gamma-ray production mechanisms. We will put an upper limit on the particle acceleration efficiency by comparing thermal X-ray and optical to GeV luminosity and search for predicted non-thermal X-rays. Understanding shocks in novae is relevant for other shock-powered transients including Type IIn supernovae, tidal disruption events and stellar mergers. NuSTAR is the only instrument capable of detecting hard X-rays from novae simultaneously with the GeV emission. The observations should be conducted now to take advantage of the simultaneous operations with Fermi and XMM.                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sokolovsky                    Kirill                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNDERSTANDING THE GAMMA-RAY PRODUCTION IN NOVA SHOCKS                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316PNV_J17261813m3809354                             @pY‡ﬁ“àŒ¿C	‘ï+@uﬂ*0U2aøıº£n@bê˘	kπå@Ìw…î»)¯@Ìw¯î»)¯80901341002@ÓÂ8-‡@Óó⁄ç∏ª@Ø!lÙ!@ØAlÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌxÁâ´Œ␀␀Î«Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#ÓWe propose a 60ks observation of a new bright (V<7.5) nova likely to be detected in gamma-rays. The observation will probe shocks within the nova ejecta, constrain the non-thermal particle acceleration and gamma-ray production mechanisms. We will put an upper limit on the particle acceleration efficiency by comparing thermal X-ray and optical to GeV luminosity and search for predicted non-thermal X-rays. Understanding shocks in novae is relevant for other shock-powered transients including Type IIn supernovae, tidal disruption events and stellar mergers. NuSTAR is the only instrument capable of detecting hard X-rays from novae simultaneously with the GeV emission. The observations should be conducted now to take advantage of the simultaneous operations with Fermi and XMM.                  Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Sokolovsky                    Kirill                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNDERSTANDING THE GAMMA-RAY PRODUCTION IN NOVA SHOCKS                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     archived  20230530HESS_J0632p57                                     ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìw¯î»)¯@Ìw˘±9Ò91002305001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌxŒ8„é␀␀Î«Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀Time has been allocated to respond to targets of opportunity during the NuSTAR primary mission. Targets may include, but are not limited to; 1) Core collapse SN and associated shocks, 2) Tidal disruption flare, 3) extreme gamma-ray bursts, 4) Solar flares, 5) SLN SN-1. The response time from a ToO trigger for the NuSTAR observatory is 48 hours, but faster response times are anticipated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Non-Proposal ToOs                                           ␀	1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL        