uSTAR+XMM (Cycle 8) are currently observing it simultaneously with JWST. In JWST Cycle 2, a new proposal to observe the NEP for 2 more years has been submitted. Given the interesting results from NuSTAR Cycle 5+6+8, we request 900 ks (4 epochs) for continuing to monitor the NEP in the hard X-ray band simultaneously with JWST: this dataset will be unique for X-ray-NIR time-domain studies. This additional monitoring will allow studies of AGN variability, 0.3-24 keV spectroscopy through simultaneous XMM observations, the faint end of the AGN population at >8 keV, and the obscured fraction.                       Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Civano                        Francesca                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TIME-DOMAIN STUDIES IN THE NEP FIELD WITH NUSTAR AND JWST                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        processed 20230530SwiftJ1727d8m1613                                 ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìy+ÍN@Ìy,±9Ò80902348006␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌyAµI2q␀␀ÏÑHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$IStellar-mass black holes in X-ray transients undergo months-long outbursts during which they explore wide-ranging accretion rates and spectral-timing states. Following transitioning to the thermal/soft state, the black hole undergoes a protracted thermal decline. The thermal/soft state includes only modest contribution from nonthermal components; instead, most of the emission comes from the thermal accretion disk continuum. Accordingly, this state is the gold-standard for spin measurements via X-ray continuum fitting. We request 4x20 ks NuSTAR observations during the thermal phase of the outburst, to monitor a bright black-hole transient in decline, in order to determine its spin. We request a corresponding set of 4x5 ks NICER observations, which significantly improves the constraint.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONTINUUM-FITTING SPIN MEASUREMENT OF A BLACK-HOLE TRANSIENT                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316SwiftJ1727d8m1613                                 @p_•„S˜œ¿0,ò_ˆî@!äqﬁi≠@%Lò_@c˛∞Ú{≤ˇ@Ìy,±9Ò@Ìy=±9Ò80902348007@÷óÁ’fœ@÷{uú‡v@ÿÌ≈&Èx’@ÿÔ&Èx’SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌyA®›|«␀␀ÏÑHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$IStellar-mass black holes in X-ray transients undergo months-long outbursts during which they explore wide-ranging accretion rates and spectral-timing states. Following transitioning to the thermal/soft state, the black hole undergoes a protracted thermal decline. The thermal/soft state includes only modest contribution from nonthermal components; instead, most of the emission comes from the thermal accretion disk continuum. Accordingly, this state is the gold-standard for spin measurements via X-ray continuum fitting. We request 4x20 ks NuSTAR observations during the thermal phase of the outburst, to monitor a bright black-hole transient in decline, in order to determine its spin. We request a corresponding set of 4x5 ks NICER observations, which significantly improves the constraint.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Steiner                       James                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONTINUUM-FITTING SPIN MEASUREMENT OF A BLACK-HOLE TRANSIENT                                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER IXPE                                                                                          ␀␀␀␀(4/4) ToO                                                                                                               processed 20230530NGC_1365                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìy=±9Ò@Ìy>Õ´∏160902007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌyAòéô˚ˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀i␀XRISM is set to observe NGC 1365 twice during the initial Performance Verification Phase, for a total of 250 ks. Here we request two simultaneous XMM-Newton (2 A ~ 120 ks) and NuSTAR (2x60 ks) observations that will greatly enhance the science output of these observations. While XRISM will probe the ionized wind with unprecedented en- ergy resolution, with the addition of XMM-Newton, we can probe the variability of that outflow on short timescales. Moreover, XMM-Newton will enable simultaneous measurements of the broad iron line and potential reverberation lags, allowing us to understand how the inner accretion flow and highly ionized outflows interact. One of XMM- Newton's legacies has been to reveal time variability and lags that help us understand gas flows around the black hole. On theActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       The many scales of feedback in AGN NGC 1365 with XRISM, XMM-Newton & NuSTAR                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316NGC_1365                                          @JÆ␀—∑Y¿Bv»¥9X@m≈§?Â…¿Kg±[W>´@tÀá+@Ìy>Õ´∏1@ÌypÕ´∏160902007002@EGQÎÖ@ 'Ìëhs@ÒñøÿD–@Òò/ÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌyÅπ\Î$ˇˇˇùHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀i␀XRISM is set to observe NGC 1365 twice during the initial Performance Verification Phase, for a total of 250 ks. Here we request two simultaneous XMM-Newton (2 A ~ 120 ks) and NuSTAR (2x60 ks) observations that will greatly enhance the science output of these observations. While XRISM will probe the ionized wind with unprecedented en- ergy resolution, with the addition of XMM-Newton, we can probe the variability of that outflow on short timescales. Moreover, XMM-Newton will enable simultaneous measurements of the broad iron line and potential reverberation lags, allowing us to understand how the inner accretion flow and highly ionized outflows interact. One of XMM- Newton's legacies has been to reveal time variability and lags that help us understand gas flows around the black hole. On theActive galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Kara                          Erin                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       The many scales of feedback in AGN NGC 1365 with XRISM, XMM-Newton & NuSTAR                                             ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM XRISM                                                                                           ␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   processed 20230530J012141d63_SADA_24054                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌypÕ´∏1@ÌyqÕ´∏160965105001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌyÅû–ó¥␀␀ÏçHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$MThe Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    processed 20150316J012141d63m000747d5                               @4f$›/†ø∆À˚µsÎ@aTv»¥9X¿O%„º”[@t£n.≤@ÌyqÕ´∏1@ÌyëèFj60965005003@„Â\∞Ú{≥@„¥†¥9X@Â