 experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    processed 20150316J004629d31p202804d7                               @'>v»¥9X@4ikπå~(@^<„º”ZÜ¿EHåÁØ∏@rzõ•„S¯@ÌyëÕ´∏1@Ìy∞èFj60965002001@„)øêñªô@‚˚Zç∏∫«@‰´ô4÷°b@‰≠Y4÷°bSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìy»≈»†Œ␀␀ÏçHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$MThe Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) observations of the supermassive black hole (SMBH) in M87, has provided a powerful experimental testbed for strong gravity. Hence the SMBHs in several nearby systems, including the SgrA*, are all potential candidates to enhance our understanding of gravitation in such extreme environments. However, providing strong constraints on several aspects of the accretion and jet physics in active galaxies that are possible through multiwavelength observations, is vital for the convergence of theoretical simulations of accretion processes. We thus propose an exploratory programme of seven active galaxies using the NuSTAR to characterise the spectral curvature and cutoff's of the hard X-ray spectra.                                                                     Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Ramakrishnan                  Venkatessh                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       CONSTRAINING THE ACCRETION AND JET PROPERTIES OF NEXT GENERATION EHT TARGETS                                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀INERTIAL only                                                                                                           processed 20230530GX_13p1                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìy∞èFj@Ìy±?r‘£30901010001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìy»Øò◊k␀␀ÏHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#8GX13+1 is a very bright, disk accreting (non magnetic) neutron star. It is the best system in which to study the accretion disc wind seen in both neutron stars and black holes as it is a persistent source, and reliably shows wind absorption features. This is the reason it was chosen as a target for the XRISM PV phase, but it is a very bright source, so XRISM will have to do count rate mitigation measures in order to study its spectrum. A simultaneous observation with NuSTAR will give a much firmer determination of its overall luminosity and broadband spectrum, especially at higher energies, which is needed to critically test theoretical wind models.                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Done                          Chris                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TESTING MODELS OF ACCRETION DISC WINDS FROM GX13+1                                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316GX_13p1                                           @qƒ2 W®¿1<64@*–:˚~ë␀?ÿ`™d¬¯8@c}XbM”@Ìy±?r‘£@ÌyŒ#Ü30901010002@ﬂl|ãCïÅ@ﬂ·DLò_@ÂºŸôôôö@ÂΩYôôôöSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìy·˚ÏGM␀␀ÏHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#8GX13+1 is a very bright, disk accreting (non magnetic) neutron star. It is the best system in which to study the accretion disc wind seen in both neutron stars and black holes as it is a persistent source, and reliably shows wind absorption features. This is the reason it was chosen as a target for the XRISM PV phase, but it is a very bright source, so XRISM will have to do count rate mitigation measures in order to study its spectrum. A simultaneous observation with NuSTAR will give a much firmer determination of its overall luminosity and broadband spectrum, especially at higher energies, which is needed to critically test theoretical wind models.                                                                                                                                               Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Done                          Chris                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       TESTING MODELS OF ACCRETION DISC WINDS FROM GX13+1                                                                      ␀␀␀%␀␀␀␀                              XRISM IXPE                                                                                          ␀␀␀SL-A2 (GX 17+2)                                                                                                         archived  20230530SRGA_J144459d2m604207                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌyŒ#Ü@ÌyŒ±9Ò80901307001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìy·Ëπ␀␀ÏîHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀hùModelling of the waveform of the X-ray pulsations of accreting ms pulsars (AMSP) is one of the most promising ways to constrain the equation of state of neutron stars. However, it requires an extremely high number of counts to break the degeneracy between the many parameters that shape the X-ray pulse profiles. The polarimetric information granted by the forthcoming IXPE mission will measure the geometry of the hot spots independently, easing the requirement. We propose a 120 ks XMM-NuSTAR ToO observation of the next outburst of an AMSP to measure the pulsar ephemeris and fold simultaneous IXPE data, model the broadband spectrum, and derive a high statistics energy-resolved pulse profile. The proposed observation will measure the mass and the radius with an accuracy of a few per cent.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Papitto                       Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       X-ray pulse waveform from an accreting ms pulsar and the equation of state                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316SRGA_J144459d2m604207                             @k™∆?¿NUœ™ÕûÑ@sƒj~˘€#ø‰K]ÃcÒA@g␀éäqﬁj@ÌyŒ±9Ò@Ìz<?r‘£80901307002A?Yu%A7ˇ¬√…ÔArHËr∞!Ar∂Ër∞!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìzy=␀␀ÏîHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀hùModelling of the waveform of the X-ray pulsations of accreting ms pulsars (AMSP) is one of the most promising ways to constrain the equation of state of neutron stars. However, it requires an extremely high number of counts to break the degeneracy between the many parameters that shape the X-ray pulse profiles. The polarimetric information granted by the forthcoming IXPE mission will measure the geometry of the hot spots independently, easing the requirement. We propose a 120 ks XMM-NuSTAR ToO observation of the next outburst of an AMSP to measure the pulsar ephemeris and fold simultaneous IXPE data, model the broadband spectrum, and derive a high statistics energy-resolved pulse profile. The proposed observation will measure the mass and the radius with an accuracy of a few per cent.     Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Papitto                       Alessandro                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	X22       X-ray pulse waveform from an accreting ms pulsar and the equation of state                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XMM IXPE INTEGRAL                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀ToO                                                                                                                     processed 20230530Crab                                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿