TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              MAGIC + LST                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀(1/2) DDT                                                                                                               archived  20230530PSR_J2215p5135                                    @tﬂ€Ùá¸π@I«’fœAÚ@XÌ ¿Éo¿ıY≥–|Ö@lÂ≤ïÈ‚@Ìz´èFj@Ìz´Õ´∏130901014001@pﬂÊö‘,@p≠v_ÿ≠¨@r ␀␀␀␀␀@r ␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìz‘»π␀␀ÏîHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#SPSR J2215+5135's X-ray orbital light curve is dominated by an IntraBinary Shock (IBS) between the pulsar and companion winds. It is of special interest since XMM measures a very hard (Gamma_X<1) spectrum, with two sharp peaks at uniquely large separation. The neutron star also has an exceptionally large, but geometry-dependent, mass estimate. With a sensitive, multi-orbit NuSTAR study we explore these aspects, measuring the peaks' >10keV peak shape evolution to probe post-shock bulk acceleration, and detecting or excluding spectral breaks from synchrotron cooling and cut-off; these probe the IBS magnetic field strength. We also fit the energy-dependent light curves to constrain the viewing geometry, checking the orbital inclination and the widely cited neutron star mass estimates.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Romani                        Roger                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A REDBACK AT HIGH ENERGY: PSR J2215+5135                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20230530PSR_J2215p5135                                    @tﬂrGE8Ô@I«1è≈Å@XÏ¬¯7¥¢¿:˚~ë␀@l∑ÁlãCñ@Ìz´Õ´∏1@ÌzÛÍN30901014002@˘·ƒó$tT@˘°d[¿7@˚‡näqﬁj@˚·^äqﬁjSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{Âïn␀␀ÏîHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀#SPSR J2215+5135's X-ray orbital light curve is dominated by an IntraBinary Shock (IBS) between the pulsar and companion winds. It is of special interest since XMM measures a very hard (Gamma_X<1) spectrum, with two sharp peaks at uniquely large separation. The neutron star also has an exceptionally large, but geometry-dependent, mass estimate. With a sensitive, multi-orbit NuSTAR study we explore these aspects, measuring the peaks' >10keV peak shape evolution to probe post-shock bulk acceleration, and detecting or excluding spectral breaks from synchrotron cooling and cut-off; these probe the IBS magnetic field strength. We also fit the energy-dependent light curves to constrain the viewing geometry, checking the orbital inclination and the widely cited neutron star mass estimates.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Romani                        Roger                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       A REDBACK AT HIGH ENERGY: PSR J2215+5135                                                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        processed 20230530B22023p33                                         @s&ìtºj@@€/ÏV’–@R?K∆ßÔû¿Ç@∑Ä4n@gÓüæv»¥@ÌzÛÍN@ÌzÙ[‰õø60463070001@[1≤ïÈ‚@ZÛJ#9¿Ï@]¸π#¢ú@]¸π#¢úSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{¡#Eh␀␀Î„Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20230530B22023p33                                         @s%È·∞â†@@€¢úwök@R?!-w1ê¿n.±ƒ2 @g‚1¯†ê.@ÌzÙ[‰õø@Ì{Õ´∏160463070002@‘©ÀökP±@‘y#ÁlãD@÷<◊CïÅ@÷?WCïÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì|{^á∆Z␀␀Î„Hea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20230530Swift_J151857d0m572147                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì{Õ´∏1@Ì{Õ´∏180901347001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{#öåSp␀␀ÏõHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$<X-ray reflection in accreting black holes occurs in the inner region of the accretion disk, and physical modeling of the spectral and timing properties of this emission enables measurement of the black hole mass and spin. The unique combination of NuSTAR s hard and NICER s soft coverage provides the broad bandpass, high count rates and energy and timing resolution required to constrain models of the time averaged energy spectrum and the reverberation lag energy spectrum on different timescales. We propose to observe any black hole transient, known or unknown, exceeding 30 mCrab during the bright hard state for 30 ks with a simultaneous NuSTAR and NICER observation in order to access unprecedented characterization of black hole spin and mass.                                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MEASURING BLACK HOLE SPIN AND MASS THROUGH X-RAY REFLECTION SPECTRA AND REVERBERATION LAGS                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316Swift_J151857d0m572147                            @l∫§®¡T ¿L´~êˇó$@tE8Ô4◊?…=Ÿb∂Æ@f¨¡Ω•@Ì{Õ´∏1@Ì{xVb‹80901347002@ƒÚQ	kπå@≈¯ø’fœB@ÿh}*ô1@ÿi˝*ô1SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{#~ìÈ?␀␀ÏõHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$<X-ray reflection in accreting black holes occurs in the inner region of the accretion disk, and physical modeling of the spectral and timing properties of this emission enables measurement of the black hole mass and spin. The unique combination of NuSTAR s hard and NICER s soft coverage provides the broad bandpass, high count rates and energy and timing resolution required to constrain models of the time averaged energy spectrum and the reverberation lag energy spectrum on different timescales. We propose to observe any black hole transient, known or unknown, exceeding 30 mCrab during the bright hard state for 30 ks with a simultaneous NuSTAR and NICER observation in