? ZÓcä	@f∏ûÏø±[@Ì{èFj@Ì{"xVb‹80901347004@æ⁄Êffff@¿,†ÿD–@—Úyõ=»@—Úyõ=»SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{CpB¿␀␀ÏõHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$<X-ray reflection in accreting black holes occurs in the inner region of the accretion disk, and physical modeling of the spectral and timing properties of this emission enables measurement of the black hole mass and spin. The unique combination of NuSTAR s hard and NICER s soft coverage provides the broad bandpass, high count rates and energy and timing resolution required to constrain models of the time averaged energy spectrum and the reverberation lag energy spectrum on different timescales. We propose to observe any black hole transient, known or unknown, exceeding 30 mCrab during the bright hard state for 30 ks with a simultaneous NuSTAR and NICER observation in order to access unprecedented characterization of black hole spin and mass.                                                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Garcia                        Javier                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MEASURING BLACK HOLE SPIN AND MASS THROUGH X-RAY REFLECTION SPECTRA AND REVERBERATION LAGS                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀ToO / Ghost-rays (Cir X-1)                                                                                              processed 20230530Mrk_590_SADA_24068                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì{"xVb‹@Ì{#±9Ò80802052001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{B˘Óçÿ␀␀ÏõHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ )After a 10-year hiatus, Mrk 590 has partially re-ignited, with repeated major X-ray and UV flare-ups captured by Swift monitoring. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document the onset of AGN activity as it occurs, since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We will use approved Swift XRT monitoring to trigger up to 4 single-visit NuSTAR observations, of which up to 3 are joint with XMM (or NICER). Together, these data are needed to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on (and then characterize this event) or if alternate physics are at play.                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Lawther                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       TOO OBSERVATIONS OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: EXPLORING THE PHYSICS OF CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    processed 20150316Mrk_590                                           @@Œ5?|ÌëøÍ)«y¶µ@de_o“Û¿L¢M“Ò©¸@tÅÓÀ˚µ@Ì{#±9Ò@Ì{;±9Ò80802652005@‹◊Ï'RTa@‹í†K]Ãd@ﬂ1è≈Å@ﬂ±è≈ÅSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{BÏÿÔ±␀␀ÏõHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀ )After a 10-year hiatus, Mrk 590 has partially re-ignited, with repeated major X-ray and UV flare-ups captured by Swift monitoring. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document the onset of AGN activity as it occurs, since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We will use approved Swift XRT monitoring to trigger up to 4 single-visit NuSTAR observations, of which up to 3 are joint with XMM (or NICER). Together, these data are needed to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on (and then characterize this event) or if alternate physics are at play.                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Lawther                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       TOO OBSERVATIONS OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: EXPLORING THE PHYSICS OF CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(3/4) ToO / INERTIAL only                                                                                               processed 202305304U_1630m472                                       @oûö¿GØX‚e@uæv»¥9?ﬁÏø±[W?@dÆ°a‰˜f@Ì{;±9Ò@Ì{<Õ´∏180902346002@r©I∫^5?@rú-w1è≈@t¶X‚e@t¶X‚eSCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{Bÿ¶ﬂ√␀␀ÏÁHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$:A few blackhole X-ray binaries (BHXBs) have been known to show recurrent intensity dips in their lightcurves during outburst. These systems also show indication of outflowing winds. The dips are believed to be caused by clumps of cold obscuring material. Until now, our knowledge of the properties of such obscurer and its relation with disk winds as well as spectral state is very limited. Because soft X-ray photons are mostly absorbed by the obscuring material, NuSTAR's high energy capabilities make it the instrument of choice to study the nature of dips and obscuration in BHXBs. We therefore seek Target of Opportunity (ToO) observations with NuSTAR, jointly with NICER, for any one of a selected list of seven known dipping BHXBs in case they go into outburst during cycle 9.                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Adegoke                       Oluwashina                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNDERSTANDING OBSCURATION IN DIPPING BLACKHOLE BINARIES                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 202305304U_1630m472                                       @ocÒA \¿G≠jOv@uÿ≠´üV?ﬂ∑È˘rG@dµøHÀí@Ì{<Õ´∏1@Ì{M#Ü80902346003@ÿG'ª/Ï@◊ÚœÀí:*@⁄òÏ€ã¨q@⁄òÏ€ã¨qSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{j
%*€␀␀ÏÁHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$:A few blackhole X-ray binaries (BHXBs) have been known to show recurrent intensity dips in their lightcurves during outburst. These systems also show indication of outflowing winds. The dips are believed to be caused by clumps of cold obscuring material. Until now, our knowledge of the properties of such obscurer and its relation with disk winds as well as spectral state is very limited. Because soft X-ray photons are mostly absorbed by the obscuring material, NuSTAR's high energy capabilities make it the instrument of choice to study the nature of dips and obscuration in BHXBs. We therefore seek Target of Opportunity (ToO) observations with NuSTAR, jointly with NICER, for any one of a selected list of seven known dipping BHXBs in case they go into outburst during cycle 9.                 Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Adegoke                       Oluwashina                    NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       UNDERSTANDING OBSCURATION IN DIPPING BLACKHOLE BINARIES                                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀(2/3) ToO/ SL-A12B013 (GX 340+0,4U 1624-49)                                                                             processed 20230530Mrk_590_SADA_24068                                ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì{M#Ü@Ì{N[‰õø80802052002␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{i˘•º~␀␀ÏõHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀ )After a 10-year hiatus, Mrk 590 has partially re-ignited, with repeated major X-ray and UV flare-ups captured by Swift monitoring. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document the onset of AGN activity as it occurs, since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We will use approved Swift XRT monitoring to trigger up to 4 single-visit NuSTAR observations, of which up to 3 are joint with XMM (or NICER). Together, these data are needed to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on (and then characterize this event) or if alternate physics are at play.                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Lawther                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       TOO OBSERVATIONS OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: EXPLORING THE PHYSICS OF CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    processed 20150316Mrk_590                                           @@ŒîFsÅÿøÈˆ+jÁ’g@deLò_˜¿L°o␀h€å@tãb∂Æ}@Ì{N[‰õø@Ì{b?r‘£80802652006@¬ó\wökQ@¬h≥CïÅ@√ˆFHËß@√ˆFHËßSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{ÖZtß␀␀ÏõHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀ )After a 10-year hiatus, Mrk 590 has partially re-ignited, with repeated major X-ray and UV flare-ups captured by Swift monitoring. We wish to seize this rare opportunity to document the onset of AGN activity as it occurs, since this can lead to significant insight on the long-standing issue of how AGNs are triggered and fueled. We will use approved Swift XRT monitoring to trigger up to 4 single-visit NuSTAR observations, of which up to 3 are joint with XMM (or NICER). Together, these data are needed to determine if a standard thin accretion disk is being built up as the AGN turns on (and then characterize this event) or if alternate physics are at play.                                                                                                                                           Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Lawther                       Daniel                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       TOO OBSERVATIONS OF A RE-AWAKENING AGN: EXPLORING THE PHYSICS OF CHANGING-LOOK AGN                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀(4/4) ToO / INERTIAL only                                                                                               processed 20230530GX_339m4                                          @o˘Dg8~¿H`K]ÃcÒ@u-á¸π#£¿[qué!ñ@dŸb∂Æ@Ì{b?r‘£@Ì{c[‰õø80902342001@v°µsÍ≥h@vüm≈÷8Ü@y␀␀␀␀␀@y␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{ÖHààâˇˇˇùHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$Despite ~50 years of compact object studies in the Galaxy, the physics of relativistic jet launching remains an active and open field of research. Luckily, with the successful launch of JWST, a new parameter space is opening up in the field of jet physics, allowing for the study of the spectral break - above which the jet becomes optically thin - on sub-second time scales, providing a crucial link between light and plasma properties. We are proposing to use the unique combination of high time resolution and hard X-ray coverage of NuSTAR to study the outburst of two BH-LMXBs in a jet-dominated hard state. We will probe evolving, rapid, sub-second variations, and, for the first time ever, we will search for correlated variability with mid-infrared spectral observations with JWST.            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Shaw                          Aarran                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING RAPID VARIABILITY IN BLACK HOLE X-RAY BINARY JETS                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20230530GX_339m4                                          @o¯M“Ò©¸¿H_ä	ﬁ@u-i≠B√ ¿Gì›óˆ+@dıY≥–}@Ì{c[‰õø@Ì{qÕ´∏180902342002@”˜©*0U@”@¸PH@÷␀$ÿD–@÷$ÿD–SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì{Ö:˘çwˇˇˇùHea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀$Despite ~50 years of compact object studies in the Galaxy, the physics of relativistic jet launching remains an active and open field of research. Luckily, with the successful launch of JWST, a new parameter space is opening up in the field of jet physics, allowing for the study of the spectral break - above which the jet becomes optically thin - on sub-second time scales, providing a crucial link between light and plasma properties. We are proposing to use the unique combination of high time resolution and hard X-ray coverage of NuSTAR to study the outburst of two BH-LMXBs in a jet-dominated hard state. We will probe evolving, rapid, sub-second variations, and, for the first time ever, we will search for correlated variability with mid-infrared spectral observations with JWST.            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Shaw                          Aarran                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       PROBING RAPID VARIABILITY IN BLACK HOLE X-RAY BINARY JETS                                                               ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              JWST/INTEGRAL/IXPE/XMM                                                                              ␀␀␀␀(1/2) ToO                                                                                                               processed 202305302MASXJ15422399m1411055                            @mtºj~˘€¿,SZÖáìﬁ@v3úæ‡@?ªI∫^@d©≤ïÈ‚@Ì{qÕ´∏1@Ì{r?r‘£60161621001@q¬:í£S@qó?Â…@s␀␀␀␀␀␀@s␀␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì{Ö+µ™J␀␀Î„Hea_16Dec2022_V6.31.1_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                  ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀