␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì}˘˜`U␀␀ÎˇHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#âWe propose four 20 ks NuSTAR observations of the high-inclination dipping low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXB) XB1916-053 and X1624-490 quasi simultaneous to XRISM PV Phase observations of these targets. The XRISM observations are needed to characterise the absorbers (column densities, line ratios and velocity width and shift) throughout the orbit. The NuSTAR observations are crucial to determine the ionising continuum and derive the ionisation parameter (and ultimately the density and location) of the plasmas from the line ratios. We will use the changes in the absorbers' properties to model the complex spectral changes throughout the orbit, including during dips, and to trace the variability of the absorber associated with changes in the source luminosity and hardness to make an accretion map.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Trigo                         Maria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MAPPING THE ACCRETION DISC WITH IONISED PLASMAS IN DIPPING SOURCES                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  201503164U_1624m49                                        @n‚^5?|Ó¿HñL/É{J@tÏµsÍ≥høúæﬂ§?Ê@f(U2a|@Ì}‡èFj@Ì}Ô±9Ò30902018002@“AN_o“@“3<∫«À@’L¸‡uˆ˝@’P<‡uˆ˝SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì}˘Ëπ␀␀ÎˇHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀#âWe propose four 20 ks NuSTAR observations of the high-inclination dipping low-mass X-ray binaries (LMXB) XB1916-053 and X1624-490 quasi simultaneous to XRISM PV Phase observations of these targets. The XRISM observations are needed to characterise the absorbers (column densities, line ratios and velocity width and shift) throughout the orbit. The NuSTAR observations are crucial to determine the ionising continuum and derive the ionisation parameter (and ultimately the density and location) of the plasmas from the line ratios. We will use the changes in the absorbers' properties to model the complex spectral changes throughout the orbit, including during dips, and to trace the variability of the absorber associated with changes in the source luminosity and hardness to make an accretion map.Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Trigo                         Maria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       MAPPING THE ACCRETION DISC WITH IONISED PLASMAS IN DIPPING SOURCES                                                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XRISM                                                                                               ␀␀␀␀(1/2)                                                                                                                   archived  20230530Sol_24090_DL                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì}Ô±9Ò@Ì}ÒèFj20801401001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì}˘◊õ‡$␀␀ÏµHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀—A multi-messenger approach to the study of solar flare accelerated particles provides important opportunities for connecting the activity on the Sun and in the heliosphere. In recent years, the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) perihelia have defined intervals in which many observatories, including NuSTAR, simultaneously observe the Sun. NuSTAR's sensitivity to hard X-rays (HXR) is unique among solar observers and has made substantial contributions towards understanding the nature of microflare-scale phenomena. To extend this effort, this proposal seeks to utilize NuSTAR s significant capabilities in the HXR range to study solar flare particle acceleration, heating, and propagation via organized co-observations with a cohort of solar instruments.                                                     Solar System Objects                                        ␀1    Peterson                      Marianne                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀N08       EXPANDING THE SCIENCE OF SOLAR FLARE-ACCELERATED PARTICLES WITH MULTI-MESSENGER CO-OBSERVATIONS                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀Solar observation dogleg setup                                                                                          processed 20150316Sol_24090_SADA                                    ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ì}ÒèFj@Ì}ÒxVb‹20801402001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ì}˘Ã
