o test competing theories. Following successful joint NuSTAR and EHT Sgr A* observation campaigns during 2017-2022, we propose a 100 ks NuSTAR exposure to overlap with the EHT observation window in April 2024 during NuSTAR AO Cycle-9. Our primary science goals are: 1) Detect bright X-ray flares, characterize each detected flare and feed this information to the EHT data analysis team; 2) Enlarge the existing NuSTAR Sgr A* flare sample and test a trend that brighter X-ray flares have harder spectra than fainter ones. A secondary science goal is to monitor a new hard X-ray source discovered merely at 1 parsec from Sgr A*.                                                              Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Zhang                         Shuo                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       JOINT NUSTAR AND EHT PROBE OF SGR A*: FLARES, BLACK HOLE SHADOWS AND A NEW HARD X-RAY SOURCE                            ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316SgrAstar                                          @pßF‹]cà¿<¯Ô4÷°b@v}2 Wßá?Õ÷8ÜYJı@c∫π#¢úx@Ì~Üî»)¯@Ì~ï[‰õø30902013002@”ÍÙ9Xb@”’cä	@’ÜsÅ◊‹@’FsÅ◊‹SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ì~©±Ó-␀␀ÏµHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀#QSimultaneous NuSTAR and EHT observations provide an exciting opportunity to reveal origin of Sgr A* flares and to test competing theories. Following successful joint NuSTAR and EHT Sgr A* observation campaigns during 2017-2022, we propose a 100 ks NuSTAR exposure to overlap with the EHT observation window in April 2024 during NuSTAR AO Cycle-9. Our primary science goals are: 1) Detect 