␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌàÖ“LúA␀␀ÏLHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316NGC3431                                           @dXnóçOﬂ¿1g†˘	l@pôµ'ª@BÉ'ª/Ï@m≈÷8ÜY@Ìàv±9Ò@ÌàÖ#Ü60160412002@”'[ä	@“˚JÙÿ@‘ŒÓlÙ!@‘—ÆlÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìà§§…ƒ␀␀ÏLHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20240422NGC_4151                                          ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌàÖ#Ü@ÌàÖÕ´∏160902010007␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìà§î€¯j␀␀ÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀#ëNGC 4151 is the brightest Seyfert-1 in the sky above 4 keV. This makes NGC 4151 a natural dicovery space for new instrumentation, and XRISM will observe it on 4 occasions during its PV phase. Joint XRISM and NuSTAR observations will make key tests of relativistic reflection, disk winds, and ultra-fast outflows that strongly require this unique combination of high spectral resolution and broad band sensitivity. This pairing will also enable the best possible measurements of key coronal properties. We request 4 NuSTAR observations of 45 ks, simultaneously with XRISM, in order to achieve breakthrough science that will set a standard for Seyfert-1s and reverberate across the black hole mass scale.                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       REVEALING REFLECTION AND UFOS IN NGC 4151 WITH XRISM AND NUSTAR                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316NGC_4151                                          @f“U2a|@C∞y=Ÿc@cu≤-V@R≥Ö∏QÏ@v
T…Öo@ÌàÖÕ´∏1@Ìàßî»)¯60902010008@ÊZªm\˙≠@Ê+˛ZÓc @Ë|m≈÷9@Ë}“m≈÷9SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìàƒæ¨/␀␀ÌHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀#ëNGC 4151 is the brightest Seyfert-1 in the sky above 4 keV. This makes NGC 4151 a natural dicovery space for new instrumentation, and XRISM will observe it on 4 occasions during its PV phase. Joint XRISM and NuSTAR observations will make key tests of relativistic reflection, disk winds, and ultra-fast outflows that strongly require this unique combination of high spectral resolution and broad band sensitivity. This pairing will also enable the best possible measurements of key coronal properties. We request 4 NuSTAR observations of 45 ks, simultaneously with XRISM, in order to achieve breakthrough science that will set a standard for Seyfert-1s and reverberate across the black hole mass scale.                                                                                                  Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Miller                        Jon                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       REVEALING REFLECTION AND UFOS IN NGC 4151 WITH XRISM AND NUSTAR                                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XRISM XMM                                                                                           ␀␀␀(4/4) / Solar flare                                                                                                     processed 20240422Mrk_766                                           ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìàßî»)¯@ÌàßÍN60901032001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìàƒ©&N!␀␀ÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$8We propose to observe the nearby, highly-accreting NLSy1 Mrk 766 with NuSTAR for 80 ks, in coordination with the PV-phase observation with XRISM. Previous observations of this source have revealed a multi-component, multi-velocity disk wind, with the detection of a complex and variable warm absorber, a highly-ionized absorber, and a variable Ultra Fast Outflow. Mrk 766 also shows complex emission in the iron K region and high-energy reflection features. Thanks to NuSTAR's high sensitivity and large broadband coverage in hard X-rays, we aim to disentangle the highly-ionized gas and its connection with other ionized phases, to study the iron line emitting region and characterize the reflection, and to investigate the flux-resolved variability of a source accreting in a high Eddington regime.Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boissay-Malaquin              Rozenn                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       ABSORPTION AND EMISSION UNDER EXTREME CONDITIONS: THE CASE OF MRK 766                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316Mrk_766                                           @g†˘	k∫@=Ã<ûÏø±@g“éäqﬁj@T{‚Ç@∑Ä@v1($x@ÌàßÍN@ÌàÂ[‰õø60901032002@ÙHƒõ•„@ÛÿC,•@ı†+=p£◊@ı°ª=p£◊SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìâ£ÔP␀␀ÌHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀$8We propose to observe the nearby, highly-accreting NLSy1 Mrk 766 with NuSTAR for 80 ks, in coordination with the PV-phase observation with XRISM. Previous observations of this source have revealed a multi-component, multi-velocity disk wind, with the detection of a complex and variable warm absorber, a highly-ionized absorber, and a variable Ultra Fast Outflow. Mrk 766 also shows complex emission in the iron K region and high-energy reflection features. Thanks to NuSTAR's high sensitivity and large broadband coverage in hard X-rays, we aim to disentangle the highly-ionized gas and its connection with other ionized phases, to study the iron line emitting region and characterize the reflection, and to investigate the flux-resolved variability of a source accreting in a high Eddington regime.Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Boissay-Malaquin              Rozenn                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       ABSORPTION AND EMISSION UNDER EXTREME CONDITIONS: THE CASE OF MRK 766                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              XRISM XMM                                                                                           ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        processed 20240422FBQSJ110340d2p372925                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌàÂ[‰õø@ÌàÂ±9Ò60160417001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìâà'qf␀␀ÏLHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                    