   Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        archived  20240422SgrAstar_SADA_24178                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌàÙèFj@Ìàı#Ü50901003001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìâk˛{£␀␀Ì Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$GWe propose a 100 ks (5 x 20 ks) NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2023 to cover five nights of VLTI GRAVITY observations of Sgr A*. We aim to combine the information about the central engine (particle acceleration) coming from spectra and light curves acquired with NuSTAR with information about the dynamics from astrometry and polarization observed by GRAVITY during flares.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Stel                          Giovanni                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SGR A* FLARES: CONNECTING PARTICLE ACCELERATION MECHANISM FROM NUSTAR WITH DYNAMICS FROM GRAVITY                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / SADA setup                                                                                                    processed 20150316SgrAstar                                          @p¶ué!ñS¿=´6zë@v{™ÕûÉ‰?Õ'ª/Ï@s°a‰˜f@Ìàı#Ü@Ìâî»)¯50902003007@€ K˘rGE@⁄·g‰%ÆÊ@ﬁ7ç∏ª@ﬁ;ç∏ªSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìâ$‹YÅT␀␀Ì Hea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀$GWe propose a 100 ks (5 x 20 ks) NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2023 to cover five nights of VLTI GRAVITY observations of Sgr A*. We aim to combine the information about the central engine (particle acceleration) coming from spectra and light curves acquired with NuSTAR with information about the dynamics from astrometry and polarization observed by GRAVITY during flares.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Stel                          Giovanni                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SGR A* FLARES: CONNECTING PARTICLE ACCELERATION MECHANISM FROM NUSTAR WITH DYNAMICS FROM GRAVITY                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                              VLT-Gravity                                                                                         ␀␀␀␀(4/5)                                                                                                                   processed 20240422SgrAstar_SADA_24178                               @p?\˙¨ŸË¿=⁄QŒ@vJõ=»K@–ñªò«„@s–wökP±@Ìâî»)¯@ÌâÍN50901003002@Åﬁ_ˆîF@Å≥;Õ5®X@É0␀␀␀␀␀@É0␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìâ$ m¸5␀␀Ì Hea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀$GWe propose a 100 ks (5 x 20 ks) NuSTAR exposure in Summer 2023 to cover five nights of VLTI GRAVITY observations of Sgr A*. We aim to combine the information about the central engine (particle acceleration) coming from spectra and light curves acquired with NuSTAR with information about the dynamics from astrometry and polarization observed by GRAVITY during flares.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Normal galaxies                                             ␀1    Stel                          Giovanni                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       SGR A* FLARES: CONNECTING PARTICLE ACCELERATION MECHANISM FROM NUSTAR WITH DYNAMICS FROM GRAVITY                        ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀SADA rally INERTIAL ONLY                                                                                                processed 20240422T_CrB                                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌâÍN@Ìâ±9Ò80901344001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìâ$æı␀a␀␀ÏZHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$#The symbiotic recurrent nova T Corona Borealis (T CrB) likely harbors a massive white dwarf near the Chandrasekhar limit. However, the methods that have been used to estimate the white dwarf mass are potentially subject to systematic errors. The forthcoming X-ray observatory, XRISM, with its microcalorimeter instrument, Resolve, has the potential to allow a direct measurement of the gravitational redshift on the white dwarf surface, and hence its mass. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR observation to support this by providing an accurate characterization of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of T CrB, and by providing a direct comparison with an X-ray continuum-based estimate of the white dwarf mass.                                                                                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       THE MASSIVE WHITE DWARF IN THE SYMBIOTIC STAR T CRB: GRAVITATIONAL RESDSHIFT VS. CONTINUUM TEMPERATURE                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316T_CrB                                             @m˙m≈÷8Ü@9ÎjÁ’fœ@E1#¢úwö@H8«‚Ç@∏@8¸˙¨ŸË>@Ìâ±9Ò@Ìâ<ÍN80901344002@>¢_ÿ≠¨@çHËß@ÒëÄŸb∑@ÒìŸb∑SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌâD˝°/h␀␀ÏZHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀$#The symbiotic recurrent nova T Corona Borealis (T CrB) likely harbors a massive white dwarf near the Chandrasekhar limit. However, the methods that have been used to estimate the white dwarf mass are potentially subject to systematic errors. The forthcoming X-ray observatory, XRISM, with its microcalorimeter instrument, Resolve, has the potential to allow a direct measurement of the gravitational redshift on the white dwarf surface, and hence its mass. Here we propose a coordinated NuSTAR observation to support this by providing an accurate characterization of the broad-band X-ray spectrum of T CrB, and by providing a direct comparison with an X-ray continuum-based estimate of the white dwarf mass.                                                                                             Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Mukai                         Koji                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       THE MASSIVE WHITE DWARF IN THE SYMBIOTIC STAR T CRB: GRAVITATIONAL RESDSHIFT VS. CONTINUUM TEMPERATURE                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XRISM                                                                                               ␀␀␀ToO / Solar flare                                                                                                       archived  202404222MASXJ15462424p6929102                            ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìâ<ÍN@Ìâ=xVb‹60160622001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌâDÂº}Í␀␀ÏZHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                