e black hole that radiates due to accretion. There is growing evidence of a significant correlation between the neutrino and hard X-ray emission from these systems, but the sample is still very scarce (four sources). We aim to significantly increase the sample size and reduce potential selection effects by conducting high-sensitivity hard X-ray observations of the two AGNs with the most significant neutrino signal. Confirming and understanding the reason of the hard X-ray -- neutrino correlation would be a breakthrough in multi-messenger astrophysics. For this we request 60 ks of observing time with NuSTAR.                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Del_Palacio                   Santiago                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       TESTING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN NEUTRINO AND HARD X-RAY EMISSION IN AGNS                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316GB6_J1542p6129                                    @mth€ã¨q@N¡4ÍJå@W‰cÒA \@F∑RT`™e@9lÃÃÃÃÕ@ÌâLî»)¯@ÌâexVb‹61001010002@·ë§‚ÎC@·bô?|Ìë@‚ÎaÖ∏R@‚ÌÅÖ∏RSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @ÌâxQÎÖ␀␀ÌHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀'AActive galactic nuclei (AGN) are galactic centers with a supermassive black hole that radiates due to accretion. There is growing evidence of a significant correlation between the neutrino and hard X-ray emission from these systems, but the sample is still very scarce (four sources). We aim to significantly increase the sample size and reduce potential selection effects by conducting high-sensitivity hard X-ray observations of the two AGNs with the most significant neutrino signal. Confirming and understanding the reason of the hard X-ray -- neutrino correlation would be a breakthrough in multi-messenger astrophysics. For this we request 60 ks of observing time with NuSTAR.                                                                                                                      Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Del_Palacio                   Santiago                      NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       TESTING THE CONNECTION BETWEEN NEUTRINO AND HARD X-RAY EMISSION IN AGNS                                                 ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                        processed 20240422J133032d00m003613d5                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌâexVb‹@Ìâf#Ü61063007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìâá˙∞º␀␀ÌHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀'ﬁWhile it has been demonstrated by numerical simulations over the past decade, the causality between galaxy mergers and AGN triggering remains an open question. And a consensus of the AGN occupation rate in mergers is still absent, which is tied to the challenge of detecting heavily obscured and/or intrinsic low-luminosity AGNs. Thanks to the unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR at energy band above 10 keV, it stands out as the only instrument with the capability to recover the intrinsic AGN luminosity and the level of obscuration. We propose to carry out observations of seven dual AGN systems with separations < 5 kpc, which signifies the late stage of mergers and are perfect targets to test the merger-induced AGN scenario and study the essential physics of SMBH feeding and feedback.        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Liu                           Xin                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
