N10       INVESTIGATING SOLAR ACTIVE REGION HEATING WITH NUSTAR AND THE MAGIXS-2 SOUNDING ROCKET                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR / dogleg rally                                                                                                  archived  20150316GX_1p4                                            @porGE8Ô¿8«X‚e?˚É{J#9¡@g8}øH@tÙa@N•@Ìãä±9Ò@Ìãã±9Ò31002031002@sCïÅ%@r¯¶L/É{@uê␀␀␀␀␀@uê␀␀␀␀␀SCIENCE                 CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìã€ºM^pˇˇˇùHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀(We request 180 ks NuSTAR observations of GX 1+4 (20 ks per month over 9 months) with 2 ks simultaneous NICER data to unravel long-term X-ray variability in this unique neutron star hosting symbiotic X-ray binary, GX 1+4. The X-ray variability is hypothesized to be caused by onset of two accretion modes: quasi-spherical (small flares) and free-fall (large flares). Increased absorption during dips suggests clumps, while low luminosity phases hint at the propeller regime. Previous NICER/Chandra monitoring could not constrain the source's spin period. NuSTAR's large photon collecting area over broadband energies is vital for interpreting the physics of this enigmatic source that has puzzled the X-ray binary community since discovery.                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pradhan                       Pragati                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       TWO ACCRETION MODES, PROPELLER EFFECT AND CLUMPY WINDS: A RECIPE FOR EXTREME VARIABILITY IN GX 1+4                      ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20240422GX_1p4                                            @poÉ{J#:¿8∆E° ¿É?˚ó$tSéÛ@eÆÊ1¯°@tıîFsÅÿ@Ìãã±9Ò@Ìãõ#Ü31002031003@‘∫KÜ¬&Å@‘®=?|Ìë@◊uèJ#9¡@◊uèJ#9¡SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìã€‚¸ñ0ˇˇˇùHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀(We request 180 ks NuSTAR observations of GX 1+4 (20 ks per month over 9 months) with 2 ks simultaneous NICER data to unravel long-term X-ray variability in this unique neutron star hosting symbiotic X-ray binary, GX 1+4. The X-ray variability is hypothesized to be caused by onset of two accretion modes: quasi-spherical (small flares) and free-fall (large flares). Increased absorption during dips suggests clumps, while low luminosity phases hint at the propeller regime. Previous NICER/Chandra monitoring could not constrain the source's spin period. NuSTAR's large photon collecting area over broadband energies is vital for interpreting the physics of this enigmatic source that has puzzled the X-ray binary community since discovery.                                                             Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Pradhan                       Pragati                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       TWO ACCRETION MODES, PROPELLER EFFECT AND CLUMPY WINDS: A RECIPE FOR EXTREME VARIABILITY IN GX 1+4                      ␀␀␀!␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   NICER                                                                                               ␀␀␀(2/9) / data gaps 33s / Solar flare                                                                                     processed 202404222RXP_J130159d6m635806                             @hlæﬂ§@¿O˝áì›óˆ@r˝¸π#¢úøÔ€Ùá¸π$@v);dZ¨@Ìãõ#Ü@ÌãõÕ´∏131001007001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìã‹6;Ï␀␀Ì Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀':PSR B1259-63 is a millisecond radio pulsar. Interaction of the pulsar wind with the Be star outflow generates a broadband emission from radio up to TeV with a unique GeV flare shortly after the periastron. To study the peculiarities of this system INTEGRAL has approved a 750 ks observation. Interpretation of INTEGRAL data are complicated by the presence of nearby X-ray pulsar 2RXP J130159.6-635806. Within current proposal we ask for 30 ksec observations of 2RXP J130159.6-635806 (Priority 1) accompanied with 30 ksec observations of PSR B1259-63 (Priority 2). These observations will allow us: (i) recover the spectral parameters of 2RXP J130159.6-635806 and use them for the accurate INTEGRAL/ISGRI data analysis: (ii) study the possible short-timescale variations of PSR B1259-63 flux.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Chernyakova                   Maria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       HARD X-RAY VIEW OF 2024 PERIASTRON PASSAGE OF PSR B1259-63                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 201503162RXP_J130159d6m635806                             @hlﬂ§?Â…¿O˛L/É{J@r˛)«y¶µø1&Èx’@v.ú‡uˆ˝@ÌãõÕ´∏1@ÌãÆ±9Ò31001007002@›∂«)^û@›kiÛ∂E¢@ﬂ”.HËß@ﬂ”.HËßSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìã‹=p£◊␀␀Ì Hea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀':PSR B1259-63 is a millisecond radio pulsar. Interaction of the pulsar wind with the Be star outflow generates a broadband emission from radio up to TeV with a unique GeV flare shortly after the periastron. To study the peculiarities of this system INTEGRAL has approved a 750 ks observation. Interpretation of INTEGRAL data are complicated by the presence of nearby X-ray pulsar 2RXP J130159.6-635806. Within current proposal we ask for 30 ksec observations of 2RXP J130159.6-635806 (Priority 1) accompanied with 30 ksec observations of PSR B1259-63 (Priority 2). These observations will allow us: (i) recover the spectral parameters of 2RXP J130159.6-635806 and use them for the accurate INTEGRAL/ISGRI data analysis: (ii) study the possible short-timescale variations of PSR B1259-63 flux.         Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Chernyakova                   Maria                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       HARD X-RAY VIEW OF 2024 PERIASTRON PASSAGE OF PSR B1259-63                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   Swift INTEGRAL                                                                                      ␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                             processed 20240422J154403d67p044610d1                               ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌãÆ±9Ò@ÌãØxVb‹61063005001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @ÌãÿY‚k␀␀Ì Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀'ﬁWhile it has been demonstrated by numerical simulations over the past decade, the causality between galaxy mergers and AGN triggering remains an open question. And a consensus of the AGN occupation rate in mergers is still absent, which is tied to the challenge of detecting heavily obscured and/or intrinsic low-luminosity AGNs. Thanks to the unprecedented sensitivity of NuSTAR at energy band above 10 keV, it stands out as the only instrument with the capability to recover the intrinsic AGN luminosity and the level of obscuration. We propose to carry out observations of seven dual AGN systems with separations < 5 kpc, which signifies the late stage of mergers and are perfect targets to test the merger-induced AGN scenario and study the essential physics of SMBH feeding and feedback.        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Liu                           Xin                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
