N10       PARTICLE ACCELERATION IN A TEV BINARY                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀(2/4) / Solar flare                                                                                                     processed 20240422NGC_7793_P13                                      ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìãœ[‰õø@Ìã–#Ü31002023001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìã„QAú¢ˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀'«NGC 7793 P13 is one of the best targets among the ultra-luminous X-ray pulsars (ULXPs) to study how mass transfer and accretion works in the super-Eddington regime. It shows long-term flux variability, monitored over the last ~10 years, but at the same a rather stable spin-up. Recently it entered a historic bright state, after an off- and dim-state in the last few years. The new bright state now affords us with the ideal opportunity to check for spectral and geometric changes in the source, which would shed new light on accretion physics beyond the Eddington limit, as well as continue monitoring the pulse period. We will build on a very successful monitoring campaign and propose to observe NGC 7793 for 2 50 ks.                                                                                Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Fuerst                        Felix                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
