N10       PARTICLE ACCELERATION IN A TEV BINARY                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316PSR_B1259m63                                      @hsC,•z¿OÏ1&Èy@rˇù≤-VøÎôôôôôö?ˆ≈Åo␀i@ÌåπÍN@Ìå«Õ´∏131002003006@ÿ•ª…ÓÀ˚@ÿiı“Ò©¸@⁄ôòõ•„T@⁄öòõ•„TSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìå„µ¶‡␀␀Ì'Hea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀'Understanding particle acceleration in relativistic shocks occurs is a key goal in high energy astrophysics. We propose to observe a binary star system, containing a pulsar and a massive star, that is known to emit TeV gamma-rays indicating acceleration of particles to high energies. The NuSTAR observations will provide information on the spectrum of electrons accelerated in the pulsar wind/stellar outflow interaction which is complementary to information on the magnetic fields near the acceleration regions that will be obtained from IXPE observations. The observations will provide unique information for a novel class, TeV binaries, and have the potential to provide new insights into relativistic shock acceleration and the propagation of high energy particles away from the shock.          Galactic Compact Sources                                    ␀1    Kaaret                        Philip                        NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
N10       PARTICLE ACCELERATION IN A TEV BINARY                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   IXPE                                                                                                ␀␀␀(3/4) / Solar flare                                                                                                     processed 202404225BZQ_J1150_SADA_24209                             ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìå«Õ´∏1@Ìå»î»)¯61060013001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìå„•ïn␀␀Ì'Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀ﬂWe propose a pilot study to observe with Swift-UVOT/XRT and NuSTAR a well-defined sample of blazars associated with IceCube neutrinos. If these blazars originate neutrinos, they are powered by hadronic processes. Within the blazar hadronic framework, the emission in the X-ray band provides the most sensitive diagnostics of the hadronic contribution to the spectral energy distribution (SED) in these sources. The Swift-UVOT/XRT observations, along with NuSTAR simultaneous spectra, will provide us with broadband, simultaneous multi-wavelength SEDs of the astrophysical objects, that will be tested with theoretical expectations from leptonic and hadronic modelling.                                                                                                                                    Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Buson                         Sara                          NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
