TOO       Targets of Opportunity                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀DDT / Solar flare                                                                                                       archived  20240422J133654d44p171040d36                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌçY±9Ò@ÌçZ?r‘£61001004001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìçqç^oÅˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀p{Detection of sub-parsec separation massive black hole binaries (MBBHs) remain elusive. Following the search for sub-pc MBBH in long-term optical data, we have found 34 excellent candidates. They are expected to be formed from galaxy mergers and therefore have considerable gas reservoir for accretion. We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of three of these MBBH candidates to search for the expected X-ray emission and understand the nature of the accretion occurring. These observations will provide further evidence for the massive black hole binarity and allow us to probe the accretion state and help us understand the link between mergers and AGN. This will allow us to explore the importance of mergers in the formation and evolution of supermassive black holes.                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Foustoul                      Vincent                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
X23       Validating close binary supermassive black hole candidates                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316J133654d44p171040d36                              @iÖì‡ﬁ”@1%÷8ÜYK@uÚV’œ™Œ@RÙ«‚Ç@∏@uÆ#9¿ÎÓ@ÌçZ?r‘£@ÌçÄxVb‹61001004002@Í]∑ú‡v@Í€º£n@ÏbZlÙ!@Ïb˙lÙ!SCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìç®~ìÈ?ˇˇˇùHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀p{Detection of sub-parsec separation massive black hole binaries (MBBHs) remain elusive. Following the search for sub-pc MBBH in long-term optical data, we have found 34 excellent candidates. They are expected to be formed from galaxy mergers and therefore have considerable gas reservoir for accretion. We propose joint XMM-Newton and NuSTAR observations of three of these MBBH candidates to search for the expected X-ray emission and understand the nature of the accretion occurring. These observations will provide further evidence for the massive black hole binarity and allow us to probe the accretion state and help us understand the link between mergers and AGN. This will allow us to explore the importance of mergers in the formation and evolution of supermassive black holes.                 Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Foustoul                      Vincent                       NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
X23       Validating close binary supermassive black hole candidates                                                              ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   XMM                                                                                                 ␀␀␀(1/2) / Solar flares & CME                                                                                              processed 20240422AT2024qfm                                         ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌçÄxVb‹@ÌçÅî»)¯81002403001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìç®hd‹ˇˇˇùHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ÖØWe propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on the recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and continue the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
C25       Mining the transient sky: Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of the fastest explosions                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 processed 20150316AT2024qfm                                         @uÂêñªò»@'˝•ú‡v@VóÂ…‰¿Fîõ•„S¯@]-˚ÁlãD@ÌçÅî»)¯@Ìç£ÍN81002403002@ÊØ#¢úx@Â‚ÜYJı@ËáB√…Ô@ËáB√…ÔSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìçƒ	‹YÅˇˇˇùHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ÖØWe propose an in-depth X-ray study of fast blue optical transients (FBOTs), a new class of transients with luminosities and time scales that challenge traditional SN models. Alternative scenarios include a failed SN of a stripped star, He-shell detonation on a white dwarf, and a SN shock breaking through a dense medium. FBOTs have mostly been studied in the optical/UV regime, which is of thermal origin and it is not sensitive to the nature of the underlying energy source. Here we propose to capitalize on the recent discovery of X-rays from the FBOT AT2018cow with unprecedented properties, and continue the first broad-band X-ray monitoring campaign of FBOTs. The primary goal is to test for the presence of engines driving the explosions and constrain their nature.                            Proposed ToOs and Directors Discretionary Time              ␀1    Margutti                      Raffaella                     NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
C25       Mining the transient sky: Chandra-NuSTAR Observations of the fastest explosions                                         ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                   Chandra                                                                                             ␀␀␀(1/4) ToO / Solar flares                                                                                                processed 20240422SDSS_J131906d79p2533                              ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@Ìç£ÍN@Ìç•èFj61061002001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìç√ı0Ï®␀␀Ì.Hea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀ﬂAWe propose to observe 5 new candidates gamma-ray narrow line Seyfert galaxies (NLSYs) with combined XRT and NuSTAR observation. These sources are detected in the 4FGL catalog, though lacking any counterpart association, and are found to be positionally coincident with known SDSS NLSys. High-quality X-ray data of these 5 candidate gamma-NLSYs provide the only means to confirm the presence of a jet in the NLSy systems (hence classifying them as gamma-NLYs), and study the energetics and properties of these newly detected (possibly) nascent jets. Moreover, only 8 gamma-NLSys are known to date. Therefore these XRT and NuSTAR observations would increase the known sample by 60%.                                                                                                                        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Marcotulli                    Lea                           NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀
