‹“ﬁ?␀␀ÏvHea_23Feb2024_V6.33_nustardas_16Feb22_v2.1.2                                    ␀␀␀␀The baseline NuSTAR extragalactic survey is comprised of three components: one deg^2 of the COSMOS field (50ks per position), 0.3 deg^2 of the ECDFS (200ks per position), and a ~3 deg^2 shallow survey (5ks per position), achieved by targeting 100 Swift-BAT identified AGNs.  Models predict that NuSTAR will detect ~200 AGNs in the COSMOS+E-CDF-S surveys and ~19 Compton-thick AGNs. The shallow survey will deliver a sample of serendipitous detection of AGNs in the NuSTAR field of view.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Harrison                      Fiona                         NULL                          NULL                          USA                           ␀EGS       Extragalactic Surveys                                                                                                   ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Solar flare                                                                                                                       ␀␀␀ / Solar flare                                                                                                          archived  20240422Centaurus_A                                       ¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀¿X¿␀␀␀␀␀@ÌçËÍN@ÌçÈ[‰õø60901034001␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀SLEW                    CPMODESTELLAR   POWER     @Ìé
Õ◊Ãl␀␀ÏvHea_30Jun2014_V6.16_nustardas_28May14_v1.4.1                                    ␀␀$KThe strong fluorescent Fe K alpha line is part of the broad-band reflection spectrum, and a key component of the spectra of Active Galactic Nuclei (AGNs) in X-rays. The exact shape of the emission line depends on the physical conditions of the environment in which it is produced. Therefore, an accurate fitting of its properties, particularly of the Compton shoulder, enables an investigation of the physics and geometry in an AGN. The unique and powerful combination of NuSTAR and XRISM will allow us to probe these conditions with unprecedented sensitivity. This combination will offer the best possible constraints on the geometry of the accretion flow that is possible by fitting the Compton shoulder. We aim to perform this investigation for Centaurus A, which is ideally suited for it.        Active galaxies and Quasars                                 ␀1    Bogensberger                  David                         NULL                          NULL                                                        ␀	N09       INVESTIGATING THE COMPTON SHOULDER OF THE FE K ALPHA LINE IN CENTAURUS A                                                ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀                                                                                                                                  ␀␀␀␀STELLAR                                                                                                                 archived  20150316Centaurus_A                                       @i)S&¡æ¿EÉjÁ’fœ@sSHé@3ìZÖáìﬁ@C…ÓÀ˚@ÌçÈ[‰õø@Ìç˘ÍN60901034002@ÿpO‰%ÆÊ@ÿ>o•„S¯@⁄u‰—∑Y@⁄u‰—∑YSCIENCE                 CPMODEINERTIAL  EIGEN     @Ìé
